(92, 88, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: introduction to web development diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=introduction+to+web+development+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/introduction+to+web+development+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/introduction+to+web+development+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/introduction+to+web+development+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=introduction+to+web+development+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>What is the Web?</h3>\n<p>The World Wide Web (often simply called \"the web\") is a system of interlinked hypertext documents accessed via the Internet. When you type a URL into a browser, you are requesting a resource that lives on a remote computer and the browser renders it for you. The web is built on a set of open standards that allow anyone to create, share, and view content across the globe.</p>\n<h3>Client‑Server Architecture</h3>\n<p>Web applications follow a <strong>client‑server model</strong>. The <strong>client</strong> is typically a web browser running on a user\'s device. It sends HTTP requests to a <strong>server</strong>, which processes the request, performs any necessary logic, and returns an HTTP response containing HTML, CSS, JavaScript, or other data.</p>\n<p>Key points of the architecture:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Stateless requests:</strong> Each HTTP request is independent; the server does not automatically remember previous interactions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ports and protocols:</strong> By default, browsers connect to port <code>80</code> for HTTP and <code>443</code> for HTTPS.</li>\n  <li><strong>Separation of concerns:</strong> The client handles presentation (what the user sees) while the server handles data storage, business logic, and security.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Overview of Web Technologies</h3>\n<p>Modern web development relies on three core technologies:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>HTML (HyperText Markup Language):</strong> Provides the structure of a web page. Elements like <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> define sections of content.</li>\n  <li><strong>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets):</strong> Controls the visual appearance—colors, layout, typography, and responsiveness.</li>\n  <li><strong>JavaScript:</strong> Adds interactivity and dynamic behavior, allowing pages to respond to user actions without reloading.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Beyond these, developers often use:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Version control:</strong> Git helps track changes and collaborate with others.</li>\n  <li><strong>Package managers:</strong> <code>npm</code> (Node Package Manager) or <code>yarn</code> install reusable libraries.</li>\n  <li><strong>Build tools:</strong> Tools like <code>webpack</code> or <code>Vite</code> bundle assets for production.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Setting Up Your Development Environment</h3>\n<p>Before writing any code, you need a comfortable workspace. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Install a code editor:</strong> <a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Visual Studio Code</a> is free, lightweight, and extensible.</li>\n  <li><strong>Install Node.js:</strong> Download the LTS version from <a href=\"https://nodejs.org/\" target=\"_blank\">nodejs.org</a>. This also installs <code>npm</code>, which you will use to manage packages.</li>\n  <li><strong>Verify installations:</strong> Open a terminal and run:\n    <pre><code>node -v\nnpm -v</code></pre>\n    You should see version numbers printed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Create a project folder:</strong>\n    <pre><code>mkdir my-first-website\ncd my-first-website</code></pre>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Initialize a Git repository (optional but recommended):</strong>\n    <pre><code>git init</code></pre>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Start a simple local server:</strong> You can use the built‑in <code>http-server</code> package.\n    <pre><code>npm install -g http-server\nhttp-server .</code></pre>\n    Open <code>http://localhost:8080</code> in your browser to see files served instantly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With these tools in place, you are ready to write your first HTML file, style it with CSS, and add a touch of JavaScript. Throughout the course we will build on this foundation, gradually introducing more complex concepts and workflows.</p>\n<p>Remember: the best way to learn web development is by experimenting. Feel free to modify the example code, break things, and then fix them. This hands‑on approach will cement the ideas presented today.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(93, 89, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: HTML basics beginner tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=HTML+basics+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/HTML+basics+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/HTML+basics+beginner+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/HTML+basics+beginner+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=HTML+basics+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding the HTML Document Structure</h3>\n<p>Every web page starts with a <strong>well‑defined structure</strong>. The browser reads the HTML file from top to bottom, looking for the <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> declaration, which tells it to use the latest HTML standard. Directly after that, the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element wraps the entire document. Inside <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> you will find two main sections: <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>. The <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> contains meta‑information such as the page title, character set, and links to style sheets, while the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> holds all the visible content that users interact with.</p>\n<p>Here is a minimal skeleton you will use for almost every page you create:</p>\n<code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My First Page&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    <!-- Page content goes here -->\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code>\n\n<h3>Common Tags You Will Use Frequently</h3>\n<p>While the document skeleton is essential, the real work happens inside the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>. Below are the most common tags you will encounter as a beginner:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>&lt;div&gt;</strong> – a generic container used to group other elements and apply CSS styling.</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;p&gt;</strong> – defines a paragraph of text.</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;a&gt;</strong> – creates a hyperlink to another page or resource.</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;h1&gt; to &lt;h6&gt;</strong> – heading elements, with <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> being the most important.</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;ul&gt;</strong> and <strong>&lt;ol&gt;</strong> – unordered (bulleted) and ordered (numbered) lists.</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;li&gt;</strong> – list item, used inside <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These tags can be combined in countless ways to build a page layout that is both semantic and accessible.</p>\n\n<h3>Creating Links and Lists</h3>\n<p>Links are the backbone of the web. The <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag requires an <code>href</code> attribute that points to the destination URL. You can also add a <code>target=\"_blank\"</code> attribute to open the link in a new tab.</p>\n<code>&lt;a href=\"https://www.example.com\" target=\"_blank\"&gt;Visit Example&lt;/a&gt;</code>\n<p>Lists help organize information. An unordered list (<code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>) uses bullet points, while an ordered list (<code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>) numbers each item. Each list item is wrapped in an <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> tag.</p>\n<code>&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;First bullet&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Second bullet&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Third bullet&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n\n&lt;ol&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;First step&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Second step&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Third step&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ol&gt;</code>\n\n<h3>Simple Form Elements</h3>\n<p>Forms let users send data to a server. Even at the beginner level, you should know the basic elements:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>&lt;form&gt;</strong> – the container that defines where the data will be submitted. It uses <code>action</code> (the URL to send data to) and <code>method</code> (usually <code>GET</code> or <code>POST</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;input&gt;</strong> – creates various types of fields. The <code>type</code> attribute determines the kind of data (e.g., <code>text</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>password</code>, <code>checkbox</code>, <code>radio</code>, <code>submit</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;textarea&gt;</strong> – a multi‑line text box for longer messages.</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;label&gt;</strong> – associates a text description with a form control, improving accessibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below is a simple contact form that demonstrates these elements:</p>\n<code>&lt;form action=\"/submit-contact\" method=\"POST\"&gt;\n  &lt;label for=\"name\"&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=\"text\" id=\"name\" name=\"name\" required&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n\n  &lt;label for=\"email\"&gt;Email:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" required&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n\n  &lt;label for=\"message\"&gt;Message:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;textarea id=\"message\" name=\"message\" rows=\"4\" cols=\"30\"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n\n  &lt;input type=\"submit\" value=\"Send Message\"&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</code>\n<p>Notice how each <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> uses the <code>for</code> attribute that matches the <code>id</code> of its corresponding input. This practice enhances usability for screen readers and keyboard navigation.</p>\n\n<h3>Putting It All Together</h3>\n<p>Now that you have explored the document skeleton, common tags, links, lists, and basic form elements, try building a small personal homepage. Start with the skeleton, add a heading, a paragraph, a navigation list of links, and a contact form at the bottom. Experiment by changing the tag order, adding <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> containers, and applying simple inline styles to see how the page reacts.</p>\n<p>Remember: HTML is the backbone of every website. Mastering these basics will give you the confidence to move on to CSS for styling and JavaScript for interactivity.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(94, 90, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS fundamentals beginner tutorial diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+fundamentals+beginner+tutorial+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+fundamentals+beginner+tutorial+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+fundamentals+beginner+tutorial+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+fundamentals+beginner+tutorial+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+fundamentals+beginner+tutorial+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Syntax and Selectors</h3>\n<p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is the language that controls the visual presentation of HTML documents. A CSS rule consists of a <strong>selector</strong> and a <strong>declaration block</strong>. The selector tells the browser which element(s) to style, while the declaration block contains one or more <code>property: value</code> pairs, each ending with a semicolon.</p>\n<code>selector {\n    property: value;\n    property2: value2;\n}</code>\n<p>Common selector types for beginners include:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><code>element</code> – selects all elements of that type (e.g., <code>p</code>).</li>\n    <li><code>.class</code> – selects elements with a specific class attribute (e.g., <code>.highlight</code>).</li>\n    <li><code>#id</code> – selects a single element with a unique ID (e.g., <code>#header</code>).</li>\n    <li><code>element, element</code> – groups selectors to apply the same styles to multiple elements.</li>\n    <li><code>element > child</code> – selects direct children.</li>\n    <li><code>element descendant</code> – selects any descendant, not just direct children.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Understanding the cascade, specificity, and inheritance is essential because they determine which rules win when multiple selectors target the same element.</p>\n<h3>Box Model Concepts</h3>\n<p>Every HTML element is rendered as a rectangular box. The CSS box model describes how the size of that box is calculated. It consists of four parts, from the inside out:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Content</strong> – the actual text, image, or other media.</li>\n    <li><strong>Padding</strong> – space between the content and the border.</li>\n    <li><strong>Border</strong> – a line that wraps the padding and content.</li>\n    <li><strong>Margin</strong> – space outside the border that separates the element from its neighbors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By default, the <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> properties refer only to the content area. To include padding and border in the total size, you can use <code>box-sizing: border-box;</code>. This makes layout calculations much easier, especially when building responsive designs.</p>\n<code>.box {\n    width: 200px;\n    padding: 20px;\n    border: 2px solid #333;\n    margin: 15px;\n    box-sizing: border-box; /* includes padding & border in the 200px */\n}</code>\n<h3>Styling Text and Colors</h3>\n<p>Typography is a powerful way to convey hierarchy and brand personality. CSS provides many properties for controlling text appearance:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><code>font-family</code> – chooses the typeface (e.g., <code>\"Arial\", sans-serif</code>).</li>\n    <li><code>font-size</code> – sets the size; using relative units like <code>rem</code> or <code>em</code> improves accessibility.</li>\n    <li><code>font-weight</code> – controls boldness (<code>normal</code>, <code>bold</code>, or numeric values).</li>\n    <li><code>line-height</code> – defines the space between lines of text.</li>\n    <li><code>text-align</code> – aligns text horizontally (<code>left</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>justify</code>).</li>\n    <li><code>color</code> – sets the text color using named colors, hex, RGB, or HSL values.</li>\n    <li><code>background-color</code> – fills the element\'s background.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of a readable paragraph style:</p>\n<code>p {\n    font-family: \"Helvetica Neue\", Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;\n    font-size: 1rem; /* 16px by default */\n    line-height: 1.5;\n    color: #333333;\n    background-color: #f9f9f9;\n    padding: 10px;\n    border-radius: 4px;\n}</code>\n<h3>Basic Layout with Flexbox</h3>\n<p>Flexbox (Flexible Box Layout) is a modern CSS layout module that makes it simple to arrange items in rows or columns, even when their size is unknown or dynamic. The two main concepts are the <strong>flex container</strong> and the <strong>flex items</strong> inside it.</p>\n<p>To enable Flexbox, set <code>display: flex;</code> on the container. Then you can control direction, alignment, spacing, and order with a handful of properties:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><code>flex-direction</code> – <code>row</code> (default) or <code>column</code>.</li>\n    <li><code>justify-content</code> – aligns items along the main axis (<code>flex-start</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>space-between</code>, etc.).</li>\n    <li><code>align-items</code> – aligns items along the cross axis (<code>stretch</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>flex-start</code>).</li>\n    <li><code>gap</code> – adds consistent spacing between items without extra margins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Simple navigation bar example:</p>\n<code>.nav {\n    display: flex;\n    flex-direction: row;\n    justify-content: space-between;\n    align-items: center;\n    background-color: #0044cc;\n    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n    gap: 1rem;\n}\n\n.nav a {\n    color: #ffffff;\n    text-decoration: none;\n    font-weight: bold;\n}</code>\n<p>In this example the navigation links automatically distribute themselves across the available width, staying centered vertically and spaced evenly. Flexbox also allows items to grow or shrink with <code>flex-grow</code> and <code>flex-shrink</code>, which you will explore in later lessons.</p>\n<p>By mastering these four foundational topics—syntax & selectors, the box model, text & color styling, and Flexbox layout—you will be equipped to build clean, responsive webpages from scratch.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(95, 91, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: beginner web development building first webpage screenshot</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=beginner+web+development+building+first+webpage+screenshot\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/beginner+web+development+building+first+webpage+screenshot\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/beginner+web+development+building+first+webpage+screenshot/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/beginner+web+development+building+first+webpage+screenshot/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=beginner+web+development+building+first+webpage+screenshot\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Combining HTML and CSS</h3>\n<p>When you start building a webpage, the first step is to understand how HTML provides the structure while CSS adds visual style. HTML defines the elements—headings, paragraphs, images, links—using tags such as <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>. CSS, on the other hand, controls colors, fonts, spacing, and layout. By linking a CSS file to your HTML document, you can keep the markup clean and maintain a consistent look across multiple pages.</p>\n<p><strong>Key point:</strong> Keep HTML focused on meaning and content, and let CSS handle presentation.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Write semantic HTML tags to describe the purpose of each section.</li>\n  <li>Use CSS selectors to target those tags and apply styles.</li>\n  <li>Separate concerns by placing CSS in an external stylesheet.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Responsive Design Principles</h3>\n<p>Responsive design ensures that your webpage looks good on phones, tablets, and desktop monitors. The core idea is to let the layout adapt to the screen width using flexible grids, fluid images, and media queries.</p>\n<p><strong>Three fundamental principles:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Fluid grids:</strong> Use percentages instead of fixed pixel values for widths, so columns expand or shrink with the viewport.</li>\n  <li><strong>Flexible images:</strong> Apply <code>max-width: 100%;</code> so images never exceed their container\'s width.</li>\n  <li><strong>Media queries:</strong> Write CSS rules that activate at specific breakpoints, for example:\n    <code>@media (max-width: 768px) { /* tablet styles */ }</code>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>By combining these techniques, you can create a single HTML file that automatically re‑flows its content for any device.</p>\n<h3>Using External Stylesheets</h3>\n<p>External stylesheets are the preferred way to apply CSS to a website. They keep your HTML tidy, make it easier to reuse styles, and improve page load performance because browsers cache the CSS file.</p>\n<p>To link an external stylesheet, place a <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> element inside the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of your HTML document:</p>\n<code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My First Webpage&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome!&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This page uses an external CSS file.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code>\n<p>In <code>styles.css</code>, you can write all of your styling rules. For example:</p>\n<code>body {\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  color: #2c3e50;\n  text-align: center;\n  margin-top: 20px;\n}\n\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  h1 { font-size: 1.5rem; }\n}</code>\n<p>Notice how the media query inside the stylesheet automatically adjusts the heading size on smaller screens.</p>\n<h3>Testing in Browsers</h3>\n<p>Testing is a crucial step before you publish a site. Different browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge) may interpret HTML and CSS slightly differently. Here are some beginner‑friendly practices:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Open the page in multiple browsers:</strong> Simply drag the <code>.html</code> file into each browser window and compare the layout.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use developer tools:</strong> Press <code>F12</code> (or right‑click → Inspect) to open the console, view applied styles, and debug layout issues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resize the window:</strong> Manually adjust the width to see how your responsive breakpoints behave.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test on real devices:</strong> If possible, open the page on a smartphone or tablet to verify touch‑friendly spacing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When you discover a problem, the developer tools let you edit CSS live. Once you find a fix, update <code>styles.css</code> and refresh the page.\n\n<p><strong>Quick checklist for a first‑page test:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>All text is readable and not clipped.</li>\n  <li>Images scale correctly and maintain aspect ratio.</li>\n  <li>Navigation links (if any) work.</li>\n  <li>No console errors appear.</li>\n  <li>Responsive breakpoints trigger at the intended widths.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By following these steps—combining clean HTML with external CSS, applying responsive design principles, and testing across browsers—you will have a solid foundation for building more complex sites in future lessons.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(96, 92, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript beginner tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+beginner+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+beginner+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Syntax and Data Types</h3><p>JavaScript is a lightweight, interpreted programming language that runs in the browser and on the server (Node.js). Its syntax is similar to other C‑style languages, which makes it easy to pick up if you have seen languages like C, Java, or Python. The language is case‑sensitive and uses <code>{}</code> braces to define blocks of code, <code>;</code> to terminate statements (although the semicolon is optional in many cases), and <code>//</code> or <code>/* */</code> for comments.</p><p>Understanding data types is the first step toward writing reliable code. JavaScript has a small set of primitive types:</p><ul><li><strong>Number</strong> – represents both integer and floating‑point values (e.g., <code>42</code>, <code>3.14</code>).</li><li><strong>String</strong> – a sequence of characters enclosed in single, double, or back‑ticks (template literals) (e.g., <code>\"Hello\"</code>, <code>\'World\'</code>, <code>`Template ${value}`</code>).</li><li><strong>Boolean</strong> – only <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</li><li><strong>Null</strong> – a deliberate non‑value, written as <code>null</code>.</li><li><strong>Undefined</strong> – a variable that has been declared but not assigned a value.</li><li><strong>Symbol</strong> – a unique identifier, useful for meta‑programming.</li></ul><p>In addition to primitives, JavaScript provides two complex types: <strong>Object</strong> and <strong>Array</strong>. Objects are collections of key‑value pairs, while arrays are ordered lists of values accessed by numeric indices.</p><h3>Variables and Operators</h3><p>Variables store data for later use. Modern JavaScript recommends using <code>let</code> for block‑scoped mutable variables and <code>const</code> for block‑scoped constants. The older <code>var</code> keyword is function‑scoped and can lead to confusing hoisting behavior, so it should be avoided in new code.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code>let age = 25; // mutable\nconst name = \"Alice\"; // immutable\nvar oldStyle = true; // avoid this</code></pre><p>JavaScript supports a rich set of operators:</p><ul><li>Arithmetic: <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>**</code> (exponentiation).</li><li>Assignment: <code>=</code>, <code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code>, etc.</li><li>Comparison: <code>==</code>, <code>===</code> (strict equality), <code>!=</code>, <code>!==</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>.</li><li>Logical: <code>&amp;&amp;</code> (AND), <code>||</code> (OR), <code>!</code> (NOT).</li><li>Conditional (ternary): <code>condition ? exprIfTrue : exprIfFalse</code>.</li></ul><p>Remember that <code>==</code> performs type coercion, while <code>===</code> checks both value and type. For beginners, always prefer <code>===</code> and <code>!==</code> to avoid unexpected bugs.</p><h3>Embedding Scripts in HTML</h3><p>To make a web page interactive, you embed JavaScript directly inside HTML files or link to external script files. The two most common methods are:</p><ul><li><strong>Inline script</strong> using the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag inside the HTML document.</li><li><strong>External script</strong> by referencing a separate <code>.js</code> file with the <code>src</code> attribute.</li></ul><p>Example of an inline script:</p><pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Inline Example&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Hello, World!&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;script&gt;\n    const message = \"Welcome to JavaScript!\";\n    alert(message);\n  &lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre><p>And an external script:</p><pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;External Example&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;script src=\"script.js\" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Check the console&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre><p>The <code>defer</code> attribute ensures the script runs after the HTML has been parsed, preventing “undefined” errors when the script tries to access DOM elements that are not yet created.</p><h3>Console Debugging</h3><p>Every modern browser provides a JavaScript console, a powerful tool for inspecting variables, tracking execution flow, and catching errors. The most common method is <code>console.log()</code>, which prints values to the console.</p><pre><code>let total = 0;\nfor (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  total += i;\n  console.log(\'After adding\', i, \'total is\', total);\n}\nconsole.log(\'Final total:\', total);</code></pre><p>Other useful console methods include:</p><ul><li><code>console.error()</code> – displays error messages in red.</li><li><code>console.warn()</code> – shows warnings.</li><li><code>console.table()</code> – renders arrays or objects as tables.</li><li><code>console.group()</code> and <code>console.groupEnd()</code> – organize related logs.</li></ul><p>When an error occurs, the browser highlights the line in the source file and provides a stack trace. By placing strategic <code>console.log()</code> statements, you can narrow down the source of a bug without stopping the page execution.</p><p>Practice tip: Open the developer tools (usually <strong>F12</strong> or <strong>Ctrl+Shift+I</strong>), switch to the “Console” tab, and start typing JavaScript commands directly. This interactive environment helps you experiment with syntax and see immediate results.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(97, 93, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript DOM manipulation tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+DOM+manipulation+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+DOM+manipulation+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+DOM+manipulation+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+DOM+manipulation+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+DOM+manipulation+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to the Document Object Model (DOM)</h3><p>The DOM is a programming interface for HTML and XML documents. It represents the page so that programs can change the document structure, style, and content. In JavaScript, the DOM allows you to interact with the page as a tree of nodes, each node being an element, attribute, or piece of text.</p><h3>Selecting DOM Elements</h3><p>Before you can manipulate anything, you need to locate the element you want to work with. JavaScript provides several methods for selecting elements:</p><ul><li><strong>document.getElementById(\'id\')</strong> – returns a single element with the matching <code>id</code> attribute.</li><li><strong>document.getElementsByClassName(\'class\')</strong> – returns an HTMLCollection of all elements that share the given class.</li><li><strong>document.getElementsByTagName(\'tag\')</strong> – returns an HTMLCollection of elements with the specified tag name.</li><li><strong>document.querySelector(\'selector\')</strong> – returns the first element that matches a CSS selector.</li><li><strong>document.querySelectorAll(\'selector\')</strong> – returns a NodeList of all elements that match the selector.</li></ul><p>Example:</p><code>const button = document.querySelector(\'#myButton\');<br>const items = document.querySelectorAll(\'.list-item\');</code><h3>Event Handling</h3><p>Events are actions that occur in the browser, such as clicks, key presses, or mouse movements. To respond to an event, you attach an <strong>event listener</strong> to a DOM node.</p><ul><li><strong>addEventListener(type, listener, options)</strong> – registers a callback function for a specific event type.</li><li><strong>removeEventListener(type, listener)</strong> – removes a previously registered listener.</li></ul><p>Common event types include <code>click</code>, <code>mouseover</code>, <code>keyup</code>, and <code>submit</code>. Here is a simple click handler:</p><code>button.addEventListener(\'click\', function(event) {<br>    console.log(\'Button was clicked!\');<br>});</code><h3>Modifying Content and Styles Dynamically</h3><p>Once you have a reference to an element, you can change its content, attributes, and CSS styles.</p><ul><li><strong>textContent</strong> – sets or returns the text inside an element.</li><li><strong>innerHTML</strong> – sets or returns the HTML markup inside an element.</li><li><strong>setAttribute(name, value)</strong> – changes or adds an attribute.</li><li><strong>style.property = value</strong> – directly modifies an inline CSS property.</li></ul><p>Example of updating a paragraph and changing its background color:</p><code>const message = document.querySelector(\'#message\');<br>message.textContent = \'Welcome to Day 6!\';<br>message.style.backgroundColor = \'#e0f7fa\';</code><h3>Simple Interactive Features</h3><p>Combining selection, events, and dynamic updates lets you build interactive widgets. Below is a tiny “to‑do list” that demonstrates all the concepts.</p><code><!-- HTML structure --><br><div id=\'todoApp\'>\n  <h4>My To‑Do List</h4>\n  <input type=\'text\' id=\'newTask\' placeholder=\'Enter a task\'>\n  <button id=\'addBtn\'>Add</button>\n  <ul id=\'taskList\'></ul>\n</div>\n\n<!-- JavaScript logic -->\n<script>\n  const input = document.querySelector(\'#newTask\');\n  const addBtn = document.querySelector(\'#addBtn\');\n  const list = document.querySelector(\'#taskList\');\n\n  addBtn.addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\n    const taskText = input.value.trim();\n    if (taskText === \'\') return;\n\n    const li = document.createElement(\'li\');\n    li.textContent = taskText;\n    li.style.cursor = \'pointer\';\n    // Strike‑through on click\n    li.addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\n      this.style.textDecoration = this.style.textDecoration === \'line-through\' ? \'none\' : \'line-through\';\n    });\n    list.appendChild(li);\n    input.value = \'\';\n  });\n</script></code><p>In this example you see:</p><ul><li>How to <strong>select</strong> inputs, buttons, and lists.</li><li>How to <strong>listen</strong> for a click event on the button.</li><li>How to <strong>create</strong> new DOM nodes (<code>document.createElement</code>).</li><li>How to <strong>modify</strong> text and inline styles dynamically.</li><li>How to attach additional listeners to newly created elements.</li></ul><p>Practice by extending the widget: add a “remove” button next to each item, or store the list in <code>localStorage</code> so it persists across page reloads.</p><h3>Key Takeaways</h3><ul><li>Use <code>querySelector</code> and <code>querySelectorAll</code> for flexible element selection.</li><li>Attach event listeners with <code>addEventListener</code> to make pages interactive.</li><li>Change content with <code>textContent</code> or <code>innerHTML</code>, and style elements via the <code>style</code> property.</li><li>Creating, appending, and removing nodes lets you build dynamic interfaces without reloading the page.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06');
INSERT INTO `content_blocks` (`id`, `topic_id`, `type`, `content`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(98, 94, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: personal profile webpage beginner tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=personal+profile+webpage+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/personal+profile+webpage+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/personal+profile+webpage+beginner+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/personal+profile+webpage+beginner+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=personal+profile+webpage+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Putting HTML, CSS, and JavaScript Together</h3>\n<p>By the end of this course you have written separate HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files. The final step is to <strong>link them together</strong> so that the browser can render a complete, interactive page. The <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> tag in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section connects a stylesheet, while the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag (usually placed just before the closing <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>) loads a JavaScript file.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>&lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"&gt;</code> – tells the browser to apply <code>styles.css</code> to the document.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;script src=\"app.js\" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code> – loads <code>app.js</code> after the HTML is parsed (the <code>defer</code> attribute is optional but recommended for performance).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the files are correctly linked, any changes you make in <code>styles.css</code> or <code>app.js</code> will instantly affect the page without needing to edit the HTML again.</p>\n\n<h3>Creating a Personal Profile Page</h3>\n<p>For this mini‑project you will build a simple personal profile page that showcases the three core technologies you have learned. The page will contain:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A header with your name and a short tagline.</li>\n  <li>A profile picture styled with CSS (rounded corners, shadow, and responsive size).</li>\n  <li>A short biography written in HTML.</li>\n  <li>An interactive \"Show More\" button that toggles additional details using JavaScript.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below is a skeleton you can copy into <code>index.html</code>. Fill in your own content where indicated.</p>\n<code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;My Personal Profile&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Your Name&lt;/h1>\n    &lt;p class=\"tagline\"&gt;A short, memorable tagline&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n  \n  &lt;section class=\"profile\"&gt;\n    &lt;img src=\"your-photo.jpg\" alt=\"Your Photo\" class=\"profile-pic\"&gt;\n    &lt;article class=\"bio\"&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Write a brief biography here. Mention your interests, background, and what you are learning.&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;button id=\"toggleBtn\"&gt;Show More&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;div id=\"moreInfo\" class=\"hidden\"&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;Add any extra details, projects, or links you want visitors to see.&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/article&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n\n  &lt;script src=\"app.js\" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code>\n<p>In <code>styles.css</code> you might add:</p>\n<code>.profile-pic {\n  width: 150px;\n  border-radius: 50%;\n  box-shadow: 0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2);\n  display: block;\n  margin: 0 auto;\n}\n.hidden { display: none; }\n</code>\n<p>And in <code>app.js</code> the toggle logic:</p>\n<code>document.getElementById(\'toggleBtn\').addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\n  const more = document.getElementById(\'moreInfo\');\n  const isHidden = more.classList.toggle(\'hidden\');\n  this.textContent = isHidden ? \'Show More\' : \'Show Less\';\n});</code>\n<p>When you open <code>index.html</code> in a browser you should see a clean, responsive profile page with a working button.</p>\n\n<h3>Review of Key Concepts</h3>\n<p>Before you take the quiz, review these fundamental ideas:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>HTML structure:</strong> elements, attributes, semantic tags, and the document outline.</li>\n  <li><strong>CSS styling:</strong> selectors, box model, layout techniques (flexbox, grid), and responsive design.</li>\n  <li><strong>JavaScript basics:</strong> variables, functions, DOM manipulation, event handling, and the importance of <code>defer</code> or <code>async</code> when loading scripts.</li>\n  <li><strong>File linking:</strong> how <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags connect external resources to an HTML page.</li>\n  <li><strong>Debugging tools:</strong> using browser DevTools to inspect elements, view console errors, and test CSS changes in real time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Make sure you can explain each point in your own words; this will help you during the quiz and future projects.</p>\n\n<h3>Quiz to Assess Learning</h3>\n<p>The following multiple‑choice questions cover the material you have practiced today. Answer them without looking at the code examples, then check the explanations to see why each answer is correct.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
