(100, 95, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: HTML form elements and input types tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=HTML+form+elements+and+input+types+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/HTML+form+elements+and+input+types+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/HTML+form+elements+and+input+types+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/HTML+form+elements+and+input+types+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=HTML+form+elements+and+input+types+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> Element</h3>\n<p>The <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> tag is the container that groups all the interactive controls you want to collect from a user. When a browser encounters a <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>, it knows that the enclosed elements belong together and can be sent to a server for processing. The most important attributes of the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> are:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>action</strong>: Specifies the URL where the form data will be submitted. If omitted, the data is sent to the same page.</li>\n  <li><strong>method</strong>: Determines how the data is transmitted. The two most common values are <code>GET</code> (appends data to the URL) and <code>POST</code> (sends data in the request body).</li>\n  <li><strong>autocomplete</strong>: Controls whether the browser should remember and suggest previously entered values.</li>\n  <li><strong>novalidate</strong>: Disables built‑in HTML validation when present.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of a basic form structure:</p>\n<code>&lt;form action=&quot;submit.php&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;\n    ...form controls go here...\n&lt;/form&gt;</code>\n\n<h3>Common Input Types</h3>\n<p>HTML5 introduced many specialized <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> types that improve user experience and validation. Below is a quick reference of the most frequently used types for beginners:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>text</strong>: Single‑line text entry.\n    <code>&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;username&quot; placeholder=&quot;Enter your name&quot;&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>password</strong>: Masks characters for sensitive data.\n    <code>&lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; name=&quot;pwd&quot;&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>email</strong>: Validates that the entered value follows an email format.\n    <code>&lt;input type=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; required&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>number</strong>: Allows only numeric input and can define <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, and <code>step</code>.\n    <code>&lt;input type=&quot;number&quot; name=&quot;age&quot; min=&quot;0&quot; max=&quot;120&quot;&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>date</strong>: Provides a calendar picker.\n    <code>&lt;input type=&quot;date&quot; name=&quot;birthday&quot;&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>checkbox</strong>: Lets users select zero or more options.\n    <code>&lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; name=&quot;subscribe&quot; value=&quot;yes&quot;&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>radio</strong>: Allows selection of exactly one option from a group.\n    <code>&lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot; name=&quot;gender&quot; value=&quot;male&quot;&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>submit</strong>: Sends the form data to the server.\n    <code>&lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;Send&quot;&gt;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choosing the right input type not only guides the user but also reduces the amount of custom JavaScript validation you need to write.</p>\n\n<h3>Labels and Accessibility</h3>\n<p>Every form control should have an associated <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>. Labels improve usability for sighted users and are essential for screen‑reader users. There are two ways to associate a label with a control:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Wrap the control inside the <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> element.\n    <code>&lt;label&gt;Username: &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;user&quot;&gt;&lt;/label&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Use the <code>for</code> attribute on the label that matches the control’s <code>id</code>.\n    <code>&lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email address&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input type=&quot;email&quot; id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot;&gt;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Key accessibility tips:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Always provide clear, concise text inside <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>.</li>\n  <li>When a control is required, indicate it both visually (e.g., an asterisk) and programmatically using the <code>required</code> attribute.</li>\n  <li>Group related controls with <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> and provide a <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> for context.</li>\n  <li>Use ARIA attributes sparingly; native HTML elements already convey most needed information.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Form Submission Basics</h3>\n<p>When a user clicks a <code>submit</code> button, the browser packages the form data according to the <code>method</code> attribute and sends it to the URL defined by <code>action</code>. Here are the steps in simple terms:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The browser validates each control that has built‑in constraints (<code>required</code>, <code>type=&quot;email&quot;</code>, <code>pattern</code>, etc.).</li>\n  <li>If validation fails, the submission is halted and the browser displays an error message.</li>\n  <li>When validation passes, the data is serialized.\n    <ul>\n      <li>For <code>GET</code>, the data appears in the query string (e.g., <code>?name=John&amp;age=30</code>).</li>\n      <li>For <code>POST</code>, the data is placed in the request body, which is more secure for sensitive information.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>The server receives the request, processes the data, and typically returns a new HTML page or a JSON response.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Simple example of a complete form that collects a name and email, then sends the data via <code>POST</code> to <code>process.php</code>:</p>\n<code>&lt;form action=&quot;process.php&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;label for=&quot;name&quot;&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; id=&quot;name&quot; name=&quot;name&quot; required&gt;\n    &lt;br&gt;\n    &lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email:&lt;/label&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;email&quot; id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; required&gt;\n    &lt;br&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;Register&quot;&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</code>\n<p>Understanding these fundamentals gives you the confidence to build interactive, accessible, and functional web forms that work across browsers and devices.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(101, 96, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS selectors and box model diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+selectors+and+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+selectors+and+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+selectors+and+box+model+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+selectors+and+box+model+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+selectors+and+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Fundamentals: Selectors &amp; Box Model</h3><p>Welcome to Day 9 of our beginner web development course! Today we dive into two core concepts that every front‑end developer must master: <strong>CSS selectors</strong> and the <strong>CSS box model</strong>. Understanding how to target HTML elements and how those elements are rendered on the page will give you the confidence to style any layout.</p><h3>1. CSS Selectors Overview</h3><p>Selectors are patterns used by CSS to decide which HTML elements a set of style rules applies to. The most common selector types are <strong>type selectors</strong>, <strong>class selectors</strong>, and <strong>ID selectors</strong>. Each has a distinct syntax and use case.</p><ul><li><strong>Type selector</strong> – targets elements by their tag name. Example: <code>p { color: #333; }</code> will style every <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> element on the page.</li><li><strong>Class selector</strong> – targets elements that have a specific <code>class</code> attribute. The syntax uses a dot (<code>.</code>) followed by the class name: <code>.highlight { background: yellow; }</code>. Multiple elements can share the same class, making it perfect for reusable styles.</li><li><strong>ID selector</strong> – targets a single element with a unique <code>id</code> attribute. The syntax uses a hash (<code>#</code>) followed by the ID: <code>#main-header { font-size: 2rem; }</code>. Because IDs must be unique within a page, this selector has the highest specificity among the three.</li></ul><h3>2. Combinators: Connecting Selectors</h3><p>Combinators let you create more precise rules by describing relationships between elements. There are four main combinators:</p><ul><li><strong>Descendant combinator (space)</strong> – selects elements that are nested anywhere inside another element. Example: <code>nav a { color: blue; }</code> styles all <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> links inside a <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> element.</li><li><strong>Child combinator (<code>&gt;</code>)</strong> – selects only direct children. Example: <code>ul &gt; li { list-style: none; }</code> affects only <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> elements that are immediate children of a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>.</li><li><strong>Adjacent sibling combinator (<code>+</code>)</strong> – selects the element that immediately follows another sibling. Example: <code>h2 + p { margin-top: 0; }</code>.</li><li><strong>General sibling combinator (<code>~</code>)</strong> – selects all siblings that follow a given element. Example: <code>.note ~ .warning { color: red; }</code>.</li></ul><p>By combining these selectors, you can write CSS that is both powerful and maintainable.</p><h3>3. The CSS Box Model</h3><p>Every HTML element is rendered as a rectangular box. The box model describes how the size of this box is calculated and how spacing is applied. The model consists of four layers, from the inside out:</p><ul><li><strong>Content</strong> – the actual text, image, or other media inside the element.</li><li><strong>Padding</strong> – space between the content and the element’s border. Padding is transparent and adds to the element’s total width and height.</li><li><strong>Border</strong> – a line that wraps around the padding and content. You can control its width, style, and color.</li><li><strong>Margin</strong> – the outermost space that separates the element from its neighboring elements. Margins collapse in certain situations, which is an important behavior to understand.</li></ul><p>When you set <code>width</code> or <code>height</code> on an element, those values refer only to the content area. The total space the element occupies is calculated as:</p><code>total width = width + padding-left + padding-right + border-left + border-right + margin-left + margin-right</code><p>and similarly for height. Modern CSS provides the <code>box-sizing</code> property to change this calculation. Setting <code>box-sizing: border-box;</code> makes the <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> include padding and border, which often simplifies layout work.</p><h3>4. Practical Examples</h3><p>Below are short snippets that illustrate the concepts we just covered.</p><ul><li><strong>Type, class, and ID selectors</strong>:</li></ul><code>/* Type selector */\nh1 { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; }\n\n/* Class selector */\n.button-primary { background: #0066cc; color: #fff; padding: 0.5rem 1rem; }\n\n/* ID selector */\n#footer { background: #222; color: #ddd; padding: 2rem; }</code><ul><li><strong>Combinator example</strong> (direct child of a navigation bar):</li></ul><code>nav &gt; ul &gt; li &gt; a { text-decoration: none; }</code><ul><li><strong>Box model styling</strong>:</li></ul><code>.card {\n  width: 300px;               /* content width */\n  padding: 20px;              /* inner space */\n  border: 2px solid #ccc;    /* border thickness */\n  margin: 15px auto;          /* outer space */\n  box-sizing: border-box;    /* include padding & border in width */\n}</code><p>Experiment with these rules in a simple HTML file. Change the values of <code>padding</code>, <code>margin</code>, and <code>border</code> to see how the element’s size and position shift.</p><h3>5. Recap</h3><p>Today you learned how to:</p><ul><li>Select elements using type, class, and ID selectors.</li><li>Combine selectors with descendant, child, adjacent sibling, and general sibling combinators.</li><li>Understand each component of the CSS box model—content, padding, border, and margin.</li><li>Apply <code>box-sizing</code> to simplify width calculations.</li></ul><p>Mastering these fundamentals will empower you to build clean, responsive layouts and write CSS that scales as your projects grow.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(102, 97, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS Flexbox layout example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+Flexbox+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+Flexbox+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+Flexbox+layout+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+Flexbox+layout+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+Flexbox+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Flexbox</h3><p>Flexbox, short for <strong>Flexible Box Layout</strong>, is a modern CSS module that makes it easier to design responsive layouts without using floats or positioning tricks. In this lesson we will explore the core concepts of a flex container, how to control the direction of items, and how to align and distribute space using justification and alignment properties. By the end of the day you will be able to create simple row‑ and column‑based layouts that adapt gracefully to different screen sizes.</p><h3>Creating a Flex Container</h3><p>The first step is to turn a regular block element into a flex container. This is done by applying <code>display: flex;</code> (or <code>display: inline-flex;</code>) to the parent element. Once the container is defined, all of its direct children automatically become flex items.</p><ul><li><strong>display: flex;</strong> – creates a block‑level flex container.</li><li><strong>display: inline-flex;</strong> – creates an inline‑level flex container.</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code>&lt;div class=\"flex-container\"&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Item 1&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Item 2&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Item 3&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n.flex-container {\n  display: flex;\n}\n</code></pre><h3>Flex Direction</h3><p>The <code>flex-direction</code> property determines the main axis along which flex items are laid out. It accepts four values:</p><ul><li><code>row</code> – items flow horizontally from left to right (default).</li><li><code>row-reverse</code> – items flow horizontally from right to left.</li><li><code>column</code> – items flow vertically from top to bottom.</li><li><code>column-reverse</code> – items flow vertically from bottom to top.</li></ul><p>Changing the direction is as simple as adding <code>flex-direction: column;</code> to the container’s CSS.</p><h3>Justify Content and Align Items</h3><p>Once the direction is set, you often need to control how the free space is distributed. This is where <code>justify-content</code> and <code>align-items</code> come into play.</p><ul><li><strong>justify-content</strong> aligns items along the main axis. Common values include <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>space-between</code>, <code>space-around</code>, and <code>space-evenly</code>.</li><li><strong>align-items</strong> aligns items along the cross axis (perpendicular to the main axis). Values are <code>stretch</code> (default), <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code>, <code>center</code>, and <code>baseline</code>.</li></ul><p>Example of centering both horizontally and vertically:</p><pre><code>.flex-container {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center; /* main‑axis */\n  align-items: center;    /* cross‑axis */\n  height: 200px;\n}\n</code></pre><h3>Flex Grow, Flex Shrink, and Flex Basis</h3><p>Flex items can be instructed to grow or shrink to fill available space using the <code>flex-grow</code> and <code>flex-shrink</code> properties. The shorthand <code>flex</code> combines three sub‑properties: <code>flex-grow</code>, <code>flex-shrink</code>, and <code>flex-basis</code>.</p><ul><li><code>flex-grow: 1;</code> – the item will take up any extra space proportionally.</li><li><code>flex-shrink: 1;</code> – the item will shrink when the container becomes too small.</li><li><code>flex-basis: 200px;</code> – the initial main‑size of the item before growing or shrinking.</li></ul><p>Using the shorthand:</p><pre><code>.item {\n  flex: 1 1 150px; /* grow 1, shrink 1, basis 150px */\n}\n</code></pre><p>When multiple items have different <code>flex-grow</code> values, the extra space is divided according to those ratios. For instance, an item with <code>flex-grow: 2</code> will receive twice as much of the remaining space as an item with <code>flex-grow: 1</code>.</p><h3>Practical Exercise</h3><p>Try building a navigation bar where the logo stays on the left, the menu items are centered, and a search icon is aligned to the right. Use <code>display: flex;</code>, <code>justify-content: space-between;</code>, and <code>align-items: center;</code> to achieve the layout.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(103, 98, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript variables and data types illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+variables+and+data+types+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+variables+and+data+types+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+variables+and+data+types+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+variables+and+data+types+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+variables+and+data+types+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Basics: Variables &amp; Data Types</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 11 of our beginner JavaScript journey! Today we will explore how to store information in a program, the different kinds of data you can work with, and the tools you need to see what’s happening behind the scenes.</p>\n<h3>1. Declaring Variables</h3>\n<p>JavaScript provides three keywords for creating variables: <code>var</code>, <code>let</code>, and <code>const</code>. Understanding their differences is essential for writing clean, predictable code.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>var</strong>: The original way to declare a variable. It is function‑scoped and can be redeclared or reassigned. Because of its hoisting behavior, <code>var</code> can lead to confusing bugs, so it is generally discouraged in modern code.</li>\n  <li><strong>let</strong>: Introduced in ES6, <code>let</code> is block‑scoped, meaning it only exists inside the nearest pair of curly braces <code>{ }</code>. You can reassign a <code>let</code> variable, but you cannot redeclare it in the same scope.</li>\n  <li><strong>const</strong>: Also block‑scoped, <code>const</code> creates a read‑only reference. You must assign a value when you declare it, and you cannot reassign the variable itself. Note that objects and arrays declared with <code>const</code> can still have their contents mutated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<code>var oldStyle = 10; // function scoped\nlet mutable = 20; // block scoped, can be changed\nconst immutable = 30; // block scoped, cannot be reassigned</code>\n<h3>2. Primitive Data Types</h3>\n<p>JavaScript has several primitive types that hold a single, immutable value.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Number</strong>: Represents both integers and floating‑point numbers. Example: <code>let age = 25;</code> or <code>let price = 19.99;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>String</strong>: Text surrounded by single, double, or back‑ticks. Example: <code>let greeting = \"Hello, world!\";</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Boolean</strong>: Logical values <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Example: <code>let isStudent = true;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Null</strong> and <strong>Undefined</strong>: Special types representing “no value” and “value not set”.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Complex Data Types</h3>\n<p>Beyond primitives, JavaScript offers structures that can hold multiple values.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Array</strong>: An ordered list of values, accessed by numeric index. Example:</li>\n</ul>\n<code>let colors = [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]; // colors[0] is \"red\"</code>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Object</strong>: A collection of key‑value pairs, useful for modeling real‑world entities. Example:</li>\n</ul>\n<code>let person = {\n  name: \"Alice\",\n  age: 28,\n  isStudent: false\n}; // person.name returns \"Alice\"</code>\n<h3>4. Using <code>console.log</code> for Debugging</h3>\n<p>The browser’s console is your best friend when learning JavaScript. <code>console.log()</code> prints values to the console, allowing you to verify that variables contain what you expect.</p>\n<code>// Example of debugging\nlet score = 42;\nconsole.log(\"Current score:\", score);\n\nlet user = { name: \"Bob\", points: 15 };\nconsole.log(\"User object:\", user);\n</code>\n<p>Open the developer tools (usually <kbd>F12</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl+Shift+I</kbd>) and look at the “Console” tab to see the output.</p>\n<h3>5. Basic Operators</h3>\n<p>Operators let you perform calculations and comparisons.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Arithmetic</strong>: <code>+</code> (addition), <code>-</code> (subtraction), <code>*</code> (multiplication), <code>/</code> (division), <code>%</code> (remainder).</li>\n  <li><strong>Assignment</strong>: <code>=</code> assigns a value; compound forms like <code>+=</code> combine assignment with an operation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Comparison</strong>: <code>===</code> (strict equality), <code>!==</code> (strict inequality), <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Logical</strong>: <code>&&</code> (AND), <code>||</code> (OR), <code>!</code> (NOT).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example combining variables and operators:</p>\n<code>let a = 5;\nlet b = 3;\nlet sum = a + b; // 8\nlet isGreater = sum > 10; // false\nconsole.log(\"Sum:\", sum, \"Is greater than 10?\", isGreater);\n</code>\n<h3>6. Putting It All Together</h3>\n<p>Let’s create a small script that uses every concept covered today.</p>\n<code>// Define a person object using const\nconst person = {\n  name: \"Charlie\",\n  age: 22,\n  hobbies: [\"coding\", \"music\", \"gaming\"]\n};\n\n// Calculate next year’s age\nlet nextYearAge = person.age + 1;\n\n// Output information\nconsole.log(\"Name:\", person.name);\nconsole.log(\"Current age:\", person.age);\nconsole.log(\"Age next year:\", nextYearAge);\nconsole.log(\"First hobby:\", person.hobbies[0]);\n\n// Boolean check\nconst isAdult = person.age >= 18;\nconsole.log(\"Is adult?\", isAdult);\n</code>\n<p>Run this code in the browser console or a <code>.js</code> file and observe the results. Experiment by changing values, using <code>var</code> instead of <code>let</code>, or adding new properties to the object.</p>\n<p>By mastering variable declarations, data types, basic operators, and console debugging, you now have a solid foundation for building more complex JavaScript programs.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(104, 99, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript control flow diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+control+flow+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+control+flow+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+control+flow+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+control+flow+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+control+flow+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Control Flow</h3>\n<p>Control flow statements determine the order in which a program\'s statements are executed. In JavaScript, mastering control flow is essential for building interactive web pages, handling user input, and creating dynamic logic. This lesson introduces the most common control flow constructs for beginners: <strong>if/else statements</strong>, <strong>switch case</strong>, <strong>for loops</strong>, <strong>while loops</strong>, and the <strong>break</strong> and <strong>continue</strong> keywords.</p>\n<h3>If / Else Statements</h3>\n<p>The <code>if</code> statement evaluates a Boolean expression and runs a block of code only when the expression is <code>true</code>. An optional <code>else</code> block runs when the condition is <code>false</code>. You can chain multiple conditions using <code>else if</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>let score = 85;\nif (score >= 90) {\n    console.log(\"Excellent!\");\n} else if (score >= 70) {\n    console.log(\"Good job!\");\n} else {\n    console.log(\"Keep trying!\");\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Key points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Condition must be inside parentheses <code>( )</code>.</li>\n<li>Code block is wrapped in curly braces <code>{ }</code>.</li>\n<li>Only the first true condition runs; subsequent <code>else if</code> blocks are ignored.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Switch Case</h3>\n<p>The <code>switch</code> statement provides a cleaner way to compare a single expression against many possible values. Each <code>case</code> represents a potential match, and the <code>default</code> case runs when no match is found.</p>\n<pre><code>let day = \"Saturday\";\nswitch (day) {\n    case \"Monday\":\n        console.log(\"Start of the work week\");\n        break;\n    case \"Friday\":\n        console.log(\"Almost weekend!\");\n        break;\n    case \"Saturday\":\n    case \"Sunday\":\n        console.log(\"Weekend time!\");\n        break;\n    default:\n        console.log(\"Just another day\");\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Remember to use <code>break</code> to prevent fall‑through, unless you intentionally want multiple cases to share the same block.</p>\n<h3>For Loops</h3>\n<p>The <code>for</code> loop repeats a block of code a known number of times. Its syntax contains three expressions: initialization, condition, and iteration.</p>\n<pre><code>for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    console.log(\"Iteration:\", i);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Typical use cases include iterating over arrays, generating repetitive markup, or counting down timers.</p>\n<h3>While Loops</h3>\n<p>A <code>while</code> loop continues as long as its condition remains <code>true</code>. It is useful when the number of iterations is not known in advance.</p>\n<pre><code>let count = 0;\nwhile (count < 3) {\n    console.log(\"Count is\", count);\n    count++;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Be careful to update the loop variable inside the block; otherwise, you may create an infinite loop.</p>\n<h3>Break and Continue</h3>\n<p>The <code>break</code> statement immediately exits the nearest enclosing loop or <code>switch</code>. The <code>continue</code> statement skips the rest of the current iteration and proceeds to the next loop cycle.</p>\n<pre><code>for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {\n    if (i % 2 === 0) continue; // skip even numbers\n    if (i > 7) break; // stop when i exceeds 7\n    console.log(i); // prints 1,3,5,7\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use <code>break</code> when you need to stop looping early, and <code>continue</code> when you want to ignore specific iterations without ending the entire loop.</p>\n<h3>Putting It All Together</h3>\n<p>Below is a small program that asks a user for a number (simulated with a variable) and prints whether the number is positive, negative, or zero. It then counts from 1 to that number, but skips multiples of 3 and stops counting once it reaches 10.</p>\n<pre><code>let input = 12; // imagine this came from prompt()\n// If / else to classify the number\nif (input > 0) {\n    console.log(\"Positive number\");\n} else if (input < 0) {\n    console.log(\"Negative number\");\n} else {\n    console.log(\"Zero\");\n}\n\n// For loop with break and continue\nfor (let i = 1; i <= input; i++) {\n    if (i % 3 === 0) continue; // skip multiples of 3\n    if (i > 10) break; // stop after 10\n    console.log(\"Count:\", i);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Practice each construct by modifying the examples: change conditions, add more <code>case</code> statements, or experiment with nested loops. Understanding these basics will give you the confidence to write more complex JavaScript programs.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(105, 100, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: DOM manipulation JavaScript tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to DOM Manipulation</h3><p>When a web page loads, the browser creates a <strong>Document Object Model (DOM)</strong> – a tree‑like representation of all HTML elements. JavaScript can walk this tree, read data, and modify the page without reloading. This lesson covers the most common techniques a beginner needs to start interacting with the DOM.</p><h3>Selecting Elements</h3><p>Before you can change anything, you must obtain a reference to the element you want to work with. Two methods are essential:</p><ul><li><code>document.getElementById(\'myId\')</code> – returns the single element whose <code>id</code> attribute matches the supplied string. It is fast and works in all browsers.</li><li><code>document.querySelector(\'.myClass\')</code> – accepts any CSS selector (class, attribute, descendant, etc.) and returns the first matching element. For multiple matches, use <code>querySelectorAll</code>, which returns a <code>NodeList</code>.</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code>const header = document.getElementById(\'page-header\');\nconst button = document.querySelector(\'button.primary\');</code></pre><h3>Changing Text and HTML Content</h3><p>Once you have a reference, you can alter what the user sees.</p><ul><li><strong>textContent</strong> – replaces only the plain text inside an element. It does not interpret HTML tags.</li><li><strong>innerHTML</strong> – replaces the entire HTML markup inside the element, allowing you to inject new tags.</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code>// Change only the text\nheader.textContent = \'Welcome to My Site\';\n\n// Insert a paragraph with bold text\nheader.innerHTML = \'&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to &lt;strong&gt;My Site&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;\';</code></pre><h3>Adding and Removing CSS Classes</h3><p>Styling is often toggled by adding or removing class names. The <code>classList</code> API provides a clean way to manage this.</p><ul><li><code>element.classList.add(\'active\')</code> – adds the class if it is not already present.</li><li><code>element.classList.remove(\'active\')</code> – removes the class if it exists.</li><li><code>element.classList.toggle(\'active\')</code> – adds the class when it is missing and removes it when it is present.</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code>const box = document.querySelector(\'#color-box\');\nbox.classList.add(\'highlight\');   // adds highlight style\nbox.classList.remove(\'hidden\');   // ensures it is visible\nbox.classList.toggle(\'collapsed\'); // toggles collapsed state</code></pre><h3>Event Listeners (click)</h3><p>Interactivity is driven by events. The most common event for beginners is a <code>click</code> on a button or link. Use <code>addEventListener</code> to attach a handler function.</p><pre><code>const btn = document.querySelector(\'#myButton\');\nbtn.addEventListener(\'click\', function(event) {\n    // Prevent default link behavior if needed\n    event.preventDefault();\n    // Change text of a paragraph\n    const msg = document.getElementById(\'message\');\n    msg.textContent = \'Button was clicked!\';\n    // Toggle a CSS class for visual feedback\n    btn.classList.toggle(\'clicked\');\n});</code></pre><p>Key points to remember:</p><ul><li>Always store a reference to the element before attaching the listener.</li><li>The callback receives an <code>event</code> object that contains useful information such as <code>event.target</code> and <code>event.type</code>.</li><li>Multiple listeners can be added to the same element; they will fire in the order they were attached.</li></ul><h3>Putting It All Together</h3><p>Below is a small, self‑contained example that demonstrates every concept covered in this lesson.</p><pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;DOM Manipulation Demo&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;style&gt;\n        .hidden { display: none; }\n        .active { background-color: #4caf50; color: white; }\n    &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1 id=\"page-header\"&gt;Original Title&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p id=\"message\" class=\"hidden\"&gt;You clicked the button!&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;button id=\"myButton\" class=\"primary\"&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n\n    &lt;script&gt;\n        const header = document.getElementById(\'page-header\');\n        const button = document.querySelector(\'#myButton\');\n        const message = document.getElementById(\'message\');\n\n        // Change header text on page load\n        header.textContent = \'Welcome to the DOM Demo\';\n\n        // Add click listener\n        button.addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\n            // Show hidden message\n            message.classList.remove(\'hidden\');\n            // Toggle button style\n            button.classList.toggle(\'active\');\n            // Update header with HTML content\n            header.innerHTML = \'&lt;span&gt;You &lt;strong&gt;clicked&lt;/strong&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;\';\n        });\n    &lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre><p>Study the example, run it in your browser, and experiment by changing selectors, class names, and the text you inject. Mastery of these basics will give you the confidence to build dynamic, interactive web pages.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06');
INSERT INTO `content_blocks` (`id`, `topic_id`, `type`, `content`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(106, 101, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design media queries navigation example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+media+queries+navigation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+media+queries+navigation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+media+queries+navigation+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+media+queries+navigation+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+media+queries+navigation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding the Viewport Meta Tag</h3>\n<p>The <strong>viewport meta tag</strong> tells browsers how to control the page\'s dimensions and scaling on different devices. Without it, mobile browsers render pages at a virtual width of about 980 px, which forces users to zoom and scroll horizontally. The most common declaration is:</p>\n<code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;</code>\n<p>This instructs the browser to set the viewport width equal to the device’s width and to start with a 1 × scale. You can also add <code>maximum-scale=1</code> or <code>user-scalable=no</code> for stricter control, but be careful not to hinder accessibility.</p>\n\n<h3>Media Query Syntax</h3>\n<p>Media queries let you apply CSS rules only when certain conditions are met, such as screen width, orientation, or resolution. The basic syntax looks like this:</p>\n<code>@media (min-width: 600px) {\n    /* CSS rules for screens 600 px and wider */\n}\n</code>\n<p>Multiple conditions can be combined with <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> (comma), or <code>not</code>. For example:</p>\n<code>@media screen and (min-width: 768px) and (orientation: landscape) {\n    /* Landscape tablets and larger */\n}\n</code>\n<p>Remember that media queries are evaluated in the order they appear, so later rules can override earlier ones if they match the same conditions.</p>\n\n<h3>Mobile‑First Approach</h3>\n<p>The <strong>mobile‑first</strong> strategy starts by writing styles for the smallest screens and then expands them using <code>min-width</code> media queries. This ensures a solid baseline for phones and reduces the amount of overridden CSS. A typical workflow looks like:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Write default styles (mobile).</li>\n    <li>Add <code>@media (min-width: 600px)</code> for small tablets.</li>\n    <li>Add <code>@media (min-width: 992px)</code> for desktops.</li>\n    <li>Fine‑tune with larger breakpoints as needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Advantages include faster load times on mobile (less CSS to parse) and a clearer, more maintainable stylesheet.</p>\n\n<h3>Responsive Navigation Example</h3>\n<p>Below is a simple, responsive navigation menu that demonstrates the concepts above. The HTML provides a semantic <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> element with a list of links. The CSS hides the menu on small screens and shows a hamburger button that toggles the list.</p>\n<code>&lt;!-- HTML --&gt;\n&lt;nav class=\"main-nav\"&gt;\n    &lt;button class=\"nav-toggle\" aria-label=\"Toggle navigation\"&gt;☰&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;ul class=\"nav-list\"&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Services&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- CSS --&gt;\n/* Base styles (mobile first) */\n.main-nav { background:#333; color:#fff; }\n.nav-list { display:none; list-style:none; margin:0; padding:0; }\n.nav-list li { padding:0.5rem 1rem; }\n.nav-list a { color:#fff; text-decoration:none; }\n.nav-toggle { background:none; border:none; color:#fff; font-size:1.5rem; padding:0.5rem 1rem; }\n\n/* Show menu when toggle is active (you would add JS to toggle a class) */\n.main-nav.active .nav-list { display:block; }\n\n/* Tablet and larger – horizontal menu */\n@media (min-width: 600px) {\n    .nav-toggle { display:none; }\n    .nav-list { display:flex; }\n    .nav-list li { padding:0.5rem 1.5rem; }\n}\n</code>\n<p>In this example:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>The <code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\"&gt;</code> tag (placed in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>) ensures the layout scales correctly.</li>\n    <li>Base CSS targets mobile devices, hiding the list and showing a toggle button.</li>\n    <li>A <code>min-width: 600px</code> media query switches to a horizontal navigation bar for tablets and desktops.</li>\n    <li>The <strong>mobile‑first</strong> mindset keeps the default styles simple and adds complexity only when needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By mastering these tools, you can build fluid, accessible interfaces that look great on any screen size.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
