(108, 102, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design layout on multiple devices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+layout+on+multiple+devices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+layout+on+multiple+devices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+layout+on+multiple+devices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+layout+on+multiple+devices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+layout+on+multiple+devices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Responsive Design Basics</h3><p>Welcome to Day 15 of the beginner web development course! In this lesson we will explore the three foundational concepts that enable a website to adapt gracefully to any screen size: the <strong>viewport meta tag</strong>, <strong>media queries</strong>, and <strong>flexible grid layouts</strong>. By the end of the day you will be able to write HTML and CSS that looks good on a phone, a tablet, and a desktop monitor without needing separate codebases for each device.</p><h3>1. The Viewport Meta Tag</h3><p>The viewport meta tag tells mobile browsers how to interpret the page’s dimensions and scaling. Without it, browsers assume a default width of about 980 px, which forces users to pinch‑zoom on small screens. Adding the tag is the first step toward a responsive design.</p><ul><li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Defines the visible area of the web page.</li><li><strong>Typical syntax:</strong></li></ul><code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;</code><p>Key parts of the <code>content</code> attribute:</p><ul><li><code>width=device-width</code> – sets the viewport width to match the device’s screen width.</li><li><code>initial-scale=1</code> – sets the initial zoom level to 100%.</li><li>Optional values such as <code>maximum-scale</code> or <code>user-scalable=no</code> can be added for special cases, but they should be used sparingly to preserve accessibility.</li></ul><p>Place this tag inside the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of every HTML document you intend to make responsive.</p><h3>2. Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are CSS techniques that apply styles only when certain conditions are met, most commonly the width of the viewport. They let you create breakpoints where the layout shifts to better suit the available space.</p><ul><li><strong>Basic structure:</strong></li></ul><code>@media (max-width: 768px) { /* CSS rules for tablets and smaller */ }</code><p>Common breakpoint ranges (you can adjust them to fit your design):</p><ul><li><code>max-width: 576px</code> – phones.</li><li><code>max-width: 768px</code> – tablets.</li><li><code>max-width: 992px</code> – small laptops.</li><li><code>min-width: 1200px</code> – large desktops.</li></ul><p>Example that changes the navigation menu from a horizontal list to a vertical stack on small screens:</p><code>@media (max-width: 600px) { .nav { flex-direction: column; } }</code><p>Remember to place media queries after the default styles so they can override them when the conditions are true.</p><h3>3. Flexible Grid Layouts</h3><p>Grids provide the structural backbone for responsive pages. Modern CSS offers two powerful grid systems: <strong>Flexbox</strong> and <strong>CSS Grid</strong>. Both create flexible containers that can grow, shrink, or wrap based on the available space.</p><h4>Flexbox Basics</h4><p>Flexbox works along a single axis (row or column). The container is declared with <code>display: flex;</code> and children become flex items.</p><ul><li><code>flex-wrap: wrap;</code> – allows items to move onto new lines when they run out of space.</li><li><code>flex: 1 1 200px;</code> – sets a flexible basis of 200 px, with the ability to grow and shrink.</li></ul><p>Simple responsive card layout using Flexbox:</p><code>.card-container { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem; } .card { flex: 1 1 250px; }</code><h4>CSS Grid Basics</h4><p>CSS Grid works in two dimensions, letting you define rows and columns simultaneously.</p><ul><li><code>display: grid;</code> – turns the element into a grid container.</li><li><code>grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));</code> – creates as many columns as will fit, each at least 200 px wide.</li></ul><p>Responsive gallery example:</p><code>.gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(150px, 1fr)); gap: 0.5rem; }</code><p>Both Flexbox and CSS Grid can be combined with media queries to fine‑tune the layout at specific breakpoints.</p><h3>Putting It All Together</h3><p>Here is a minimal HTML skeleton that demonstrates all three concepts:</p><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Responsive Demo&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;style&gt;\n        .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }\n        .item { background: #f2f2f2; padding: 1rem; text-align: center; }\n        @media (max-width: 600px) {\n            .grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }\n        }\n    &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;section class=\"grid\"&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Box 1&lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Box 2&lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Box 3&lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Box 4&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/section&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code><p>When you resize the browser window, the grid automatically reflows from four columns to a single column at the 600 px breakpoint, thanks to the viewport tag, the media query, and the flexible grid definition.</p><p>Practice Exercise: Create a simple portfolio page that uses the viewport meta tag, at least two media‑query breakpoints, and a Flexbox or Grid layout to showcase project thumbnails. Test it on a phone emulator and on a desktop to see the changes in action.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(109, 103, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS Flexbox diagram showing main axis and cross axis</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+Flexbox+diagram+showing+main+axis+and+cross+axis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+Flexbox+diagram+showing+main+axis+and+cross+axis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+Flexbox+diagram+showing+main+axis+and+cross+axis/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+Flexbox+diagram+showing+main+axis+and+cross+axis/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+Flexbox+diagram+showing+main+axis+and+cross+axis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding the Flex Container and Flex Items</h3>\n<p>In CSS Flexbox, the <strong>flex container</strong> is the parent element that establishes a flexible formatting context for its direct children, known as <strong>flex items</strong>. By setting <code>display: flex;</code> or <code>display: inline-flex;</code> on a container, you enable a powerful layout system that automatically distributes space along a chosen axis.</p>\n<h3>Main Axis vs. Cross Axis</h3>\n<p>The <strong>main axis</strong> is the primary line along which flex items are laid out. By default, this axis runs horizontally from left to right, but you can change its direction with the <code>flex-direction</code> property. The <strong>cross axis</strong> runs perpendicular to the main axis—vertically when the main axis is horizontal, and horizontally when the main axis is vertical. Understanding the relationship between these two axes is essential for controlling alignment and spacing.</p>\n<h3>Key Flex Properties for the Container</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>justify-content</code>: Aligns flex items along the main axis. Values include <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>space-between</code>, <code>space-around</code>, and <code>space-evenly</code>.</li>\n<li><code>align-items</code>: Aligns flex items along the cross axis. Common values are <code>stretch</code> (default), <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code>, <code>center</code>, and <code>baseline</code>.</li>\n<li><code>flex-wrap</code>: Determines whether items stay on a single line (<code>nowrap</code>) or wrap onto multiple lines (<code>wrap</code> or <code>wrap-reverse</code>).</li>\n<li><code>flex-direction</code>: Sets the direction of the main axis. Options are <code>row</code>, <code>row-reverse</code>, <code>column</code>, and <code>column-reverse</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Essential Flex Properties for Items</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>flex-grow</code>: Defines how much a flex item will grow relative to the rest when extra space is available.</li>\n<li><code>flex-shrink</code>: Controls how a flex item shrinks when there is not enough space.</li>\n<li><code>flex-basis</code>: Sets the initial main‑size of an item before free space is distributed.</li>\n<li><code>align-self</code>: Allows an individual item to override the container\'s <code>align-items</code> setting.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Building Simple Flex Layouts</h3>\n<p>Let’s walk through three practical examples that illustrate how the concepts above come together.</p>\n<h4>Example 1: Horizontal Navigation Bar</h4>\n<pre><code>&lt;nav class=\"navbar\"&gt;\n  &lt;ul class=\"nav-list\"&gt;\n    &lt;li class=\"nav-item\"&gt;Home&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li class=\"nav-item\"&gt;About&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li class=\"nav-item\"&gt;Services&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li class=\"nav-item\"&gt;Contact&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n\n.nav-list {\n  display: flex;               /* Establish flex container */\n  justify-content: space-between; /* Distribute items across main axis */\n  align-items: center;         /* Center items on cross axis */\n  list-style: none;\n  padding: 0;\n  margin: 0;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>In this navigation bar, <code>justify-content: space-between</code> pushes the first and last links to the edges while evenly spacing the middle items.</p>\n<h4>Example 2: Card Grid with Wrapping</h4>\n<pre><code>&lt;section class=\"card-grid\"&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"card\"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"card\"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"card\"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"card\"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/section&gt;\n\n.card-grid {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;               /* Allow items to move to next line */\n  justify-content: center;      /* Center rows on main axis */\n  gap: 1rem;                     /* Add consistent spacing */\n}\n\n.card {\n  flex: 1 1 200px;               /* Grow, shrink, and set base width */\n  background: #f9f9f9;\n  padding: 1rem;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>flex: 1 1 200px</code> shorthand ensures each card starts at 200 px, grows to fill extra space, and shrinks when the viewport narrows.</p>\n<h4>Example 3: Vertical Centering of a Modal</h4>\n<pre><code>&lt;div class=\"overlay\"&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"modal\"&gt;Welcome!&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n.overlay {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;   /* Center horizontally */\n  align-items: center;       /* Center vertically */\n  position: fixed;\n  inset: 0;\n  background: rgba(0,0,0,0.5);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>By applying both <code>justify-content</code> and <code>align-items</code> with the value <code>center</code>, the modal is perfectly centered regardless of screen size.</p>\n<h3>Key Takeaways</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The flex container defines the main and cross axes; all direct children become flex items.</li>\n<li><code>justify-content</code> controls distribution along the main axis, while <code>align-items</code> manages alignment on the cross axis.</li>\n<li>Individual items can grow, shrink, or set a basis using <code>flex-grow</code>, <code>flex-shrink</code>, and <code>flex-basis</code>.</li>\n<li>Combining <code>flex-wrap</code> with <code>gap</code> or <code>margin</code> creates responsive, gap‑aware layouts without complex float or positioning tricks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practice by creating a simple header, a responsive card gallery, and a centered login modal. Experiment with different <code>justify-content</code> and <code>align-items</code> values to see how the layout reacts in real time.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(110, 104, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS Grid layout example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+Grid+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+Grid+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+Grid+layout+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+Grid+layout+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+Grid+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Grid Essentials</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 17 of our beginner web development course! In this lesson you will learn the fundamentals of <strong>CSS Grid</strong>, a powerful two‑dimensional layout system that makes building complex, responsive designs much easier than ever before. By the end of the day you will be able to create a grid container, define rows and columns (tracks), place items precisely using grid lines, build responsive grid templates, and understand when to choose Grid over Flexbox.</p>\n<h3>1. Grid Container and Tracks</h3>\n<p>The first step in using CSS Grid is to turn a parent element into a <code>grid container</code>. This is done with the <code>display: grid;</code> declaration. Once the container is a grid, you can define the horizontal and vertical tracks – the columns and rows that make up the layout.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Creating a container:</strong> <code>.grid { display: grid; }</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Defining columns:</strong> Use <code>grid-template-columns</code> with values such as <code>repeat(3, 1fr)</code> to create three equal columns, or <code>200px 1fr 2fr</code> for mixed sizes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Defining rows:</strong> Use <code>grid-template-rows</code> in the same way. For example, <code>grid-template-rows: 100px auto 50px;</code> creates three rows with fixed, automatic, and fixed heights.</li>\n  <li><strong>Gap between tracks:</strong> The <code>gap</code> (or <code>row-gap</code> / <code>column-gap</code>) property adds spacing without extra markup. Example: <code>gap: 20px;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>When you set up tracks, the browser automatically creates an invisible grid of lines. These lines are numbered starting at 1 on each edge, which you will use later to position items.</p>\n<h3>2. Placing Items with Grid Lines</h3>\n<p>Each grid item can be placed anywhere on the grid by referencing the line numbers. The most common properties are <code>grid-column-start</code>, <code>grid-column-end</code>, <code>grid-row-start</code>, and <code>grid-row-end</code>. A shorthand, <code>grid-column</code> or <code>grid-row</code>, accepts a start/end pair separated by a slash.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Simple placement:</strong> <code>.item { grid-column: 2 / 4; grid-row: 1 / 3; }</code> puts the item spanning columns 2‑3 and rows 1‑2.</li>\n  <li><strong>Using <code>span</code> keyword:</strong> <code>grid-column: 1 / span 2;</code> starts at line 1 and spans two columns.</li>\n  <li><strong>Named lines:</strong> You can name lines in the template, e.g., <code>grid-template-columns: [start] 1fr [content-start] 2fr [content-end] 1fr [end];</code> and then place items with <code>grid-column: start / end;</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Automatic placement:</strong> If you omit placement properties, the browser fills items in source order, respecting any <code>grid-auto-flow</code> settings.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Understanding grid lines gives you pixel‑perfect control, which is especially useful for magazine‑style layouts, dashboards, and image galleries.</p>\n<h3>3. Creating Responsive Grid Templates</h3>\n<p>One of Grid’s biggest strengths is its ability to adapt to different screen sizes with minimal code. The <code>repeat()</code> function combined with <code>minmax()</code> and the <code>auto-fill</code> or <code>auto-fit</code> keywords lets you build fluid grids that automatically add or remove columns.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Responsive columns:</strong> <code>grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr));</code> creates as many 200‑pixel‑wide columns as will fit, each expanding to fill remaining space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Media queries:</strong> You can change the track definition at breakpoints. Example:\n    <code>@media (max-width: 600px) { .grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }</code>\n    forces a single‑column layout on small devices.</li>\n  <li><strong>Row auto‑sizing:</strong> <code>grid-auto-rows: minmax(100px, auto);</code> ensures rows grow to fit their content while staying at least 100 px tall.</li>\n  <li><strong>Combining with <code>gap</code>:</strong> Adding <code>gap: 15px;</code> keeps consistent spacing across breakpoints without extra margin hacks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With these techniques you can build a photo gallery that shows three columns on desktop, two on tablet, and one on mobile, all with a single CSS rule set.</p>\n<h3>4. Grid vs Flexbox Comparison</h3>\n<p>Both Grid and Flexbox are layout modules, but they solve different problems. Understanding their strengths helps you decide which to use for a given design.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Dimension:</strong> Flexbox is one‑dimensional (either a row or a column). Grid is two‑dimensional, handling rows and columns simultaneously.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use case:</strong> Flexbox excels at aligning items along a single axis, such as navigation bars or simple card rows. Grid shines when you need a full page layout, complex dashboards, or magazine‑style compositions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ordering:</strong> Flexbox can reorder items with <code>order</code>, but Grid can place items anywhere using line numbers, making it easier to create asymmetrical designs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Responsiveness:</strong> Both support media queries, yet Grid’s <code>auto-fill</code>/<code>auto-fit</code> patterns often require fewer breakpoints for fluid column changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Browser support:</strong> Modern browsers fully support both, but older versions of Internet Explorer lack full Grid support. For legacy projects, Flexbox may be safer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In practice, you will frequently combine the two: a Grid for the overall page skeleton and Flexbox for components inside individual grid cells (e.g., a navigation menu inside a header grid area).</p>\n<p>Take time today to experiment with the code snippets, resize your browser window, and observe how the layout adapts. Mastering CSS Grid will give you a solid foundation for building modern, responsive web pages.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(111, 105, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript beginner tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+beginner+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+beginner+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+beginner+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Variables and Data Types</h3>\n<p>JavaScript is a loosely typed language, which means you do not need to declare the type of a variable before using it. The <strong>var</strong>, <strong>let</strong>, and <strong>const</strong> keywords are used to create variables. <code>let</code> is block‑scoped, <code>var</code> is function‑scoped, and <code>const</code> creates a read‑only reference.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>let age = 25;</code> – a number.</li>\n<li><code>const name = \"Alice\";</code> – a string.</li>\n<li><code>let isStudent = true;</code> – a boolean.</li>\n<li><code>let scores = [85, 92, 78];</code> – an array.</li>\n<li><code>let person = {firstName: \"Bob\", lastName: \"Smith\"};</code> – an object.</li>\n<li><code>let nothing = null;</code> – a null value.</li>\n<li><code>let unknown;</code> – <code>undefined</code> because it has not been assigned.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Understanding these primitive types (<strong>Number</strong>, <strong>String</strong>, <strong>Boolean</strong>, <strong>Null</strong>, <strong>Undefined</strong>) and complex types (<strong>Object</strong>, <strong>Array</strong>) is essential before moving on to expressions.</p>\n\n<h3>Operators and Expressions</h3>\n<p>Operators allow you to manipulate values and produce new results. JavaScript supports arithmetic, assignment, comparison, logical, and ternary operators.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arithmetic:</strong> <code>+, -, *, /, %</code> – e.g., <code>let total = price * quantity;</code></li>\n<li><strong>Assignment:</strong> <code>=, +=, -=, *=, /=</code> – e.g., <code>counter += 1;</code></li>\n<li><strong>Comparison:</strong> <code>==, ===, !=, !==, >, <, >=, <=</code> – <code>===</code> checks both value and type.</li>\n<li><strong>Logical:</strong> <code>&&, ||, !</code> – used to combine boolean expressions.</li>\n<li><strong>Ternary:</strong> <code>condition ? exprIfTrue : exprIfFalse</code> – a compact if‑else.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of a combined expression:</p>\n<code>let discount = (isMember && purchaseAmount > 100) ? purchaseAmount * 0.1 : 0;</code>\n<p>Notice how parentheses clarify precedence, and the ternary operator returns a value that can be stored directly.</p>\n\n<h3>Control Structures (if, loops)</h3>\n<p>Control structures dictate the flow of execution. The <code>if</code> statement evaluates a condition and runs a block of code when the condition is true.</p>\n<code>\nif (score >= 90) {\n    console.log(\"Excellent!\");\n} else if (score >= 70) {\n    console.log(\"Good job.\");\n} else {\n    console.log(\"Keep trying.\");\n}\n</code>\n<p>Loops repeat a block of code. The most common loops are <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, and <code>do...while</code>.</p>\n<code>\n// For loop iterating over an array\nfor (let i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {\n    console.log(`Score ${i + 1}: ${scores[i]}`);\n}\n\n// While loop counting down\nlet count = 5;\nwhile (count > 0) {\n    console.log(count);\n    count--;\n}\n</code>\n<p>When working with objects or arrays, the <code>for...of</code> and <code>for...in</code> constructs are handy:</p>\n<code>\nfor (let value of scores) {\n    console.log(value);\n}\n\nfor (let key in person) {\n    console.log(`${key}: ${person[key]}`);\n}\n</code>\n\n<h3>Embedding JavaScript in HTML</h3>\n<p>JavaScript can be placed directly inside an HTML document using the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag. There are three common approaches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inline script:</strong> Write code between <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags in the HTML file.\n<code>&lt;script&gt;\n    console.log(\'Hello from inline script\');\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></li>\n<li><strong>External file:</strong> Store JavaScript in a separate <code>.js</code> file and reference it with the <code>src</code> attribute.\n<code>&lt;script src=\"app.js\" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code>\n    Using <code>defer</code> ensures the script runs after the HTML is parsed.</li>\n<li><strong>Event attributes:</strong> Attach JavaScript to HTML elements via attributes such as <code>onclick</code>.\n<code>&lt;button onclick=\"alert(\'Clicked!\')\"&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;</code>\n    While convenient for quick demos, this method is discouraged in production code.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Best practice is to keep JavaScript separate from markup, load scripts at the end of the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> or use <code>defer</code>, and avoid global variables when possible.</p>\n<p>Here is a minimal HTML page that demonstrates all concepts covered today:</p>\n<code>\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;JavaScript Intro&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;script src=\"script.js\" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome, <span id=\"user\"></span>!&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;button id=\"greetBtn\"&gt;Greet&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code>\n<p>The external <code>script.js</code> could contain:</p>\n<code>\nlet userName = prompt(\'Enter your name:\');\ndocument.getElementById(\'user\').textContent = userName;\n\ndocument.getElementById(\'greetBtn\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\n    alert(`Hello, ${userName}!`);\n});\n</code>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, you should be comfortable declaring variables, performing operations, controlling program flow, and embedding JavaScript inside an HTML document.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(112, 106, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: DOM manipulation JavaScript example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=DOM+manipulation+JavaScript+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to DOM Manipulation</h3>\n<p>In this lesson you will learn how to interact with the <strong>Document Object Model (DOM)</strong> using plain JavaScript. The DOM is a programming interface that represents the structure of an HTML document as a tree of nodes. By mastering DOM manipulation you can change the content, attributes, and style of a page without reloading it, creating dynamic and responsive user experiences.</p>\n<h3>Selecting Elements</h3>\n<p>The first step in any DOM operation is to locate the element you want to work with. JavaScript provides several methods for this purpose. The most common ones are <code>getElementById</code> and <code>querySelector</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>getElementById</strong>: Returns a single element whose <code>id</code> attribute matches the supplied string. It is fast because IDs are unique.\n<code>const header = document.getElementById(\'main-header\');</code></li>\n<li><strong>querySelector</strong>: Accepts any CSS selector and returns the first matching element. It is more flexible and can target classes, attributes, pseudo‑classes, etc.\n<code>const firstButton = document.querySelector(\'.btn-primary\');</code></li>\n<li><strong>querySelectorAll</strong>: Similar to <code>querySelector</code> but returns a <code>NodeList</code> of all matching elements.\n<code>const items = document.querySelectorAll(\'ul li.active\');</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Modifying Content and Attributes</h3>\n<p>Once you have a reference to an element, you can change its text, HTML, or attributes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>textContent</strong> – sets or returns the plain text inside an element.\n<code>header.textContent = \'Welcome to Day 19!\';</code></li>\n<li><strong>innerHTML</strong> – allows you to insert HTML markup.\n<code>header.innerHTML = \'&lt;span class=\"highlight\"&gt;Day 19&lt;/span&gt;\';</code></li>\n<li><strong>setAttribute / getAttribute</strong> – manipulate any attribute, such as <code>src</code>, <code>alt</code>, or custom data attributes.\n<code>const img = document.querySelector(\'img\');\nimg.setAttribute(\'src\', \'images/new-photo.jpg\');\nlet altText = img.getAttribute(\'alt\');</code></li>\n<li><strong>classList</strong> – add, remove, or toggle CSS classes.\n<code>header.classList.add(\'highlight\');\nheader.classList.toggle(\'hidden\');</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Event Listeners and Handlers</h3>\n<p>Interactivity is achieved by responding to user actions such as clicks, keyboard input, or mouse movements. The <code>addEventListener</code> method attaches a function (the handler) to a specific event type.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Basic click handler</strong>\n<code>const btn = document.querySelector(\'#submitBtn\');\nbtn.addEventListener(\'click\', function(event) {\n    alert(\'Button was clicked!\');\n});</code></li>\n<li><strong>Using arrow functions and the event object</strong>\n<code>document.addEventListener(\'keydown\', (e) => {\n    if (e.key === \'Enter\') {\n        console.log(\'Enter key pressed\');\n    }\n});</code></li>\n<li><strong>Removing an event listener</strong>\n<code>function logResize() { console.log(window.innerWidth); }\nwindow.addEventListener(\'resize\', logResize);\n// later when you no longer need it\nwindow.removeEventListener(\'resize\', logResize);\n</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Dynamic Style Updates</h3>\n<p>Changing the visual appearance of elements on the fly is a common requirement. You can modify inline styles directly, or toggle CSS classes that contain predefined rules.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inline style manipulation</strong>\n<code>const box = document.querySelector(\'.box\');\nbox.style.backgroundColor = \'#4caf50\';\nbox.style.width = \'200px\';\nbox.style.transition = \'all 0.3s ease\';</code></li>\n<li><strong>Class toggling for themes</strong>\n<code>const themeBtn = document.querySelector(\'#themeToggle\');\nthemeBtn.addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\n    document.body.classList.toggle(\'dark-mode\');\n});</code></li>\n<li><strong>Responsive updates based on scroll position</strong>\n<code>window.addEventListener(\'scroll\', () => {\n    const nav = document.querySelector(\'nav\');\n    if (window.scrollY > 100) {\n        nav.classList.add(\'scrolled\');\n    } else {\n        nav.classList.remove(\'scrolled\');\n    }\n});</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Putting It All Together</h3>\n<p>Below is a short, self‑contained example that demonstrates selecting an element, updating its content, reacting to a click, and changing its style.</p>\n<code>\n// HTML structure (for reference)\n// &lt;h1 id=\"title\"&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;\n// &lt;button id=\"changeBtn\" class=\"btn&quot;&gt;Change Title&lt;/button&gt;\n\nconst title = document.getElementById(\'title\');\nconst btn   = document.getElementById(\'changeBtn\');\n\nbtn.addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\n    title.textContent = \'DOM Manipulation Mastered!\';\n    title.style.color = \'#ff5722\';\n    title.classList.add(\'animated\');\n});\n</code>\n<p>Practice by creating your own small project: a to‑do list, a live search filter, or a simple image gallery. The concepts covered today—selection, content/attribute changes, event handling, and dynamic styling—are the building blocks for every interactive web application.</p>\n<h3>Summary</h3>\n<p>Today you learned how to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Select elements with <code>getElementById</code>, <code>querySelector</code>, and <code>querySelectorAll</code>.</li>\n<li>Modify text, HTML, attributes, and CSS classes.</li>\n<li>Attach and remove event listeners to respond to user actions.</li>\n<li>Update styles directly or via class toggling to create dynamic visual effects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep experimenting, and remember that the best way to master DOM manipulation is by building real‑world features.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06');
