(116, 109, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design basics illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+basics+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+basics+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+basics+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+basics+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+basics+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding the Viewport Meta Tag</h3>\n<p>When a web page is displayed on a mobile device, the browser needs to know how to scale the layout. The <code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\"&gt;</code> tag provides this instruction. By default, browsers assume a virtual viewport width of about 980 px, which makes desktop‑oriented sites appear tiny on phones. Adding the following line inside the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> tells the browser to match the viewport width to the device width and to use an initial zoom level of 1:</p>\n<code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;</code>\n<p><strong>Key attributes</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>width=device-width</strong> – sets the viewport width equal to the screen’s width in CSS pixels.</li>\n  <li><strong>initial-scale=1</strong> – prevents the browser from automatically zooming out.</li>\n  <li><strong>maximum-scale</strong> and <strong>minimum-scale</strong> – optional limits for user zoom.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without this tag, media queries may never fire because the page is rendered at a much larger virtual width. Always place the viewport meta tag as the first element after the <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> for consistent behavior across browsers.</p>\n\n<h3>Using Media Queries for Breakpoints</h3>\n<p>Media queries let you apply CSS rules only when certain conditions are true, most commonly the width of the viewport. A <strong>breakpoint</strong> is the width at which the layout changes to better fit the screen.</p>\n<code>@media (max-width: 768px) {\n  /* Styles for tablets and smaller */\n}\n\n@media (max-width: 480px) {\n  /* Styles for phones */\n}</code>\n<p>Typical breakpoints for a beginner course:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>1200px</strong> – large desktops.</li>\n  <li><strong>992px</strong> – standard desktops.</li>\n  <li><strong>768px</strong> – tablets (portrait).</li>\n  <li><strong>480px</strong> – smartphones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When writing media queries, use <code>min-width</code> for a “mobile‑first” approach. This means you start with the base styles for the smallest screens and add layers of enhancements as the viewport grows:</p>\n<code>@media (min-width: 480px) { /* tablets and up */ }\n@media (min-width: 768px) { /* small laptops and up */ }\n@media (min-width: 992px) { /* larger laptops */ }\n@media (min-width: 1200px) { /* wide monitors */ }</code>\n<p>Remember to keep the number of breakpoints manageable. Too many cause maintenance headaches, while too few can lead to awkward layouts.</p>\n\n<h3>Creating Flexible Grid Layouts</h3>\n<p>Grids are the backbone of responsive design. Modern CSS offers two powerful systems: <strong>Flexbox</strong> and <strong>CSS Grid</strong>. Both create flexible containers that adapt to the available space.</p>\n<p><strong>Flexbox example – a simple navigation bar that wraps on small screens:</strong></p>\n<code>.nav {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap; /* Allows items to move to the next line */\n  justify-content: space-between;\n}\n\n.nav a {\n  flex: 1 1 150px; /* Grow, shrink, base width */\n  text-align: center;\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n}</code>\n<p>In this snippet each link will try to occupy at least 150 px, but will shrink or grow as needed. When the total width exceeds the viewport, the <code>flex-wrap</code> property forces the links onto a new row.</p>\n<p><strong>CSS Grid example – a three‑column layout that collapses to one column on phones:</strong></p>\n<code>.grid-container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);\n  gap: 1rem;\n}\n\n@media (max-width: 480px) {\n  .grid-container {\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr; /* Single column */\n  }\n}</code>\n<p>Using <code>1fr</code> (fractional unit) makes each column share the remaining space equally, ensuring the layout stays fluid. The <code>gap</code> property adds consistent spacing without extra margin hacks.</p>\n<p>For beginners, start with a mobile‑first grid:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Define a single column as the default.</li>\n  <li>Add <code>grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);</code> at the first breakpoint (e.g., 600 px).</li>\n  <li>Expand to three or four columns at larger breakpoints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Combine Flexbox for one‑dimensional components (like navigation or card decks) and CSS Grid for two‑dimensional structures (overall page layout). This hybrid approach gives you the flexibility to build clean, responsive interfaces without relying on fixed widths.</p>\n<p>Finally, always test your designs on real devices or using browser dev‑tools responsive mode. Check that images, videos, and iframes also scale by applying <code>max-width: 100%;</code> and <code>height: auto;</code> to prevent overflow.</p>\n<p>By mastering the viewport meta tag, strategic media queries, and flexible grid systems, you will be equipped to create web pages that look great on any screen size.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(117, 110, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS Flexbox diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+Flexbox+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+Flexbox+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+Flexbox+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+Flexbox+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+Flexbox+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Flexbox</h3>\n<p>Flexbox, short for <strong>Flexible Box Layout</strong>, is a modern CSS module that makes it easier to design responsive layouts without using floats or positioning tricks. It introduces a new layout model that allows containers to <strong>distribute space</strong> among items and align them in any direction, even when their size is unknown or dynamic. In this lesson you will learn the core concepts of a flex container, its child items, and the most frequently used properties such as <code>flex-direction</code>, <code>flex-wrap</code>, <code>order</code>, <code>justify-content</code>, and <code>align-items</code>.</p>\n<h3>Creating a Flex Container and Its Items</h3>\n<p>To start using Flexbox you simply set the <code>display</code> property of a parent element to <code>flex</code> (or <code>inline-flex</code> for inline behavior). The moment you do this, all direct children of that element become <strong>flex items</strong>. Example:</p>\n<code> .gallery {\n    display: flex; /* establishes a flex container */\n    gap: 1rem;   /* optional spacing between items */\n }\n .gallery img {\n    flex: 1 1 200px; /* grow, shrink, basis */\n }</code>\n<p>In the snippet above, <code>.gallery</code> is the flex container, while each <code>img</code> tag inside it is a flex item. The container now controls the main‑axis layout, and the items can be sized, ordered, and aligned using flex properties.</p>\n<h3>Flex Direction, Wrap, and Order</h3>\n<p>The <code>flex-direction</code> property defines the main axis along which flex items are placed. It accepts four values:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>row</code> – items flow horizontally from left to right (default).</li>\n<li><code>row-reverse</code> – horizontal flow but reversed.</li>\n<li><code>column</code> – items stack vertically from top to bottom.</li>\n<li><code>column-reverse</code> – vertical flow in reverse order.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the container’s width (or height, for column direction) is insufficient to hold all items, the <code>flex-wrap</code> property determines whether items should stay on a single line or wrap onto multiple lines. The three possible values are <code>nowrap</code> (default), <code>wrap</code>, and <code>wrap-reverse</code>. Combining <code>flex-direction</code> with <code>flex-wrap</code> gives you powerful control over grid‑like layouts without writing media queries.</p>\n<p>Each flex item also has an <code>order</code> property that lets you rearrange the visual order without changing the HTML source. Items with a lower order value appear before those with a higher value. The default order is <code>0</code>, and negative numbers are allowed, which can be handy for accessibility or design tweaks.</p>\n<h3>Justify‑Content and Align‑Items</h3>\n<p>Once the main axis is defined, you can control how remaining space is distributed using <code>justify-content</code>. This property works on the main axis and accepts values such as <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>space-between</code>, <code>space-around</code>, and <code>space-evenly</code>. For example, <code>justify-content: center;</code> centers all items along the main axis, while <code>space-between</code> places the first item at the start, the last at the end, and distributes the rest evenly.</p>\n<p>The cross‑axis alignment is handled by <code>align-items</code>. It aligns flex items perpendicular to the main axis and supports values like <code>stretch</code> (default), <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code>, <code>center</code>, and <code>baseline</code>. If you set <code>align-items: center;</code> on a row‑oriented container, all items will be vertically centered within the container’s height.</p>\n<p>Both <code>justify-content</code> and <code>align-items</code> can be combined to achieve complex centering scenarios, such as a fully centered card:</p>\n<code>.card {\n    display: flex;\n    justify-content: center; /* horizontal centering */\n    align-items: center;    /* vertical centering */\n    height: 300px;\n    border: 1px solid #ccc;\n }</code>\n<h3>Practical Tips and Common Pitfalls</h3>\n<p>When you first experiment with Flexbox, keep these guidelines in mind:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Always set <code>display: flex</code> on the container before applying any flex‑related properties.</li>\n<li>Remember that <code>flex-direction</code> changes the meaning of <code>justify-content</code> and <code>align-items</code> because they are tied to the main and cross axes.</li>\n<li>Use <code>gap</code> (supported in modern browsers) instead of margins for consistent spacing between items.</li>\n<li>Test your layout with different content lengths; the <code>flex</code> shorthand (<code>flex: 1 1 auto</code>) helps items grow and shrink gracefully.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By mastering these fundamentals, you will be able to build fluid, responsive interfaces that adapt to any screen size with minimal code.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(118, 111, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS Grid layout diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+Grid+layout+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+Grid+layout+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+Grid+layout+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+Grid+layout+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+Grid+layout+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Grid Layout</h3><p>Welcome to Day 24 of our beginner web development course. In this lesson we will explore <strong>CSS Grid Layout</strong>, a powerful two‑dimensional system that lets you create complex, responsive designs with relatively simple code. By the end of the day you will be able to define a grid container, set up rows and columns using track sizing, and place items precisely with grid lines or named areas.</p><h3>Defining a Grid Container</h3><p>The first step in using CSS Grid is to turn an element into a grid container. This is done by applying <code>display: grid;</code> (or <code>display: inline-grid;</code> for inline‑level containers). Once the container is defined, all of its direct children automatically become grid items.</p><p>Example:</p><code>&lt;div class=\"grid-container\"&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Item 1&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Item 2&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"item\"&gt;Item 3&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;\n  .grid-container {\n    display: grid; /* activates grid layout */\n    gap: 10px;    /* optional spacing between tracks */\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code><p>Notice that we have not yet defined any rows or columns. The grid will automatically place items in a single column, one after another. To create a more useful layout we need to add track definitions.</p><h3>Creating Rows and Columns with Track Sizing</h3><p>Tracks are the structural lines that make up the grid. You can define them using <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code>. There are several sizing functions you should become familiar with:</p><ul><li><strong>Fixed lengths</strong> – e.g., <code>200px</code> creates a column that is always 200 pixels wide.</li><li><strong>Flexible fractions</strong> – the <code>fr</code> unit distributes remaining space. <code>1fr 2fr</code> creates two columns where the second is twice as wide as the first.</li><li><strong>Auto</strong> – lets the track size itself based on its content.</li><li><strong>Min‑max</strong> – <code>minmax(100px, 1fr)</code> guarantees a minimum of 100 px but can grow up to a fraction of the free space.</li></ul><p>Putting it together:</p><code>.grid-container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); /* three equal columns */\n  grid-template-rows: 150px auto 100px;   /* three rows with different sizing */\n  gap: 15px;\n}</code><p>The <code>repeat()</code> function is a shorthand that reduces repetition. In the example above we create a three‑column layout where each column takes an equal share of the available width.</p><h3>Placing Items Using Grid Lines and Areas</h3><p>Once the tracks are defined, you can position items in several ways:</p><ul><li><strong>Line numbers</strong> – every row and column line is numbered starting at 1. Use <code>grid-column-start</code>, <code>grid-column-end</code>, <code>grid-row-start</code>, and <code>grid-row-end</code> to specify where an item should begin and finish.</li><li><strong>Span keyword</strong> – <code>grid-column: 2 / span 2;</code> starts at line 2 and spans two tracks.</li><li><strong>Named grid areas</strong> – you can give a rectangular region a name with <code>grid-template-areas</code> and then place items by referencing that name.</li></ul><p>Example using line numbers:</p><code>.item:nth-child(1) {\n  grid-column: 1 / 3;   /* spans first two columns */\n  grid-row: 1 / 2;      /* occupies the first row */\n}\n.item:nth-child(2) {\n  grid-column: 3 / 4;   /* sits in the third column */\n  grid-row: 1 / 3;      /* spans two rows */\n}</code><p>Example using named areas:</p><code>.grid-container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;\n  grid-template-rows: auto;\n  grid-template-areas:\n    \"header header\"\n    \"sidebar main\"\n    \"footer footer\";\n  gap: 20px;\n}\n.header  { grid-area: header; }\n.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }\n.main    { grid-area: main; }\n.footer  { grid-area: footer; }\n</code><p>Named areas make the markup more readable, especially for larger layouts. The HTML stays the same; only the CSS assigns each item to its designated area.</p><p>In practice, you will combine these techniques. Start with a simple grid, add rows and columns using <code>fr</code> units for flexibility, and then fine‑tune placement with line numbers or areas. Remember to test your layout at different viewport widths – the grid will automatically re‑flow when you use responsive units like <code>fr</code> or <code>minmax()</code>.</p><p>That concludes today’s lesson on CSS Grid Layout. Experiment with the code snippets, modify track sizes, and try both line‑based and area‑based placement to see which approach feels most intuitive for your projects.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(119, 112, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive navigation menu example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+navigation+menu+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+navigation+menu+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+navigation+menu+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+navigation+menu+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+navigation+menu+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Designing a Hamburger Menu Icon</h3>\n<p>The hamburger icon is the visual cue that tells users a hidden navigation panel is available. For beginners, the simplest approach is to use three stacked <code>span</code> elements inside a button. The button should have <code>type=\'button\'</code> and an <code>aria-label=\'Open menu\'</code> that describes its purpose, for example <code>aria-label=\'Open menu\'</code>. Using CSS, each <code>span</code> is given a fixed height, a background color, and a margin to create the classic three‑line look.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HTML structure</strong>: a <code>button</code> element with a class like <code>menu-toggle</code> that contains three empty <code>span</code> tags.</li>\n<li><strong>CSS basics</strong>: set <code>display: block</code>, <code>width: 30px</code>, <code>height: 3px</code>, <code>background: #333</code>, and <code>margin: 5px 0</code>. Use <code>cursor: pointer</code> to indicate interactivity.</li>\n<li><strong>Responsive scaling</strong>: use <code>rem</code> units or <code>vw</code> units so the icon grows or shrinks with the viewport.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the menu is open, you may want to transform the icon into an X. This can be achieved with a CSS class that rotates the first and third lines and fades out the middle line.</p>\n\n<h3>Toggling Navigation with JavaScript</h3>\n<p>JavaScript connects the button to the hidden navigation panel. The typical pattern is to add or remove a class such as <code>is-open</code> on the navigation container. The script should also update the button’s <code>aria-expanded</code> attribute so screen readers receive accurate state information.</p>\n<code>\ndocument.querySelector(\'.menu-toggle\').addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\n  const nav = document.querySelector(\'.primary-nav\');\n  const expanded = this.getAttribute(\'aria-expanded\') === \'true\' || false;\n  this.setAttribute(\'aria-expanded\', !expanded);\n  nav.classList.toggle(\'is-open\');\n});\n</code>\n<p>Key points to remember:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <code>document.querySelector</code> to target the button and the navigation element.</li>\n<li>Toggle a CSS class that controls <code>display</code> or <code>transform</code> properties.</li>\n<li>Always keep the <code>aria-expanded</code> attribute in sync with the visual state.</li>\n<li>For progressive enhancement, place the script at the bottom of the <code>body</code> or use <code>defer</code> on the <code>script</code> tag.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Ensuring Keyboard Accessibility</h3>\n<p>Keyboard users must be able to open, navigate, and close the menu without a mouse. The button element is naturally focusable, but you need to handle two additional interactions: pressing <code>Enter</code> or <code>Space</code> on the button, and pressing <code>Escape</code> to close the menu.</p>\n<code>\ndocument.addEventListener(\'keydown\', function(e) {\n  const nav = document.querySelector(\'.primary-nav\');\n  const toggle = document.querySelector(\'.menu-toggle\');\n\n  if (e.key === \'Escape\' && nav.classList.contains(\'is-open\')) {\n    nav.classList.remove(\'is-open\');\n    toggle.setAttribute(\'aria-expanded\', \'false\');\n    toggle.focus();\n  }\n});\n</code>\n<p>When the menu becomes visible, move focus to the first link inside the navigation. This can be done by adding <code>tabindex=\'-1\'</code> to the container and calling <code>focus()</code> after the class is added.</p>\n<code>\n toggle.addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\n   const nav = document.querySelector(\'.primary-nav\');\n   nav.classList.toggle(\'is-open\');\n   if (nav.classList.contains(\'is-open\')) {\n     const firstLink = nav.querySelector(\'a\');\n     firstLink.focus();\n   }\n });\n</code>\n<p>Additional accessibility tips:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Provide a visible focus outline for all interactive elements.</li>\n<li>Ensure the navigation order is logical when the menu opens.</li>\n<li>Test with a screen reader to verify that the <code>aria-label</code> and <code>aria-expanded</code> values are announced correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By combining a simple HTML structure, a few lines of CSS, and a small JavaScript module, you can create a responsive, accessible navigation menu that works on mobile devices and desktop browsers alike.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(120, 113, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Bootstrap grid system responsive layout example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Bootstrap+grid+system+responsive+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Bootstrap+grid+system+responsive+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Bootstrap+grid+system+responsive+layout+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Bootstrap+grid+system+responsive+layout+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Bootstrap+grid+system+responsive+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Bootstrap Grid System Basics</h3>\n<p>Bootstrap’s grid system is the foundation for building responsive layouts. It uses a series of <strong>containers</strong>, <strong>rows</strong>, and <strong>columns</strong> to align content. The grid is based on a 12‑column layout, which means any row can be divided into up to twelve equal parts. By combining column classes, you can create complex structures that automatically adjust to different screen sizes.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>.container</code> – provides a fixed‑width wrapper that adapts at each breakpoint.</li>\n  <li><code>.container-fluid</code> – stretches the wrapper to the full width of the viewport.</li>\n  <li><code>.row</code> – creates a horizontal group of columns and clears the float.</li>\n  <li><code>.col-*-*</code> – defines how many of the 12 columns an element should span at a given breakpoint (e.g., <code>.col-md-6</code> occupies half the row on medium screens and larger).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of a simple two‑column layout:</p>\n<code>&lt;div class=\"container\"&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"row\"&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\"col-md-8\"&gt;Main content&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\"col-md-4\"&gt;Sidebar&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code>\n<p>When the viewport shrinks below the <code>md</code> breakpoint (768px), the columns automatically stack vertically, ensuring readability on mobile devices.</p>\n\n<h3>Using Utility Classes for Spacing and Visibility</h3>\n<p>Bootstrap includes a rich set of utility classes that let you adjust margin, padding, and visibility without writing custom CSS. These classes follow a consistent naming pattern, making them easy to remember.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Spacing</strong>: <code>.m{t|b|l|r|x|y}-{0-5}</code> for margin, <code>.p{t|b|l|r|x|y}-{0-5}</code> for padding. The number represents a multiple of <code>0.25rem</code> (e.g., <code>.mt-3</code> adds <code>1rem</code> of top margin).</li>\n  <li><strong>Visibility</strong>: <code>.d-{breakpoint}-{value}</code> controls display. For example, <code>.d-none d-md-block</code> hides an element on extra‑small and small screens but shows it on medium and larger screens.</li>\n  <li><strong>Responsive display</strong>: <code>.d-sm-inline</code>, <code>.d-lg-flex</code>, etc., let you change the display type at specific breakpoints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sample usage:</p>\n<code>&lt;div class=\"p-3 mb-4 d-none d-lg-block\"&gt;\n  This box is padded, has a bottom margin, and only appears on large screens.\n&lt;/div&gt;</code>\n\n<h3>Implementing Responsive Components (Cards, Navbar)</h3>\n<p>Bootstrap ships with ready‑made components that are already responsive. Two of the most common are <strong>cards</strong> and the <strong>navbar</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cards</strong>: A flexible content container with multiple variants. Use <code>.card</code>, <code>.card-body</code>, <code>.card-title</code>, and <code>.card-img-top</code> to build a card. The grid can be combined with cards to create responsive card decks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Navbar</strong>: A navigation header that collapses into a hamburger menu on smaller screens. The key classes are <code>.navbar</code>, <code>.navbar-expand-{breakpoint}</code>, <code>.navbar-light</code> or <code>.navbar-dark</code>, and <code>.bg-*</code> for background colors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of a responsive card grid:</p>\n<code>&lt;div class=\"container\"&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"row row-cols-1 row-cols-sm-2 row-cols-lg-3 g-4\"&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\"col\"&gt;\n      &lt;div class=\"card h-100\"&gt;\n        &lt;img src=\"...\" class=\"card-img-top\" alt=\"...\"&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"card-body\"&gt;\n          &lt;h5 class=\"card-title\"&gt;Card title&lt;/h5&gt;\n          &lt;p class=\"card-text\"&gt;Some quick example text.&lt;/p&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n    <!-- Repeat .col for more cards -->\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code>\n<p>The <code>.row-cols-*</code> classes automatically adjust the number of columns per row at different breakpoints, creating a fluid card layout.</p>\n<p>Example of a responsive navbar:</p>\n<code>&lt;nav class=\"navbar navbar-expand-md navbar-dark bg-primary\"&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"container-fluid\"&gt;\n    &lt;a class=\"navbar-brand\" href=\"#\"&gt;MySite&lt;/a&gt;\n    &lt;button class=\"navbar-toggler\" type=\"button\" data-bs-toggle=\"collapse\" data-bs-target=\"#navbarNav\" aria-controls=\"navbarNav\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-label=\"Toggle navigation\"&gt;\n      &lt;span class=\"navbar-toggler-icon\"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\"collapse navbar-collapse\" id=\"navbarNav\"&gt;\n      &lt;ul class=\"navbar-nav ms-auto\"&gt;\n        &lt;li class=\"nav-item\"&gt;&lt;a class=\"nav-link active\" href=\"#\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li class=\"nav-item\"&gt;&lt;a class=\"nav-link\" href=\"#\"&gt;Features&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li class=\"nav-item\"&gt;&lt;a class=\"nav-link\" href=\"#\"&gt;Pricing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;</code>\n<p>The <code>.navbar-expand-md</code> class tells the navbar to stay expanded on medium screens (≥768px) and collapse into a toggler on smaller devices, providing an optimal navigation experience across all devices.</p>\n<p>By mastering the grid, utility classes, and responsive components, beginners can quickly prototype professional‑looking web pages that look great on phones, tablets, and desktops.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06');
INSERT INTO `content_blocks` (`id`, `topic_id`, `type`, `content`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(121, 114, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: portfolio page layout example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=portfolio+page+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/portfolio+page+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/portfolio+page+layout+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/portfolio+page+layout+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=portfolio+page+layout+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Structuring a Project Showcase Section</h3>\n<p>When you build a portfolio page, the <strong>project showcase</strong> is the heart of the site. It tells visitors what you can do and why they should hire you. Start by creating a dedicated <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> that groups all projects together. Inside this section, use a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with a class like <code>project-card</code> for each individual project. This approach keeps the markup clean and makes styling with CSS straightforward.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>&lt;section id=\"projects\"&gt;</code> – the container for the whole showcase.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;h2&gt;My Projects&lt;/h2&gt;</code> – a heading that introduces the section.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;div class=\"project-card\"&gt;</code> – a reusable block for each project.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;h3&gt;Project Title&lt;/h3&gt;</code> – the name of the work.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;p&gt;Brief description&lt;/p&gt;</code> – a short paragraph that explains the problem, solution, and technologies used.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;a href=\"...\" target=\"_blank\"&gt;Live Demo&lt;/a&gt;</code> – a link to the live version.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;a href=\"...\" target=\"_blank\"&gt;Source Code&lt;/a&gt;</code> – a link to the repository.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By repeating the <code>project-card</code> block, you can add as many projects as you need without changing the overall structure. Later, CSS Grid or Flexbox will let you arrange the cards in rows and columns, making the layout responsive.</p>\n\n<h3>Embedding Images, Videos, and External Links</h3>\n<p>Visual media brings your projects to life. Use the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag for screenshots and the <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> tag for short demos. Remember to add <code>alt</code> attributes to images for accessibility and SEO.</p>\n<code>\n&lt;img src=\"images/project1-thumbnail.jpg\" alt=\"Screenshot of Project 1\" class=\"project-img\"&gt;\n</code>\n<p>For video, the <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> element supports multiple source formats. Include controls so visitors can play, pause, and adjust volume.</p>\n<code>\n&lt;video controls width=\"320\"&gt;\n  &lt;source src=\"videos/project1-demo.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"&gt;\n  Your browser does not support the video tag.\n&lt;/video&gt;\n</code>\n<p>If you prefer to embed a YouTube demo, use an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>. Set the <code>allowfullscreen</code> attribute so the video can expand to full screen.</p>\n<code>\n&lt;iframe width=\"560\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID\" \n        title=\"YouTube video player\" frameborder=\"0\" \n        allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" \n        allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;\n</code>\n<p>External links, such as a live demo or a GitHub repository, should open in a new tab. Use <code>target=\"_blank\"</code> and always add <code>rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"</code> for security.</p>\n<code>\n&lt;a href=\"https://example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"&gt;Live Demo&lt;/a&gt;\n</code>\n\n<h3>Creating a Simple Contact Form with HTML5 Validation</h3>\n<p>A contact form lets potential clients reach out directly. HTML5 provides built‑in validation attributes, so you can create a functional form without JavaScript.</p>\n<code>\n&lt;form action=\"/send-message\" method=\"POST\" id=\"contact-form\"&gt;\n  &lt;label for=\"name\"&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=\"text\" id=\"name\" name=\"name\" required minlength=\"2\" placeholder=\"Your name\"&gt;\n\n  &lt;label for=\"email\"&gt;Email:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" required placeholder=\"you@example.com\"&gt;\n\n  &lt;label for=\"subject\"&gt;Subject:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=\"text\" id=\"subject\" name=\"subject\" required maxlength=\"100\"&gt;\n\n  &lt;label for=\"message\"&gt;Message:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;textarea id=\"message\" name=\"message\" rows=\"5\" required minlength=\"10\" placeholder=\"Write your message here...\"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;\n\n  &lt;button type=\"submit\"&gt;Send Message&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;\n</code>\n<p>Key validation attributes used:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>required</strong> – ensures the field is not left empty.</li>\n  <li><strong>type=\"email\"</strong> – checks for a valid email format.</li>\n  <li><strong>minlength / maxlength</strong> – controls the length of text inputs.</li>\n  <li><strong>placeholder</strong> – provides a hint to the user.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the user submits the form, the browser will automatically display error messages if any field fails validation. This gives a smooth user experience while keeping the code lightweight.</p>\n<p>Once you have the HTML in place, you can enhance the form with CSS to match the rest of your portfolio’s design, and later add server‑side handling (e.g., using a service like Formspree or a custom backend) to actually send the email.</p>\n<p>Putting it all together, your portfolio page will have a well‑structured project showcase, rich media that demonstrates your work, and a functional contact form that invites collaboration. Practice by creating three sample projects, embedding screenshots and a short video for each, and testing the form validation in different browsers.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(122, 115, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: final web development project review and quiz infographic</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=final+web+development+project+review+and+quiz+infographic\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/final+web+development+project+review+and+quiz+infographic\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/final+web+development+project+review+and+quiz+infographic/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/final+web+development+project+review+and+quiz+infographic/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=final+web+development+project+review+and+quiz+infographic\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Review of Key Concepts from the Course</h3>\n<p>Over the past 27 days you have built a solid foundation in web development. This final review brings together the most important ideas so you can see how they fit together when you create real-world sites.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HTML Basics</strong>: Document structure (<code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>), semantic elements (<code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code>), and proper use of attributes.</li>\n<li><strong>CSS Fundamentals</strong>: Selectors, the cascade, specificity, box model, layout techniques (<code>flexbox</code> and <code>grid</code>), and responsive design with media queries.</li>\n<li><strong>JavaScript Essentials</strong>: Variables, data types, functions, DOM manipulation, event handling, and a brief look at ES6 features such as <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, arrow functions, and template literals.</li>\n<li><strong>Version Control with Git</strong>: Initialising a repository, committing changes, branching, merging, and pushing to remote services like GitHub.</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility &amp; Performance</strong>: Using semantic markup, ARIA attributes, image optimisation, and basic performance testing tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keeping these concepts in mind will help you troubleshoot, optimise, and extend any project you start.</p>\n\n<h3>Final Project Checklist and Best Practices</h3>\n<p>Before you submit your final project, run through this checklist to ensure a professional, polished result.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HTML Validation</strong>: Run your pages through the W3C validator and fix any errors or warnings.</li>\n<li><strong>Responsive Design</strong>: Verify that the layout works on at least three viewport widths (mobile, tablet, desktop) using browser dev tools.</li>\n<li><strong>CSS Organisation</strong>: Group related rules, use comments, and consider a naming convention such as BEM.</li>\n<li><strong>JavaScript Quality</strong>: Remove console statements, use <code>\"use strict\"</code>, and ensure functions are modular.</li>\n<li><strong>Git History</strong>: Have clear, atomic commit messages; include a <code>README.md</code> that explains setup, features, and any known issues.</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility Checks</strong>: Test keyboard navigation, provide alt text for images, and ensure sufficient colour contrast.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance Optimisation</strong>: Compress images, minify CSS/JS, and check the page load time with Lighthouse or PageSpeed Insights.</li>\n<li><strong>Documentation</strong>: Add inline comments where logic is complex, and include a short video demo if possible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Following these best practices not only improves your grade but also mirrors the workflow of professional development teams.</p>\n\n<h3>Comprehensive Quiz Covering All Modules</h3>\n<p>The quiz below contains five multiple‑choice questions. Answer each one before checking the explanations. This will reinforce your learning and highlight any areas that need a quick review.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(123, 115, 'quiz', '28', 2, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(124, 116, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: beginner web developer deploying website hosting FTP DNS settings illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=beginner+web+developer+deploying+website+hosting+FTP+DNS+settings+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/beginner+web+developer+deploying+website+hosting+FTP+DNS+settings+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/beginner+web+developer+deploying+website+hosting+FTP+DNS+settings+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/beginner+web+developer+deploying+website+hosting+FTP+DNS+settings+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=beginner+web+developer+deploying+website+hosting+FTP+DNS+settings+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Choosing a Hosting Provider</h3>\n<p>Before your site can be seen by anyone on the internet, it needs a place to live. A <strong>hosting provider</strong> supplies the server space, network connectivity, and tools that make your files reachable 24/7. When you are just starting out, look for a provider that offers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Easy-to-use control panel</strong> (cPanel, Plesk, or a custom dashboard) so you can manage files, databases, and email without deep technical knowledge.</li>\n<li><strong>Free SSL certificates</strong> via Let’s Encrypt or similar services, which are essential for security and SEO.</li>\n<li><strong>Support for FTP/SFTP</strong> because you will need a reliable way to upload your site files.</li>\n<li><strong>Reasonable pricing</strong> with a clear renewal policy; many beginners start with shared hosting plans that cost $3‑$10 per month.</li>\n<li><strong>Good uptime guarantees</strong> (99.9% or higher) to keep your site online.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Popular beginner‑friendly hosts include Bluehost, SiteGround, HostGator, and DreamHost. All of them provide a one‑click installer for popular platforms (WordPress, Joomla) and a simple domain registration process.</p>\n<h3>Uploading Files via FTP/SFTP</h3>\n<p>Once you have a hosting account, the next step is to move the files you created on your local computer to the remote server. The most common method is using <strong>FTP (File Transfer Protocol)</strong> or its secure counterpart <strong>SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol)</strong>. Here’s a quick workflow:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Install an FTP client</strong> – free tools like FileZilla, WinSCP, or Cyberduck work on Windows, macOS, and Linux.</li>\n<li><strong>Gather connection details</strong> from your host’s control panel. You will need:\n<ul>\n<li>Hostname (e.g., <code>ftp.yourdomain.com</code>)</li>\n<li>Username</li>\n<li>Password</li>\n<li>Port number (21 for FTP, 22 for SFTP)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Connect to the server</strong>:\n<code>\n# Example using FileZilla\nHost: ftp.yourdomain.com\nPort: 21\nProtocol: FTP – File Transfer Protocol\n</code>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Navigate to the public folder</strong>. Most hosts use a directory named <code>public_html</code> or <code>www</code>. This is where the web server looks for the homepage (usually <code>index.html</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Upload your files</strong>. Drag and drop the entire project folder from the left pane (your local machine) to the right pane (the server). Wait for the transfer to finish.</li>\n<li><strong>Verify the upload</strong> by visiting <code>http://yourdomain.com</code> in a browser. If you see your site, the files are in the right place.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you prefer the command line, a simple SFTP command looks like this:</p>\n<code>sftp username@ftp.yourdomain.com</code>\n<p>After logging in, use <code>put</code> to transfer a file or <code>mput *</code> to upload everything in the current directory.</p>\n<h3>Configuring Domain and DNS Settings</h3>\n<p>Even if your files are on the server, visitors need a readable address like <code>www.myawesomewebsite.com</code>. This is where <strong>Domain Name System (DNS)</strong> comes into play. DNS translates human‑friendly domain names into the numeric IP address of your server.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Register a domain</strong> through your host or a dedicated registrar such as Namecheap, GoDaddy, or Google Domains.</li>\n<li><strong>Locate the DNS management panel</strong>. Most registrars provide a page titled “DNS Settings,” “Nameservers,” or “Zone Editor.”</li>\n<li><strong>Set the nameservers</strong> to point at your hosting provider. For example, if you use SiteGround, you might replace the default nameservers with <code>ns1.siteground.net</code> and <code>ns2.siteground.net</code>. This tells the internet to ask SiteGround for the DNS records of your domain.</li>\n<li><strong>Add an A record</strong> that points your root domain (<code>@</code>) to the IP address of your server. The record will look like:\n<code>\n@   IN   A   192.0.2.123\n</code>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Create a CNAME record</strong> for <code>www</code> that points to the root domain, ensuring both <code>example.com</code> and <code>www.example.com</code> resolve to the same site.\n<code>\nwww   IN   CNAME   @\n</code>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Wait for propagation</strong>. DNS changes can take anywhere from a few minutes to 48 hours to spread across the globe. During this time, you may see the old site or a “server not found” message.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once propagation is complete, revisit your domain in a browser. If you see the same page you uploaded via FTP, congratulations! Your website is live and reachable worldwide.</p>\n<p>In the next lesson we will explore how to set up a simple <strong>HTTPS</strong> certificate, add basic analytics, and perform routine backups to keep your site safe.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(125, 117, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development course review and portfolio planning</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+course+review+and+portfolio+planning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+course+review+and+portfolio+planning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+course+review+and+portfolio+planning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+course+review+and+portfolio+planning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+course+review+and+portfolio+planning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Course Review: Core Concepts Recap</h3>\n<p>Over the past 29 days you have built a solid foundation in web development. <strong>HTML</strong> gave you the structure of a page, <strong>CSS</strong> added visual styling, and <strong>JavaScript</strong> introduced interactivity. You also explored essential tools such as <code>Git</code> for version control, <code>VS Code</code> as a code editor, and the command line for navigating files. Finally, you learned how to host static sites on platforms like GitHub Pages and Netlify. This final day ties everything together and points you toward the next phase of your learning journey.</p>\n<h3>Planning Your Portfolio Project</h3>\n<p>A portfolio is the most effective way to showcase the skills you have acquired. Follow these steps to plan a project that highlights each major topic:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Define the purpose:</strong> Decide whether your portfolio will be a personal showcase, a resume site, or a collection of mini‑apps.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sketch the layout:</strong> Use paper or a tool like Figma to draft the header, navigation, project gallery, about section, and contact form.</li>\n  <li><strong>Choose the tech stack:</strong> For a beginner portfolio, plain <code>HTML</code>, <code>CSS</code>, and <code>JavaScript</code> are sufficient. You may also experiment with a CSS framework (e.g., Tailwind or Bootstrap) or a static site generator like <code>Eleventy</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Set up the repository:</strong> Create a new GitHub repository, clone it locally, and initialize a <code>README.md</code> that outlines the project goals.</li>\n  <li><strong>Break the work into milestones:</strong> Example milestones – create the homepage markup, style the navigation bar, add a responsive grid for projects, implement a modal popup with JavaScript, and finally deploy.</li>\n  <li><strong>Document as you go:</strong> Write clear commit messages, add comments to your code, and keep a simple changelog.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Building the Portfolio – Sample Code Snippets</h3>\n<p>Below are a few reusable snippets you can copy into your project.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!-- Responsive navigation bar --&gt;\n&lt;nav class=\"navbar\"&gt;\n  &lt;ul class=\"nav-list\"&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#home\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#projects\"&gt;Projects&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#about\"&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#contact\"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Simple modal for project details --&gt;\n&lt;div id=\"modal\" class=\"modal hidden\"&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\"modal-content\"&gt;\n    &lt;span class=\"close\"&gt;&times;&lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Project Title&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Description of the project goes here.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n&lt;script&gt;\n  const modal = document.getElementById(\'modal\');\n  const closeBtn = document.querySelector(\'.close\');\n  document.querySelectorAll(\'.project-card\').forEach(card =&gt; {\n    card.addEventListener(\'click\', () =&gt; modal.classList.remove(\'hidden\'));\n  });\n  closeBtn.addEventListener(\'click\', () =&gt; modal.classList.add(\'hidden\'));\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n<h3>Deploying and Sharing Your Work</h3>\n<p>When your portfolio is ready, push the final commit to GitHub and enable GitHub Pages from the repository settings. If you prefer Netlify, drag the folder onto the Netlify dashboard and let the platform handle the build. After deployment, share the live URL on LinkedIn, Twitter, and developer communities to attract feedback and potential collaborators.</p>\n<h3>Resources for Continued Learning</h3>\n<p>Learning never stops. Here are curated resources to deepen your expertise:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>HTML &amp; CSS:</strong> <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML\" target=\"_blank\">MDN Web Docs – HTML</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS\" target=\"_blank\">MDN Web Docs – CSS</a></li>\n  <li><strong>JavaScript:</strong> <a href=\"https://javascript.info/\" target=\"_blank\">JavaScript.info</a>, <a href=\"https://frontendmasters.com/books/front-end-handbook/2023/\" target=\"_blank\">Front‑End Handbook 2023</a></li>\n  <li><strong>Version Control:</strong> <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2\" target=\"_blank\">Pro Git Book</a></li>\n  <li><strong>Frameworks:</strong> <a href=\"https://reactjs.org/\" target=\"_blank\">React</a>, <a href=\"https://vuejs.org/\" target=\"_blank\">Vue</a>, <a href=\"https://angular.io/\" target=\"_blank\">Angular</a></li>\n  <li><strong>Design &amp; Prototyping:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.figma.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Figma</a>, <a href=\"https://tailwindcss.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Tailwind CSS</a></li>\n  <li><strong>Career Guidance:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.freecodecamp.org/news/how-to-get-a-web-development-job/\" target=\"_blank\">FreeCodeCamp – Job Guide</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Next Steps: Setting Goals for the Next 30 Days</h3>\n<p>To keep momentum, set concrete, measurable goals. For example:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Complete a small JavaScript project (e.g., a to‑do list) each week.</li>\n  <li>Learn the basics of a front‑end framework and build a single‑page app.</li>\n  <li>Contribute to an open‑source repository on GitHub.</li>\n  <li>Attend at least one local or virtual developer meetup.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember, the journey from beginner to professional is a marathon, not a sprint. Celebrate the milestones you have already achieved, and keep building, experimenting, and sharing. Good luck, and happy coding!</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:06:06', '2026-04-03 13:06:06'),
(126, 118, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development career paths</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+career+paths\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+career+paths\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+career+paths/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+career+paths/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+career+paths\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Welcome to Web Development</h3><p>Web development is the process of building and maintaining websites and web applications. It involves a combination of technical skills, such as programming languages, databases, and web frameworks, as well as soft skills, such as communication, teamwork, and problem-solving.</p><p>The field of web development has evolved significantly over the years, and it continues to grow and change as new technologies and trends emerge. In this course, we will explore the basics of web development, including the history of the web, career paths, and the skills and tools needed to become a web developer.</p><h3>History of Web Development</h3><p>The history of web development dates back to the late 1980s, when the World Wide Web was first invented by Tim Berners-Lee. The early web was simple, with basic text-based websites and limited functionality. However, as the web grew in popularity, new technologies and tools emerged, such as JavaScript, CSS, and HTML.</p><p>Today, the web is a complex and dynamic platform, with millions of websites and web applications. The web has become an essential part of modern life, and web development has become a highly sought-after career path. Some of the key milestones in the history of web development include:</p><ul><li>The invention of the World Wide Web in 1989</li><li>The introduction of JavaScript in 1995</li><li>The development of CSS in 1996</li><li>The emergence of web frameworks such as Ruby on Rails and Django in the early 2000s</li><li>The rise of mobile devices and responsive web design in the 2010s</li></ul><h3>Career Paths in Web Development</h3><p>There are many career paths available in web development, depending on your interests, skills, and experience. Some of the most common career paths include:</p><ul><li><strong>Front-end developer</strong>: responsible for building the user interface and user experience of a website or web application using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript</li><li><strong>Back-end developer</strong>: responsible for building the server-side logic and database integration of a website or web application using languages such as Java, Python, and Ruby</li><li><strong>Full-stack developer</strong>: responsible for building both the front-end and back-end of a website or web application</li><li><strong>Web designer</strong>: responsible for creating the visual design and user experience of a website or web application</li><li><strong>DevOps engineer</strong>: responsible for ensuring the smooth operation of a website or web application, including deployment, scaling, and maintenance</li></ul><p>To get started with web development, you will need to learn some basic programming skills, such as <code>HTML</code>, <code>CSS</code>, and <code>JavaScript</code>. You will also need to familiarize yourself with web frameworks, databases, and other tools and technologies. With practice and dedication, you can build a successful career in web development and create dynamic and interactive web applications that engage and inspire users.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(127, 119, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: visual studio code interface</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=visual+studio+code+interface\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/visual+studio+code+interface\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/visual+studio+code+interface/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/visual+studio+code+interface/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=visual+studio+code+interface\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Setting Up the Development Environment</h3><p>Welcome to Day 2 of our web development course. Today, we will be setting up our development environment, which is an essential step in starting our web development journey. We will be installing a code editor, setting up a version control system, and introducing ourselves to the basics of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.</p><h3>Installing a Code Editor</h3><p>A code editor is a software application that allows us to write, edit, and manage our code. There are many code editors available, but some popular ones include Visual Studio Code, Sublime Text, and Atom. For this course, we will be using Visual Studio Code.</p><p>To install Visual Studio Code, follow these steps:</p><ul><li>Go to the Visual Studio Code website and download the installer for your operating system.</li><li>Run the installer and follow the prompts to install Visual Studio Code.</li><li>Once installed, open Visual Studio Code and familiarize yourself with the interface.</li></ul><p>Visual Studio Code has many features that make it an ideal code editor, including syntax highlighting, code completion, and debugging tools.</p><h3>Setting Up a Version Control System</h3><p>A version control system is a software application that helps us manage different versions of our code. This is useful for tracking changes, collaborating with others, and reverting to previous versions if something goes wrong. For this course, we will be using Git.</p><p>To set up Git, follow these steps:</p><ul><li>Go to the Git website and download the installer for your operating system.</li><li>Run the installer and follow the prompts to install Git.</li><li>Once installed, open a terminal or command prompt and type <code>git --version</code> to verify that Git is installed correctly.</li></ul><p>Git has many commands that we will be using throughout this course, including <code>git init</code> to initialize a new repository, <code>git add</code> to stage changes, and <code>git commit</code> to commit changes.</p><h3>Introduction to HTML, CSS, and JavaScript</h3><p>HTML, CSS, and JavaScript are the building blocks of the web. HTML (Hypertext Markup Language) is used to structure and organize content on the web. CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is used to style and layout content on the web. JavaScript is used to add interactivity to web pages.</p><p>Let\'s take a look at a simple example of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript:</p><p>HTML: <code>&lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;</code></p><p>CSS: <code>h1 { color: blue; }</code></p><p>JavaScript: <code>console.log(&quot;Hello World&quot;);</code></p><p>In this example, we have an HTML heading element with the text <strong>Hello World</strong>. We have a CSS rule that sets the color of the heading element to blue. We have a JavaScript statement that logs <strong>Hello World</strong> to the console.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(128, 120, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: html basics tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=html+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/html+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/html+basics+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/html+basics+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=html+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to HTML</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language used to create web pages. It is the backbone of a website, providing the structure and content that the web browser renders to the user. HTML consists of a series of elements, represented by tags, which are used to wrap around content and apply meaning to it.</p><h3>HTML Tags and Attributes</h3><p>HTML tags are used to define the structure and content of a web page. They are represented by a keyword surrounded by angle brackets, such as <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>. Most HTML tags have a closing tag, which is the same keyword preceded by a forward slash, such as <code>&lt;/html&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>. HTML attributes are used to provide additional information about an element, such as its style, size, or behavior. They are added to the opening tag of an element and consist of a name and a value, separated by an equals sign, such as <code>style=&quot;color: blue;&quot;</code> or <code>width=&quot;500&quot;</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>Types of HTML Tags:</strong><ul><li>Container tags: These tags have a closing tag and can contain other elements, such as <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>.</li><li>Empty tags: These tags do not have a closing tag and cannot contain other elements, such as <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>.</li><li>Inline tags: These tags are used to apply a style or formatting to a portion of text, such as <code>&lt;b&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;i&gt;</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Common HTML Attributes:</strong><ul><li><code>id</code>: Used to assign a unique identifier to an element.</li><li><code>class</code>: Used to assign a class name to an element, which can be used to apply styles or behaviors.</li><li><code>style</code>: Used to apply inline styles to an element.</li><li><code>href</code>: Used to specify the URL of a linked resource.</li></ul></li></ul><h3>Basic HTML Structure</h3><p>The basic structure of an HTML document consists of a series of elements, including the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element, the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> element, and the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> element. The <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element is the root element of the document and contains all the other elements. The <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> element contains metadata about the document, such as its title, character encoding, and links to external stylesheets or scripts. The <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> element contains the content of the document, such as text, images, and other media.</p><p>A basic HTML document might look like this: <code>&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My Web Page&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to my web page&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(129, 121, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: html structure and semantics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=html+structure+and+semantics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/html+structure+and+semantics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/html+structure+and+semantics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/html+structure+and+semantics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=html+structure+and+semantics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>HTML Structure and Semantics</h3><p>On the fourth day of our web development course, we will be exploring the basics of HTML structure and semantics. HTML, or HyperText Markup Language, is the standard markup language used to create web pages. It provides the structure and content of a web page, while CSS and JavaScript add style and interactivity.</p><p>Let\'s start with the basics of HTML headings and paragraphs. HTML headings are defined using the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h4&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h5&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> tags. These tags are used to define the hierarchy of headings on a web page, with <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> being the most important and <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> being the least important.</p><h3>HTML Headings</h3><p>For example, the following code uses the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> tag to define a top-level heading:</p><p><code>&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to Our Website&lt;/h1&gt;</code></p><p>HTML paragraphs are defined using the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag. This tag is used to group a block of text together, and can be used to add structure to a web page.</p><h3>HTML Paragraphs</h3><p>For example, the following code uses the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag to define a paragraph of text:</p><p><code>&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text. It can contain multiple sentences, and can be used to add structure to a web page.&lt;/p&gt;</code></p><p>Next, let\'s talk about HTML lists and links. HTML lists are defined using the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> tags. The <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> tag is used to define an unordered list, while the <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> tag is used to define an ordered list. The <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> tag is used to define a list item.</p><h3>HTML Lists</h3><p>For example, the following code uses the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> tags to define an unordered list:</p><ul><li>Item 1</li><li>Item 2</li><li>Item 3</li></ul><p><code>&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 2&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 3&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code></p><p>HTML links are defined using the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag. This tag is used to create a hyperlink to another web page or to an email address.</p><h3>HTML Links</h3><p>For example, the following code uses the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag to define a link to another web page:</p><p><code>&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.example.com&quot;&gt;Visit Example.com&lt;/a&gt;</code></p><p>Finally, let\'s talk about the introduction to HTML semantics. HTML semantics refer to the use of HTML tags to provide meaning to the structure of a web page. This can include the use of tags such as <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> to define the different parts of a web page.</p><h3>Introduction to HTML Semantics</h3><p>For example, the following code uses the <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> tag to define the header section of a web page:</p><p><code>&lt;header&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to Our Website&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;nav&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;/header&gt;</code></p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>HTML headings are defined using the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> tags.</li><li>HTML paragraphs are defined using the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag.</li><li>HTML lists are defined using the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> tags.</li><li>HTML links are defined using the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag.</li><li>HTML semantics refer to the use of HTML tags to provide meaning to the structure of a web page.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56');
