(142, 133, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS properties and values</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+properties+and+values\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+properties+and+values\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+properties+and+values/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+properties+and+values/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+properties+and+values\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Properties and Values</h3><p>CSS properties are used to control the layout and appearance of HTML elements. They are defined using a property name followed by a colon and the property value. For example, <code>color: blue;</code> sets the text color of an element to blue.</p><p>There are many different CSS properties, including <strong>background-color</strong>, <strong>font-size</strong>, <strong>margin</strong>, and <strong>padding</strong>. Each property has its own set of allowed values, which can be specified using different data types.</p><h3>Data Types</h3><p>CSS property values can be specified using a variety of data types, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Lengths</strong>: These are used to specify measurements, such as <code>10px</code> or <code>20em</code>.</li><li><strong>Percentages</strong>: These are used to specify values as a percentage of another value, such as <code>50%</code>.</li><li><strong>Colors</strong>: These are used to specify colors, such as <code>red</code> or <code>#ff0000</code>.</li><li><strong>Strings</strong>: These are used to specify text values, such as <code>\'hello world\'</code>.</li><li><strong>Keywords</strong>: These are used to specify predefined values, such as <code>none</code> or <code>inherit</code>.</li></ul><h3>Units and Measurements</h3><p>CSS uses various units to measure lengths, including:</p><ul><li><strong>px</strong> (pixels): This is the most common unit, and is used to specify measurements in pixels.</li><li><strong>em</strong>: This unit is relative to the font size of the element, and is often used to specify measurements for text.</li><li><strong>rem</strong>: This unit is relative to the root element of the document, and is often used to specify measurements for layouts.</li><li><strong>cm</strong> (centimeters), <strong>mm</strong> (millimeters), and <strong>in</strong> (inches): These units are used to specify measurements in physical units.</li><li><strong>vw</strong> (viewport width) and <strong>vh</strong> (viewport height): These units are used to specify measurements relative to the size of the viewport.</li></ul><p>Understanding CSS properties and values is essential for building and styling web pages. By using the correct data types and units, you can create layouts and designs that are both visually appealing and functional.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(143, 134, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: box model diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/box+model+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/box+model+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to the Box Model</h3><p>The box model is a fundamental concept in web development that describes the structure of an HTML element as a rectangular box. Understanding the box model is crucial for creating well-structured and visually appealing web pages. In this section, we will explore the basics of the box model and its components.</p><h3>Box Model Basics</h3><p>The box model consists of four main parts: content, padding, border, and margin. Each part plays a vital role in defining the size and appearance of an element.</p><ul><li><strong>Content Area</strong>: The content area is the innermost part of the box model, where the actual content of the element is displayed.</li><li><strong>Padding</strong>: The padding is the space between the content area and the border. It is used to add some breathing room around the content.</li><li><strong>Border</strong>: The border is the visible outline of the box model. It can be styled with different colors, widths, and styles.</li><li><strong>Margin</strong>: The margin is the space between the box model and other elements on the page. It is used to create some distance between elements.</li></ul><h3>Margin and Padding</h3><p>Margin and padding are two essential components of the box model that can be used to control the spacing and layout of elements. The main difference between margin and padding is that margin is used to create space between elements, while padding is used to create space between the content and the border.</p><p>For example, to add a margin of 20 pixels to an element, you can use the following CSS code: <code>margin: 20px;</code>. Similarly, to add a padding of 10 pixels to an element, you can use the following CSS code: <code>padding: 10px;</code>.</p><h3>Border and Outline</h3><p>The border and outline are two related but distinct components of the box model. The border is the visible outline of the box model, while the outline is a decorative border that is drawn outside the border.</p><p>The border can be styled with different colors, widths, and styles using the following CSS properties: <code>border-color</code>, <code>border-width</code>, and <code>border-style</code>. For example, to create a red border with a width of 2 pixels and a solid style, you can use the following CSS code: <code>border: 2px solid red;</code>.</p><p>The outline, on the other hand, can be styled using the following CSS properties: <code>outline-color</code>, <code>outline-width</code>, and <code>outline-style</code>. For example, to create a blue outline with a width of 1 pixel and a dashed style, you can use the following CSS code: <code>outline: 1px dashed blue;</code>.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(144, 135, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css layout and positioning</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+layout+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+layout+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+layout+and+positioning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+layout+and+positioning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+layout+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Layout and Positioning</h3><p>Welcome to Day 18 of our web development course. Today, we will be covering the basics of CSS layout and positioning. This topic is crucial in creating visually appealing and user-friendly websites.</p><p>The <strong>display property</strong> is used to define the display type of an element. It can have values such as block, inline, inline-block, flex, grid, etc. The display property is used to control the layout of an element and its relationship with other elements.</p><ul><li><code>display: block;</code> - Occupies the full width available, and starts on a new line.</li><li><code>display: inline;</code> - Occupies only the space needed for its content, and does not start on a new line.</li><li><code>display: inline-block;</code> - Combines the properties of block and inline, allowing elements to be placed side by side while maintaining block-level features.</li></ul><p>The <strong>position property</strong> is used to define the positioning of an element. It can have values such as static, relative, absolute, fixed, sticky, etc. The position property is used to control the position of an element in relation to its parent element or the viewport.</p><ul><li><code>position: static;</code> - The default position, where the element is positioned according to the normal flow of the document.</li><li><code>position: relative;</code> - The element is positioned relative to its normal position, without removing it from the normal flow.</li><li><code>position: absolute;</code> - The element is positioned relative to its nearest positioned ancestor, and is removed from the normal flow.</li></ul><p>The <strong>float property</strong> is used to define how an element should float within its parent element. It can have values such as left, right, none, etc. The float property is used to create layouts where elements need to be placed side by side.</p><ul><li><code>float: left;</code> - The element floats to the left of its parent element.</li><li><code>float: right;</code> - The element floats to the right of its parent element.</li></ul><p>The <strong>clear property</strong> is used to define how an element should behave when it encounters a floated element. It can have values such as left, right, both, none, etc. The clear property is used to control the position of an element in relation to floated elements.</p><ul><li><code>clear: left;</code> - The element is moved below any floated elements on the left.</li><li><code>clear: right;</code> - The element is moved below any floated elements on the right.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, the display, position, float, and clear properties are essential in creating complex layouts and positioning elements in a web page. Understanding these properties will help you to create visually appealing and user-friendly websites.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(145, 136, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Web Design</h3><p>Responsive web design is an approach to web design that allows websites to adapt to different screen sizes and devices. This means that a website will look good and function well on a variety of devices, from small smartphones to large desktop monitors.</p><p>In this section, we will cover the basics of responsive web design, including media queries and flexible grids. We will also discuss how to use these techniques to create a responsive website.</p><h3>Responsive Web Design Basics</h3><p>A responsive website is one that uses a combination of flexible grids, images, and media queries to adapt to different screen sizes and devices. The key to creating a responsive website is to use relative units, such as percentages or ems, instead of absolute units, such as pixels.</p><ul><li><strong>Flexible Grids</strong>: A flexible grid is a grid system that uses relative units to define the size of columns and rows. This allows the grid to adapt to different screen sizes and devices.</li><li><strong>Media Queries</strong>: Media queries are used to apply different styles to a website based on the screen size or device type. For example, you can use a media query to apply a different stylesheet to a website when it is viewed on a smartphone.</li><li><strong>Flexible Images</strong>: Flexible images are images that can be scaled up or down to fit different screen sizes and devices. This can be achieved using the <code>max-width</code> property and setting it to 100%.</li></ul><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are a powerful tool in responsive web design. They allow you to apply different styles to a website based on the screen size or device type. For example, you can use a media query to apply a different stylesheet to a website when it is viewed on a smartphone.</p><p>Here is an example of a media query: <code>@media only screen and (max-width: 768px) { /* styles */ }</code>. This media query will apply the styles to any screen with a maximum width of 768px.</p><h3>Flexible Grids</h3><p>A flexible grid is a grid system that uses relative units to define the size of columns and rows. This allows the grid to adapt to different screen sizes and devices. For example, you can use the <code>grid-template-columns</code> property to define the size of columns in a grid.</p><p>Here is an example of a flexible grid: <code>.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr); }</code>. This will create a grid with 12 columns, each taking up an equal amount of space.</p><p>In conclusion, responsive web design is an important aspect of web development. By using media queries, flexible grids, and flexible images, you can create a website that looks good and functions well on a variety of devices.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(146, 137, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: mobile-first design and accessibility</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=mobile-first+design+and+accessibility\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/mobile-first+design+and+accessibility\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/mobile-first+design+and+accessibility/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/mobile-first+design+and+accessibility/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=mobile-first+design+and+accessibility\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Mobile-First Design and Accessibility</h3><p>As we continue our journey in web development, it\'s essential to consider the importance of mobile-first design and accessibility in our websites. In this section, we will explore the principles of mobile-first design, accessibility guidelines, and the role of semantic HTML in creating inclusive and user-friendly websites.</p><h3>Mobile-First Design</h3><p>Mobile-first design is an approach to web development that involves designing for smaller screens first, and then working your way up to larger screens. This approach has become increasingly popular as more and more people access the internet through their mobile devices.</p><p>The benefits of mobile-first design include:<ul><li>Improved user experience on smaller screens</li><li>Increased focus on essential content and features</li><li>Easier to add features and content for larger screens</li></ul></p><p>To implement mobile-first design, you can use CSS media queries to apply different styles based on screen size. For example:<code>/* Mobile-first design */\n@media (min-width: 768px) {\n  /* styles for larger screens */\n}\n@media (max-width: 767px) {\n  /* styles for smaller screens */\n}</code></p><h3>Accessibility Guidelines</h3><p>Accessibility is a critical aspect of web development that involves making your website usable by people with disabilities. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG 2.1) provide a set of guidelines for making web content more accessible.</p><p>Some key accessibility guidelines include:<ul><li>Providing alternative text for images</li><li>Using semantic HTML to provide structure and meaning to content</li><li>Ensuring sufficient color contrast between text and background</li><li>Providing closed captions for audio and video content</li></ul></p><p>For example, you can use the <strong>alt</strong> attribute to provide alternative text for images:<code><img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"A description of the image\"></code></p><h3>Semantic HTML</h3><p>Semantic HTML is a way of writing HTML that provides meaning and structure to web content. By using semantic HTML elements, you can help screen readers and other assistive technologies understand the content and structure of your website.</p><p>Some examples of semantic HTML elements include:<ul><li><code>header</code>: defines the header section of a document or section</li><li><code>nav</code>: defines a section of navigation links</li><li><code>main</code>: defines the main content section of a document</li><li><code>section</code>: defines a self-contained section of related content</li><li><code>article</code>: defines an independent piece of content, such as a blog post or news article</li></ul></p><p>By using semantic HTML, you can create a more accessible and user-friendly website that provides a better experience for all users.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(147, 138, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css preprocessors and build tools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+preprocessors+and+build+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+preprocessors+and+build+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+preprocessors+and+build+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+preprocessors+and+build+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+preprocessors+and+build+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Preprocessors and Build Tools</h3><p>CSS preprocessors are tools that allow you to write CSS code in a more efficient and modular way. They provide features like variables, nesting, and functions that make it easier to manage complex CSS codebases.</p><p>Two popular CSS preprocessors are Sass and Less. Both of these preprocessors provide similar features, but they have some differences in their syntax and functionality.</p><h3>Sass</h3><p>Sass is one of the most popular CSS preprocessors. It provides features like variables, nesting, and functions that make it easier to write and manage CSS code.</p><p>For example, you can define a variable in Sass like this: <code>$primary-color: #333;</code> and then use it throughout your CSS code like this: <code>body { background-color: $primary-color; }</code></p><h3>Less</h3><p>Less is another popular CSS preprocessor. It provides features like variables, nesting, and functions that make it easier to write and manage CSS code.</p><p>For example, you can define a variable in Less like this: <code>@primary-color: #333;</code> and then use it throughout your CSS code like this: <code>body { background-color: @primary-color; }</code></p><h3>Build Tools</h3><p>Build tools are used to automate tasks like compiling CSS preprocessors, minifying code, and optimizing images. Two popular build tools are Gulp and Webpack.</p><p>Gulp is a task runner that allows you to automate tasks like compiling CSS preprocessors, minifying code, and optimizing images. It provides a simple and flexible way to manage your build process.</p><p>Webpack is a module bundler that allows you to bundle your code into a single file. It provides features like code splitting, tree shaking, and minification that make it easier to optimize your code for production.</p><p>For example, you can use Gulp to compile your Sass code like this: <code>gulp.src(\'sass/**/*.scss\').pipe(sass()).pipe(gulp.dest(\'css\'));</code> and you can use Webpack to bundle your code like this: <code>module.exports = { entry: \'./index.js\', output: { path: \'./dist\', filename: \'bundle.js\' } };</code></p><ul><li>CSS preprocessors provide features like variables, nesting, and functions that make it easier to manage complex CSS codebases.</li><li>Sass and Less are two popular CSS preprocessors that provide similar features.</li><li>Build tools like Gulp and Webpack are used to automate tasks like compiling CSS preprocessors, minifying code, and optimizing images.</li><li>Gulp is a task runner that provides a simple and flexible way to manage your build process.</li><li>Webpack is a module bundler that provides features like code splitting, tree shaking, and minification that make it easier to optimize your code for production.</li></ul><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>CSS preprocessors make it easier to manage complex CSS codebases.</li><li>Sass and Less are two popular CSS preprocessors.</li><li>Gulp and Webpack are two popular build tools.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
