(149, 139, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Frameworks</h3><p>CSS frameworks are pre-built sets of CSS classes and rules that can be used to style and layout web pages quickly and efficiently. They provide a set of pre-designed UI components, such as buttons, forms, and navigation menus, that can be easily customized and combined to create a unique and consistent design.</p><p>In this lesson, we will introduce three popular CSS frameworks: Bootstrap, Tailwind CSS, and Materialize CSS. Each framework has its own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of which one to use will depend on the specific needs of your project.</p><h3>Bootstrap</h3><p>Bootstrap is one of the most popular CSS frameworks, developed by Twitter. It provides a comprehensive set of pre-built UI components, including buttons, forms, navigation menus, and more. Bootstrap is known for its ease of use and flexibility, making it a great choice for beginners and experienced developers alike.</p><ul><li>Pros: easy to use, flexible, and customizable</li><li>Cons: can be bloated, and some components may not be suitable for all projects</li></ul><p>Example of using Bootstrap: <code>&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/css/bootstrap.min.css&quot;&gt;</code></p><h3>Tailwind CSS</h3><p>Tailwind CSS is a utility-first CSS framework that allows you to write custom CSS classes using a set of pre-defined utility classes. It provides a lot of flexibility and customization options, making it a great choice for projects that require a high degree of customization.</p><ul><li>Pros: highly customizable, and provides a lot of flexibility</li><li>Cons: can be overwhelming for beginners, and requires a good understanding of CSS</li></ul><p>Example of using Tailwind CSS: <code>&lt;div class=&quot;flex justify-center items-center h-screen&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></p><h3>Materialize CSS</h3><p>Materialize CSS is a CSS framework developed by Google, based on the Material Design principles. It provides a set of pre-built UI components, including buttons, forms, and navigation menus, that follow the Material Design guidelines.</p><ul><li>Pros: provides a consistent and beautiful design, and is easy to use</li><li>Cons: may not be suitable for projects that require a high degree of customization</li></ul><p>Example of using Materialize CSS: <code>&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/materialize/1.0.0/css/materialize.min.css&quot;&gt;</code></p><p>In conclusion, CSS frameworks can save you a lot of time and effort when building web pages. By choosing the right framework for your project, you can create a consistent and beautiful design, and focus on writing custom CSS code only when necessary. <strong>Remember to always check the documentation and examples for each framework to get the most out of them.</strong></p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(150, 140, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building Responsive Websites</h3><p>Responsive web design is an approach to web design that makes web pages render well on a variety of devices and window or screen sizes. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of building responsive websites, including mobile-first design, responsive images, and media queries.</p><h3>Mobile-First Design</h3><p>Mobile-first design is an approach to web design where we design for small screens first, and then work our way up to larger screens. This approach has several advantages, including <strong>faster page loads</strong> and <strong>improved user experience</strong> on mobile devices.</p><p>To implement mobile-first design, we start by designing the layout and content for small screens, and then use media queries to add styles and layout adjustments for larger screens. For example, we can use the following code to set the font size for mobile devices: <code>body { font-size: 16px; }</code></p><h3>Responsive Images</h3><p>Responsive images are images that adjust their size and resolution based on the screen size and device. We can use the <code>img</code> tag with the <code>srcset</code> attribute to specify multiple image sources for different screen sizes. For example: <code><img src=\'image.jpg\' srcset=\'image-small.jpg 480w, image-medium.jpg 800w, image-large.jpg 1200w\'></code></p><p>We can also use the <code>pictures</code> tag to specify multiple image sources and provide a fallback for older browsers. For example: <code><picture><source media=\'(max-width: 600px)\' srcset=\'image-small.jpg\'><source media=\'(max-width: 800px)\' srcset=\'image-medium.jpg\'><img src=\'image-large.jpg\'></picture></code></p><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are used to apply different styles based on different conditions, such as screen size, device type, and orientation. We can use the <code>@media</code> rule to define a media query, and specify the styles that should be applied when the condition is met.</p><p>For example, we can use the following code to apply a different layout for screens with a maximum width of 600px: <code>@media (max-width: 600px) { body { font-size: 14px; } }</code></p><p>We can also use media queries to apply different styles based on the device type, such as desktop or mobile. For example: <code>@media (max-width: 800px) and (orientation: portrait) { body { font-size: 16px; } }</code></p><p>Some common media query conditions include: <ul><li><code>(max-width: 600px)</code>: applies to screens with a maximum width of 600px</li><li><code>(min-width: 800px)</code>: applies to screens with a minimum width of 800px</li><li><code>(orientation: portrait)</code>: applies to devices in portrait orientation</li><li><code>(orientation: landscape)</code>: applies to devices in landscape orientation</li></ul></p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(151, 141, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css preprocessors</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+preprocessors/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+preprocessors/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Preprocessors</h3><p>CSS preprocessors are tools that allow you to write CSS code in a more efficient and modular way. They provide features such as variables, nesting, and functions, which are not available in traditional CSS. In this lesson, we will explore three popular CSS preprocessors: Sass, Less, and PostCSS.</p><h3>Sass</h3><p>Sass (Syntactically Awesome StyleSheets) is one of the most popular CSS preprocessors. It was created by Hampton Catlin and is now maintained by the Sass team. Sass provides a lot of features that make writing CSS code easier and more efficient. Some of the key features of Sass include:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: You can define variables in Sass and use them throughout your code. For example: <code>$color: #fff;</code></li><li><strong>Nesting</strong>: You can nest selectors in Sass, which makes your code more readable and easier to maintain. For example: <code>.nav { ul { list-style: none; } }</code></li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: You can define functions in Sass and use them to perform complex calculations. For example: <code>$font-size: 16px; @function calculate-line-height($font-size) { @return $font-size * 1.5; }</code></li></ul><h3>Less</h3><p>Less (Leaner Style Sheets) is another popular CSS preprocessor. It was created by Alexis Sellier and is now maintained by the Less team. Less provides many of the same features as Sass, including variables, nesting, and functions. Some of the key features of Less include:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: You can define variables in Less and use them throughout your code. For example: <code>@color: #fff;</code></li><li><strong>Nesting</strong>: You can nest selectors in Less, which makes your code more readable and easier to maintain. For example: <code>.nav { ul { list-style: none; } }</code></li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: You can define functions in Less and use them to perform complex calculations. For example: <code>@font-size: 16px; .calculate-line-height(@font-size) { line-height: @font-size * 1.5; }</code></li></ul><h3>PostCSS</h3><p>PostCSS is a different kind of CSS preprocessor. It\'s not a language, but rather a tool that allows you to use future CSS features today. PostCSS provides a lot of features that are not available in traditional CSS, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Autoprefixer</strong>: PostCSS can automatically add vendor prefixes to your CSS code, so you don\'t have to worry about compatibility issues.</li><li><strong>Future CSS features</strong>: PostCSS allows you to use future CSS features, such as CSS variables and custom properties, today.</li><li><strong>Plugins</strong>: PostCSS has a large ecosystem of plugins that can be used to perform a wide range of tasks, from simple tasks like minification and compression to complex tasks like image optimization and caching.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, CSS preprocessors are powerful tools that can help you write more efficient and modular CSS code. Sass, Less, and PostCSS are three popular preprocessors that provide a lot of features and benefits. By using a CSS preprocessor, you can write better CSS code and improve your workflow.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(152, 142, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css components web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+components+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+components+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+components+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+components+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+components+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Creating Reusable CSS Components</h3><p>Welcome to Day 25 of our web development course, where we will explore the concept of creating reusable CSS components. Reusable components are essential in web development as they help reduce code duplication, improve maintainability, and enhance the overall efficiency of our workflow.</p><h3>CSS Modules</h3><p>CSS modules are a popular approach to creating reusable CSS components. They allow us to write CSS code that is scoped to a specific component, making it easier to manage and reuse styles across our application. To use CSS modules, we need to create a new CSS file with a <code>.module.css</code> extension. For example, if we have a <code>button.css</code> file, we would rename it to <code>button.module.css</code>.</p><p>Here\'s an example of how to use CSS modules in our React component: <code>import styles from \'./button.module.css\';</code> and then apply the styles to our component using the <code>className</code> attribute: <code>&lt;button className={styles.button}&gt;Click me!&lt;/button&gt;</code></p><h3>BEM Methodology</h3><p>BEM (Block, Element, Modifier) is a naming convention for CSS classes that helps us create reusable and maintainable components. The BEM methodology consists of three parts: <strong>Block</strong>, <strong>Element</strong>, and <strong>Modifier</strong>. The block represents the component itself, the element represents a part of the component, and the modifier represents a variation of the component.</p><ul><li><strong>Block</strong>: The block represents the component itself, for example, <code>header</code> or <code>footer</code>.</li><li><strong>Element</strong>: The element represents a part of the component, for example, <code>header__nav</code> or <code>footer__copyright</code>.</li><li><strong>Modifier</strong>: The modifier represents a variation of the component, for example, <code>header--dark</code> or <code>footer--light</code>.</li></ul><p>Using the BEM methodology, we can create reusable components with a clear and consistent naming convention.</p><h3>OOCSS</h3><p>OOCSS (Object-Oriented CSS) is a methodology for writing CSS that focuses on creating reusable and modular components. OOCSS is based on the principles of object-oriented programming, where we create objects that can be reused and combined to create more complex components.</p><p>The key principles of OOCSS are: <strong>separation of structure and skin</strong>, <strong>separation of container and content</strong>, and <strong>using a strict naming convention</strong>. By following these principles, we can create reusable and maintainable CSS components that can be easily combined to create complex layouts.</p><p>In conclusion, creating reusable CSS components is an essential skill for any web developer. By using CSS modules, BEM methodology, and OOCSS, we can create efficient, maintainable, and scalable CSS code that improves our workflow and reduces code duplication.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(153, 143, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css layouts tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+layouts+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+layouts+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+layouts+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+layouts+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+layouts+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding CSS Layouts</h3><p>CSS layouts are used to control the positioning and arrangement of elements on a web page. In this lesson, we will cover three important CSS layout techniques: Flexbox, Grid, and Floats. These techniques will help you create responsive and visually appealing web pages.</p><p><strong>Flexbox</strong> is a one-dimensional layout system that allows you to easily align and distribute elements within a container. It is useful for creating navigation bars, menus, and other types of lists. To use Flexbox, you need to set the <code>display</code> property of the container element to <code>flex</code> or <code>inline-flex</code>.</p><ul><li>The <code>flex-direction</code> property is used to specify the direction of the flexible items.</li><li>The <code>justify-content</code> property is used to specify the alignment of the flexible items.</li><li>The <code>align-items</code> property is used to specify the vertical alignment of the flexible items.</li></ul><p><strong>Grid</strong> is a two-dimensional layout system that allows you to create complex and responsive layouts. It is useful for creating image galleries, dashboards, and other types of grid-based layouts. To use Grid, you need to set the <code>display</code> property of the container element to <code>grid</code> or <code>inline-grid</code>.</p><ul><li>The <code>grid-template-columns</code> property is used to specify the number and width of the grid columns.</li><li>The <code>grid-template-rows</code> property is used to specify the number and height of the grid rows.</li><li>The <code>grid-gap</code> property is used to specify the gap between the grid cells.</li></ul><p><strong>Floats</strong> are used to create layouts where elements are floated to the left or right of their parent container. They are useful for creating simple layouts, such as wrapping text around an image. To use Floats, you need to set the <code>float</code> property of the element to <code>left</code> or <code>right</code>.</p><ul><li>The <code>clear</code> property is used to specify which side of the element should be cleared of floated elements.</li><li>The <code>overflow</code> property is used to specify how to handle overflow content.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, Flexbox, Grid, and Floats are three important CSS layout techniques that will help you create responsive and visually appealing web pages. By mastering these techniques, you will be able to create complex and professional-looking layouts with ease.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56');
