(154, 144, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css animations and transitions and gradients</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+animations+and+transitions+and+gradients\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+animations+and+transitions+and+gradients\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+animations+and+transitions+and+gradients/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+animations+and+transitions+and+gradients/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+animations+and+transitions+and+gradients\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Advanced CSS Techniques</h3><p>Welcome to Day 27 of our web development course, where we will be exploring advanced CSS techniques. These techniques will help you create visually appealing and interactive web pages. In this lesson, we will cover CSS animations, CSS transitions, and CSS gradients.</p><h3>CSS Animations</h3><p>CSS animations allow you to create animations using CSS. You can use the <code>@keyframes</code> rule to define the animation and the <code>animation</code> property to apply it to an element. For example:</p><ul><li>The <code>@keyframes</code> rule is used to define the animation. You can specify the animation name, duration, and other properties.</li><li>The <code>animation</code> property is used to apply the animation to an element. You can specify the animation name, duration, timing function, and other properties.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple CSS animation:</p><code>/* Define the animation */<br>@keyframes example {<br>  0% { background-color: red; }<br>  100% { background-color: yellow; }<br>}<br><br>/* Apply the animation to an element */<br>.example {<br>  animation: example 5s;<br>}</code><h3>CSS Transitions</h3><p>CSS transitions allow you to create smooth transitions between different states of an element. You can use the <code>transition</code> property to specify the properties that you want to transition and the duration of the transition. For example:</p><ul><li>The <code>transition</code> property is used to specify the properties that you want to transition and the duration of the transition.</li><li>You can use the <code>:hover</code> pseudo-class to create a hover effect.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple CSS transition:</p><code>/* Apply the transition to an element */<br>.example {<br>  background-color: red;<br>  transition: background-color 2s;<br>}<br><br>/* Create a hover effect */<br>.example:hover {<br>  background-color: yellow;<br>}</code><h3>CSS Gradients</h3><p>CSS gradients allow you to create gradient effects using CSS. You can use the <code>linear-gradient</code> or <code>radial-gradient</code> function to create a gradient. For example:</p><ul><li>The <code>linear-gradient</code> function is used to create a linear gradient.</li><li>The <code>radial-gradient</code> function is used to create a radial gradient.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple CSS gradient:</p><code>/* Create a linear gradient */<br>.example {<br>  background-image: linear-gradient(red, yellow);<br>}<br><br>/* Create a radial gradient */<br>.example {<br>  background-image: radial-gradient(red, yellow);<br>}</code><p>In conclusion, advanced CSS techniques such as CSS animations, CSS transitions, and CSS gradients can help you create visually appealing and interactive web pages. With practice and experience, you can use these techniques to create complex and engaging web pages.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>CSS animations allow you to create animations using CSS.</li><li>CSS transitions allow you to create smooth transitions between different states of an element.</li><li>CSS gradients allow you to create gradient effects using CSS.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(155, 145, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css optimization techniques</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+optimization+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+optimization+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+optimization+techniques/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+optimization+techniques/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+optimization+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Best Practices and Optimization</h3><p>CSS optimization is crucial for improving the performance and maintainability of web applications. In this section, we will discuss the best practices for writing efficient CSS code.</p><p>One of the key aspects of CSS optimization is to follow a set of coding standards. This includes using a consistent naming convention, organizing CSS rules in a logical order, and using comments to explain complex code.</p><ul><li>Use a preprocessor like Sass or Less to write more efficient and modular CSS code.</li><li>Use a CSS linter to check for errors and inconsistencies in the code.</li><li>Use a consistent naming convention, such as BEM or OOCSS, to make the code more readable and maintainable.</li></ul><p>Another important aspect of CSS optimization is browser compatibility. Different browsers have different rendering engines and may interpret CSS rules differently. To ensure cross-browser compatibility, it\'s essential to test the code in different browsers and use vendor prefixes where necessary.</p><p>For example, to apply a CSS rule that works in both Chrome and Firefox, you can use the following code: <code>.class { -webkit-transform: translateX(10px); -moz-transform: translateX(10px); -ms-transform: translateX(10px); -o-transform: translateX(10px); transform: translateX(10px); }</code></p><p><strong>Best Practices for CSS Optimization:</strong></p><ul><li>Minify and compress CSS files to reduce the file size and improve page load times.</li><li>Use CSS sprites to reduce the number of HTTP requests and improve page load times.</li><li>Avoid using <code>!important</code> as it can make the code harder to maintain and debug.</li><li>Use a CSS framework like Bootstrap or Foundation to speed up development and improve consistency.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices and optimization techniques, you can write more efficient and maintainable CSS code that works across different browsers and devices.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(156, 145, 'quiz', '31', 2, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(157, 146, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Frameworks</h3><p>CSS frameworks are pre-built sets of CSS classes and rules that can be used to style and layout web pages quickly and efficiently. They provide a set of pre-defined classes and rules that can be used to create responsive, mobile-first, and consistent designs. In this lesson, we will introduce three popular CSS frameworks: Bootstrap, Tailwind CSS, and Materialize CSS.</p><h3>Bootstrap</h3><p>Bootstrap is one of the most popular CSS frameworks used for building responsive and mobile-first web applications. It was created by Twitter and is now maintained by the Bootstrap team. Bootstrap provides a wide range of pre-built CSS classes and rules that can be used to create responsive layouts, navigation bars, alerts, and more.</p><p>Some of the key features of Bootstrap include:</p><ul><li><strong>Responsive Grid System</strong>: Bootstrap provides a responsive grid system that allows you to create layouts that adapt to different screen sizes.</li><li><strong>Pre-built Components</strong>: Bootstrap provides a wide range of pre-built components such as navigation bars, alerts, and modals that can be easily customized.</li><li><strong>Easy to Use</strong>: Bootstrap is easy to use and provides a simple and intuitive syntax for creating responsive layouts.</li></ul><p>Example of using Bootstrap to create a navigation bar:</p><code>&lt;nav class=&quot;navbar navbar-expand-lg navbar-light bg-light&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;div class=&quot;container-fluid&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;a class=&quot;navbar-brand&quot; href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Brand Name&lt;/a&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;button class=&quot;navbar-toggler&quot; type=&quot;button&quot; data-bs-toggle=&quot;collapse&quot; data-bs-target=&quot;#navbarSupportedContent&quot; aria-controls=&quot;navbarSupportedContent&quot; aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot; aria-label=&quot;Toggle navigation&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;span class=&quot;navbar-toggler-icon&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/button&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;div class=&quot;collapse navbar-collapse&quot; id=&quot;navbarSupportedContent&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;ul class=&quot;navbar-nav me-auto mb-2 mb-lg-0&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;li class=&quot;nav-item&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;a class=&quot;nav-link active&quot; aria-current=&quot;page&quot; href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/ul&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;/nav&gt;</code><h3>Tailwind CSS</h3><p>Tailwind CSS is a utility-first CSS framework that allows you to create custom designs without leaving your HTML. It was created by Adam Wathan and is now maintained by the Tailwind Labs team. Tailwind CSS provides a wide range of pre-built utility classes that can be used to create custom designs.</p><p>Some of the key features of Tailwind CSS include:</p><ul><li><strong>Utility-First Approach</strong>: Tailwind CSS provides a utility-first approach that allows you to create custom designs without leaving your HTML.</li><li><strong>Highly Customizable</strong>: Tailwind CSS is highly customizable and allows you to create custom designs that fit your needs.</li><li><strong>Fast and Efficient</strong>: Tailwind CSS is fast and efficient and allows you to create custom designs quickly.</li></ul><p>Example of using Tailwind CSS to create a button:</p><code>&lt;button class=&quot;bg-blue-500 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-bold py-2 px-4 rounded&quot;&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;</code><h3>Materialize CSS</h3><p>Materialize CSS is a CSS framework based on Google\'s Material Design. It was created by the Materialize team and is now maintained by the community. Materialize CSS provides a wide range of pre-built CSS classes and rules that can be used to create material design-inspired layouts and components.</p><p>Some of the key features of Materialize CSS include:</p><ul><li><strong>Material Design Inspired</strong>: Materialize CSS provides a material design-inspired look and feel that is consistent with Google\'s Material Design guidelines.</li><li><strong>Pre-built Components</strong>: Materialize CSS provides a wide range of pre-built components such as navigation bars, alerts, and modals that can be easily customized.</li><li><strong>Easy to Use</strong>: Materialize CSS is easy to use and provides a simple and intuitive syntax for creating material design-inspired layouts.</li></ul><p>Example of using Materialize CSS to create a navigation bar:</p><code>&lt;nav&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;div class=&quot;nav-wrapper&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot; class=&quot;brand-logo&quot;&gt;Brand Name&lt;/a&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;ul id=&quot;nav-mobile&quot; class=&quot;right hide-on-med-and-down&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/ul&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;/nav&gt;</code><p>In conclusion, CSS frameworks are powerful tools that can be used to create responsive, mobile-first, and consistent designs quickly and efficiently. Bootstrap, Tailwind CSS, and Materialize CSS are three popular CSS frameworks that provide a wide range of pre-built CSS classes and rules that can be used to create custom designs. By understanding how to use these frameworks, you can create high-quality web applications that meet the needs of your users.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(158, 147, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design tutorials</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+tutorials\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+tutorials\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+tutorials/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+tutorials/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+tutorials\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building Responsive Layouts</h3><p>Building responsive layouts is an essential skill for any web developer. It allows you to create websites that adapt to different screen sizes and devices, providing a better user experience. In this lesson, we will cover three key concepts: Grid System, Flexbox, and Media Queries.</p><h3>Grid System</h3><p>A grid system is a structure that consists of rows and columns, used to organize content on a web page. It helps to create a consistent and harmonious design. To create a grid system, you can use CSS Grid, which is a powerful tool for building two-dimensional grids.</p><ul><li><strong>Grid Container</strong>: The grid container is the element that contains the grid items. You can define it using the <code>display: grid;</code> property.</li><li><strong>Grid Items</strong>: The grid items are the elements that are placed inside the grid container. You can define them using the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties.</li><li><strong>Grid Tracks</strong>: The grid tracks are the rows and columns of the grid. You can define them using the <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> properties.</li></ul><h3>Flexbox</h3><p>Flexbox is a layout mode that allows you to create flexible and responsive layouts. It is particularly useful for creating navigation bars, footers, and other elements that need to adapt to different screen sizes.</p><ul><li><strong>Flex Container</strong>: The flex container is the element that contains the flex items. You can define it using the <code>display: flex;</code> property.</li><li><strong>Flex Items</strong>: The flex items are the elements that are placed inside the flex container. You can define them using the <code>flex-basis</code> and <code>flex-grow</code> properties.</li><li><strong>Flex Direction</strong>: The flex direction determines the direction of the flex items. You can define it using the <code>flex-direction</code> property.</li></ul><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are used to apply different styles based on different conditions, such as screen size or device type. They are essential for creating responsive layouts that adapt to different devices and screen sizes.</p><ul><li><strong>Media Query Syntax</strong>: The media query syntax consists of the <code>@media</code> keyword, followed by a condition, and then a block of styles.</li><li><strong>Breakpoints</strong>: Breakpoints are the points at which the layout changes. You can define them using media queries, for example, <code>@media (max-width: 768px)</code> for a tablet screen size.</li><li><strong>Mobile-First Approach</strong>: The mobile-first approach involves designing for small screens first, and then adding more styles and layout for larger screens. This approach is recommended for creating responsive layouts.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, building responsive layouts requires a good understanding of grid systems, flexbox, and media queries. By using these concepts, you can create websites that adapt to different screen sizes and devices, providing a better user experience.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(159, 148, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css preprocessors</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+preprocessors/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+preprocessors/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Preprocessors</h3><p>CSS preprocessors are tools that allow you to write CSS code in a more efficient and modular way. They provide features like variables, nesting, and functions, which are not available in traditional CSS. In this lesson, we will explore three popular CSS preprocessors: Sass, Less, and Stylus.</p><h3>Sass</h3><p>Sass (Syntactically Awesome StyleSheets) is one of the most popular CSS preprocessors. It was created by Hampton Catlin and is now maintained by the Sass team. Sass provides a lot of features like variables, nesting, and functions, which make it a powerful tool for writing CSS code.</p><p>Some of the key features of Sass include:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: You can define variables in Sass using the <code>$</code> symbol. For example: <code>$primary-color: #333;</code></li><li><strong>Nesting</strong>: You can nest selectors in Sass, which makes it easier to write CSS code. For example: <code>.header { background-color: $primary-color; .nav { color: #fff; } }</code></li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: You can define functions in Sass using the <code>@function</code> directive. For example: <code>@function add($a, $b) { @return $a + $b; }</code></li></ul><h3>Less</h3><p>Less (Leaner Style Sheets) is another popular CSS preprocessor. It was created by Alexis Sellier and is now maintained by the Less team. Less provides a lot of features like variables, nesting, and functions, which are similar to Sass.</p><p>Some of the key features of Less include:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: You can define variables in Less using the <code>@</code> symbol. For example: <code>@primary-color: #333;</code></li><li><strong>Nesting</strong>: You can nest selectors in Less, which makes it easier to write CSS code. For example: <code>.header { background-color: @primary-color; .nav { color: #fff; } }</code></li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: You can define functions in Less using the <code>@function</code> directive. For example: <code>@add: ~\"@{a} + @{b}\";</code></li></ul><h3>Stylus</h3><p>Stylus is a newer CSS preprocessor that provides a lot of features like variables, nesting, and functions. It was created by TJ Holowaychuk and is now maintained by the Stylus team.</p><p>Some of the key features of Stylus include:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: You can define variables in Stylus using the <code>$</code> symbol or the <code>=</code> symbol. For example: <code>$primary-color = #333;</code> or <code>primary-color = #333;</code></li><li><strong>Nesting</strong>: You can nest selectors in Stylus, which makes it easier to write CSS code. For example: <code>.header { background-color: $primary-color; .nav { color: #fff; } }</code></li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: You can define functions in Stylus using the <code>function</code> keyword. For example: <code>add(a, b) { return a + b; }</code></li></ul><p>In conclusion, CSS preprocessors like Sass, Less, and Stylus provide a lot of features that make it easier to write CSS code. They provide variables, nesting, and functions, which are not available in traditional CSS. By using a CSS preprocessor, you can write more efficient and modular CSS code.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(160, 149, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development components</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Creating Reusable UI Components</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the concept of creating reusable UI components, which is a crucial aspect of modern web development. Reusable components enable developers to write efficient, modular, and maintainable code. We will delve into three key topics: Component-Based Architecture, CSS Modules, and Web Components.</p><h3>Component-Based Architecture</h3><p>Component-Based Architecture is a design pattern that emphasizes the creation of self-contained, reusable components. Each component represents a small piece of the user interface and can be easily combined to form more complex UI elements. This approach has several benefits, including improved code organization, reduced duplication, and enhanced maintainability.</p><ul><li><strong>Advantages of Component-Based Architecture:</strong></li><li>Improved code reusability</li><li>Reduced code duplication</li><li>Easier maintenance and updates</li><li>Enhanced scalability</li></ul><h3>CSS Modules</h3><p>CSS Modules are a popular approach to styling components in a modular and reusable way. They allow developers to write CSS code that is scoped to a specific component, eliminating the risk of global CSS conflicts. CSS Modules are particularly useful when working with Component-Based Architecture, as they enable developers to style each component independently.</p><p>To use CSS Modules, you need to create a CSS file with a <code>.module.css</code> extension. For example:</p><code>.component {<br>  background-color: #f2f2f2;<br>  padding: 20px;<br>}</code><p>Then, in your JavaScript file, you can import the CSS module and use it to style your component:</p><code>import styles from \'./component.module.css\';<br><br>function Component() {<br>  return <div className={styles.component}>Component content</div>;<br>}</code><h3>Web Components</h3><p>Web Components are a set of web platform APIs that allow developers to create custom, reusable HTML elements. They provide a way to extend the HTML vocabulary and create new, custom elements that can be used in web pages. Web Components consist of three main technologies: Custom Elements, HTML Templates, and HTML Imports.</p><ul><li><strong>Web Components Technologies:</strong></li><li>Custom Elements: allow developers to create custom HTML elements</li><li>HTML Templates: provide a way to define reusable HTML templates</li><li>HTML Imports: enable developers to import HTML documents into other HTML documents</li></ul><p>By combining Component-Based Architecture, CSS Modules, and Web Components, developers can create robust, maintainable, and scalable web applications with reusable UI components.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(161, 150, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: accessibility features in web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=accessibility+features+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/accessibility+features+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/accessibility+features+in+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/accessibility+features+in+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=accessibility+features+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Implementing Accessibility Features</h3><p>As a web developer, it\'s essential to create websites that are accessible to everyone, including people with disabilities. In this lesson, we\'ll cover the basics of semantic HTML, ARIA attributes, and screen readers. By the end of this lesson, you\'ll have a solid understanding of how to implement accessibility features in your web applications.</p><h3>Semantic HTML</h3><p>Semantic HTML is the use of HTML elements to convey meaning about the structure of a web page, rather than just its presentation. This is important for accessibility because screen readers and other assistive technologies rely on semantic HTML to interpret the content of a web page. Some examples of semantic HTML elements include <code>header</code>, <code>nav</code>, <code>main</code>, <code>section</code>, <code>article</code>, <code>aside</code>, and <code>footer</code>.</p><p>For example, instead of using a <code>div</code> element to create a header, you should use the <code>header</code> element: <code>&lt;header&gt;My Website&lt;/header&gt;</code>. This tells screen readers and other assistive technologies that this element is a header, and provides a clear structure to the web page.</p><h3>ARIA Attributes</h3><p>ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) attributes are used to provide additional information about the structure and functionality of a web page to assistive technologies. They are especially useful for dynamic web applications, where the content and structure of the page may change frequently.</p><p>Some common ARIA attributes include <code>role</code>, <code>aria-label</code>, and <code>aria-describedby</code>. For example, you can use the <code>role</code> attribute to indicate that a <code>div</code> element is a button: <code>&lt;div role=&quot;button&quot;&gt;Click me!&lt;/div&gt;</code>. You can also use the <code>aria-label</code> attribute to provide a text description of an element: <code>&lt;input type=&quot;search&quot; aria-label=&quot;Search input&quot;&gt;</code>.</p><h3>Screen Readers</h3><p>Screen readers are software applications that read the content of a web page out loud to the user. They are commonly used by people who are blind or have low vision. Some popular screen readers include JAWS, NVDA, and VoiceOver.</p><p>When using a screen reader, the user can navigate the web page using the keyboard, and the screen reader will read out the content of each element as they focus on it. For example, if the user focuses on a <code>header</code> element, the screen reader will read out the text content of the element, as well as its role (e.g. &quot;header&quot;).</p><p>To test your web page with a screen reader, you can use the following steps:<ul><li>Download and install a screen reader (e.g. NVDA)</li><li>Launch the screen reader and navigate to your web page</li><li>Use the keyboard to navigate the web page, and listen to the screen reader read out the content of each element</li><li>Test that all interactive elements (e.g. buttons, links) are accessible and usable with the keyboard</li></ul></p><p>By following these steps and using semantic HTML, ARIA attributes, and testing with screen readers, you can create web pages that are accessible to everyone, including people with disabilities. Remember to <strong>always test your web pages with a screen reader</strong> to ensure that they are accessible and usable.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(162, 151, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css code organization best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+code+organization+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+code+organization+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+code+organization+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+code+organization+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+code+organization+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Best Practices for CSS Code Organization</h3><p>As a web developer, writing clean and organized CSS code is crucial for maintaining and scaling your projects. In this lesson, we will cover the best practices for CSS code organization, including CSS linting, code formatting, and modular CSS.</p><h3>CSS Linting</h3><p>CSS linting is the process of checking your CSS code for errors and warnings. It helps you catch mistakes and improve the quality of your code. There are several CSS linters available, including <strong>StyleLint</strong> and <strong>CSSLint</strong>. These tools can be integrated into your code editor or build process to provide real-time feedback on your code.</p><p>Some common CSS linting rules include:</p><ul><li>Checking for invalid selectors and properties</li><li>Enforcing consistent naming conventions and code formatting</li><li>Detecting duplicate rules and properties</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following <code>stylelint.config.js</code> file to configure StyleLint:</p><code>module.exports = {<br>  \'rules\': {<br>    \'color-function-notation\': \'legacy\',<br>    \'max-nesting-depth\': 3,<br>  },<br>};</code><h3>Code Formatting</h3><p>Code formatting is an essential aspect of CSS code organization. It involves using consistent indentation, spacing, and line breaks to make your code easy to read and understand. There are several code formatting tools available, including <strong>Prettier</strong> and <strong>Beautify</strong>.</p><p>Some best practices for code formatting include:</p><ul><li>Using a consistent number of spaces for indentation (e.g., 2 or 4 spaces)</li><li>Adding line breaks after each selector and property</li><li>Using a consistent order for properties (e.g., alphabetical order)</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following <code>prettierrc.json</code> file to configure Prettier:</p><code>{<br>  \'printWidth\': 120,<br>  \'tabWidth\': 2,<br>  \'useTabs\': false,<br>  \'semi\': true,<br>  \'singleQuote\': true,<br>  \'trailingComma\': \'all\',<br>}</code><h3>Modular CSS</h3><p>Modular CSS involves breaking down your CSS code into smaller, independent modules. This approach makes it easier to maintain and reuse your code. There are several modular CSS frameworks available, including <strong>BEM</strong> and <strong>SMACSS</strong>.</p><p>Some best practices for modular CSS include:</p><ul><li>Using a consistent naming convention for classes and IDs</li><li>Breaking down complex components into smaller modules</li><li>Using a preprocessor like Sass or Less to write modular CSS</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following <code>button.css</code> file to define a modular button component:</p><code>.button {<br>  background-color: #4CAF50;<br>  color: #fff;<br>  padding: 10px 20px;<br>  border: none;<br>  border-radius: 5px;<br>  cursor: pointer;<br>}</code><p>In conclusion, following best practices for CSS code organization is essential for writing clean, maintainable, and scalable code. By using CSS linting, code formatting, and modular CSS, you can improve the quality of your code and make it easier to work with.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(163, 152, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css review and assessment</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+review+and+assessment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+review+and+assessment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+review+and+assessment/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+review+and+assessment/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+review+and+assessment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Review and Assessment of CSS Skills</h3><p>Welcome to Day 35 of our web development course. Today, we will review the CSS skills you have learned so far and assess your understanding through a quiz and project feedback.</p><p>Let\'s start by reviewing the key CSS concepts we have covered. These include <strong>selectors</strong>, <strong>properties</strong>, and <strong>values</strong>. We have also learned about different types of selectors, such as <code>element selectors</code>, <code>class selectors</code>, and <code>id selectors</code>.</p><p>Another important topic we covered is the <strong>CSS box model</strong>, which includes the <code>margin</code>, <code>border</code>, <code>padding</code>, and <code>content</code> areas of an element. We have also learned about <strong>CSS positioning</strong>, including <code>static</code>, <code>relative</code>, <code>absolute</code>, and <code>fixed</code> positioning.</p><ul><li>CSS selectors and properties</li><li>CSS box model and positioning</li><li>Working with colors, fonts, and text styles in CSS</li><li>Using CSS to create responsive and mobile-friendly designs</li></ul><p>Now, let\'s move on to the quiz. The quiz will test your understanding of the CSS concepts we have covered. Please choose the correct answer for each question.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
