(166, 154, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: backend project structure</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=backend+project+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/backend+project+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/backend+project+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/backend+project+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=backend+project+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Setting up a Backend Project</h3><p>Welcome to Day 37 of our web development course. Today, we will be covering the basics of setting up a backend project. This includes understanding the project structure, package management, and environment variables.</p><h3>Project Structure</h3><p>A well-organized project structure is essential for any backend project. This helps in maintaining the codebase and makes it easier for other developers to understand the project. A typical backend project structure includes the following directories:</p><ul><li><strong>app</strong>: This directory contains the application code, including routes, controllers, and models.</li><li><strong>config</strong>: This directory contains configuration files, such as database connections and environment variables.</li><li><strong>public</strong>: This directory contains static assets, such as images, CSS, and JavaScript files.</li><li><strong>routes</strong>: This directory contains route definitions for the application.</li></ul><p>For example, a simple backend project structure using Node.js and Express.js might look like this:</p><code>{\n  app/\n    controllers/\n    models/\n    routes/\n  config/\n    database.js\n    environment.js\n  public/\n    images/\n    css/\n    js/\n  routes/\n    user.js\n    product.js\n}</code><h3>Package Management</h3><p>Package management is an essential part of any backend project. It helps in managing dependencies and libraries used in the project. The most popular package manager for Node.js is npm (Node Package Manager). To manage packages, you can use the following commands:</p><ul><li><code>npm init</code>: Initializes a new npm project.</li><li><code>npm install</code>: Installs dependencies specified in the package.json file.</li><li><code>npm install &lt;package&gt;</code>: Installs a specific package.</li></ul><p>For example, to install Express.js, you can use the following command:</p><code>npm install express</code><h3>Environment Variables</h3><p>Environment variables are used to store sensitive information, such as database connections and API keys. They can be set in a .env file or in the operating system. To use environment variables in a Node.js project, you can use the dotenv package.</p><p>For example, to set environment variables in a .env file, you can add the following lines:</p><code>DB_HOST=localhost\nDB_USER=root\nDB_PASSWORD=password</code><p>Then, in your Node.js code, you can access these variables using the following syntax:</p><code>const dbHost = process.env.DB_HOST;\nconst dbUser = process.env.DB_USER;\nconst dbPassword = process.env.DB_PASSWORD;</code><p>In conclusion, setting up a backend project requires a well-organized project structure, package management, and environment variables. By following these best practices, you can create a maintainable and scalable backend project.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(167, 155, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: RESTful API diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=RESTful+API+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/RESTful+API+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/RESTful+API+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/RESTful+API+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=RESTful+API+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Creating RESTful APIs</h3><p>RESTful APIs, or Application Programming Interfaces, are a crucial part of web development. They allow different applications to communicate with each other and exchange data. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of creating RESTful APIs, including API endpoints, HTTP methods, and the request/response cycle.</p><h3>API Endpoints</h3><p>An API endpoint is a URL that an application uses to interact with an API. It is the point of entry for an application to access the API\'s functionality. API endpoints can be used to perform various actions, such as retrieving data, creating new data, updating existing data, or deleting data.</p><ul><li><strong>GET</strong>: Retrieve data from the API</li><li><strong>POST</strong>: Create new data in the API</li><li><strong>PUT</strong>: Update existing data in the API</li><li><strong>DELETE</strong>: Delete data from the API</li></ul><h3>HTTP Methods</h3><p>HTTP methods, also known as request methods, are used to indicate the action that the client wants to perform on the server. The most common HTTP methods are:</p><ul><li><code>GET</code>: Retrieve data from the server</li><li><code>POST</code>: Send data to the server to create a new resource</li><li><code>PUT</code>: Send data to the server to update an existing resource</li><li><code>DELETE</code>: Request the server to delete a resource</li></ul><p>For example, if we want to retrieve a list of users from the API, we would use the <code>GET</code> method and the API endpoint <code>/users</code>.</p><h3>Request/Response Cycle</h3><p>The request/response cycle is the process by which a client sends a request to the server and the server responds with the requested data. The cycle consists of the following steps:</p><ol><li>The client sends an HTTP request to the server with the requested API endpoint and HTTP method.</li><li>The server receives the request and processes it.</li><li>The server sends an HTTP response back to the client with the requested data.</li><li>The client receives the response and processes it.</li></ol><p>For example, if we want to retrieve a list of users from the API, the request/response cycle would be:</p><ul><li>The client sends a <code>GET</code> request to the API endpoint <code>/users</code>.</li><li>The server receives the request and retrieves the list of users from the database.</li><li>The server sends an HTTP response with the list of users back to the client.</li><li>The client receives the response and displays the list of users to the user.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(168, 156, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: database integration web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=database+integration+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/database+integration+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/database+integration+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/database+integration+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=database+integration+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Database Integration</h3><p>Database integration is a crucial aspect of web development, as it allows us to store and manage data efficiently. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of database modeling, SQL and NoSQL databases, and Object-Relational Mappers (ORMs).</p><h3>Database Modeling</h3><p>Database modeling is the process of creating a conceptual representation of the data structures and relationships within a database. It involves identifying the entities, attributes, and relationships between them. A well-designed database model is essential for ensuring data consistency, reducing data redundancy, and improving data integrity.</p><ul><li><strong>Entity-Relationship Diagrams (ERDs)</strong>: ERDs are a visual representation of the database model, showing the relationships between entities.</li><li><strong>Database Schema</strong>: A database schema is a blueprint of the database structure, including the relationships between tables.</li></ul><h3>SQL/NoSQL Databases</h3><p>There are two primary types of databases: SQL and NoSQL. SQL databases use a fixed schema and are ideal for complex transactions, while NoSQL databases use a dynamic schema and are suitable for big data and real-time web applications.</p><ul><li><strong>SQL Databases</strong>: Examples include MySQL, PostgreSQL, and Microsoft SQL Server. SQL databases use a fixed schema and support ACID transactions.</li><li><strong>NoSQL Databases</strong>: Examples include MongoDB, Cassandra, and Redis. NoSQL databases use a dynamic schema and support high scalability and flexibility.</li></ul><p>SQL databases use a query language called <code>SQL (Structured Query Language)</code> to manage and manipulate data. NoSQL databases use a variety of query languages, including <code>MongoDB\'s query language</code> and <code>Cassandra\'s CQL</code>.</p><h3>Object-Relational Mappers (ORMs)</h3><p>ORMs are libraries that simplify the interaction between web applications and databases. They provide a high-level interface for performing CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations and reduce the amount of boilerplate code.</p><ul><li><strong>Benefits of ORMs</strong>: ORMs provide a layer of abstraction, making it easier to switch between different databases. They also reduce the risk of SQL injection attacks and improve code readability.</li><li><strong>Popular ORMs</strong>: Examples include Hibernate, Entity Framework, and Django\'s ORM.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, database integration is a critical aspect of web development. By understanding database modeling, SQL and NoSQL databases, and ORMs, developers can create efficient and scalable web applications.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(169, 157, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: authentication and authorization in web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=authentication+and+authorization+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/authentication+and+authorization+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/authentication+and+authorization+in+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/authentication+and+authorization+in+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=authentication+and+authorization+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Authentication and Authorization</h3><p>Authentication and authorization are crucial components of web development, ensuring that only authorized users can access and interact with your application. In this lesson, we\'ll delve into the world of user authentication, role-based access control, and JSON web tokens.</p><h3>User Authentication</h3><p>User authentication is the process of verifying the identity of a user. This can be achieved through various methods, including username and password combinations, biometric authentication, and social media login integrations. When a user attempts to log in, the application checks their credentials against a stored database of user information.</p><ul><li><strong>Username and Password</strong>: The most common method of authentication, where users enter their unique username and password.</li><li><strong>Biometric Authentication</strong>: Uses unique physical characteristics, such as fingerprints or facial recognition, to verify a user\'s identity.</li><li><strong>Social Media Login</strong>: Allows users to log in using their social media accounts, such as Facebook or Google.</li></ul><h3>Role-Based Access Control</h3><p>Role-based access control (RBAC) is a security approach that restricts access to certain features and data based on a user\'s role within the application. This ensures that users can only perform actions that are authorized for their specific role.</p><ul><li><strong>Admin</strong>: Typically has full access to all features and data, with the ability to manage user roles and permissions.</li><li><strong>Moderator</strong>: Has limited access to features and data, with the ability to perform specific tasks such as content moderation.</li><li><strong>User</strong>: Has restricted access to features and data, with limited ability to perform tasks.</li></ul><h3>JSON Web Tokens</h3><p>JSON web tokens (JWT) are a compact, URL-safe means of representing claims to be transferred between two parties. They consist of a header, payload, and signature, and are often used for authentication and authorization purposes.</p><p>A typical JWT workflow involves the following steps:</p><ol><li>The user logs in with their credentials.</li><li>The server verifies the credentials and generates a JWT.</li><li>The JWT is sent to the client, where it is stored locally.</li><li>The client includes the JWT in subsequent requests to the server.</li><li>The server verifies the JWT and grants access to authorized resources.</li></ol><p>Here\'s an example of a JWT payload in JSON format: <code>{\"sub\":\"1234567890\",\"name\":\"John Doe\",\"admin\":true}</code></p><p>In this example, the payload contains the user\'s subject (ID), name, and admin status.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(170, 158, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: error handling and logging in web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=error+handling+and+logging+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/error+handling+and+logging+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/error+handling+and+logging+in+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/error+handling+and+logging+in+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=error+handling+and+logging+in+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Error Handling and Logging</h3><p>Error handling and logging are crucial aspects of web development. They help developers identify and fix errors, making their applications more robust and reliable. In this section, we will explore the different types of errors, error handling strategies, and logging mechanisms.</p><h3>Error Types</h3><p>There are several types of errors that can occur in a web application. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Syntax Errors</strong>: These occur when there is a mistake in the code syntax, such as a missing or extra bracket.</li><li><strong>Runtime Errors</strong>: These occur when the code is executed, such as when a variable is not defined or a function is called with the wrong parameters.</li><li><strong>Logic Errors</strong>: These occur when the code is syntactically correct but does not produce the desired output, such as when a loop is not properly terminated.</li></ul><p>Understanding the different types of errors is essential for effective error handling.</p><h3>Error Handling Strategies</h3><p>There are several error handling strategies that can be employed in web development. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Try-Catch Blocks</strong>: These are used to catch and handle errors in the code. The <code>try</code> block contains the code that may throw an error, while the <code>catch</code> block contains the code that will be executed if an error occurs.</li><li><strong>Error Callbacks</strong>: These are functions that are called when an error occurs. They can be used to handle errors in asynchronous code.</li><li><strong>Global Error Handlers</strong>: These are functions that are called when an error occurs in any part of the application. They can be used to handle errors that are not caught by other error handling mechanisms.</li></ul><p>Effective error handling strategies can help prevent errors from bringing down an entire application.</p><h3>Logging Mechanisms</h3><p>Logging mechanisms are used to record errors and other events in an application. They can be used to:</p><ul><li><strong>Debug Errors</strong>: Logging can help developers identify the cause of errors by providing detailed information about the error, such as the error message and the line of code where the error occurred.</li><li><strong>Monitor Application Performance</strong>: Logging can be used to monitor the performance of an application, such as the number of requests per second and the response time.</li><li><strong>Identify Security Issues</strong>: Logging can be used to identify security issues, such as unauthorized access attempts and suspicious activity.</li></ul><p>Some common logging mechanisms include:</p><ul><li><strong>Console Logging</strong>: This involves logging errors and other events to the console.</li><li><strong>File Logging</strong>: This involves logging errors and other events to a file.</li><li><strong>Database Logging</strong>: This involves logging errors and other events to a database.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, error handling and logging are essential components of web development. By understanding the different types of errors, employing effective error handling strategies, and using logging mechanisms, developers can create more robust and reliable applications.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(171, 159, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: cloud deployment strategies</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=cloud+deployment+strategies\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/cloud+deployment+strategies\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/cloud+deployment+strategies/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/cloud+deployment+strategies/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=cloud+deployment+strategies\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Deploying a Backend Application</h3><p>Deploying a backend application is a crucial step in making your application available to users. In this section, we will discuss the different deployment strategies, cloud platforms, and containerization.</p><h3>Deployment Strategies</h3><p>There are several deployment strategies that you can use to deploy your backend application. Some of the most common strategies include:</p><ul><li><strong>Traditional Deployment</strong>: This involves deploying your application on a physical server or a virtual machine. You have full control over the infrastructure, but it can be expensive and time-consuming to manage.</li><li><strong>Cloud Deployment</strong>: This involves deploying your application on a cloud platform such as AWS, Google Cloud, or Microsoft Azure. Cloud platforms provide scalability, reliability, and cost-effectiveness, but you have limited control over the infrastructure.</li><li><strong>Containerization</strong>: This involves packaging your application and its dependencies into a container that can be deployed on any platform that supports containers. Containerization provides consistency, efficiency, and scalability, but it can be complex to manage.</li></ul><h3>Cloud Platforms</h3><p>Cloud platforms provide a range of services and tools that make it easy to deploy and manage backend applications. Some of the most popular cloud platforms include:</p><ul><li><strong>AWS (Amazon Web Services)</strong>: AWS provides a wide range of services including EC2, S3, RDS, and Lambda. It is a popular choice among developers due to its scalability, reliability, and cost-effectiveness.</li><li><strong>Google Cloud</strong>: Google Cloud provides a range of services including Compute Engine, Cloud Storage, Cloud SQL, and Cloud Functions. It is a popular choice among developers due to its scalability, reliability, and cost-effectiveness.</li><li><strong>Microsoft Azure</strong>: Microsoft Azure provides a range of services including Virtual Machines, Storage, Databases, and Functions. It is a popular choice among developers due to its scalability, reliability, and cost-effectiveness.</li></ul><h3>Containerization</h3><p>Containerization involves packaging your application and its dependencies into a container that can be deployed on any platform that supports containers. Some of the most popular containerization tools include:</p><ul><li><strong>Docker</strong>: Docker is a popular containerization tool that provides a lightweight and efficient way to package and deploy applications.</li><li><strong>Kubernetes</strong>: Kubernetes is a container orchestration tool that provides a way to automate the deployment, scaling, and management of containers.</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following <code>docker run</code> command to deploy a container:</p><code>docker run -p 8080:8080 my-backend-application</code><p>This command tells Docker to run a container from the <code>my-backend-application</code> image and map port 8080 on the host machine to port 8080 in the container.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(172, 160, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: front-end frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Front-end Frameworks</h3><p>Front-end frameworks are essential tools for building responsive, efficient, and visually appealing web applications. In this lesson, we will explore three popular front-end frameworks: Bootstrap, Material-UI, and Tailwind CSS.</p><h3>Bootstrap</h3><p>Bootstrap is one of the most widely used front-end frameworks. It provides a comprehensive set of pre-designed UI components, layouts, and utilities to help developers build responsive web applications quickly. Bootstrap is known for its simplicity, flexibility, and large community support.</p><p>Some of the key features of Bootstrap include:</p><ul><li><strong>Grid System</strong>: A responsive grid system that allows developers to create complex layouts with ease.</li><li><strong>Pre-designed Components</strong>: Bootstrap provides a wide range of pre-designed UI components, such as navigation bars, alerts, and modals.</li><li><strong>Customizable</strong>: Bootstrap allows developers to customize its components and layouts using CSS and JavaScript.</li></ul><p>Example of using Bootstrap in HTML:</p><code>&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/css/bootstrap.min.css&quot;&gt;</code><h3>Material-UI</h3><p>Material-UI is a popular front-end framework developed by Google. It is based on the Material Design philosophy, which emphasizes simplicity, cleanliness, and intuitive design. Material-UI provides a wide range of pre-designed UI components and layouts that can be easily customized.</p><p>Some of the key features of Material-UI include:</p><ul><li><strong>Material Design Components</strong>: Material-UI provides a wide range of pre-designed UI components, such as buttons, cards, and navigation drawers.</li><li><strong>Responsive Design</strong>: Material-UI is designed to be responsive, making it easy to build web applications that work on multiple devices.</li><li><strong>Customizable</strong>: Material-UI allows developers to customize its components and layouts using CSS and JavaScript.</li></ul><p>Example of using Material-UI in HTML:</p><code>&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;https://fonts.googleapis.com/icon?family=Material+Icons&quot;&gt;</code><h3>Tailwind CSS</h3><p>Tailwind CSS is a utility-first front-end framework that allows developers to build custom UI components without writing custom CSS. It provides a set of pre-defined classes that can be used to style HTML elements.</p><p>Some of the key features of Tailwind CSS include:</p><ul><li><strong>Utility Classes</strong>: Tailwind CSS provides a wide range of utility classes that can be used to style HTML elements, such as text color, background color, and padding.</li><li><strong>Customizable</strong>: Tailwind CSS allows developers to customize its utility classes and create custom components.</li><li><strong>Responsive Design</strong>: Tailwind CSS is designed to be responsive, making it easy to build web applications that work on multiple devices.</li></ul><p>Example of using Tailwind CSS in HTML:</p><code>&lt;div class=&quot;bg-blue-500 text-white p-4&quot;&gt;Hello World!&lt;/div&gt;</code><p>In conclusion, front-end frameworks are essential tools for building responsive, efficient, and visually appealing web applications. Bootstrap, Material-UI, and Tailwind CSS are three popular front-end frameworks that provide a wide range of pre-designed UI components, layouts, and utilities to help developers build web applications quickly.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(173, 161, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: bootstrap grid system</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=bootstrap+grid+system\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/bootstrap+grid+system\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/bootstrap+grid+system/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/bootstrap+grid+system/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=bootstrap+grid+system\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Bootstrap Basics</h3><p>Bootstrap is a popular front-end framework used for building responsive and mobile-first web applications. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of Bootstrap, including the Grid System, Components, and Utilities.</p><h3>Grid System</h3><p>The Bootstrap Grid System is a powerful tool for creating responsive layouts. It is based on a 12-column grid, where each column can be subdivided into smaller columns. The grid system is responsive, meaning it adapts to different screen sizes and devices.</p><ul><li><strong>Container</strong>: The container is the outermost element of the grid system. It is used to wrap the entire grid and can be either fixed or fluid.</li><li><strong>Rows</strong>: Rows are used to define horizontal groups of columns. Each row can contain up to 12 columns.</li><li><strong>Columns</strong>: Columns are the basic building blocks of the grid system. They can be used to create a variety of layouts, from simple to complex.</li></ul><p>Example of a basic grid system:</p><code>&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;div class=&quot;row&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;div class=&quot;col-md-4&quot;&gt;Column 1&lt;/div&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;div class=&quot;col-md-4&quot;&gt;Column 2&lt;/div&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;div class=&quot;col-md-4&quot;&gt;Column 3&lt;/div&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;/div&gt;</code><h3>Components</h3><p>Bootstrap provides a wide range of pre-built components that can be used to create common UI elements, such as navigation bars, alerts, and modals. These components are easy to use and can be customized to fit your needs.</p><ul><li><strong>Navigation Bar</strong>: A navigation bar is a common component used to provide navigation links to different parts of a website.</li><li><strong>Alerts</strong>: Alerts are used to display important messages to the user, such as success or error messages.</li><li><strong>Modals</strong>: Modals are used to display content in a popup window, such as a login form or a confirmation message.</li></ul><p>Example of a navigation bar:</p><code>&lt;nav class=&quot;navbar navbar-expand-lg navbar-light bg-light&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;a class=&quot;navbar-brand&quot; href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Bootstrap&lt;/a&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;button class=&quot;navbar-toggler&quot; type=&quot;button&quot; data-toggle=&quot;collapse&quot; data-target=&quot;#navbarSupportedContent&quot; aria-controls=&quot;navbarSupportedContent&quot; aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot; aria-label=&quot;Toggle navigation&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;span class=&quot;navbar-toggler-icon&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/button&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;div class=&quot;collapse navbar-collapse&quot; id=&quot;navbarSupportedContent&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;ul class=&quot;navbar-nav mr-auto&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;li class=&quot;nav-item active&quot;&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;a class=&quot;nav-link&quot; href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home &lt;span class=&quot;sr-only&quot;&gt;(current)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/ul&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;/nav&gt;</code><h3>Utilities</h3><p>Bootstrap provides a wide range of utility classes that can be used to style and layout elements. These classes can be used to add margin, padding, color, and other styles to elements.</p><ul><li><strong>Margin and Padding</strong>: Bootstrap provides classes for adding margin and padding to elements, such as <code>mt-1</code>, <code>mb-2</code>, <code>pt-3</code>, and <code>pb-4</code>.</li><li><strong>Color</strong>: Bootstrap provides classes for adding color to elements, such as <code>text-primary</code>, <code>text-secondary</code>, and <code>text-success</code>.</li><li><strong>Display</strong>: Bootstrap provides classes for changing the display of elements, such as <code>d-none</code>, <code>d-block</code>, and <code>d-inline</code>.</li></ul><p>Example of using utility classes:</p><code>&lt;p class=&quot;mt-5 mb-3 text-primary&quot;&gt;This is a paragraph with margin top 5, margin bottom 3, and primary text color.&lt;/p&gt;</code><p>In conclusion, Bootstrap provides a powerful set of tools for building responsive and mobile-first web applications. The Grid System, Components, and Utilities are just a few of the many features that make Bootstrap a popular choice among web developers.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(174, 162, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive design examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Design</h3><p>Responsive design is an approach to web development that focuses on creating websites that provide an optimal viewing experience across a wide range of devices, from desktop computers to mobile phones. This is achieved by using a combination of flexible grids, images, and media queries to create a layout that adapts to different screen sizes and devices.</p><h3>Mobile-first Approach</h3><p>The mobile-first approach is a design strategy that involves designing for smaller screens first, and then working your way up to larger screens. This approach is useful because it forces you to prioritize the most important content and features, and to design a layout that is simple and easy to use on smaller screens.</p><p>When using the mobile-first approach, you start by designing the layout and content for the smallest screen size, and then use media queries to add more content and features as the screen size increases. This approach helps to ensure that your website is usable and accessible on all devices, and that the layout is optimized for each screen size.</p><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are a key component of responsive design, and are used to apply different styles based on different conditions, such as screen size or device type. Media queries are defined using the <code>@media</code> rule, followed by a condition, such as <code>max-width: 768px</code>, and then the styles that should be applied when that condition is met.</p><p>For example, you might use a media query to apply a different layout to a website on smaller screens, such as <code>@media (max-width: 768px) { ... }</code>. This media query would apply the styles inside the curly braces to any device with a screen width of 768px or less.</p><ul><li><strong>Types of Media Queries:</strong></li><li>Max-width: applies styles when the screen width is less than or equal to a certain value</li><li>Min-width: applies styles when the screen width is greater than or equal to a certain value</li><li>Max-height: applies styles when the screen height is less than or equal to a certain value</li><li>Min-height: applies styles when the screen height is greater than or equal to a certain value</li></ul><p>By using media queries, you can create a responsive design that adapts to different screen sizes and devices, and provides an optimal viewing experience for all users.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(175, 163, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: material ui components</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=material+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/material+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/material+ui+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/material+ui+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=material+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Material-UI</h3><p>Material-UI is a popular React library used for building user interfaces. It provides a wide range of pre-built components and tools to help developers create responsive, mobile-first, and consistent UIs. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of getting started with Material-UI, its components, and themes.</p><h3>Getting Started</h3><p>To get started with Material-UI, you need to install it in your project using npm or yarn. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal: <code>npm install @material-ui/core</code> or <code>yarn add @material-ui/core</code>. Once installed, you can import the components and use them in your React application.</p><p>Material-UI provides a <strong>CssBaseline</strong> component that resets the CSS baseline for your application. This ensures that your application has a consistent look and feel across different browsers and devices. You can use it by importing it and rendering it at the top of your application.</p><h3>Components</h3><p>Material-UI provides a wide range of pre-built components that you can use to build your UI. Some of the most commonly used components include:</p><ul><li><strong>Button</strong>: A basic button component that can be used to trigger actions.</li><li><strong>TextField</strong>: A text input field that can be used to collect user input.</li><li><strong>Select</strong>: A dropdown select component that can be used to select options.</li><li><strong>DataTable</strong>: A table component that can be used to display data.</li></ul><p>These components are highly customizable and can be styled using Material-UI\'s theme system. You can also create your own custom components using Material-UI\'s building blocks.</p><h3>Themes</h3><p>Material-UI provides a theme system that allows you to customize the look and feel of your application. A theme is an object that defines the color palette, typography, and other visual aspects of your application. You can create your own custom theme by overriding the default theme values.</p><p>Material-UI provides a <strong>ThemeProvider</strong> component that wraps your application and provides the theme context to all components. You can use it to apply your custom theme to your application.</p><p>Some of the key aspects of a Material-UI theme include:</p><ul><li><strong>Color palette</strong>: The set of colors used in your application.</li><li><strong>Typography</strong>: The font family, size, and style used in your application.</li><li><strong>Spacing</strong>: The spacing between components and elements.</li></ul><p>By customizing these aspects, you can create a unique and consistent look and feel for your application.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56');
