(217, 200, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: k-means clustering example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=k-means+clustering+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/k-means+clustering+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/k-means+clustering+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/k-means+clustering+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=k-means+clustering+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to K-Means Clustering</h3><p>K-Means clustering is an unsupervised machine learning algorithm used to group similar data points into clusters. The algorithm works by iteratively updating the centroids of the clusters and reassigning the data points to the closest cluster.</p><h3>K-Means Algorithm</h3><p>The K-Means algorithm involves the following steps:</p><ul><li><strong>Initialization</strong>: Randomly initialize the centroids of the clusters.</li><li><strong>Assignment</strong>: Assign each data point to the closest cluster based on the Euclidean distance.</li><li><strong>Update</strong>: Update the centroids of the clusters by calculating the mean of the data points assigned to each cluster.</li><li><strong>Repeat</strong>: Repeat the assignment and update steps until convergence or a stopping criterion is reached.</li></ul><p>The K-Means algorithm can be implemented using the following Python code:</p><pre><code>import numpy as np<br>from sklearn.cluster import KMeans<br><br># Generate random data<br>np.random.seed(0)<br>X = np.random.rand(100, 2)<br><br># Create a KMeans object with 3 clusters<br>kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=3)<br><br># Fit the model to the data<br>kmeans.fit(X)<br><br># Get the cluster labels<br>labels = kmeans.labels_<br><br># Get the cluster centers<br>centers = kmeans.cluster_centers_<br></code></pre><h3>Choosing the Right Number of Clusters</h3><p>Choosing the right number of clusters is a critical step in K-Means clustering. There are several methods to determine the optimal number of clusters, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Elbow Method</strong>: Plot the sum of squared errors (SSE) against the number of clusters and choose the point where the rate of decrease of SSE becomes less steep (the elbow point).</li><li><strong>Silhouette Method</strong>: Calculate the silhouette score for each data point and choose the number of clusters that maximizes the average silhouette score.</li><li><strong>Calinski-Harabasz Index</strong>: Calculate the Calinski-Harabasz index for each number of clusters and choose the number of clusters that maximizes the index.</li></ul><p>The following Python code demonstrates how to use the elbow method to choose the right number of clusters:</p><pre><code>import numpy as np<br>from sklearn.cluster import KMeans<br>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt<br><br># Generate random data<br>np.random.seed(0)<br>X = np.random.rand(100, 2)<br><br># Calculate the sum of squared errors for each number of clusters<br>sse = []<br>for k in range(1, 11):<br>    kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=k)<br>    kmeans.fit(X)<br>    sse.append(kmeans.inertia_)<br><br># Plot the sum of squared errors against the number of clusters<br>plt.plot(range(1, 11), sse)<br>plt.xlabel(\'Number of Clusters\')<br>plt.ylabel(\'Sum of Squared Errors\')<br>plt.show()<br></code></pre><h3>Cluster Evaluation Metrics</h3><p>There are several metrics to evaluate the quality of the clusters, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Silhouette Score</strong>: Measures the separation between clusters and the cohesion within clusters.</li><li><strong>Calinski-Harabasz Index</strong>: Measures the ratio of between-cluster variance to within-cluster variance.</li><li><strong>Davies-Bouldin Index</strong>: Measures the similarity between clusters based on their centroid distances and scatter within the clusters.</li></ul><p>The following Python code demonstrates how to calculate the silhouette score for a given number of clusters:</p><pre><code>import numpy as np<br>from sklearn.cluster import KMeans<br>from sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score<br><br># Generate random data<br>np.random.seed(0)<br>X = np.random.rand(100, 2)<br><br># Create a KMeans object with 3 clusters<br>kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=3)<br><br># Fit the model to the data<br>kmeans.fit(X)<br><br># Calculate the silhouette score<br>score = silhouette_score(X, kmeans.labels_)<br>print(\'Silhouette Score:\', score)<br></code></pre>', 1, '2026-04-04 02:05:54', '2026-04-04 02:05:54'),
(218, 201, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: hierarchical clustering dendrogram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=hierarchical+clustering+dendrogram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/hierarchical+clustering+dendrogram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/hierarchical+clustering+dendrogram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/hierarchical+clustering+dendrogram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=hierarchical+clustering+dendrogram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Hierarchical Clustering</h3><p>Hierarchical clustering is a type of unsupervised machine learning algorithm that groups similar objects into clusters. It is called hierarchical because it builds a hierarchy of clusters, either by merging smaller clusters into larger ones (agglomerative clustering) or by splitting larger clusters into smaller ones (divisive clustering). In this lesson, we will explore both types of hierarchical clustering and learn how to visualize the results using dendrograms.</p><h3>Agglomerative Clustering</h3><p>Agglomerative clustering is a bottom-up approach that starts with each data point as its own cluster. The algorithm then merges the two closest clusters together, forming a new cluster. This process is repeated until all data points are in a single cluster. The key steps in agglomerative clustering are:</p><ul><li>Start with each data point as its own cluster</li><li>Calculate the distance between each pair of clusters</li><li>Merge the two closest clusters together</li><li>Repeat steps 2-3 until all data points are in a single cluster</li></ul><p>The distance between clusters can be calculated using various methods, such as single linkage (minimum distance between any two points in the clusters), complete linkage (maximum distance between any two points in the clusters), or average linkage (average distance between all points in the clusters).</p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.cluster.hierarchy import linkage\nfrom scipy.cluster.hierarchy import dendrogram\n\n# Generate some sample data\nnp.random.seed(0)\ndata = np.random.multivariate_normal([0, 0], [[1, 0.75], [0.75, 1]], 50)\n\n# Perform agglomerative clustering\nZ = linkage(data, \'ward\')\n\n# Plot the dendrogram\ndendrogram(Z)</code></pre><h3>Divisive Clustering</h3><p>Divisive clustering is a top-down approach that starts with all data points in a single cluster. The algorithm then splits the cluster into two smaller clusters, based on the points that are farthest apart. This process is repeated until each data point is in its own cluster. The key steps in divisive clustering are:</p><ul><li>Start with all data points in a single cluster</li><li>Split the cluster into two smaller clusters, based on the points that are farthest apart</li><li>Repeat step 2 until each data point is in its own cluster</li></ul><p>Divisive clustering is less common than agglomerative clustering, but it can be useful for certain types of data. However, it can be more computationally expensive and may not always produce the same results as agglomerative clustering.</p><h3>Dendrograms</h3><p>A dendrogram is a tree-like diagram that shows the hierarchy of clusters produced by hierarchical clustering. The x-axis represents the data points, and the y-axis represents the distance between clusters. The dendrogram can be used to visualize the results of hierarchical clustering and to determine the optimal number of clusters.</p><p>In the dendrogram, each merge or split is represented by a horizontal line, and the height of the line represents the distance between the clusters. The dendrogram can be cut at a certain height to determine the number of clusters. For example, if we cut the dendrogram at a height of 5, we might get 3 clusters.</p><pre><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy.cluster.hierarchy import dendrogram\n\n# Plot the dendrogram\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 7))\ndendrogram(Z)\nplt.title(\'Dendrogram of the Data\')\nplt.show()</code></pre><p>In conclusion, hierarchical clustering is a powerful tool for grouping similar objects into clusters. Agglomerative clustering is a bottom-up approach that builds a hierarchy of clusters by merging smaller clusters together, while divisive clustering is a top-down approach that splits larger clusters into smaller ones. Dendrograms provide a visual representation of the hierarchy of clusters and can be used to determine the optimal number of clusters.</p>', 1, '2026-04-04 02:05:54', '2026-04-04 02:05:54'),
(219, 202, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: principal component analysis</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=principal+component+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/principal+component+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/principal+component+analysis/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/principal+component+analysis/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=principal+component+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Principal Component Analysis (PCA)</h3><p>Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a widely used dimensionality reduction technique in data science. It is a statistical method that transforms a set of correlated variables into a new set of uncorrelated variables, called principal components, which capture the variance within the data.</p><h3>PCA Theory</h3><p>The goal of PCA is to find the principal components that describe the variance within the data. The first principal component explains the most variance, the second principal component explains the second most variance, and so on. The principal components are calculated by finding the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix of the data.</p><p>The steps involved in PCA are:</p><ul><li>Standardize the data by subtracting the mean and dividing by the standard deviation for each feature.</li><li>Calculate the covariance matrix of the standardized data.</li><li>Calculate the eigenvectors and eigenvalues of the covariance matrix.</li><li>Sort the eigenvectors in descending order of their corresponding eigenvalues.</li><li>Select the top k eigenvectors, where k is the number of principal components to retain.</li></ul><h3>PCA Applications</h3><p>PCA has many applications in data science, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Data Visualization</strong>: PCA can be used to reduce the dimensionality of high-dimensional data, making it easier to visualize.</li><li><strong>Noise Reduction</strong>: PCA can be used to reduce the noise in the data by retaining only the top principal components.</li><li><strong>Feature Extraction</strong>: PCA can be used to extract the most important features from the data.</li><li><strong>Anomaly Detection</strong>: PCA can be used to detect anomalies in the data by identifying data points that are far away from the mean of the principal components.</li></ul><h3>PCA Example</h3><p>Let\'s consider an example of applying PCA to the Iris dataset, which has 4 features (sepal length, sepal width, petal length, and petal width) and 150 samples.</p><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\n# Load the Iris dataset\niris = pd.read_csv(\'iris.csv\')\n\n# Standardize the data\nscaler = StandardScaler()\niris_std = scaler.fit_transform(iris)\n\n# Apply PCA\npca = PCA(n_components=2)\niris_pca = pca.fit_transform(iris_std)\n\n# Print the explained variance ratio\nprint(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)</code></pre><p>In this example, we first standardize the data using the StandardScaler from scikit-learn. Then, we apply PCA to the standardized data, retaining only the top 2 principal components. The explained variance ratio is printed, which shows the proportion of variance explained by each principal component.</p><p>The resulting principal components can be used for data visualization, noise reduction, feature extraction, or anomaly detection.</p>', 1, '2026-04-04 02:05:54', '2026-04-04 02:05:54'),
(220, 203, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: dimensionality reduction techniques</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=dimensionality+reduction+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/dimensionality+reduction+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/dimensionality+reduction+techniques/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/dimensionality+reduction+techniques/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=dimensionality+reduction+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to t-SNE and Autoencoders</h3><p>Welcome to Day 26 of our 30-day Data Science course. Today, we will be exploring two important dimensionality reduction techniques: t-SNE and Autoencoders. These techniques are used to reduce the number of features in a dataset while preserving the most important information.</p><h3>t-SNE (t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding)</h3><p>t-SNE is a non-linear dimensionality reduction technique that is particularly well-suited for visualizing high-dimensional data. It works by converting the high-dimensional data into a lower-dimensional representation, typically 2D or 3D, while preserving the local structure of the data.</p><p>The t-SNE algorithm consists of the following steps:</p><ul><li>Compute the pairwise distances between all data points in the high-dimensional space.</li><li>Convert the distances into probabilities using a Gaussian distribution.</li><li>Compute the pairwise distances between all data points in the lower-dimensional space.</li><li>Convert the distances into probabilities using a t-distribution.</li><li>Minimize the difference between the two sets of probabilities using gradient descent.</li></ul><p>The result is a lower-dimensional representation of the data that preserves the local structure of the high-dimensional data.</p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# Generate some sample data\nnp.random.seed(0)\ndata = np.random.rand(100, 10)\n\n# Perform t-SNE\ntsne = TSNE(n_components=2, random_state=0)\ndata_tsne = tsne.fit_transform(data)\n\n# Plot the results\nplt.scatter(data_tsne[:, 0], data_tsne[:, 1])\nplt.show()</code></pre><h3>Autoencoders</h3><p>Autoencoders are a type of neural network that can be used for dimensionality reduction. They consist of an encoder and a decoder, where the encoder maps the input data to a lower-dimensional representation, and the decoder maps the lower-dimensional representation back to the original input data.</p><p>The autoencoder is trained to minimize the difference between the input data and the reconstructed data. This forces the autoencoder to learn a compact representation of the data that captures the most important features.</p><p>Autoencoders can be used for a variety of tasks, including dimensionality reduction, anomaly detection, and generative modeling.</p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Input, Dense\nfrom tensorflow.keras.models import Model\n\n# Generate some sample data\nnp.random.seed(0)\ndata = np.random.rand(100, 10)\n\n# Define the autoencoder\ninput_dim = 10\nencoding_dim = 2\n\ninput_layer = Input(shape=(input_dim,))\nencoder = Dense(encoding_dim, activation=\'relu\')(input_layer)\ndecoder = Dense(input_dim, activation=\'sigmoid\')(encoder)\n\nautoencoder = Model(input_layer, decoder)\n\n# Compile the autoencoder\nautoencoder.compile(optimizer=\'adam\', loss=\'binary_crossentropy\')\n\n# Train the autoencoder\nautoencoder.fit(data, data, epochs=100, batch_size=10, shuffle=True)\n\n# Use the autoencoder for dimensionality reduction\nencoder_model = Model(input_layer, encoder)\ndata_encoded = encoder_model.predict(data)</code></pre><h3>Comparison of Dimensionality Reduction Techniques</h3><p>There are many dimensionality reduction techniques available, each with its own strengths and weaknesses. Here are some of the most common techniques:</p><ul><li><strong>PCA (Principal Component Analysis)</strong>: A linear technique that is widely used for dimensionality reduction. It works by finding the principal components of the data, which are the directions of maximum variance.</li><li><strong>t-SNE (t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding)</strong>: A non-linear technique that is particularly well-suited for visualizing high-dimensional data. It works by converting the high-dimensional data into a lower-dimensional representation, typically 2D or 3D, while preserving the local structure of the data.</li><li><strong>Autoencoders</strong>: A type of neural network that can be used for dimensionality reduction. They consist of an encoder and a decoder, where the encoder maps the input data to a lower-dimensional representation, and the decoder maps the lower-dimensional representation back to the original input data.</li><li><strong>LLE (Local Linear Embedding)</strong>: A non-linear technique that works by finding the local linear relationships between data points. It is particularly well-suited for data that has a complex, non-linear structure.</li></ul><p>The choice of dimensionality reduction technique depends on the specific problem and the characteristics of the data. It is often useful to try out several different techniques and compare the results.</p>', 1, '2026-04-04 02:05:54', '2026-04-04 02:05:54'),
(221, 204, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: anomaly detection in data science</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=anomaly+detection+in+data+science\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/anomaly+detection+in+data+science\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/anomaly+detection+in+data+science/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/anomaly+detection+in+data+science/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=anomaly+detection+in+data+science\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Density Estimation and Anomaly Detection</h3><p>Density estimation is a fundamental concept in statistics and data science that involves estimating the underlying probability distribution of a dataset. It has numerous applications, including anomaly detection, which is the process of identifying data points that are significantly different from the majority of the data.</p><p>In this section, we will delve into the concepts of density estimation and anomaly detection, and explore how to implement them using Python. We will also discuss One-Class SVM, a popular algorithm for anomaly detection.</p><h3>Density Estimation</h3><p>Density estimation is the process of estimating the underlying probability distribution of a dataset. The goal is to create a model that can generate new data points that are similar to the existing data. There are several methods for density estimation, including:</p><ul><li>Parametric methods: These methods assume a specific distribution (e.g., Gaussian, Poisson) and estimate the parameters of that distribution.</li><li>Non-parametric methods: These methods do not assume a specific distribution and instead use the data to estimate the distribution. Examples include kernel density estimation (KDE) and histograms.</li></ul><p>One of the most popular non-parametric methods for density estimation is kernel density estimation (KDE). KDE works by placing a kernel (a small, symmetric function) at each data point and summing the contributions of all kernels to estimate the density at a given point.</p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import gaussian_kde\n\n# Generate some sample data\nnp.random.seed(0)\ndata = np.random.normal(0, 1, 100)\n\n# Create a KDE object\nkde = gaussian_kde(data)\n\n# Evaluate the density at a given point\nx = 0.5\ndensity = kde.evaluate(x)\nprint(density)</code></pre><h3>Anomaly Detection</h3><p>Anomaly detection is the process of identifying data points that are significantly different from the majority of the data. There are several approaches to anomaly detection, including:</p><ul><li>Statistical methods: These methods use statistical tests (e.g., z-scores, modified Z-scores) to identify data points that are unlikely to occur under a given distribution.</li><li>Machine learning methods: These methods use algorithms (e.g., One-Class SVM, Local Outlier Factor) to identify data points that are anomalous.</li></ul><p>One of the most popular machine learning methods for anomaly detection is One-Class SVM. One-Class SVM is a type of support vector machine that is trained on a dataset to identify the decision boundary between the normal data points and the anomalies.</p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import OneClassSVM\n\n# Generate some sample data\nnp.random.seed(0)\ndata = np.random.normal(0, 1, 100)\n\n# Create a One-Class SVM object\nsvm = OneClassSVM(kernel=\'rbf\', gamma=0.1, nu=0.1)\n\n# Fit the model to the data\nsvm.fit(data.reshape(-1, 1))\n\n# Predict whether a new data point is an anomaly\nx = 5\nprediction = svm.predict(x.reshape(1, -1))\nprint(prediction)</code></pre><p>In this example, we generate some sample data from a normal distribution and create a One-Class SVM object. We then fit the model to the data and use it to predict whether a new data point is an anomaly. The <code>predict</code> method returns an array of shape <code>(n_samples,)</code> where <code>n_samples</code> is the number of data points to predict. The values in this array are either <code>1</code> (inliers) or <code>-1</code> (outliers).</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this section, we discussed density estimation and anomaly detection, and explored how to implement them using Python. We also discussed One-Class SVM, a popular algorithm for anomaly detection. Density estimation is a fundamental concept in statistics and data science, and anomaly detection has numerous applications in fields such as finance, healthcare, and cybersecurity.</p>', 1, '2026-04-04 02:05:54', '2026-04-04 02:05:54'),
(222, 205, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: unsupervised learning project</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=unsupervised+learning+project\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/unsupervised+learning+project\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/unsupervised+learning+project/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/unsupervised+learning+project/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=unsupervised+learning+project\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Unsupervised Learning Project Overview</h3><p>Unsupervised learning is a type of machine learning where the model is trained on unlabeled data. The goal of unsupervised learning is to discover patterns, relationships, or groupings within the data. In this project, we will be applying unsupervised learning techniques to a dataset to identify clusters and reduce dimensionality.</p><h3>Data Preprocessing</h3><p>Before we can apply unsupervised learning algorithms, we need to preprocess the data. This includes handling missing values, scaling/normalizing the data, and encoding categorical variables. We will be using the <code>pd.read_csv</code> function to load the dataset and the <code>preprocessing</code> module from <code>sklearn</code> to scale and encode the data.</p><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n# Load the dataset\ndata = pd.read_csv(\'dataset.csv\')\n# Scale the data\nscaler = StandardScaler()\ndata_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(data)</code></pre><h3>Clustering</h3><p>Clustering is a type of unsupervised learning algorithm that groups similar data points into clusters. We will be using the <code>KMeans</code> algorithm from <code>sklearn.cluster</code> to cluster the data. The number of clusters (k) is a hyperparameter that needs to be tuned.</p><pre><code>from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n# Initialize the KMeans model\nkmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=5)\n# Fit the model to the scaled data\nkmeans.fit(data_scaled)</code></pre><h3>Dimensionality Reduction</h3><p>Dimensionality reduction is a technique used to reduce the number of features in the dataset while retaining most of the information. We will be using the <code>PCA</code> algorithm from <code>sklearn.decomposition</code> to reduce the dimensionality of the data.</p><pre><code>from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n# Initialize the PCA model\npca = PCA(n_components=2)\n# Fit the model to the scaled data\npca.fit(data_scaled)\n# Transform the data\ndata_reduced = pca.transform(data_scaled)</code></pre><p>The resulting reduced data can be visualized using a scatter plot to identify clusters and patterns.</p><ul><li>Load the dataset and preprocess the data by handling missing values, scaling/normalizing, and encoding categorical variables.</li><li>Apply the KMeans clustering algorithm to the preprocessed data and tune the hyperparameter (number of clusters).</li><li>Apply the PCA dimensionality reduction algorithm to the preprocessed data and tune the hyperparameter (number of components).</li><li>Visualize the reduced data using a scatter plot to identify clusters and patterns.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-04 02:05:54', '2026-04-04 02:05:54'),
