(8, 13, 'text', '<div><br></div><div><img src=\"https://www.internshipadda.com/public/uploads/images/media_698388456a003_1770227781.png\" alt=\"Uploaded image\" style=\"--tw-scale-x: 1; --tw-scale-y: 1; --tw-pan-x: ; --tw-pan-y: ; --tw-pinch-zoom: ; --tw-scroll-snap-strictness: proximity; --tw-gradient-from-position: ; --tw-gradient-via-position: ; --tw-gradient-to-position: ; --tw-ordinal: ; --tw-slashed-zero: ; --tw-numeric-figure: ; --tw-numeric-spacing: ; --tw-numeric-fraction: ; --tw-ring-inset: ; --tw-ring-offset-width: 0px; --tw-ring-offset-color: #fff; --tw-ring-color: rgb(59 130 246 / 0.5); --tw-ring-offset-shadow: 0 0 #0000; --tw-ring-shadow: 0 0 #0000; --tw-shadow: 0 0 #0000; --tw-shadow-colored: 0 0 #0000; --tw-blur: ; --tw-brightness: ; --tw-contrast: ; --tw-grayscale: ; --tw-hue-rotate: ; --tw-invert: ; --tw-saturate: ; --tw-sepia: ; --tw-drop-shadow: ; --tw-backdrop-blur: ; --tw-backdrop-brightness: ; --tw-backdrop-contrast: ; --tw-backdrop-grayscale: ; --tw-backdrop-hue-rotate: ; --tw-backdrop-invert: ; --tw-backdrop-opacity: ; --tw-backdrop-saturate: ; --tw-backdrop-sepia: ; --tw-contain-size: ; --tw-contain-layout: ; --tw-contain-paint: ; --tw-contain-style: ;\"></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Python is a high-level, interpreted, general-purpose programming language that has transformed from a niche hobbyist project in the early 1990s into the most dominant force in modern computing. To truly master Python, one must look beyond the code and understand the architectural decisions made by its creator, Guido van Rossum. Python was built on the principle that \"readability counts.\" In the software world, this is known as a High-Level Language. Unlike lower-level languages (like C or Assembly), which require the programmer to manage CPU cycles and memory addresses manually, Python provides a</div><div>n abstraction layer. It handles the \"machine-talk\" so that you can focus on \"logic-talk.\"</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>A core pillar of Python’s identity is that it is an Interpreted Language. To understand this, imagine a translator. In a \"Compiled\" language like C++, the computer translates the entire book (your code) into machine language before you can read a single page. If there is a mistake on page 100, the translation fails entirely. Python, however, works like a live interpreter at a conference. It reads and executes your code line-by-line. This is handled by the Python Interpreter. This approach is a double-edged sword: while it makes Python slightly slower than compiled languages for heavy mathematical processing, it makes development incredibly flexible. If your code has an error on line 10, the program will execute lines 1 through 9 perfectly, allowing you to see exactly where things went wrong.</div><div>The philosophy of Python is formalized in a document called PEP 20, also known as \"The Zen of Python.\" It argues that \"Simple is better than complex\" and \"Sparse is better than dense.\" This mindset is why Python has become the gold standard for Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning, and Data Science. Because the syntax is clean and lacks the \"boilerplate\" (repetitive, necessary code) of other languages, researchers can write complex algorithms that actually look like the mathematical formulas they represent. By starting this journey, you are gaining access to a massive ecosystem. Whether it’s web servers, automated scripts, or neural networks, Python provides the bridge between human thought and digital execution.</div><div><br></div><div>Example Logic:</div><div>print(\"System Online\") — This command interacts with the standard output stream of your operating system.</div><div>import math; print(math.sqrt(16)) — This demonstrates how Python uses modularity to bring in complex math tools only when needed.</div><div>help(len) — Python\'s built-in introspection allows the language to explain its own functions to the user.</div><div>print(type(\"10\")) — This shows that Python classifies every piece of data into a specific category or \"class.\"</div><div>exit() — This command signals the interpreter to stop the current process and return control to the operating system.</div><div>Key Takeaways:</div><div>The Interpreter: The Python Interpreter (often CPython) converts your source code into \"bytecode,\" which is then run on a Python Virtual Machine (PVM).</div><div>Cross-Platform Nature: Python code is highly portable. Because the interpreter handles the hardware communication, the same script can usually run on Windows, macOS, and Linux without any modifications.</div><div>Extensive Standard Library: Python comes \"batteries included,\" meaning it has a vast collection of pre-written code that handles everything from web connections to file management right out of the box.</div>', NULL, 0, '2026-02-04 17:57:51', '2026-02-04 17:57:51'),
(9, 14, 'text', '<div>One of the most common misconceptions for beginners is that a variable is a \"bucket\" that holds a value. In Python, it is more accurate to think of a variable as a Label or a Pointer that refers to an object in the computer\'s memory. When you write x = 10, Python creates an object (the number 10) in a specific memory address and then \"tags\" that address with the name x.</div><div>Python uses a system called Dynamic Typing. In \"Statically Typed\" languages like Java or C#, you must declare the type of data a variable will hold for its entire life (e.g., \"This variable is an Integer\"). Python is different; it determines the data type at the moment of assignment. If you reassign x = \"Hello\", Python simply moves the x label from the memory address of the number 10 to the memory address of the text \"Hello\". The old object (the number 10) is then eventually cleared away by an internal process called Garbage Collection, which automatically frees up RAM that is no longer being used.</div><div>However, this freedom requires a disciplined approach to Identifiers (naming variables). Professional code is not just written for computers; it is written for other humans. This is why naming conventions are critical. In Python, we strictly follow snake_case—lowercase words separated by underscores. A variable named u_s is a bad variable; a variable named user_session_timeout is a great one because it explains its own purpose.</div><div>There are strict syntax rules for these labels:</div><div>They must start with a letter or an underscore (_).</div><div>They cannot start with a number.</div><div>They can only contain alphanumeric characters and underscores.</div><div>They are case-sensitive; age, Age, and AGE are three different locations in memory.</div><div>Understanding this pointer-based logic is essential when you move into more advanced topics like lists or objects, where multiple variables might point to the same memory address. If you change the data through one label, the other label will see the change too. Mastering variables is the first step in learning how to manage the \"state\" of your application efficiently.</div><div><br></div><div><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_69878d990d30e_1770491289.jpeg\" alt=\"Uploaded image\"></div><div><br></div><div>Example Logic:</div><div><br></div><div>total_price = 499.99 — A clear, descriptive variable using snake_case.</div><div>is_authenticated = False — A \"Boolean\" variable used to track a specific state in an application.</div><div>_secret_key = \"AX92\" — Starting a variable with an underscore is a convention used to signal that the variable is intended for internal use only.</div><div>a = b = c = 0 — Python allows \"Chained Assignment,\" where multiple labels point to the same initial value in memory.</div><div>current_year = 2024; current_year = 2025 — Demonstrating how a label can be reassigned to a new object in memory.</div><div>Key Takeaways:</div><div>Memory Management: Python’s \"Reference Counting\" system tracks how many labels are pointing to an object. When the count hits zero, the object is deleted.</div><div>Reserved Keywords: You cannot use words like class, def, if, or return as variable names because they are part of the Python language\'s \"vocabulary.\"</div><div>Self-Documenting Code: Always choose names that describe the \"what\" and the \"why\" of the data being stored.</div>', NULL, 0, '2026-02-07 19:08:56', '2026-02-07 19:08:56'),
(10, 15, 'text', '<div>Numerical computation is the heart of programming. Whether you are calculating the path of a satellite or the total of a shopping cart, you need to understand how Python categorizes and processes numbers. Python primarily uses two numeric types for daily tasks: Integers (int) and Floating-Point Numbers (float).</div><div>​Integers are whole numbers. Python 3 is unique because it supports Arbitrary Precision Integers. In many other languages, if a number gets too large, it \"overflows,\" leading to incorrect negative results or crashes. Python, however, will automatically use more of your computer’s RAM to accommodate numbers of any size. You can calculate 10^{100} and Python will provide the exact result without any loss of data.</div><div>​Floating-Point Numbers are used for decimals. While they appear simple, they are technically complex. Computers use the IEEE 754 standard to represent decimals in binary. Because of this, certain decimal fractions (like 0.1) cannot be represented perfectly in binary, similar to how 1/3 cannot be represented perfectly in decimal (0.333...). This leads to a phenomenon called Floating-Point Error. For instance, in Python, 0.1 + 0.2 might result in 0.30000000000000004. As a developer, you must be aware of this when building financial software; you would never compare two floats for exact equality (==). Instead, you check if the difference between them is very small.</div><div>​Python also includes Complex Numbers (a + bj) for specialized engineering and scientific fields. One of Python\'s most \"intelligent\" behaviors is Implicit Promotion. If you add an integer and a float (e.g., 5 + 2.0), Python recognizes that the float is more \"capable\" of holding precise information. It automatically \"promotes\" the 5 to 5.0 and returns a float (7.0). Furthermore, Python 3 changed the way division works: the / operator always returns a float (e.g., 4 / 2 is 2.0), ensuring that developers don\'t accidentally lose fractional data—a common \"logic bug\" in older programming languages.</div><div><br></div><div><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_69878fa5e2ce7_1770491813.jpeg\" alt=\"Uploaded image\"></div><div><br></div><div>​Example Logic:</div><div><br></div><div>​items_in_stock = 150 — A standard integer used for counting items.</div><div>​exchange_rate = 82.55 — A float used for precise currency conversion.</div><div>​result = 10 / 3 — Shows how Python handles long decimal results in division.</div><div>​huge_val = 1_000_000_000 — Python allows underscores in numbers to make them easier for humans to read (the interpreter ignores them).</div><div>​imaginary_val = 5 + 3j — A complex number where 5 is the real part and 3j is the imaginary part.</div><div>​Key Takeaways:</div><div>​Type Hierarchy: Python will always try to convert \"narrow\" types (int) to \"wider\" types (float) during math to preserve data.</div><div>​The id() function: You can use id() to see the actual memory address of a number. Interestingly, Python \"interns\" small integers (usually -5 to 256) to save memory.</div><div>​Math Module: For advanced operations like power, square roots, or trigonometry, Python provides the math module, which is written in highly optimized C code.</div>', NULL, 0, '2026-02-07 19:18:02', '2026-02-07 19:18:02'),
