(15, 28, 'text', '<h3>Welcome to the World of C++</h3><p>C++ is one of the most powerful and influential programming languages in the history of computing. Developed by Bjarne Stroustrup at Bell Labs in 1979, it was originally conceived as an extension of the C language, adding object-oriented features. Today, it is the backbone of high-performance software, including operating systems like Windows and macOS, triple-A video games, financial trading systems, and even the software that powers Mars rovers. In this first lesson, we will explore the environment setup and the fundamental structure of a C++ program.</p><h3>The C++ Environment and Installation</h3><p>To begin writing C++, you need two essential tools: a <strong>Compiler</strong> and a <strong>Text Editor</strong> (or an Integrated Development Environment, known as an IDE). A compiler is a special program that translates the human-readable code you write into machine-readable instructions that the computer\'s CPU can execute. Popular compilers include GCC (GNU Compiler Collection), Clang, and Microsoft Visual C++.</p><p>For beginners, using an IDE like <strong>Visual Studio Code</strong>, <strong>Code::Blocks</strong>, or <strong>CLion</strong> is highly recommended because they combine the editor and compiler into one interface. If you are on Windows, installing the MinGW-w64 compiler or using Visual Studio is common. On macOS, the Xcode Command Line Tools provide the Clang compiler. On Linux, GCC is usually pre-installed or easily available via the package manager.</p><h3>Anatomy of a C++ Program</h3><p>Every C++ program follows a specific structure. Let\'s look at the classic \'Hello World\' example to understand the basic building blocks:</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    // This is a comment - it is ignored by the compiler\n    std::cout << \"Hello, C++ World!\" << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre><p>Let’s break this down line by line:</p><ul><li><strong>#include &lt;iostream&gt;:</strong> This is a preprocessor directive. It tells the compiler to include the Standard Input/Output Stream library, which allows us to display text on the screen.</li><li><strong>int main():</strong> This is the entry point of every C++ program. When you run your code, the computer starts execution from the first line inside this function. The \'int\' means the function returns an integer value to the operating system.</li><li><strong>Curly Braces { }:</strong> These mark the beginning and end of a block of code. Everything inside these braces belongs to the main function.</li><li><strong>std::cout:</strong> Short for \'character output\', this is used to print text. The \'std::\' prefix indicates that \'cout\' belongs to the standard namespace.</li><li><strong>&lt;&lt;:</strong> These are insertion operators, directing the string \"Hello, C++ World!\" to the output stream.</li><li><strong>return 0;:</strong> This signals that the program finished successfully.</li></ul><h3>Comments and Whitespace</h3><p>Readable code is as important as working code. <strong>Comments</strong> are notes written for humans that the compiler ignores. In C++, you can use <code>//</code> for single-line comments or <code>/* ... */</code> for multi-line comments. <strong>Whitespace</strong> refers to spaces, tabs, and newlines. While the compiler generally ignores extra whitespace, using it effectively makes your code much easier to maintain and debug.</p><blockquote>💡 <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Always use meaningful comments to explain \'why\' you are doing something, rather than \'what\' you are doing. The code should ideally be clear enough to show the \'what\' on its own.</blockquote><h3>Standard Input and Output</h3><p>Interaction is key to software. We use <code>std::cout</code> for output and <code>std::cin</code> for input. When using <code>std::cout</code>, you can chain multiple outputs together using multiple <code>&lt;&lt;</code> operators. For example, <code>std::cout << \"Age: \" << 25 << std::endl;</code>. The <code>std::endl</code> command not only moves the cursor to a new line but also flushes the output buffer, ensuring the text appears immediately on the screen.</p>', 'Getting Started and Basic Syntax', 1, '2026-03-25 09:05:51', '2026-03-25 09:05:51'),
(16, 29, 'text', '<h3>Understanding Data Types</h3><p>In C++, every piece of data has a specific type. This is because C++ is a <strong>statically-typed language</strong>, meaning the compiler needs to know how much memory to allocate for a variable and how to interpret the bits stored there. Choosing the right data type is crucial for performance and memory efficiency.</p><ul><li><strong>int:</strong> Used for whole numbers (integers), such as 5, -10, or 42. Usually occupies 4 bytes of memory.</li><li><strong>double:</strong> Used for floating-point numbers (decimals) with high precision, such as 3.14159.</li><li><strong>char:</strong> Stores a single character, like \'A\' or \'$\', enclosed in single quotes.</li><li><strong>bool:</strong> Represents a boolean value, either <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</li><li><strong>float:</strong> Similar to double but with less precision and smaller memory footprint.</li></ul><h3>Variables and Constants</h3><p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a named storage location in memory. To use a variable, you must declare it by specifying its type and name. You can also initialize it with a value at the time of declaration. For example:</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int age = 21;\ndouble price = 19.99;\nchar grade = \'A\';\nbool isGraduated = true;</code></pre><p>If you have a value that should never change during the program\'s execution, you use the <code>const</code> keyword. This tells the compiler to protect the variable from being modified. For instance, <code>const double PI = 3.14159;</code> ensures that your program won\'t accidentally change the value of PI later, which would lead to logic errors.</p><h3>Arithmetic and Assignment Operators</h3><p>Operators are symbols that perform operations on variables and values. C++ includes standard arithmetic operators: <code>+</code> (addition), <code>-</code> (subtraction), <code>*</code> (multiplication), <code>/</code> (division), and <code>%</code> (modulus/remainder). The modulus operator is particularly useful for checking if a number is even or odd (e.g., <code>number % 2 == 0</code>).</p><p>The assignment operator <code>=</code> is used to give a variable a value. However, C++ also offers compound assignment operators like <code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code>, <code>*=</code>, and <code>/=</code>. For example, <code>score += 10;</code> is a shorter way of writing <code>score = score + 10;</code>. These are widely used in game development and loops to update values efficiently.</p><h3>Relational and Logical Operators</h3><p>To make decisions, we need to compare values. <strong>Relational operators</strong> return a boolean (true/false) result. These include <code>==</code> (equal to), <code>!=</code> (not equal to), <code>&gt;</code> (greater than), <code>&lt;</code> (less than), <code>&gt;=</code>, and <code>&lt;=</code>. Note that <code>==</code> is for comparison, while <code>=</code> is for assignment—a common source of bugs for beginners!</p><p><strong>Logical operators</strong> allow us to combine multiple conditions: <code>&&</code> (Logical AND), <code>||</code> (Logical OR), and <code>!</code> (Logical NOT). For example, a user can log in only if <code>hasCorrectUsername && hasCorrectPassword</code> is true.</p><blockquote>💡 <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> When working with integers, be careful with division. <code>5 / 2</code> will result in <code>2</code>, not <code>2.5</code>, because the fractional part is truncated. To get <code>2.5</code>, at least one of the numbers must be a double or float.</blockquote>', 'Data Types and Operators', 1, '2026-03-25 09:05:51', '2026-03-25 09:05:51'),
(17, 30, 'text', '<h3>The Logic of Selection</h3><p>Programs rarely run in a straight line from top to bottom. They need to make decisions based on user input or environmental factors. This is known as <strong>Control Flow</strong>. The primary way we implement selection in C++ is through the <code>if</code> statement.</p><h3>The If, Else If, and Else Ladder</h3><p>The <code>if</code> statement evaluates a condition inside parentheses. If the condition is true, the block of code inside the braces executes. If not, the program moves to the next block. We can chain conditions using <code>else if</code> and provide a final fallback with <code>else</code>.</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int temperature = 25;\n\nif (temperature > 30) {\n    std::cout << \"It\'s a hot day!\";\n} else if (temperature > 15) {\n    std::cout << \"The weather is pleasant.\";\n} else {\n    std::cout << \"It\'s cold outside.\";\n}</code></pre><p>In this real-world example, the program checks the temperature and prints exactly one message. Once a condition is met, the rest of the ladder is skipped, which makes the program efficient.</p><h3>Switch Case Logic</h3><p>When you have a single variable that you want to compare against many possible constant values (like a menu selection), a <code>switch</code> statement is often cleaner than a long if-else ladder. It works primarily with integers and characters.</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int choice = 2;\nswitch (choice) {\n    case 1:\n        std::cout << \"Opening Settings...\";\n        break;\n    case 2:\n        std::cout << \"Starting Game...\";\n        break;\n    default:\n        std::cout << \"Invalid selection.\";\n}</code></pre><p>The <code>break</code> keyword is vital here. Without it, the program would \'fall through\' and execute all subsequent cases, even if their conditions weren\'t met. The <code>default</code> case acts like the <code>else</code> in an if-statement, handling any values not explicitly covered.</p><h3>Conditional Ternary Operator</h3><p>C++ provides a shorthand way to write simple if-else statements called the <strong>Ternary Operator</strong> (<code>? :</code>). It takes the form: <code>condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false;</code>. For example, <code>std::string status = (age >= 18) ? \"Adult\" : \"Minor\";</code>. This is excellent for keeping code concise when performing simple assignments based on a condition.</p><h3>Logical Branching and Nesting</h3><p>You can also place if-statements inside other if-statements. This is called <strong>nesting</strong>. While powerful, nesting too deeply can make your code hard to read. It is often better to use logical operators (<code>&&</code>, <code>||</code>) to combine conditions into a single <code>if</code> statement where possible. This improves the \'Logical Branching\' of your application, making the flow of execution easier to trace for other developers.</p><blockquote>💡 <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Always use curly braces <code>{}</code> even for single-line if statements. This prevents errors if you later decide to add a second line of code to that block.</blockquote>', 'Control Flow and Conditionals', 1, '2026-03-25 09:05:51', '2026-03-25 09:05:51'),
(18, 31, 'text', '<h3>The Power of Iteration</h3><p>One of the greatest strengths of a computer is its ability to perform repetitive tasks with perfect accuracy and incredible speed. In C++, we achieve this through <strong>loops</strong>. Whether you are processing millions of bank transactions or rendering 60 frames per second in a game, loops are the engine behind the scenes.</p><h3>The For Loop</h3><p>The <code>for</code> loop is used when you know exactly how many times you want to repeat a block of code. It consists of three parts: initialization, condition, and increment/decrement.</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    std::cout << \"Iteration: \" << i << std::endl;\n}</code></pre><p>In this example, <code>int i = 0</code> sets our starting point. <code>i < 5</code> is the condition checked before every loop. <code>i++</code> updates our counter after each loop. This structure is perfect for iterating through collections of data or performing a task a specific number of times.</p><h3>While and Do-While Loops</h3><p>The <code>while</code> loop is more flexible. It continues to run as long as its condition remains true. This is ideal for situations where you don\'t know the exact number of iterations in advance, such as waiting for a user to press a specific key or reading data from a file until the end is reached.</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int energy = 100;\nwhile (energy > 0) {\n    std::cout << \"Keep running...\";\n    energy -= 20;\n}</code></pre><p>A <strong>do-while</strong> loop is a variation where the code block runs <em>at least once</em> before the condition is checked. This is commonly used for user menus where you want to show the options first and then check if the user wants to quit.</p><h3>Break and Continue</h3><p>Sometimes you need to alter the flow of a loop from the inside. The <code>break</code> statement immediately exits the loop entirely. The <code>continue</code> statement, however, skips the rest of the current iteration and jumps straight to the next check. For instance, you might use <code>continue</code> to skip processing \'negative\' values in a list while continuing to process the rest.</p><h3>Nested Loops</h3><p>Just like if-statements, loops can be nested. A loop inside a loop is often used for working with multi-dimensional data, like coordinates on a 2D grid or pixels in an image. For every single iteration of the outer loop, the inner loop completes its entire cycle. While powerful, be cautious: nested loops can significantly slow down your program if the data set is very large.</p><blockquote>💡 <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Avoid \'Infinite Loops\' by ensuring your loop condition will eventually become false. An infinite loop will cause your program to hang or crash as it consumes CPU resources indefinitely.</blockquote>', 'Loops and Iteration', 1, '2026-03-25 09:05:51', '2026-03-25 09:05:51'),
(19, 32, 'text', '<h3>Modular Programming with Functions</h3><p>As programs grow in size, putting everything inside the <code>main()</code> function becomes unmanageable. <strong>Functions</strong> allow us to break our code into smaller, reusable pieces. This follows the <strong>DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself)</strong> principle, making code easier to test, debug, and maintain.</p><h3>Defining and Calling Functions</h3><p>A function definition consists of a return type, a name, and parameters. To use a function, you \'call\' it by its name followed by parentheses. If a function doesn\'t need to return a value, we use the <code>void</code> keyword.</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">void greetUser(std::string name) {\n    std::cout << \"Hello, \" << name << \"!\";\n}\n\nint addNumbers(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}</code></pre><p>In this example, <code>greetUser</code> takes a string but returns nothing, while <code>addNumbers</code> takes two integers and returns their sum. The <code>return</code> statement sends the result back to whoever called the function.</p><h3>Local vs Global Scope</h3><p>Scope refers to where a variable is accessible. Variables declared inside a function are <strong>local</strong> to that function; they are created when the function starts and destroyed when it ends. <strong>Global</strong> variables are declared outside of all functions and can be accessed from anywhere. However, using global variables is generally discouraged because they make the program\'s state harder to track and can lead to unexpected side effects.</p><h3>Function Overloading</h3><p>C++ supports a powerful feature called <strong>Function Overloading</strong>. This allows you to have multiple functions with the same name, provided they have different parameter lists (different types or number of arguments). For example, you could have an <code>add()</code> function for integers and another <code>add()</code> function for doubles. The compiler is smart enough to know which one to use based on the data you pass in.</p><h3>Practical Use Case: Math Libraries</h3><p>Consider a simple calculator app. Instead of writing the logic for addition, subtraction, and multiplication every time a user clicks a button, you write these as separate functions. This way, if you find a bug in your \'multiply\' logic, you only have to fix it in one place, and every part of your app that uses that function will automatically be updated.</p><blockquote>💡 <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Keep your functions small and focused on a single task. If a function is doing five different things, it\'s usually better to split it into five smaller functions.</blockquote>', 'Functions and Scope', 1, '2026-03-25 09:05:51', '2026-03-25 09:05:51'),
(20, 33, 'text', '<h3>Storing Collections of Data</h3><p>Up until now, we have stored single values in individual variables. But what if you need to store the names of 100 students or the prices of 50 items? Creating 50 different variables would be inefficient. This is where <strong>Arrays</strong> come in. An array is a collection of elements of the same type stored in contiguous memory locations.</p><h3>Declaring and Accessing Arrays</h3><p>To declare an array, you specify the type, the name, and the number of elements in square brackets. In C++, array indexing starts at <strong>0</strong>. This means the first element is at index 0, the second at index 1, and so on.</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int scores[5] = {90, 85, 70, 95, 80};\nstd::cout << scores[0]; // Outputs 90\nscores[2] = 75; // Changes 70 to 75</code></pre><p>It is important to be careful with array bounds. Accessing <code>scores[10]</code> when the array only has 5 elements will result in \'undefined behavior\', which can crash your program or lead to security vulnerabilities.</p><h3>Multidimensional Arrays</h3><p>Arrays can have more than one dimension. A 2D array is like a table with rows and columns. This is commonly used for game boards (like Chess or Tic-Tac-Toe) or mathematical matrices. For example, <code>int matrix[3][3];</code> creates a 3x3 grid of integers.</p><h3>The std::string Class</h3><p>While C++ can use C-style character arrays, the modern and preferred way to handle text is the <code>std::string</code> class from the <code>&lt;string&gt;</code> library. Unlike basic arrays, <code>std::string</code> objects can grow and shrink in size automatically and come with many helpful built-in functions.</p><h3>Common String Operations</h3><p>The <code>std::string</code> class makes text manipulation easy. You can concatenate (join) strings using the <code>+</code> operator, find the length using <code>.length()</code>, or extract a portion of the text using <code>.substr()</code>.</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">std::string firstName = \"John\";\nstd::string lastName = \"Doe\";\nstd::string fullName = firstName + \" \" + lastName;\nstd::cout << \"Length: \" << fullName.length();</code></pre><p>These operations are essential for modern software, where user interaction, file handling, and web communication all rely heavily on processing text data.</p><blockquote>💡 <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> When iterating through an array, use a <code>for</code> loop. For modern C++ (C++11 and later), you can use the \'range-based for loop\': <code>for (int x : scores) { ... }</code>, which is safer and cleaner.</blockquote>', 'Arrays and String Handling', 1, '2026-03-25 09:05:51', '2026-03-25 09:05:51'),
(21, 34, 'text', '<h3>Demystifying Pointers</h3><p>Pointers are often considered the most challenging topic for C++ beginners, but they are also one of the most powerful features of the language. A <strong>pointer</strong> is simply a variable that stores the <strong>memory address</strong> of another variable. Understanding pointers is the key to mastering memory management and writing high-performance code.</p><h3>Memory Addresses and Dereferencing</h3><p>Every variable is stored at a specific location in the computer\'s RAM, identified by a hexadecimal address. You can get the address of a variable using the \'address-of\' operator (<code>&</code>). A pointer variable is declared using the asterisk (<code>*</code>).</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int var = 10;\nint* ptr = &var; // ptr now holds the address of var\n\nstd::cout << ptr;  // Prints the memory address\nstd::cout << *ptr; // Prints 10 (Dereferencing)</code></pre><p><strong>Dereferencing</strong> (using <code>*</code> on an existing pointer) allows you to access or modify the value stored at that address. It\'s like having the address of a house; dereferencing is like going inside the house to see who lives there.</p><h3>Pass by Value vs. Pass by Reference</h3><p>When you pass a variable to a function by default, C++ makes a <strong>copy</strong> of that variable (Pass by Value). If the function changes the value, the original variable remains unchanged. However, if you pass a pointer or use a reference (<code>&</code> in the parameter list), the function can modify the original variable directly. This is much more efficient for large data structures because it avoids the overhead of making copies.</p><h3>Introduction to Dynamic Memory</h3><p>Most variables we\'ve used are \'automatic\'—they are created and destroyed automatically on the <strong>Stack</strong>. However, sometimes you need to allocate memory while the program is running, based on user input. This is <strong>Dynamic Memory Allocation</strong>, and it happens on the <strong>Heap</strong>.</p><p>We use the <code>new</code> keyword to request memory and, crucially, the <code>delete</code> keyword to free that memory when we are done. Failing to use <code>delete</code> leads to a <strong>memory leak</strong>, where your program slowly consumes all available RAM.</p><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int* dynamicInt = new int; // Allocate memory on heap\n*dynamicInt = 50;\ndelete dynamicInt; // Free memory</code></pre><blockquote>💡 <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Always initialize your pointers. A pointer that doesn\'t point to anything should be set to <code>nullptr</code>. This prevents \'dangling pointers\' which can cause hard-to-find crashes.</blockquote>', 'Pointers and Memory Basics', 1, '2026-03-25 09:05:51', '2026-03-25 09:05:51'),
(22, 35, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development basics HTML CSS JavaScript</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+basics+HTML+CSS+JavaScript\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+basics+HTML+CSS+JavaScript\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+basics+HTML+CSS+JavaScript/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+basics+HTML+CSS+JavaScript/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+basics+HTML+CSS+JavaScript\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Development</h3><p>Welcome to the world of web development! Today, we will explore the three foundational technologies of the web: <strong>HTML</strong>, <strong>CSS</strong>, and <strong>JavaScript</strong>. These tools work together to create the websites and web applications we use daily.</p>\n\n<h3>HTML: The Structure of the Web</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the backbone of every webpage. It defines the structure and content using elements like headings, paragraphs, lists, and links. Here’s a basic example:</p><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My First Webpage&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello, World!&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;List item 1&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;List item 2&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code><p>Key HTML concepts include:</p><ul><li><strong>Elements:</strong> Tags like &lt;h1&gt; or &lt;p&gt; define content types.</li><li><strong>Attributes:</strong> Add extra information to elements, e.g., &lt;a href=\"https://example.com\">Link&lt;/a&gt;.</li><li><strong>Semantic Tags:</strong> Use &lt;header&gt;, &lt;nav&gt;, &lt;footer&gt; for better readability and accessibility.</li></ul>\n\n<h3>CSS: Styling the Web</h3><p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) controls the visual design of a webpage. It uses selectors to target HTML elements and apply styles. Here’s an example:</p><code>/* Internal CSS */\n&lt;style&gt;\n  body {\n    font-family: Arial;\n    background-color: #f0f0f0;\n  }\n  h1 {\n    color: blue;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code><p>Common CSS properties include:</p><ul><li><strong>Text Styling:</strong> color, font-size, font-weight.</li><li><strong>Layout:</strong> display (block/inline), margin, padding.</li><li><strong>Box Model:</strong> Understand how width, height, padding, and borders interact.</li></ul><p>CSS can be added inline (within HTML elements), internally (via &lt;style&gt; tags), or externally (via a .css file linked with &lt;link&gt;).</p>\n\n<h3>JavaScript: Making the Web Interactive</h3><p>JavaScript adds dynamic behavior to websites. It can respond to user actions, update content, and communicate with servers. Here’s a simple script:</p><code>&lt;script&gt;\n  function greet() {\n    alert(\"Welcome to Web Development!\");\n  }\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;button onclick=\"greet()\">Click Me&lt;/button&gt;</code><p>Core JavaScript concepts for beginners:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables:</strong> Store data with let, const, or var (e.g., let name = \"Alice\").</li><li><strong>Functions:</strong> Reusable blocks of code (e.g., function add(a, b) { return a + b; }).</li><li><strong>Events:</strong> Trigger actions on user input (e.g., onclick, onsubmit).</li></ul><p>JavaScript runs in the browser and works directly with HTML/CSS through the Document Object Model (DOM).</p>', 'Introduction to Web Development', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(23, 36, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development environment setup code editor git vs code</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+environment+setup+code+editor+git+vs+code\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+environment+setup+code+editor+git+vs+code\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+environment+setup+code+editor+git+vs+code/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+environment+setup+code+editor+git+vs+code/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+environment+setup+code+editor+git+vs+code\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Welcome to Day 2: Setting Up Your Web Development Environment!</h3>\n<p>Yesterday, we took our first steps into the exciting world of web development, understanding the basics of how the web works. Today, we\'re going to get practical. Before we can start writing code effectively, we need to set up our workspace – our development environment. Think of it like a carpenter needing the right tools before building a house. A well-configured environment makes coding easier, more efficient, and helps prevent common headaches.</p>\n<p>We\'ll cover three crucial components today: <strong>Code Editors</strong> (where you write your code), <strong>IDEs</strong> (more comprehensive development tools), and <strong>Version Control Systems</strong> (how you manage and track changes to your code).</p>\n\n<h3>Code Editors: Your Digital Notepad for Code</h3>\n<p>At its core, a code editor is a text editor specifically designed for writing code. While you <em>could</em> write HTML, CSS, or JavaScript in a simple text editor like Notepad, a dedicated code editor offers a multitude of features that significantly enhance your productivity and make coding a much smoother experience.</p>\n\n<h4>Why Use a Code Editor?</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Syntax Highlighting:</strong> Different parts of your code (tags, attributes, values, comments) are displayed in different colors, making it much easier to read and understand the structure of your code.</li>\n    <li><strong>Auto-completion/IntelliSense:</strong> As you type, the editor suggests possible code snippets or completes keywords, reducing typing errors and speeding up development.</li>\n    <li><strong>Error Detection:</strong> Many editors can highlight potential syntax errors as you type, helping you catch mistakes early.</li>\n    <li><strong>Extensions/Plugins:</strong> You can extend the functionality of your editor with countless add-ons for linting, formatting, debugging, language support, and much more.</li>\n    <li><strong>Integrated Terminal:</strong> Run commands directly from your editor without switching applications.</li>\n    <li><strong>Version Control Integration:</strong> Seamlessly interact with Git and other version control systems.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Popular Code Editors:</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Visual Studio Code (VS Code):</strong> This is arguably the most popular and recommended code editor for web development, especially for beginners. It\'s free, open-source, highly customizable, and has a massive ecosystem of extensions. We will focus on VS Code for this course.</li>\n    <li><strong>Sublime Text:</strong> A very fast, lightweight, and powerful editor known for its speed and sleek interface. It\'s shareware, meaning you can use it for free with occasional reminders to purchase a license.</li>\n    <li><strong>Atom:</strong> Developed by GitHub, Atom is another free and open-source editor that is highly hackable and customizable.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Action Item: Installing Visual Studio Code</h4>\n<p>Let\'s get VS Code installed on your machine:</p>\n<ol>\n    <li>Go to the official VS Code website: <a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/\" target=\"_blank\">code.visualstudio.com</a></li>\n    <li>Download the installer for your operating system (Windows, macOS, Linux).</li>\n    <li>Run the installer and follow the prompts. It\'s generally recommended to accept the default settings, including adding \"Open with Code\" context menu options.</li>\n    <li>Once installed, open VS Code. Take a moment to explore its interface.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>IDEs: Integrated Development Environments</h3>\n<p>While code editors are fantastic, some developers, especially in larger projects or specific technology stacks, opt for an <strong>Integrated Development Environment (IDE)</strong>.</p>\n\n<h4>What is an IDE?</h4>\n<p>An IDE is a more comprehensive software application that provides extensive facilities to computer programmers for software development. It typically includes:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>All the features of a code editor (syntax highlighting, auto-completion, etc.).</li>\n    <li>A debugger (for finding and fixing errors in your code).</li>\n    <li>Build automation tools (for compiling and packaging your code).</li>\n    <li>Advanced project management features.</li>\n    <li>More robust refactoring tools.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Code Editor vs. IDE:</h4>\n<p>The line between a powerful code editor with many extensions (like VS Code) and a lightweight IDE can sometimes blur. However, generally:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Code Editors:</strong> Lighter, faster, more flexible, typically focused on text editing with added features. Great for web development and scripting.</li>\n    <li><strong>IDEs:</strong> Heavier, more resource-intensive, often designed for specific languages or frameworks (e.g., Java, .NET, complex enterprise applications). They offer a more \'all-in-one\' solution out of the box.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Examples of IDEs for Web Development:</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>WebStorm:</strong> A powerful commercial IDE specifically for JavaScript, HTML, and CSS development, created by JetBrains.</li>\n    <li><strong>Visual Studio (not VS Code):</strong> Microsoft\'s full-fledged IDE, primarily used for .NET, C#, and C++ development, but can also be used for web development with specific extensions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion for Beginners:</strong> For learning modern web development, a powerful code editor like <strong>VS Code is more than sufficient and highly recommended</strong>. It offers the perfect balance of features, performance, and extensibility without the overhead of a full IDE.</p>\n\n<h3>Version Control Systems (VCS): Managing Your Code\'s History</h3>\n<p>Imagine working on a project, making changes, and then realizing you broke something crucial. Or perhaps you\'re collaborating with others, and everyone is making changes to the same files. How do you keep track of everything? That\'s where <strong>Version Control Systems (VCS)</strong> come in.</p>\n\n<h4>What is Version Control?</h4>\n<p>A VCS is a system that records changes to a file or set of files over time so that you can recall specific versions later. It\'s like having an unlimited \'undo\' button for your entire project, along with a detailed log of who changed what, when, and why.</p>\n\n<h4>Why is Version Control Essential?</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Tracking Changes:</strong> See every modification made to your code.</li>\n    <li><strong>Reverting Errors:</strong> Easily go back to a previous, working version of your code if you introduce a bug.</li>\n    <li><strong>Collaboration:</strong> Multiple developers can work on the same project simultaneously without overwriting each other\'s work.</li>\n    <li><strong>Branching:</strong> Create separate lines of development for new features or bug fixes, merging them back into the main project when ready.</li>\n    <li><strong>Backup:</strong> Your code history is stored, often remotely, providing a robust backup.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Introducing Git: The Industry Standard</h4>\n<p><strong>Git</strong> is by far the most widely used modern version control system in the world. It\'s a distributed VCS, meaning every developer\'s working copy of the code is also a complete repository that can track changes and history.</p>\n\n<h4>Basic Git Concepts:</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Repository (Repo):</strong> The project folder that Git tracks. It contains all your project files and the complete history of changes.</li>\n    <li><strong>Commit:</strong> A snapshot of your repository at a specific point in time. Each commit has a unique ID and a message describing the changes made.</li>\n    <li><strong>Branch:</strong> A separate line of development. You can create branches to work on new features or experiments without affecting the main codebase.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Action Item: Installing Git</h4>\n<p>Let\'s get Git installed on your machine:</p>\n<ol>\n    <li>Go to the official Git website: <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/\" target=\"_blank\">git-scm.com</a></li>\n    <li>Download the installer for your operating system.</li>\n    <li>Run the installer. For most users, accepting the default options is fine. Pay attention during the installation, but you typically don\'t need to change advanced settings for basic use.</li>\n    <li>After installation, open your terminal or command prompt (or the integrated terminal in VS Code) and type: <br><code>git --version</code><br>You should see the installed Git version number, confirming a successful installation.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>What about GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket?</h4>\n<p>These are web-based hosting services for Git repositories. While Git is the tool you install locally, services like GitHub provide a place to store your repositories remotely, collaborate with others, and manage your projects through a web interface. We\'ll dive deeper into using Git and GitHub in a future lesson.</p>\n\n<h3>Summary of Day 2</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ve laid the essential groundwork for your web development journey. You\'ve installed a powerful <strong>Code Editor (VS Code)</strong>, understood the difference between editors and <strong>IDEs</strong>, and set up <strong>Git</strong>, your crucial tool for version control. These tools will be your constant companions as you write, manage, and evolve your web projects.</p>\n<p>Tomorrow, we\'ll start putting these tools to use by diving into the fundamental building block of every webpage: HTML!</p>', 'Setting Up the Development Environment', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(24, 37, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: HTML document structure example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=HTML+document+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/HTML+document+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/HTML+document+structure+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/HTML+document+structure+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=HTML+document+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>HTML Tags: The Building Blocks</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) uses <strong>tags</strong> to define the structure and meaning of web content. Tags are enclosed in angle brackets and usually come in pairs: an opening tag and a closing tag. For example, <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> starts a paragraph, and <code>&lt;/p&gt;</code> ends it.</p><p>Common HTML tags include:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> for headings (e.g., <code>&lt;h1&gt;Main Title&lt;/h1&gt;</code>)</li><li><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> for paragraphs (e.g., <code>&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;</code>)</li><li><code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> for unordered and ordered lists</li><li><code>&lt;li&gt;</code> for list items (nested inside <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>)</li><li><code>&lt;a&gt;</code> for hyperlinks (e.g., <code>&lt;a href=\"https://example.com&quot;&gt;Click here&lt;/a&gt;</code>)</li></ul><p>Some tags, like <code>&lt;br&gt;</code> (line break) or <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> (image), are <strong>self-closing</strong> and do not require a closing tag.</p>\n\n<h3>Attributes: Adding Details to Tags</h3><p>Attributes provide additional information about HTML elements. They are added inside the opening tag and follow the format <code>attribute=\"value\"</code>. Common attributes include:</p><ul><li><code>href</code> in <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tags to specify the link destination</li><li><code>src</code> in <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tags to define the image source</li><li><code>alt</code> in <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tags for accessibility and SEO</li><li><code>class</code> or <code>id</code> for styling with CSS</li></ul><p>Example: <code>&lt;img src=\"photo.jpg\" alt=\"A beautiful sunset\" /&gt;</code></p><p>Boolean attributes like <code>checked</code> or <code>disabled</code> do not require a value. Example: <code>&lt;input type=\"checkbox\" checked /&gt;</code>.</p>\n\n<h3>Structuring Web Content</h3><p>Proper HTML structure ensures your website is organized, accessible, and easy to style. Start with the basic document structure:</p><pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Page Title&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This is the homepage.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre><p>Use semantic tags to group content meaningfully:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;header&gt;</code> for top navigation or site branding</li><li><code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> for menus</li><li><code>&lt;main&gt;</code> for the primary content</li><li><code>&lt;section&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> for distinct blocks of content</li><li><code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> for copyright or contact info</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code>&lt;header&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;My Website&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;nav&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;/header&gt;</code></pre><p>Always close tags properly and nest elements logically to avoid errors.</p>', 'HTML Basics', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(25, 38, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css basics tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+basics+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+basics+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Fundamentals</h3><p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is a styling language used to control the layout and appearance of web pages. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of CSS, including selectors, properties, and values. Understanding these concepts is crucial for creating visually appealing and user-friendly websites.</p><p>CSS selectors are used to target specific HTML elements on a web page. They allow you to apply styles to a particular element or group of elements. There are several types of selectors, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Element Selectors</strong>: These selectors target HTML elements by their tag name. For example, <code>p</code> selects all paragraph elements on a page.</li><li><strong>Class Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements with a specific class attribute. For example, <code>.header</code> selects all elements with the class <code>header</code>.</li><li><strong>ID Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements with a specific ID attribute. For example, <code>#nav</code> selects the element with the ID <code>nav</code>.</li></ul><p>CSS properties are used to define the styles that are applied to selected elements. Properties include things like <code>color</code>, <code>background-color</code>, <code>font-size</code>, and many more. For example:</p><p><code>h1 { color: blue; }</code> applies a blue color to all <code>h1</code> elements.</p><p>CSS values are used to specify the value of a property. Values can be keywords, numbers, lengths, percentages, or colors. For example:</p><ul><li><code>10px</code> is a length value that specifies a size of 10 pixels.</li><li><code>red</code> is a keyword value that specifies the color red.</li><li><code>50%</code> is a percentage value that specifies 50% of the parent element\'s size.</li></ul><p>Understanding how to combine selectors, properties, and values is key to creating effective CSS styles. For example:</p><p><code>.header { background-color: #f2f2f2; padding: 20px; }</code> applies a light gray background color and 20 pixels of padding to all elements with the class <code>header</code>.</p><p>Let\'s take a look at some more examples of CSS selectors, properties, and values in action:</p><ul><li><code>body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; }</code> applies the Arial font to the entire body of the HTML document.</li><li><code>a { text-decoration: none; }</code> removes the underline from all links on a page.</li><li><code>img { width: 100%; height: auto; }</code> sets the width of all images to 100% of their parent element and automatically adjusts the height to maintain the aspect ratio.</li></ul>', 'CSS Fundamentals', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(26, 39, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript</h3><p>JavaScript is a high-level, dynamic, and interpreted programming language that is used for client-side scripting on the web. It is a crucial language for web development, allowing developers to create interactive and dynamic web pages. In this lesson, we will introduce the basics of JavaScript, including variables, data types, and functions.</p><h3>Variables in JavaScript</h3><p>In JavaScript, a variable is a container that holds a value. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in a program. To declare a variable in JavaScript, you use the <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, or <code>var</code> keyword followed by the name of the variable and the assignment operator (=). For example:</p><ul><li><code>let x = 5;</code> declares a variable x and assigns it the value 5.</li><li><code>const PI = 3.14;</code> declares a constant variable PI and assigns it the value 3.14.</li><li><code>var name = \'John\';</code> declares a variable name and assigns it the value \'John\'.</li></ul><p>It\'s worth noting that <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> are block-scoped, meaning they are only accessible within the block they are declared in, while <code>var</code> is function-scoped, meaning it is accessible throughout the function it is declared in.</p><h3>Data Types in JavaScript</h3><p>JavaScript has several built-in data types, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Number</strong>: a numeric value, such as 5 or 3.14.</li><li><strong>String</strong>: a sequence of characters, such as \'hello\' or \"hello\".</li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: a true or false value.</li><li><strong>Null</strong>: a special value that represents the absence of any object value.</li><li><strong>Undefined</strong>: a special value that represents an uninitialized variable.</li><li><strong>Object</strong>: a collection of key-value pairs, such as { name: \'John\', age: 30 }.</li><li><strong>Array</strong>: a collection of values, such as [1, 2, 3] or [\'a\', \'b\', \'c\'].</li></ul><p>JavaScript is a dynamically-typed language, meaning you don\'t need to declare the data type of a variable before using it. The data type of a variable is determined at runtime, based on the value assigned to it.</p><h3>Functions in JavaScript</h3><p>A function is a block of code that can be executed multiple times from different parts of a program. Functions are useful for organizing code, reducing repetition, and making code more reusable. To declare a function in JavaScript, you use the <code>function</code> keyword followed by the name of the function and a list of parameters in parentheses. For example:</p><ul><li><code>function greet(name) { console.log(\'Hello, \' + name); }</code> declares a function greet that takes a name parameter and logs a greeting message to the console.</li><li><code>function add(x, y) { return x + y; }</code> declares a function add that takes two parameters x and y and returns their sum.</li></ul><p>Functions can be called by using the function name followed by a list of arguments in parentheses. For example:</p><ul><li><code>greet(\'John\');</code> calls the greet function with the argument \'John\'.</li><li><code>let result = add(2, 3);</code> calls the add function with the arguments 2 and 3 and assigns the result to a variable result.</li></ul>', 'Introduction to JavaScript', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(27, 40, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building a Simple Web Page</h3><p>Welcome to Day 6 of our web development course. Today, we will be learning about the basics of building a simple web page using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. By the end of this lesson, you will have a solid understanding of how to structure your web page, add styles, and make it interactive.</p><h3>HTML Structure</h3><p>HTML (Hypertext Markup Language) is the backbone of any web page. It provides the structure and content of the page. A basic HTML document consists of a series of elements, represented by tags. These tags are used to define the different parts of the page, such as headings, paragraphs, images, and links.</p><p>Here is an example of a basic HTML document:<code>&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My First Web Page&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Web Page&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This is my first web page.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></p><p>The HTML document is divided into two main parts: the <strong>head</strong> and the <strong>body</strong>. The head section contains metadata about the document, such as the title, character encoding, and links to external stylesheets or scripts. The body section contains the content of the page.</p><h3>CSS Styling</h3><p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is used to add styles to your web page. It allows you to control the layout, appearance, and behavior of the page. CSS styles can be applied to individual elements, or to groups of elements.</p><p>Here is an example of how to add CSS styles to an HTML document:<code>&lt;style&gt;\n  body {\n    background-color: #f2f2f2;\n  }\n  h1 {\n    color: #00698f;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></p><p>This code adds a style to the body element, setting the background color to a light gray. It also adds a style to the h1 element, setting the text color to a blue color.</p><h3>JavaScript Interactivity</h3><p>JavaScript is a programming language that allows you to add interactivity to your web page. It can be used to respond to user input, animate elements, and update the content of the page.</p><p>Here is an example of how to add JavaScript code to an HTML document:<code>&lt;script&gt;\n  function greet() {\n    alert(\'Hello, World!\');\n  }\n  greet();\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></p><p>This code defines a function called <strong>greet</strong>, which displays an alert box with the message \'Hello, World!\'. The function is then called, which displays the alert box.</p><h3>Key Concepts</h3><ul><li>HTML provides the structure and content of a web page</li><li>CSS is used to add styles to a web page</li><li>JavaScript is used to add interactivity to a web page</li><li>HTML documents consist of a series of elements, represented by tags</li><li>CSS styles can be applied to individual elements, or to groups of elements</li><li>JavaScript code can be used to respond to user input, animate elements, and update the content of the page</li></ul>', 'Building a Simple Web Page', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(28, 41, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development quiz review</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+quiz+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+quiz+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+quiz+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+quiz+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+quiz+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 7: Review and Quiz</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 7! Today, we will consolidate your knowledge of <strong>HTML</strong>, <strong>CSS</strong>, and <strong>JavaScript</strong> through a structured review and a short quiz. Let’s revisit the core concepts you’ve learned so far.</p>\n\n<h3>HTML Review</h3>\n<p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the foundation of web development. It structures content using <strong>tags</strong> and <strong>elements</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Basic Structure:</strong> Every HTML document starts with <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code>, followed by <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tags.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Elements:</strong> Use <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> for headings, <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> for paragraphs, and <code>&lt;a href=\"...\"&gt;</code> for hyperlinks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lists:</strong> Ordered lists use <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>, unordered lists use <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, and list items use <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>CSS Review</h3>\n<p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) controls the visual styling of HTML elements. It uses <strong>selectors</strong> and <strong>properties</strong> to define styles.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Linking CSS:</strong> Add <code>&lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"&gt;</code> in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section of your HTML file.</li>\n  <li><strong>Selectors:</strong> Element selectors (e.g., <code>p { color: blue; }</code>), class selectors (e.g., <code>.my-class { ... }</code>), and ID selectors (e.g., <code>#my-id { ... }</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Box Model:</strong> Every element has content, padding, border, and margin. Use <code>border: 1px solid black;</code> to define borders.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>JavaScript Review</h3>\n<p>JavaScript adds interactivity to websites. It uses <strong>variables</strong>, <strong>functions</strong>, and <strong>events</strong> to manipulate web pages dynamically.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Variables:</strong> Declare variables with <code>let</code> or <code>const</code>. Example: <code>let name = \"Alice\";</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Functions:</strong> Define reusable code blocks with <code>function myFunction() { ... }</code> or arrow functions <code>const add = (a, b) => a + b;</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Alerts and Logs:</strong> Use <code>alert(\"Hello!\");</code> to show popups or <code>console.log(\"Debug\");</code> for debugging.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Now that we’ve reviewed the essentials, let’s test your understanding with a quiz! 🎉</p>', 'Review and Quiz', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(29, 42, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: HTML page structure example with semantic elements</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=HTML+page+structure+example+with+semantic+elements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/HTML+page+structure+example+with+semantic+elements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/HTML+page+structure+example+with+semantic+elements/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/HTML+page+structure+example+with+semantic+elements/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=HTML+page+structure+example+with+semantic+elements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>HTML Basics</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the foundation of every webpage. It defines the structure and content using <strong>tags</strong> that browsers interpret to display text, images, and other elements. A basic HTML document starts with a <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> declaration, followed by the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> root element. Inside, the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> contains metadata like the page title, while the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> holds visible content.</p><p>Common HTML elements include:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> for headings</li><li><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> for paragraphs</li><li><code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> for unordered lists</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><code>&lt;h3&gt;Welcome&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 2&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code><h3>Semantic HTML</h3><p>Semantic HTML uses tags that clearly describe their meaning to both browsers and developers. Unlike non-semantic tags like <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, semantic elements like <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> improve <strong>accessibility</strong> and <strong>SEO</strong> by providing context about the content.</p><p>Key semantic elements include:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;header&gt;</code>: Contains introductory content</li><li><code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>: Defines navigation links</li><li><code>&lt;main&gt;</code>: Represents the primary content</li><li><code>&lt;footer&gt;</code>: Holds footer information</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><code>&lt;header&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;My Website&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;nav&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Home&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;About&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;/header&gt;</code><h3>Page Structure</h3><p>A well-structured webpage uses semantic elements to organize content logically. The <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> typically includes a <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> for navigation, a <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> for primary content, and a <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> for contact or legal information. Inside <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, elements like <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> group related content.</p><p><strong>Full Example:</strong></p><code>&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;My Blog&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;\n      &lt;ul&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Home&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Posts&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n  \n  &lt;main&gt;\n    &lt;article&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;First Post&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This is my first blog post.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/article&gt;\n    \n    &lt;aside&gt;\n      &lt;h3&gt;Categories&lt;/h3&gt;\n      &lt;ul&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Tech&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Travel&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;/aside&gt;\n  &lt;/main&gt;\n  \n  &lt;footer&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;© 2024 My Blog&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</code><p>This structure ensures clarity for developers and accessibility for users, while helping search engines understand the page\'s purpose.</p>', 'Introduction to HTML Structure', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(30, 43, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS selectors and properties examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+selectors+and+properties+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+selectors+and+properties+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+selectors+and+properties+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+selectors+and+properties+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+selectors+and+properties+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Introduction</h3>\n<p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is a stylesheet language used to control the presentation of HTML elements. It allows developers to define colors, fonts, spacing, and layout for web pages. CSS works by selecting HTML elements and applying styling rules to them.</p>\n\n<p><strong>How CSS Works:</strong> Browsers read CSS rules and apply them to matching HTML elements. CSS can be added to a webpage in three ways:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Inline:</strong> Styles applied directly to an element using the <code>style</code> attribute.<br>\n  <code>&lt;p style=\"color: blue;\">\"></code></li>\n  <li><strong>Internal:</strong> Styles defined within a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> tag in the HTML document.<br>\n  <code>&lt;style&gt;br p { color: red; } &lt;/style&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>External:</strong> Styles stored in a separate <code>.css</code> file linked via <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> in the HTML.<br>\n  <code>&lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">&lt;/code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>CSS Selectors</h3>\n<p>Selectors determine which HTML elements receive styling. Common types include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Element Selector:</strong> Targets elements by tag name.<br>\n  <code>p { font-size: 16px; }</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Class Selector:</strong> Targets elements with a specific class (starts with <code>.</code>).<br>\n  <code>.highlight { background: yellow; }</code><br>\n  <code>&lt;p class=\"highlight\">Text&lt;/p&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>ID Selector:</strong> Targets a single element with a specific ID (starts with <code>#</code>).<br>\n  <code>#header { color: white; }</code><br>\n  <code>&lt;h1 id=\"header\">Title&lt;/h1&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Attribute Selector:</strong> Targets elements with specific attributes.<br>\n  <code>input[type=\"text\"] { border: 1px solid #ccc; }</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Grouping Selector:</strong> Apply same styles to multiple selectors.<br>\n  <code>h1, h2 { font-family: Arial; }</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Universal Selector:</strong> Targets all elements (<code>*</code>).<br>\n  <code>* { margin: 0; padding: 0; }</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>CSS Properties</h3>\n<p>Properties define the styling rules applied to elements. Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Color & Background:</strong><br>\n  <code>color: #0000FF; background-color: #FFFFFF;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Font Styling:</strong><br>\n  <code>font-family: \"Times New Roman\"; font-size: 20px;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Text Formatting:</strong><br>\n  <code>text-align: center; text-decoration: underline;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Box Model Properties:</strong><br>\n  <code>margin: 10px; padding: 5px; border: 2px solid black;</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Layout Control:</strong><br>\n  <code>display: block; width: 100%;</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Best Practices:</strong> Use classes for reusable styles, avoid overusing IDs, and organize CSS for readability. Modern tools like CSS preprocessors (Sass) and frameworks (Bootstrap) build on these fundamentals.</p>', 'CSS Fundamentals and Selectors', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(31, 44, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css layout styles</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+layout+styles\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+layout+styles\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+layout+styles/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+layout+styles/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+layout+styles\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Styles and Layout</h3><p>Welcome to Day 10 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be exploring the world of CSS styles and layout. CSS, or Cascading Style Sheets, is a styling language used to control the layout and appearance of web pages. In this lesson, we will cover three important topics: the CSS Box Model, CSS Positioning, and CSS Display.</p><h3>CSS Box Model</h3><p>The CSS Box Model is a fundamental concept in web development. It is a rectangular box that wraps around every HTML element, and it consists of four main parts: <strong>content</strong>, <strong>padding</strong>, <strong>border</strong>, and <strong>margin</strong>. The content area is where the element\'s content is displayed, the padding is the space between the content and the border, the border is the visible outline of the box, and the margin is the space between the box and other elements.</p><ul><li><strong>Content</strong>: The content area is the innermost part of the box, where the element\'s content is displayed.</li><li><strong>Padding</strong>: The padding is the space between the content and the border. It can be set using the <code>padding</code> property.</li><li><strong>Border</strong>: The border is the visible outline of the box. It can be set using the <code>border</code> property.</li><li><strong>Margin</strong>: The margin is the space between the box and other elements. It can be set using the <code>margin</code> property.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use the CSS Box Model in a CSS rule: <code>.box { width: 200px; height: 100px; padding: 20px; border: 1px solid black; margin: 10px; }</code></p><h3>CSS Positioning</h3><p>CSS Positioning is used to control the position of an element on a web page. There are five main positioning schemes: <strong>static</strong>, <strong>relative</strong>, <strong>absolute</strong>, <strong>fixed</strong>, and <strong>sticky</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Static</strong>: The default positioning scheme. Elements are positioned in the normal flow of the document.</li><li><strong>Relative</strong>: Elements are positioned relative to their normal position. The <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties can be used to offset the element from its normal position.</li><li><strong>Absolute</strong>: Elements are positioned absolutely, relative to their nearest positioned ancestor. The <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties can be used to position the element.</li><li><strong>Fixed</strong>: Elements are positioned fixed, relative to the viewport. The <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties can be used to position the element.</li><li><strong>Sticky</strong>: Elements are positioned sticky, relative to their nearest positioned ancestor. The <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties can be used to position the element.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use CSS Positioning in a CSS rule: <code>.positioned { position: absolute; top: 50px; left: 100px; }</code></p><h3>CSS Display</h3><p>CSS Display is used to control the display type of an element. The most common display types are <strong>block</strong>, <strong>inline</strong>, <strong>inline-block</strong>, and <strong>none</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Block</strong>: Elements are displayed as block-level elements, taking up the full width of their parent element.</li><li><strong>Inline</strong>: Elements are displayed as inline-level elements, taking up only the space needed for their content.</li><li><strong>Inline-block</strong>: Elements are displayed as inline-level elements, but can have a width and height set.</li><li><strong>None</strong>: Elements are not displayed at all.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use CSS Display in a CSS rule: <code>.display { display: inline-block; width: 100px; height: 50px; }</code></p><p>In conclusion, the CSS Box Model, CSS Positioning, and CSS Display are all important concepts in web development. By mastering these concepts, you will be able to create complex and visually appealing web pages.</p>', 'Working with CSS Styles and Layout', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(32, 45, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript variables data types tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+variables+data+types+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+variables+data+types+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+variables+data+types+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+variables+data+types+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+variables+data+types+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 11: Introduction to JavaScript and Variables</h3>\n\n<p>Welcome to Day 11 of our Web Development journey! So far, we\'ve mastered the structure of web pages with <strong>HTML</strong> and styled them beautifully with <strong>CSS</strong>. Today, we\'re going to add the final, crucial ingredient to our web development toolkit: <strong>JavaScript</strong>. JavaScript is the programming language that brings interactivity and dynamic behavior to your websites, transforming static pages into engaging user experiences.</p>\n\n<h4>What is JavaScript?</h4>\n<p><strong>JavaScript (JS)</strong> is a lightweight, interpreted, or just-in-time compiled programming language with first-class functions. While it is most well-known as the scripting language for web pages, it\'s also used in many non-browser environments (like Node.js). On the web, JavaScript allows you to implement complex features on web pages, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Interactive maps and animations</li>\n    <li>Form validation</li>\n    <li>Dynamic content updates without reloading the page</li>\n    <li>User interface elements like dropdown menus, carousels, and pop-ups</li>\n    <li>And much more!</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Think of it this way: HTML is the noun (the content), CSS is the adjective (the presentation), and JavaScript is the verb (the action).</p>\n\n<h4>How to Include JavaScript in HTML</h4>\n<p>There are a few ways to add JavaScript to your HTML documents:</p>\n\n<h5>1. Internal JavaScript</h5>\n<p>You can embed JavaScript code directly within your HTML using the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags. This is typically placed either in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section or, more commonly, just before the closing <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code> tag. Placing it at the end of the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> ensures that the HTML content is loaded and rendered before the JavaScript tries to interact with it.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Internal JS Example&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello from HTML!&lt;/h1&gt;\n\n    &lt;script&gt;\n        // This is an internal JavaScript comment\n        console.log(\"Hello from internal JavaScript!\");\n    &lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h5>2. External JavaScript</h5>\n<p>For larger projects, it\'s best practice to keep your JavaScript code in separate <code>.js</code> files. This makes your code more organized, reusable, and easier to maintain. You link to an external JavaScript file using the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag with a <code>src</code> attribute pointing to your <code>.js</code> file.</p>\n<p>First, create a file named <code>script.js</code> (or any other name you prefer) in the same directory as your HTML file:</p>\n<pre><code>// script.js\nconsole.log(\"Hello from external JavaScript!\");</code></pre>\n<p>Then, link it in your HTML:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;External JS Example&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello from HTML!&lt;/h1&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- It\'s generally best to place script tags at the end of the body --&gt;\n    &lt;script src=\"script.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Best Practice:</strong> Always place your <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags just before the closing <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code> tag. This ensures that the HTML content is fully loaded and available for JavaScript to manipulate, preventing potential errors.</p>\n\n<h4>Your First JavaScript Code: <code>console.log()</code></h4>\n<p>The <code>console.log()</code> function is an essential tool for every JavaScript developer. It allows you to display messages, variables, or other data in your browser\'s developer console. This is incredibly useful for debugging and understanding what your code is doing.</p>\n<p>To open the developer console:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Chrome/Firefox/Edge:</strong> Right-click anywhere on the page and select \"Inspect\" or \"Inspect Element\", then navigate to the \"Console\" tab.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Try it yourself:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;\n    console.log(\"This message will appear in the console!\");\n    console.log(123);\n    console.log(true);\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Understanding Variables</h4>\n<p>In programming, a <strong>variable</strong> is like a named container or a storage location that holds a value. You can put data into this container, retrieve it, and even change the data inside it later. Variables are fundamental because they allow us to store and manage data dynamically within our programs.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Why use variables?</strong> They make your code more readable, flexible, and reusable. Instead of repeating a value multiple times, you can store it in a variable and refer to the variable. If the value needs to change, you only update it in one place.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Declaring Variables: <code>var</code>, <code>let</code>, and <code>const</code></h4>\n<p>JavaScript provides three keywords to declare variables:</p>\n\n<h5>1. <code>var</code> (The Old Way)</h5>\n<p><code>var</code> was the original way to declare variables in JavaScript. While it still works, it has some quirks (like function-scoping and hoisting) that can lead to unexpected behavior, especially in larger applications. For modern JavaScript, it\'s generally recommended to use <code>let</code> or <code>const</code> instead.</p>\n<pre><code>var userName = \"Alice\";\nconsole.log(userName); // Output: Alice\nuserName = \"Bob\"; // Can be re-assigned\nconsole.log(userName); // Output: Bob\nvar userName = \"Charlie\"; // Can be re-declared (though not recommended)\nconsole.log(userName); // Output: Charlie</code></pre>\n\n<h5>2. <code>let</code> (The Modern, Mutable Way)</h5>\n<p>Introduced in ES6 (ECMAScript 2015), <code>let</code> is the preferred way to declare variables whose values might change. It is <strong>block-scoped</strong>, meaning it\'s only accessible within the block of code (e.g., inside curly braces <code>{}</code> of an <code>if</code> statement or <code>for</code> loop) where it\'s defined.</p>\n<pre><code>let userAge = 30;\nconsole.log(userAge); // Output: 30\nuserAge = 31; // Can be re-assigned\nconsole.log(userAge); // Output: 31\n// let userAge = 32; // ERROR: Cannot re-declare \'userAge\' in the same scope</code></pre>\n\n<h5>3. <code>const</code> (The Modern, Immutable Way)</h5>\n<p>Also introduced in ES6, <code>const</code> is used to declare variables whose values are intended to remain constant (unchanged) throughout the program. Like <code>let</code>, it is <strong>block-scoped</strong>. Once a <code>const</code> variable is assigned a value, it cannot be re-assigned or re-declared.</p>\n<pre><code>const PI = 3.14159;\nconsole.log(PI); // Output: 3.14159\n// PI = 3.14; // ERROR: Assignment to constant variable.\n\nconst websiteName = \"My Awesome Site\";\nconsole.log(websiteName); // Output: My Awesome Site</code></pre>\n<p><strong>When to use what?</strong> As a general rule, use <code>const</code> by default. If you know the variable\'s value will need to change, then use <code>let</code>. Avoid <code>var</code> in new code.</p>\n\n<h5>Variable Naming Conventions</h5>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Start with a letter, <code>_</code> (underscore), or <code>$</code> (dollar sign).</strong> Cannot start with a number.</li>\n    <li><strong>Use camelCase:</strong> Start with a lowercase letter, and capitalize the first letter of subsequent words (e.g., <code>firstName</code>, <code>totalAmount</code>). This is the standard in JavaScript.</li>\n    <li><strong>Be descriptive:</strong> Choose names that clearly indicate the variable\'s purpose (e.g., <code>numberOfStudents</code> instead of <code>n</code>).</li>\n    <li><strong>Avoid reserved keywords:</strong> Don\'t use words that JavaScript uses for its own features (e.g., <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, <code>function</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Basic JavaScript Data Types</h4>\n<p>Data types classify the different kinds of values that variables can hold. JavaScript has several built-in data types, but we\'ll focus on the most common primitive types today:</p>\n\n<h5>1. String</h5>\n<p>Represents textual data. Strings are enclosed in single quotes (<code>\'</code>), double quotes (<code>\"</code>), or backticks (<code>`</code>) for template literals (which we\'ll cover later).</p>\n<pre><code>let greeting = \"Hello, world!\";\nlet message = \'This is a string.\';\nlet userName = `John Doe`; // Template literal\nconsole.log(greeting, typeof greeting); // Output: Hello, world! string</code></pre>\n\n<h5>2. Number</h5>\n<p>Represents both integer and floating-point numbers. There\'s no separate integer type in JavaScript.</p>\n<pre><code>let age = 25;\nlet price = 19.99;\nlet temperature = -5;\nconsole.log(age, typeof age); // Output: 25 number\nconsole.log(price, typeof price); // Output: 19.99 number</code></pre>\n\n<h5>3. Boolean</h5>\n<p>Represents a logical entity and can have two values: <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Booleans are fundamental for conditional logic.</p>\n<pre><code>let isActive = true;\nlet hasPermission = false;\nconsole.log(isActive, typeof isActive); // Output: true boolean</code></pre>\n\n<h5>4. Null</h5>\n<p>Represents the intentional absence of any object value. It means \"nothing\" or \"empty\" in a specific, explicit way.</p>\n<pre><code>let selectedItem = null;\nconsole.log(selectedItem, typeof selectedItem); // Output: null object (Note: typeof null is a historical bug in JS, it\'s a primitive type)</code></pre>\n\n<h5>5. Undefined</h5>\n<p>Represents a variable that has been declared but has not yet been assigned a value. It also means a function that doesn\'t return anything explicitly returns <code>undefined</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>let quantity;\nconsole.log(quantity, typeof quantity); // Output: undefined undefined\n\nlet response = undefined;\nconsole.log(response, typeof response); // Output: undefined undefined</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Summary</h4>\n<p>Today, you\'ve taken your first exciting steps into the world of JavaScript! You learned what JavaScript is, how to include it in your HTML, and how to use <code>console.log()</code> for debugging. Most importantly, you grasped the concept of variables, how to declare them using <code>var</code>, <code>let</code>, and <code>const</code>, and the basic data types they can hold: String, Number, Boolean, Null, and Undefined.</p>\n<p>This foundational knowledge is crucial for everything you\'ll do with JavaScript moving forward. Practice declaring variables and logging their values and types to the console. Experiment with changing values and observing the results. The more you practice, the more comfortable you\'ll become!</p>', 'Introduction to JavaScript and Variables', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(33, 46, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript conditional loops functions</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+conditional+loops+functions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+conditional+loops+functions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+conditional+loops+functions/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+conditional+loops+functions/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+conditional+loops+functions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Conditional Statements</h3>\n<p>Conditional statements allow your code to make decisions based on conditions. The most common structure is <code>if...else</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>if</strong>: Executes code if the condition is true.</li>\n  <li><strong>else if</strong>: Checks additional conditions.</li>\n  <li><strong>else</strong>: Executes code if none of the above conditions are true.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>\n<code>\nlet age = 18;\nif (age >= 18) {\n  console.log(\"You can vote\");\n} else {\n  console.log(\"You cannot vote\");\n}\n</code>\n<p>Use comparison operators (<code>==, !=, >, <, >=, <=</code>) and logical operators (<code>&&, ||, !</code>) to build conditions.</p>\n\n<h3>JavaScript Loops</h3>\n<p>Loops automate repetitive tasks. JavaScript supports three main types:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>for</strong>: Ideal for known iteration counts.</li>\n  <li><strong>while</strong>: Runs while a condition is true.</li>\n  <li><strong>do...while</strong>: Guarantees at least one execution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example: for loop</strong></p>\n<code>\nfor (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n  console.log(\"Iteration:\", i);\n}\n</code>\n<p><strong>Example: while loop</strong></p>\n<code>\nlet count = 0;\nwhile (count < 3) {\n  console.log(\"Count:\", count);\n  count++;\n}\n</code>\n<p>Use <code>break</code> to exit loops early and <code>continue</code> to skip iterations.</p>\n\n<h3>JavaScript Functions</h3>\n<p>Functions are reusable blocks of code. Define them using the <code>function</code> keyword or arrow syntax.</p>\n<code>\nfunction greet(name) {\n  return \"Hello, \" + name;\n}\nconsole.log(greet(\"Alice\"));\n</code>\n<p><strong>Key Concepts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Parameters</strong>: Inputs passed to a function.</li>\n  <li><strong>Return</strong>: Sends a value back to the caller.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scope</strong>: Variables declared inside a function are local.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example: Function with Default Parameters</strong></p>\n<code>\nfunction calculateArea(radius = 1) {\n  return Math.PI * radius * radius;\n}\nconsole.log(calculateArea()); // Uses default value\n</code>\n<p>Functions improve code organization and maintainability.</p>', 'JavaScript Conditional Statements and Loops', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(34, 47, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript arrays and objects visual explanation</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+arrays+and+objects+visual+explanation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+arrays+and+objects+visual+explanation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+arrays+and+objects+visual+explanation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+arrays+and+objects+visual+explanation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+arrays+and+objects+visual+explanation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Arrays</h3>\n<p>Arrays are used to store multiple values in a single variable. They are ordered collections of data, indexed by numbers starting at 0.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Creating an array:</strong> <code>let fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\'];</code></li>\n<li><strong>Accessing elements:</strong> <code>console.log(fruits[0]); // \'apple\'</code></li>\n<li><strong>Adding elements:</strong> <code>fruits.push(\'grape\'); // adds to the end</code></li>\n<li><strong>Removing elements:</strong> <code>fruits.pop(); // removes from the end</code></li>\n<li><strong>Looping through arrays:</strong>\n<code>\nfor (let i = 0; i < fruits.length; i++) {\n  console.log(fruits[i]);\n}\n</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>JavaScript Objects</h3>\n<p>Objects are unordered collections of key-value pairs. They represent real-world entities with properties and behaviors.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Creating an object:</strong>\n<code>\nlet car = {\n  brand: \'Tesla\',\n  model: \'Model S\',\n  year: 2022,\n  drive: function() { console.log(\'Driving...\'); }\n};\n</code></li>\n<li><strong>Accessing properties:</strong> <code>console.log(car.brand); // \'Tesla\'</code></li>\n<li><strong>Modifying properties:</strong> <code>car.year = 2023;</code></li>\n<li><strong>Calling methods:</strong> <code>car.drive(); // \'Driving...\'</code></li>\n<li><strong>Adding new properties:</strong> <code>car.color = \'red\';</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)</h3>\n<p>JSON is a lightweight data format used to exchange data between systems. It resembles JavaScript objects but is language-independent.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>JSON structure example:</strong>\n<code>\n{\n  \"name\": \"Alice\",\n  \"age\": 25,\n  \"isStudent\": false\n}\n</code></li>\n<li><strong>Converting JSON to JavaScript object:</strong>\n<code>\nlet data = \'{\"name\": \"Bob\"}\';\nlet obj = JSON.parse(data);\n</code></li>\n<li><strong>Converting JavaScript object to JSON:</strong>\n<code>\nlet json = JSON.stringify(car);\n</code></li>\n<li><strong>Use case:</strong> JSON is commonly used in APIs to send data to or from a server.</li>\n</ul>', 'Understanding JavaScript Arrays and Objects', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(35, 48, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript DOM tree structure</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+DOM+tree+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+DOM+tree+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+DOM+tree+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+DOM+tree+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+DOM+tree+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript DOM Manipulation and Events</h3>\n\n<h3>DOM Introduction</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Document Object Model (DOM)</strong> is a programming interface for HTML and XML documents. It represents the page as a tree of objects, where each node is an object representing a part of the document (e.g., elements, text, attributes).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Root Node:</strong> The topmost node is <code>document</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Element Nodes:</strong> Represent HTML tags like <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Text Nodes:</strong> Contain the actual text inside elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> Accessing an element by ID:</p>\n<code>const heading = document.getElementById(\'main-heading\');</code>\n<p>This retrieves the DOM node with <code>id=\"main-heading\"</code>, allowing you to manipulate it programmatically.</p>\n\n<h3>DOM Manipulation</h3>\n<p>JavaScript lets you dynamically modify content, structure, and styles of a webpage.</p>\n<h4>Changing Content</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>innerHTML</code>: Modifies HTML content (including parsing HTML strings).</li>\n  <li><code>textContent</code>: Sets or gets plain text (does not parse HTML).</li>\n</ul>\n<code>document.getElementById(\'demo\').innerHTML = \'&lt;strong&gt;New Content&lt;/strong&gt;\';</code>\n\n<h4>Creating and Appending Elements</h4>\n<code>\nconst newParagraph = document.createElement(\'p\');\nnewParagraph.textContent = \'This is a new paragraph.\';\ndocument.body.appendChild(newParagraph);\n</code>\n\n<h4>Modifying Attributes</h4>\n<code>\nconst image = document.getElementById(\'my-image\');\nimage.setAttribute(\'src\', \'new-image.jpg\');\n</code>\n\n<h4>Removing Elements</h4>\n<code>\nconst element = document.getElementById(\'remove-me\');\nelement.remove(); // Or: element.parentNode.removeChild(element);\n</code>\n\n<h3>Events</h3>\n<p>Events are actions or occurrences detected by JavaScript (e.g., clicks, keypresses). Use <code>addEventListener()</code> to respond to events.</p>\n\n<h4>Basic Event Handling</h4>\n<code>\ndocument.getElementById(\'my-button\').addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\n  alert(\'Button clicked!\');\n});\n</code>\n\n<h4>Common Events</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>click</code>: Triggered by mouse clicks.</li>\n  <li><code>load</code>: Fires when a resource (e.g., page or image) finishes loading.</li>\n  <li><code>change</code>: Used for form inputs (e.g., dropdowns, text fields).</li>\n  <li><code>keydown</code>: Fires when a key is pressed.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Event Bubbling vs Capturing</h4>\n<p>Events propagate in two phases:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Bubbling:</strong> Event travels upward from the target element to the root.</li>\n  <li><strong>Capturing:</strong> Event travels downward from the root to the target.</li>\n</ul>\n<code>\nelement.addEventListener(\'click\', handler, true); // Capturing phase\n</code>\n\n<h4>Preventing Default Actions</h4>\n<code>\nconst link = document.getElementById(\'my-link\');\nlink.addEventListener(\'click\', function(event) {\n  event.preventDefault(); // Stops the link from navigating\n});\n</code>', 'JavaScript DOM Manipulation and Events', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(36, 49, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design fundamentals media queries flexible layout examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+fundamentals+media+queries+flexible+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+fundamentals+media+queries+flexible+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+fundamentals+media+queries+flexible+layout+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+fundamentals+media+queries+flexible+layout+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+fundamentals+media+queries+flexible+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 15: Responsive Web Design Fundamentals</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 15! Today, we\'re diving into one of the most crucial aspects of modern web development: Responsive Web Design. In an era where users access websites from a vast array of devices – desktops, laptops, tablets, and smartphones – ensuring your website looks and functions beautifully on all of them is not just a good idea, it\'s a necessity.</p>\n<p>Responsive Web Design is an approach to web design that makes web pages render well on a variety of devices and screen sizes by using a mix of flexible grids and layouts, images, and an intelligent use of CSS media queries.</p>\n\n<h3>Understanding Layout and Positioning for Responsiveness</h3>\n<p>The foundation of any responsive design lies in creating layouts that can adapt. Gone are the days of fixed-width designs; we now embrace fluidity and flexibility.</p>\n\n<h4>Flexible Layouts</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Viewport:</strong> The visible area of a web page. On mobile devices, the viewport is often smaller than the desktop equivalent. We control it using the <code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"&gt;</code> tag in our HTML\'s <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. This tells browsers to set the viewport width to the device\'s width and set the initial zoom level to 1.</li>\n    <li><strong>Relative Units:</strong> Instead of fixed pixel values (<code>px</code>) for widths, heights, and font sizes, we use relative units like percentages (<code>%</code>), viewport units (<code>vw</code>, <code>vh</code>), and ems/rems (<code>em</code>, <code>rem</code>). This allows elements to scale proportionally to their parent or the viewport.</li>\n    <li><strong>Fluid Grids:</strong> Instead of fixed columns, we design layouts where columns take up a percentage of the available width. This ensures that content reflows and resizes gracefully.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Modern Layout Techniques</h4>\n<p>While we won\'t deep-dive into every property today, it\'s important to know the primary tools for flexible layouts:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Flexbox (Flexible Box Layout):</strong> A one-dimensional layout method for arranging items in rows or columns. It\'s incredibly powerful for distributing space and aligning items within a container.</li>\n    <li><strong>CSS Grid Layout:</strong> A two-dimensional layout system that allows you to design complex responsive web layouts more easily. It\'s perfect for laying out major page regions or defining the relationships between parts of a control.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Basic Positioning Concepts</h4>\n<p>While not exclusively for responsiveness, understanding how elements are positioned is key to controlling their flow and adaptability:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong><code>position: static;</code>:</strong> The default value. Elements are positioned according to the normal flow of the document.</li>\n    <li><strong><code>position: relative;</code>:</strong> The element is positioned relative to its normal position. Using <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> will offset it from where it would normally be. Crucially, it leaves a gap for its original position.</li>\n    <li><strong><code>position: absolute;</code>:</strong> The element is removed from the normal document flow and positioned relative to its closest positioned ancestor (not <code>static</code>). If no such ancestor exists, it\'s positioned relative to the initial containing block (the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element).</li>\n    <li><strong><code>position: fixed;</code>:</strong> The element is removed from the normal document flow and positioned relative to the viewport. It stays in the same place even if the page is scrolled.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Working with Media Queries</h3>\n<p>Media queries are the heart of responsive design. They allow you to apply CSS styles conditionally, based on the characteristics of the device or viewport.</p>\n\n<h4>What are Media Queries?</h4>\n<p>A media query consists of a media type (e.g., <code>screen</code>, <code>print</code>) and zero or more expressions that check for specific media features (e.g., <code>min-width</code>, <code>max-height</code>, <code>orientation</code>).</p>\n\n<h4>Syntax and Common Features</h4>\n<p>Here\'s the basic syntax:</p>\n<pre><code>@media media-type and (media-feature) {\n  /* CSS rules to apply when the condition is true */\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Some common media features:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong><code>min-width</code>:</strong> Applies styles when the viewport width is <em>at least</em> the specified value.</li>\n    <li><strong><code>max-width</code>:</strong> Applies styles when the viewport width is <em>at most</em> the specified value.</li>\n    <li><strong><code>orientation</code>:</strong> Checks if the viewport is in <code>portrait</code> or <code>landscape</code> mode.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Example: Changing Layout Based on Screen Size</h4>\n<p>Let\'s say we have a simple layout with a sidebar and main content. On small screens, we want the sidebar to stack below the main content.</p>\n<pre><code>/* Default styles (mobile-first approach) */\n.container {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: column; /* Stack vertically by default */\n}\n\n.main-content {\n  width: 100%;\n  background-color: lightblue;\n  padding: 15px;\n}\n\n.sidebar {\n  width: 100%;\n  background-color: lightcoral;\n  padding: 15px;\n}\n\n/* Media query for larger screens (e.g., tablets and desktops) */\n@media screen and (min-width: 768px) {\n  .container {\n    flex-direction: row; /* Arrange horizontally on larger screens */\n  }\n\n  .main-content {\n    width: 70%;\n  }\n\n  .sidebar {\n    width: 30%;\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>In this example, we start with a mobile-first approach, designing for smaller screens first, then using a media query to apply different styles for wider screens.</p>\n\n<h3>Responsive Image and Media Handling</h3>\n<p>Images and videos are often the biggest culprits for slow loading times and broken layouts on different devices. Handling them responsively is crucial.</p>\n\n<h4>Responsive Images</h4>\n<ol>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Simple Scaling:</strong> The most basic approach is to ensure images don\'t overflow their containers.\n        <pre><code>img {\n  max-width: 100%; /* Image will shrink to fit its container */\n  height: auto;    /* Maintain aspect ratio */\n  display: block;  /* Remove extra space below image */\n}\n</code></pre>\n        <p>This ensures images scale down but never grow beyond their original size, preventing pixelation.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong><code>srcset</code> and <code>sizes</code> attributes:</strong> For providing different image resolutions based on screen density or viewport width.\n        <pre><code>&lt;img srcset=\"small.jpg 500w, medium.jpg 1000w, large.jpg 1500w\"\n     sizes=\"(max-width: 600px) 480px, (max-width: 1200px) 800px, 1200px\"\n     src=\"medium.jpg\" alt=\"Responsive Image Example\"&gt;\n</code></pre>\n        <p>The browser will choose the most appropriate image from the <code>srcset</code> based on the <code>sizes</code> attribute (which describes the image\'s width at different viewport sizes) and the device\'s pixel density.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>The <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code> element:</strong> For \"art direction,\" where you want to display entirely different image crops or versions based on media queries (e.g., a landscape image on desktop, a portrait image on mobile).\n        <pre><code>&lt;picture&gt;\n  &lt;source media=\"(min-width: 768px)\" srcset=\"desktop-hero.jpg\"&gt;\n  &lt;source media=\"(max-width: 767px)\" srcset=\"mobile-hero.jpg\"&gt;\n  &lt;img src=\"default-hero.jpg\" alt=\"Hero Image\"&gt;\n&lt;/picture&gt;\n</code></pre>\n    </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Responsive Videos and Iframes</h4>\n<p>Embedded videos (e.g., from YouTube) and iframes often have fixed dimensions, causing them to overflow on smaller screens. A common technique is to wrap them in a container and use padding to maintain their aspect ratio.</p>\n<pre><code>.video-container {\n  position: relative;\n  padding-bottom: 56.25%; /* 16:9 aspect ratio (height / width = 9 / 16 = 0.5625) */\n  height: 0;\n  overflow: hidden;\n}\n\n.video-container iframe,\n.video-container video {\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 0;\n  left: 0;\n  width: 100%;\n  height: 100%;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>&lt;div class=\"video-container\"&gt;\n  &lt;iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/your-video-id\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This method allows the iframe/video to fill its container while the container itself maintains a responsive aspect ratio.</p>\n\n<p>Mastering these fundamentals will empower you to build websites that provide an excellent user experience across all devices. Practice using media queries and flexible layouts to see the magic of responsive design!</p>', 'Responsive Web Design Fundamentals', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(37, 50, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: HTML5 semantic elements structure example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=HTML5+semantic+elements+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/HTML5+semantic+elements+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/HTML5+semantic+elements+structure+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/HTML5+semantic+elements+structure+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=HTML5+semantic+elements+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>HTML5 Semantic Elements</h3><p>HTML5 introduced <strong>semantic elements</strong> to improve document structure and accessibility. These elements clearly describe their meaning to browsers and developers. Unlike non-semantic elements like <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, semantic elements define the purpose of content.</p><ul><li><code>&lt;header&gt;</code>: Represents introductory content or a set of navigational links.</li><li><code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>: Defines a section for navigation links.</li><li><code>&lt;section&gt;</code>: Groups thematically related content.</li><li><code>&lt;article&gt;</code>: Self-contained composition (e.g., blog post).</li><li><code>&lt;aside&gt;</code>: Sidebar content related to the main content.</li><li><code>&lt;footer&gt;</code>: Footer information like copyright or contact details.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><code>&lt;header&gt;&lt;h1&gt;My Website&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;nav&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Home&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;About&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;&lt;/header&gt;&lt;section&gt;&lt;article&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Blog Post&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Content goes here...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/article&gt;&lt;/section&gt;&lt;aside&gt;&lt;p&gt;Related links...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;&lt;footer&gt;© 2024&lt;/footer&gt;</code>', 'HTML5 and CSS3 Basics', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(38, 51, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Bootstrap vs Materialize CSS comparison</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Bootstrap+vs+Materialize+CSS+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Bootstrap+vs+Materialize+CSS+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Bootstrap+vs+Materialize+CSS+comparison/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Bootstrap+vs+Materialize+CSS+comparison/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Bootstrap+vs+Materialize+CSS+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 17: Front-End Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Today, we explore two popular front-end frameworks: <strong>Bootstrap</strong> and <strong>Materialize CSS</strong>. These tools simplify responsive design and UI component creation. We’ll cover their core features, compare their approaches, and provide hands-on examples.</p>\n\n<h3>Bootstrap Grid System</h3>\n<p>Bootstrap’s grid system is a responsive, mobile-first layout structure based on a 12-column design. It uses <code>.container</code>, <code>.row</code>, and <code>.col-</code> classes to create flexible layouts.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Containers</strong>: Wrap content in <code>.container</code> (fixed width) or <code>.container-fluid</code> (full width).</li>\n  <li><strong>Rows</strong>: <code>.row</code> holds columns and clears floats.</li>\n  <li><strong>Columns</strong>: Use <code>.col-sm-</code>, <code>.col-md-</code>, etc., to define column widths for different screen sizes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong>:</p>\n<code>&lt;div class=\"container\">\n  &lt;div class=\"row\">\n    &lt;div class=\"col-sm-6\" style=\"background: #f8f9fa; padding: 10px;\">\n      Left Column\n    &lt;/div>\n    &lt;div class=\"col-sm-6\" style=\"background: #e9ecef; padding: 10px;\">\n      Right Column\n    &lt;/div>\n  &lt;/div>\n&lt;/div></code>\n<p>This creates a two-column layout on small screens and above. On smaller devices, columns stack vertically.</p>\n\n<h3>Materialize CSS Components</h3>\n<p><strong>Materialize CSS</strong> implements Google’s Material Design principles, offering pre-styled components for modern UIs. Key components include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Buttons</strong>: Use <code>.btn</code> with modifiers like <code>.waves-effect</code> for ripple effects.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cards</strong>: <code>.card</code> containers for content with headers, images, and actions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Navigation</strong>: Pre-built navbars, dropdowns, and sidebars.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example: Button</strong></p>\n<code>&lt;a class=\"waves-effect waves-light btn\">Submit&lt;/a>\n&lt;a class=\"waves-effect waves-light btn-flat\">Cancel&lt;/a></code>\n<p><strong>Example: Card</strong></p>\n<code>&lt;div class=\"card\">\n  &lt;div class=\"card-content\">\n    &lt;span class=\"card-title\">Welcome&lt;/span>\n    &lt;p>This is a Materialize card.&lt;/p>\n  &lt;/div>\n  &lt;div class=\"card-action\">\n    &lt;a href=\"#\">Learn More&lt;/a>\n  &lt;/div>\n&lt;/div></code>\n\n<h3>Comparing Bootstrap and Materialize</h3>\n<p>Both frameworks streamline development, but they differ in design philosophy and features:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Design Language</strong>: Bootstrap uses a traditional, flexible grid, while Materialize enforces Google’s Material Design aesthetics.</li>\n  <li><strong>Component Variety</strong>: Bootstrap has more third-party plugins, but Materialize offers built-in components like parallax and modals.</li>\n  <li><strong>Customization</strong>: Bootstrap’s <code>_variables.scss</code> allows deep customization. Materialize uses CSS variables for easier theming.</li>\n  <li><strong>Performance</strong>: Materialize’s file size is slightly smaller than Bootstrap’s, but both are optimized for production.</li>\n  <li><strong>Community</strong>: Bootstrap has a larger community and more tutorials, making it beginner-friendly for general use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When to Choose Which?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use <strong>Bootstrap</strong> for projects requiring maximum flexibility and compatibility with existing tools.</li>\n  <li>Use <strong>Materialize</strong> if you want a modern, cohesive Material Design look with minimal effort.</li>\n</ul>', 'Front-End Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(39, 52, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript basics variables functions objects</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+basics+variables+functions+objects\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+basics+variables+functions+objects\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+basics+variables+functions+objects/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+basics+variables+functions+objects/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+basics+variables+functions+objects\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Basics: Variables and Data Types</h3><p>JavaScript uses <strong>variables</strong> to store data. Declare variables with <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, or <code>var</code>. <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> are preferred in modern JS.</p><ul><li><code>let age = 25;</code> - Reassignable variable</li><li><code>const name = \'Alice\';</code> - Constant value</li><li><code>var city = \'Paris\';</code> - Older variable type (avoid in new code)</li></ul><p><strong>Data types</strong> include:</p><ul><li><strong>Strings</strong>: <code>let greeting = \'Hello\';</code></li><li><strong>Numbers</strong>: <code>let price = 9.99;</code></li><li><strong>Booleans</strong>: <code>let isMember = true;</code></li><li><strong>Arrays</strong>: <code>let colors = [\'red\', \'green\'];</code></li><li><strong>Objects</strong>: <code>let user = { name: \'Bob\', id: 1 };</code></li></ul><p>Check types with <code>typeof</code>: <code>console.log(typeof age); // \'number\'</code></p><h3>Control Structures and Loops</h3><p><strong>Conditional statements</strong> execute code based on conditions:</p><pre><code>if (age >= 18) { console.log(\'Adult\'); } \nelse { console.log(\'Minor\'); }</code></pre><p><strong>Switch statements</strong> handle multiple possibilities:</p><pre><code>switch (day) { \n  case \'Monday\': console.log(\'Start of week\'); break; \n  default: console.log(\'Other day\'); }</code></pre><p><strong>Loops</strong> repeat code blocks:</p><ul><li><strong>For loop</strong>: <code>for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) { console.log(i); }</code></li><li><strong>While loop</strong>: <code>let j = 0; while (j < 3) { console.log(j); j++; }</code></li><li><strong>Do-while loop</strong>: Always runs once <code>do { ... } while (condition);</code></li></ul><h3>Functions and Objects</h3><p><strong>Functions</strong> execute reusable code:</p><pre><code>function add(a, b) { return a + b; }\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // 5</code></pre><p><strong>Arrow functions</strong> (modern syntax): <code>const multiply = (a, b) => a * b;</code></p><p><strong>Objects</strong> store key-value pairs:</p><pre><code>const book = { \n  title: \'JavaScript Guide\', \n  read: function() { console.log(\'Reading...\'); } \n}; \nconsole.log(book.title); // \'JavaScript Guide\' \nbook.read(); // Calls the method</code></pre><p>Use <strong>dot notation</strong> (<code>object.key</code>) or <strong>bracket notation</code> (<code>object[\'key\']</code>) to access properties.</p>', 'JavaScript Basics', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(40, 53, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: front end development tools vs code chrome devtools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=front+end+development+tools+vs+code+chrome+devtools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/front+end+development+tools+vs+code+chrome+devtools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/front+end+development+tools+vs+code+chrome+devtools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/front+end+development+tools+vs+code+chrome+devtools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=front+end+development+tools+vs+code+chrome+devtools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Welcome to Day 19: Mastering Front-End Development Tools!</h3>\n<p>As you progress in your journey to become a proficient front-end developer, writing code is only half the battle. The other half involves using powerful tools that streamline your workflow, help you debug issues, and ensure your code is robust and efficient. Today, we\'ll dive into some of the most essential tools in a front-end developer\'s arsenal: Visual Studio Code extensions and Chrome DevTools.</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Studio Code Extensions: Supercharging Your Editor</h3>\n<p>Visual Studio Code (VS Code) is arguably the most popular code editor for web development, and a big reason for its popularity is its vast ecosystem of extensions. Extensions are add-ons that enhance VS Code\'s functionality, making you more productive and your code better.</p>\n\n<h4>How to Install Extensions:</h4>\n<p>It\'s simple! Open VS Code, click on the <strong>Extensions icon</strong> in the Activity Bar on the side (it looks like four squares, one detached), or press <code>Ctrl+Shift+X</code> (Windows/Linux) / <code>Cmd+Shift+X</code> (macOS). Then, search for the extension by name and click \'Install\'.</p>\n\n<h4>Essential Extensions for Beginners:</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Live Server:</strong>\n        <p>This is a must-have for front-end development. Instead of manually refreshing your browser every time you make a change to your HTML, CSS, or JavaScript files, Live Server launches a local development server with live reload capability. Save your file, and your browser automatically updates!</p>\n        <p><strong>How to use:</strong> Right-click on an HTML file in the VS Code Explorer and select \'Open with Live Server\', or click the \'Go Live\' button in the status bar at the bottom right.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Prettier - Code formatter:</strong>\n        <p>Maintaining consistent code style across a project (or even just your own files) can be tedious. Prettier automatically formats your JavaScript, HTML, CSS, and more, ensuring a consistent and readable code style. It saves you time and helps avoid style-related arguments in teams.</p>\n        <p><strong>How to use:</strong> Install it, then you can set it to format on save (recommended) by going to <code>File &gt; Preferences &gt; Settings</code>, searching for \'Format On Save\', and checking the box. You might also need to set Prettier as your default formatter.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>HTML CSS Support / IntelliSense for CSS class names in HTML:</strong>\n        <p>These extensions provide intelligent code completion for HTML and CSS. When you\'re typing CSS properties or HTML tags, they offer suggestions, saving you keystrokes and preventing typos. IntelliSense for CSS class names, specifically, provides autocompletion for CSS class definitions found in your workspace.</p>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Chrome DevTools: Your Browser\'s Secret Weapon</h3>\n<p>Chrome DevTools is a set of web developer tools built directly into the Google Chrome browser. They allow you to inspect and modify HTML and CSS on the fly, debug JavaScript, analyze network activity, and much more. Learning to use DevTools effectively is crucial for front-end debugging and optimization.</p>\n\n<h4>How to Open DevTools:</h4>\n<p>There are several ways:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Right-click anywhere on a webpage and select <strong>\'Inspect\'</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Press <code>F12</code>.</li>\n    <li>Press <code>Ctrl+Shift+I</code> (Windows/Linux) or <code>Cmd+Option+I</code> (macOS).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Key DevTools Features:</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Elements Panel:</strong>\n        <p>This panel allows you to inspect and modify the HTML and CSS of any webpage in real-time. You can see the full DOM tree, select any element, and view/edit its applied CSS styles. This is incredibly useful for testing out CSS changes without modifying your source code.</p>\n        <p><strong>Example:</strong> Select an element, and in the \'Styles\' sub-panel, you can uncheck CSS properties, change values, or add new rules to see how they affect the layout instantly.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Console Panel:</strong>\n        <p>The Console is your direct line to the JavaScript running on the page. It\'s used for logging information, reporting errors, and running JavaScript commands directly. It\'s an indispensable tool for debugging.</p>\n        <p><strong>Using <code>console.log()</code>:</strong></p>\n        <p>In your JavaScript code, you can use <code>console.log()</code> to output values, messages, or objects to the console. This helps you understand the flow of your program and the state of your variables at different points.</p>\n        <pre><code>let userName = \"Alice\";\nconsole.log(\"Hello, \" + userName + \"!\");\n\nlet numbers = [10, 20, 30];\nconsole.log(numbers);\n\nfunction add(a, b) {\n    console.log(\"Adding \" + a + \" and \" + b);\n    return a + b;\n}\nadd(5, 7);</code></pre>\n        <p>You can also use <code>console.warn()</code> for warnings, <code>console.error()</code> for errors, and <code>console.info()</code> for informational messages, which appear with different icons/colors in the console.</p>\n        <p><strong>Running JavaScript in the Console:</strong> You can type and execute JavaScript code directly into the console. This is great for testing small snippets, manipulating the page, or checking variable values.</p>\n        <pre><code>document.body.style.backgroundColor = \'lightblue\';\nlet myVar = 10;\nmyVar * 2; // Output: 20</code></pre>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Sources Panel (The Debugger):</strong>\n        <p>While <code>console.log()</code> is great for simple checks, the debugger in the Sources panel provides a more powerful way to step through your JavaScript code line by line, inspect variables, and understand the execution flow.</p>\n        <p><strong>Setting Breakpoints:</strong> A breakpoint is a deliberate stopping point in your code. When the JavaScript execution reaches a breakpoint, it pauses, allowing you to examine the program\'s state.</p>\n        <p><strong>How to set:</strong> In the Sources panel, navigate to your JavaScript file. Click on the line number where you want the execution to pause. A blue marker will appear.</p>\n        <p><strong>Debugger Controls:</strong> Once paused at a breakpoint, you\'ll see controls:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Resume script execution (Play icon):</strong> Continues execution until the next breakpoint or the end of the script.</li>\n            <li><strong>Step over next function call (Arrow over):</strong> Executes the current line and moves to the next, stepping over any function calls on that line.</li>\n            <li><strong>Step into next function call (Arrow down):</strong> If the current line involves a function call, it steps into that function\'s code.</li>\n            <li><strong>Step out of current function (Arrow up):</strong> Jumps out of the current function and continues execution at the point where the function was called.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <p><strong>Inspecting Variables (Scope):</strong> When paused, the \'Scope\' pane (usually on the right) shows you the values of local and global variables, helping you track how data changes throughout your program.</p>\n        <p><strong>Call Stack:</strong> The \'Call Stack\' pane shows you the sequence of function calls that led to the current point of execution, which is invaluable for understanding how your code arrived at a particular state.</p>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ve learned about essential tools that will significantly boost your productivity and problem-solving skills as a front-end developer. VS Code extensions like Live Server and Prettier simplify your coding environment, while Chrome DevTools\' Elements, Console, and Sources panels provide unparalleled power for inspecting, debugging, and understanding your web applications. Practice using these tools regularly; they are your best friends in the world of web development!</p>', 'Front-End Development Tools', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(41, 54, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Git version control workflow</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Git+version+control+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Git+version+control+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Git+version+control+workflow/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Git+version+control+workflow/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Git+version+control+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 20: Version Control with Git</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 20 of our web development journey! Today, we\'re diving into an absolutely essential tool for any developer: <strong>Version Control with Git</strong>. Git is a powerful system that helps you track changes to your code, collaborate with others, and revert to previous versions if something goes wrong. Mastering Git is a fundamental skill that will save you countless headaches and make you a more efficient developer.</p>\n\n<h4>What is Version Control?</h4>\n<p>Imagine working on a large project. You make changes, your teammate makes changes, and suddenly, you\'re not sure which version is the latest or who changed what. Version control systems (VCS) like Git solve this problem by providing a history of every change made to your project, allowing you to:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Track changes:</strong> See what was changed, when, and by whom.</li>\n    <li><strong>Collaborate:</strong> Work on the same project with multiple people without overwriting each other\'s work.</li>\n    <li><strong>Revert:</strong> Easily go back to an earlier, stable version of your code.</li>\n    <li><strong>Experiment:</strong> Create separate \"branches\" to try out new features without affecting the main project.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>1. Initializing a Git Repository</h3>\n<p>The first step to using Git for any project is to initialize a Git repository. This tells Git to start tracking changes in your project directory. You\'ll typically do this once per project.</p>\n\n<h4>Checking Git Installation</h4>\n<p>Before we begin, ensure Git is installed on your system. Open your terminal or command prompt and type:</p>\n<pre><code>git --version</code></pre>\n<p>If you see a version number, Git is installed. If not, please refer to the official Git website (<a href=\"https://git-scm.com/downloads\" target=\"_blank\">git-scm.com/downloads</a>) for installation instructions.</p>\n\n<h4>Creating a New Repository</h4>\n<p>Navigate to your project folder using the terminal. For example, if you have a folder named <code>my-website</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>cd my-website</code></pre>\n<p>Once inside your project folder, initialize the Git repository:</p>\n<pre><code>git init</code></pre>\n<p>You should see a message like <code>Initialized empty Git repository in /path/to/my-website/.git/</code>. This creates a hidden <code>.git</code> directory inside your project, which is where Git stores all its tracking information.</p>\n\n<h4>Checking Repository Status</h4>\n<p>After initializing, it\'s good practice to check the status of your repository:</p>\n<pre><code>git status</code></pre>\n<p>Initially, Git will tell you that you\'re on the <code>main</code> (or <code>master</code>) branch and that there are no commits yet. It will also list any untracked files in your directory.</p>\n\n<h4>Ignoring Unnecessary Files with <code>.gitignore</code></h4>\n<p>Not all files in your project need to be tracked by Git. For example, temporary files, build outputs, or sensitive configuration files should often be ignored. You can tell Git to ignore these files by creating a file named <code>.gitignore</code> in the root of your project directory.</p>\n<p>Open <code>.gitignore</code> (create it if it doesn\'t exist) and add patterns for files/folders you want to ignore. Each line is a pattern:</p>\n<pre><code># Example .gitignore content\nnode_modules/\n.env\n*.log\nbuild/\n.DS_Store</code></pre>\n<p>After creating or modifying <code>.gitignore</code>, run <code>git status</code> again. You\'ll notice that the ignored files no longer appear as untracked.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Committing and Pushing Changes</h3>\n<p>Now that our repository is initialized, let\'s learn how to save our work and share it with others.</p>\n\n<h4>The Git Workflow: Add, Commit, Push</h4>\n<p>Git uses a three-stage process for saving changes:</p>\n<ol>\n    <li><strong>Working Directory:</strong> Where you make changes to your files.</li>\n    <li><strong>Staging Area (Index):</strong> A temporary area where you select which changes you want to include in your next commit.</li>\n    <li><strong>Local Repository:</strong> Where your committed changes are permanently stored on your computer.</li>\n    <li><strong>Remote Repository:</strong> A copy of your repository hosted online (e.g., GitHub, GitLab), allowing for collaboration and backup.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Staging Changes with <code>git add</code></h4>\n<p>After making changes to your files, you need to tell Git which changes you want to save. This is done by adding them to the staging area:</p>\n<pre><code># To stage a specific file:\ngit add index.html\n\n# To stage multiple files:\ngit add index.html style.css\n\n# To stage all changes in the current directory (be careful with this!):\ngit add .</code></pre>\n<p>After staging, run <code>git status</code> again. You\'ll see your files listed under \"Changes to be committed.\"</p>\n\n<h4>Committing Changes with <code>git commit</code></h4>\n<p>Once changes are staged, you can commit them to your local repository. A commit is like taking a snapshot of your project at a specific point in time. Each commit requires a message describing the changes made.</p>\n<pre><code>git commit -m \"Initial commit: Added basic HTML structure\"</code></pre>\n<p>Good commit messages are concise, descriptive, and explain <strong>what</strong> was changed and <strong>why</strong>. After committing, <code>git status</code> will show that your working tree is clean.</p>\n\n<h4>Connecting to a Remote Repository (e.g., GitHub)</h4>\n<p>To share your code or back it up online, you\'ll connect your local repository to a remote one. We\'ll use GitHub as an example. First, create a new empty repository on GitHub (don\'t initialize it with a README or license).</p>\n<p>Once created, GitHub will provide commands to link your local repo. It usually looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code>git remote add origin https://github.com/your-username/your-repo-name.git</code></pre>\n<p><code>origin</code> is the conventional name for your primary remote repository.</p>\n\n<h4>Pushing Changes with <code>git push</code></h4>\n<p>Finally, to send your committed changes from your local repository to the remote repository (e.g., GitHub), you use <code>git push</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>git push -u origin main</code></pre>\n<ul>\n    <li><code>-u</code> (or <code>--set-upstream</code>) tells Git to remember that your local <code>main</code> branch is connected to the <code>main</code> branch on <code>origin</code>. You only need to do this the first time you push a new branch.</li>\n    <li>Subsequent pushes to the same branch can simply be <code>git push</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You may be prompted for your GitHub username and password/personal access token.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Branching and Merging</h3>\n<p>Branching is one of Git\'s most powerful features. It allows you to diverge from the main line of development and continue working without messing up the main project. This is crucial for developing new features, fixing bugs, or experimenting.</p>\n\n<h4>Understanding Branches</h4>\n<p>Think of the <code>main</code> (or <code>master</code>) branch as the stable, production-ready version of your project. When you want to add a new feature, you create a new branch from <code>main</code>. You work on that feature in isolation on your new branch. Once the feature is complete and tested, you merge it back into <code>main</code>.</p>\n\n<h4>Listing Branches</h4>\n<p>To see all branches in your local repository:</p>\n<pre><code>git branch</code></pre>\n<p>The currently active branch will have an asterisk next to it.</p>\n\n<h4>Creating a New Branch</h4>\n<p>To create a new branch (e.g., for a new feature called <code>add-contact-form</code>):</p>\n<pre><code>git branch add-contact-form</code></pre>\n<p>This command only creates the branch; it doesn\'t switch to it.</p>\n\n<h4>Switching Branches</h4>\n<p>To switch to an existing branch:</p>\n<pre><code>git checkout add-contact-form</code></pre>\n<p>You can also create and switch to a new branch in one command:</p>\n<pre><code>git checkout -b new-feature-branch</code></pre>\n<p>Now, any changes you make and commit will be on <code>new-feature-branch</code>, not <code>main</code>.</p>\n\n<h4>Merging Branches</h4>\n<p>Once you\'ve completed your work on a feature branch (e.g., <code>add-contact-form</code>) and committed all your changes, you\'ll want to integrate those changes back into your <code>main</code> branch.</p>\n<ol>\n    <li><strong>Switch to the target branch:</strong> First, switch to the branch you want to merge <em>into</em> (usually <code>main</code>).</li>\n    <pre><code>git checkout main</code></pre>\n    <li><strong>Merge the feature branch:</strong> Now, merge your feature branch into <code>main</code>.</li>\n    <pre><code>git merge add-contact-form</code></pre>\n</ol>\n<p>Git will attempt to combine the changes. If there are no conflicting changes (i.e., different changes to the same line of code in both branches), Git will perform a \"fast-forward\" merge or a 3-way merge automatically. If there are conflicts, Git will pause the merge and ask you to resolve them manually. Git will mark the conflicting sections in the files, and you\'ll need to edit them to choose which changes to keep, then <code>git add</code> and <code>git commit</code> the resolved files.</p>\n\n<h4>Deleting Branches</h4>\n<p>After a feature branch has been successfully merged into <code>main</code> and you no longer need it, you can delete it:</p>\n<pre><code>git branch -d add-contact-form</code></pre>\n<p>Use <code>-D</code> (uppercase) if you want to force delete a branch that hasn\'t been merged yet (use with caution!).</p>\n\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ve learned the fundamental operations of Git: initializing repositories, staging and committing changes, pushing to a remote, and the powerful concepts of branching and merging. This is just the beginning of your Git journey, but these core commands will enable you to manage your projects effectively, collaborate with others, and maintain a robust history of your work. Practice these commands regularly, and Git will become an indispensable part of your development workflow!</p>\n', 'Version Control with Git', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(42, 55, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web security concepts diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+security+concepts+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+security+concepts+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+security+concepts+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+security+concepts+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+security+concepts+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Common Web Attacks</h3><p>Web applications face security threats from malicious actors. Three major attacks include:</p><ul><li><strong>SQL Injection</strong>: Attackers inject malicious SQL code into input fields to manipulate databases. Example: <code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = \'admin\' --\' AND password = \'...\'</code> bypasses authentication.</li><li><strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</strong>: Malicious scripts are injected into trusted websites. Example: <code>&lt;script&gt;alert(\'Stolen cookies!\')&lt;/script&gt;</code> steals user data.</li><li><strong>Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)</strong>: Users are tricked into performing unintended actions. Example: A forged request to transfer funds from their account.</li></ul><p>Prevention: Always sanitize inputs, use parameterized queries for databases, and implement CSRF tokens.</p>\n\n<h3>Using HTTPS and SSL Certificates</h3><p>HTTPS encrypts data between browsers and servers using SSL/TLS certificates. Key concepts:</p><ul><li><strong>SSL/TLS</strong>: Protocols that authenticate servers and encrypt data. Certificates are issued by Certificate Authorities (CAs).</li><li><strong>How HTTPS works</strong>:<ol><li>Browser requests HTTPS URL</li><li>Server sends SSL certificate</li><li>Browser verifies certificate validity</li><li>Encrypted connection is established</li></ol></li><li><strong>Obtaining SSL certificates</strong>: Use free services like Let\'s Encrypt or purchase from CAs. Most hosting platforms provide automated installation.</li></ul><p>Always enforce HTTPS with <code>HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS)</code> headers.</p>\n\n<h3>Validating User Input</h3><p>Invalid input is the root of many security issues. Best practices:</p><ul><li><strong>Client-Side Validation</strong>: Immediate feedback using JavaScript. Example: <code>if (!email.includes(\'@\')) { alert(\'Invalid email\') }</code></li><li><strong>Server-Side Validation</strong>: Final security layer. PHP example: <code>filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)</code></li><li><strong>Sanitization</strong>: Remove dangerous characters. PHP: <code>htmlspecialchars($user_input)</code></li></ul><p>Use frameworks like Express.js (Node.js) or Django (Python) that provide built-in validation tools.</p>', 'Web Security Fundamentals', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(43, 56, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Node.js Express.js server-side routing</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Node.js+Express.js+server-side+routing\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Node.js+Express.js+server-side+routing\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Node.js+Express.js+server-side+routing/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Node.js+Express.js+server-side+routing/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Node.js+Express.js+server-side+routing\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Welcome to Day 22: Intro to Backend Development with Node.js!</h3>\n<p>Congratulations on making it this far in your web development journey! Up until now, we\'ve primarily focused on the <strong>frontend</strong>—the part of the website users interact with directly in their browser (HTML, CSS, JavaScript). Today, we\'re taking a significant step into the world of <strong>backend development</strong>. The backend is the server-side of a website, responsible for storing and organizing data, and serving that data to the frontend.</p>\n<p>For our introduction to backend development, we\'ll be using <strong>Node.js</strong> and the <strong>Express.js</strong> framework. This powerful combination allows us to build robust and scalable web applications using JavaScript, the language you already know!</p>\n\n<h3>What is Node.js? The Basics</h3>\n<p><strong>Node.js</strong> is an open-source, cross-platform JavaScript runtime environment. What does that mean?</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>JavaScript Runtime:</strong> Traditionally, JavaScript could only run in a web browser. Node.js allows you to run JavaScript code outside of a browser, directly on a server, on your local machine, or even on embedded devices.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>Built on Chrome\'s V8 Engine:</strong> Node.js uses the same high-performance V8 JavaScript engine that powers Google Chrome. This makes Node.js incredibly fast.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>Non-blocking, Event-driven I/O:</strong> This is a core concept of Node.js. It means Node.js can handle many operations concurrently without waiting for one operation to complete before starting another. This makes it very efficient for I/O-heavy applications like web servers.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>JavaScript Everywhere:</strong> One of the biggest advantages for you as a web developer is that you can now use JavaScript for both your frontend and backend. This streamlines development, reduces context switching, and allows for code sharing.</p>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Installing Node.js and Your First Script</h4>\n<p>Before we dive in, you need to have Node.js installed on your system. If you haven\'t already, download and install it from the official website: <a href=\"https://nodejs.org/en/download\" target=\"_blank\">nodejs.org</a>. The installation package usually includes <strong>npm</strong> (Node Package Manager), which we\'ll use extensively.</p>\n<p>To verify your installation, open your terminal or command prompt and type:</p>\n<pre><code>node -v\nnpm -v</code></pre>\n<p>You should see the installed versions of Node.js and npm.</p>\n\n<p>Let\'s write a very simple Node.js script. Create a file named <code>hello.js</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>// hello.js\nconsole.log(\"Hello from Node.js!\");\n\nconst sum = (a, b) => a + b;\nconsole.log(\"2 + 3 =\", sum(2, 3));</code></pre>\n<p>Now, run this script using Node.js:</p>\n<pre><code>node hello.js</code></pre>\n<p>You should see the output in your terminal. This demonstrates that Node.js can execute JavaScript files directly.</p>\n\n<h3>Express.js Setup: Building a Web Server</h3>\n<p>While Node.js can create a server on its own, it can be quite verbose for common web tasks. This is where <strong>Express.js</strong> comes in. Express is a minimal and flexible Node.js web application framework that provides a robust set of features for web and mobile applications.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>Simplifies Server Creation:</strong> Express provides an easy way to set up routes, handle HTTP requests, and send responses.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>Middleware:</strong> It supports middleware functions that can execute code, make changes to request and response objects, and end the request-response cycle.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>Routing:</strong> Express offers a powerful routing system to define how your application responds to different client requests to specific endpoints.</p>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Setting Up Your First Express Project</h4>\n<p>Let\'s create a new project and install Express:</p>\n<ol>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>Create a project directory:</strong></p>\n        <pre><code>mkdir my-express-app\ncd my-express-app</code></pre>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>Initialize a new Node.js project:</strong> This creates a <code>package.json</code> file, which keeps track of your project\'s metadata and dependencies.</p>\n        <pre><code>npm init -y</code></pre>\n        <p>The <code>-y</code> flag answers \"yes\" to all prompts, creating a default <code>package.json</code>.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <p><strong>Install Express.js:</strong></p>\n        <pre><code>npm install express</code></pre>\n        <p>This command downloads the Express package and its dependencies and adds them to a <code>node_modules</code> folder. It also updates your <code>package.json</code> with Express as a dependency.</p>\n    </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Basic Express Server Code</h4>\n<p>Now, create a file named <code>app.js</code> (or <code>index.js</code>) in your <code>my-express-app</code> directory:</p>\n<pre><code>// app.js\nconst express = require(\'express\'); // Import the Express library\nconst app = express();            // Create an Express application instance\nconst port = 3000;                // Define the port your server will listen on\n\n// Define a route for the root URL (\'/\')\n// When a GET request comes to \'/\', execute this function\napp.get(\'/\', (req, res) => {\n  res.send(\'Hello from your first Express server!\'); // Send a response back to the client\n});\n\n// Start the server and listen for incoming requests on the specified port\napp.listen(port, () => {\n  console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}`);\n});</code></pre>\n\n<p>To run your Express server, open your terminal in the <code>my-express-app</code> directory and type:</p>\n<pre><code>node app.js</code></pre>\n<p>You should see the message: <code>Server running at http://localhost:3000</code>. Now, open your web browser and navigate to <code>http://localhost:3000</code>. You should see \"Hello from your first Express server!\" displayed in your browser. Congratulations, you\'ve just built and run your first backend server!</p>\n\n<h3>Server-side Routing</h3>\n<p><strong>Routing</strong> is the process of determining how an application responds to a client request to a particular endpoint, which is a URI (or path) and a specific HTTP request method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.).</p>\n<p>Express makes routing incredibly simple using methods like <code>app.get()</code>, <code>app.post()</code>, <code>app.put()</code>, and <code>app.delete()</code>, corresponding to the common HTTP methods.</p>\n\n<h4>Defining Multiple Routes</h4>\n<p>Let\'s add more routes to our <code>app.js</code> file:</p>\n<pre><code>// app.js (continued)\nconst express = require(\'express\');\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.get(\'/\', (req, res) => {\n  res.send(\'<h1>Welcome to the Homepage!</h1><p>Explore our site.</p>\');\n});\n\napp.get(\'/about\', (req, res) => {\n  res.send(\'<h2>About Us</h2><p>We are a team of passionate web developers.</p>\');\n});\n\napp.get(\'/contact\', (req, res) => {\n  res.send(\'<h2>Contact Us</h2><p>Email: info@example.com</p>\');\n});\n\n// A route for handling requests to any other path (404 Not Found)\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n    res.status(404).send(\'<h1>404 Not Found</h1><p>Sorry, the page you are looking for does not exist.</p>\');\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () => {\n  console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}`);\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Restart your server (<code>Ctrl+C</code> then <code>node app.js</code>) and visit:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><code>http://localhost:3000/</code></li>\n    <li><code>http://localhost:3000/about</code></li>\n    <li><code>http://localhost:3000/contact</code></li>\n    <li><code>http://localhost:3000/nonexistent</code> (to see the 404 page)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each URL path now triggers a different function and sends a different response.</p>\n\n<h4>Route Parameters</h4>\n<p>Often, you\'ll need to create routes that can accept variable parts, like a user ID or a product ID. Express handles this with <strong>route parameters</strong>.</p>\n<pre><code>// app.js (continued, add this before the 404 handler)\n\napp.get(\'/users/:id\', (req, res) => {\n  const userId = req.params.id; // Access the \'id\' parameter from the URL\n  res.send(`<h2>User Profile</h2><p>Displaying profile for user with ID: <strong>${userId}</strong></p>`);\n});\n\napp.get(\'/products/:category/:productId\', (req, res) => {\n    const category = req.params.category;\n    const productId = req.params.productId;\n    res.send(`<h3>Product Details</h3><p>Category: ${category}, Product ID: ${productId}</p>`);\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Restart your server and try visiting:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><code>http://localhost:3000/users/123</code></li>\n    <li><code>http://localhost:3000/users/john_doe</code></li>\n    <li><code>http://localhost:3000/products/electronics/laptop-x200</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <code>req.params</code> object contains properties mapped to the named route parameters. This is incredibly useful for fetching specific data from a database based on the URL.</p>\n\n<h4>Query Parameters (Brief Mention)</h4>\n<p>Besides route parameters, you might encounter <strong>query parameters</strong>, which are appended to the URL after a question mark (<code>?</code>), like <code>/search?q=nodejs&amp;sort=new</code>. You can access these using <code>req.query</code> in Express.</p>\n<pre><code>// Example of accessing query parameters\napp.get(\'/search\', (req, res) => {\n  const searchTerm = req.query.q;\n  const sortBy = req.query.sort;\n  res.send(`Searching for: <strong>${searchTerm}</strong>, sorted by: <strong>${sortBy}</strong>`);\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Visit <code>http://localhost:3000/search?q=express&amp;sort=popularity</code> to see this in action.</p>\n\n<h3>Summary and Next Steps</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ve taken your first exciting steps into backend development! You learned:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>What <strong>Node.js</strong> is and why it\'s a powerful runtime for JavaScript outside the browser.</li>\n    <li>How to set up a basic web server using the <strong>Express.js</strong> framework.</li>\n    <li>How to define different <strong>server-side routes</strong> to handle various URL requests.</li>\n    <li>How to use <strong>route parameters</strong> to create dynamic URLs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is just the beginning. In future lessons, we\'ll dive deeper into handling different types of HTTP requests (POST, PUT, DELETE), processing form data, connecting to databases, and much more. For now, experiment with creating more routes and understanding how your browser interacts with your server!</p>\n', 'Intro to Backend Development with Node.js', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(44, 57, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: MongoDB Mongoose Node.js database connection CRUD operations</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=MongoDB+Mongoose+Node.js+database+connection+CRUD+operations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/MongoDB+Mongoose+Node.js+database+connection+CRUD+operations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/MongoDB+Mongoose+Node.js+database+connection+CRUD+operations/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/MongoDB+Mongoose+Node.js+database+connection+CRUD+operations/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=MongoDB+Mongoose+Node.js+database+connection+CRUD+operations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Welcome to Day 23: Connecting to a Database with MongoDB!</h3><p>Today is a monumental day in your web development journey! Up until now, you\'ve been building applications that store data temporarily in memory or perhaps in simple JSON files. While great for learning, real-world applications need a robust, persistent way to store information. That\'s where databases come in! Today, we\'ll dive into MongoDB, a popular NoSQL database, and learn how to connect our Node.js applications to it using Mongoose.</p><h3>1. Understanding and Installing MongoDB</h3><p><strong>What is MongoDB?</strong></p><ul><li>MongoDB is a powerful, open-source <strong>NoSQL database</strong>. Unlike traditional relational databases (like MySQL or PostgreSQL) that use tables and rows, MongoDB is a <strong>document-oriented database</strong>.</li><li>It stores data in flexible, JSON-like documents called <strong>BSON</strong> (Binary JSON). This means your data doesn\'t need a predefined schema, making it highly adaptable to changing application requirements.</li><li>It\'s excellent for handling large volumes of unstructured or semi-structured data and is a favorite for modern web applications due to its scalability and flexibility.</li></ul><p><strong>Installation (Local Setup):</strong></p><p>For a beginner, the most straightforward way to get started is to install MongoDB locally on your machine. The steps vary slightly depending on your operating system (Windows, macOS, Linux), but the general process involves:</p><ol><li><strong>Download MongoDB Community Server:</strong> Visit the official MongoDB website (<a href=\"https://www.mongodb.com/try/download/community\" target=\"_blank\">www.mongodb.com/try/download/community</a>) and download the appropriate version for your OS.</li><li><strong>Follow Installation Instructions:</strong> The official documentation provides detailed, step-by-step guides for each OS. It\'s crucial to follow these carefully.</li><li><strong>Start the MongoDB Server (<code>mongod</code>):</strong> Once installed, you\'ll typically start the MongoDB daemon (the server process) from your terminal. This process usually runs in the background and listens for connections.</li></ol><p><strong>Cloud Alternative (MongoDB Atlas):</strong></p><p>For production applications or if you prefer not to manage a local server, MongoDB Atlas is a cloud-hosted database service. It offers a free tier that\'s perfect for learning and small projects. While we\'ll focus on local connection today, be aware of this powerful option for the future!</p><h3>2. Mongoose Integration: Bridging Node.js and MongoDB</h3><p>Directly interacting with MongoDB from Node.js can be done using the native MongoDB driver. However, for most applications, an Object Data Modeling (ODM) library like <strong>Mongoose</strong> makes life much easier.</p><p><strong>What is Mongoose?</strong></p><ul><li>Mongoose is an elegant MongoDB object modeling tool for Node.js.</li><li>It provides a schema-based solution to model your application data. This means you can define the structure of your documents (what fields they should have, their types, validation rules, etc.) even though MongoDB itself is schema-less.</li><li>It simplifies interactions with the database, handling many complexities behind the scenes.</li></ul><p><strong>Installation:</strong></p><p>First, navigate to your project directory in the terminal and install Mongoose:</p><pre><code>npm install mongoose</code></pre><p><strong>Connecting to MongoDB with Mongoose:</strong></p><p>Here\'s how you establish a connection in your Node.js application:</p><pre><code>// app.js or db.js<br>const mongoose = require(\'mongoose\');<br><br>const DB_URI = \'mongodb://localhost:27017/mydatabase\'; // Replace \'mydatabase\' with your desired database name<br><br>mongoose.connect(DB_URI, {<br>  useNewUrlParser: true,<br>  useUnifiedTopology: true,<br>  // useFindAndModify: false, // Recommended for newer versions of Mongoose<br>  // useCreateIndex: true,    // Recommended for newer versions of Mongoose<br>})<br>.then(() =&gt; console.log(\'MongoDB connected successfully!\'))<br>.catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'MongoDB connection error:\', err));<br><br>// You can also listen for connection events<br>mongoose.connection.on(\'error\', err =&gt; {<br>  console.error(\'Mongoose connection error:\', err);<br>});<br><br>mongoose.connection.on(\'disconnected\', () =&gt; {<br>  console.log(\'Mongoose disconnected from DB\');<br>});<br><br>process.on(\'SIGINT\', () =&gt; {<br>  mongoose.connection.close(() =&gt; {<br>    console.log(\'Mongoose connection closed due to app termination\');<br>    process.exit(0);<br>  });<br>});</code></pre><p><strong>Defining a Schema and Creating a Model:</strong></p><p>Let\'s create a simple schema for a \'Task\' and then a \'Model\' from it. A schema defines the structure, and a model is a constructor compiled from the schema that allows you to interact with the collection.</p><pre><code>// models/Task.js<br>const mongoose = require(\'mongoose\');<br><br>const taskSchema = new mongoose.Schema({<br>  title: {<br>    type: String,<br>    required: true,<br>    trim: true // Removes whitespace from both ends of a string<br>  },<br>  description: {<br>    type: String,<br>    required: false,<br>    trim: true<br>  },<br>  completed: {<br>    type: Boolean,<br>    default: false<br>  },<br>  createdAt: {<br>    type: Date,<br>    default: Date.now<br>  }<br>});<br><br>const Task = mongoose.model(\'Task\', taskSchema);<br><br>module.exports = Task;</code></pre><h3>3. CRUD Operations with Mongoose</h3><p>CRUD stands for <strong>Create, Read, Update, Delete</strong> – the four fundamental operations you perform on data in a database. Let\'s see how Mongoose helps us with these.</p><p>Assume you have connected to the database and imported your <code>Task</code> model:</p><pre><code>// In your main application file (e.g., app.js) or a controller<br>require(\'./db\'); // Assuming your connection logic is in db.js<br>const Task = require(\'./models/Task\');</code></pre><h4>A. Create (Adding new documents)</h4><p>You can create new documents in a couple of ways:</p><ul><li><strong>Using <code>.save()</code> on an instance:</strong></li></ul><pre><code>const newTask = new Task({<br>  title: \'Learn MongoDB\',<br>  description: \'Complete the MongoDB and Mongoose lesson for Day 23.\'<br>});<br><br>newTask.save()<br>  .then(task =&gt; console.log(\'Task created:\', task))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error creating task:\', err));</code></pre><ul><li><strong>Using <code>.create()</code> directly on the Model:</strong> (This is often more concise)</li></ul><pre><code>Task.create({<br>  title: \'Build a REST API\',<br>  description: \'Implement CRUD operations for a task manager.\',<br>  completed: false<br>})<br>  .then(task =&gt; console.log(\'Task created:\', task))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error creating task:\', err));</code></pre><h4>B. Read (Retrieving documents)</h4><p>Mongoose provides powerful methods to query your database.</p><ul><li><strong><code>.find()</code>:</strong> Retrieves all documents or documents matching specific criteria.</li></ul><pre><code>// Find all tasks<br>Task.find({})<br>  .then(tasks =&gt; console.log(\'All tasks:\', tasks))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error finding tasks:\', err));<br><br>// Find tasks that are not completed<br>Task.find({ completed: false })<br>  .then(incompleteTasks =&gt; console.log(\'Incomplete tasks:\', incompleteTasks))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error finding incomplete tasks:\', err));</code></pre><ul><li><strong><code>.findOne()</code>:</strong> Retrieves the first document that matches the criteria.</li></ul><pre><code>Task.findOne({ title: \'Learn MongoDB\' })<br>  .then(task =&gt; console.log(\'Found specific task:\', task))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error finding one task:\', err));</code></pre><ul><li><strong><code>.findById()</code>:</strong> Retrieves a document by its unique <code>_id</code>.</li></ul><pre><code>const taskId = \'654321098765432109876543\'; // Replace with an actual _id from your DB<br>Task.findById(taskId)<br>  .then(task =&gt; console.log(\'Found task by ID:\', task))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error finding task by ID:\', err));</code></pre><h4>C. Update (Modifying existing documents)</h4><p>You can update documents using various methods.</p><ul><li><strong><code>.updateOne()</code> / <code>.updateMany()</code>:</strong> Update one or multiple documents matching a query.</li></ul><pre><code>// Update one task\'s description<br>Task.updateOne(<br>  { title: \'Learn MongoDB\' },<br>  { description: \'Master MongoDB and Mongoose by the end of the week.\' }<br>)<br>  .then(result =&gt; console.log(\'Update result:\', result)) // result contains { n: 1, nModified: 1, ok: 1 }<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error updating task:\', err));<br><br>// Mark all tasks as completed<br>Task.updateMany(<br>  { completed: false },<br>  { completed: true }<br>)<br>  .then(result =&gt; console.log(\'Updated multiple tasks:\', result))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error updating multiple tasks:\', err));</code></pre><ul><li><strong><code>.findByIdAndUpdate()</code>:</strong> Find a document by ID and update it.</li></ul><pre><code>const taskIdToUpdate = \'654321098765432109876543\'; // Replace with an actual _id<br>Task.findByIdAndUpdate(<br>  taskIdToUpdate,<br>  { completed: true },<br>  { new: true } // Return the updated document instead of the original<br>)<br>  .then(updatedTask =&gt; console.log(\'Updated task by ID:\', updatedTask))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error updating task by ID:\', err));</code></pre><h4>D. Delete (Removing documents)</h4><p>To remove documents from your collection.</p><ul><li><strong><code>.deleteOne()</code> / <code>.deleteMany()</code>:</strong> Delete one or many documents matching a query.</li></ul><pre><code>// Delete a single task by title<br>Task.deleteOne({ title: \'Learn MongoDB\' })<br>  .then(result =&gt; console.log(\'Delete result:\', result)) // { n: 1, ok: 1, deletedCount: 1 }<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error deleting task:\', err));<br><br>// Delete all completed tasks<br>Task.deleteMany({ completed: true })<br>  .then(result =&gt; console.log(\'Deleted multiple tasks:\', result))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error deleting multiple tasks:\', err));</code></pre><ul><li><strong><code>.findByIdAndDelete()</code>:</strong> Find a document by ID and delete it.</li></ul><pre><code>const taskIdToDelete = \'654321098765432109876543\'; // Replace with an actual _id<br>Task.findByIdAndDelete(taskIdToDelete)<br>  .then(deletedTask =&gt; console.log(\'Deleted task by ID:\', deletedTask))<br>  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(\'Error deleting task by ID:\', err));</code></pre><h3>Summary</h3><p>Today, you\'ve taken a massive leap forward by learning how to connect your Node.js applications to a persistent database. You now understand the basics of MongoDB, how Mongoose acts as a crucial bridge, and how to perform the essential CRUD operations to manage your data. This knowledge forms the backbone of almost every dynamic web application you\'ll build!</p>', 'Connecting to a Database with MongoDB', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(45, 58, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: frontend backend communication axios</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=frontend+backend+communication+axios\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/frontend+backend+communication+axios\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/frontend+backend+communication+axios/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/frontend+backend+communication+axios/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=frontend+backend+communication+axios\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 24: Frontend-Backend Communication with Axios</h3>\n<p>Welcome back, future web developers! Today is a crucial day where we bridge the gap between your beautiful frontend interfaces and the powerful backend data. We\'ll learn how to make your web applications dynamic by fetching and sending data using a popular JavaScript library called <strong>Axios</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>1. Understanding Frontend-Backend Communication</h3>\n<p>So far, you\'ve built static HTML pages and added interactivity with JavaScript. But what if your website needs to display user-specific data, fetch a list of products, or save user input? This is where the frontend (what the user sees and interacts with) needs to talk to the backend (the server that stores data and runs business logic).</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Frontend:</strong> Your HTML, CSS, and JavaScript running in the user\'s browser. It requests data and displays it.</li>\n    <li><strong>Backend:</strong> A server application (e.g., built with Node.js, Python, PHP) that handles database interactions, authentication, and serves data through APIs (Application Programming Interfaces).</li>\n    <li><strong>APIs:</strong> A set of rules that allow different software applications to communicate with each other. For web development, these are often RESTful APIs that use HTTP requests.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Introducing Axios for API Calls</h3>\n<p>While browsers have a built-in <code>fetch</code> API for making HTTP requests, <strong>Axios</strong> is a widely used, promise-based HTTP client for the browser and Node.js. It simplifies making requests and offers several advantages, especially for beginners:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Easier Syntax:</strong> Often more concise and readable than <code>fetch</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Automatic JSON Transformation:</strong> Axios automatically parses JSON responses, so you don\'t need to call <code>.json()</code> explicitly.</li>\n    <li><strong>Better Error Handling:</strong> Differentiates between network errors and HTTP errors more clearly.</li>\n    <li><strong>Browser Compatibility:</strong> Works well across different browsers.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>How to Include Axios in Your Project</h4>\n<p>For beginner projects, the easiest way to use Axios is via a CDN (Content Delivery Network). Add this script tag to the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> or just before your closing <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code> tag in your HTML file, <strong>before</strong> your own script file:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!-- In your HTML file --&gt;\n&lt;script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/axios/dist/axios.min.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;script src=\"your-script.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h3>3. Making Your First Axios API Call (GET Request)</h3>\n<p>The most common type of request is a <strong>GET</strong> request, used to retrieve data from a server. Let\'s fetch some dummy data from a public API called <a href=\"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/\" target=\"_blank\">JSONPlaceholder</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>Example: Fetching a List of Posts</h4>\n<p>Let\'s create a simple HTML file and a JavaScript file to fetch and log some posts.</p>\n\n<p><strong><code>index.html</code>:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Axios Posts Example&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Posts from JSONPlaceholder&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;div id=\"posts-container\"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- Include Axios first --&gt;\n    &lt;script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/axios/dist/axios.min.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n    &lt;!-- Then your custom script --&gt;\n    &lt;script src=\"app.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong><code>app.js</code>:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>// Make a GET request to fetch posts\naxios.get(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts\')\n    .then(function (response) {\n        // handle success\n        console.log(\'Successfully fetched posts:\', response.data);\n        // response.data contains the actual data from the API\n    })\n    .catch(function (error) {\n        // handle error\n        console.error(\'Error fetching posts:\', error);\n    })\n    .finally(function () {\n        // always executed\n        console.log(\'Request finished.\');\n    });</code></pre>\n<p>Open your <code>index.html</code> in a browser and check the developer console. You should see the array of posts logged!</p>\n\n<h3>4. Fetching Data Dynamically and Displaying It</h3>\n<p>Logging data to the console is useful, but we want to display it on our webpage. Let\'s modify our <code>app.js</code> to dynamically create HTML elements and append them to our <code>#posts-container</code>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modified <code>app.js</code>:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>const postsContainer = document.getElementById(\'posts-container\');\n\naxios.get(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts?_limit=5\') // Fetch only 5 posts for brevity\n    .then(function (response) {\n        const posts = response.data; // The array of post objects\n\n        if (posts.length === 0) {\n            postsContainer.innerHTML = \'&lt;p&gt;No posts found.&lt;/p&gt;\';\n            return;\n        }\n\n        posts.forEach(post => {\n            const postElement = document.createElement(\'div\');\n            postElement.classList.add(\'post-item\'); // Add a class for potential styling\n            postElement.innerHTML = `\n                &lt;h4&gt;${post.title}&lt;/h4&gt;\n                &lt;p&gt;${post.body}&lt;/p&gt;\n                &lt;small&gt;User ID: ${post.userId}&lt;/small&gt;\n            `;\n            postsContainer.appendChild(postElement);\n        });\n    })\n    .catch(function (error) {\n        console.error(\'Error fetching posts:\', error);\n        postsContainer.innerHTML = \'&lt;p style=\"color: red;\"&gt;Failed to load posts. Please try again later.&lt;/p&gt;\';\n    })\n    .finally(function () {\n        console.log(\'Request to fetch posts completed.\');\n    });</code></pre>\n<p>Now, when you open <code>index.html</code>, you should see the titles and bodies of the first 5 posts displayed directly on your page!</p>\n\n<h3>5. Robust Error Handling with Axios</h3>\n<p>In real-world applications, network requests can fail for many reasons: the server might be down, the internet connection might be lost, or the API endpoint might be incorrect. It\'s crucial to handle these errors gracefully to provide a good user experience.</p>\n\n<p>Axios\'s <code>.catch()</code> block is where you handle errors. The <code>error</code> object provided by Axios gives you detailed information:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><code>error.response</code>: If the server responded with a status code that falls out of the range of 2xx (e.g., 404 Not Found, 500 Internal Server Error). This object contains <code>data</code>, <code>status</code>, and <code>headers</code> from the server\'s response.</li>\n    <li><code>error.request</code>: If the request was made but no response was received (e.g., network error, server not responding).</li>\n    <li><code>error.message</code>: A generic error message.</li>\n    <li><code>error.config</code>: The configuration that was used for the request.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Example: Handling Different Error Scenarios</h4>\n<pre><code>axios.get(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/non-existent-endpoint\') // This will cause a 404 error\n    .then(response => {\n        console.log(\'Data:\', response.data);\n    })\n    .catch(error => {\n        if (error.response) {\n            // The request was made and the server responded with a status code\n            // that falls out of the range of 2xx\n            console.error(\'Server responded with an error:\', error.response.status);\n            console.error(\'Error data:\', error.response.data);\n            postsContainer.innerHTML = `&lt;p style=\"color: red;\"&gt;Error: ${error.response.status} - ${error.response.data.message || \'Resource not found\'}&lt;/p&gt;`;\n        } else if (error.request) {\n            // The request was made but no response was received\n            console.error(\'No response received:\', error.request);\n            postsContainer.innerHTML = \'&lt;p style=\"color: red;\"&gt;Network Error: Could not connect to the server.&lt;/p&gt;\';\n        } else {\n            // Something happened in setting up the request that triggered an Error\n            console.error(\'Error setting up request:\', error.message);\n            postsContainer.innerHTML = `&lt;p style=\"color: red;\"&gt;An unexpected error occurred: ${error.message}&lt;/p&gt;`;\n        }\n    });</code></pre>\n<p>By checking <code>error.response</code> and <code>error.request</code>, you can provide more specific and helpful feedback to your users, making your applications much more robust.</p>\n\n<h3>Summary</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ve taken a significant step in building dynamic web applications. You learned:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>The importance of frontend-backend communication via APIs.</li>\n    <li>How to use <strong>Axios</strong> to make HTTP requests.</li>\n    <li>How to perform <strong>GET requests</strong> to fetch data from an API.</li>\n    <li>How to <strong>dynamically display fetched data</strong> on your webpage.</li>\n    <li>Crucially, how to implement <strong>robust error handling</strong> to manage different failure scenarios.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This knowledge is fundamental for building almost any modern web application that interacts with real data. In future lessons, we\'ll explore other types of requests like POST, PUT, and DELETE to send and modify data on the backend.</p>', 'Frontend-Backend Communication with Axios', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(46, 59, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JWT and Passport.js authentication flow diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JWT+and+Passport.js+authentication+flow+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JWT+and+Passport.js+authentication+flow+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JWT+and+Passport.js+authentication+flow+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JWT+and+Passport.js+authentication+flow+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JWT+and+Passport.js+authentication+flow+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>User Authentication & Authorization</h3>\n<p>Authentication verifies a user’s identity, while authorization determines what they can access. Today, we’ll implement <strong>JWT (JSON Web Tokens)</strong>, use <strong>Passport.js</strong> for strategy-based authentication, and secure API endpoints.</p>\n\n<h3>JWT Implementation</h3>\n<p>JWTs are compact tokens that store user data securely. They consist of three parts: <strong>header</strong>, <strong>payload</strong>, and <strong>signature</strong>. Here’s how to implement them:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Install the <code>jsonwebtoken</code> package: <code>npm install jsonwebtoken</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Generate a token after user login:</li>\n</ul>\n<code>\nconst jwt = require(\'jsonwebtoken\');\nconst generateToken = (user) => {\n  return jwt.sign({ id: user.id, username: user.username }, \'your-secret-key\', { expiresIn: \'1h\' });\n};\n</code>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Send the token to the client (e.g., in a response header or JSON body).</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Store the token on the client (e.g., <code>localStorage</code> or cookies) and send it in the <code>Authorization</code> header for subsequent requests.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Passport.js Setup</h3>\n<p>Passport.js is a middleware for authentication strategies. For JWT, we’ll use the <code>passport-jwt</code> strategy:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Install packages: <code>npm install passport passport-jwt</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Configure Passport in your server file:</li>\n</ul>\n<code>\nconst passport = require(\'passport\');\nconst JwtStrategy = require(\'passport-jwt\').Strategy;\nconst ExtractJwt = require(\'passport-jwt\').ExtractJwt;\n\npassport.use(new JwtStrategy({\n  jwtFromRequest: ExtractJwt.fromAuthHeaderAsBearerToken(),\n  secretOrKey: \'your-secret-key\'\n}, (jwtPayload, done) => {\n  // Find user in database by ID from jwtPayload\n  User.findById(jwtPayload.id, (err, user) => {\n    if (err) return done(err, false);\n    if (user) return done(null, user);\n    else return done(null, false);\n  });\n}));\n</code>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Initialize Passport in your Express app: <code>app.use(passport.initialize());</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Secure API Endpoints</h3>\n<p>Use Passport middleware to protect routes. Create a middleware function to verify tokens:</p>\n<code>\nconst ensureAuthenticated = (req, res, next) => {\n  passport.authenticate(\'jwt\', { session: false }, (err, user) => {\n    if (err || !user) return res.status(401).json({ error: \'Unauthorized\' });\n    req.user = user;\n    next();\n  })(req, res, next);\n};\n</code>\n<p>Apply it to routes requiring authentication:</p>\n<code>\napp.get(\'/api/protected\', ensureAuthenticated, (req, res) => {\n  res.json({ message: `Welcome, ${req.user.username}!` });\n});\n</code>\n<p>Public routes (e.g., login/registration) skip authentication:</p>\n<code>\napp.post(\'/api/login\', (req, res) => {\n  // Validate user credentials and generate JWT\n});\n</code>\n\n<h3>Best Practices</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use HTTPS to encrypt token transmission.</li>\n  <li>Keep tokens short-lived and refresh them securely.</li>\n  <li>Never store sensitive data in JWT payloads.</li>\n  <li>Store tokens securely on the client (e.g., HTTP-only cookies).</li>\n</ul>', 'User Authentication & Authorization', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(47, 60, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: heroku deployment, vercel deployment, environment variables, ci/cd pipeline</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=heroku+deployment%2C+vercel+deployment%2C+environment+variables%2C+ci%2Fcd+pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/heroku+deployment%2C+vercel+deployment%2C+environment+variables%2C+ci%2Fcd+pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/heroku+deployment%2C+vercel+deployment%2C+environment+variables%2C+ci%2Fcd+pipeline/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/heroku+deployment%2C+vercel+deployment%2C+environment+variables%2C+ci%2Fcd+pipeline/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=heroku+deployment%2C+vercel+deployment%2C+environment+variables%2C+ci%2Fcd+pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Project Deployment Basics</h1>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Today, we\'re going to learn about project deployment basics. This includes deploying our web applications to cloud platforms like Heroku and Vercel, understanding environment variables, and learning the basics of Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD).</p>\n<h2>Heroku Deployment</h2>\n<p>Heroku is a cloud platform that allows us to deploy our web applications with ease. Here\'s a step-by-step guide on how to deploy a web application to Heroku:</p>\n<ol>\n    <li><strong>Sign up for a Heroku account</strong></li>\n    <p>Go to the Heroku website and sign up for an account. It\'s free!</p>\n    <li><strong>Install the Heroku CLI</strong></li>\n    <p>After signing up, install the Heroku CLI on your computer. This will allow you to interact with the Heroku platform from your terminal.</p>\n    <li><strong>Initialize a new Heroku app</strong></li>\n    <p>Run the command <code>heroku create</code> in your terminal to initialize a new Heroku app.</p>\n    <li><strong>Deploy your code</strong></li>\n    <p>Run the command <code>git add .</code> and <code>git commit -m \"initial commit\"</code> to stage and commit your code. Then, run the command <code>git push heroku main</code> to deploy your code to Heroku.</p>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vercel Deployment</h2>\n<p>Vercel is another cloud platform that allows us to deploy our web applications with ease. Here\'s a step-by-step guide on how to deploy a web application to Vercel:</p>\n<ol>\n    <li><strong>Sign up for a Vercel account</strong></li>\n    <p>Go to the Vercel website and sign up for an account. It\'s free!</p>\n    <li><strong>Install the Vercel CLI</strong></li>\n    <p>After signing up, install the Vercel CLI on your computer. This will allow you to interact with the Vercel platform from your terminal.</p>\n    <li><strong>Initialize a new Vercel app</strong></li>\n    <p>Run the command <code>vercel create</code> in your terminal to initialize a new Vercel app.</p>\n    <li><strong>Deploy your code</strong></li>\n    <p>Run the command <code>git add .</code> and <code>git commit -m \"initial commit\"</code> to stage and commit your code. Then, run the command <code>vercel deploy</code> to deploy your code to Vercel.</p>\n</ol>\n<h2>Environment Variables</h2>\n<p>Environment variables are used to store sensitive information such as API keys, database credentials, and more. Here\'s how to use environment variables in your web application:</p>\n<p>First, you need to create a <code>.env</code> file in the root of your project. This file will store all your environment variables. For example:</p>\n<code>\nDB_USERNAME=myuser\nDB_PASSWORD=mypassword\nAPI_KEY=myapikey\n</code>\n<p>Then, you need to install a package such as <code>dotenv</code> to load the environment variables from the <code>.env</code> file. For example:</p>\n<code>\nconst dotenv = require(\'dotenv\');\ndotenv.config();\n</code>\n<p>Finally, you can access the environment variables in your code using the <code>process.env</code> object. For example:</p>\n<code>\nconst dbUsername = process.env.DB_USERNAME;\nconst dbPassword = process.env.DB_PASSWORD;\nconst apiKey = process.env.API_KEY;\n</code>\n<h2>CI/CD Basics</h2>\n<p>CI/CD stands for Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment. It\'s a software development practice that involves automating the build, test, and deployment of our web application.</p>\n<p>Here\'s a high-level overview of the CI/CD pipeline:</p>\n<ol>\n    <li><strong>Build</strong></li>\n    <p>The build stage involves compiling our code and creating a deployable artifact.</p>\n    <li><strong>Test</strong></li>\n    <p>The test stage involves running automated tests to ensure our code is working as expected.</p>\n    <li><strong>Deploy</strong></li>\n    <p>The deploy stage involves deploying our code to a production environment.</p>\n</ol>\n<p>There are many tools available for implementing CI/CD, including Jenkins, Travis CI, CircleCI, and more.</p>\n<h3>Quiz:</h3>\n<ul>\n    <li>What is Heroku?</li>\n    <li>What is Vercel?</li>\n    <li>What is an environment variable?</li>\n    <li>What is CI/CD?</li>\n    <li>What are the three stages of a CI/CD pipeline?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Answers:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Heroku is a cloud platform that allows us to deploy our web applications with ease.</li>\n    <li>Vercel is another cloud platform that allows us to deploy our web applications with ease.</li>\n    <li>An environment variable is a variable that stores sensitive information such as API keys, database credentials, and more.</li>\n    <li>CI/CD stands for Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment, which is a software development practice that involves automating the build, test, and deployment of our web application.</li>\n    <li>The three stages of a CI/CD pipeline are Build, Test, and Deploy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Image Query:</h3>\n<p>heroku deployment, vercel deployment, environment variables, ci/cd pipeline</p>\n<h3>Summary:</h3>\n<p>Today, we learned about project deployment basics, including deploying our web applications to cloud platforms like Heroku and Vercel, understanding environment variables, and learning the basics of Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD).</p>\n<p>We also learned how to use environment variables in our web application and how to implement a CI/CD pipeline.</p>\n<p>Next time, we\'ll learn about advanced topics in web development, including authentication and authorization, and more.</p>\n<h3>Homework:</h3>\n<p>Deploy your web application to Heroku or Vercel and learn how to use environment variables in your code.</p>\n<p>Implement a CI/CD pipeline using a tool like Jenkins or Travis CI.</p>\n<p>Practice using environment variables and CI/CD pipelines in your code.</p>\n</h1>', 'Project Deployment Basics', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(48, 61, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Full-stack web app development workflow</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Full-stack+web+app+development+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Full-stack+web+app+development+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Full-stack+web+app+development+workflow/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Full-stack+web+app+development+workflow/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Full-stack+web+app+development+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Full-Stack Project Development</h2>\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 27 of our Web Development course. Today, we will learn how to build a to-do app from scratch, integrate frontend and backend, and interact with a database.</p>\n<h3>Project Overview</h3>\n<p>The to-do app will allow users to create, read, update, and delete tasks. We will use HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Node.js, Express.js, and MongoDB to build this app.</p>\n<h3>HTML Structure</h3>\n<pre><code>\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;To-Do App&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;To-Do List&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;input id=\'task\' type=\'text\' placeholder=\'Enter task\'&gt;\n    &lt;button id=\'add-task\'&gt;Add Task&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;ul id=\'task-list\'&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;script src=\'script.js\'&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This HTML structure includes a heading, input field, button, unordered list, and a script tag to link our JavaScript file.</p>\n<h3>Frontend Development</h3>\n<p>We will use JavaScript to add event listeners to the input field and button. When the button is clicked, we will create a new task and add it to the unordered list.</p>\n<pre><code>\n// Get the input field and button elements\nconst taskInput = document.getElementById(\'task\');\nconst addTaskButton = document.getElementById(\'add-task\');\nconst taskList = document.getElementById(\'task-list\');\n\n// Add event listener to the button\naddTaskButton.addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\n  // Get the task value from the input field\n  const taskValue = taskInput.value.trim();\n\n  // Check if the task value is not empty\n  if (taskValue !== \'\') {\n    // Create a new list item element\n    const listItem = document.createElement(\'LI\');\n    listItem.textContent = taskValue;\n\n    // Add the list item to the unordered list\n    taskList.appendChild(listItem);\n\n    // Clear the input field\n    taskInput.value = \'\';\n  }\n});\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Backend Development</h3>\n<p>We will use Node.js and Express.js to create a RESTful API to interact with our database.</p>\n<pre><code>\n// Import the required modules\nconst express = require(\'express\');\nconst app = express();\nconst mongoose = require(\'mongoose\');\n\n// Connect to the MongoDB database\nmongoose.connect(\'mongodb://localhost/todo-app\', { useNewUrlParser: true, useUnifiedTopology: true });\n\n// Define the task schema\nconst taskSchema = new mongoose.Schema({\n  task: String\n});\n\n// Create the task model\nconst Task = mongoose.model(\'Task\', taskSchema);\n\n// Define the API endpoints\napp.get(\'/tasks\', (req, res) => {\n  Task.find().then(tasks => {\n    res.json(tasks);\n  }).catch(err => {\n    res.status(500).json({ message: \'Error fetching tasks\' });\n  });\n});\n\napp.post(\'/tasks\', (req, res) => {\n  const task = new Task({ task: req.body.task });\n  task.save().then(() => {\n    res.json({ message: \'Task created successfully\' });\n  }).catch(err => {\n    res.status(500).json({ message: \'Error creating task\' });\n  });\n});\n\napp.put(\'/tasks/:id\', (req, res) => {\n  const taskId = req.params.id;\n  Task.findByIdAndUpdate(taskId, { task: req.body.task }, { new: true }).then(task => {\n    res.json(task);\n  }).catch(err => {\n    res.status(500).json({ message: \'Error updating task\' });\n  });\n});\n\napp.delete(\'/tasks/:id\', (req, res) => {\n  const taskId = req.params.id;\n  Task.findByIdAndRemove(taskId).then(() => {\n    res.json({ message: \'Task deleted successfully\' });\n  }).catch(err => {\n    res.status(500).json({ message: \'Error deleting task\' });\n  });\n});\n\n// Start the server\nconst port = 3000;\napp.listen(port, () => {\n  console.log(`Server started on port ${port}`);\n});\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Database Interaction</h3>\n<p>We will use MongoDB to store our tasks. We will define a task schema and create a task model using Mongoose.</p>\n<pre><code>\n// Import the required modules\nconst mongoose = require(\'mongoose\');\n\n// Connect to the MongoDB database\nmongoose.connect(\'mongodb://localhost/todo-app\', { useNewUrlParser: true, useUnifiedTopology: true });\n\n// Define the task schema\nconst taskSchema = new mongoose.Schema({\n  task: String\n});\n\n// Create the task model\nconst Task = mongoose.model(\'Task\', taskSchema);\n\n// Create a new task\nconst task = new Task({ task: \'Buy milk\' });\ntask.save().then(() => {\n  console.log(\'Task created successfully\');\n}).catch(err => {\n  console.log(\'Error creating task:\', err);\n});\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>In this day of our Web Development course, we learned how to build a to-do app from scratch, integrate frontend and backend, and interact with a database. We used HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Node.js, Express.js, and MongoDB to build this app.</p>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>What is the purpose of the task schema in the backend code?</li>\n  <li>What is the difference between the GET and POST methods in the backend code?</li>\n  <li>How do we interact with the MongoDB database in the backend code?</li>\n  <li>What is the purpose of the event listener in the frontend code?</li>\n  <li>How do we add a new task to the unordered list in the frontend code?</li>\n</ol>\n\", \"short search query\": \"full-stack project development to-do app', 'Full-Stack Project Development', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(49, 62, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development quiz and debugging</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+quiz+and+debugging\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+quiz+and+debugging\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+quiz+and+debugging/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+quiz+and+debugging/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+quiz+and+debugging\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Week 4 Review & Quiz</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 28! Today we consolidate your learning from Week 4 with a quiz on backend concepts, debugging exercises, and code optimization techniques. Let\'s begin with a structured review.</p>\n\n<h3>Backend Concepts Quiz</h3>\n<p>Test your understanding of server-side logic, APIs, and databases:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>HTTP Methods:</strong> GET retrieves data, POST submits data, PUT updates, DELETE removes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Server-Side Languages:</strong> PHP, Python (Django/Flask), Node.js, Ruby on Rails.</li>\n  <li><strong>Databases:</strong> SQL (MySQL, PostgreSQL) vs. NoSQL (MongoDB, Firebase).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Debugging Exercises</h3>\n<p>Debugging is critical for identifying and fixing errors. Practice with these scenarios:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Scenario 1:</strong> A 500 Internal Server Error occurs when a PHP script divides by zero. Use <code>try-catch</code> blocks to handle exceptions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scenario 2:</strong> A JavaScript API call returns undefined data. Check the CORS headers and use <code>console.log()</code> to trace the response.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scenario 3:</strong> A SQL query returns duplicate results. Add <code>DISTINCT</code> or fix JOIN conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Code Optimization</h3>\n<p>Optimize performance with these strategies:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Caching:</strong> Use Redis or browser caching for static assets.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minification:</strong> Compress CSS/JS files with tools like UglifyJS.</li>\n  <li><strong>Database Indexing:</strong> Add indexes to frequently queried columns.</li>\n  <li><strong>Asynchronous Tasks:</strong> Offload heavy processing with queues (e.g., Celery for Python).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Example: Optimizing PHP Code</h3>\n<pre><code>\n// Before: Inefficient loop\n$result = [];\nfor ($i = 0; $i < count($data); $i++) {\n  $result[] = $data[$i] * 2;\n}\n\n// After: Use array_map for better performance\n$result = array_map(fn($x) => $x * 2, $data);\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Why it works:</strong> <code>array_map()</code> is optimized in PHP\'s engine and reduces overhead compared to manual loops.</p>', 'Week 4 Review & Quiz', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(50, 63, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development project workflow</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+project+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+project+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+project+workflow/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+project+workflow/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+project+workflow\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Final Project Development and Testing</h3>\n\n<p>Welcome to the final day of your web development journey! Today, you’ll combine your HTML, CSS, and JavaScript skills to build and test a complete project. This day focuses on practical implementation, debugging, and ensuring your website functions as intended.</p>\n\n<h3>HTML: Final Project Structure</h3>\n\n<p>Begin by creating a semantic HTML structure for your project. Use elements like <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> to organize your content. Here’s an example:</p>\n\n<pre><code>&lt;<!DOCTYPE html>&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My Final Project&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;header&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Website&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;nav&gt;\n        &lt;ul&gt;\n          &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n          &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;/ul&gt;\n      &lt;/nav&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n    &lt;main&gt;\n      &lt;section id=\"content\"&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;This is the main section.&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/section&gt;\n    &lt;/main&gt;\n    &lt;footer&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;© 2023 My Website&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/footer&gt;\n    &lt;script src=\"script.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h3>CSS: Styling Your Project</h3>\n\n<p>Use CSS to style your HTML elements. Add a <code>styles.css</code> file and define rules for layout, colors, and fonts. Example:</p>\n\n<pre><code>body {\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\n\nheader {\n  background-color: #333;\n  color: white;\n  padding: 1em;\n}\n\nnav ul {\n  list-style: none;\n  padding: 0;\n}\n\nnav li {\n  display: inline;\n  margin-right: 10px;\n}\n\nfooter {\n  text-align: center;\n  padding: 1em;\n  background-color: #f4f4f4;\n}</code></pre>\n\n<h3>JavaScript: Adding Interactivity</h3>\n\n<p>JavaScript brings your website to life. Create a <code>script.js</code> file and add functionality like event listeners or dynamic content. Example:</p>\n\n<pre><code>document.addEventListener(\"DOMContentLoaded\", function() {\n  const button = document.createElement(\"button\");\n  button.textContent = \"Click Me!\";\n  button.addEventListener(\"click\", function() {\n    alert(\"Button clicked!\");\n  });\n  document.getElementById(\"content\").appendChild(button);\n});</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Debugging: Fixing Errors</h3>\n\n<p>Debugging is crucial for identifying and resolving issues. Use these tools and techniques:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Console Logging:</strong> Add <code>console.log()</code> to track variable values and execution flow.</li>\n  <li><strong>Browser DevTools:</strong> Open the browser’s developer tools (F12) to inspect elements, check the console for errors, and debug JavaScript step-by-step.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Errors:</strong> Look for typos, missing semicolons, or incorrect selectors. For example, <code>document.getElemetById</code> (typo) will not work.</li>\n  <li><strong>Responsive Testing:</strong> Use the browser’s device toolbar to test how your site looks on different screen sizes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Final Steps</h3>\n\n<p>Once your project is built:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Test all links and forms to ensure they work correctly.</li>\n  <li>Validate your HTML and CSS using tools like <a href=\"https://validator.w3.org/\" target=\"_blank\">W3C Validator</a>.</li>\n  <li>Deploy your project using platforms like GitHub Pages or Netlify.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Congratulations! You’ve completed your final project. Keep practicing to refine your skills and build more complex web applications.</p>', 'Final Project Development and Testing', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(51, 64, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development project deployment and code review</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+project+deployment+and+code+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+project+deployment+and+code+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+project+deployment+and+code+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+project+deployment+and+code+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+project+deployment+and+code+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Final Project Submission and Review</h3>\n<p>Congratulations! Today marks the culmination of your web development journey. We\'ll focus on deploying your final project, conducting a code review, and reinforcing best practices for professional-grade code.</p>\n\n<h3>Project Deployment</h3>\n<p>Deployment is the process of making your website accessible online. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Build your project</strong>: Run <code>npm run build</code> or <code>npm run deploy</code> depending on your framework</li>\n  <li><strong>Choose a hosting platform</strong>: Popular options include GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, or Firebase</li>\n  <li><strong>Configure deployment settings</strong>: For GitHub Pages, go to <code>Settings > Pages</code> and select your build folder</li>\n  <li><strong>Verify live site</strong>: Test all functionality in a real browser environment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example GitHub Actions workflow snippet:</p>\n<code>\nname: Deploy\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v2\n      - name: Deploy to Netlify\n        uses: netlify/actions/deploy@master\n</code>\n\n<h3>Code Review Process</h3>\n<p>A code review ensures your project meets quality standards. Focus on these aspects:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Code structure</strong>: Is your HTML semantic? Are CSS/JS properly organized?</li>\n  <li><strong>Readability</strong>: Are variables and functions named descriptively?</li>\n  <li><strong>Error handling</strong>: Do forms validate inputs? Are there fallback states?</li>\n  <li><strong>Performance</strong>: Are images optimized? Are there unnecessary console logs?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Review checklist example:</p>\n<code>\n// Bad practice\nfunction calc(a, b) { return a + b; }\n\n// Good practice\n/**\n * Calculates sum of two numbers\n * @param {number} num1 - First number\n * @param {number} num2 - Second number\n * @returns {number} Sum result\n */\nfunction calculateSum(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 + num2;\n}\n</code>\n\n<h3>Professional Best Practices</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Version control</strong>: Commit frequently with clear messages like <code>feat: add contact form validation</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Documentation</strong>: Write README.md files explaining installation and usage</li>\n  <li><strong>Security</strong>: Use HTTPS, sanitize user inputs, and avoid hardcoding secrets</li>\n  <li><strong>Testing</strong>: Verify cross-browser compatibility and responsive design</li>\n  <li><strong>Code cleanliness</strong>: Remove commented-out code and fix linter warnings</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Final tip: Use <code>git log --oneline --graph</code> to visualize your commit history before submission.</p>', 'Final Project Submission and Review', 1, '2026-04-07 07:35:20', '2026-04-07 07:35:20'),
(52, 65, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_69ee0769075cc_1777207145.jpg\" alt=\"Uploaded image\"></p></div><p>Web development is the process of building and maintaining websites. It involves a combination of technical skills, including programming languages, databases, and web frameworks. In this course, we will cover the basics of web development, including HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.</p><h3>What is HTML?</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language used to create web pages. It is used to define the structure and content of a web page, including headings, paragraphs, images, links, and more. HTML is made up of a series of elements, which are represented by tags. These tags are used to wrap around content and apply meaning to it.</p><p>For example, the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> tag is used to define a heading, while the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag is used to define a paragraph. HTML elements can also have attributes, which provide additional information about the element. For example, the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag has an <code>href</code> attribute, which specifies the URL of the link.</p><h3>Basic HTML Structure</h3><ul><li><strong>DOCTYPE</strong>: The document type declaration, which tells the browser that the document is written in HTML5.</li><li><strong>html</strong>: The root element of the HTML document.</li><li><strong>head</strong>: The head section, which contains metadata about the document, such as the title and character encoding.</li><li><strong>body</strong>: The body section, which contains the content of the HTML document.</li></ul><p>A basic HTML document might look like this: <code>&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n&lt;title&gt;My Web Page&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to my web page&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></p><h3>What is CSS?</h3><p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is a styling language used to control the layout and appearance of web pages. It is used to separate the presentation of a document from its structure, which is defined in HTML. CSS is made up of a series of rules, which are applied to elements on a web page.</p><p>For example, you might use CSS to change the color of a heading, or to add a border to an image. CSS rules are made up of a selector, which specifies the element to which the rule applies, and a declaration, which specifies the style to be applied.</p><p>For example: <code>h1 { color: blue; }</code> This rule applies to all <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> elements on a web page and sets their color to blue.</p><h3>What is JavaScript?</h3><p>JavaScript is a programming language used to add interactivity to web pages. It is used to create dynamic effects, such as animations and pop-ups, and to respond to user input, such as clicks and keyboard input.</p><p>JavaScript is made up of a series of statements, which are executed by the browser in order. These statements can be used to manipulate the structure and content of a web page, and to interact with the user.</p><p>For example: <code>console.log(\'Hello World!\');</code> This statement writes the string \'Hello World!\' to the browser console.</p><h3>Getting Started with Web Development</h3><p>To get started with web development, you will need a few basic tools. These include a text editor, such as Notepad or TextEdit, and a web browser, such as Google Chrome or Mozilla Firefox.</p><p>You will also need to learn the basics of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. This will involve learning the syntax and structure of each language, as well as how to use them to create web pages.</p>', 'Introduction to Web Development', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-26 12:39:12'),
(53, 66, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_69ee04131f5d7_1777206291.png\" alt=\"Uploaded image\"></p></div>\n  <p>Welcome to Day 2 of our Web Development course. In this session, we will be discussing the essential tools required to set up a development environment. This includes code editors, version control, and Integrated Development Environments (IDEs).</p>\n  <h3>Code Editors</h3>\n  <p>A code editor is a software application that allows you to write and edit code. There are various code editors available, each with its own set of features and functionalities. Some popular code editors include:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Visual Studio Code (VS Code)</strong>: A lightweight, open-source code editor developed by Microsoft. It offers a wide range of features, including code completion, debugging, and version control integration.</li>\n    <li><strong>Sublime Text</strong>: A popular code editor known for its speed, agility, and extensive feature set. It offers features such as code completion, syntax highlighting, and a wide range of plugins.</li>\n    <li><strong>Atom</strong>: A free, open-source code editor developed by GitHub. It offers a wide range of features, including code completion, debugging, and version control integration.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>When choosing a code editor, consider the following factors:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Feature set</strong>: What features do you need? Do you need code completion, debugging, or version control integration?</li>\n    <li><strong>Platform support</strong>: Is the code editor available on your operating system of choice?</li>\n    <li><strong>Community support</strong>: Is the code editor actively maintained and updated by a large community of developers?</li>\n  </ul>\n  <h3>Version Control</h3>\n  <p>Version control is a system that allows you to track changes to your code over time. It provides a way to collaborate with others, revert changes, and keep a record of all changes made to your code. Some popular version control systems include:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Git</strong>: A widely used, open-source version control system developed by Linus Torvalds. It offers a wide range of features, including branching, merging, and remote repositories.</li>\n    <li><strong>Mercurial</strong>: A fast, lightweight version control system developed by Matt Mackall. It offers features such as branching, merging, and remote repositories.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>When choosing a version control system, consider the following factors:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Scalability</strong>: Can the version control system handle large projects with multiple developers?</li>\n    <li><strong>Security</strong>: Is the version control system secure, with features such as encryption and access control?</li>\n    <li><strong>Community support</strong>: Is the version control system actively maintained and updated by a large community of developers?</li>\n  </ul>\n  <h3>Integrated Development Environments (IDEs)</h3>\n  <p>An Integrated Development Environment (IDE) is a software application that provides a comprehensive development environment for coding, debugging, and testing. Some popular IDEs include:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Visual Studio</strong>: A commercial IDE developed by Microsoft. It offers a wide range of features, including code completion, debugging, and testing.</li>\n    <li><strong>IntelliJ IDEA</strong>: A commercial IDE developed by JetBrains. It offers a wide range of features, including code completion, debugging, and testing.</li>\n    <li><strong>NetBeans</strong>: A free, open-source IDE developed by Apache. It offers a wide range of features, including code completion, debugging, and testing.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>When choosing an IDE, consider the following factors:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Feature set</strong>: What features do you need? Do you need code completion, debugging, or testing?</li>\n    <li><strong>Platform support</strong>: Is the IDE available on your operating system of choice?</li>\n    <li><strong>Community support</strong>: Is the IDE actively maintained and updated by a large community of developers?</li>\n  </ul>', 'Setting Up the Development Environment', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-26 12:25:17'),
(54, 67, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_69ee04fb68e50_1777206523.png\" alt=\"Uploaded image\"></p></div><h3>Introduction to HTML Fundamentals</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language used to create web pages. It provides the structure and content of a web page, while CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is used for styling and layout, and JavaScript is used for dynamic effects and interactions. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of HTML structure, tags, and attributes.</p><h3>HTML Structure</h3><p>An HTML document consists of a series of elements, which are represented by tags. These tags are used to define the structure and content of a web page. The basic structure of an HTML document includes the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element, which is the root element of the document. Inside the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element, there are two main elements: <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>.</p><p>The <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> element contains metadata about the document, such as the title, character encoding, and links to external stylesheets or scripts. The <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> element contains the content of the web page, such as text, images, and other media.</p><h3>HTML Tags</h3><p>HTML tags are used to define the different elements of a web page. There are two types of tags: opening tags and closing tags. Opening tags are used to start an element, and closing tags are used to end an element. For example, the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag is used to start a paragraph, and the <code>&lt;/p&gt;</code> tag is used to end a paragraph.</p><p>Some common HTML tags include:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> - <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code>: heading elements</li><li><code>&lt;p&gt;</code>: paragraph element</li><li><code>&lt;img&gt;</code>: image element</li><li><code>&lt;a&gt;</code>: anchor element (link)</li><li><code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>: unordered and ordered list elements</li><li><code>&lt;li&gt;</code>: list item element</li></ul><h3>HTML Attributes</h3><p>HTML attributes are used to provide additional information about an element. They are added to the opening tag of an element and consist of a name and a value. For example, the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> element has an attribute called <code>href</code>, which specifies the URL of the link.</p><p>Some common HTML attributes include:</p><ul><li><code>href</code>: specifies the URL of a link</li><li><code>src</code>: specifies the URL of an image</li><li><code>alt</code>: specifies the alternative text for an image</li><li><code>title</code>: specifies the title of a page or element</li><li><code>style</code>: specifies the styles for an element</li></ul><p><strong>Example of HTML Code</strong></p><p><code>&lt;html&gt;<br>&lt;head&gt;<br>&lt;title&gt;My First Web Page&lt;/title&gt;<br>&lt;/head&gt;<br>&lt;body&gt;<br>&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Web Page&lt;/h1&gt;<br>&lt;p&gt;This is my first web page.&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"An image\"&gt;<br>&lt;/body&gt;<br>&lt;/html&gt;</code></p>', 'HTML Fundamentals', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-26 12:28:53'),
(55, 68, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_69ee058c67043_1777206668.png\" alt=\"Uploaded image\"></p></div><h3>CSS Fundamentals</h3><p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is a styling language used to control the layout and appearance of web pages. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of CSS, including selectors, properties, and values. Understanding these concepts is crucial for creating visually appealing and user-friendly websites.</p><p>CSS selectors are used to target specific elements on a web page. There are several types of selectors, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Element Selectors</strong>: These selectors target HTML elements by their tag name. For example, <code>p</code> would select all paragraph elements on the page.</li><li><strong>Class Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements by their class attribute. For example, <code>.header</code> would select all elements with the class <code>header</code>.</li><li><strong>ID Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements by their ID attribute. For example, <code>#nav</code> would select the element with the ID <code>nav</code>.</li><li><strong>Attribute Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements by their attributes. For example, <code>input[type=\'text\']</code> would select all text input fields.</li></ul><p>CSS properties are used to define the styles that are applied to the selected elements. Some common properties include:</p><ul><li><strong>Color</strong>: This property sets the color of the text. For example, <code>color: #fff;</code> would set the text color to white.</li><li><strong>Background-Color</strong>: This property sets the background color of an element. For example, <code>background-color: #333;</code> would set the background color to a dark gray.</li><li><strong>Font-Size</strong>: This property sets the size of the text. For example, <code>font-size: 16px;</code> would set the font size to 16 pixels.</li><li><strong>Margin</strong>: This property sets the margin (space) around an element. For example, <code>margin: 10px;</code> would set a margin of 10 pixels around the element.</li></ul><p>CSS values are used to define the values of the properties. For example, in the property <code>color: #fff;</code>, <code>#fff</code> is the value. CSS values can be:</p><ul><li><strong>Lengths</strong>: These values define a length or size. For example, <code>10px</code> or <code>20%</code>.</li><li><strong>Colors</strong>: These values define a color. For example, <code>#fff</code> or <code>rgb(255, 255, 255)</code>.</li><li><strong>Keywords</strong>: These values define a specific value. For example, <code>bold</code> or <code>italic</code>.</li></ul><p>Understanding how to use CSS selectors, properties, and values is essential for creating effective and visually appealing web pages. By combining these concepts, you can create complex and dynamic layouts that enhance the user experience.</p>', 'CSS Fundamentals', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-26 12:31:41'),
(56, 69, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><br></p><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_69ee0648d40b4_1777206856.png\" alt=\"Uploaded image\"><br></p></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript</h3><p>JavaScript is a high-level, dynamic, and interpreted programming language that is primarily used for client-side scripting on the web. It allows you to create interactive web pages, update content dynamically, and respond to user interactions.</p><p>In this lesson, we will cover the basics of JavaScript, including variables, data types, and functions. These are the building blocks of any programming language, and understanding them is essential to becoming proficient in JavaScript.</p><h3>Variables</h3><p>In JavaScript, a variable is a container that stores a value. You can think of it as a labeled box where you can store a value. Variables are declared using the <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, or <code>var</code> keywords.</p><ul><li><code>let</code>: This keyword is used to declare a variable that can be reassigned a new value.</li><li><code>const</code>: This keyword is used to declare a constant variable that cannot be reassigned a new value.</li><li><code>var</code>: This keyword is used to declare a variable that can be reassigned a new value, but it is function scoped.</li></ul><p>For example:</p><code>let name = \'John Doe\';<br>const PI = 3.14;<br>var age = 25;</code><p>As you can see, we have declared three variables: <code>name</code>, <code>PI</code>, and <code>age</code>. The <code>name</code> variable can be reassigned a new value, the <code>PI</code> variable is a constant and cannot be reassigned, and the <code>age</code> variable can be reassigned a new value.</p><h3>Data Types</h3><p>JavaScript has several data types, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Number</strong>: This data type represents a numeric value, such as <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, or <code>3.14</code>.</li><li><strong>String</strong>: This data type represents a sequence of characters, such as <code>\'hello\'</code> or <code>\'goodbye\'</code>. Strings can be enclosed in single quotes or double quotes.</li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: This data type represents a true or false value.</li><li><strong>Null</strong>: This data type represents the absence of any value.</li><li><strong>Undefined</strong>: This data type represents an uninitialized variable.</li><li><strong>Object</strong>: This data type represents a collection of key-value pairs.</li><li><strong>Array</strong>: This data type represents a collection of values.</li></ul><p>For example:</p><code>let num = 10;<br>let str = \'hello\';<br>let bool = true;<br>let nullValue = null;<br>let undefinedValue;<br>let obj = { name: \'John Doe\', age: 25 };<br>let arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];</code><h3>Functions</h3><p>A function is a block of code that can be executed multiple times from different parts of your program. Functions are useful for organizing your code, reducing repetition, and making your code more reusable.</p><p>A function typically consists of a name, parameters, and a body. The name of the function is used to identify it, the parameters are used to pass values to the function, and the body is where the code is executed.</p><p>For example:</p><code>function greet(name) {<br>  console.log(\'Hello, \' + name);<br>}</code><p>This function takes a <code>name</code> parameter and logs a greeting message to the console. You can call this function by passing a value for the <code>name</code> parameter, like this:</p><code>greet(\'John Doe\');</code><p>This will output <code>Hello, John Doe</code> to the console.</p>', 'Introduction to JavaScript', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-26 12:35:03'),
(57, 70, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_69ee07142f59f_1777207060.png\" alt=\"Uploaded image\"></p></div><h3>Introduction to Building a Simple Web Page</h3><p>Welcome to Day 6 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be learning about the basics of building a simple web page using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. These three technologies are the foundation of web development and are used to create the structure, layout, and interactive elements of a website.</p><h3>HTML - HyperText Markup Language</h3><p>HTML is used to create the structure and content of a web page. It consists of a series of elements, represented by tags, that are used to wrap around content and apply meaning to it. For example, the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> tag is used to represent a heading, while the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag is used to represent a paragraph of text.</p><p>Some common HTML tags include:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> - heading</li><li><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> - paragraph</li><li><code>&lt;img&gt;</code> - image</li><li><code>&lt;a&gt;</code> - hyperlink</li><li><code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> - unordered list</li><li><code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> - ordered list</li></ul><p>Let\'s take a look at an example of HTML code:</p><p><code>&lt;html&gt;<br>&lt;head&gt;<br>&lt;title&gt;My Web Page&lt;/title&gt;<br>&lt;/head&gt;<br>&lt;body&gt;<br>&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Web Page&lt;/h1&gt;<br>&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;/body&gt;<br>&lt;/html&gt;</code></p><h3>CSS - Cascading Style Sheets</h3><p>CSS is used to control the layout and appearance of a web page. It consists of a series of rules, known as styles, that are applied to HTML elements. For example, you can use CSS to change the color and font of a heading, or to add a background image to a web page.</p><p>Some common CSS properties include:</p><ul><li><code>color</code> - sets the color of text</li><li><code>background-color</code> - sets the background color of an element</li><li><code>font-size</code> - sets the size of text</li><li><code>font-family</code> - sets the font of text</li><li><code>margin</code> - sets the margin of an element</li><li><code>padding</code> - sets the padding of an element</li></ul><p>Let\'s take a look at an example of CSS code:</p><p><code>body {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;background-color: #f2f2f2;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;font-family: Arial, sans-serif;<br>}<br><br>h1 {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;color: #00698f;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;font-size: 36px;<br>}</code></p><h3>JavaScript</h3><p>JavaScript is used to add interactive elements to a web page. It consists of a series of scripts, known as functions, that are executed by a web browser. For example, you can use JavaScript to create a dropdown menu, or to validate a form.</p><p>Some common JavaScript concepts include:</p><ul><li><strong>variables</strong> - used to store values</li><li><strong>functions</strong> - used to execute blocks of code</li><li><strong>conditional statements</strong> - used to make decisions</li><li><strong>loops</strong> - used to repeat blocks of code</li><li><strong>events</strong> - used to respond to user interactions</li></ul><p>Let\'s take a look at an example of JavaScript code:</p><p><code>let name = \'John Doe\';<br><br>function greet() {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(\'Hello, \' + name);<br>}<br><br>greet();</code></p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In conclusion, building a simple web page requires a combination of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. HTML is used to create the structure and content of a web page, while CSS is used to control the layout and appearance. JavaScript is used to add interactive elements and respond to user interactions. By combining these three technologies, you can create a dynamic and engaging web page that provides a great user experience.</p>', 'Building a Simple Web Page', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-26 12:37:47'),
(58, 71, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Web Development Basics Quiz and Review</h3>\n<h4>HTML Review</h4>\n<p>HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language. It is used to create structure and content on a web page.</p>\n\n<h4>Example of HTML Code:</h4>\n<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Web Development Basics&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Web Development Basics&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;HTML&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;CSS&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;JavaScript&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Strong text&lt;/strong&gt; is used to make text bold.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>CSS Review</h4>\n<p>CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets. It is used to control the layout and appearance of a web page.</p>\n\n<h4>Example of CSS Code:</h4>\n<pre><code>&lt;style&gt;\n  body {\n    background-color: #f2f2f2;\n  }\n  h1 {\n    color: blue;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>JavaScript Review</h4>\n<p>JavaScript is a programming language used to add interactivity to a web page.</p>\n\n<h4>Example of JavaScript Code:</h4>\n<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;\n  console.log(\'Hello World!\');\n&lt;/script&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Quiz Time!</h4>\n\n<h4>HTML, CSS, and JavaScript Quiz:</h4>\n\n<h4>1. What is the purpose of the &lt;title&gt; tag in HTML?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) To create a heading</li>\n  <li>B) To set the title of the webpage</li>\n  <li>C) To create a link</li>\n  <li>D) To create a paragraph</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>2. Which of the following is a valid CSS selector?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) #header h1</li>\n  <li>B) header h1</li>\n  <li>C) header h2</li>\n  <li>D) header h3</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>3. What is the purpose of the &lt;script&gt; tag in HTML?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) To create a heading</li>\n  <li>B) To add interactivity to a webpage</li>\n  <li>C) To create a link</li>\n  <li>D) To create a paragraph</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4. Which of the following is a valid HTML element?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) &lt;p&gt;</li>\n  <li>B) &lt;div&gt;</li>\n  <li>C) &lt;span&gt;</li>\n  <li>D) &lt;img&gt;</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>5. What is the purpose of the &lt;style&gt; tag in HTML?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) To create a heading</li>\n  <li>B) To add interactivity to a webpage</li>\n  <li>C) To control the layout and appearance of a webpage</li>\n  <li>D) To create a link</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>6. Which of the following is a valid CSS property?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) color</li>\n  <li>B) background-color</li>\n  <li>C) font-size</li>\n  <li>D) All of the above</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>7. What is the purpose of the &lt;ul&gt; tag in HTML?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) To create a heading</li>\n  <li>B) To create an unordered list</li>\n  <li>C) To create a link</li>\n  <li>D) To create a paragraph</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>8. Which of the following is a valid JavaScript function?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) function helloWorld() { console.log(\'Hello World!\'); }</li>\n  <li>B) function helloWorld() { console.log(\'Hello World!\'); return 0; }</li>\n  <li>C) function helloWorld() { console.log(\'Hello World!\'); return \'Hello World!\'; }</li>\n  <li>D) function helloWorld() { console.log(\'Hello World!\'); }</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>9. What is the purpose of the &lt;code&gt; tag in HTML?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) To create a heading</li>\n  <li>B) To highlight code</li>\n  <li>C) To create a link</li>\n  <li>D) To create a paragraph</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>10. Which of the following is a valid CSS value for the background-color property?</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>A) red</li>\n  <li>B) #ff0000</li>\n  <li>C) rgb(255, 0, 0)</li>\n  <li>D) All of the above</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Quiz Answers:</h4>\n\n<h4>1. B) To set the title of the webpage</h4>\n\n<h4>2. A) #header h1</h4>\n\n<h4>3. B) To add interactivity to a webpage</h4>\n\n<h4>4. A) &lt;p&gt;</h4>\n\n<h4>5. C) To control the layout and appearance of a webpage</h4>\n\n<h4>6. D) All of the above</h4>\n\n<h4>7. B) To create an unordered list</h4>\n\n<h4>8. A) function helloWorld() { console.log(\'Hello World!\'); }</h4>\n\n<h4>9. B) To highlight code</h4>\n\n<h4>10. D) All of the above</h4>\n', 'Web Development Basics Quiz and Review', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(59, 72, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: html structure basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=html+structure+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/html+structure+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/html+structure+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/html+structure+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=html+structure+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to HTML Structure</h3><p>Welcome to Day 8 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be covering the basics of HTML structure, including headings, paragraphs, links, and images. HTML (Hypertext Markup Language) is the standard markup language used to create web pages.</p><p>HTML documents are made up of a series of elements, which are represented by tags. These tags are used to define the structure and content of a web page. In this lesson, we will be focusing on the basic structure of an HTML document.</p><h3>HTML Basics</h3><p>Every HTML document starts with a <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> tag, which is the root element of the document. Inside the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> tag, there are two main elements: <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>. The <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> element contains metadata about the document, such as the title, character encoding, and links to external stylesheets or scripts.</p><p>The <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> element contains the content of the HTML document. This is where you will put all the headings, paragraphs, links, images, and other elements that make up the web page.</p><h3>Headings</h3><p>Headings are used to define the structure of a web page. There are six levels of headings, ranging from <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> (the most important) to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> (the least important). Headings are defined using the following tags: <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h4&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h5&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>Heading 1: &lt;h1&gt;</strong> - used for the main title of the page</li><li><strong>Heading 2: &lt;h2&gt;</strong> - used for subheadings</li><li><strong>Heading 3: &lt;h3&gt;</strong> - used for sub-subheadings</li><li><strong>Heading 4: &lt;h4&gt;</strong> - used for minor headings</li><li><strong>Heading 5: &lt;h5&gt;</strong> - used for very minor headings</li><li><strong>Heading 6: &lt;h6&gt;</strong> - used for the least important headings</li></ul><h3>Paragraphs</h3><p>Paragraphs are used to define blocks of text. They are defined using the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag. You can put any amount of text inside a paragraph element, and it will be displayed as a block of text.</p><p>For example: <code>&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;</code></p><h3>Links</h3><p>Links are used to connect to other web pages or resources. They are defined using the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag. The <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag has an attribute called <code>href</code>, which specifies the URL of the link.</p><p>For example: <code>&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.example.com&quot;&gt;Visit example.com&lt;/a&gt;</code></p><h3>Images</h3><p>Images are used to add visual interest to a web page. They are defined using the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag. The <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag has two main attributes: <code>src</code> and <code>alt</code>. The <code>src</code> attribute specifies the URL of the image, and the <code>alt</code> attribute specifies the text to display if the image cannot be loaded.</p><p>For example: <code>&lt;img src=&quot;image.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;An image&quot;&gt;</code></p>', 'Introduction to HTML Structure', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(60, 73, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Fundamentals</h3><p>Welcome to Day 9 of our Web Development course, where we will be covering the basics of CSS. CSS, or Cascading Style Sheets, is a styling language used to control the layout and appearance of web pages. In this lesson, we will be discussing CSS syntax, selectors, properties, and the box model.</p><h3>CSS Syntax</h3><p>CSS syntax is made up of a set of rules, known as styles, that are applied to elements on a web page. A style consists of a selector, a property, and a value. The selector is used to target the element, the property is the aspect of the element that we want to change, and the value is the new value that we want to assign to the property.</p><p>For example, the following CSS code: <code>p { color: blue; }</code> selects all paragraph elements on the web page and sets their text color to blue.</p><h3>Selectors and Properties</h3><p>Selectors are used to target specific elements on a web page. There are several types of selectors, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Element Selectors</strong>: These select elements based on their tag name. For example, <code>p</code> selects all paragraph elements.</li><li><strong>Class Selectors</strong>: These select elements based on their class attribute. For example, <code>.header</code> selects all elements with a class of \'header\'.</li><li><strong>ID Selectors</strong>: These select elements based on their ID attribute. For example, <code>#header</code> selects the element with an ID of \'header\'.</li></ul><p>Properties are the aspects of an element that we want to change. Some common properties include:</p><ul><li><strong>Color</strong>: This sets the text color of an element.</li><li><strong>Background-color</strong>: This sets the background color of an element.</li><li><strong>Font-size</strong>: This sets the font size of an element.</li></ul><p>For example, the following CSS code: <code>.header { background-color: #f2f2f2; font-size: 24px; }</code> selects all elements with a class of \'header\' and sets their background color to a light gray and their font size to 24 pixels.</p><h3>Box Model</h3><p>The box model is a fundamental concept in CSS that describes the structure of an element. The box model consists of four parts:</p><ul><li><strong>Content Area</strong>: This is the area where the content of the element is displayed.</li><li><strong>Padding</strong>: This is the space between the content area and the border.</li><li><strong>Border</strong>: This is the visible outline of the element.</li><li><strong>Margin</strong>: This is the space between the element and other elements on the web page.</li></ul><p>The following CSS code: <code>.box { width: 200px; height: 100px; padding: 20px; border: 1px solid black; margin: 10px; }</code> creates a box that is 200 pixels wide, 100 pixels high, with a padding of 20 pixels, a border that is 1 pixel wide and solid black, and a margin of 10 pixels.</p><p>In conclusion, CSS syntax, selectors, properties, and the box model are all important concepts to understand when working with CSS. By mastering these concepts, you will be able to create visually appealing and well-structured web pages.</p>', 'CSS Fundamentals', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
