(61, 74, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css layout and positioning tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+layout+and+positioning+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+layout+and+positioning+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+layout+and+positioning+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+layout+and+positioning+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+layout+and+positioning+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Layout and Positioning</h3><p>Welcome to Day 10 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be exploring the world of CSS layout and positioning. This is a crucial topic in web development, as it allows us to control the arrangement and placement of elements on our web pages.</p><h3>Display Property</h3><p>The display property is used to specify how an element is displayed. It can take on several values, including <code>block</code>, <code>inline</code>, <code>inline-block</code>, and <code>none</code>. The <code>block</code> value displays an element as a block element, taking up the full width of its parent container. The <code>inline</code> value displays an element as an inline element, allowing other elements to be placed next to it. The <code>inline-block</code> value displays an element as an inline element, but also allows us to set its width and height. The <code>none</code> value hides an element from view.</p><ul><li><strong>Block-level elements</strong> include <code>h1</code>, <code>p</code>, <code>div</code>, and <code>ul</code>. These elements take up the full width of their parent container and start on a new line.</li><li><strong>Inline elements</strong> include <code>span</code>, <code>a</code>, and <code>img</code>. These elements do not take up the full width of their parent container and do not start on a new line.</li></ul><h3>Position Property</h3><p>The position property is used to specify the positioning scheme of an element. It can take on several values, including <code>static</code>, <code>relative</code>, <code>absolute</code>, <code>fixed</code>, and <code>sticky</code>. The <code>static</code> value is the default value, and it means that an element is positioned according to the normal flow of the document. The <code>relative</code> value positions an element relative to its normal position. The <code>absolute</code> value positions an element relative to its nearest positioned ancestor. The <code>fixed</code> value positions an element relative to the viewport. The <code>sticky</code> value positions an element relative to its nearest positioned ancestor, but also allows it to be scrolled.</p><ul><li><strong>Static positioning</strong> is the default positioning scheme, and it means that an element is positioned according to the normal flow of the document.</li><li><strong>Relative positioning</strong> allows us to position an element relative to its normal position. We can use the <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties to specify the position of the element.</li><li><strong>Absolute positioning</strong> allows us to position an element relative to its nearest positioned ancestor. We can use the <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties to specify the position of the element.</li><li><strong>Fixed positioning</strong> allows us to position an element relative to the viewport. We can use the <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties to specify the position of the element.</li><li><strong>Sticky positioning</strong> allows us to position an element relative to its nearest positioned ancestor, but also allows it to be scrolled. We can use the <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties to specify the position of the element.</li></ul><h3>Float and Clear</h3><p>The float property is used to specify how an element should float within its parent container. It can take on several values, including <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, and <code>none</code>. The <code>left</code> value floats an element to the left of its parent container, while the <code>right</code> value floats an element to the right of its parent container. The <code>none</code> value means that an element does not float.</p><p>The clear property is used to specify how an element should behave when it encounters a floated element. It can take on several values, including <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>both</code>, and <code>none</code>. The <code>left</code> value clears an element to the left of a floated element, while the <code>right</code> value clears an element to the right of a floated element. The <code>both</code> value clears an element to both the left and right of a floated element. The <code>none</code> value means that an element does not clear.</p><ul><li><strong>Float</strong> allows us to float an element to the left or right of its parent container.</li><li><strong>Clear</strong> allows us to clear an element to the left or right of a floated element.</li></ul>', 'CSS Layout and Positioning', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(62, 75, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript</h3><p>JavaScript is a high-level, dynamic, and interpreted programming language that is primarily used for client-side scripting on the web. It allows developers to create interactive web pages, web applications, and mobile applications. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of JavaScript, including variables, data types, operators, and control structures.</p><h3>JavaScript Basics</h3><p>JavaScript is a scripting language that is executed on the client-side, meaning that it runs on the user\'s web browser. It is used to add interactivity to web pages, create dynamic effects, and respond to user interactions. JavaScript is also used in mobile and desktop applications, game development, and server-side programming.</p><p>Some of the key features of JavaScript include:</p><ul><li>Dynamic typing: JavaScript is a dynamically-typed language, which means that you do not need to declare the data type of a variable before using it.</li><li>First-class functions: JavaScript functions are first-class citizens, which means that they can be passed as arguments to other functions, returned as values from functions, and stored in variables.</li><li>Prototype-based inheritance: JavaScript uses a prototype-based inheritance model, which allows objects to inherit properties and methods from other objects.</li></ul><h3>Variables and Data Types</h3><p>In JavaScript, a variable is a name given to a value. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in a program. There are several data types in JavaScript, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Number</strong>: The number data type represents a numeric value, such as 1, 2, or 3.14.</li><li><strong>String</strong>: The string data type represents a sequence of characters, such as \'hello\' or \"hello\".</li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: The boolean data type represents a true or false value.</li><li><strong>Null</strong>: The null data type represents the absence of any value.</li><li><strong>Undefined</strong>: The undefined data type represents an uninitialized variable or a variable that has not been declared.</li><li><strong>Object</strong>: The object data type represents a collection of key-value pairs, such as { name: \'John\', age: 30 }.</li><li><strong>Array</strong>: The array data type represents a collection of values, such as [1, 2, 3] or [\'a\', \'b\', \'c\'].</li></ul><p>JavaScript variables are declared using the <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, or <code>var</code> keywords. For example:</p><code>let name = \'John\';<br>const age = 30;<br>var occupation = \'Developer\';</code><h3>Operators and Control Structures</h3><p>JavaScript has a variety of operators that can be used to perform arithmetic, comparison, logical, and assignment operations. Some of the most common operators include:</p><ul><li><strong>Arithmetic operators</strong>: +, -, \\*, /, %, etc.</li><li><strong>Comparison operators</strong>: ==, !=, ===, !==, >, <, >= , <=</li><li><strong>Logical operators</strong>: &&, ||, !</li><li><strong>Assignment operators</strong>: =, +=, -=, \\*=, /=, %=, etc.</li></ul><p>Control structures are used to control the flow of a program\'s execution. Some of the most common control structures in JavaScript include:</p><ul><li><strong>If-else statements</strong>: Used to execute a block of code if a condition is true or false.</li><li><strong>Switch statements</strong>: Used to execute a block of code based on the value of a variable or expression.</li><li><strong>Loops</strong>: Used to execute a block of code repeatedly, such as a for loop or while loop.</li></ul><p>For example:</p><code>if (age > 18) {<br>  console.log(\'You are an adult.\');<br>} else {<br>  console.log(\'You are a minor.\');<br>}</code><p>This code checks if the value of the <code>age</code> variable is greater than 18. If it is, it logs \'You are an adult.\' to the console. Otherwise, it logs \'You are a minor.\'.</p>', 'Introduction to JavaScript', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(63, 76, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript functions and events</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+functions+and+events\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+functions+and+events\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+functions+and+events/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+functions+and+events/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+functions+and+events\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Functions and Events</h3><p>Welcome to Day 12 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be covering three important topics in JavaScript: Functions, Event Listeners, and DOM Manipulation. By the end of this lesson, you will have a solid understanding of how to create and use functions, attach event listeners to elements, and manipulate the Document Object Model (DOM) to create dynamic web pages.</p><h3>Functions</h3><p>A function is a block of code that can be executed multiple times from different parts of your program. Functions are useful for organizing your code, reducing repetition, and making your programs more modular and reusable. In JavaScript, you can define a function using the <code>function</code> keyword followed by the name of the function and a list of parameters in parentheses.</p><p>Here is an example of a simple JavaScript function:</p><code>function greet(name) {<br>  console.log(\'Hello, \' + name);<br>}</code><p>This function takes one parameter, <code>name</code>, and logs a greeting message to the console. You can call this function by its name, followed by the parameter in parentheses:</p><code>greet(\'John\');</code><p>This would output <code>Hello, John</code> to the console.</p><h3>Event Listeners</h3><p>An event listener is a function that is attached to an element and executed in response to a specific event, such as a click, hover, or key press. Event listeners are used to add interactivity to your web pages and respond to user actions. In JavaScript, you can attach an event listener to an element using the <code>addEventListener</code> method.</p><p>Here is an example of attaching an event listener to a button element:</p><code>const button = document.getElementById(\'myButton\');<br>button.addEventListener(\'click\', function() {<br>  console.log(\'Button clicked!\');<br>});</code><p>In this example, we first get a reference to the button element using <code>document.getElementById</code>. Then, we attach an event listener to the button using <code>addEventListener</code>. The first argument is the type of event we want to listen for (in this case, a <code>click</code> event), and the second argument is the function that will be executed when the event occurs.</p><h3>DOM Manipulation</h3><p>The Document Object Model (DOM) is a programming interface for HTML and XML documents. It represents the structure of a document as a tree of nodes, where each node represents an element, attribute, or piece of text. DOM manipulation refers to the process of dynamically adding, removing, or modifying elements and attributes in the DOM.</p><p>In JavaScript, you can manipulate the DOM using various methods and properties, such as <code>createElement</code>, <code>appendChild</code>, <code>removeChild</code>, and <code>innerHTML</code>. Here is an example of creating a new paragraph element and adding it to the page:</p><code>const paragraph = document.createElement(\'p\');<br>paragraph.textContent = \'This is a new paragraph.\';<br>document.body.appendChild(paragraph);</code><p>This code creates a new <code>p</code> element, sets its text content, and appends it to the <code>body</code> element of the document.</p><p>Some common DOM manipulation methods include:</p><ul><li><code>createElement</code>: creates a new element</li><li><code>appendChild</code>: adds a new child element to a parent element</li><li><code>removeChild</code>: removes a child element from a parent element</li><li><code>innerHTML</code>: sets or gets the HTML content of an element</li><li><code>textContent</code>: sets or gets the text content of an element</li></ul><p>Some common DOM events include:</p><ul><li><code>click</code>: triggered when an element is clicked</li><li><code>hover</code>: triggered when an element is hovered over</li><li><code>keydown</code>: triggered when a key is pressed</li><li><code>load</code>: triggered when a page or resource is loaded</li></ul><p>By combining functions, event listeners, and DOM manipulation, you can create dynamic and interactive web pages that respond to user actions and events.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Functions are reusable blocks of code that can be executed multiple times</li><li>Event listeners are functions that are attached to elements and executed in response to specific events</li><li>DOM manipulation refers to the process of dynamically adding, removing, or modifying elements and attributes in the DOM</li></ul>', 'JavaScript Functions and Events', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(64, 77, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development tools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Development Tools and Resources</h3><p>Welcome to Day 13 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be exploring the various tools and resources that are essential for any web developer. These tools will help you write, manage, and debug your code more efficiently.</p><h3>Code Editors</h3><p>A code editor is a software application that allows you to write, edit, and manage your code. There are many code editors available, each with its own set of features and functionalities. Some popular code editors include:</p><ul><li><strong>Visual Studio Code (VS Code)</strong>: A free, open-source code editor developed by Microsoft. It is known for its speed, flexibility, and extensive library of extensions.</li><li><strong>Sublime Text</strong>: A popular code editor that is known for its speed, ease of use, and extensive library of plugins.</li><li><strong>Atom</strong>: A free, open-source code editor that is known for its customizability and flexibility.</li></ul><p>When choosing a code editor, consider the following factors:</p><ul><li><strong>Language support</strong>: Does the code editor support the programming languages you want to use?</li><li><strong>Extensions and plugins</strong>: Are there extensions and plugins available to enhance the functionality of the code editor?</li><li><strong>User interface</strong>: Is the user interface intuitive and easy to use?</li></ul><h3>Version Control Systems</h3><p>A version control system is a software application that helps you manage and track changes to your code. It allows you to collaborate with others, revert to previous versions of your code, and manage different versions of your project. Some popular version control systems include:</p><ul><li><strong>Git</strong>: A free, open-source version control system that is widely used in the web development community.</li><li><strong>Mercurial</strong>: A free, open-source version control system that is known for its speed and ease of use.</li><li><strong>Subversion (SVN)</strong>: A version control system that is widely used in enterprise environments.</li></ul><p>To use a version control system, you will need to:</p><ul><li><strong>Initialize a repository</strong>: Create a new repository for your project using the version control system.</li><li><strong>Commit changes</strong>: Commit changes to your code using the <code>commit</code> command.</li><li><strong>Push changes</strong>: Push changes to a remote repository using the <code>push</code> command.</li></ul><h3>Debugging Tools</h3><p>Debugging tools are software applications that help you identify and fix errors in your code. Some popular debugging tools include:</p><ul><li><strong>Chrome DevTools</strong>: A set of debugging tools that are built into the Google Chrome browser.</li><li><strong>Firefox Developer Edition</strong>: A version of the Firefox browser that includes a set of debugging tools.</li><li><strong>Console.log</strong>: A JavaScript function that allows you to log messages to the console.</li></ul><p>To use debugging tools, you will need to:</p><ul><li><strong>Open the debugging tool</strong>: Open the debugging tool, such as Chrome DevTools or Firefox Developer Edition.</li><li><strong>Set breakpoints</strong>: Set breakpoints in your code to pause execution and inspect variables.</li><li><strong>Inspect variables</strong>: Inspect variables and expressions to understand the state of your application.</li></ul>', 'Web Development Tools and Resources', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(65, 78, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Web Development Best Practices</h3><p>As a web developer, it\'s essential to follow best practices to ensure your website is maintainable, efficient, and secure. In this section, we\'ll cover code organization, performance optimization, and security considerations.</p><h3>Code Organization</h3><p>Code organization is critical to maintaining a clean and scalable codebase. Here are some tips to help you keep your code organized:</p><ul><li>Use a consistent naming convention: Choose a naming convention and stick to it throughout your code. This will make it easier for others to understand your code and for you to maintain it.</li><li>Use modular code: Break down your code into smaller, independent modules. This will make it easier to update and maintain individual components without affecting the entire codebase.</li><li>Use comments: Comments are essential for explaining what your code does and why. This will help others understand your code and make it easier for you to remember what you did.</li><li>Use version control: Version control systems like Git allow you to track changes to your code and collaborate with others.</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following code structure:</p><code>{<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;header&quot;: {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;title&quot;: &quot;My Website&quot;,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;nav&quot;: [&#39;Home&#39;, &#39;About&#39;, &#39;Contact&#39;]<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;},<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;main&quot;: {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;content&quot;: &quot;Welcome to my website&quot;,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;footer&quot;: &quot;Copyright 2024&quot;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br>}</code><h3>Performance Optimization</h3><p>Performance optimization is crucial for ensuring your website loads quickly and efficiently. Here are some tips to help you optimize your website\'s performance:</p><ul><li><strong>Minify and compress files</strong>: Minifying and compressing files reduces their size, making them faster to download.</li><li><strong>Use caching</strong>: Caching stores frequently-used resources in memory, reducing the need for repeated requests to the server.</li><li><strong>Optimize images</strong>: Compressing images reduces their file size, making them faster to download.</li><li><strong>Use a content delivery network (CDN)</strong>: A CDN stores copies of your website\'s resources in different locations, reducing the distance between users and your website.</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following code to minify and compress files:</p><code>gulp.task(&#39;minify&#39;, function() {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;gulp.src(&#39;js/*.js&#39;)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;.pipe(uglify())<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;.pipe(gulp.dest(&#39;dist/js&#39;));<br>});</code><h3>Security Considerations</h3><p>Security is a critical aspect of web development. Here are some tips to help you secure your website:</p><ul><li><strong>Validate user input</strong>: Validating user input helps prevent SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.</li><li><strong>Use HTTPS</strong>: HTTPS encrypts data transmitted between the client and server, making it more difficult for hackers to intercept sensitive information.</li><li><strong>Use secure passwords</strong>: Using secure passwords and salting and hashing passwords helps prevent unauthorized access to your website.</li><li><strong>Keep software up-to-date</strong>: Keeping your software and dependencies up-to-date helps prevent vulnerabilities and exploits.</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following code to validate user input:</p><code>if (isset($_POST[&#39;username&#39;]) && isset($_POST[&#39;password&#39;])) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;$username = $_POST[&#39;username&#39;];<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;$password = $_POST[&#39;password&#39;];<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;if (validate_input($username, $password)) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;// Login successful<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;} else {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;// Login failed<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br>}</code>', 'Web Development Best Practices', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(66, 79, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: html forms tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=html+forms+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/html+forms+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/html+forms+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/html+forms+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=html+forms+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to HTML Forms</h3><p>HTML forms are used to collect user input, and they are a crucial part of any web application. In this section, we will cover the basics of HTML forms, form validation, and form submission.</p><p>A basic HTML form consists of a <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element, which contains various input elements such as text fields, checkboxes, and buttons. The <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element has several attributes, including <code>action</code>, <code>method</code>, and <code>enctype</code>.</p><ul><li><code>action</code>: specifies the URL where the form data will be sent</li><li><code>method</code>: specifies the HTTP method to use when sending the form data (e.g., GET or POST)</li><li><code>enctype</code>: specifies how the form data will be encoded when sent to the server</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic HTML form:</p><p><code>&lt;form action=\"/submit\" method=\"post\"&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;label for=\"name\"&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;input type=\"text\" id=\"name\" name=\"name\"&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;label for=\"email\"&gt;Email:&lt;/label&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\"&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;input type=\"submit\" value=\"Submit\"&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;/form&gt;</code></p><h3>Form Validation</h3><p>Form validation is used to ensure that the user enters valid data into the form. There are two types of form validation: client-side validation and server-side validation.</p><p>Client-side validation is performed using JavaScript and is used to validate the form data before it is sent to the server. This type of validation is useful for checking simple errors, such as empty fields or invalid email addresses.</p><p>Server-side validation is performed using a programming language such as PHP or Python and is used to validate the form data after it has been sent to the server. This type of validation is useful for checking more complex errors, such as duplicate usernames or invalid passwords.</p><p>Here is an example of client-side form validation using JavaScript:</p><p><code>&lt;script&gt;</code><br><code>const form = document.querySelector(\'form\');</code><br><code>form.addEventListener(\'submit\', (e) =&gt; {</code><br><code>  const name = document.querySelector(\'#name\').value;</code><br><code>  const email = document.querySelector(\'#email\').value;</code><br><code>  if (name === \'\' || email === \'\') {</code><br><code>    e.preventDefault();</code><br><code>    alert(\'Please fill in all fields.\');</code><br><code>  }</code><br><code>});</code><br><code>&lt;/script&gt;</code></p><h3>Form Submission</h3><p>When a form is submitted, the form data is sent to the server using the HTTP method specified in the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element. The server then processes the form data and returns a response to the client.</p><p>There are two ways to submit a form: using the <code>&lt;input type=\"submit\"&gt;</code> element or using JavaScript.</p><p>Here is an example of submitting a form using the <code>&lt;input type=\"submit\"&gt;</code> element:</p><p><code>&lt;form action=\"/submit\" method=\"post\"&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;label for=\"name\"&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;input type=\"text\" id=\"name\" name=\"name\"&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;label for=\"email\"&gt;Email:&lt;/email&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\"&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;input type=\"submit\" value=\"Submit\"&gt;</code><br><code>&lt;/form&gt;</code></p><p>And here is an example of submitting a form using JavaScript:</p><p><code>&lt;script&gt;</code><br><code>const form = document.querySelector(\'form\');</code><br><code>form.addEventListener(\'submit\', (e) =&gt; {</code><br><code>  e.preventDefault();</code><br><code>  const formData = new FormData(form);</code><br><code>  fetch(\'/submit\', {</code><br><code>    method: \'POST\',</code><br><code>    body: formData</code><br><code>  })</code><br><code>  .then((response) =&gt; response.json())</code><br><code>  .then((data) =&gt; console.log(data))</code><br><code>  .catch((error) =&gt; console.error(error));</code><br><code>});</code><br><code>&lt;/script&gt;</code></p><p>In conclusion, HTML forms are a powerful tool for collecting user input, and they are a crucial part of any web application. By using form validation and submission, you can ensure that your forms are secure and user-friendly.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>HTML forms are used to collect user input</li><li>Form validation is used to ensure that the user enters valid data</li><li>Form submission is used to send the form data to the server</li><li>Client-side validation is performed using JavaScript</li><li>Server-side validation is performed using a programming language such as PHP or Python</li></ul>', 'Introduction to HTML Forms', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(67, 80, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css layouts and positioning</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+layouts+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+layouts+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+layouts+and+positioning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+layouts+and+positioning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+layouts+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Layouts and Positioning</h3><p>Welcome to Day 16 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be covering CSS Grid, CSS Flexbox, and CSS Positioning. These are essential concepts for creating responsive and visually appealing web pages.</p><p>CSS Grid and CSS Flexbox are two powerful layout systems that allow you to create complex layouts with ease. CSS Positioning, on the other hand, allows you to position elements in your web page with precision.</p><h3>CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid is a two-dimensional grid system that allows you to create complex layouts. It is based on a grid container and grid items. The grid container is the parent element that contains the grid items.</p><ul><li><strong>Grid Container:</strong> The grid container is the parent element that contains the grid items. You can create a grid container by setting the <code>display</code> property to <code>grid</code> or <code>inline-grid</code>.</li><li><strong>Grid Items:</strong> Grid items are the child elements of the grid container. You can create grid items by placing them inside the grid container.</li><li><strong>Grid Tracks:</strong> Grid tracks are the rows and columns of the grid. You can define grid tracks using the <code>grid-template-rows</code> and <code>grid-template-columns</code> properties.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic CSS Grid layout:</p><code>.grid-container {<br>  display: grid;<br>  grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr;<br>  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;<br>}<br>.grid-item {<br>  background-color: #ccc;<br>  padding: 20px;<br>}</code><p>This code creates a grid container with two rows and two columns. The grid items are placed inside the grid container and are styled with a background color and padding.</p><h3>CSS Flexbox</h3><p>CSS Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout system that allows you to create flexible layouts. It is based on a flex container and flex items. The flex container is the parent element that contains the flex items.</p><ul><li><strong>Flex Container:</strong> The flex container is the parent element that contains the flex items. You can create a flex container by setting the <code>display</code> property to <code>flex</code> or <code>inline-flex</code>.</li><li><strong>Flex Items:</strong> Flex items are the child elements of the flex container. You can create flex items by placing them inside the flex container.</li><li><strong>Flex Direction:</strong> The flex direction determines the direction of the flex items. You can set the flex direction using the <code>flex-direction</code> property.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic CSS Flexbox layout:</p><code>.flex-container {<br>  display: flex;<br>  flex-direction: row;<br>}<br>.flex-item {<br>  background-color: #ccc;<br>  padding: 20px;<br>  margin: 10px;<br>}</code><p>This code creates a flex container with a row direction. The flex items are placed inside the flex container and are styled with a background color, padding, and margin.</p><h3>CSS Positioning</h3><p>CSS Positioning allows you to position elements in your web page with precision. There are five types of positioning: static, relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky.</p><ul><li><strong>Static Positioning:</strong> Static positioning is the default positioning type. Elements with static positioning are positioned in the normal document flow.</li><li><strong>Relative Positioning:</strong> Relative positioning allows you to position an element relative to its normal position. You can set the <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties to position the element.</li><li><strong>Absolute Positioning:</strong> Absolute positioning allows you to position an element relative to its nearest positioned ancestor. You can set the <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties to position the element.</li><li><strong>Fixed Positioning:</strong> Fixed positioning allows you to position an element relative to the viewport. You can set the <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties to position the element.</li><li><strong>Sticky Positioning:</strong> Sticky positioning allows you to position an element relative to its nearest positioned ancestor, while also keeping it within the viewport. You can set the <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code> properties to position the element.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic CSS Positioning layout:</p><code>.positioned-element {<br>  position: absolute;<br>  top: 50px;<br>  left: 50px;<br>}</code><p>This code creates an element with absolute positioning, 50 pixels from the top and left of its nearest positioned ancestor.</p>', 'CSS Layouts and Positioning', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(68, 81, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript Basics</h3><p>Welcome to Day 17 of our Web Development course, where we will be introducing the basics of JavaScript. JavaScript is a high-level, dynamic, and interpreted programming language that is primarily used for client-side scripting on the web. It allows you to create interactive web pages, web applications, and mobile applications.</p><p>In this lesson, we will cover the basics of JavaScript, including variables, data types, and operators. These are the building blocks of any programming language, and understanding them is crucial for creating robust and efficient JavaScript code.</p><h3>JavaScript Variables</h3><p>In JavaScript, a variable is a container that stores a value. You can think of it as a labeled box where you can store a value. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in your JavaScript code.</p><p>To declare a variable in JavaScript, you use the <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, or <code>var</code> keyword followed by the name of the variable and the assignment operator (=). For example:</p><ul><li><code>let x = 5;</code> declares a variable x and assigns it the value 5.</li><li><code>const PI = 3.14;</code> declares a constant variable PI and assigns it the value 3.14.</li><li><code>var name = \'John Doe\';</code> declares a variable name and assigns it the value \'John Doe\'.</li></ul><p>It\'s worth noting that <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> are block-scoped, meaning they are only accessible within the block they are defined in, while <code>var</code> is function-scoped, meaning it is accessible throughout the function it is defined in.</p><h3>JavaScript Data Types</h3><p>JavaScript has several built-in data types that determine the type of value a variable can hold. The main data types in JavaScript are:</p><ul><li><strong>Number</strong>: represents a numeric value, such as 1, 2, 3, etc.</li><li><strong>String</strong>: represents a sequence of characters, such as \'hello\', \'world\', etc. Strings are enclosed in single quotes or double quotes.</li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: represents a true or false value.</li><li><strong>Null</strong>: represents the absence of any object value.</li><li><strong>Undefined</strong>: represents an uninitialized variable or a variable that has not been declared.</li><li><strong>Object</strong>: represents a collection of key-value pairs, such as { name: \'John Doe\', age: 30 }.</li><li><strong>Array</strong>: represents a collection of values, such as [1, 2, 3], [\'a\', \'b\', \'c\'], etc.</li></ul><p>Understanding the different data types in JavaScript is important because it determines how you can use and manipulate the data in your code.</p><h3>JavaScript Operators</h3><p>JavaScript operators are used to perform operations on variables and values. There are several types of operators in JavaScript, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Arithmetic Operators</strong>: +, -, \\*, /, %, etc. These operators are used to perform arithmetic operations, such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, etc.</li><li><strong>Comparison Operators</strong>: ==, !=, ===, !==, >, <, >=, <=, etc. These operators are used to compare values and return a boolean result.</li><li><strong>Logical Operators</strong>: &&, ||, !. These operators are used to perform logical operations, such as AND, OR, and NOT.</li><li><strong>Assignment Operators</strong>: =, +=, -=, \\*=, /=, etc. These operators are used to assign a value to a variable.</li></ul><p>Understanding how to use operators in JavaScript is important because it allows you to perform operations on variables and values, and make decisions based on conditions.</p>', 'Introduction to JavaScript Basics', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
