(69, 82, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript functions tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding JavaScript Functions</h3><p>JavaScript functions are blocks of code that can be executed multiple times from different parts of your program. They are useful for organizing your code, reducing repetition, and making your programs more efficient.</p><p>In this lesson, we will cover three ways to declare functions in JavaScript: function declaration, function expression, and arrow functions.</p><h3>JavaScript Function Declaration</h3><p>A function declaration is the most common way to define a function in JavaScript. It consists of the <code>function</code> keyword followed by the name of the function and a list of parameters in parentheses.</p><p>The general syntax of a function declaration is:</p><ul><li><code>function functionName(parameter1, parameter2) { code to be executed }</code></li></ul><p>For example:</p><ul><li><code>function greet(name) { console.log(\'Hello, \' + name + \'!\'); }</code></li></ul><p>You can then call the function by its name, followed by the arguments in parentheses:</p><ul><li><code>greet(\'John\'); // Output: Hello, John!</code></li></ul><h3>JavaScript Function Expression</h3><p>A function expression is similar to a function declaration, but it is assigned to a variable. The syntax is:</p><ul><li><code>var functionName = function(parameter1, parameter2) { code to be executed };</code></li></ul><p>For example:</p><ul><li><code>var greet = function(name) { console.log(\'Hello, \' + name + \'!\'); };</code></li></ul><p>Function expressions are often used as arguments to other functions or as values in data structures.</p><h3>JavaScript Arrow Functions</h3><p>Arrow functions, also known as fat arrow functions, are a shorthand way to define functions. They consist of an argument list in parentheses, followed by an arrow (<code>=&gt;</code>), and then the function body.</p><p>The general syntax of an arrow function is:</p><ul><li><code>var functionName = (parameter1, parameter2) =&gt; { code to be executed };</code></li></ul><p>For example:</p><ul><li><code>var greet = (name) =&gt; { console.log(\'Hello, \' + name + \'!\'); };</code></li></ul><p>Arrow functions are often used when you need to define a small, one-time-use function, such as when sorting an array or handling an event.</p><p><strong>Key differences between function declaration, function expression, and arrow functions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Function Declaration:</strong> Can be called before they are defined, and the function name is hoisted to the top of the scope.</li><li><strong>Function Expression:</strong> Cannot be called before they are defined, and the variable name is hoisted to the top of the scope, but the function assignment is not.</li><li><strong>Arrow Functions:</strong> Similar to function expressions, but with a more concise syntax and some differences in the way they handle <code>this</code> and <code>arguments</code>.</li></ul><p>In summary, JavaScript functions are a powerful tool for organizing and reusing code. Understanding the different ways to declare functions, including function declaration, function expression, and arrow functions, will help you write more efficient and effective JavaScript code.</p>', 'Understanding JavaScript Functions', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(70, 83, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript dom manipulation</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+dom+manipulation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+dom+manipulation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript DOM Manipulation</h3><p>JavaScript DOM manipulation is a crucial aspect of web development, allowing you to dynamically interact with and modify the structure and content of a web page. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of JavaScript DOM selection, creation, and events.</p><h3>JavaScript DOM Selection</h3><p>DOM selection is the process of targeting specific elements within a web page. This can be achieved using various methods, including:</p><ul><li><code>document.getElementById(\'id\')</code>: selects an element with the specified ID</li><li><code>document.getElementsByClassName(\'class\')</code>: selects all elements with the specified class</li><li><code>document.getElementsByTagName(\'tag\')</code>: selects all elements with the specified tag name</li><li><code>document.querySelector(\'selector\')</code>: selects the first element that matches the specified CSS selector</li><li><code>document.querySelectorAll(\'selector\')</code>: selects all elements that match the specified CSS selector</li></ul><p>For example, to select an element with the ID <code>header</code>, you can use the following code: <code>const header = document.getElementById(\'header\');</code></p><h3>JavaScript DOM Creation</h3><p>DOM creation involves dynamically adding new elements to a web page. This can be achieved using the following methods:</p><ul><li><code>document.createElement(\'tag\')</code>: creates a new element with the specified tag name</li><li><code>document.createTextNode(\'text\')</code>: creates a new text node with the specified text</li><li><code>element.appendChild(child)</code>: adds a child element to the specified parent element</li><li><code>element.insertBefore(child, reference)</code>: inserts a child element before the specified reference element</li></ul><p>For example, to create a new paragraph element and add it to the body of the document, you can use the following code: <code>const paragraph = document.createElement(\'p\'); paragraph.textContent = \'This is a new paragraph.\'; document.body.appendChild(paragraph);</code></p><h3>JavaScript DOM Events</h3><p>DOM events allow you to respond to user interactions, such as clicks, hover, and keyboard input. This can be achieved using the following methods:</p><ul><li><code>element.addEventListener(\'event\', function)</code>: adds an event listener to the specified element</li><li><code>element.removeEventListener(\'event\', function)</code>: removes an event listener from the specified element</li></ul><p>For example, to add a click event listener to a button element, you can use the following code: <code>const button = document.getElementById(\'button\'); button.addEventListener(\'click\', function() { alert(\'Button clicked!\'); });</code></p><p>Some common DOM events include:</p><ul><li><code>click</code>: triggered when an element is clicked</li><li><code>mouseover</code>: triggered when the mouse is moved over an element</li><li><code>mouseout</code>: triggered when the mouse is moved out of an element</li><li><code>keydown</code>: triggered when a key is pressed</li><li><code>keyup</code>: triggered when a key is released</li></ul><p>By mastering JavaScript DOM manipulation, you can create dynamic and interactive web pages that respond to user input and provide a rich user experience.</p>', 'JavaScript DOM Manipulation', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(71, 84, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: front-end frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Front-end Frameworks</h3><p>Welcome to Day 20 of our Web Development course, where we will be exploring the world of front-end frameworks. Front-end frameworks are essential tools for building dynamic, interactive, and responsive web applications. In this lesson, we will introduce three popular front-end frameworks: React, Angular, and Vue.js.</p><p>Front-end frameworks provide a structured approach to building web applications, making it easier to manage complexity, reuse code, and collaborate with team members. They offer a set of pre-built components, tools, and libraries that simplify the development process and improve application performance.</p><h3>React</h3><p>React is a JavaScript library developed by Facebook. It is one of the most popular front-end frameworks used in web development. React allows you to build reusable UI components, making it easy to manage complex interfaces. Its key features include:</p><ul><li><strong>Virtual DOM</strong>: React uses a virtual DOM to optimize rendering and reduce the number of DOM mutations.</li><li><strong>JSX</strong>: React uses JSX, a syntax extension for JavaScript, to make it easier to write HTML-like code in your JavaScript files.</li><li><strong>Components</strong>: React encourages a component-based architecture, making it easy to reuse code and manage complexity.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple React component: <code>function Hello() { return <h1>Hello World!</h1>; }</code></p><h3>Angular</h3><p>Angular is a full-fledged JavaScript framework developed by Google. It is a popular choice for building complex, data-driven web applications. Angular provides a rich set of features, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Templates</strong>: Angular uses HTML templates to define the structure of your application.</li><li><strong>Dependency Injection</strong>: Angular provides a built-in dependency injection system, making it easy to manage dependencies between components.</li><li><strong>Services</strong>: Angular provides a set of built-in services, such as HTTP and routing, to simplify application development.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple Angular component: <code>@Component({ selector: \'app-hello\', template: \'<h1>Hello World!</h1>\' })</code></p><h3>Vue.js</h3><p>Vue.js is a progressive and flexible JavaScript framework developed by Evan You. It is known for its simplicity, ease of use, and robust ecosystem. Vue.js provides a set of features, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Templates</strong>: Vue.js uses HTML templates to define the structure of your application.</li><li><strong>Reactivity</strong>: Vue.js provides a reactive system, making it easy to manage state changes and updates.</li><li><strong>Components</strong>: Vue.js encourages a component-based architecture, making it easy to reuse code and manage complexity.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple Vue.js component: <code>new Vue({ el: \'#hello\', data: { message: \'Hello World!\' } })</code></p><p>In conclusion, React, Angular, and Vue.js are three popular front-end frameworks used in web development. Each framework has its strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of which one to use depends on the specific needs of your project. By understanding the basics of each framework, you can make informed decisions and build robust, scalable, and maintainable web applications.</p>', 'Introduction to Front-end Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(73, 86, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css selectors tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+selectors+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+selectors+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+selectors+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+selectors+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+selectors+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Selectors</h3><p>CSS selectors are used to target specific elements on a web page and apply styles to them. They are a fundamental part of CSS and are used to select elements based on their name, attributes, and relationships with other elements.</p><p>In this lesson, we will cover the basics of CSS selectors, including the different types of selectors and how to use them to target elements on a web page.</p><h3>CSS Selector Types</h3><p>There are several types of CSS selectors, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Element Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements based on their name. For example, <code>p</code> would select all paragraph elements on a web page.</li><li><strong>Class Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements based on their class attribute. For example, <code>.header</code> would select all elements with a class of <code>header</code>.</li><li><strong>ID Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements based on their ID attribute. For example, <code>#header</code> would select the element with an ID of <code>header</code>.</li><li><strong>Attribute Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements based on their attributes. For example, <code>[hreflang]</code> would select all elements with a <code>hreflang</code> attribute.</li></ul><p>These are just a few examples of the different types of CSS selectors. There are many more, and we will cover them in more detail as we progress through the course.</p><h3>CSS Properties</h3><p>CSS properties are used to specify the styles that are applied to elements that are selected by CSS selectors. There are many different CSS properties, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Color Properties</strong>: These properties are used to specify the color of text, backgrounds, and borders. For example, <code>color: #fff;</code> would set the text color to white.</li><li><strong>Font Properties</strong>: These properties are used to specify the font family, size, and style of text. For example, <code>font-family: Arial, sans-serif;</code> would set the font family to Arial.</li><li><strong>Layout Properties</strong>: These properties are used to specify the layout of elements on a web page. For example, <code>display: block;</code> would set the display property of an element to block.</li></ul><p>These are just a few examples of the different types of CSS properties. There are many more, and we will cover them in more detail as we progress through the course.</p><h3>CSS Values</h3><p>CSS values are used to specify the values of CSS properties. There are many different types of CSS values, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Length Values</strong>: These values are used to specify lengths, such as the width or height of an element. For example, <code>width: 100px;</code> would set the width of an element to 100 pixels.</li><li><strong>Percentage Values</strong>: These values are used to specify percentages, such as the width or height of an element as a percentage of its parent element. For example, <code>width: 50%;</code> would set the width of an element to 50% of its parent element.</li><li><strong>Color Values</strong>: These values are used to specify colors, such as the color of text or backgrounds. For example, <code>color: #fff;</code> would set the text color to white.</li></ul><p>These are just a few examples of the different types of CSS values. There are many more, and we will cover them in more detail as we progress through the course.</p><p>In conclusion, CSS selectors, properties, and values are all important parts of CSS and are used to style and layout elements on a web page. By understanding how to use these different components, you can create complex and visually appealing web pages.</p>', 'Introduction to CSS Selectors', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(74, 87, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css box model diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+box+model+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+box+model+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Box Model</h3><p>The CSS Box Model is a fundamental concept in web development that describes the structure and layout of HTML elements. It is a rectangular box that wraps around every HTML element, and it consists of four main parts: margin, border, padding, and content. Understanding the CSS Box Model is crucial for building and designing web pages.</p><h3>Components of the CSS Box Model</h3><p>The CSS Box Model has four main components:</p><ul><li><strong>Content Area</strong>: This is the area where the content of the HTML element is displayed. It is the innermost part of the box model.</li><li><strong>Padding</strong>: This is the space between the content area and the border. It is used to add some breathing room around the content.</li><li><strong>Border</strong>: This is the visible outline of the box model. It can be set to different styles, widths, and colors.</li><li><strong>Margin</strong>: This is the space between the box model and other elements on the web page. It is used to add some space around the element.</li></ul><h3>Margins</h3><p>Margins are used to add space around an HTML element. They can be set using the <code>margin</code> property, which can take four values: <code>margin-top</code>, <code>margin-right</code>, <code>margin-bottom</code>, and <code>margin-left</code>. For example:</p><p><code>margin: 10px 20px 30px 40px;</code></p><p>This will set the top margin to 10px, the right margin to 20px, the bottom margin to 30px, and the left margin to 40px.</p><h3>Padding</h3><p>Padding is used to add space between the content area and the border. It can be set using the <code>padding</code> property, which can take four values: <code>padding-top</code>, <code>padding-right</code>, <code>padding-bottom</code>, and <code>padding-left</code>. For example:</p><p><code>padding: 10px 20px 30px 40px;</code></p><p>This will set the top padding to 10px, the right padding to 20px, the bottom padding to 30px, and the left padding to 40px.</p><h3>Borders</h3><p>Borders are used to add a visible outline to the box model. They can be set using the <code>border</code> property, which can take three values: <code>border-width</code>, <code>border-style</code>, and <code>border-color</code>. For example:</p><p><code>border: 1px solid #000;</code></p><p>This will set the border width to 1px, the border style to solid, and the border color to black.</p><h3>Example Use Case</h3><p>Here is an example of how to use the CSS Box Model to style an HTML element:</p><p><code>.box {</code></p><p><code>  width: 200px;</code></p><p><code>  height: 100px;</code></p><p><code>  margin: 10px 20px;</code></p><p><code>  padding: 10px;</code></p><p><code>  border: 1px solid #000;</code></p><p><code>}</code></p><p>This will create a box with a width of 200px, a height of 100px, a margin of 10px on the top and bottom, and 20px on the left and right, a padding of 10px, and a border of 1px solid black.</p>', 'Understanding CSS Box Model', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(75, 88, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: simple website layout design</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=simple+website+layout+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/simple+website+layout+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/simple+website+layout+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/simple+website+layout+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=simple+website+layout+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building a Simple Website Layout</h3><p>Welcome to Day 24 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be learning about building a simple website layout using HTML, CSS, and responsive design. By the end of this lesson, you will have a basic understanding of how to structure your website and make it look good on different devices.</p><h3>HTML Structure</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the backbone of any website. It provides the structure and content of a webpage. To build a simple website layout, we need to understand the basic HTML elements. Let\'s start with the basic HTML structure:</p><ul><li><strong>Header</strong>: The header section of a website typically contains the logo, navigation menu, and other important links.</li><li><strong>Navigation Bar</strong>: The navigation bar provides links to different pages of the website.</li><li><strong>Main Content</strong>: The main content section is where the main content of the webpage is displayed.</li><li><strong>Footer</strong>: The footer section typically contains copyright information, contact details, and other secondary links.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic HTML structure:</p><code>&lt;html&gt;<br>&lt;head&gt;<br>&lt;title&gt;My Website&lt;/title&gt;<br>&lt;/head&gt;<br>&lt;body&gt;<br>&lt;header&gt;<br>&lt;nav&gt;<br>&lt;ul&gt;<br>&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&lt;/ul&gt;<br>&lt;/nav&gt;<br>&lt;/header&gt;<br>&lt;main&gt;<br>&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Website&lt;/h1&gt;<br>&lt;p&gt;This is the main content of the webpage.&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;/main&gt;<br>&lt;footer&gt;<br>&lt;p&gt;&copy; 2024 My Website&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;/footer&gt;<br>&lt;/body&gt;<br>&lt;/html&gt;</code><h3>CSS Layout</h3><p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is used to control the layout and appearance of a webpage. To build a simple website layout, we need to understand how to use CSS to style our HTML elements. Let\'s start with the basic CSS layout:</p><ul><li><strong>Display Property</strong>: The display property is used to specify how an element is displayed. Common values include block, inline, and inline-block.</li><li><strong>Position Property</strong>: The position property is used to specify the position of an element. Common values include static, relative, absolute, and fixed.</li><li><strong>Float Property</strong>: The float property is used to specify how an element should float. Common values include left, right, and none.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic CSS layout:</p><code>body {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;font-family: Arial, sans-serif;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;margin: 0;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;padding: 0;<br>}<br><br>header {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;background-color: #333;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;color: #fff;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;padding: 20px;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;text-align: center;<br>}<br><br>nav ul {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;list-style: none;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;margin: 0;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;padding: 0;<br>}<br><br>nav li {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;display: inline-block;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;margin-right: 20px;<br>}<br><br>main {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;display: block;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;margin: 20px;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;padding: 20px;<br>}<br><br>footer {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;background-color: #333;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;color: #fff;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;padding: 10px;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;text-align: center;<br>}</code><h3>Responsive Design</h3><p>Responsive design is an approach to web design that makes webpages render well on a variety of devices and screen sizes. To build a responsive website layout, we need to understand how to use media queries to apply different styles based on different screen sizes.</p><ul><li><strong>Media Queries</strong>: Media queries are used to apply different styles based on different screen sizes. For example, we can use the following media query to apply a different style for screens with a maximum width of 768px:</li></ul><code>@media (max-width: 768px) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;main {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; margin: 10px;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; padding: 10px;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br>}</code><p>By using media queries, we can create a responsive website layout that adapts to different screen sizes and devices.</p>', 'Building a Simple Website Layout', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(76, 89, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css flexbox layout</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+flexbox+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+flexbox+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+flexbox+layout/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+flexbox+layout/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+flexbox+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Flexbox</h3><p>CSS Flexbox is a layout mode that allows you to design complex layouts with less code and more flexibility. It is a powerful tool for creating responsive and mobile-friendly websites. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of CSS Flexbox and how to use it to create flexible and responsive layouts.</p><h3>Flex Container</h3><p>A Flex Container is the parent element that contains the flex items. To create a Flex Container, you need to add the <code>display: flex;</code> or <code>display: inline-flex;</code> property to the parent element. The <code>display: flex;</code> property makes the container a block-level flex container, while the <code>display: inline-flex;</code> property makes it an inline-level flex container.</p><p>Here is an example of how to create a Flex Container:<code>.flex-container {<br>  display: flex;<br>  flex-direction: row;<br>  justify-content: space-between;<br>  align-items: center;<br>}</code></p><h3>Flex Items</h3><p>Flex Items are the child elements of the Flex Container. By default, flex items are displayed in a row and take up equal space in the container. You can adjust the layout of flex items using various properties, such as <code>flex-direction</code>, <code>justify-content</code>, and <code>align-items</code>.</p><p>Here are some common properties used to style flex items:<ul><li><code>flex-basis</code>: sets the initial width of the flex item</li><li><code>flex-grow</code>: sets the ability of the flex item to grow and take up more space</li><li><code>flex-shrink</code>: sets the ability of the flex item to shrink and take up less space</li><li><code>align-self</code>: sets the alignment of the flex item within the container</li></ul></p><p>Here is an example of how to style flex items:<code>.flex-item {<br>  flex-basis: 20%;<br>  flex-grow: 1;<br>  margin: 10px;<br>  padding: 20px;<br>  background-color: #f2f2f2;<br>  border: 1px solid #ccc;<br>}</code></p><h3>Benefits of Using CSS Flexbox</h3><p>There are many benefits to using CSS Flexbox, including:<ul><li><strong>Easy to create complex layouts</strong>: Flexbox makes it easy to create complex layouts with less code and more flexibility.</li><li><strong>Responsive and mobile-friendly</strong>: Flexbox allows you to create layouts that are responsive and mobile-friendly, making it easy to adapt to different screen sizes and devices.</li><li><strong>Less code and more flexibility</strong>: Flexbox requires less code than other layout methods, making it easier to maintain and update your website.</li></ul></p><h3>Common Use Cases for CSS Flexbox</h3><p>CSS Flexbox is commonly used in a variety of situations, including:<ul><li><strong>Navigation menus</strong>: Flexbox is often used to create navigation menus that are responsive and easy to use.</li><li><strong>Image galleries</strong>: Flexbox is useful for creating image galleries that are responsive and easy to navigate.</li><li><strong>Responsive layouts</strong>: Flexbox is essential for creating responsive layouts that adapt to different screen sizes and devices.</li></ul></p>', 'Working with CSS Flexbox', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(77, 90, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive navigation menu</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+navigation+menu\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+navigation+menu\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+navigation+menu/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+navigation+menu/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+navigation+menu\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Navigation Menu</h3><p>As we continue our journey in web development, we will learn about creating a responsive navigation menu. A responsive navigation menu is a crucial element in web design, as it allows users to navigate through a website easily, regardless of the device they are using. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of responsive design, navigation menu, and CSS media queries.</p><h3>What is Responsive Design?</h3><p>Responsive design is an approach to web design that focuses on creating websites that provide an optimal viewing experience across a wide range of devices, from desktop computers to mobile phones. This is achieved by using flexible grids, images, and media queries to create a website that adapts to different screen sizes and devices.</p><p>A responsive website has several key characteristics, including:<ul><li><strong>Flexible grid</strong>: The website\'s layout is based on a flexible grid that adjusts to different screen sizes.</li><li><strong>Flexible images</strong>: Images on the website are flexible and can be resized to fit different screen sizes.</li><li><strong>Media queries</strong>: The website uses media queries to apply different styles based on different screen sizes and devices.</li></ul></p><h3>Creating a Navigation Menu</h3><p>A navigation menu is a crucial element in web design, as it allows users to navigate through a website easily. A navigation menu typically consists of a list of links to different pages on the website.</p><p>To create a navigation menu, we can use the following HTML code:<code>&lt;nav&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;</code></p><h3>Using CSS Media Queries</h3><p>CSS media queries are used to apply different styles based on different screen sizes and devices. We can use media queries to create a responsive navigation menu that adapts to different screen sizes.</p><p>For example, we can use the following CSS code to create a responsive navigation menu that changes its layout on small screens:<code>@media (max-width: 768px) {\n  nav ul {\n    flex-direction: column;\n  }\n  nav li {\n    width: 100%;\n  }\n}</code></p><p>This code uses a media query to apply different styles to the navigation menu when the screen size is 768px or less. On small screens, the navigation menu changes its layout to a vertical list, with each link taking up the full width of the screen.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we learned about creating a responsive navigation menu using responsive design, navigation menu, and CSS media queries. We covered the basics of responsive design, including flexible grids, images, and media queries. We also learned how to create a navigation menu using HTML and CSS, and how to use media queries to create a responsive navigation menu that adapts to different screen sizes.</p>', 'Creating a Responsive Navigation Menu', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(78, 91, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript Basics</h3><p>Welcome to Day 27 of our Web Development course, where we will be introducing the basics of JavaScript. JavaScript is a high-level, dynamic, and interpreted programming language that is used for client-side scripting on the web. It is a fundamental language for any web developer to learn, as it allows you to create interactive and dynamic web pages.</p><p>In this lesson, we will be covering three main topics: JavaScript Variables, JavaScript Data Types, and JavaScript Functions. These topics are essential for any beginner to understand and will provide a solid foundation for further learning.</p><h3>JavaScript Variables</h3><p>In JavaScript, a variable is a container that stores a value. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in a program. To declare a variable in JavaScript, you use the <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, or <code>var</code> keyword followed by the name of the variable and the assignment operator (=).</p><p>For example:</p><ul><li><code>let x = 5;</code> declares a variable x and assigns it the value 5.</li><li><code>const PI = 3.14;</code> declares a constant variable PI and assigns it the value 3.14.</li><li><code>var name = \'John\';</code> declares a variable name and assigns it the value \'John\'.</li></ul><p>It\'s worth noting that <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> are block-scoped, meaning they are only accessible within the block they are declared in, while <code>var</code> is function-scoped, meaning it is accessible throughout the function it is declared in.</p><h3>JavaScript Data Types</h3><p>JavaScript has several built-in data types, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Number</strong>: a numeric value, such as 5 or 3.14.</li><li><strong>String</strong>: a sequence of characters, such as \'hello\' or \"hello\".</li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: a true or false value.</li><li><strong>Null</strong>: a null value, which represents the absence of any object value.</li><li><strong>Undefined</strong>: an undefined value, which represents an uninitialized variable or a variable that has not been declared.</li><li><strong>Object</strong>: an unordered collection of key-value pairs, such as {name: \'John\', age: 30}.</li><li><strong>Array</strong>: an ordered collection of values, such as [1, 2, 3] or [\'a\', \'b\', \'c\'].</li></ul><p>For example:</p><ul><li><code>let x = 5;</code> declares a variable x of type Number.</li><li><code>let name = \'John\';</code> declares a variable name of type String.</li><li><code>let isAdmin = true;</code> declares a variable isAdmin of type Boolean.</li></ul><h3>JavaScript Functions</h3><p>A function in JavaScript is a block of code that can be executed multiple times from different parts of your program. Functions are useful for organizing your code, reducing repetition, and making your code more reusable.</p><p>To declare a function in JavaScript, you use the <code>function</code> keyword followed by the name of the function and a list of parameters in parentheses.</p><p>For example:</p><ul><li><code>function greet(name) { console.log(\'Hello, \' + name); }</code> declares a function greet that takes a single parameter name and logs a greeting message to the console.</li><li><code>function add(x, y) { return x + y; }</code> declares a function add that takes two parameters x and y and returns their sum.</li></ul><p>Functions can be called by using the function name followed by a list of arguments in parentheses. For example:</p><ul><li><code>greet(\'John\');</code> calls the greet function with the argument \'John\'.</li><li><code>let result = add(2, 3);</code> calls the add function with the arguments 2 and 3 and assigns the result to the variable result.</li></ul>', 'Introduction to JavaScript Basics', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
