(79, 92, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript dom manipulation</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+dom+manipulation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+dom+manipulation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript DOM Manipulation</h3><p>JavaScript DOM (Document Object Model) manipulation is a crucial aspect of web development, allowing developers to dynamically interact with and modify web pages. The DOM represents the structure of a web page as a tree-like data structure, with each node representing an element, attribute, or piece of text.</p><h3>Understanding DOM Elements</h3><p>DOM elements are the building blocks of a web page, and can be manipulated using JavaScript. There are several types of DOM elements, including:</p><ul><li><strong>HTML elements</strong>: These are the standard HTML tags, such as <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>.</li><li><strong>Attribute nodes</strong>: These represent the attributes of an HTML element, such as <code>id</code>, <code>class</code>, and <code>style</code>.</li><li><strong>Text nodes</strong>: These represent the text content of an HTML element.</li></ul><p>JavaScript provides several methods for selecting and manipulating DOM elements, including <code>document.getElementById()</code>, <code>document.getElementsByClassName()</code>, and <code>document.querySelectorAll()</code>.</p><h3>Understanding DOM Events</h3><p>DOM events are actions that occur on a web page, such as mouse clicks, keyboard input, and page loads. JavaScript can be used to respond to these events, allowing developers to create interactive web pages. There are several types of DOM events, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Mouse events</strong>: These include events such as <code>click</code>, <code>dblclick</code>, and <code>mouseover</code>.</li><li><strong>Keyboard events</strong>: These include events such as <code>keydown</code> and <code>keyup</code>.</li><li><strong>Load events</strong>: These include events such as <code>load</code> and <code>DOMContentLoaded</code>.</li></ul><p>JavaScript provides several methods for handling DOM events, including <code>addEventListener()</code> and <code>removeEventListener()</code>.</p><h3>Manipulating the DOM</h3><p>Once a DOM element has been selected, JavaScript can be used to manipulate its properties and attributes. This can include changing the text content of an element, adding or removing classes, and modifying the element\'s style.</p><p>For example, the following code uses JavaScript to change the text content of a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> element: <code>document.getElementById(\'myParagraph\').textContent = \'New text content\';</code></p><p>Similarly, the following code uses JavaScript to add a class to a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> element: <code>document.getElementById(\'myDiv\').classList.add(\'new-class\');</code></p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In conclusion, JavaScript DOM manipulation is a powerful tool for creating interactive web pages. By understanding DOM elements and events, developers can use JavaScript to dynamically modify web pages and respond to user input.</p>', 'Understanding JavaScript DOM Manipulation', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(80, 93, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css grid layout design</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+grid+layout+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+grid+layout+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+grid+layout+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+grid+layout+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+grid+layout+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows you to create complex, responsive designs with ease. It provides a two-dimensional grid system that enables you to arrange elements in a flexible and efficient way. In this lesson, we will introduce you to the basics of CSS Grid and how to use it to create responsive web designs.</p><h3>What is CSS Grid?</h3><p>CSS Grid is a CSS module that allows you to create grid-based layouts. It provides a set of properties and values that enable you to define the structure and layout of a grid container and its child elements. With CSS Grid, you can create complex layouts with multiple rows and columns, and easily manage the size and position of elements within the grid.</p><p>CSS Grid is different from other layout systems like Flexbox, which is a one-dimensional layout system. CSS Grid provides more flexibility and control over the layout, making it ideal for creating complex, responsive designs.</p><h3>Basic Concepts of CSS Grid</h3><ul><li><strong>Grid Container</strong>: The grid container is the element that defines the grid. It is the parent element that contains the grid items.</li><li><strong>Grid Items</strong>: Grid items are the child elements of the grid container. They are the elements that are arranged within the grid.</li><li><strong>Grid Tracks</strong>: Grid tracks are the rows and columns of the grid. They are defined by the <code>grid-template-rows</code> and <code>grid-template-columns</code> properties.</li><li><strong>Grid Cells</strong>: Grid cells are the individual units of the grid. They are the spaces where the grid items are placed.</li></ul><h3>Creating a CSS Grid</h3><p>To create a CSS Grid, you need to define the grid container and the grid items. You can do this by adding the <code>display: grid</code> property to the grid container and defining the grid tracks using the <code>grid-template-rows</code> and <code>grid-template-columns</code> properties.</p><p>For example:</p><code>.grid-container {<br>  display: grid;<br>  grid-template-rows: 200px 300px;<br>  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;<br>}</code><p>This code defines a grid container with two rows and two columns. The first row is 200px high, and the second row is 300px high. The first column is 1fr wide, and the second column is 2fr wide.</p><h3>Layout Design with CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid provides a lot of flexibility and control over the layout. You can use it to create complex, responsive designs with ease. Some of the key benefits of using CSS Grid for layout design include:</p><ul><li><strong>Easy to create complex layouts</strong>: CSS Grid makes it easy to create complex layouts with multiple rows and columns.</li><li><strong>Flexible and responsive</strong>: CSS Grid is flexible and responsive, making it ideal for creating designs that work on different devices and screen sizes.</li><li><strong>Easy to manage grid items</strong>: CSS Grid provides a lot of control over the grid items, making it easy to manage their size and position.</li></ul><h3>Responsive Web Design with CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid is ideal for creating responsive web designs. It provides a lot of flexibility and control over the layout, making it easy to create designs that work on different devices and screen sizes.</p><p>Some of the key benefits of using CSS Grid for responsive web design include:</p><ul><li><strong>Easy to create responsive layouts</strong>: CSS Grid makes it easy to create responsive layouts that work on different devices and screen sizes.</li><li><strong>Flexible and adaptable</strong>: CSS Grid is flexible and adaptable, making it ideal for creating designs that work on different devices and screen sizes.</li><li><strong>Easy to manage grid items</strong>: CSS Grid provides a lot of control over the grid items, making it easy to manage their size and position on different devices and screen sizes.</li></ul>', 'Introduction to CSS Grid', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(81, 94, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css grid layout</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+grid+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+grid+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+grid+layout/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+grid+layout/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+grid+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building a Simple Web Page with CSS Grid</h3><p>Welcome to Day 30 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be learning about CSS Grid and how to use it to build a simple web page. CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows you to create complex, two-dimensional grid-based layouts with ease.</p><p>Before we dive into CSS Grid, let\'s take a quick look at the basics of HTML5. HTML5 is the latest version of the HyperText Markup Language, which is the standard markup language used to create web pages. HTML5 provides a range of new features and improvements over its predecessors, including improved semantic meaning, better support for multimedia, and more.</p><h3>Basic HTML5 Structure</h3><p>A basic HTML5 document consists of a few key elements. These include the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element, which is the root element of the document, the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> element, which contains metadata about the document, and the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> element, which contains the content of the page.</p><p>Here is an example of a basic HTML5 document structure:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;html&gt;</code>: The root element of the document</li><li><code>&lt;head&gt;</code>: The head section of the document, which contains metadata</li><li><code>&lt;body&gt;</code>: The body section of the document, which contains the content</li></ul><p>Within the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> element, you can add a range of other elements to create the content of your page. These might include headings (<code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>, etc.), paragraphs (<code>&lt;p&gt;</code>), images (<code>&lt;img&gt;</code>), and more.</p><h3>Introduction to CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows you to create complex, two-dimensional grid-based layouts with ease. A grid is made up of a series of rows and columns, which can be used to layout content in a flexible and responsive way.</p><p>To create a grid, you need to define a container element and set its <code>display</code> property to <code>grid</code> or <code>inline-grid</code>. You can then define the rows and columns of the grid using the <code>grid-template-rows</code> and <code>grid-template-columns</code> properties.</p><p>Here is an example of how you might define a simple grid:</p><p><code>container {</code></p><p><code>  display: grid;</code></p><p><code>  grid-template-rows: 1fr 2fr;</code></p><p><code>  grid-template-columns: 1fr 3fr;</code></p><p><code>}</code></p><p>This would create a grid with two rows and two columns, where the first row takes up one fractional unit of space and the second row takes up two fractional units of space, and the first column takes up one fractional unit of space and the second column takes up three fractional units of space.</p><h3>Building a Simple Web Page with CSS Grid</h3><p>Now that we have covered the basics of HTML5 and CSS Grid, let\'s build a simple web page using these technologies. Our web page will have a header, a main content area, and a footer.</p><p>Here is the HTML5 code for our web page:</p><p><code>&lt;html&gt;</code></p><p><code>  &lt;head&gt;</code></p><p><code>    &lt;title&gt;Simple Web Page&lt;/title&gt;</code></p><p><code>    &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;styles.css&quot;&gt;</code></p><p><code>  &lt;/head&gt;</code></p><p><code>  &lt;body&gt;</code></p><p><code>    &lt;header&gt;Header&lt;/header&gt;</code></p><p><code>    &lt;main&gt;Main Content&lt;/main&gt;</code></p><p><code>    &lt;footer&gt;Footer&lt;/footer&gt;</code></p><p><code>  &lt;/body&gt;</code></p><p><code>&lt;/html&gt;</code></p><p>And here is the CSS code to layout our web page using CSS Grid:</p><p><code>body {</code></p><p><code>  display: grid;</code></p><p><code>  grid-template-rows: 100px 1fr 50px;</code></p><p><code>  grid-template-columns: 1fr;</code></p><p><code>  height: 100vh;</code></p><p><code>}</code></p><p><code>header {</code></p><p><code>  background-color: #f0f0f0;</code></p><p><code>  grid-row: 1;</code></p><p><code>}</code></p><p><code>main {</code></p><p><code>  grid-row: 2;</code></p><p><code>}</code></p><p><code>footer {</code></p><p><code>  background-color: #f0f0f0;</code></p><p><code>  grid-row: 3;</code></p><p><code>}</code></p><p>This code creates a grid with three rows and one column, where the first row takes up 100 pixels of space, the second row takes up one fractional unit of space, and the third row takes up 50 pixels of space. The <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> elements are then placed into the grid using the <code>grid-row</code> property.</p><p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p><p>In this lesson, we have covered the basics of HTML5 and CSS Grid, and built a simple web page using these technologies. We have learned how to define a grid, add rows and columns, and place content into the grid using the <code>grid-row</code> and <code>grid-column</code> properties.</p>', 'Building a Simple Web Page with CSS Grid', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(82, 95, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css grid layout</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+grid+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+grid+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+grid+layout/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+grid+layout/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+grid+layout\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Grid Container Properties</h3><p>CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows you to create complex, two-dimensional grid-based layouts. In this lesson, we will explore the CSS Grid container properties, which are used to define the structure and behavior of a grid container.</p><h3>CSS Grid Container</h3><p>A CSS Grid container is an HTML element that has been set to <code>display: grid</code> or <code>display: inline-grid</code>. This element will contain one or more grid items, which are the child elements of the grid container.</p><p>When an element is set to <code>display: grid</code>, it will become a block-level grid container, while <code>display: inline-grid</code> will create an inline-level grid container. The difference between these two values is that a block-level grid container will start on a new line, while an inline-level grid container will sit on the same line as other inline elements.</p><h3>Grid Template Columns</h3><p>The <code>grid-template-columns</code> property is used to define the number and size of columns in a grid container. This property takes a list of values, where each value represents the size of a column.</p><p>For example, <code>grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr 3fr</code> will create a grid with three columns, where the first column is one fractional unit, the second column is two fractional units, and the third column is three fractional units.</p><ul><li><strong>Fractional Units (fr)</strong>: A fractional unit is a unit of measurement that represents a proportion of the available space in a grid container.</li><li><strong>Pixel Units (px)</strong>: You can also use pixel units to define the size of columns, for example, <code>grid-template-columns: 100px 200px 300px</code>.</li><li><strong>Percentage Units (%)</strong>: Percentage units can also be used to define the size of columns, for example, <code>grid-template-columns: 10% 20% 30%</code>.</li></ul><h3>Grid Template Rows</h3><p>The <code>grid-template-rows</code> property is used to define the number and size of rows in a grid container. This property takes a list of values, where each value represents the size of a row.</p><p>For example, <code>grid-template-rows: 1fr 2fr 3fr</code> will create a grid with three rows, where the first row is one fractional unit, the second row is two fractional units, and the third row is three fractional units.</p><p>Just like with columns, you can use fractional units, pixel units, and percentage units to define the size of rows.</p><p>Here is an example of how you can use the <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> properties together to create a grid:</p><p><code>.grid-container {</code><br><code>  display: grid;</code><br><code>  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr 3fr;</code><br><code>  grid-template-rows: 1fr 2fr 3fr;</code><br><code>}</code></p><p>This will create a grid with three columns and three rows, where each column and row is sized using fractional units.</p>', 'Understanding CSS Grid Container Properties', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(83, 96, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css grid layout examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+grid+layout+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+grid+layout+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Grid Item Properties</h3><p>CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows you to create complex, two-dimensional grid-based layouts. In the previous lessons, we learned about CSS Grid Container properties. Today, we will focus on CSS Grid Item properties, which are used to control the behavior of individual grid items within a grid container.</p><h3>Grid Column Property</h3><p>The <code>grid-column</code> property is used to specify the column position of a grid item. This property can take one or two values: <code>grid-column-start</code> and <code>grid-column-end</code>. The <code>grid-column-start</code> value specifies the starting column line of the grid item, while the <code>grid-column-end</code> value specifies the ending column line.</p><ul><li><strong>grid-column-start</strong>: specifies the starting column line of the grid item</li><li><strong>grid-column-end</strong>: specifies the ending column line of the grid item</li></ul><p>For example, if you want to place a grid item in the second column, you can use the following code: <code>grid-column: 2 / 3;</code>. This will place the grid item in the second column, starting from the second column line and ending at the third column line.</p><h3>Grid Row Property</h3><p>The <code>grid-row</code> property is used to specify the row position of a grid item. This property can take one or two values: <code>grid-row-start</code> and <code>grid-row-end</code>. The <code>grid-row-start</code> value specifies the starting row line of the grid item, while the <code>grid-row-end</code> value specifies the ending row line.</p><ul><li><strong>grid-row-start</strong>: specifies the starting row line of the grid item</li><li><strong>grid-row-end</strong>: specifies the ending row line of the grid item</li></ul><p>For example, if you want to place a grid item in the second row, you can use the following code: <code>grid-row: 2 / 3;</code>. This will place the grid item in the second row, starting from the second row line and ending at the third row line.</p><h3>Other Grid Item Properties</h3><p>In addition to the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties, there are several other grid item properties that you can use to control the behavior of grid items. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>grid-area</strong>: specifies the name of the grid area that the grid item belongs to</li><li><strong>justify-self</strong>: specifies the horizontal alignment of the grid item within its grid cell</li><li><strong>align-self</strong>: specifies the vertical alignment of the grid item within its grid cell</li></ul><p>These properties can be used to create complex, customized grid layouts that meet the needs of your application.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we learned about CSS Grid Item properties, including the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties. We also learned about other grid item properties, such as <code>grid-area</code>, <code>justify-self</code>, and <code>align-self</code>. With these properties, you can create complex, customized grid layouts that meet the needs of your application.</p>', 'Practicing CSS Grid Item Properties', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(84, 97, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Web Design</h3><p>Responsive web design is an approach to web design that makes web pages render well on a variety of devices and screen sizes. This is achieved by using flexible grids, images, and media queries. In this lesson, we will learn how to create a responsive web page using CSS Grid and media queries.</p><h3>What is CSS Grid?</h3><p>CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system that allows you to create complex layouts with ease. It is based on a grid system, where you can define rows and columns to place your content. CSS Grid is a powerful tool for creating responsive layouts, as it allows you to define the layout of your page in a flexible and dynamic way.</p><h3>Basic Concepts of CSS Grid</h3><ul><li><strong>Grid Container</strong>: The element that contains the grid.</li><li><strong>Grid Items</strong>: The elements that are placed inside the grid container.</li><li><strong>Grid Tracks</strong>: The rows and columns of the grid.</li><li><strong>Grid Cells</strong>: The individual cells of the grid, where the grid items are placed.</li></ul><p>To create a CSS Grid, you need to define the grid container and the grid items. You can do this by using the <code>display: grid</code> property on the grid container, and the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties on the grid items.</p><h3>Creating a Responsive Web Page with CSS Grid</h3><p>To create a responsive web page with CSS Grid, you need to define the grid container and the grid items, and use media queries to define the layout of the page for different screen sizes. Here is an example of how you can create a simple responsive web page using CSS Grid:</p><p><code>.grid-container {</code><br><code>  display: grid;</code><br><code>  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);</code><br><code>  grid-gap: 10px;</code><br><code>}</code></p><p><code>.grid-item {</code><br><code>  background-color: #ccc;</code><br><code>  padding: 20px;</code><br><code>}</code></p><p>In this example, we define a grid container with three columns, and a grid gap of 10px. We also define a grid item with a background color and padding.</p><h3>Using Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are used to define the layout of the page for different screen sizes. You can use media queries to change the grid template columns, grid gap, and other properties of the grid container and grid items. Here is an example of how you can use media queries to change the layout of the page for different screen sizes:</p><p><code>@media (max-width: 768px) {</code><br><code>  .grid-container {</code><br><code>    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);</code><br><code>  }</code><br><code>}</code></p><p><code>@media (max-width: 480px) {</code><br><code>  .grid-container {</code><br><code>    grid-template-columns: 1fr;</code><br><code>  }</code><br><code>}</code></p><p>In this example, we use media queries to change the grid template columns of the grid container for different screen sizes. For screen sizes below 768px, we use two columns, and for screen sizes below 480px, we use one column.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we learned how to create a responsive web page using CSS Grid and media queries. We covered the basic concepts of CSS Grid, including grid containers, grid items, grid tracks, and grid cells. We also learned how to use media queries to define the layout of the page for different screen sizes.</p>', 'Creating a Responsive Web Page with CSS Grid', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(85, 98, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css grid debugging tools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+grid+debugging+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+grid+debugging+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+grid+debugging+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+grid+debugging+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+grid+debugging+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Debugging Common CSS Grid Issues</h3><p>Welcome to Day 34 of our Web Development course. Today, we\'re going to focus on debugging common CSS Grid issues. CSS Grid is a powerful tool for creating complex layouts, but it can be frustrating to work with when things don\'t go as planned. In this lesson, we\'ll cover some common issues that you may encounter and provide tips on how to troubleshoot them.</p><p>Before we dive into the common issues, let\'s review the basics of CSS Grid. CSS Grid is a two-dimensional grid system that allows you to create complex layouts using rows and columns. You can define a grid container using the <code>display: grid</code> property, and then define the grid items using the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties.</p><h3>CSS Grid Debugging</h3><p>Debugging CSS Grid issues can be challenging, but there are some tools and techniques that can help. Here are a few tips:</p><ul><li>Use the browser\'s developer tools to inspect the grid container and grid items. You can use the <code>grid</code> overlay in the Elements tab to visualize the grid and see how the items are laid out.</li><li>Use the <code>grid-template-areas</code> property to define the grid template areas. This can help you visualize the grid and identify any issues with the layout.</li><li>Use the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties to define the grid items. Make sure to use the correct syntax and values for these properties.</li></ul><h3>Common Issues</h3><p>Here are some common issues that you may encounter when working with CSS Grid:</p><ul><li><strong>Grid items not aligning properly</strong>: This can be due to incorrect values for the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties, or due to conflicting styles or layouts.</li><li><strong>Grid container not sizing correctly</strong>: This can be due to incorrect values for the <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> properties, or due to conflicting styles or layouts.</li><li><strong>Grid items overlapping</strong>: This can be due to incorrect values for the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties, or due to conflicting styles or layouts.</li></ul><h3>Troubleshooting</h3><p>To troubleshoot CSS Grid issues, follow these steps:</p><ol><li>Check the browser\'s developer tools to see if there are any errors or warnings related to the grid container or grid items.</li><li>Inspect the grid container and grid items to see if there are any conflicting styles or layouts.</li><li>Check the values for the <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> properties to make sure they are correct.</li><li>Check the values for the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties to make sure they are correct.</li><li>Use the <code>grid-template-areas</code> property to define the grid template areas and visualize the grid.</li></ol><p>By following these steps and using the tips and techniques outlined in this lesson, you should be able to debug common CSS Grid issues and create complex layouts with ease.</p>', 'Debugging Common CSS Grid Issues', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(86, 99, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css grid layout examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+grid+layout+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+grid+layout+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Advanced CSS Grid Techniques</h3><p>As we have learned in the previous lessons, CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows us to create complex and responsive layouts. In this lesson, we will explore some advanced CSS Grid techniques that will take our layout skills to the next level.</p><h3>Grid Auto Flow</h3><p>Grid Auto Flow is a property that allows us to control the flow of grid items. By default, grid items are placed in the grid in the order they appear in the HTML, but with Grid Auto Flow, we can change this behavior. There are two values for Grid Auto Flow: <code>row</code> and <code>column</code>. The <code>row</code> value will place grid items in rows, while the <code>column</code> value will place them in columns.</p><ul><li><strong>Grid Auto Flow Row:</strong> This will place grid items in rows, and when a row is full, it will move to the next row.</li><li><strong>Grid Auto Flow Column:</strong> This will place grid items in columns, and when a column is full, it will move to the next column.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use Grid Auto Flow: <code>.grid-container { display: grid; grid-auto-flow: row; }</code></p><h3>Grid Auto Rows</h3><p>Grid Auto Rows is a property that allows us to control the size of grid rows. By default, grid rows are sized based on the content of the grid items, but with Grid Auto Rows, we can set a specific size for all rows. There are several values for Grid Auto Rows, including <code>auto</code>, <code>fr</code>, and <code>px</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>Grid Auto Rows Auto:</strong> This will size grid rows based on the content of the grid items.</li><li><strong>Grid Auto Rows Fr:</strong> This will size grid rows based on a fractional unit, where the fraction represents a proportion of the available space.</li><li><strong>Grid Auto Rows Px:</strong> This will size grid rows based on a fixed size in pixels.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use Grid Auto Rows: <code>.grid-container { display: grid; grid-auto-rows: 1fr; }</code></p><h3>Advanced CSS Grid Techniques</h3><p>Now that we have learned about Grid Auto Flow and Grid Auto Rows, let\'s explore some advanced CSS Grid techniques. One technique is to use Grid Auto Flow and Grid Auto Rows together to create a complex layout. For example, we can use Grid Auto Flow to place grid items in rows, and then use Grid Auto Rows to size the rows based on a fractional unit.</p><p>Another technique is to use CSS Grid with other layout systems, such as Flexbox. We can use CSS Grid to create a complex layout, and then use Flexbox to align and justify the grid items.</p><p>In conclusion, advanced CSS Grid techniques allow us to create complex and responsive layouts. By using Grid Auto Flow and Grid Auto Rows, we can control the flow and size of grid items, and create advanced layouts. With practice and experience, we can master these techniques and create beautiful and functional layouts.</p>', 'Advanced CSS Grid Techniques', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(87, 100, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: front-end frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Front-end Frameworks</h3><p>Welcome to Day 36 of our Web Development course. Today, we\'re going to explore the world of front-end frameworks, which are essential tools for building responsive, mobile-first, and consistent web applications. Front-end frameworks provide a set of pre-designed UI components, layouts, and styles that can save you time and effort when building your web projects.</p><p>There are many front-end frameworks available, but we\'ll focus on two popular ones: Bootstrap and Materialize. Both frameworks are widely used in the industry and have their own strengths and weaknesses.</p><h3>Bootstrap</h3><p>Bootstrap is one of the most popular front-end frameworks, created by Twitter. It\'s a free, open-source framework that provides a comprehensive set of UI components, layouts, and styles. Bootstrap is known for its simplicity, flexibility, and ease of use.</p><p>Some of the key features of Bootstrap include:</p><ul><li><strong>Responsive design</strong>: Bootstrap provides a mobile-first approach, which means your website will look great on all devices, from smartphones to desktops.</li><li><strong>Pre-designed UI components</strong>: Bootstrap offers a wide range of pre-designed UI components, such as buttons, forms, navigation bars, and more.</li><li><strong>Grid system</strong>: Bootstrap\'s grid system allows you to create complex layouts with ease.</li><li><strong>Customizable</strong>: Bootstrap provides a wide range of customization options, so you can tailor the framework to your needs.</li></ul><p>To get started with Bootstrap, you can include the following <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> tag in your HTML file: <code>&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' href=\'https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/css/bootstrap.min.css\'&gt;</code></p><h3>Materialize</h3><p>Materialize is another popular front-end framework, created by Google. It\'s based on Google\'s Material Design principles, which provide a consistent and intuitive user experience.</p><p>Some of the key features of Materialize include:</p><ul><li><strong>Material Design principles</strong>: Materialize follows Google\'s Material Design principles, which provide a consistent and intuitive user experience.</li><li><strong>Pre-designed UI components</strong>: Materialize offers a wide range of pre-designed UI components, such as buttons, forms, navigation bars, and more.</li><li><li><strong>Responsive design</strong>: Materialize provides a mobile-first approach, which means your website will look great on all devices, from smartphones to desktops.</li><li><strong>Customizable</strong>: Materialize provides a wide range of customization options, so you can tailor the framework to your needs.</li></ul><p>To get started with Materialize, you can include the following <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> tag in your HTML file: <code>&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' href=\'https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/materialize/1.0.0/css/materialize.min.css\'&gt;</code></p><h3>CSS Frameworks</h3><p>CSS frameworks are a type of front-end framework that provides a set of pre-designed CSS styles and layouts. They\'re often used in conjunction with other front-end frameworks, such as Bootstrap or Materialize.</p><p>Some popular CSS frameworks include:</p><ul><li><strong>Tailwind CSS</strong>: A utility-first CSS framework that provides a set of pre-designed CSS classes.</li><li><strong>Bulma</strong>: A modern CSS framework that provides a set of pre-designed CSS classes and layouts.</li><li><strong>Foundation</strong>: A responsive front-end framework that provides a set of pre-designed CSS classes and layouts.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, front-end frameworks are essential tools for building responsive, mobile-first, and consistent web applications. Bootstrap and Materialize are two popular front-end frameworks that provide a wide range of pre-designed UI components, layouts, and styles. CSS frameworks, such as Tailwind CSS, Bulma, and Foundation, provide a set of pre-designed CSS styles and layouts that can be used in conjunction with other front-end frameworks.</p>', 'Introduction to Front-end Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(88, 101, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Web Design</h3><p>Responsive web design is an approach to web design that makes web pages render well on a variety of devices and screen sizes. This is achieved by using a combination of flexible grids, images, and media queries. In this lesson, we will explore the key concepts of responsive web design, including media queries, flexbox, and grid systems.</p><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are a fundamental component of responsive web design. They allow you to apply different styles to your web page based on the device or screen size. Media queries use the <code>@media</code> rule to define the styles that should be applied. For example:</p><ul><li><code>@media (max-width: 600px) { /* styles for small screens */ }</code></li><li><code>@media (min-width: 601px) and (max-width: 1200px) { /* styles for medium screens */ }</code></li><li><code>@media (min-width: 1201px) { /* styles for large screens */ }</code></li></ul><p>Media queries can be used to change the layout, font size, and other styles of your web page based on the screen size.</p><h3>Flexbox</h3><p>Flexbox is a layout mode that allows you to easily create flexible and responsive layouts. It is particularly useful for creating navigation bars, footers, and other elements that need to be flexible. To use flexbox, you need to add the <code>display: flex;</code> property to the parent element. For example:</p><ul><li><code>.container { display: flex; flex-direction: row; }</code></li><li><code>.item { flex: 1; }</code></li></ul><p>Flexbox provides a number of benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Easy alignment</strong>: Flexbox makes it easy to align elements horizontally and vertically.</li><li><strong>Flexible sizing</strong>: Flexbox allows elements to grow or shrink based on the available space.</li><li><strong>Easy ordering</strong>: Flexbox allows you to easily change the order of elements.</li></ul><h3>Grid System</h3><p>A grid system is a layout system that divides the web page into rows and columns. It is particularly useful for creating complex layouts. To use a grid system, you need to add the <code>display: grid;</code> property to the parent element. For example:</p><ul><li><code>.container { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }</code></li><li><code>.item { grid-column: 1; }</code></li></ul><p>Grid systems provide a number of benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Easy layout</strong>: Grid systems make it easy to create complex layouts.</li><li><strong>Flexible sizing</strong>: Grid systems allow elements to grow or shrink based on the available space.</li><li><strong>Easy alignment</strong>: Grid systems make it easy to align elements horizontally and vertically.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, responsive web design is an essential skill for any web developer. By using media queries, flexbox, and grid systems, you can create web pages that render well on a variety of devices and screen sizes.</p>', 'Responsive Web Design', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(89, 102, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript dom manipulation</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+dom+manipulation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+dom+manipulation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+dom+manipulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript and DOM Manipulation</h3><p>Welcome to day 38 of our web development course. Today, we will be exploring the basics of JavaScript and how it interacts with the Document Object Model (DOM) to manipulate web page elements. JavaScript is a powerful programming language that allows us to add dynamic effects to our web pages, respond to user interactions, and update the content of our web pages in real-time.</p><h3>JavaScript Basics</h3><p>Before we dive into DOM manipulation, let\'s cover some basic concepts in JavaScript. JavaScript is a high-level, dynamic language that is executed on the client-side, meaning that it runs on the user\'s web browser. Here are some key concepts to get started with JavaScript:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: In JavaScript, we use the <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> keywords to declare variables. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in our programs.</li><li><strong>Data Types</strong>: JavaScript has several built-in data types, including <code>number</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>boolean</code>, <code>array</code>, and <code>object</code>.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: Functions are reusable blocks of code that take arguments and return values. We use the <code>function</code> keyword to define a function in JavaScript.</li></ul><h3>DOM Elements</h3><p>The Document Object Model (DOM) is a programming interface for HTML and XML documents. It represents the structure of a document as a tree of objects, where each object represents a node in the tree. Here are some key concepts related to DOM elements:</p><ul><li><strong>Nodes</strong>: A node is an object that represents a single element in the DOM tree. There are several types of nodes, including <code>Element</code>, <code>Text</code>, and <code>Comment</code> nodes.</li><li><strong>Elements</strong>: An element is a type of node that represents an HTML element, such as a <code>div</code>, <code>span</code>, or <code>p</code> element.</li><li><strong>Attributes</strong>: Attributes are key-value pairs that are associated with an element. For example, the <code>id</code> attribute specifies a unique identifier for an element.</li></ul><h3>Event Listeners</h3><p>Event listeners are functions that are attached to elements and respond to specific events, such as mouse clicks, keyboard input, or page loads. Here are some key concepts related to event listeners:</p><ul><li><strong>Events</strong>: An event is an action that occurs on an element, such as a mouse click or a key press.</li><li><strong>Event Listeners</strong>: An event listener is a function that is attached to an element and responds to a specific event.</li><li><strong>Event Handlers</strong>: An event handler is a function that is called when an event occurs. Event handlers can be used to update the DOM, make API requests, or perform other actions in response to an event.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to add an event listener to an element using JavaScript: <code>document.getElementById(\'myButton\').addEventListener(\'click\', function() { console.log(\'Button clicked!\'); });</code></p>', 'JavaScript and DOM Manipulation', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(90, 103, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: node js and express js logos</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=node+js+and+express+js+logos\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/node+js+and+express+js+logos\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/node+js+and+express+js+logos/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/node+js+and+express+js+logos/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=node+js+and+express+js+logos\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Back-end Development</h3><p>Welcome to Day 39 of our Web Development course. Today, we\'re going to explore the world of back-end development, which is responsible for handling server-side logic, database integration, and API connectivity. In this lesson, we\'ll delve into the basics of server-side programming, Node.js, and Express.js.</p><p>Server-side programming refers to the process of writing code that runs on the server, as opposed to client-side programming, which runs on the client\'s web browser. Server-side programming is used to create dynamic web pages, handle user input, and interact with databases. Some popular server-side programming languages include Java, Python, Ruby, and PHP.</p><h3>What is Node.js?</h3><p>Node.js is a JavaScript runtime environment that allows developers to run JavaScript on the server-side. It provides an event-driven, non-blocking I/O model, which makes it lightweight and efficient. Node.js is built on Chrome\'s V8 JavaScript engine and is widely used for real-time web applications, microservices, and APIs.</p><p>Some key features of Node.js include:</p><ul><li><strong>Asynchronous programming</strong>: Node.js uses callbacks, promises, and async/await to handle asynchronous operations.</li><li><strong>Event-driven programming</strong>: Node.js uses events to handle user interactions, network requests, and other system events.</li><li><strong>Modular design</strong>: Node.js has a vast ecosystem of packages and modules that can be easily installed and managed using npm (Node Package Manager).</li></ul><h3>What is Express.js?</h3><p>Express.js is a popular Node.js web framework that provides a flexible and modular way to build web applications. It provides a thin layer of abstraction over Node.js, making it easier to handle HTTP requests, route URLs, and render templates.</p><p>Some key features of Express.js include:</p><ul><li><strong>Request and response objects</strong>: Express.js provides a simple and intuitive API for handling HTTP requests and responses.</li><li><strong>Routing</strong>: Express.js provides a powerful routing system that allows developers to map URLs to specific handlers.</li><li><strong>Middleware</strong>: Express.js provides a middleware system that allows developers to add custom functionality to the request-response cycle.</li></ul><p>Here\'s an example of a simple Express.js application:</p><code>const express = require(\'express\');\nconst app = express();\n\napp.get(\'/\', (req, res) => {\n  res.send(\'Hello World!\');\n});\n\napp.listen(3000, () => {\n  console.log(\'Server started on port 3000\');\n});</code><p>In this example, we create an Express.js application, define a route for the root URL, and start the server on port 3000.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In conclusion, back-end development is a critical aspect of web development that involves server-side programming, database integration, and API connectivity. Node.js and Express.js are popular technologies used for building scalable and efficient back-end applications. In the next lesson, we\'ll explore more advanced topics in back-end development, including database integration and authentication.</p>', 'Introduction to Back-end Development', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(91, 104, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: database management systems</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=database+management+systems\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/database+management+systems\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/database+management+systems/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/database+management+systems/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=database+management+systems\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Database Management Systems</h3><p>A database management system (DBMS) is a software system that allows you to define, create, maintain, and manipulate databases. It acts as an intermediary between the user and the database, providing a way to store, retrieve, and manage data in a structured and controlled manner.</p><h3>Relational Databases</h3><p>Relational databases are a type of DBMS that stores data in tables, with each table having rows and columns. Each row represents a single record, and each column represents a field or attribute of that record. Relational databases are based on the relational model, which is a mathematical concept that describes how data is organized and related.</p><p>Some key features of relational databases include:</p><ul><li><strong>Tables</strong>: Relational databases store data in tables, which are also known as relations.</li><li><strong>Rows</strong>: Each row in a table represents a single record or tuple.</li><li><strong>Columns</strong>: Each column in a table represents a field or attribute of a record.</li><li><strong>Primary Key</strong>: A primary key is a unique identifier for each record in a table.</li><li><strong>Foreign Key</strong>: A foreign key is a field in a table that refers to the primary key of another table.</li></ul><p>Examples of relational databases include MySQL, PostgreSQL, and Microsoft SQL Server.</p><h3>NoSQL Databases</h3><p>NoSQL databases, also known as non-relational databases, are a type of DBMS that does not use the traditional table-based relational model. Instead, NoSQL databases use a variety of data models, such as key-value, document, graph, and column-family stores.</p><p>NoSQL databases are designed to handle large amounts of unstructured or semi-structured data and provide high scalability and performance. They are often used in big data and real-time web applications.</p><p>Some key features of NoSQL databases include:</p><ul><li><strong>Flexible schema</strong>: NoSQL databases have a flexible schema, which means that the structure of the data can change dynamically.</li><li><strong>High scalability</strong>: NoSQL databases are designed to handle large amounts of data and scale horizontally.</li><li><strong>High performance</strong>: NoSQL databases provide high performance and low latency.</li></ul><p>Examples of NoSQL databases include MongoDB, Cassandra, and Redis.</p><h3>MongoDB</h3><p>MongoDB is a popular NoSQL database that uses a document-based data model. It stores data in JSON-like documents called BSON (Binary Serialized Object Notation), which allows for flexible and dynamic schema design.</p><p>Some key features of MongoDB include:</p><ul><li><strong>Document-based data model</strong>: MongoDB stores data in JSON-like documents, which allows for flexible and dynamic schema design.</li><li><strong>High scalability</strong>: MongoDB is designed to handle large amounts of data and scale horizontally.</li><li><strong>High performance</strong>: MongoDB provides high performance and low latency.</li><li><strong>Rich query language</strong>: MongoDB has a rich query language that allows for complex queries and data analysis.</li></ul><p>MongoDB is widely used in web and mobile applications, and is particularly well-suited for real-time analytics and big data applications.</p><p>Here is an example of a MongoDB document: <code>{ \"name\": \"John Doe\", \"age\": 30, \"address\": { \"street\": \"123 Main St\", \"city\": \"Anytown\", \"state\": \"CA\", \"zip\": \"12345\" } }</code></p>', 'Database Management Systems', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(92, 105, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web security best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+security+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+security+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+security+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+security+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+security+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Security and Best Practices</h3><p>As a web developer, it\'s essential to understand the importance of web security and best practices to protect your website and users from potential threats. In this lesson, we\'ll cover common web vulnerabilities, security measures, and code reviews to help you develop secure web applications.</p><h3>Common Web Vulnerabilities</h3><p>There are several common web vulnerabilities that you should be aware of, including:</p><ul><li><strong>SQL Injection</strong>: occurs when an attacker injects malicious SQL code into a web application\'s database, allowing them to access sensitive data.</li><li><strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</strong>: occurs when an attacker injects malicious JavaScript code into a web application, allowing them to steal user data or take control of the user\'s session.</li><li><strong>Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)</strong>: occurs when an attacker tricks a user into performing an unintended action on a web application, allowing them to access sensitive data or take control of the user\'s account.</li></ul><p>These vulnerabilities can be prevented by using prepared statements, validating user input, and implementing security measures such as encryption and secure protocols.</p><h3>Security Measures</h3><p>To protect your web application from common web vulnerabilities, you should implement the following security measures:</p><ul><li><strong>Use HTTPS</strong>: encrypts data transmitted between the client and server, making it difficult for attackers to intercept sensitive data.</li><li><strong>Validate User Input</strong>: ensures that user input is valid and sanitized, preventing malicious code from being injected into your web application.</li><li><strong>Use Prepared Statements</strong>: separates code from user input, preventing SQL injection attacks.</li><li><strong>Implement Authentication and Authorization</strong>: ensures that only authorized users can access sensitive data and perform actions on your web application.</li></ul><p>By implementing these security measures, you can significantly reduce the risk of your web application being compromised by common web vulnerabilities.</p><h3>Code Reviews</h3><p>Code reviews are an essential part of web development, allowing you to identify and fix security vulnerabilities and bugs in your code. When performing a code review, you should:</p><ul><li><strong>Check for SQL Injection</strong>: review your code to ensure that user input is properly sanitized and validated.</li><li><strong>Check for XSS</strong>: review your code to ensure that user input is properly escaped and validated.</li><li><strong>Check for CSRF</strong>: review your code to ensure that security tokens are properly implemented and validated.</li><li><strong>Check for Authentication and Authorization</strong>: review your code to ensure that authentication and authorization mechanisms are properly implemented and validated.</li></ul><p>By performing regular code reviews, you can identify and fix security vulnerabilities and bugs in your code, ensuring that your web application is secure and reliable.</p><p>Example of secure code: <code>const express = require(\'express\'); const app = express(); app.use(express.json()); app.post(\'/login\', (req, res) => { const { username, password } = req.body; // Validate user input if (!username || !password) { return res.status(400).send(\'Invalid username or password\'); } // Use prepared statements to prevent SQL injection const query = \'SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = $1 AND password = $2\'; const values = [username, password]; // Execute the query db.query(query, values, (err, results) => { if (err) { return res.status(500).send(\'Error logging in\'); } if (results.length === 0) { return res.status(401).send(\'Invalid username or password\'); } // Implement authentication and authorization const user = results[0]; req.session.user = user; res.send(\'Logged in successfully\'); }); });</code></p>', 'Web Security and Best Practices', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(93, 106, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: project management tools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=project+management+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/project+management+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/project+management+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/project+management+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=project+management+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Project Planning and Implementation</h3><p>As we near the end of our web development course, it\'s essential to learn about project planning and implementation. In this chapter, we will cover agile methodologies, project management tools, and version control. These concepts will help you manage and deliver your web development projects efficiently.</p><h3>Agile Methodologies</h3><p>Agile methodologies are a set of principles and practices that aim to deliver working software in short cycles, known as sprints or iterations. The core values of agile development include:</p><ul><li>Individuals and interactions over processes and tools</li><li>Working software over comprehensive documentation</li><li>Customer collaboration over contract negotiation</li><li>Responding to change over following a plan</li></ul><p>Some popular agile frameworks include Scrum, Kanban, and Lean. These frameworks provide a structured approach to implementing agile principles in your project.</p><h3>Project Management Tools</h3><p>Project management tools help you plan, organize, and track your project\'s progress. Some popular project management tools include:</p><ul><li><strong>Asana</strong>: A task management tool that helps you assign and track tasks</li><li><strong>Trello</strong>: A visual project management tool that uses boards, lists, and cards to organize tasks</li><li><strong>Jira</strong>: A comprehensive project management tool that includes features like issue tracking, agile project planning, and team collaboration</li></ul><p>These tools help you stay organized, collaborate with your team, and deliver your project on time.</p><h3>Version Control</h3><p>Version control systems help you manage changes to your codebase over time. The most popular version control system is <code>Git</code>. Git allows you to create a repository of your code, track changes, and collaborate with others.</p><p>Some key Git concepts include:</p><ul><li><code>Repository</code>: The central location where all your code is stored</li><li><code>Branch</code>: A separate line of development in your repository</li><li><code>Commit</code>: A snapshot of your code at a particular point in time</li><li><code>Push</code>: The act of uploading your changes to a remote repository</li><li><code>Pull</code>: The act of downloading changes from a remote repository</li></ul><p>By using version control, you can ensure that your codebase is stable, secure, and easy to maintain.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this chapter, we covered agile methodologies, project management tools, and version control. These concepts are essential for delivering successful web development projects. Remember to choose the right agile framework, project management tool, and version control system for your project\'s needs.</p>', 'Project Planning and Implementation', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(94, 107, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Web Design</h3><p>Responsive web design is an approach to web design that makes web pages render well on a variety of devices and window or screen sizes. In this chapter, we will cover the basics of responsive web design, including media queries, flexible grids, and responsive images.</p><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are a fundamental component of responsive web design. They allow you to apply different styles based on the characteristics of the device being used to view your website. For example, you can use media queries to apply different styles for desktop, tablet, and mobile devices.</p><p>Media queries are defined using the <code>@media</code> rule in CSS. The <code>@media</code> rule specifies the conditions under which the styles will be applied. For example:</p><ul><li><code>@media (max-width: 600px) { /* styles for small screens */ }</code></li><li><code>@media (min-width: 601px) and (max-width: 1200px) { /* styles for medium screens */ }</code></li><li><code>@media (min-width: 1201px) { /* styles for large screens */ }</code></li></ul><p>These media queries will apply the styles defined inside the curly brackets when the screen width is less than or equal to 600px, between 601px and 1200px, and greater than 1200px, respectively.</p><h3>Flexible Grids</h3><p>A flexible grid is a grid system that is based on relative units, such as percentages or ems, rather than fixed units, such as pixels. This allows the grid to adapt to different screen sizes and devices.</p><p>To create a flexible grid, you can use the <code>display: grid</code> property in CSS, along with the <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> properties. For example:</p><p><code>.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr); grid-gap: 10px; }</code></p><p>This will create a grid with 12 columns, each taking up an equal amount of space. The <code>grid-gap</code> property is used to add a gap between the grid cells.</p><h3>Responsive Images</h3><p>Responsive images are images that adapt to different screen sizes and devices. There are several ways to make images responsive, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Using the <code>max-width</code> property</strong>: You can set the <code>max-width</code> property to 100% to ensure that the image does not exceed the width of its container.</li><li><strong>Using the <code>height</code> property</strong>: You can set the <code>height</code> property to auto to ensure that the image maintains its aspect ratio.</li><li><strong>Using the <code>picture</code> element</strong>: The <code>picture</code> element allows you to define multiple sources for an image, and the browser will choose the best one based on the screen size and device.</li></ul><p>For example:</p><p><code><img src=\'image.jpg\' alt=\'An image\' style=\'max-width: 100%; height: auto;\'></code></p><p>This will ensure that the image does not exceed the width of its container and maintains its aspect ratio.</p>', 'Introduction to Responsive Web Design', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(95, 108, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Implementing Responsive Design</h3><p>As we continue our journey in web development, it\'s essential to understand the importance of responsive design. With the rise of mobile devices, it\'s crucial to create websites that adapt to different screen sizes and devices. In this lesson, we\'ll explore the mobile-first approach, viewport meta tag, and responsive navigation menus.</p><h3>Mobile-First Approach</h3><p>The mobile-first approach is a design strategy that involves creating a website for mobile devices first and then scaling up to larger screens. This approach has several benefits, including improved page load times, better user experience, and easier maintenance. When designing for mobile devices, we need to consider the limited screen size and optimize our content accordingly.</p><ul><li>Start by designing for small screens (typically 320px or less)</li><li>Use a single-column layout to simplify the design</li><li>Prioritize content and remove unnecessary elements</li><li>Use responsive images and compress files to reduce page load times</li></ul><p>By following the mobile-first approach, we can ensure that our website is accessible and usable on a variety of devices.</p><h3>Viewport Meta Tag</h3><p>The viewport meta tag is a crucial element in responsive design. It controls the zooming and scaling of a webpage on mobile devices. Without the viewport meta tag, mobile devices will render the webpage at a default zoom level, which can lead to a poor user experience.</p><p>The basic syntax of the viewport meta tag is as follows: <code>&lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot;&gt;</code></p><ul><li><strong>width=device-width</strong>: sets the width of the viewport to the device\'s screen width</li><li><strong>initial-scale=1.0</strong>: sets the initial zoom level to 1.0 (no zooming)</li></ul><p>By including the viewport meta tag in our HTML document, we can ensure that our website is displayed correctly on mobile devices.</p><h3>Responsive Navigation Menus</h3><p>Navigation menus are an essential component of any website. However, they can be challenging to design for smaller screens. A responsive navigation menu should be able to adapt to different screen sizes and devices.</p><p>Here are some tips for creating responsive navigation menus:</p><ul><li>Use a <strong>toggle button</strong> to hide and show the navigation menu on smaller screens</li><li>Use <strong>media queries</strong> to apply different styles based on screen size</li><li>Use <strong>flexbox</strong> or <strong>grid</strong> to create a responsive layout</li></ul><p>For example, we can use the following HTML and CSS code to create a basic responsive navigation menu: <code>&lt;nav&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;</code> <code>nav ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; }</code> <code>nav li { width: 100%; }</code> <code>@media (min-width: 768px) { nav li { width: auto; } }</code></p><p>By using these techniques, we can create a responsive navigation menu that adapts to different screen sizes and devices.</p>', 'Implementing Responsive Design', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(96, 109, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css preprocessors</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+preprocessors/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+preprocessors/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Preprocessors</h3><p>CSS preprocessors are tools that allow you to write CSS code in a more efficient and modular way. They provide features such as variables, nesting, and functions, which are not available in traditional CSS. In this lesson, we will explore three popular CSS preprocessors: Sass, Less, and Stylus.</p><h3>What is Sass?</h3><p>Sass (Syntactically Awesome Stylesheets) is one of the most popular CSS preprocessors. It was created by Hampton Catlin and is now maintained by the Sass team. Sass provides a wide range of features, including variables, nesting, and functions, which make it easier to write and maintain CSS code.</p><p>Some of the key features of Sass include:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: Sass allows you to define variables, which can be used to store values such as colors, fonts, and sizes.</li><li><strong>Nesting</strong>: Sass allows you to nest CSS selectors, which makes it easier to write CSS code and reduces the amount of code you need to write.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: Sass provides a range of built-in functions, such as <code>darken()</code> and <code>lighten()</code>, which can be used to manipulate colors and other values.</li></ul><h3>What is Less?</h3><p>Less (Leaner Style Sheets) is another popular CSS preprocessor. It was created by Alexis Sellier and is now maintained by the Less team. Less provides many of the same features as Sass, including variables, nesting, and functions.</p><p>Some of the key features of Less include:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: Less allows you to define variables, which can be used to store values such as colors, fonts, and sizes.</li><li><strong>Nesting</strong>: Less allows you to nest CSS selectors, which makes it easier to write CSS code and reduces the amount of code you need to write.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: Less provides a range of built-in functions, such as <code>darken()</code> and <code>lighten()</code>, which can be used to manipulate colors and other values.</li></ul><h3>What is Stylus?</h3><p>Stylus is a CSS preprocessor that provides a more flexible and expressive syntax than Sass or Less. It was created by TJ Holowaychuk and is now maintained by the Stylus team. Stylus provides many of the same features as Sass and Less, including variables, nesting, and functions.</p><p>Some of the key features of Stylus include:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: Stylus allows you to define variables, which can be used to store values such as colors, fonts, and sizes.</li><li><strong>Nesting</strong>: Stylus allows you to nest CSS selectors, which makes it easier to write CSS code and reduces the amount of code you need to write.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: Stylus provides a range of built-in functions, such as <code>darken()</code> and <code>lighten()</code>, which can be used to manipulate colors and other values.</li></ul><h3>Comparison of Sass, Less, and Stylus</h3><p>All three CSS preprocessors provide many of the same features, including variables, nesting, and functions. However, there are some key differences between them.</p><p>Sass is generally considered to be the most mature and widely-used CSS preprocessor, with a large community of developers and a wide range of tools and resources available. Less is also widely-used, and provides many of the same features as Sass. Stylus is less widely-used than Sass or Less, but provides a more flexible and expressive syntax.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In conclusion, CSS preprocessors such as Sass, Less, and Stylus provide a range of features that make it easier to write and maintain CSS code. By using a CSS preprocessor, you can write more efficient and modular code, and take advantage of features such as variables, nesting, and functions. Whether you choose to use Sass, Less, or Stylus, you will be able to write better CSS code and improve your overall development workflow.</p>', 'Understanding CSS Preprocessors', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(97, 110, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: sass logo</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=sass+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/sass+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/sass+logo/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/sass+logo/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=sass+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Sass</h3><p>Sass, which stands for Syntactically Awesome StyleSheets, is a preprocessor scripting language that is interpreted or compiled into Cascading Style Sheets (CSS). It is designed to make CSS more efficient, elegant, and easy to maintain. In this lesson, we will explore how to use Sass for efficient styling, focusing on variables, nesting, and mixins.</p><h3>Variables in Sass</h3><p>Variables in Sass allow you to store and reuse values in your stylesheets. This is particularly useful for maintaining consistency across your website or application. You can declare a variable using the dollar sign ($) followed by the variable name and the assignment operator (:). For example:</p><code>$primary-color: #333;</code><p>Once declared, you can use the variable in your CSS rules like this:</p><code>body { background-color: $primary-color; }</code><p>Using variables makes it easy to update the value of a property across your entire stylesheet. Simply change the value of the variable, and it will be reflected everywhere it is used.</p><h3>Nesting in Sass</h3><p>Nesting is another powerful feature of Sass that allows you to nest CSS rules within one another. This helps to reduce repetition and makes your code more readable. For example, instead of writing:</p><code>.nav { background-color: #333; } .nav ul { list-style: none; } .nav li { display: inline-block; }</code><p>You can nest the rules like this:</p><code>.nav { background-color: #333; ul { list-style: none; } li { display: inline-block; } }</code><p>Nesting also allows you to take advantage of the parent selector (&) to reference the parent element. This is useful when you want to apply different styles based on the state of the parent element. For example:</p><code>.nav { background-color: #333; &:hover { background-color: #444; } }</code><h3>Mixins in Sass</h3><p>Mixins are reusable blocks of CSS code that can be used to apply a set of styles to multiple elements. You can declare a mixin using the <code>@mixin</code> directive followed by the mixin name and a block of CSS code. For example:</p><code>@mixin reset-box-model { box-sizing: border-box; -moz-box-sizing: border-box; -webkit-box-sizing: border-box; }</code><p>Once declared, you can use the mixin in your CSS rules like this:</p><code>div { @include reset-box-model; }</code><p>Mixins can also accept arguments, which allows you to customize the styles applied by the mixin. For example:</p><code>@mixin border-radius($radius) { border-radius: $radius; -moz-border-radius: $radius; -webkit-border-radius: $radius; }</code><p>You can then use the mixin like this:</p><code>div { @include border-radius(10px); }</code><h3>Best Practices for Using Sass</h3><ul><li><strong>Use meaningful variable names</strong> to make your code easy to understand and maintain.</li><li><strong>Use nesting judiciously</strong> to avoid overly complex and hard-to-read code.</li><li><strong>Use mixins to reuse code</strong> and reduce repetition in your stylesheets.</li><li><strong>Keep your mixins and variables organized</strong> by using a consistent naming convention and grouping related code together.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices and using variables, nesting, and mixins effectively, you can write more efficient, elegant, and maintainable CSS code with Sass.</p>', 'Using Sass for Efficient Styling', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(98, 111, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css grid layout examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+grid+layout+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+grid+layout+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+grid+layout+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Advanced CSS Topics</h3><p>Welcome to Day 47 of our Web Development course, where we will be exploring advanced CSS topics. Today, we will cover CSS Grid, Flexbox, and Transforms, which are essential tools for creating complex and responsive layouts.</p><h3>CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows you to create two-dimensional grid-based layouts. It is particularly useful for creating complex and responsive layouts, such as dashboards, magazines, and galleries. The key concept in CSS Grid is the grid container, which is defined by setting <code>display: grid</code> or <code>display: inline-grid</code> on an element.</p><ul><li><strong>Grid Container</strong>: The grid container is the element that contains the grid items. It is defined by setting <code>display: grid</code> or <code>display: inline-grid</code> on an element.</li><li><strong>Grid Items</strong>: Grid items are the elements that are direct children of the grid container. They can be any HTML element, such as <code>div</code>, <code>img</code>, or <code>p</code>.</li><li><strong>Grid Tracks</strong>: Grid tracks are the rows and columns of the grid. They can be defined using the <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> properties.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic CSS Grid layout:</p><code>.grid-container {<br>  display: grid;<br>  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);<br>  grid-template-rows: repeat(2, 1fr);<br>  gap: 10px;<br>}</code><h3>Flexbox</h3><p>Flexbox is a layout mode that allows you to create flexible and responsive layouts. It is particularly useful for creating one-dimensional layouts, such as navigation bars, footers, and sidebars. The key concept in Flexbox is the flex container, which is defined by setting <code>display: flex</code> or <code>display: inline-flex</code> on an element.</p><ul><li><strong>Flex Container</strong>: The flex container is the element that contains the flex items. It is defined by setting <code>display: flex</code> or <code>display: inline-flex</code> on an element.</li><li><strong>Flex Items</strong>: Flex items are the elements that are direct children of the flex container. They can be any HTML element, such as <code>div</code>, <code>img</code>, or <code>p</code>.</li><li><strong>Flex Direction</strong>: The flex direction determines the direction in which the flex items are laid out. It can be set using the <code>flex-direction</code> property.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic Flexbox layout:</p><code>.flex-container {<br>  display: flex;<br>  flex-direction: row;<br>  justify-content: space-between;<br>  align-items: center;<br>}</code><h3>Transforms</h3><p>Transforms allow you to apply transformations to elements, such as rotations, translations, and scaling. They can be used to create complex and interactive effects, such as animations and transitions.</p><ul><li><strong>2D Transforms</strong>: 2D transforms allow you to apply transformations to elements in two-dimensional space. They can be used to create effects such as rotations, translations, and scaling.</li><li><strong>3D Transforms</strong>: 3D transforms allow you to apply transformations to elements in three-dimensional space. They can be used to create effects such as rotations, translations, and scaling in 3D space.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a basic transform:</p><code>.transformed-element {<br>  transform: rotate(45deg);<br>}</code><p>In conclusion, CSS Grid, Flexbox, and Transforms are powerful tools for creating complex and responsive layouts. By mastering these technologies, you can create web pages that are both visually appealing and user-friendly.</p>', 'Advanced CSS Topics', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(99, 112, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: front-end build tools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=front-end+build+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/front-end+build+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/front-end+build+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/front-end+build+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=front-end+build+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Front-end Build Tools</h3><p>As a front-end developer, you will often find yourself working on complex projects that involve multiple files, libraries, and dependencies. Managing these dependencies and optimizing your code for production can be a daunting task. This is where front-end build tools come in. In this lesson, we will explore three popular front-end build tools: Webpack, Gulp, and Rollup.</p><h3>Webpack</h3><p>Webpack is a popular front-end build tool that allows you to manage your code dependencies and optimize your code for production. It was created by Tobias Koppers in 2012 and has since become one of the most widely used build tools in the front-end development community.</p><p>Some of the key features of Webpack include:</p><ul><li><strong>Module bundling</strong>: Webpack allows you to bundle your code into a single file, making it easier to manage and optimize.</li><li><strong>Dependency management</strong>: Webpack can manage your dependencies for you, making it easy to include libraries and frameworks in your project.</li><li><strong>Code optimization</strong>: Webpack can optimize your code for production, including minification, compression, and tree shaking.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple Webpack configuration file:</p><code>module.exports = {<br>  entry: \'./src/index.js\',<br>  output: {<br>    filename: \'bundle.js\',<br>    path: \'./dist\'<br>  }<br>};</code><h3>Gulp</h3><p>Gulp is another popular front-end build tool that allows you to automate tasks and manage your code dependencies. It was created by Eric Schoffstall in 2013 and has since become a popular choice among front-end developers.</p><p>Some of the key features of Gulp include:</p><ul><li><strong>Task automation</strong>: Gulp allows you to automate tasks such as minification, compression, and concatenation.</li><li><strong>Dependency management</strong>: Gulp can manage your dependencies for you, making it easy to include libraries and frameworks in your project.</li><li><strong>Plugin ecosystem</strong>: Gulp has a large ecosystem of plugins that can be used to extend its functionality.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple Gulp task:</p><code>const gulp = require(\'gulp\');<br>const uglify = require(\'gulp-uglify\');<br><br>gulp.task(\'minify\', () => {<br>  return gulp.src(\'src/index.js\')<br>    .pipe(uglify())<br>    .pipe(gulp.dest(\'dist\'));<br>});</code><h3>Rollup</h3><p>Rollup is a newer front-end build tool that allows you to bundle your code into a single file. It was created by Rich Harris in 2016 and has since become a popular choice among front-end developers.</p><p>Some of the key features of Rollup include:</p><ul><li><strong>Tree shaking</strong>: Rollup can remove unused code from your bundle, resulting in smaller file sizes.</li><li><strong>ES6 module support</strong>: Rollup supports ES6 modules out of the box, making it easy to use modern JavaScript features.</li><li><strong>Plugin ecosystem</strong>: Rollup has a growing ecosystem of plugins that can be used to extend its functionality.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple Rollup configuration file:</p><code>import { rollup } from \'rollup\';<br><br>rollup({<br>  entry: \'src/index.js\',<br>  format: \'iife\'<br>})<br>.then(bundle => {<br>  bundle.write({<br>    file: \'dist/bundle.js\',<br>    format: \'iife\'<br>  });<br>});</code><p>In conclusion, Webpack, Gulp, and Rollup are all powerful front-end build tools that can help you manage your code dependencies and optimize your code for production. Each tool has its own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of which tool to use will depend on the specific needs of your project.</p>', 'Introduction to Front-end Build Tools', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(100, 113, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web performance optimization</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+performance+optimization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+performance+optimization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+performance+optimization/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+performance+optimization/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+performance+optimization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Optimizing Web Applications for Performance</h3><p>As a web developer, it\'s essential to ensure that your web application is optimized for performance. A slow-loading website can lead to a poor user experience, resulting in high bounce rates and low engagement. In this lesson, we\'ll explore three techniques to optimize web applications for performance: code splitting, minification, and caching.</p><h3>Code Splitting</h3><p>Code splitting is a technique that involves splitting your code into smaller chunks, called bundles, which can be loaded on demand. This approach helps reduce the initial load time of your web application, as only the necessary code is loaded initially. The remaining code is loaded as the user navigates through the application.</p><p>For example, if you have a web application with multiple pages, you can split your code into separate bundles for each page. When the user navigates to a page, the corresponding bundle is loaded, reducing the amount of code that needs to be loaded initially.</p><ul><li><strong>Benefits of code splitting:</strong></li><li>Reduced initial load time</li><li>Improved user experience</li><li>Smaller bundle sizes</li></ul><h3>Minification</h3><p>Minification is the process of removing unnecessary characters from your code, such as whitespace, comments, and line breaks. This reduces the size of your code, resulting in faster load times and improved performance.</p><p>There are several tools available to minify your code, including UglifyJS and Gzip. These tools can be integrated into your build process to automatically minify your code.</p><p>For example, the following code snippet:</p><code>function add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}</code><p>Can be minified to:</p><code>function add(a,b){return a+b;}</code><h3>Caching</h3><p>Caching is a technique that involves storing frequently-used data in memory, so that it can be quickly retrieved instead of being re-computed or re-fetched from a remote server. This can significantly improve the performance of your web application, as it reduces the number of requests made to the server.</p><p>There are several types of caching, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Browser caching:</strong> storing data in the user\'s browser cache</li><li><strong>Server-side caching:</strong> storing data in the server\'s memory</li><li><strong>Database caching:</strong> storing data in the database cache</li></ul><p>For example, if you have a web application that fetches data from a remote API, you can cache the response data in the user\'s browser cache. This way, when the user makes the same request again, the data can be retrieved from the cache instead of being re-fetched from the API.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>To optimize your web application for performance, follow these best practices:</p><ul><li><strong>Use code splitting to reduce initial load time</strong></li><li><strong>Minify your code to reduce bundle sizes</strong></li><li><strong>Use caching to reduce the number of requests made to the server</strong></li><li><strong>Optimize images and other media to reduce file sizes</strong></li><li><strong>Use a content delivery network (CDN) to reduce latency</strong></li></ul>', 'Optimizing Web Applications for Performance', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(101, 114, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: front-end frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Front-end Frameworks</h3><p>As we continue our journey in web development, it\'s essential to explore front-end frameworks that can simplify and accelerate our development process. In this lesson, we\'ll delve into three popular front-end frameworks: React, Angular, and Vue.js. These frameworks have gained immense popularity in recent years due to their ability to handle complex user interfaces and provide a robust set of tools for building scalable applications.</p><p>Let\'s start with <strong>React</strong>, a JavaScript library developed by Facebook. React is known for its component-based architecture, which allows developers to break down their application into smaller, reusable components. This approach makes it easier to manage and maintain complex interfaces. React also uses a virtual DOM, which improves performance by reducing the number of DOM mutations.</p><ul><li>Key features of React include:</li><li>Component-based architecture</li><li>Virtual DOM</li><li>Declarative programming style</li><li>Large community and ecosystem</li></ul><p>Next, we have <strong>Angular</strong>, a full-fledged JavaScript framework developed by Google. Angular is known for its opinionated approach to application development, providing a rich set of features out of the box. It includes a powerful template language, dependency injection, and a robust set of tools for building complex applications. Angular is ideal for large-scale enterprise applications and is widely used in industries such as finance and healthcare.</p><ul><li>Key features of Angular include:</li><li>Opinionated framework with a rich set of features</li><li>Powerful template language</li><li>Dependency injection</li><li>Robust set of tools for building complex applications</li></ul><p>Last but not least, we have <strong>Vue.js</strong>, a progressive and flexible JavaScript framework developed by Evan You. Vue.js is known for its simplicity and ease of use, making it an ideal choice for developers who are new to front-end frameworks. It includes a robust set of features such as a reactive component system, a virtual DOM, and a powerful template language. Vue.js is widely used in industries such as e-commerce and media.</p><ul><li>Key features of Vue.js include:</li><li>Progressive and flexible framework</li><li>Reactive component system</li><li>Virtual DOM</li><li>Powerful template language</li></ul><p>When choosing a front-end framework, it\'s essential to consider the specific needs of your project. <code>React</code> is ideal for complex, data-driven applications, while <code>Angular</code> is suitable for large-scale enterprise applications. <code>Vue.js</code> is a great choice for smaller applications or prototyping. Ultimately, the choice of framework depends on your personal preference, the size and complexity of your project, and the type of application you\'re building.</p>', 'Introduction to Front-end Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(102, 115, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building Responsive Web Pages</h3><p>As a web developer, it\'s essential to create websites that are accessible and look great on various devices, including desktops, laptops, tablets, and mobile phones. In this lesson, we will explore three crucial techniques for building responsive web pages: Media Queries, Flexbox, and CSS Grid.</p><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are a fundamental concept in responsive web design. They allow you to apply different styles based on specific conditions, such as screen size, device type, or orientation. You can use media queries to hide or show elements, change layouts, or adjust font sizes.</p><p>Here is an example of a basic media query:</p><code>@media only screen and (max-width: 600px) {<br>  /* styles for small screens */<br>  body {<br>    font-size: 14px;<br>  }<br>}<br></code><p>In this example, the styles inside the media query will be applied only when the screen width is 600px or less.</p><h3>Flexbox</h3><p>Flexbox is a layout mode that allows you to easily create flexible and responsive layouts. It provides a more efficient way to design complex layouts without using floats or positioning. With Flexbox, you can create rows or columns of elements that can adapt to different screen sizes.</p><p>Here are the basic properties of Flexbox:</p><ul><li><strong>display:</strong> sets the display property of an element to flex or inline-flex</li><li><strong>flex-direction:</strong> specifies the direction of the flex items (row, column, row-reverse, column-reverse)</li><li><strong>justify-content:</strong> aligns the flex items horizontally (flex-start, center, space-between, space-around)</li><li><strong>align-items:</strong> aligns the flex items vertically (flex-start, center, baseline, stretch)</li></ul><p>Here is an example of using Flexbox to create a responsive navigation bar:</p><code>.nav {<br>  display: flex;<br>  flex-direction: row;<br>  justify-content: space-between;<br>  align-items: center;<br>}<br></code><h3>CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system that allows you to create complex and responsive layouts. It provides a more efficient way to design grid-based layouts without using tables or floats. With CSS Grid, you can create rows and columns of elements that can adapt to different screen sizes.</p><p>Here are the basic properties of CSS Grid:</p><ul><li><strong>display:</strong> sets the display property of an element to grid or inline-grid</li><li><strong>grid-template-columns:</strong> specifies the number and width of columns</li><li><strong>grid-template-rows:</strong> specifies the number and height of rows</li><li><strong>grid-gap:</strong> specifies the gap between grid items</li></ul><p>Here is an example of using CSS Grid to create a responsive image gallery:</p><code>.gallery {<br>  display: grid;<br>  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);<br>  grid-template-rows: repeat(2, 1fr);<br>  grid-gap: 10px;<br>}<br></code><p>In this example, the gallery will have three columns and two rows, with a gap of 10px between each item.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we covered the basics of Media Queries, Flexbox, and CSS Grid. These techniques are essential for building responsive web pages that look great on various devices. By using media queries, you can apply different styles based on specific conditions. With Flexbox and CSS Grid, you can create flexible and responsive layouts that adapt to different screen sizes.</p>', 'Building Responsive Web Pages', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(103, 116, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: RESTful API diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=RESTful+API+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/RESTful+API+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/RESTful+API+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/RESTful+API+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=RESTful+API+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to RESTful APIs</h3><p>RESTful APIs, or Application Programming Interfaces, are an essential part of web development. They allow different applications to communicate with each other and exchange data in a standardized way. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of RESTful APIs, including API endpoints, HTTP methods, and JSON data.</p><h3>API Endpoints</h3><p>An API endpoint is a URL that an application uses to interact with an API. It is the entry point for an API, and it is used to send and receive data. API endpoints can be thought of as a way to access a specific resource or service provided by the API. For example, a social media platform might have an API endpoint for retrieving a user\'s profile information, another for posting a new update, and another for retrieving a list of friends.</p><ul><li><strong>API Endpoint Example:</strong> <code>https://api.example.com/users/123</code></li><li><strong>API Endpoint Example:</strong> <code>https://api.example.com/posts</code></li></ul><h3>HTTP Methods</h3><p>HTTP methods are used to indicate the action that should be performed on a resource. The most common HTTP methods are:</p><ul><li><strong>GET:</strong> Retrieve a resource</li><li><strong>POST:</strong> Create a new resource</li><li><strong>PUT:</strong> Update an existing resource</li><li><strong>DELETE:</strong> Delete a resource</li></ul><p>For example, if we wanted to retrieve a user\'s profile information, we would use the <strong>GET</strong> method and send a request to the API endpoint <code>https://api.example.com/users/123</code>. If we wanted to create a new post, we would use the <strong>POST</strong> method and send a request to the API endpoint <code>https://api.example.com/posts</code>.</p><h3>JSON Data</h3><p>JSON, or JavaScript Object Notation, is a lightweight data interchange format that is easy to read and write. It is commonly used for exchanging data between web servers and web applications. JSON data is represented as a collection of key-value pairs, where each key is a string and each value can be a string, number, boolean, array, or object.</p><p>For example, the following is an example of JSON data that represents a user\'s profile information:</p><code>{<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;name&quot;: &quot;John Doe&quot;,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;email&quot;: &quot;johndoe@example.com&quot;,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;age&quot;: 30<br>}</code><p>This JSON data has three key-value pairs: <strong>name</strong>, <strong>email</strong>, and <strong>age</strong>. The values for these keys are <strong>John Doe</strong>, <strong>johndoe@example.com</strong>, and <strong>30</strong>, respectively.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we learned about the basics of RESTful APIs, including API endpoints, HTTP methods, and JSON data. We saw how API endpoints are used to access specific resources or services provided by an API, and how HTTP methods are used to indicate the action that should be performed on a resource. We also learned about JSON data and how it is used to exchange data between web servers and web applications.</p>', 'Understanding RESTful APIs', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(104, 117, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: user authentication diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=user+authentication+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/user+authentication+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/user+authentication+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/user+authentication+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=user+authentication+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Implementing User Authentication</h3><p>User authentication is a crucial aspect of web development, as it allows users to access restricted areas of a website or application while preventing unauthorized access. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of user authentication, including login and registration forms, session management, and token-based authentication.</p><p>Let\'s start with the basics. When a user attempts to access a restricted area of a website, they are typically prompted to log in or register. The login process involves verifying the user\'s credentials, such as their username and password, against a database of stored user information.</p><h3>Login/Registration Forms</h3><p>A login form typically consists of two input fields: one for the username and one for the password. The registration form, on the other hand, requires additional information, such as the user\'s email address, name, and password confirmation.</p><p>Here is an example of a basic login form:</p><code>&lt;form id=&quot;login-form&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;username&quot;&gt;Username:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; id=&quot;username&quot; name=&quot;username&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;password&quot;&gt;Password:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; id=&quot;password&quot; name=&quot;password&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Login&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</code><p>And here is an example of a basic registration form:</p><code>&lt;form id=&quot;register-form&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;username&quot;&gt;Username:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; id=&quot;username&quot; name=&quot;username&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;email&quot; id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;password&quot;&gt;Password:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; id=&quot;password&quot; name=&quot;password&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;confirm-password&quot;&gt;Confirm Password:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; id=&quot;confirm-password&quot; name=&quot;confirm-password&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Register&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</code><h3>Session Management</h3><p>Once a user has logged in, their credentials are stored in a session. A session is a way to store data about a user\'s interaction with a website, such as their login status, preferences, and other relevant information.</p><p>There are several ways to manage sessions, including:</p><ul>\n  <li><strong>Server-side sessions</strong>: This involves storing session data on the server, using a unique identifier to associate the data with the user\'s browser.</li>\n  <li><strong>Client-side sessions</strong>: This involves storing session data on the client-side, using cookies or local storage to store the data.</li>\n  <li><strong>Token-based sessions</strong>: This involves storing a token on the client-side, which is used to authenticate the user on each request.</li>\n</ul><p>Here is an example of how to store session data on the server-side using Node.js and Express:</p><code>const express = require(&quot;express&quot;);\nconst app = express();\nconst session = require(&quot;express-session&quot;);\napp.use(session({\n  secret: &quot;secret-key&quot;,\n  resave: false,\n  saveUninitialized: true\n}));</code><h3>Token-based Auth</h3><p>Token-based authentication involves generating a unique token for each user, which is used to authenticate the user on each request. This approach is commonly used in RESTful APIs and single-page applications.</p><p>Here is an example of how to generate a token using JSON Web Tokens (JWT):</p><code>const jwt = require(&quot;jsonwebtoken&quot;);\nconst token = jwt.sign({\n  username: &quot;john-doe&quot;,\n  email: &quot;john.doe@example.com&quot;\n}, &quot;secret-key&quot;, {\n  expiresIn: &quot;1h&quot;\n});</code><p>The token can then be sent to the client-side, where it is stored in local storage or cookies. On each subsequent request, the token is sent back to the server, where it is verified and used to authenticate the user.</p>', 'Implementing User Authentication', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(105, 118, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web storage and cookies</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+storage+and+cookies\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+storage+and+cookies\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+storage+and+cookies/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+storage+and+cookies/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+storage+and+cookies\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Storage and Cookies</h3><p>As a web developer, it is essential to understand how to store data locally on a user\'s browser. In this lesson, we will explore two types of web storage: Local Storage and Session Storage, as well as Cookie Management.</p><h3>Local Storage</h3><p>Local Storage, also known as <strong>localStorage</strong>, is a type of web storage that allows you to store data locally on a user\'s browser. The data is stored as key-value pairs and is persistent, meaning it remains even after the user closes their browser.</p><p>Here are some key features of Local Storage:</p><ul><li>Data is stored as key-value pairs</li><li>Data is persistent and remains even after the user closes their browser</li><li>Data is limited to 5MB per origin (domain, protocol, and port)</li><li>Data is accessible using the <code>localStorage</code> object</li></ul><p>Example of using Local Storage:</p><code>localStorage.setItem(\'name\', \'John Doe\');\nconsole.log(localStorage.getItem(\'name\')); // Output: John Doe</code><h3>Session Storage</h3><p>Session Storage, also known as <strong>sessionStorage</strong>, is a type of web storage that allows you to store data locally on a user\'s browser. The data is stored as key-value pairs and is only available for the duration of the session, meaning it is deleted when the user closes their browser.</p><p>Here are some key features of Session Storage:</p><ul><li>Data is stored as key-value pairs</li><li>Data is only available for the duration of the session</li><li>Data is limited to 5MB per origin (domain, protocol, and port)</li><li>Data is accessible using the <code>sessionStorage</code> object</li></ul><p>Example of using Session Storage:</p><code>sessionStorage.setItem(\'name\', \'John Doe\');\nconsole.log(sessionStorage.getItem(\'name\')); // Output: John Doe</code><h3>Cookie Management</h3><p>Cookies are small text files that are stored on a user\'s browser. They are used to store data that needs to be accessed across multiple requests, such as user preferences or authentication information.</p><p>Here are some key features of Cookies:</p><ul><li>Data is stored as key-value pairs</li><li>Data is limited to 4KB per cookie</li><li>Data is accessible using the <code>document.cookie</code> object</li><li>Cookies can be set to expire after a certain period of time</li></ul><p>Example of using Cookies:</p><code>document.cookie = \'name=John Doe; expires=Fri, 31 Dec 2024 23:59:59 GMT\';\nconsole.log(document.cookie); // Output: name=John Doe</code><p>In conclusion, Local Storage, Session Storage, and Cookie Management are all important tools for storing data locally on a user\'s browser. By understanding how to use these tools, you can create more dynamic and interactive web applications.</p>', 'Web Storage and Cookies', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(106, 119, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web security threats</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+security+threats\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+security+threats\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+security+threats/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+security+threats/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+security+threats\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Security</h3><p>Welcome to Day 55 of our Web Development course, where we will be exploring the crucial topic of Web Security. As a web developer, it is essential to understand the common vulnerabilities that can compromise your website and learn how to protect against them.</p><p>In this lesson, we will be covering three critical topics: Common Web Vulnerabilities, SQL Injection, and Cross-site Scripting (XSS). By the end of this lesson, you will have a solid understanding of these security threats and how to defend against them.</p><h3>Common Web Vulnerabilities</h3><p>There are several common web vulnerabilities that can put your website at risk. Some of the most common include:</p><ul><li><strong>SQL Injection</strong>: This occurs when an attacker injects malicious SQL code into your website\'s database, allowing them to access sensitive data.</li><li><strong>Cross-site Scripting (XSS)</strong>: This happens when an attacker injects malicious JavaScript code into your website, allowing them to steal user data or take control of the user\'s session.</li><li><strong>Cross-site Request Forgery (CSRF)</strong>: This occurs when an attacker tricks a user into performing an unintended action on your website.</li><li><strong>Buffer Overflow</strong>: This happens when an attacker sends more data to your website than it is expecting, causing the website to crash or allowing the attacker to execute malicious code.</li></ul><h3>SQL Injection</h3><p>SQL Injection is a type of web vulnerability that occurs when an attacker injects malicious SQL code into your website\'s database. This can allow the attacker to access sensitive data, such as user passwords or credit card numbers.</p><p>For example, suppose you have a login form that uses the following SQL query to authenticate users:</p><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = \'$username\' AND password = \'$password\';</code><p>If an attacker enters the following input:</p><code>username: \' OR 1=1 --</code><p>The SQL query becomes:</p><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = \'\' OR 1=1 --\' AND password = \'$password\';</code><p>This query will always return true, allowing the attacker to gain access to the website without a valid password.</p><h3>Cross-site Scripting (XSS)</h3><p>Cross-site Scripting (XSS) is a type of web vulnerability that occurs when an attacker injects malicious JavaScript code into your website. This can allow the attacker to steal user data or take control of the user\'s session.</p><p>For example, suppose you have a comment form that allows users to enter their name and a comment. If an attacker enters the following input:</p><code><script>alert(\'XSS\')</script></code><p>The JavaScript code will be executed, allowing the attacker to steal user data or take control of the user\'s session.</p><p>To protect against XSS, it is essential to validate and sanitize all user input. This can be done using HTML escaping, which replaces special characters with their corresponding HTML entities.</p><p>For example, the input:</p><code><script>alert(\'XSS\')</script></code><p>Would be escaped as:</p><code>&lt;script&gt;alert(&#x27;XSS&#x27;)&lt;/script&gt;</code><p>This prevents the JavaScript code from being executed, protecting your website from XSS attacks.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In conclusion, web security is a critical aspect of web development. By understanding common web vulnerabilities, such as SQL Injection and Cross-site Scripting (XSS), you can take steps to protect your website and users from these threats.</p><p>Remember to always validate and sanitize user input, and use HTML escaping to prevent XSS attacks. Additionally, use prepared statements and parameterized queries to prevent SQL Injection.</p>', 'Introduction to Web Security', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(107, 120, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development concepts</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Reviewing Web Development Concepts</h3><p>As we near the end of our web development course, it\'s essential to review the fundamental concepts that we\'ve covered so far. In this lesson, we\'ll be reviewing HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and front-end frameworks. These technologies are the building blocks of the web, and understanding them is crucial for any aspiring web developer.</p><h3>HTML Review</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is used to create the structure and content of web pages. It consists of a series of elements, represented by tags, that define the different parts of a web page. Some common HTML elements include headings (<code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code>), paragraphs (<code>&lt;p&gt;</code>), links (<code>&lt;a&gt;</code>), images (<code>&lt;img&gt;</code>), and lists (<code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>).</p><ul><li><strong>Headings</strong>: Define the heading of a section or page, with <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> being the most important and <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> being the least important.</li><li><strong>Paragraphs</strong>: Define a paragraph of text, and can contain other elements such as links and images.</li><li><strong>Links</strong>: Define a hyperlink to another web page or resource, and can be used to navigate between pages or to link to external resources.</li><li><strong>Images</strong>: Define an image to be displayed on the web page, and can be used to add visual interest and illustrate points.</li><li><strong>Lists</strong>: Define a list of items, which can be ordered (<code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>) or unordered (<code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>), and can be used to present information in a clear and concise manner.</li></ul><h3>CSS Review</h3><p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is used to control the layout and visual styling of web pages. It consists of a series of rules, known as styles, that define the appearance of HTML elements. Some common CSS concepts include selectors, properties, and values.</p><ul><li><strong>Selectors</strong>: Define which HTML elements to apply styles to, and can be based on the element\'s name, class, or ID.</li><li><strong>Properties</strong>: Define the specific aspect of an element\'s appearance to be styled, such as color, font, or size.</li><li><strong>Values</strong>: Define the specific value of a property, such as a color or font size.</li></ul><h3>JavaScript Review</h3><p>JavaScript is a programming language used to add interactivity to web pages. It consists of a series of statements, known as scripts, that define the behavior of web pages. Some common JavaScript concepts include variables, data types, functions, and events.</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: Store and manipulate data, and can be used to store user input or calculate values.</li><li><strong>Data Types</strong>: Define the type of data stored in a variable, such as numbers, strings, or booleans.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: Define a block of code that can be executed multiple times, and can be used to perform tasks or calculate values.</li><li><strong>Events</strong>: Define a response to a user interaction, such as a click or key press, and can be used to add interactivity to web pages.</li></ul><h3>Front-end Frameworks Review</h3><p>Front-end frameworks are pre-built sets of CSS and JavaScript files that provide a set of pre-defined styles and functionality for building web applications. Some common front-end frameworks include Bootstrap, React, and Angular.</p><ul><li><strong>Bootstrap</strong>: A popular front-end framework that provides a set of pre-defined CSS styles and JavaScript components for building responsive web applications.</li><li><strong>React</strong>: A JavaScript library for building user interfaces, and is known for its component-based architecture and virtual DOM.</li><li><strong>Angular</strong>: A JavaScript framework for building complex web applications, and is known for its opinionated approach to application development.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, reviewing web development concepts is essential for any aspiring web developer. By understanding HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and front-end frameworks, developers can build robust, scalable, and maintainable web applications that provide a great user experience.</p>', 'Reviewing Web Development Concepts', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(108, 121, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Frameworks</h3><p>CSS frameworks are pre-defined sets of CSS classes and rules that can be used to style and layout web pages quickly and efficiently. They provide a set of pre-designed UI components, such as buttons, forms, and navigation bars, that can be easily customized and combined to create complex web applications.</p><p>In this lesson, we will introduce three popular CSS frameworks: Bootstrap, Tailwind CSS, and Materialize CSS. We will explore their features, advantages, and use cases, and provide examples of how to use them in web development projects.</p><h3>Bootstrap</h3><p>Bootstrap is one of the most popular CSS frameworks, developed by Twitter. It provides a comprehensive set of pre-designed UI components, including buttons, forms, navigation bars, and more. Bootstrap is known for its ease of use, flexibility, and large community of developers who contribute to its development and provide support.</p><ul><li>Key features of Bootstrap include:<ul><li>Responsive design: Bootstrap provides a responsive design system that adapts to different screen sizes and devices.</li><li>Pre-designed UI components: Bootstrap provides a wide range of pre-designed UI components that can be easily customized and combined.</li><li>JavaScript plugins: Bootstrap includes a set of JavaScript plugins that provide additional functionality, such as modal windows, tooltips, and more.</li></ul></li></ul><p>Example of using Bootstrap in an HTML file:<code>&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/css/bootstrap.min.css&quot;&gt;</code></p><h3>Tailwind CSS</h3><p>Tailwind CSS is a utility-first CSS framework that provides a set of pre-defined classes for styling HTML elements. It is known for its flexibility and customizability, allowing developers to create unique and complex designs quickly and efficiently.</p><ul><li>Key features of Tailwind CSS include:<ul><li>Utility-first approach: Tailwind CSS provides a set of pre-defined classes for styling HTML elements, such as <code>text-lg</code> for large text or <code>bg-blue-500</code> for a blue background.</li><li>Customizable: Tailwind CSS allows developers to customize the framework to fit their specific needs, including changing the color palette, font sizes, and more.</li><li>Lightweight: Tailwind CSS is a lightweight framework, resulting in faster page loads and improved performance.</li></ul></li></ul><p>Example of using Tailwind CSS in an HTML file:<code>&lt;div class=&quot;bg-blue-500 text-lg p-4&quot;&gt;Hello World!&lt;/div&gt;</code></p><h3>Materialize CSS</h3><p>Materialize CSS is a CSS framework developed by Google, based on the Material Design principles. It provides a set of pre-designed UI components, including buttons, forms, navigation bars, and more, that follow the Material Design guidelines.</p><ul><li>Key features of Materialize CSS include:<ul><li>Material Design principles: Materialize CSS follows the Material Design principles, providing a consistent and intuitive user interface.</li><li>Pre-designed UI components: Materialize CSS provides a wide range of pre-designed UI components that can be easily customized and combined.</li><li>Responsive design: Materialize CSS provides a responsive design system that adapts to different screen sizes and devices.</li></ul></li></ul><p>Example of using Materialize CSS in an HTML file:<code>&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/materialize/1.0.0/css/materialize.min.css&quot;&gt;</code></p><p>In conclusion, CSS frameworks such as Bootstrap, Tailwind CSS, and Materialize CSS can greatly simplify the process of styling and laying out web pages. By providing pre-designed UI components and a set of pre-defined classes, these frameworks can help developers create complex web applications quickly and efficiently. <strong>It is essential to choose the right framework for your project, considering factors such as the complexity of the design, the size of the project, and the level of customization required.</strong></p>', 'Introduction to CSS Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(109, 122, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive ui components</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+ui+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+ui+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building Responsive UI Components</h3><p>As a beginner in web development, it\'s essential to understand how to create responsive UI components that adapt to different screen sizes and devices. In this lesson, we will cover three crucial topics: Grid System, Flexbox, and Media Queries. These concepts will help you build robust and responsive user interfaces.</p><h3>Grid System</h3><p>A Grid System is a two-dimensional layout system that helps you create complex layouts using rows and columns. It\'s based on a grid container and grid items. The grid container is the parent element, and the grid items are the child elements.</p><ul><li><strong>Grid Container:</strong> The grid container is defined using the <code>display: grid;</code> or <code>display: inline-grid;</code> property.</li><li><strong>Grid Items:</strong> The grid items are the child elements of the grid container.</li><li><strong>Grid Tracks:</strong> The grid tracks are the rows and columns of the grid.</li><li><strong>Grid Cells:</strong> The grid cells are the individual units of the grid, formed by the intersection of grid tracks.</li></ul><p>Here\'s an example of a basic grid system:</p><code>&lt;div class=&quot;grid-container&quot;&gt;<br>&lt;div class=&quot;grid-item&quot;&gt;Item 1&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;div class=&quot;grid-item&quot;&gt;Item 2&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;div class=&quot;grid-item&quot;&gt;Item 3&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;/div&gt;</code><code>.grid-container {<br>  display: grid;<br>  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);<br>  grid-gap: 10px;<br>}</code><h3>Flexbox</h3><p>Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout system that helps you create flexible layouts using a flex container and flex items. It\'s ideal for creating navigation bars, footers, and other components that require flexibility.</p><ul><li><strong>Flex Container:</strong> The flex container is defined using the <code>display: flex;</code> or <code>display: inline-flex;</code> property.</li><li><strong>Flex Items:</strong> The flex items are the child elements of the flex container.</li><li><strong>Flex Direction:</strong> The flex direction determines the direction of the flex items.</li><li><strong>Justify Content:</strong> The justify content property determines the alignment of the flex items.</li></ul><p>Here\'s an example of a basic flexbox layout:</p><code>&lt;div class=&quot;flex-container&quot;&gt;<br>&lt;div class=&quot;flex-item&quot;&gt;Item 1&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;div class=&quot;flex-item&quot;&gt;Item 2&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;div class=&quot;flex-item&quot;&gt;Item 3&lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;/div&gt;</code><code>.flex-container {<br>  display: flex;<br>  flex-direction: row;<br>  justify-content: space-between;<br>}</code><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are used to apply different styles based on different conditions, such as screen size, device type, or orientation. They help you create responsive designs that adapt to different environments.</p><p>Here\'s an example of a basic media query:</p><code>@media (max-width: 768px) {<br>  .grid-container {<br>    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);<br>  }<br>}</code><p>This media query applies a different grid template to the grid container when the screen width is less than or equal to 768px.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we covered the basics of building responsive UI components using Grid System, Flexbox, and Media Queries. These concepts are essential for creating robust and responsive user interfaces. Practice using these concepts to create your own responsive designs.</p>', 'Building Responsive UI Components', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(110, 123, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css preprocessors</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+preprocessors/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+preprocessors/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+preprocessors\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Preprocessors</h3><p>CSS preprocessors are tools that allow you to write CSS code in a more efficient and modular way. They provide features such as variables, nesting, and functions, which are not available in traditional CSS. In this lesson, we will explore three popular CSS preprocessors: Sass, Less, and Stylus.</p><h3>What is Sass?</h3><p>Sass (Syntactically Awesome StyleSheets) is a CSS preprocessor that was created in 2006. It is one of the most popular CSS preprocessors and is widely used in the web development community. Sass allows you to write CSS code in a more concise and efficient way, using features such as variables, nesting, and functions.</p><p>For example, you can use variables in Sass to define a color scheme for your website:</p><code>.container {<br>  background-color: $primary-color;<br>  color: $secondary-color;<br>}</code><p>In this example, <code>$primary-color</code> and <code>$secondary-color</code> are variables that can be defined at the top of your Sass file.</p><h3>What is Less?</h3><p>Less (Leaner Style Sheets) is another popular CSS preprocessor that was created in 2009. It is similar to Sass, but has some differences in its syntax and features. Less is known for its simplicity and ease of use, making it a great choice for beginners.</p><p>For example, you can use variables in Less to define a color scheme for your website:</p><code>.container {<br>  background-color: @primary-color;<br>  color: @secondary-color;<br>}</code><p>In this example, <code>@primary-color</code> and <code>@secondary-color</code> are variables that can be defined at the top of your Less file.</p><h3>What is Stylus?</h3><p>Stylus is a CSS preprocessor that was created in 2010. It is known for its flexibility and customizability, making it a great choice for advanced users. Stylus allows you to write CSS code in a more concise and efficient way, using features such as variables, nesting, and functions.</p><p>For example, you can use variables in Stylus to define a color scheme for your website:</p><code>.container {<br>  background-color: primary-color;<br>  color: secondary-color;<br>}</code><p>In this example, <code>primary-color</code> and <code>secondary-color</code> are variables that can be defined at the top of your Stylus file.</p><h3>Comparison of Sass, Less, and Stylus</h3><p>Here is a comparison of the three CSS preprocessors:</p><ul><li><strong>Sass</strong>: Most popular, widely used, and has a large community of developers.</li><li><strong>Less</strong>: Simple and easy to use, great for beginners.</li><li><strong>Stylus</strong>: Flexible and customizable, great for advanced users.</li></ul><p>All three preprocessors have their own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of which one to use depends on your personal preferences and needs.</p><h3>Benefits of Using CSS Preprocessors</h3><p>Using CSS preprocessors can bring many benefits to your web development workflow, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Faster development time</strong>: CSS preprocessors allow you to write CSS code more efficiently, using features such as variables and nesting.</li><li><strong>Improved code organization</strong>: CSS preprocessors allow you to organize your code in a more modular way, using features such as imports and functions.</li><li><strong>Better code reuse</strong>: CSS preprocessors allow you to reuse code more easily, using features such as variables and mixins.</li></ul><p>Overall, CSS preprocessors can help you write more efficient, modular, and reusable CSS code, making your web development workflow faster and more efficient.</p>', 'Implementing CSS Preprocessors', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(111, 124, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Development Best Practices</h3><p>Welcome to Day 60 of our Web Development course. Today, we will be discussing the importance of following best practices in web development. Best practices are guidelines that help developers write clean, efficient, and maintainable code. In this lesson, we will be covering three key topics: Code Organization, Code Review, and Testing.</p><h3>Code Organization</h3><p>Code organization is the process of structuring your code in a way that makes it easy to understand, modify, and maintain. A well-organized codebase is essential for any web development project. Here are some tips for organizing your code:</p><ul><li><strong>Separate Concerns</strong>: Separate your code into different files or folders based on their functionality. For example, you can have separate files for HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.</li><li><strong>Use a Consistent Naming Convention</strong>: Use a consistent naming convention throughout your code. This makes it easier to understand and maintain your code.</li><li><strong>Use Comments</strong>: Use comments to explain what your code is doing. This makes it easier for other developers to understand your code.</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following code structure:</p><code>{\n  \"html\": \"index.html\",\n  \"css\": \"styles.css\",\n  \"js\": \"script.js\"\n}</code><h3>Code Review</h3><p>Code review is the process of reviewing someone else\'s code to ensure it meets the required standards. Code review is an essential part of web development as it helps to catch errors, improve code quality, and ensure that the code is maintainable. Here are some tips for conducting a code review:</p><ul><li><strong>Check for Syntax Errors</strong>: Check the code for syntax errors. This includes checking for missing or mismatched brackets, parentheses, and tags.</li><li><strong>Check for Logic Errors</strong>: Check the code for logic errors. This includes checking if the code is doing what it is supposed to do.</li><li><strong>Check for Performance Issues</strong>: Check the code for performance issues. This includes checking if the code is optimized for performance.</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following code to check for syntax errors:</p><code>try {\n  // code to be reviewed\n} catch (error) {\n  console.error(error);\n}</code><h3>Testing</h3><p>Testing is the process of verifying that your code works as expected. Testing is an essential part of web development as it helps to catch errors and ensure that the code is working correctly. Here are some tips for testing your code:</p><ul><li><strong>Unit Testing</strong>: Write unit tests to test individual units of code. This includes testing functions, methods, and classes.</li><li><strong>Integration Testing</strong>: Write integration tests to test how different units of code work together.</li><li><strong>End-to-End Testing</strong>: Write end-to-end tests to test the entire application from start to finish.</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following code to write a unit test:</p><code>describe(\"add function\", () => {\n  it(\"should add two numbers\", () => {\n    expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);\n  });\n});</code><p>In conclusion, following best practices in web development is essential for writing clean, efficient, and maintainable code. By following the tips outlined in this lesson, you can improve the quality of your code and ensure that it is working correctly.</p>', 'Web Development Best Practices', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(112, 125, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript Frameworks</h3><p>JavaScript frameworks are essential tools for building robust, scalable, and maintainable web applications. In this lesson, we will introduce three popular JavaScript frameworks: React, Angular, and Vue.js. These frameworks have gained significant traction in recent years and are widely used in the industry.</p><p>Before we dive into the details of each framework, let\'s first understand what a JavaScript framework is. A JavaScript framework is a set of pre-defined components, libraries, and tools that help developers build web applications quickly and efficiently. Frameworks provide a structured approach to building applications, making it easier to manage complexity and maintain code quality.</p><h3>React</h3><p>React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces. It was developed by Facebook and is now maintained by Facebook and a community of developers. React is known for its component-based architecture, which makes it easy to build reusable UI components.</p><ul><li><strong>Key Features of React:</strong></li><li>Component-based architecture</li><li>Virtual DOM (a lightweight in-memory representation of the real DOM)</li><li>One-way data binding (components only receive updates from their parents)</li><li>JSX (a syntax extension for JavaScript that allows you to write HTML-like code in your JavaScript files)</li></ul><p>React is a popular choice for building complex, data-driven interfaces. Its component-based architecture makes it easy to manage state and props, and its virtual DOM ensures that the application remains performant even with complex updates.</p><h3>Angular</h3><p>Angular is a full-fledged JavaScript framework for building complex web applications. It was developed by Google and is now maintained by Google and a community of developers. Angular is known for its opinionated approach to building applications, which means it provides a set of pre-defined guidelines and best practices for building applications.</p><ul><li><strong>Key Features of Angular:</strong></li><li>Opinionated approach to building applications</li><li>Two-way data binding (components can update their parents and vice versa)</li><li>Dependency injection (a design pattern that allows components to receive dependencies from their parents)</li><li>Templates (a way to define the structure and layout of components using HTML-like syntax)</li></ul><p>Angular is a popular choice for building complex, enterprise-level applications. Its opinionated approach ensures that applications are built with a consistent architecture, and its two-way data binding makes it easy to manage state and props.</p><h3>Vue.js</h3><p>Vue.js is a progressive and flexible JavaScript framework for building web applications. It was developed by Evan You and is now maintained by the Vue.js team and a community of developers. Vue.js is known for its simplicity and ease of use, making it a popular choice for building small to medium-sized applications.</p><ul><li><strong>Key Features of Vue.js:</strong></li><li>Progressive and flexible architecture</li><li>Two-way data binding (components can update their parents and vice versa)</li><li>Templates (a way to define the structure and layout of components using HTML-like syntax)</li><li>Reactive components (components that automatically update when their dependencies change)</li></ul><p>Vue.js is a popular choice for building small to medium-sized applications. Its progressive and flexible architecture makes it easy to build applications with a simple and consistent architecture, and its two-way data binding makes it easy to manage state and props.</p><p>In conclusion, React, Angular, and Vue.js are three popular JavaScript frameworks that have gained significant traction in recent years. Each framework has its own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of framework depends on the specific needs and requirements of the project. By understanding the key features and benefits of each framework, developers can make informed decisions about which framework to use for their next project.</p><p><code>const frameworks = [\'React\', \'Angular\', \'Vue.js\'];</code></p><p>Which framework will you choose for your next project? The answer depends on your specific needs and requirements. However, with this introduction to JavaScript frameworks, you are now equipped with the knowledge to make an informed decision.</p>', 'Introduction to JavaScript Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(113, 126, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development ui components</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+ui+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+ui+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building Reusable UI Components</h3><p>As we continue our journey in web development, it\'s essential to learn about building reusable UI components. This approach is based on a component-based architecture, which allows us to break down our application into smaller, independent pieces that can be easily reused throughout our project. In this lesson, we\'ll explore the basics of component-based architecture, props, and state, and how they can help us create reusable UI components.</p><h3>Component-Based Architecture</h3><p>A component-based architecture is a design pattern that emphasizes the use of self-contained, modular components that can be easily composed to form a larger application. Each component represents a small piece of the UI, such as a button, input field, or navigation menu. By breaking down our application into smaller components, we can reuse them in different parts of our project, reducing code duplication and making our codebase more maintainable.</p><p>The key characteristics of a component-based architecture include:</p><ul><li><strong>Modularity</strong>: Each component is a self-contained unit that can be developed, tested, and maintained independently.</li><li><strong>Reusability</strong>: Components can be reused throughout the application, reducing code duplication and improving maintainability.</li><li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: Components can be easily composed to form a larger application, allowing for a high degree of customization and flexibility.</li></ul><h3>Props</h3><p>Props, short for properties, are a way to pass data from a parent component to a child component. They allow us to customize the behavior and appearance of a component without modifying its internal code. Props are immutable, meaning they cannot be changed by the child component, and are typically used to pass static data or configuration options.</p><p>For example, if we have a <code>Button</code> component that accepts a <code>label</code> prop, we can pass a string value to customize the button\'s text:</p><p><code>&lt;Button label=&quot;Click me!&quot; /&gt;</code></p><p>In this example, the <code>Button</code> component receives the <code>label</code> prop and uses it to display the button\'s text.</p><h3>State</h3><p>State, on the other hand, refers to the dynamic data that a component uses to render its UI. Unlike props, state is mutable and can be changed by the component itself. State is typically used to store data that changes over time, such as user input, network responses, or animation states.</p><p>For example, if we have a <code>Counter</code> component that displays a count value, we can use state to store the current count and update it when the user clicks a button:</p><p><code>const [count, setCount] = useState(0);</code></p><p>In this example, the <code>Counter</code> component uses the <code>useState</code> hook to create a state variable <code>count</code> and an <code>setCount</code> function to update it.</p><h3>Building Reusable UI Components</h3><p>Now that we\'ve covered the basics of component-based architecture, props, and state, let\'s talk about how to build reusable UI components. Here are some best practices to keep in mind:</p><ul><li><strong>Keep it simple</strong>: Avoid complex logic and focus on a single task per component.</li><li><strong>Use props and state</strong>: Use props to pass data from parent components and state to store dynamic data.</li><li><strong>Make it customizable</strong>: Use props to allow parent components to customize the component\'s behavior and appearance.</li><li><strong>Test and iterate</strong>: Test your components thoroughly and iterate on their design and implementation as needed.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices and using a component-based architecture, props, and state, you can create reusable UI components that are easy to maintain, customize, and reuse throughout your application.</p>', 'Building Reusable UI Components', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(114, 127, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web project folder structure example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+project+folder+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+project+folder+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+project+folder+structure+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+project+folder+structure+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+project+folder+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Project Requirements</h3>\n<p>Before starting any web development project, it is essential to define clear requirements. This ensures everyone involved understands the project’s goals and scope. Requirements are typically divided into three categories:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Functional Requirements:</strong> What the system must do (e.g., user authentication, form validation).</li>\n  <li><strong>Non-Functional Requirements:</strong> How the system should perform (e.g., load time < 2 seconds, responsive design).</li>\n  <li><strong>Technical Requirements:</strong> Tools and technologies to use (e.g., React, Node.js, PostgreSQL).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steps to Gather Requirements:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Brainstorm with stakeholders (clients, users).</li>\n  <li>Write user stories: <code>As a [user role], I want [goal] so that [benefit].</code></li>\n  <li>Create a checklist of features and prioritize them.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Project Structure</h3>\n<p>A well-organized project structure improves maintainability and collaboration. For a typical web app, use this folder hierarchy:</p>\n<pre>\n<code>\nproject-root/\n├── public/            # Static files (e.g., index.html)\n├── src/               # Source code\n│   ├── components/    # Reusable UI elements\n│   ├── pages/         # Page-specific logic\n│   └── assets/        # Images, fonts\n├── styles/            # CSS/SCSS files\n├── scripts/           # Custom build tools\n├── package.json       # Project metadata and dependencies\n└── .gitignore         # Files to exclude from version control\n</code>\n</pre>\n<p>Use a <code>package.json</code> file to manage dependencies and scripts. Run <code>npm init -y</code> to generate it automatically.</p>\n\n<h3>Version Control</h3>\n<p>Version control systems like Git track changes to your code over time. Start by initializing a repository:</p>\n<code>git init</code>\n<p>Create a <code>.gitignore</code> file to exclude sensitive or unnecessary files (e.g., <code>node_modules/</code>). Basic branching strategy:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>main</code>: Stable, production-ready code.</li>\n  <li><code>develop</code>: Integration of new features.</li>\n  <li><code>feature/xxx</code>: Isolated work for specific tasks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Commit changes frequently with descriptive messages: <code>git commit -m \"Add login form validation\"</code></p>', 'Final Project Planning and Setup', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(115, 128, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Web Design</h3><p>Responsive web design is an approach to web design that makes web pages render well on a variety of devices and window or screen sizes. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of responsive web design, including media queries, flexible grids, and responsive images.</p><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are a fundamental component of responsive web design. They allow you to apply different styles based on certain conditions, such as screen size or device type. Media queries are defined using the <code>@media</code> rule, followed by a condition, and then the styles that should be applied if the condition is true.</p><p>For example, the following media query applies styles to screens with a maximum width of 768 pixels:</p><p><code>@media (max-width: 768px) {</code></p><p><code>  /* styles to apply */</code></p><p><code>}</code></p><p>This can be useful for applying different layouts or styles to different screen sizes. You can also use media queries to apply styles based on other conditions, such as device orientation or screen resolution.</p><h3>Flexible Grids</h3><p>A flexible grid is a grid system that is based on relative units, such as percentages or ems, rather than fixed units, such as pixels. This allows the grid to adapt to different screen sizes and devices. Flexible grids are often used in conjunction with media queries to create responsive layouts.</p><p>There are several different types of flexible grids, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Fluid Grids</strong>: These grids use percentages to define the width of columns and other elements. This allows the grid to adapt to different screen sizes.</li><li><strong> Elastic Grids</strong>: These grids use ems to define the width of columns and other elements. This allows the grid to adapt to different font sizes and screen resolutions.</li></ul><p>Flexible grids can be created using CSS, and can be used to create complex and responsive layouts.</p><h3>Responsive Images</h3><p>Responsive images are images that adapt to different screen sizes and devices. There are several techniques for creating responsive images, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Using the <code>max-width</code> property</strong>: This property can be used to set the maximum width of an image, allowing it to scale down to fit smaller screens.</li><li><strong>Using the <code>width</code> property with a percentage value</strong>: This can be used to set the width of an image as a percentage of its parent element, allowing it to scale up or down to fit different screen sizes.</li><li><strong>Using the <code>picture</code> element</strong>: This element allows you to specify multiple sources for an image, and can be used to provide different versions of an image for different screen sizes or devices.</li></ul><p>Responsive images are an important part of responsive web design, as they allow images to adapt to different screen sizes and devices, and can help to improve the overall user experience.</p>', 'Introduction to Responsive Web Design', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(116, 129, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive design examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Implementing Responsive Design</h3><p>Responsive design is a crucial aspect of web development, as it ensures that websites are accessible and user-friendly across various devices and screen sizes. In this lesson, we will explore the mobile-first approach, responsive navigation, and cross-browser compatibility.</p><h3>Mobile-First Approach</h3><p>The mobile-first approach involves designing and developing websites for mobile devices first, and then scaling up to larger screens. This approach has several benefits, including improved page loading times, better user experience, and increased conversion rates.</p><ul><li><strong>Benefits of Mobile-First Approach:</strong></li><li>Improved page loading times: Mobile devices have limited processing power and memory, so optimizing for mobile devices first ensures that websites load quickly and efficiently.</li><li>Better user experience: Mobile devices have smaller screens, so designing for mobile devices first forces developers to prioritize content and simplify the user interface.</li><li>Increased conversion rates: Mobile devices are often used for specific tasks, such as making purchases or searching for information, so optimizing for mobile devices first can lead to higher conversion rates.</li></ul><p>To implement the mobile-first approach, developers can use CSS media queries to apply different styles based on screen size. For example:</p><code>/* Mobile-first approach */\n@media only screen and (max-width: 768px) {\n  /* styles for mobile devices */\n}\n@media only screen and (min-width: 769px) {\n  /* styles for larger screens */\n}</code><h3>Responsive Navigation</h3><p>Responsive navigation is critical for ensuring that websites are accessible and user-friendly across various devices and screen sizes. There are several techniques for creating responsive navigation, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Techniques for Responsive Navigation:</strong></li><li>Toggle navigation: This involves hiding the navigation menu on smaller screens and displaying a toggle button that users can click to access the menu.</li><li>Accordion navigation: This involves using accordion-style menus that expand and collapse on smaller screens.</li><li>Off-canvas navigation: This involves hiding the navigation menu off-screen on smaller screens and using a button to toggle the menu on and off.</li></ul><p>For example, to create a toggle navigation menu, developers can use the following HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code:</p><code>&lt;!-- HTML --&gt;\n&lt;nav id=&quot;nav&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;button id=&quot;nav-toggle&quot;&gt;Toggle Navigation&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- CSS --&gt;\n#nav ul {\n  list-style: none;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n}\n\n#nav-toggle {\n  display: none;\n}\n\n@media only screen and (max-width: 768px) {\n  #nav ul {\n    display: none;\n  }\n  #nav-toggle {\n    display: block;\n  }\n}\n\n&lt;!-- JavaScript --&gt;\nconst navToggle = document.getElementById(&quot;nav-toggle&quot;);\nconst nav = document.getElementById(&quot;nav&quot;);\nnavToggle.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, () => {\n  nav.classList.toggle(&quot;open&quot;);\n});</code><h3>Cross-Browser Compatibility</h3><p>Cross-browser compatibility is critical for ensuring that websites are accessible and user-friendly across various browsers and devices. There are several techniques for achieving cross-browser compatibility, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Techniques for Cross-Browser Compatibility:</strong></li><li>Using standard HTML, CSS, and JavaScript: Developers should use standard HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to ensure that websites are compatible with most browsers.</li><li>Testing across multiple browsers: Developers should test websites across multiple browsers to identify and fix compatibility issues.</li><li>Using browser prefixes: Developers can use browser prefixes to ensure that CSS styles are applied correctly across different browsers.</li></ul><p>For example, to use browser prefixes, developers can use the following CSS code:</p><code>/* Browser prefixes */\n-webkit-border-radius: 10px;\n-moz-border-radius: 10px;\n-ms-border-radius: 10px;\n-o-border-radius: 10px;\nborder-radius: 10px;</code>', 'Implementing Responsive Design', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(117, 130, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css grid flexbox animations</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+grid+flexbox+animations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+grid+flexbox+animations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+grid+flexbox+animations/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+grid+flexbox+animations/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+grid+flexbox+animations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Advanced CSS Topics</h3><p>Welcome to Day 66 of our Web Development course, where we will be exploring three advanced CSS topics: CSS Grid, CSS Flexbox, and CSS Animations. These topics will help you take your web development skills to the next level and create more complex and responsive layouts.</p><h3>CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows you to create two-dimensional grid-based layouts. It is similar to tables, but offers more flexibility and control. With CSS Grid, you can define rows and columns, and place items within those rows and columns.</p><ul><li><strong>Grid Container</strong>: The grid container is the element that contains the grid items. You can define a grid container by setting <code>display: grid;</code> or <code>display: inline-grid;</code> on an element.</li><li><strong>Grid Items</strong>: Grid items are the elements that are placed within the grid container. You can define grid items by setting <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties on an element.</li><li><strong>Grid Template Columns and Rows</strong>: You can define the number and size of columns and rows in the grid container using the <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> properties.</li></ul><p>Example of CSS Grid:</p><code>.grid-container {<br>  display: grid;<br>  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr 1fr;<br>  grid-template-rows: 100px 200px;<br>}<br>.grid-item {<br>  grid-column: 1 / 3;<br>  grid-row: 1 / 2;<br>}</code><h3>CSS Flexbox</h3><p>CSS Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout system that allows you to create flexible and responsive layouts. It is similar to CSS Grid, but is used for one-dimensional layouts, such as horizontal or vertical layouts.</p><ul><li><strong>Flex Container</strong>: The flex container is the element that contains the flex items. You can define a flex container by setting <code>display: flex;</code> or <code>display: inline-flex;</code> on an element.</li><li><strong>Flex Items</strong>: Flex items are the elements that are placed within the flex container. You can define flex items by setting <code>flex-basis</code>, <code>flex-grow</code>, and <code>flex-shrink</code> properties on an element.</li><li><strong>Justify Content and Align Items</strong>: You can define how the flex items are aligned within the flex container using the <code>justify-content</code> and <code>align-items</code> properties.</li></ul><p>Example of CSS Flexbox:</p><code>.flex-container {<br>  display: flex;<br>  justify-content: space-between;<br>  align-items: center;<br>}<br>.flex-item {<br>  flex-basis: 20%;<br>  flex-grow: 1;<br>  flex-shrink: 0;<br>}</code><h3>CSS Animations</h3><p>CSS Animations allow you to create animations and transitions on web pages using CSS. You can define keyframe animations and apply them to elements using the <code>animation</code> property.</p><ul><li><strong>Keyframe Animations</strong>: You can define keyframe animations using the <code>@keyframes</code> rule. You can specify the animation name, duration, and timing function.</li><li><strong>Animation Property</strong>: You can apply an animation to an element by setting the <code>animation</code> property. You can specify the animation name, duration, and timing function.</li><li><strong>Transition Property</strong>: You can define a transition effect on an element by setting the <code>transition</code> property. You can specify the property to transition, duration, and timing function.</li></ul><p>Example of CSS Animation:</p><code>@keyframes example {<br>  0% {<br>    transform: translateX(0);<br>  }<br>  100% {<br>    transform: translateX(100px);<br>  }<br>}<br>.animate {<br>  animation: example 2s infinite;<br>}</code><p>In conclusion, CSS Grid, CSS Flexbox, and CSS Animations are powerful tools that can help you create complex and responsive layouts. With practice and experience, you can master these advanced CSS topics and take your web development skills to the next level.</p>', 'Advanced CSS Topics', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(118, 131, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript Frameworks</h3><p>JavaScript frameworks are essential tools for building robust, scalable, and maintainable web applications. In this lesson, we will explore three popular JavaScript frameworks: React, Angular, and Vue.js. Each framework has its strengths and weaknesses, and understanding their differences is crucial for selecting the right tool for your project.</p><h3>React</h3><p>React is a JavaScript library developed by Facebook. It is used for building user interfaces and can be used for developing single-page applications. React is known for its component-based architecture, which makes it easy to manage and reuse code. React components are self-contained pieces of code that represent a part of the user interface.</p><ul><li><strong>Key Features of React:</strong></li><li>Component-based architecture</li><li>Virtual DOM (a lightweight in-memory representation of the real DOM)</li><li>One-way data binding (components only receive updates from their parents)</li><li>JSX (a syntax extension for JavaScript that allows HTML-like code in JavaScript files)</li></ul><p>React is a popular choice among developers due to its simplicity, flexibility, and large community of developers who contribute to its ecosystem. However, it can be challenging to learn for beginners, especially those without prior experience with JavaScript.</p><h3>Angular</h3><p>Angular is a full-fledged JavaScript framework developed by Google. It is used for building complex web applications and is known for its opinionated approach to development. Angular provides a rich set of features out of the box, including dependency injection, services, and forms management.</p><ul><li><strong>Key Features of Angular:</strong></li><li>Opinionated framework with a rich set of features</li><li>Dependency injection (a design pattern that allows components to be loosely coupled)</li><li>Services (a way to share data between components)</li><li>Forms management (a set of APIs for managing form data and validation)</li></ul><p>Angular is a good choice for large-scale applications that require a high degree of structure and organization. However, its complexity and steep learning curve can make it challenging for beginners to learn.</p><h3>Vue.js</h3><p>Vue.js is a progressive and flexible JavaScript framework developed by Evan You. It is designed to be approachable and easy to learn, making it a great choice for beginners. Vue.js is known for its simplicity, flexibility, and robust set of features, including reactivity, components, and routing.</p><ul><li><strong>Key Features of Vue.js:</strong></li><li>Progressive and flexible framework</li><li>Reactivity (a system for automatically updating the UI when data changes)</li><li>Components (self-contained pieces of code that represent a part of the UI)</li><li>Routing (a system for managing client-side routes)</li></ul><p>Vue.js is a popular choice among developers due to its ease of use, flexibility, and growing ecosystem. However, it may lack the maturity and large community of developers that React and Angular have.</p><h3>Comparison of React, Angular, and Vue.js</h3><p>Each framework has its strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of which one to use depends on the specific needs of your project. Here is a brief comparison of the three frameworks:</p><ul><li><strong>React:</strong> Simple, flexible, and widely adopted, but can be challenging to learn for beginners.</li><li><strong>Angular:</strong> Opinionated, feature-rich, and well-structured, but complex and challenging to learn.</li><li><strong>Vue.js:</strong> Approachable, flexible, and easy to learn, but may lack the maturity and large community of React and Angular.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, React, Angular, and Vue.js are three popular JavaScript frameworks that can help you build robust, scalable, and maintainable web applications. By understanding the strengths and weaknesses of each framework, you can make an informed decision about which one to use for your next project.</p>', 'Introduction to JavaScript Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(119, 132, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: react web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=react+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/react+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/react+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/react+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=react+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building a Web Application with React</h3><p>Welcome to day 68 of our web development course, where we will be exploring the world of React and learning how to build a web application using this powerful JavaScript library. React is a popular choice for building user interfaces and single-page applications, and is used by many top companies around the world.</p><h3>React Components</h3><p>In React, a component is a small, reusable piece of code that represents a part of your user interface. Components can be thought of as building blocks, and can be combined to create more complex interfaces. There are two main types of components in React: functional components and class components.</p><ul><li><strong>Functional Components</strong>: These are the simplest type of component, and are defined as a function that returns JSX (a syntax extension for JavaScript that allows you to write HTML-like code in your JavaScript files). Functional components are stateless, meaning they do not have their own state, and are often used for simple, presentational components.</li><li><strong>Class Components</strong>: These are more complex than functional components, and are defined as a class that extends the <code>React.Component</code> class. Class components have their own state and lifecycle methods, and are often used for more complex, interactive components.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple functional component:</p><code>function HelloWorld() {<br>  return <h1>Hello, World!</h1>;<br>}</code><p>And here is an example of a simple class component:</p><code>class HelloWorld extends React.Component {<br>  render() {<br>    return <h1>Hello, World!</h1>;<br>  }<br>}</code><h3>React State</h3><p>In React, state refers to the data that changes over time in your application. State is used to store the current state of your application, and is updated using the <code>setState()</code> method. There are several key concepts to understand when working with state in React:</p><ul><li><strong>State is Local</strong>: Each component has its own state, and state is not shared between components.</li><li><strong>State is Immutable</strong>: State should not be mutated directly, but instead should be updated using the <code>setState()</code> method.</li><li><strong>State is Updated Asynchronously</strong>: When you call <code>setState()</code>, the update is not immediate, but instead is batched and updated at a later time.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of using state in a class component:</p><code>class Counter extends React.Component {<br>  constructor(props) {<br>    super(props);<br>    this.state = { count: 0 };<br>  }<br>  <br>  handleClick = () => {<br>    this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });<br>  }<br>  <br>  render() {<br>    return (<br>      <div><br>        <p>Count: {this.state.count}</p><br>        <button onClick={this.handleClick}>Increment</button><br>      </div><br>    );<br>  }<br>}</code><h3>React Props</h3><p>In React, props (short for properties) are used to pass data from a parent component to a child component. Props are read-only, meaning they should not be changed by the child component. There are several key concepts to understand when working with props:</p><ul><li><strong>Props are Read-Only</strong>: Props should not be changed by the child component.</li><li><strong>Props are Passed from Parent to Child</strong>: Props are passed from a parent component to a child component using the JSX syntax.</li><li><strong>Props can be Defaulted</strong>: Props can be given default values using the <code>defaultProps</code> property.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of using props in a functional component:</p><code>function Greeting(props) {<br>  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>;<br>}</code><p>And here is an example of using props in a class component:</p><code>class Greeting extends React.Component {<br>  render() {<br>    return <h1>Hello, {this.props.name}!</h1>;<br>  }<br>}</code><p>In this example, the <code>name</code> prop is passed from a parent component to the <code>Greeting</code> component, and is used to display a personalized greeting.</p>', 'Building a Web Application with React', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(120, 133, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: react state management</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=react+state+management\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/react+state+management\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/react+state+management/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/react+state+management/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=react+state+management\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to State Management in React</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the concept of state management in React applications. State management refers to the process of managing the state of an application, which includes the data and properties that change over time. Effective state management is crucial for building scalable and maintainable React applications.</p><h3>React Hooks</h3><p>React Hooks provide a way to manage state in functional components. The <code>useState</code> hook is used to add state to functional components. It takes an initial value as an argument and returns an array with two elements: the current state value and a function to update the state.</p><ul><li>The <code>useState</code> hook is used to manage local state in functional components.</li><li>The <code>useReducer</code> hook is used to manage more complex state logic.</li><li>The <code>useContext</code> hook is used to subscribe to context changes.</li></ul><p>Example of using <code>useState</code> hook: <code>const [count, setCount] = useState(0);</code></p><h3>Redux</h3><p>Redux is a state management library that helps manage global state by providing a single source of truth for the entire application. It consists of three main components: actions, reducers, and the store.</p><ul><li><strong>Actions</strong>: These are payloads that are sent to the store to trigger state changes.</li><li><strong>Reducers</strong>: These are pure functions that take the current state and an action, and return a new state.</li><li><strong>Store</strong>: This is the central location that holds the entire state of the application.</li></ul><p>Example of using Redux: <code>const store = createStore(reducer, initialState);</code></p><h3>Context API</h3><p>The Context API is a built-in React API that provides a way to share data between components without passing props down manually. It consists of two main components: the <code>Context</code> object and the <code>Provider</code> component.</p><ul><li>The <code>Context</code> object is created using the <code>React.createContext</code> method.</li><li>The <code>Provider</code> component is used to wrap the components that need access to the context.</li></ul><p>Example of using Context API: <code>const ThemeContext = React.createContext();</code></p><h3>Comparison of State Management Approaches</h3><p>In this lesson, we have explored three different approaches to state management in React: React Hooks, Redux, and Context API. Each approach has its own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of which one to use depends on the specific needs of the application.</p><ul><li><strong>React Hooks</strong>: Suitable for small to medium-sized applications with simple state logic.</li><li><strong>Redux</strong>: Suitable for large and complex applications with multiple interconnected components.</li><li><strong>Context API</strong>: Suitable for sharing data between components without passing props down manually.</li></ul>', 'State Management in React', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(121, 134, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design tools and deployment</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+tools+and+deployment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+tools+and+deployment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+tools+and+deployment/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+tools+and+deployment/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+tools+and+deployment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 70: Final Project - Building a Responsive Web Application</h3>\n<p>Welcome to the final project of your web development journey! Today, you\'ll apply everything you\'ve learned to create a fully functional, responsive web application. We\'ll break this into three phases: planning, implementation, and deployment.</p>\n\n<h3>Project Planning</h3>\n<p>Begin by defining your app\'s purpose. Ask:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>What problem does it solve?</li>\n  <li>Who is the target audience?</li>\n  <li>What core features are essential?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Create <strong>user stories</strong> (e.g., \"As a user, I want to search for recipes by ingredient\"). Sketch wireframes using tools like Figma or Balsamiq. Choose your tech stack: HTML/CSS/JavaScript for front-end, plus Bootstrap or Tailwind for responsiveness. Use GitHub for version control with <code>git init</code> and <code>git commit</code>.</p>\n\n<h3>Project Implementation</h3>\n<p>1. **Folder Structure**: Organize files as:</p>\n<code>\n  project-root/<br>\n  ├── index.html<br>\n  ├── styles/<br>\n  │   └── main.css<br>\n  ├── scripts/<br>\n  │   └── app.js<br>\n  └── assets/<br>\n      └── images/<br>\n</code>\n\n<p>2. **Responsive Design**: Use media queries in CSS:</p>\n<code>@media (max-width: 768px) {<br>  .container { width: 100%; }<br>}</code>\n\n<p>Implement Flexbox/Grid for layout. Add interactivity with JavaScript (e.g., form validation). Test responsiveness using browser developer tools and real devices.</p>\n\n<h3>Project Deployment</h3>\n<p>Choose a hosting platform:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>GitHub Pages</strong>: Free, ideal for static sites. Use <code>gh-pages</code> branch.</li>\n  <li><strong>Netlify/Vercel</strong>: Drag-and-drop deployments with custom domains.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps for GitHub Pages:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Push code to GitHub.</li>\n  <li>Go to Repository > Settings > Pages.</li>\n  <li>Select <code>main</code> branch and <code>/ (root)</code> as source.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Post-deployment: Monitor performance with Lighthouse, gather user feedback, and plan future updates.</p>', 'Final Project: Building a Responsive Web Application', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(122, 135, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: react hooks introduction</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=react+hooks+introduction\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/react+hooks+introduction\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/react+hooks+introduction/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/react+hooks+introduction/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=react+hooks+introduction\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to React Hooks</h3><p>React Hooks is a feature introduced in React 16.8 that allows you to use state and other React features in functional components. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of React Hooks and learn how to use them to build more complex and dynamic user interfaces.</p><p>React Hooks provides a way to \'hook into\' React state and lifecycle methods from functional components. This allows you to use React features such as state, context, and lifecycle methods in functional components, making them more powerful and flexible.</p><h3>useState Hook</h3><p>The <code>useState</code> hook is used to add state to functional components. It takes an initial value as an argument and returns an array with two elements: the current state value and a function to update the state.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use the <code>useState</code> hook:</p><ul><li><code>import { useState } from \'react\';</code></li><li><code>function Counter() {</code></li><li><code>  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);</code></li><li><code>  return (</code></li><li><code>    <div></code></li><li><code>      <p>Count: {count}</p></code></li><li><code>      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>+</button></code></li><li><code>    </div></code></li><li><code>  );</code></li><li><code>}</code></li></ul><p>In this example, the <code>useState</code> hook is used to create a state variable <code>count</code> with an initial value of 0. The <code>setCount</code> function is used to update the state.</p><h3>useEffect Hook</h3><p>The <code>useEffect</code> hook is used to handle side effects in functional components. It takes a function as an argument that is executed after the component has rendered.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use the <code>useEffect</code> hook:</p><ul><li><code>import { useState, useEffect } from \'react\';</code></li><li><code>function Counter() {</code></li><li><code>  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);</code></li><li><code>  useEffect(() => {</code></li><li><code>    document.title = `Count: ${count}`;</code></li><li><code>  }, [count]);</code></li><li><code>  return (</code></li><li><code>    <div></code></li><li><code>      <p>Count: {count}</p></code></li><li><code>      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>+</button></code></li><li><code>    </div></code></li><li><code>  );</code></li><li><code>}</code></li></ul><p>In this example, the <code>useEffect</code> hook is used to update the document title with the current count. The effect is only re-run when the count changes.</p><p>React Hooks provides a way to build more complex and dynamic user interfaces using functional components. The <code>useState</code> and <code>useEffect</code> hooks are two of the most commonly used hooks in React.</p><p>By using React Hooks, you can create more efficient and scalable applications. In the next lesson, we will explore more advanced topics in React Hooks.</p>', 'Introduction to React Hooks', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(123, 136, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: react app structure</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=react+app+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/react+app+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/react+app+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/react+app+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=react+app+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building a Simple React App</h3><p>Welcome to day 72 of our web development course. Today, we will be learning about building a simple React app. React is a popular JavaScript library used for building user interfaces. It allows us to create reusable UI components and manage the state of our application.</p><h3>React App Structure</h3><p>A typical React app consists of several files and folders. The main files are <code>index.js</code>, <code>App.js</code>, and <code>index.html</code>. The <code>index.js</code> file is the entry point of our application, where we render our React components to the DOM. The <code>App.js</code> file contains our main <code>App</code> component, which is the root component of our application. The <code>index.html</code> file is the HTML file that contains the DOM element where our React components will be rendered.</p><p>The React app structure typically looks like this:</p><ul><li><code>public</code> folder: contains the <code>index.html</code> file</li><li><code>src</code> folder: contains the JavaScript files, including <code>index.js</code> and <code>App.js</code></li><li><code>node_modules</code> folder: contains the installed dependencies</li><li><code>package.json</code> file: contains the metadata of our project</li></ul><h3>JSX</h3><p>JSX is a syntax extension for JavaScript that allows us to write HTML-like code in our JavaScript files. It is used to create React elements, which are the building blocks of our React app. JSX is similar to HTML, but it has some differences. For example, in JSX, we use <code>className</code> instead of <code>class</code> to specify the CSS class of an element.</p><p>Here is an example of JSX:</p><code>const element = &lt;div&gt;<br>  &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;<br>&lt;/div&gt;;</code><p>This JSX code creates a <code>div</code> element that contains an <code>h1</code> element with the text <strong>Hello World</strong>.</p><h3>Components</h3><p>Components are the core of React. They are reusable pieces of code that represent a part of our UI. A component can contain other components, which allows us to create complex UI structures. There are two types of components in React: functional components and class components.</p><p>A functional component is a function that returns a React element. Here is an example of a functional component:</p><code>function HelloWorld() {<br>  return &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;;<br>}</code><p>A class component is a class that extends the <code>React.Component</code> class. Here is an example of a class component:</p><code>class HelloWorld extends React.Component {<br>  render() {<br>    return &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;;<br>  }<br>}</code><p>Both of these components will render an <code>h1</code> element with the text <strong>Hello World</strong>. However, class components have some additional features, such as the ability to handle state changes and lifecycle methods.</p>', 'Building a Simple React App', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
