(124, 137, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: react state management</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=react+state+management\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/react+state+management\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/react+state+management/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/react+state+management/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=react+state+management\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to State Management in React</h3><p>State management is a crucial aspect of building robust and scalable React applications. As your application grows, managing state can become complex and challenging. In this lesson, we will explore two popular state management libraries in React: Redux and React Context API.</p><h3>What is State Management?</h3><p>State management refers to the process of managing the state of your application. State can include user input, data fetched from an API, or any other data that changes over time. Effective state management is essential to ensure that your application is predictable, scalable, and easy to maintain.</p><h3>Redux</h3><p>Redux is a popular state management library that helps you manage global state by providing a single source of truth for your application\'s state. Redux is based on the Flux architecture, which is designed to handle complex, data-driven applications.</p><ul><li><strong>Key Concepts:</strong></li><li>Store: The central location that holds the entire state of the application.</li><li>Actions: Payloads that trigger state changes.</li><li>Reducers: Pure functions that take the current state and an action, and return a new state.</li><li>Dispatch: The process of sending an action to the store to update the state.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how you can use Redux in a React application:</p><code>import { createStore } from \'redux\';\nconst initialState = { count: 0 };\nconst reducer = (state = initialState, action) => {\n  switch (action.type) {\n    case \'INCREMENT\':\n      return { count: state.count + 1 };\n    case \'DECREMENT\':\n      return { count: state.count - 1 };\n    default:\n      return state;\n  }\n};\nconst store = createStore(reducer);\n</code><h3>React Context API</h3><p>The React Context API is a built-in state management solution that allows you to share state between components without passing props down manually. The Context API is a simple and lightweight solution that is easy to use and understand.</p><ul><li><strong>Key Concepts:</strong></li><li>Context: The object that holds the state and provides it to the components.</li><li>Provider: The component that wraps the application and provides the context to the components.</li><li>Consumer: The component that uses the context and re-renders when the context changes.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how you can use the React Context API in a React application:</p><code>import { createContext, useState } from \'react\';\nconst CountContext = createContext();\nconst CountProvider = ({ children }) => {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  return (\n    <CountContext.Provider value={{ count, setCount }}>\n      {children}\n    </CountContext.Provider>\n  );\n};\n</code><h3>Comparison of Redux and React Context API</h3><p>Both Redux and React Context API are popular state management solutions, but they have different use cases and advantages. Redux is a more robust and scalable solution that is suitable for complex, data-driven applications. The React Context API is a simpler and more lightweight solution that is suitable for smaller applications or prototyping.</p><ul><li><strong>Advantages of Redux:</strong></li><li>More scalable and robust.</li><li>Better suited for complex, data-driven applications.</li><li>Provides a single source of truth for the application\'s state.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Advantages of React Context API:</strong></li><li>Simpler and more lightweight.</li><li>Easier to use and understand.</li><li>Provides a simple way to share state between components.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, state management is a crucial aspect of building robust and scalable React applications. Redux and React Context API are two popular state management libraries that can help you manage global state and provide a single source of truth for your application\'s state. By understanding the key concepts and advantages of each library, you can choose the best solution for your application\'s needs.</p>', 'State Management in React', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(125, 138, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: react router</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=react+router\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/react+router\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/react+router/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/react+router/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=react+router\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to React Routing and Navigation</h3><p>React Router is a popular library used for routing and navigation in React applications. It provides a simple and efficient way to manage client-side routing, allowing users to navigate between different views or pages in an application.</p><p>In this lesson, we will explore the basics of React Router and learn how to implement routing and navigation in a React application.</p><h3>What is React Router?</h3><p>React Router is a JavaScript library that enables client-side routing in React applications. It allows developers to define multiple routes in an application and navigate between them using links.</p><p>React Router provides several benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Client-side routing</strong>: React Router enables client-side routing, which means that the application can navigate between different views or pages without requiring a full page reload.</li><li><strong>Declarative routing</strong>: React Router uses a declarative approach to routing, which means that developers define the routes and the library takes care of the navigation.</li><li><strong>Dynamic routing</strong>: React Router supports dynamic routing, which allows developers to generate routes dynamically based on user input or other factors.</li></ul><h3>Key Concepts in React Router</h3><p>There are several key concepts in React Router that developers need to understand:</p><ul><li><strong>Routes</strong>: A route is a mapping between a URL and a component. In React Router, routes are defined using the <code>Route</code> component.</li><li><strong>Links</strong>: A link is a way to navigate between routes. In React Router, links are created using the <code>Link</code> component.</li><li><strong>Router</strong>: The router is the component that manages the routing in an application. In React Router, the router is created using the <code>BrowserRouter</code> component.</li></ul><h3>Implementing Routing and Navigation</h3><p>To implement routing and navigation in a React application, developers need to follow these steps:</p><ol><li><strong>Install React Router</strong>: The first step is to install React Router using npm or yarn.</li><li><strong>Import React Router</strong>: The next step is to import React Router into the application.</li><li><strong>Define routes</strong>: The next step is to define the routes in the application using the <code>Route</code> component.</li><li><strong>Create links</strong>: The next step is to create links between the routes using the <code>Link</code> component.</li><li><strong>Render the router</strong>: The final step is to render the router using the <code>BrowserRouter</code> component.</li></ol><p>Here is an example of how to implement routing and navigation in a React application:</p><code>&lt;BrowserRouter&gt;\n  &lt;div&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Home&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;Link to=&quot;/about&quot;&gt;About&lt;/Link&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;Route path=&quot;/about&quot; component={About} /&gt;\n&lt;/BrowserRouter&gt;</code><p>In this example, we define a route for the <code>/about</code> page and create a link to it from the home page.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we learned about React Router and how to implement routing and navigation in a React application. We covered the key concepts in React Router, including routes, links, and routers, and learned how to define routes and create links between them.</p><p>With this knowledge, developers can create complex and dynamic routing systems in their React applications, providing a better user experience and improving the overall performance of the application.</p>', 'React Routing and Navigation', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(126, 139, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: React optimization best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=React+optimization+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/React+optimization+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/React+optimization+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/React+optimization+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=React+optimization+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>React Best Practices and Optimization</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 75 of our beginner course. Today we’ll dive deep into the principles that keep React applications fast, maintainable, and scalable. By the end of this lesson you’ll understand how to write clean, reusable components, how to avoid common performance pitfalls, and how to leverage React’s built‑in tools for optimization.</p>\n<h3>React Best Practices</h3>\n<p>Good code is more than just working. It’s readable, testable, and future‑proof. Below are the core practices every React developer should adopt.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Component Naming and Structure</strong> – Use PascalCase for component names and keep the file name consistent with the component. For example, <code>ProfileCard.jsx</code> should export <code>ProfileCard</code>. This convention makes it obvious that the file contains a component.</li>\n<li><strong>Single Responsibility</strong> – Each component should do one thing. If a component grows beyond a few dozen lines, split it into smaller, focused pieces. This improves reusability and testability.</li>\n<li><strong>Declarative Rendering</strong> – Prefer returning JSX directly over manipulating the DOM. For example, use <code>{items.map(item => <Item key={item.id} data={item} />)}</code> instead of imperative loops that push elements into an array.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid Inline Functions in JSX</strong> – Inline functions create a new reference on every render, which can trigger unnecessary re‑renders. Bind event handlers in the constructor or use the class fields syntax: <code>handleClick = () => { /* logic */ }</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Use PropTypes or TypeScript</strong> – Defining prop types catches bugs early. With TypeScript you get compile‑time safety, while PropTypes provide runtime checks in development.</li>\n<li><strong>Memoization with React.memo</strong> – Wrap pure functional components with <code>React.memo</code> to prevent re‑renders when props haven’t changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Effect Cleanup</strong> – Always return a cleanup function from <code>useEffect</code> when you set up subscriptions or timers. This prevents memory leaks.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid State in Unnecessary Components</strong> – Lift state up only when multiple components need it. For isolated UI, keep state local to the component.</li>\n<li><strong>Use Context Sparingly</strong> – Context is powerful but can cause re‑renders of all consumers when the value changes. Use it only for truly global data such as theme or authentication.</li>\n<li><strong>Consistent Styling</strong> – Adopt a styling strategy (CSS modules, styled‑components, or plain CSS). Keep styles scoped to components to avoid clashes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Optimization Techniques</h3>\n<p>Once your code follows best practices, you can focus on performance. React offers several built‑in strategies to make your app run faster.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Code Splitting with React.lazy and Suspense</strong> – Load components only when they’re needed. For example:\n<code>const Dashboard = React.lazy(() => import(\'./Dashboard\'));\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}> \n      <Dashboard />\n    </Suspense>\n  );\n}</code></li>\n<li><strong>Lazy Loading Images</strong> – Use the <code>loading=\"lazy\"</code> attribute or libraries like <code>react-lazyload</code> to defer off‑screen images.</li>\n<li><strong>Use of useCallback and useMemo</strong> – Memoize callbacks and computed values to avoid recreating them on every render. For example:\n<code>const memoizedCallback = useCallback(() => {\n  doSomething(a, b);\n}, [a, b]);</code></li>\n<li><strong>Windowing Large Lists</strong> – Libraries such as <code>react-window</code> render only the visible portion of a long list, drastically reducing DOM nodes.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid Anonymous Functions in Render</strong> – Instead of <code>onClick={() => doSomething() }</code>, bind the handler once or use <code>useCallback</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Batch State Updates</strong> – React batches state updates inside event handlers automatically, but when using async functions, wrap updates in <code>ReactDOM.unstable_batchedUpdates</code> if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Server‑Side Rendering (SSR)</strong> – Pre‑render pages on the server to improve initial load time and SEO. Frameworks like Next.js simplify SSR.</li>\n<li><strong>Static Site Generation (SSG)</strong> – For pages that don’t change often, pre‑build them at compile time. This gives the fastest possible load.</li>\n<li><strong>Use of Web Workers</strong> – Offload heavy computations to a web worker so the main thread stays responsive.</li>\n<li><strong>Tree Shaking</strong> – Import only the functions you need from libraries. For example, <code>import { debounce } from \'lodash-es\';</code> instead of importing the entire lodash.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Performance Optimization</h3>\n<p>Performance is not just about speed; it’s about perceived responsiveness. Below are actionable steps to make your React app feel snappy.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Measure with React DevTools Profiler</strong> – The Profiler tab shows how long each component takes to render. Look for components that take more than 16ms (one frame at 60fps).</li>\n<li><strong>Optimize Rendering Paths</strong> – Use <code>React.memo</code> for components that receive the same props frequently. Combine with <code>useMemo</code> for expensive calculations.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid Unnecessary Re‑renders</strong> – When passing objects or arrays as props, ensure they’re stable. Create them outside the component or memoize them.</li>\n<li><strong>Use shouldComponentUpdate in Class Components</strong> – For class components, override <code>shouldComponentUpdate</code> to prevent re‑renders when props and state haven’t changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimize CSS</strong> – Use critical CSS for above‑the‑fold content. Minify and combine stylesheets.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduce JavaScript Bundle Size</strong> – Split vendor code, remove dead code, and use dynamic imports for rarely used features.</li>\n<li><strong>Leverage Browser Caching</strong> – Set proper cache headers for static assets. Use hashed filenames so browsers can cache aggressively.</li>\n<li><strong>Use IntersectionObserver for Lazy Loading</strong> – Instead of scroll listeners, use <code>IntersectionObserver</code> to detect when an element enters the viewport.</li>\n<li><strong>Prefetch Data</strong> – Use <code>link rel=\"prefetch\"</code> or <code>React.lazy</code> with <code>Suspense</code> to fetch data before it’s needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Profile with Chrome DevTools</strong> – Inspect network, timeline, and memory to spot bottlenecks such as large payloads or memory leaks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By combining clean architecture, thoughtful component design, and targeted performance tweaks, you’ll create React applications that are not only functional but also fast and maintainable. Practice these techniques on a small project, profile it, and iterate until you see measurable improvements. Happy coding!</p>', 'React Best Practices and Optimization', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(127, 140, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: React testing screenshot</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=React+testing+screenshot\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/React+testing+screenshot\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/React+testing+screenshot/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/React+testing+screenshot/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=React+testing+screenshot\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 76: Introduction to React Testing</h3><p>Welcome to Day 76 of our beginner web development course! Today we’ll dive into the world of <strong>React Testing</strong>, the tools that help you ensure your components work as expected. We’ll cover the core concepts, the popular testing library <strong>Jest</strong>, and the component testing utility <strong>Enzyme</strong>. By the end of the day, you’ll understand why testing matters, how to write simple tests, and how to run them locally.</p><h3>Why Test React Components?</h3><p>React components are the building blocks of your UI. When you change a component or refactor a piece of code, you risk breaking existing behavior. Tests act as a safety net:</p><ul><li><strong>Regression protection</strong> – Catch bugs early when you modify code.</li><li><strong>Documentation</strong> – Tests describe how a component is expected to behave.</li><li><strong>Confidence</strong> – Deploy with peace of mind knowing your UI works.</li></ul><h3>Getting Started with Jest</h3><p>Jest is a zero‑configuration testing framework created by Facebook. It’s the default test runner for Create React App and works well with React.</p><p>Installation (if not already present):</p><code>npm install --save-dev jest @testing-library/react @testing-library/jest-dom</code><p>Basic Jest configuration is usually auto‑detected, but you can add a <code>jest.config.js</code> file:</p><code>module.exports = { testEnvironment: \'jsdom\' };</code><p>Running tests is as simple as:</p><code>npm test</code><p>Jest will look for files ending in <code>.test.js</code> or <code>.spec.js</code>.</p><h3>Enzyme – A React Testing Utility</h3><p>Enzyme provides a set of APIs to render components and inspect their output. It’s especially useful for shallow rendering, which renders only the component under test and not its children.</p><p>Installation:</p><code>npm install --save-dev enzyme enzyme-adapter-react-16</code><p>Configure Enzyme in a <code>setupTests.js</code> file:</p><code>import { configure } from \'enzyme\';\nimport Adapter from \'enzyme-adapter-react-16\';\nconfigure({ adapter: new Adapter() });</code><p>Now you can write tests that use <code>shallow</code>, <code>mount</code>, or <code>render</code>.</p><h3>Writing Your First Test</h3><p>Let’s test a simple <code>Button</code> component:</p><code>import React from \'react\';\nexport default function Button({ label, onClick }) {\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;\n}</code><p>Test file <code>Button.test.js</code>:</p><code>import React from \'react\';\nimport { shallow } from \'enzyme\';\nimport Button from \'./Button\';\n\ntest(\'renders the correct label\', () => {\n  const wrapper = shallow(<Button label=\"Click Me\" />);\n  expect(wrapper.text()).toBe(\'Click Me\');\n});\n\ntest(\'calls onClick when button is clicked\', () => {\n  const mockFn = jest.fn();\n  const wrapper = shallow(<Button label=\"Click Me\" onClick={mockFn} />);\n  wrapper.find(\'button\').simulate(\'click\');\n  expect(mockFn).toHaveBeenCalled();\n});</code><p>Run <code>npm test</code> and you should see both tests pass.</p><h3>Snapshot Testing</h3><p>Snapshots capture the rendered output of a component and compare it against a stored snapshot file. They’re great for detecting unexpected changes.</p><p>Example:</p><code>import React from \'react\';\nimport renderer from \'react-test-renderer\';\nimport Button from \'./Button\';\n\ntest(\'Button snapshot\', () => {\n  const tree = renderer\n    .create(<Button label=\"Snapshot\" />)\n    .toJSON();\n  expect(tree).toMatchSnapshot();\n});</code><p>On first run, Jest creates <code>Button.test.js.snap</code>. Subsequent runs compare the output to this snapshot.</p><h3>Simulating Events and Testing Async Behavior</h3><p>Enzyme’s <code>simulate</code> method lets you trigger events. For async actions (like API calls), you can use <code>async/await</code> with <code>act</code> from React DOM test utils.</p><code>import { act } from \'react-dom/test-utils\';\nimport { mount } from \'enzyme\';\nimport FetchComponent from \'./FetchComponent\';\n\ntest(\'fetches data on mount\', async () => {\n  const wrapper = mount(<FetchComponent />);\n  await act(async () => {\n    // Wait for promises to resolve\n  });\n  wrapper.update();\n  expect(wrapper.find(\'li\').length).toBeGreaterThan(0);\n});</code><h3>Best Practices for React Testing</h3><ul><li>Test the <strong>public API</strong> of components, not internal implementation.</li><li>Prefer <strong>React Testing Library</strong> over Enzyme for user‑centric tests (e.g., query by role, label).</li><li>Keep tests <strong>fast</strong> – shallow render for isolated tests, full mount only when needed.</li><li>Use <strong>mock functions</strong> to isolate components from external dependencies.</li><li>Run tests in <strong>watch mode</strong> during development to catch regressions early.</li></ul><h3>Resources to Continue Learning</h3><ul><li>Official Jest Docs: <a href=\"https://jestjs.io/docs/en/getting-started\" target=\"_blank\">jestjs.io</a></li><li>Enzyme Guide: <a href=\"https://enzymejs.github.io/enzyme/\" target=\"_blank\">enzymejs.github.io</a></li><li>React Testing Library: <a href=\"https://testing-library.com/docs/react-testing-library/intro\" target=\"_blank\">testing-library.com</a></li><li>Testing React with Jest and Enzyme Tutorial: <a href=\"https://www.taniarascia.com/testing-react-components-with-jest-and-enzyme/\" target=\"_blank\">taniarascia.com</a></li></ul><p>By the end of today, you should be comfortable setting up Jest, writing basic component tests with Enzyme, and understanding the value of snapshots and event simulation. In tomorrow’s session, we’ll explore more advanced testing patterns and integrate coverage reporting.</p>', 'Introduction to React Testing', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(128, 141, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: React advanced concepts illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=React+advanced+concepts+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/React+advanced+concepts+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/React+advanced+concepts+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/React+advanced+concepts+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=React+advanced+concepts+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Advanced React Concepts</h3>\n<p>In this session we dive deeper into React beyond the basics. We will explore how to optimize performance with <strong>React.memo</strong>, understand the role of callbacks in component communication, and learn advanced patterns such as render props and higher‑order components. By the end of the day you will be able to write cleaner, faster, and more maintainable React code.</p>\n<h3>1. Advanced React: Component Composition & State Management</h3>\n<p>React’s power lies in its composability. Instead of monolithic components, we break UI into small, reusable pieces. Advanced composition involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Render Props</strong>: A function prop that returns JSX, allowing dynamic rendering.</li>\n<li><strong>Higher‑Order Components (HOCs)</strong>: Functions that take a component and return a new component with added functionality.</li>\n<li><strong>Compound Components</strong>: Components that share state internally but expose a public API for consumers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of a render prop:</p>\n<code>{`function DataFetcher({url, render}) {\n  const [data, setData] = React.useState(null);\n  React.useEffect(() => {\n    fetch(url).then(r => r.json()).then(setData);\n  }, [url]);\n  return render(data);\n}`}</code>\n<p>Using it:</p>\n<code>{`<DataFetcher url=\"/api/items\" render={data => (\n  <ul>{data?.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>\n)} />`}</code>\n<p>HOCs are useful for cross‑cutting concerns such as authentication:</p>\n<code>{`function withAuth(WrappedComponent) {\n  return function AuthComponent(props) {\n    const { user } = React.useContext(AuthContext);\n    if (!user) return <Redirect to=\"/login\" />;\n    return <WrappedComponent {...props} />;\n  };\n}`}</code>\n<h3>2. React.memo: Preventing Unnecessary Renders</h3>\n<p>React renders components whenever their parent renders, even if the props have not changed. <strong>React.memo</strong> is a higher‑order component that memoizes the result of a functional component. It performs a shallow comparison of props and skips rendering if nothing changed.</p>\n<p>Basic usage:</p>\n<code>{`const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function List({items}) {\n  console.log(\'Rendering list\');\n  return <ul>{items.map(i => <li key={i.id}>{i.value}</li>)}</ul>;\n});`}</code>\n<p>When <code>items</code> reference stays the same, the component will not re‑render. If you need a custom comparison, pass a second argument:</p>\n<code>{`const MemoizedComponent = React.memo(Component, (prevProps, nextProps) => {\n  return prevProps.id === nextProps.id;\n});`}</code>\n<p>Common pitfalls:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Using inline functions or objects as props will create new references on every render, causing <code>React.memo</code> to re‑render.</li>\n<li>For deeply nested objects, consider <code>useMemo</code> or <code>useCallback</code> to keep references stable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. React Callbacks: Passing Functions Down the Tree</h3>\n<p>Callbacks are the primary way to lift state up or communicate from child to parent. They allow a child component to notify its parent about events or data changes.</p>\n<p>Typical pattern:</p>\n<code>{`function Parent() {\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\n  const handleIncrement = () => setCount(c => c + 1);\n  return <Child onIncrement={handleIncrement} />;\n}`}</code>\n<p>Child component:</p>\n<code>{`function Child({onIncrement}) {\n  return <button onClick={onIncrement}>Increment</button>;\n}`}</code>\n<p>When the button is clicked, <code>handleIncrement</code> runs in the parent’s context, updating state.</p>\n<p>Performance tip: Wrap callbacks with <code>useCallback</code> to prevent unnecessary re‑creation:</p>\n<code>{`const memoizedCallback = React.useCallback(() => {\n  // expensive logic\n}, [dependencies]);`}</code>\n<p>In combination with <code>React.memo</code>, this ensures that memoized children only re‑render when the actual data they care about changes.</p>\n<h3>4. useCallback vs useMemo vs React.memo</h3>\n<p>These hooks and HOC work together to optimize rendering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>useCallback</strong> returns a memoized function reference.</li>\n<li><strong>useMemo</strong> returns a memoized value.</li>\n<li><strong>React.memo</strong> memoizes a component’s output.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of combined usage:</p>\n<code>{`const Parent = () => {\n  const [value, setValue] = React.useState(0);\n  const increment = React.useCallback(() => setValue(v => v + 1), []);\n  const expensiveValue = React.useMemo(() => computeExpensive(value), [value]);\n  return <Child onIncrement={increment} value={expensiveValue} />;\n};`}</code>\n<h3>5. Common Advanced Patterns</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Context API</strong> for global state.</li>\n<li><strong>Suspense & Lazy Loading</strong> for code splitting.</li>\n<li><strong>Custom Hooks</strong> to encapsulate logic.</li>\n<li><strong>Portal</strong> for rendering outside the component hierarchy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each pattern builds on the core concepts of props, state, and lifecycle. Mastering them will enable you to tackle complex applications with confidence.</p>\n<h3>6. Summary & Takeaways</h3>\n<p>Today’s key points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <strong>React.memo</strong> to avoid re‑rendering unchanged components.</li>\n<li>Wrap callbacks with <code>useCallback</code> to keep references stable.</li>\n<li>Combine <code>React.memo</code>, <code>useCallback</code>, and <code>useMemo</code> for optimal performance.</li>\n<li>Leverage advanced composition patterns like render props, HOCs, and compound components to create reusable UI.</li>\n<li>Always test performance impact with React DevTools profiler.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Next steps: Implement a small project that uses these patterns—perhaps a todo list with filtering, memoized list items, and a callback to add new tasks. Measure performance before and after optimization.</p>', 'Advanced React Concepts', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(129, 142, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Responsive Design</h3>\n<p>Responsive design is the practice of creating web pages that automatically adjust their layout, typography, and images to provide an optimal viewing experience across a wide range of devices—from large desktop monitors to small smartphone screens. The goal is to deliver a single, flexible design that feels natural and usable, regardless of the device’s size, resolution, or orientation.</p>\n<p>At its core, responsive design relies on three key concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fluid grids</strong> – Elements are sized using relative units such as percentages or viewport units instead of fixed pixels.</li>\n<li><strong>Flexible images</strong> – Images scale proportionally with their containers, often using CSS rules like <code>max-width: 100%; height: auto;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Media queries</strong> – Conditional CSS that applies different styles when certain conditions (e.g., screen width) are met.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When combined, these techniques allow a website to reflow its content, rearrange elements, and adjust typography so that it remains readable and visually balanced on any device.</p>\n<h3>Media Queries</h3>\n<p>Media queries are the cornerstone of responsive design. They let you target specific device characteristics—such as width, height, orientation, and resolution—and apply CSS rules only when those conditions are true.</p>\n<p>Here’s a simple example that changes the background color of the body when the viewport width is 600 pixels or less:</p>\n<code>@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  body {\n    background: #f0f0f0;\n  }\n}</code>\n<p>Common media query breakpoints used in practice include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>320px – Small phones (portrait)</li>\n<li>480px – Small phones (landscape)</li>\n<li>768px – Tablets (portrait)</li>\n<li>1024px – Tablets (landscape) or small laptops</li>\n<li>1200px – Desktops and large laptops</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It’s best to design for content first and then decide where to place breakpoints based on the actual layout needs. This approach, known as <strong>mobile‑first</strong>, starts with the smallest viewport and progressively adds styles for larger screens using <code>min-width</code> media queries.</p>\n<p>Example of a mobile‑first media query:</p>\n<code>@media (min-width: 768px) {\n  .sidebar {\n    display: block;\n  }\n}</code>\n<h3>Flexbox</h3>\n<p>Flexbox (Flexible Box Layout) is a CSS module designed to simplify the creation of responsive layouts. It provides a powerful set of properties that let you control the alignment, spacing, and order of items within a container, without relying on floats or complex calculations.</p>\n<p>To enable Flexbox, set <code>display: flex;</code> on a container. The following example creates a horizontal navigation bar that automatically distributes space between items:</p>\n<code>.nav {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n}</code>\n<p>Key Flexbox properties include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>flex-direction</strong> – Sets the main axis direction (row, row‑reverse, column, column‑reverse).</li>\n<li><strong>justify-content</strong> – Aligns items along the main axis (flex-start, flex-end, center, space-between, space-around, space-evenly).</li>\n<li><strong>align-items</strong> – Aligns items along the cross axis (stretch, flex-start, flex-end, center, baseline).</li>\n<li><strong>flex-wrap</strong> – Allows items to wrap onto multiple lines when there isn’t enough space.</li>\n<li><strong>flex-grow / flex-shrink / flex-basis</strong> – Controls how items grow, shrink, and set their initial size.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Flexbox shines in responsive navigation, card grids, and any layout where items need to adapt to varying screen widths. For example, a three‑column card layout can be achieved with:</p>\n<code>.cards {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n  gap: 1rem;\n}\n.card {\n  flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 1rem);\n  min-width: 200px;\n}</code>\n<p>When the viewport becomes too narrow, the <code>flex-wrap: wrap;</code> rule forces cards to stack vertically, maintaining a clean and accessible design across all devices.</p>\n<p>In summary, responsive design, media queries, and Flexbox work hand‑in‑hand to create adaptable, efficient, and maintainable web interfaces. By mastering these concepts, you’ll be able to build sites that look great and perform well on every screen size.</p>', 'Introduction to Responsive Web Design', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(130, 143, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Responsive Images</h3>\n<p>Images are one of the biggest performance bottlenecks on the web. When you design for a wide range of devices, you need to serve the right image size for each viewport. The modern way to do this is with the <code>srcset</code> and <code>sizes</code> attributes, or the <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code> element for art direction.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>srcset</strong> – lists multiple image sources with descriptors (e.g., 300w, 600w).</li>\n<li><strong>sizes</strong> – tells the browser what width the image will be displayed at for different viewport widths.</li>\n<li><strong>picture</strong> – allows you to specify different images for different media queries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example using <code>srcset</code>:</p>\n<code>&lt;img src=\'small.jpg\' srcset=\'small.jpg 300w, medium.jpg 600w, large.jpg 1200w\' sizes=\'(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 50vw\' alt=\'Responsive example\'&gt;</code>\n<p>With this markup, the browser automatically chooses the best image based on the device pixel ratio and viewport size, reducing bandwidth usage.</p>\n<p>For art direction, use <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code>:</p>\n<code>&lt;picture&gt;\n  &lt;source media=\'(min-width: 800px)\' srcset=\'desktop.jpg\'&gt;\n  &lt;source media=\'(min-width: 400px)\' srcset=\'tablet.jpg\'&gt;\n  &lt;img src=\'mobile.jpg\' alt=\'Responsive art direction\'&gt;\n&lt;/picture&gt;</code>\n<p>Remember to add <code>alt</code> text for accessibility. Also, set <code>max-width: 100%; height: auto;</code> in CSS to ensure images never overflow their containers.</p>\n<h3>Responsive Tables</h3>\n<p>Tables can break layout on small screens because they have a fixed width and many columns. The trick is to make them scrollable or reformat them into a vertical stack.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Horizontal scrolling</strong> – wrap the table in a container with <code>overflow-x: auto</code> and <code>display: block</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stacked layout</strong> – hide the header and display each cell as a block with a label.</li>\n<li><strong>CSS Grid</strong> – convert the table into a grid for maximum flexibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example for horizontal scrolling:</p>\n<code>&lt;div style=\'overflow-x:auto;\'&gt;\n  &lt;table&gt;\n    &lt;thead&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Age&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;City&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;/thead&gt;\n    &lt;tbody&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Alice&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;30&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;NYC&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Bob&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;25&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;LA&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;/tbody&gt;\n  &lt;/table&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code>\n<p>Example for stacked layout (mobile-first CSS):</p>\n<code>&lt;style&gt;\n  @media (max-width: 600px) {\n    table, thead, tbody, th, td, tr { display: block; }\n    th { position: absolute; top: -9999px; left: -9999px; }\n    td { position: relative; padding-left: 50%; }\n    td::before {\n      content: attr(data-label);\n      position: absolute;\n      left: 0;\n      width: 45%;\n      padding-left: 10px;\n      font-weight: bold;\n    }\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Age&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;City&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td data-label=\'Name\'&gt;Alice&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td data-label=\'Age\'&gt;30&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td data-label=\'City\'&gt;NYC&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td data-label=\'Name\'&gt;Bob&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td data-label=\'Age\'&gt;25&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td data-label=\'City\'&gt;LA&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;</code>\n<p>These techniques keep your data readable on all devices without sacrificing the table’s semantic structure.</p>\n<h3>Responsive Navigation</h3>\n<p>Navigation menus need to adapt from a horizontal bar on desktops to a collapsible hamburger menu on mobile. A common pattern uses CSS media queries to hide the full menu and show a button that toggles a vertical list.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HTML structure</strong> – <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> for links.</li>\n<li><strong>CSS media queries</strong> – <code>@media (max-width: 768px)</code> to switch layout.</li>\n<li><strong>JavaScript toggle</strong> – add an event listener to the button to add/remove <code>open</code> class.</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility</strong> – use <code>aria-controls</code>, <code>aria-expanded</code>, and <code>role=\'button\'</code> for screen readers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example markup:</p>\n<code>&lt;nav&gt;\n  &lt;button id=\'navToggle\' aria-controls=\'primaryNav\' aria-expanded=\'false\'&gt;☰&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;ul id=\'primaryNav\' class=\'nav-list\'&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\'#home\'&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\'#about\'&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\'#contact\'&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;</code>\n<p>CSS:</p>\n<code>&lt;style&gt;\n  .nav-list { list-style: none; display: flex; gap: 1rem; }\n  @media (max-width: 768px) {\n    .nav-list { flex-direction: column; display: none; }\n    .nav-list.open { display: flex; }\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code>\n<p>JavaScript toggle:</p>\n<code>&lt;script&gt;\n  const toggle = document.getElementById(\'navToggle\');\n  const nav = document.getElementById(\'primaryNav\');\n  toggle.addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\n    const expanded = toggle.getAttribute(\'aria-expanded\') === \'true\';\n    toggle.setAttribute(\'aria-expanded\', !expanded);\n    nav.classList.toggle(\'open\');\n  });\n&lt;/script&gt;</code>\n<p>With this setup, the navigation collapses into a button on small screens, expands when clicked, and remains fully accessible. You can further enhance the experience with CSS transitions, icon fonts, or a library like <strong>Bootstrap</strong> or <strong>Tailwind CSS</strong> for ready-made components.</p>\n<p>By mastering responsive images, tables, and navigation, you ensure that your website delivers a consistent, performant, and accessible experience across all devices.</p>', 'Implementing Responsive Design', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(131, 144, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS Grid animation example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+Grid+animation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+Grid+animation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+Grid+animation+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+Grid+animation+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+Grid+animation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Grid: Building Complex Layouts with Ease</h3><p>CSS Grid has revolutionized how we structure web pages. Unlike Flexbox, which is one-dimensional, Grid allows you to create two-dimensional layouts that adapt to any screen size. In this section, we’ll dive into advanced Grid concepts such as named grid lines, implicit tracks, and the <strong>subgrid</strong> feature.</p><ul><li><strong>Named Grid Lines</strong>: By naming rows and columns, you can reference them in your layout. For example, <code>grid-template-columns: [header-start] 1fr [content-start] 3fr [content-end] 1fr [header-end];</code> lets you align items precisely.</li><li><strong>Implicit Tracks</strong>: When you place an item outside the defined grid, CSS automatically creates new rows or columns. You can control this behavior with <code>grid-auto-rows</code> and <code>grid-auto-columns</code>.</li><li><strong>Subgrid</strong>: A powerful feature that allows child elements to inherit the grid structure of their parent. This is especially useful for nested components where you want consistent alignment.</li></ul><p>Here’s a practical example: a dashboard with a sidebar, header, main content, and footer. By combining <code>grid-template-areas</code> and named lines, you can achieve a clean, responsive design without media queries.</p><h3>CSS Animations: Adding Life to Your UI</h3><p>Animations let you transform static elements into interactive experiences. While transitions handle simple state changes, animations provide full control over keyframes, timing, and sequencing. Let’s explore advanced animation techniques.</p><ul><li><strong>Keyframes</strong>: Define multiple stages of an animation. Use percentages to create complex motion paths. Example: <code>@keyframes spin { 0% { transform: rotate(0deg); } 50% { transform: rotate(180deg); } 100% { transform: rotate(360deg); } }</code></li><li><strong>Animation Delay & Iteration</strong>: Chain multiple animations by staggering delays or setting <code>animation-iteration-count</code> to <code>infinite</code> for endless loops.</li><li><strong>Animation Direction</strong>: The <code>animation-direction</code> property can reverse the animation or alternate between forward and reverse, creating a bounce effect.</li><li><strong>Animation Timing Functions</strong>: Beyond the standard <code>ease</code> and <code>linear</code>, you can craft custom cubic-bezier curves or use <code>steps()</code> for frame-based animations.</li></ul><p>Example: a loading spinner that spins while a page fetches data. By combining <code>animation</code> with <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code>, you can create a smooth, engaging indicator.</p><h3>CSS Transitions: Smooth State Changes</h3><p>Transitions are the lightweight cousin of animations, ideal for hover effects, focus states, and toggling classes. Advanced transition techniques involve:</p><ul><li><strong>Transition Delays</strong>: Create a staggered effect when multiple elements appear. For instance, <code>transition-delay: 0.1s;</code> on the second element.</li><li><strong>Transition Properties</strong>: Instead of <code>transition: all 0.3s;</code>, specify only the properties that change, such as <code>transition: transform 0.3s ease-out, opacity 0.3s ease-out;</code>.</li><li><strong>Using <code>will-change</code></strong>: Hint the browser about upcoming changes to improve performance. Example: <code>will-change: transform;</code>.</li><li><strong>Transitioning Non-Animatable Properties</strong>: While you can’t animate <code>display</code>, you can animate <code>max-height</code> or <code>opacity</code> to mimic show/hide behavior.</li></ul><p>Practical use: a dropdown menu that slides down on hover. By setting <code>max-height: 0;</code> and transitioning to <code>max-height: 200px;</code>, you achieve a smooth reveal without JavaScript.</p><p>Combining Grid, Animations, and Transitions allows you to build modern, responsive interfaces that feel alive. Experiment with these techniques, tweak timing functions, and watch your designs transform from static to dynamic.</p>', 'Advanced CSS Techniques', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(132, 145, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript frameworks comparison</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+frameworks+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+frameworks+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+frameworks+comparison/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+frameworks+comparison/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+frameworks+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript Frameworks</h3>\n<p>JavaScript frameworks are the backbone of modern web development. They provide a structured way to build complex, interactive user interfaces, manage state, and communicate with servers. In this lesson, we’ll explore three of the most popular frameworks: <strong>React</strong>, <strong>Angular</strong>, and <strong>Vue.js</strong>. By the end, you’ll understand the core concepts of each, know when to choose one over the others, and have a basic project setup to start experimenting.</p>\n<h3>React – A Component‑Based Library</h3>\n<p>React, developed by Facebook, is often called a library, but it’s used as a full framework when combined with tools like React Router and Redux. Its core idea is that the UI is made up of reusable <strong>components</strong> that manage their own state and render based on props.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>JSX Syntax</strong>: JSX lets you write HTML‑like code inside JavaScript. For example: <code>const Button = props => &lt;button&gt;{props.label}&lt;/button&gt;;</code></li>\n<li><strong>Virtual DOM</strong>: React keeps a lightweight representation of the DOM and calculates the minimal changes needed, which speeds up rendering.</li>\n<li><strong>State &amp; Props</strong>: Components can hold internal state with <code>useState</code> or class <code>this.state</code>, and receive data from parents via props.</li>\n<li><strong>Hooks</strong>: Introduced in React 16.8, hooks like <code>useEffect</code> and <code>useContext</code> allow functional components to handle side effects and global state.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Getting Started:</p>\n<code>npx create-react-app my-app\ncd my-app\nnpm start</code>\n<p>This command scaffolds a new React project with Webpack, Babel, and a dev server.</p>\n<h3>Angular – A Full‑Featured MVC Framework</h3>\n<p>Angular, maintained by Google, is a complete framework that follows the Model‑View‑Controller pattern. It uses TypeScript, which adds static typing to JavaScript, making large codebases easier to manage.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Modules &amp; Components</strong>: Everything is organized into <code>@NgModule</code> and <code>@Component</code> decorators.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependency Injection</strong>: Angular’s DI system automatically provides services to components, promoting modularity.</li>\n<li><strong>Two‑Way Data Binding</strong>: The <code>[(ngModel)]</code> syntax keeps the view and model in sync.</li>\n<li><strong>RxJS Integration</strong>: Angular heavily uses reactive programming for handling asynchronous data streams.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Getting Started:</p>\n<code>npm install -g @angular/cli\nng new my-app\ncd my-app\nng serve</code>\n<p>Angular’s CLI generates a full project structure, including components, services, and routing out of the box.</p>\n<h3>Vue.js – The Progressive Framework</h3>\n<p>Vue.js, created by Evan You, is known for its gentle learning curve and flexibility. It can be added incrementally to existing projects or used as a full framework with Vue CLI.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reactivity System</strong>: Vue tracks dependencies and updates the DOM automatically when data changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Single‑File Components</strong>: Vue files (.vue) encapsulate template, script, and style in one file.</li>\n<li><strong>Directives</strong>: Shorthand syntax like <code>v-bind</code> and <code>v-on</code> make DOM manipulation declarative.</li>\n<li><strong>Ecosystem</strong>: Vue Router, Vuex, and Nuxt.js provide routing, state management, and server‑side rendering.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Getting Started:</p>\n<code>npm install -g @vue/cli\nvue create my-app\ncd my-app\nnpm run serve</code>\n<p>Vue CLI sets up a project with hot module replacement and a dev server.</p>\n<h3>Comparing the Three</h3>\n<p>While all three frameworks aim to simplify UI development, they differ in philosophy and tooling:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Learning Curve</strong>: Vue is often the easiest for beginners, followed by React, then Angular.</li>\n<li><strong>Community &amp; Ecosystem</strong>: React has the largest ecosystem, Angular is strong in enterprise, and Vue has a rapidly growing community.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance</strong>: All three are highly performant, but React’s Virtual DOM and Angular’s Ahead‑Of‑Time compilation give subtle advantages in certain scenarios.</li>\n<li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: React offers the most flexibility, Angular provides a batteries‑included approach, and Vue sits in the middle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choosing the Right Framework</p>\n<p>Consider your project size, team expertise, and long‑term maintenance. For small to medium projects, Vue or React are great choices. For large enterprise applications that require strict structure and type safety, Angular is often preferred.</p>\n<p>Next Steps</p>\n<p>Try building a simple “Todo” app in each framework to see how component structure, state management, and routing differ. Experiment with the official documentation and community tutorials to deepen your understanding.</p>\n<p>Happy coding!</p>', 'Introduction to JavaScript Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(133, 146, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: react component architecture diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=react+component+architecture+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/react+component+architecture+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/react+component+architecture+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/react+component+architecture+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=react+component+architecture+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>React Components</h3>\n<p>Components are the building blocks of any React application. Think of them as reusable, self‑contained pieces of UI that can be composed together to create complex interfaces. A component can be a simple button, a form, or an entire page. In React, components are written as JavaScript functions or ES6 classes that return JSX – a syntax extension that looks like HTML but is actually JavaScript.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Functional Components</strong> – The modern, preferred way to write components. They are plain JavaScript functions that accept a single <code>props</code> argument and return JSX.</li>\n<li><strong>Class Components</strong> – Older style that uses ES6 classes extending <code>React.Component</code>. They provide lifecycle methods and internal state via <code>this.state</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stateless vs Stateful</strong> – Stateless components receive data via props and render UI. Stateful components manage their own data and can change over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here’s a quick example of a functional component that displays a greeting:</p>\n<code>function Greeting(props) {\n  return &lt;h1&gt;Hello, {props.name}!&lt;/h1&gt;;\n}\n</code>\n<p>Notice how we use curly braces to embed JavaScript expressions inside JSX. When you use this component elsewhere, you pass the <code>name</code> prop like so:</p>\n<code>&lt;Greeting name=\"Alice\" /&gt;\n</code>\n<p>React will render <code>&lt;h1&gt;Hello, Alice!&lt;/h1&gt;</code> in the DOM.</p>\n<h3>React State</h3>\n<p>State represents data that can change over time and influences what the UI looks like. In functional components, state is managed with the <code>useState</code> hook. In class components, state is stored in <code>this.state</code> and updated with <code>this.setState</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Initializing State</strong> – You provide an initial value to <code>useState</code> or set it in the constructor.</li>\n<li><strong>Updating State</strong> – Calling the updater function returned by <code>useState</code> triggers a re‑render with the new value.</li>\n<li><strong>State Shape</strong> – State can be a primitive value, an object, an array, or even a complex tree. Use immutability principles to avoid accidental mutations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of a counter component using <code>useState</code>:</p>\n<code>import { useState } from \'react\';\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  const increment = () => setCount(count + 1);\n  const decrement = () => setCount(count - 1);\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Count: {count}&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={increment}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={decrement}&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n</code>\n<p>When the user clicks the buttons, <code>setCount</code> updates the <code>count</code> state, causing React to re‑render the component with the new value.</p>\n<h3>React Props</h3>\n<p>Props (short for properties) are read‑only data passed from a parent component to a child component. They allow you to customize a component’s behavior and appearance without modifying its internal implementation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Passing Props</strong> – Use JSX attributes to pass values: <code>&lt;Child title=\"Welcome\" /&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Default Props</strong> – Provide fallback values with <code>Component.defaultProps</code> (class) or default parameter values in functional components.</li>\n<li><strong>Prop Types</strong> – Use the <code>prop-types</code> library to enforce type checking during development.</li>\n<li><strong>Children Prop</strong> – The special <code>children</code> prop lets you nest JSX inside a component: <code>&lt;Wrapper&gt;Hello&lt;/Wrapper&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below is a simple component that receives a <code>title</code> prop and displays it inside a styled card:</p>\n<code>function Card({ title, children }) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=\"card\"&gt;\n      &lt;h3&gt;{title}&lt;/h3&gt;\n      &lt;div className=\"content\"&gt;{children}&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\n// Usage\n&lt;Card title=\"Profile\"&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;This is the user profile card.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/Card&gt;\n</code>\n<p>Notice how the <code>Card</code> component is completely generic – it only cares about the <code>title</code> and whatever is passed as <code>children</code>. This pattern encourages composition over inheritance and makes your UI highly modular.</p>\n<p>Combining components, state, and props allows you to build rich, interactive web applications. In the next days, you’ll learn how to manage global state, handle side effects, and integrate APIs, but today you’ve laid the foundation for building reusable UI pieces with React.</p>', 'Building a Web Application with React', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(134, 147, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development best practices illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+best+practices+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+best+practices+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+best+practices+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+best+practices+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+best+practices+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Code Quality</h3>\n<p>Code quality is the foundation of every maintainable web application. For beginners, it’s essential to understand that clean code is not just about aesthetics; it’s about readability, reusability, and reducing bugs. Good code quality leads to faster onboarding of new developers, easier debugging, and a smoother deployment pipeline.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Consistent Naming Conventions</strong>: Use descriptive names for variables, functions, and components. For example, <code>const userName = \'Alice\';</code> is clearer than <code>const u = \'Alice\';</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Single Responsibility Principle</strong>: A function or component should do one thing and do it well. If a function grows beyond a single concern, split it into smaller helpers.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid Deep Nesting</strong>: Deeply nested structures make code hard to read. Refactor by extracting sub‑components or using early returns.</li>\n<li><strong>Comment Wisely</strong>: Comments should explain *why* something is done, not *what* is done. The code itself should be self‑explanatory.</li>\n<li><strong>Use Linting Tools</strong>: Tools like <code>ESLint</code> or <code>Stylelint</code> enforce style rules automatically. Configure a basic rule set in <code>.eslintrc.json</code> and run it before committing.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep Functions Small</strong>: Aim for functions that are 20 lines or less. Smaller functions are easier to test and reason about.</li>\n<li><strong>Prefer Declarative Code</strong>: Use array methods like <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, and <code>reduce</code> instead of loops when possible. Declarative code reads like a description of intent.</li>\n<li><strong>Handle Errors Gracefully</strong>: Use <code>try/catch</code> blocks for asynchronous operations and provide meaningful error messages.</li>\n<li><strong>Version Control Discipline</strong>: Commit small, logical changes. Include clear commit messages that describe the intent.</li>\n<li><strong>Document Public APIs</strong>: If you expose functions or components, add JSDoc comments so others can understand expected parameters and return types.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Code Review</h3>\n<p>Code reviews are a collaborative practice that improves quality, spreads knowledge, and catches bugs early. For beginners, learning how to give and receive constructive feedback is invaluable.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Set a Review Checklist</strong>: Create a simple list of items to check—naming, formatting, logic, edge cases, and test coverage. Share this checklist with your team.</li>\n<li><strong>Use Pull Requests (PRs)</strong>: Every change should go through a PR. This allows automated checks to run and gives reviewers context.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep PRs Small</strong>: Aim for <strong>200 lines or less</strong> per PR. Small PRs are easier to review quickly and reduce merge conflicts.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask Questions, Not Orders</strong>: Frame feedback as questions. For example, instead of “Change this name,” say “Could we rename this variable for clarity?”</li>\n<li><strong>Focus on Intent, Not Style</strong>: While style matters, prioritize understanding the developer’s intent. If a change is confusing, ask for clarification before suggesting fixes.</li>\n<li><strong>Use Inline Comments</strong>: When pointing out an issue, add a comment directly next to the problematic line. This keeps context close to the code.</li>\n<li><strong>Encourage Pair Programming</strong>: Pair sessions can reduce the number of reviews needed and help junior developers learn best practices on the fly.</li>\n<li><strong>Automate Where Possible</strong>: Configure CI pipelines to run linters, tests, and static analysis. Reviewers can focus on logic and design rather than style errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Celebrate Good Work</strong>: Positive reinforcement boosts morale. Highlight well‑written code or clever solutions during reviews.</li>\n<li><strong>Learn from Feedback</strong>: Treat every review as a learning opportunity. Keep a personal log of common feedback points and work on them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Testing</h3>\n<p>Testing is the safety net that ensures your web application behaves as expected. Beginners often think testing is optional, but a solid test suite saves time and frustration later.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unit Tests</strong>: Test individual functions or components in isolation. Use frameworks like <code>Jest</code> or <code>Mocha</code>. Example:\n<code>test(\'adds two numbers\', () => {\n  const result = add(2, 3);\n  expect(result).toBe(5);\n});</code></li>\n<li><strong>Integration Tests</strong>: Verify that multiple parts of your application work together. For React, <code>React Testing Library</code> can render components and simulate user interactions.</li>\n<li><strong>End‑to‑End (E2E) Tests</strong>: Tools like <code>Cypress</code> or <code>Playwright</code> automate a browser to test real user flows. They catch issues that unit or integration tests may miss.</li>\n<li><strong>Test‑Driven Development (TDD)</strong>: Write a failing test first, then implement the minimal code to pass it. This encourages clear specifications and reduces over‑engineering.</li>\n<li><strong>Mock External Services</strong>: When testing API calls, use mocks or stubs to simulate responses. This keeps tests fast and deterministic.</li>\n<li><strong>Coverage Reports</strong>: Generate coverage reports to see which lines are exercised. Aim for at least 80% coverage, but remember that quality matters more than quantity.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous Integration</strong>: Run your test suite on every commit. A failing build should block merges until the issue is resolved.</li>\n<li><strong>Testing Edge Cases</strong>: Think about unusual inputs—empty strings, null values, large numbers—and write tests for them.</li>\n<li><strong>Document Test Cases</strong>: Add comments to explain why a test exists, especially for complex logic. Future developers will appreciate the context.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep Tests Maintainable</strong>: Refactor test code just as you refactor production code. Shared utilities and setup functions reduce duplication.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By mastering code quality, code review, and testing, you’ll build a solid foundation for scalable, reliable web applications. These practices not only improve the product but also accelerate your growth as a developer.</p>', 'Web Development Best Practices', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(135, 148, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development final project deployment</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+final+project+deployment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+final+project+deployment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+final+project+deployment/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+final+project+deployment/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+final+project+deployment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Project Review</h3>\n<p>At the end of this course, you have built a complete web application that incorporates HTML, CSS, JavaScript, a backend API, and database interactions. The final project review is your opportunity to reflect on the architecture, code quality, and user experience of your application. Start by creating a <strong>README</strong> that explains the purpose of the project, the main features, and the technologies used. This document should also include a brief <code>setup</code> guide so that anyone can clone the repository and run the app locally.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Feature Checklist:</strong> List all the core functionalities (e.g., user authentication, CRUD operations, real‑time updates). Verify that each item is fully implemented and passes manual tests.</li>\n<li><strong>Code Quality:</strong> Run <code>npm run lint</code> (or the equivalent for your stack) to ensure consistent formatting. Look for duplicated logic and consider extracting reusable components or helper functions.</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> Use semantic HTML tags (<code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code>) and ARIA attributes where necessary. Run an accessibility audit with Lighthouse to catch any violations.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance:</strong> Measure page load times with Lighthouse or WebPageTest. Identify large assets, unused CSS, or blocking JavaScript and optimize accordingly (e.g., image compression, code splitting).</li>\n<li><strong>Testing:</strong> Add unit tests for critical components and API endpoints. If you used a testing framework like Jest or Mocha, run <code>npm test</code> and ensure coverage is above 70%.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Deployment Options</h3>\n<p>Deploying your application means making it available to users over the internet. There are several deployment models you can choose from, each with its own trade‑offs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Static Site Hosting:</strong> Ideal for front‑end only projects or static sites generated by frameworks like Gatsby or Hugo. Services such as <strong>GitHub Pages</strong>, <strong>Netlify</strong>, and <strong>Vercel</strong> provide free tiers that automatically build and deploy from your Git repository.</li>\n<li><strong>Platform as a Service (PaaS):</strong> Platforms like <strong>Heroku</strong>, <strong>Render</strong>, and <strong>Azure App Service</strong> let you push code and let the provider handle the server, scaling, and environment variables. They support multiple languages and frameworks out of the box.</li>\n<li><strong>Containerization:</strong> Docker containers encapsulate your application and its dependencies. Deploy containers to services such as <strong>Amazon ECS</strong>, <strong>Google Cloud Run</strong>, or <strong>DigitalOcean App Platform</strong>. This approach gives you greater control over the runtime environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Serverless Functions:</strong> If your backend is lightweight, consider using <strong>AWS Lambda</strong>, <strong>Azure Functions</strong>, or <strong>Netlify Functions</strong>. These run code in response to HTTP requests without managing servers.</li>\n<li><strong>Traditional VPS:</strong> For full control, you can deploy to a virtual private server (e.g., <strong>Linode</strong>, <strong>DigitalOcean Droplet</strong>) and manually configure Nginx, Node, or your chosen stack.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To choose the right option, ask yourself:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do I need a database? If so, does the platform support managed databases?</li>\n<li>What is my budget? Free tiers are great for prototypes but may have limits.</li>\n<li>How much traffic do I anticipate? PaaS and serverless automatically scale, while VPS requires manual scaling.</li>\n<li>Do I want zero‑maintenance hosting? Static site hosts and serverless functions handle most maintenance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once you decide, follow the platform’s documentation to set up continuous deployment. For example, to deploy a React app on Netlify, push your code to GitHub, connect the repo in Netlify’s dashboard, and set the build command to <code>npm run build</code> with the publish directory <code>build</code>. Netlify will automatically run the build and host the site on a CDN.</p>\n<h3>Security Considerations</h3>\n<p>Deploying to production introduces new security challenges. Protecting your users and data should be a top priority.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HTTPS Everywhere:</strong> Use TLS certificates to encrypt traffic. Services like <strong>Let’s Encrypt</strong> provide free certificates. Configure your web server (Nginx, Apache) or platform to redirect all HTTP traffic to HTTPS.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure Headers:</strong> Add HTTP headers such as <code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code>, <code>Content-Security-Policy</code>, <code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code>, and <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code> to mitigate clickjacking, XSS, MIME sniffing, and enforce HTTPS.</li>\n<li><strong>Authentication & Authorization:</strong> Store passwords hashed with a strong algorithm (bcrypt, Argon2). Use JSON Web Tokens (JWT) or sessions with secure, HttpOnly cookies. Enforce role‑based access control for sensitive endpoints.</li>\n<li><strong>Input Validation:</strong> Never trust client data. Validate and sanitize all inputs on the server side. Use libraries like <code>express-validator</code> or <code>joi</code> to enforce schemas.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependency Management:</strong> Run <code>npm audit</code> or <code>yarn audit</code> regularly to detect known vulnerabilities. Keep dependencies up to date and remove unused packages.</li>\n<li><strong>Rate Limiting & Brute‑Force Protection:</strong> Implement rate limiting on login and API routes. Services like <strong>Cloudflare</strong> or <strong>AWS WAF</strong> can provide additional protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Environment Variables:</strong> Store secrets (API keys, DB credentials) in environment variables, not in source code. Use a secrets manager if available (e.g., <strong>AWS Secrets Manager</strong>, <strong>Azure Key Vault</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring & Logging:</strong> Set up application logging (e.g., Winston, Morgan) and monitor logs for suspicious activity. Use alerting tools to notify you of errors or potential breaches.</li>\n<li><strong>Backup Strategy:</strong> Schedule regular database backups and store them in a separate location. Test restoration procedures to ensure data integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance & Privacy:</strong> If you handle personal data, review regulations such as GDPR or CCPA. Provide privacy notices and obtain user consent where required.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By systematically reviewing your project, selecting an appropriate deployment strategy, and implementing robust security practices, you will deliver a professional, reliable, and safe web application that can scale from a portfolio showcase to a production‑grade product.</p>', 'Final Project Review and Deployment', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(136, 149, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: website performance optimization illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=website+performance+optimization+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/website+performance+optimization+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/website+performance+optimization+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/website+performance+optimization+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=website+performance+optimization+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>Website performance is a critical factor that determines how quickly users can interact with your web pages. Even a beautifully designed site can feel sluggish if the browser has to download, parse, and render a large amount of data. In today’s lesson we will explore three core techniques that dramatically improve load times: <strong>minification</strong>, <strong>Gzip compression</strong>, and <strong>browser caching</strong>. By the end of the day you will understand why each technique matters, how to implement it, and which tools are available to automate the process.</p>\n\n<h3>Minification</h3>\n<p>Minification is the process of removing all unnecessary characters from source code—such as whitespace, comments, and line breaks—without affecting its functionality. The result is a smaller file size, which translates to faster downloads.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Why Minify?</strong> A typical JavaScript file can be 10 KB after minification, but 30 KB before. The same principle applies to CSS and HTML.</li>\n<li><strong>Common Tools</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>JavaScript: <code>uglify-js</code>, <code>terser</code>, <code>google-closure-compiler</code></li>\n<li>CSS: <code>clean-css</code>, <code>cssnano</code></li>\n<li>HTML: <code>html-minifier</code></li>\n<li>Build Systems: <code>gulp</code>, <code>webpack</code>, <code>parcel</code>, <code>rollup</code></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Example</strong> – Using <code>uglify-js</code> from the command line:\n<code>uglifyjs src/app.js -o dist/app.min.js -c -m</code>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Automating Minification</strong> – In <code>webpack</code>, you can add the <code>TerserPlugin</code> to the <code>optimization</code> section so that every build produces a minified bundle.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Gzip Compression</h3>\n<p>Even after minification, the files your server sends over the network can still be large. Gzip compression reduces the size of files on the fly before they leave the server. The browser automatically decompresses the data, so the end user experiences a faster load.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>How It Works</strong> – The server sets the <code>Content-Encoding: gzip</code> header. The browser requests the resource, and the server streams a compressed version.</li>\n<li><strong>Server Configuration</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Apache: Add <code>AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/css text/javascript application/javascript application/json</code> to <code>.htaccess</code> or <code>httpd.conf</code>.</li>\n<li>Nginx: Include <code>gzip on;</code> and <code>gzip_types text/html text/css application/javascript application/json;</code> in <code>nginx.conf</code>.</li>\n<li>Node.js (Express): Use the <code>compression</code> middleware:\n<code>const compression = require(\'compression\');\napp.use(compression());</code></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Compression Ratio</strong> – A typical 30 KB CSS file can shrink to 8 KB with Gzip, a 15 % reduction in download time on a 3 Mbps connection.</li>\n<li><strong>Testing</strong> – Tools like <code>curl -I -H \"Accept-Encoding: gzip\" https://example.com/style.css</code> will show the <code>Content-Encoding</code> header. Browser dev tools (Network tab) also display the compressed size.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Browser Caching</h3>\n<p>Browser caching tells the client how long it can store a resource locally before it must check for updates. Proper caching can eliminate repeated downloads for static assets, drastically reducing page load times on subsequent visits.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cache-Control Header</strong> – This header instructs the browser how to cache a resource. Example:\n<code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000</code> (one year).</li>\n<li><strong>ETag and Last-Modified</strong> – These headers allow the browser to validate cached content. If the resource hasn’t changed, the server sends a <code>304 Not Modified</code> response, saving bandwidth.</li>\n<li><strong>Versioning Strategy</strong> – Append a hash or version number to filenames (e.g., <code>app.4a3f1.js</code>). When the content changes, the hash changes, forcing the browser to fetch the new file.</li>\n<li><strong>Server Configuration</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Apache: <code><FilesMatch \"\\.(js|css|png|jpg|jpeg|gif|svg)$\">Header set Cache-Control \"max-age=31536000, public\"</code></li>\n<li>Nginx: <code>location ~* \\.js$ { expires 365d; }</code></li>\n<li>Netlify / Vercel: Add <code>Cache-Control</code> headers in <code>netlify.toml</code> or <code>vercel.json</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Testing Caching</strong> – Use the browser’s Network tab to inspect the <code>Age</code> and <code>Cache-Control</code> headers. Tools like <code>webpagetest.org</code> can also report cache hit ratios.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Putting It All Together</h3>\n<p>In a real-world workflow, you would combine these techniques in a build pipeline:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write clean, modular code.</li>\n<li>Use a bundler (e.g., <code>webpack</code>) to bundle JavaScript and CSS.</li>\n<li>Apply minification plugins during the build.</li>\n<li>Generate hashed filenames for cache busting.</li>\n<li>Serve the output through a web server configured for Gzip and appropriate cache headers.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>By automating these steps, you ensure that every deployment is optimized for speed, giving users a seamless experience regardless of their device or network.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Takeaways</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Minification reduces file size by stripping unnecessary characters.</li>\n<li>Gzip compression further shrinks the data transmitted over the network.</li>\n<li>Browser caching keeps static assets locally, eliminating repeated downloads.</li>\n<li>Combining all three yields the greatest performance gains.</li>\n<li>Use build tools and server configuration to automate the process.</li>\n</ul>\n', 'Optimizing Website Performance', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24');
