(137, 150, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web security concepts illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+security+concepts+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+security+concepts+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+security+concepts+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+security+concepts+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+security+concepts+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Security</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 86 of our web development journey! Today we shift our focus from building functional websites to protecting them. Web applications are powerful, but they also expose sensitive data and users to a range of attacks. In this lesson, we’ll explore three of the most common vulnerabilities: <strong>SQL Injection</strong>, <strong>Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS)</strong>, and <strong>Cross‑Site Request Forgery (CSRF)</strong>. By the end, you’ll understand how these attacks work, why they’re dangerous, and how to defend against them.</p>\n<h3>1. SQL Injection</h3>\n<p>SQL Injection occurs when an attacker manipulates user input to alter the structure of an SQL query. If your application directly inserts user data into a query string, the attacker can inject malicious SQL code that the database will execute.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Typical scenario:</strong> A login form that builds a query like:\n<code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = \'\" + username + \"\' AND password = \'\" + password + \"\';</code></li>\n<li><strong>Attack vector:</strong> The attacker enters a username such as <code>\' OR \'1\'=\'1</code> which transforms the query into a statement that always evaluates to true, granting unauthorized access.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequences:</strong> Data theft, data loss, privilege escalation, and full database compromise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most effective defense is to use <strong>parameterized queries</strong> (also known as prepared statements). Instead of concatenating strings, you bind user input to placeholders that the database treats as data, not executable code.</p>\n<code>const stmt = db.prepare(\'SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? AND password = ?\');\nstmt.execute([username, password]);</code>\n<p>Another layer of protection is to employ an ORM that automatically handles parameterization, and to enforce strict input validation and sanitization. Additionally, limit database permissions: the application should only have the minimum privileges required to perform its tasks.</p>\n<h3>2. Cross‑Site Scripting (XSS)</h3>\n<p>XSS attacks allow attackers to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. The attacker’s script runs in the victim’s browser, potentially stealing cookies, session tokens, or performing actions on behalf of the user.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Types of XSS:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stored XSS:</strong> Malicious code is saved on the server (e.g., in a comment field) and served to all users.</li>\n<li><strong>Reflected XSS:</strong> The attacker crafts a URL that includes malicious code; when the victim clicks it, the code is reflected in the response.</li>\n<li><strong>DOM‑based XSS:</strong> The vulnerability lies in client‑side JavaScript that manipulates the DOM based on untrusted input.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Common entry points:</strong> User comments, search queries, URL parameters, and any input that is displayed back to the user.</li>\n<li><strong>Defense in depth:</strong></li>\n<li>Use a robust templating engine that automatically escapes output.</li>\n<li>Apply Content Security Policy (CSP) headers to restrict the sources from which scripts can load.</li>\n<li>Validate and sanitize input on both client and server sides.</li>\n<li>Encode output appropriately (HTML entities for text, JSON escaping for data inserted into scripts).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example of safe output using a templating engine:</p>\n<code>{{ userComment }}</code>\n<p>Here, the engine will escape characters like <, >, &, and \" so that the browser treats them as text, not executable code.</p>\n<h3>3. Cross‑Site Request Forgery (CSRF)</h3>\n<p>CSRF tricks a logged‑in user’s browser into sending an unwanted request to a target site. Because the browser automatically includes authentication cookies, the request appears legitimate to the server, potentially changing data or performing actions without the user’s consent.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Typical attack flow:</strong> The victim visits a malicious site that contains an auto‑submitted form or an image tag pointing to <code>https://bank.com/transfer?amount=1000&to=attacker</code>. The browser sends the request with the victim’s bank cookies, completing the transfer.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention techniques:</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Anti‑CSRF tokens:</strong> Generate a random token per user session and embed it in forms. The server validates the token on form submission.</li>\n<li><strong>SameSite cookie attribute:</strong> Set cookies with <code>SameSite=Lax</code> or <code>SameSite=Strict</code> to restrict cross‑origin requests.</li>\n<li><strong>Double submit cookies:</strong> Send the token both as a cookie and as a request header or form field, then compare them on the server.</li>\n<li><strong>Check Referer/Origin headers:</strong> Verify that the request originates from your own domain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Implementing CSRF protection is straightforward in most frameworks. For example, in Express.js you can use the <code>csurf</code> middleware, which automatically generates and validates tokens.</p>\n<code>const csrf = require(\'csurf\');\napp.use(csrf({ cookie: true }));\napp.get(\'/form\', (req, res) => {\n  res.render(\'form\', { csrfToken: req.csrfToken() });\n});</code>\n<p>Remember, CSRF protection is only effective when the application relies on cookies for authentication. If you use token‑based authentication (e.g., JWT in headers), CSRF is largely mitigated, but you must still guard against XSS to protect the token.</p>\n<p>In summary, secure web development requires a layered approach: validate and sanitize all input, escape all output, use parameterized database queries, enforce strict authentication mechanisms, and protect against state‑changing requests with CSRF tokens. By integrating these practices into your development workflow, you’ll build resilient applications that protect both your users and your data.</p>', 'Introduction to Web Security', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(138, 151, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development best practices illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+best+practices+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+best+practices+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+best+practices+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+best+practices+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+best+practices+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Code Review</h3><p><strong>What is a code review?</strong> A code review is a systematic examination of source code by one or more developers other than the original author. It helps catch bugs, improve code quality, and share knowledge across the team. Even for a beginner, learning how to give and receive constructive feedback is essential.</p><p>Why do we do it? <strong>Benefits include:</strong></p><ul><li>Early detection of errors before they reach production.</li><li>Consistent coding style across the project.</li><li>Knowledge transfer – developers learn new techniques from peers.</li><li>Better documentation through comments added during review.</li></ul><p>How to perform a good review:</p><ul><li><strong>Start with a checklist.</strong> Common items: naming conventions, error handling, security checks, and performance concerns.</li><li><strong>Keep it short and focused.</strong> Review a few files at a time – 200 lines or less.</li><li>Use <code>git diff</code> to view changes side‑by‑side. This makes spotting issues easier.</li><li>Ask questions instead of making statements. For example, \"Why did you choose this variable name?\" invites discussion.</li><li>End with a summary: what was good, what needs improvement, and next steps.</li></ul><p>Tools that help:</p><ul><li><strong>GitHub Pull Requests</strong> – built‑in review interface with inline comments.</li><li><strong>GitLab Merge Requests</strong> – similar features, plus CI integration.</li><li><strong>Gerrit</strong> – a more advanced, code‑centric review system.</li></ul><p>Remember: a code review is a learning opportunity, not a critique. Encourage a positive culture by praising good practices and offering suggestions in a respectful tone.</p><h3>Version Control</h3><p><strong>Why version control matters</strong> – it records every change, lets you revert to previous states, and supports collaboration. The most common system is <strong>Git</strong>, which is distributed, meaning every clone has the full history.</p><p>Key concepts for beginners:</p><ul><li><strong>Repository</strong> – the folder containing your project and its history.</li><li><strong>Commit</strong> – a snapshot of changes with a message describing the intent.</li><li><strong>Branch</strong> – a parallel line of development. Use branches for features, bug fixes, or experiments.</li><li><strong>Merge</strong> – combining changes from one branch into another.</li><li><strong>Pull Request / Merge Request</strong> – a request to merge a branch after review.</li></ul><p>Basic workflow:</p><ul><li>Initialize: <code>git init</code></li><li>Add files: <code>git add .</code></li><li>Commit: <code>git commit -m \"Initial commit\"</code></li><li>Push to remote: <code>git push origin main</code></li></ul><p>Branching example:</p><ul><li>Create a new branch: <code>git checkout -b feature-login</code></li><li>Make changes, commit, and push: <code>git push -u origin feature-login</code></li><li>Open a Pull Request on GitHub, get it reviewed, then merge.</li></ul><p>Important practices:</p><ul><li>Write clear, concise commit messages. Use the imperative mood: \"Add login page\".</li><li>Commit often – small, logical units of work.</li><li>Pull changes from the main branch before pushing: <code>git pull --rebase</code>.</li><li>Use tags for releases: <code>git tag v1.0.0</code>.</li></ul><p>Learning resources: <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs\">Git documentation</a>, <a href=\"https://www.atlassian.com/git/tutorials\">Atlassian Git tutorials</a>.</p><h3>Testing</h3><p><strong>What is testing?</strong> Testing is the process of verifying that your code behaves as expected. For beginners, focus on unit tests and basic integration tests.</p><p>Why test?</p><ul><li>Catch bugs early.</li><li>Ensure new changes don’t break existing features.</li><li>Document intended behavior.</li></ul><p>Common testing frameworks:</p><ul><li><strong>Jest</strong> – JavaScript testing framework with a simple API.</li><li><strong>Mocha & Chai</strong> – flexible testing and assertion libraries.</li><li><strong>Testing Library</strong> – encourages tests that resemble user interactions.</li></ul><p>Writing a simple unit test in Jest:</p><pre><code>import sum from \'./sum\';\n\ntest(\'adds 1 + 2 to equal 3\', () => {\n  expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);\n});</code></pre><p>Tips for beginners:</p><ul><li>Start with core functionality – test the most critical paths.</li><li>Use descriptive test names.</li><li>Mock external dependencies (e.g., API calls) to isolate the unit under test.</li><li>Run tests automatically with <code>npm test</code> or <code>yarn test</code>.</li></ul><p>Continuous Integration (CI) ties testing to deployment. Services like GitHub Actions or Travis CI can run your test suite on every push or pull request, ensuring code quality before merging.</p><p>Beyond unit tests, consider:</p><ul><li><strong>Integration tests</strong> – test how modules interact.</li><li><strong>End‑to‑end tests</strong> – simulate user workflows using tools like Cypress.</li><li>Performance tests to measure load times.</li></ul><p>Remember: tests are not a replacement for code reviews, but they complement each other. Together, they create a robust development workflow that keeps your project reliable and maintainable.</p>', 'Web Development Best Practices', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(139, 152, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: website deployment illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=website+deployment+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/website+deployment+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/website+deployment+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/website+deployment+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=website+deployment+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>Deploying a website is the final step that turns your local project into a live application that users can access from anywhere. In this lesson you will learn three common deployment methods: FTP, SFTP, and cloud hosting. By the end of the day you will have a working copy of your site on a public server and understand the pros and cons of each approach.</p>\n<h3>FTP (File Transfer Protocol)</h3>\n<p>FTP is one of the oldest ways to move files from your computer to a web server. It works over a standard TCP connection and uses two ports: 21 for commands and a dynamic port for data transfer. FTP is simple to set up but it sends credentials in plain text, which is a security risk.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When to use FTP:</strong> Small projects, shared hosting, or when your hosting provider only offers an FTP interface.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical workflow:</strong> Write your site locally, open an FTP client, connect to the server, and drag‑drop files into the public_html folder.</li>\n<li><strong>Common FTP clients:</strong> FileZilla, Cyberduck, WinSCP.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example FTP connection string:</p>\n<code>ftp://username:password@yourdomain.com:21</code>\n<p>Remember to replace <code>username</code>, <code>password</code>, and <code>yourdomain.com</code> with your actual credentials. Once connected, you can upload, delete, or edit files directly on the server.</p>\n<h3>SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol)</h3>\n<p>SFTP is a secure alternative to FTP that runs over an SSH tunnel. It encrypts both commands and data, protecting your credentials and file contents from eavesdropping.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When to use SFTP:</strong> Any situation where security matters – e.g., production sites, sensitive data, or when your hosting provider requires SSH access.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical workflow:</strong> Use an SFTP client, connect with your SSH key or password, and transfer files just like FTP.</li>\n<li><strong>Common SFTP clients:</strong> FileZilla (supports SFTP), Cyberduck, WinSCP.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example SFTP command line:</p>\n<code>sftp username@yourdomain.com</code>\n<p>After authentication, you can navigate with <code>cd</code> and transfer files with <code>put</code> or <code>get</code>. For example:</p>\n<code>put index.html public_html/</code>\n<p>SFTP is the preferred method for most modern hosting plans because it keeps your data safe while still being user‑friendly.</p>\n<h3>Cloud Hosting</h3>\n<p>Cloud hosting moves the concept of deployment beyond a single server. Instead of a dedicated machine, you use a pool of virtual servers that can scale automatically. Popular cloud providers include Amazon Web Services (AWS), Google Cloud Platform (GCP), Microsoft Azure, and DigitalOcean.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Benefits:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Scalability – automatically handle traffic spikes.</li>\n<li>Reliability – redundant servers reduce downtime.</li>\n<li>Flexibility – choose operating systems, databases, and services.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Common deployment patterns:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Static Site Hosting:</strong> Upload your HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to a bucket or storage service (e.g., AWS S3, Google Cloud Storage) and serve them via a CDN.</li>\n<li><strong>Containerized Apps:</strong> Package your site in a Docker container and deploy to a managed service like AWS ECS or Google Cloud Run.</li>\n<li><strong>Traditional VPS:</strong> Spin up a virtual machine, install a web server (Apache, Nginx), and push your files via Git or SFTP.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Getting started with a free tier:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Sign up for the AWS Free Tier, create an S3 bucket, and enable static website hosting.</li>\n<li>Upload your <code>index.html</code> and <code>style.css</code> files.</li>\n<li>Set the bucket policy to allow public read access.</li>\n<li>Access your site at <code>http://yourbucket.s3-website-us-east-1.amazonaws.com</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>While cloud hosting offers powerful features, it also introduces a learning curve. Start with a simple static site to get comfortable with the console, then explore more advanced services as you grow.</p>\n<h3>Choosing the Right Deployment Method</h3>\n<p>When deciding how to deploy, consider:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security:</strong> FTP is insecure; SFTP or SSH is recommended.</li>\n<li><strong>Project size:</strong> Small sites can use FTP; larger or dynamic sites benefit from cloud hosting.</li>\n<li><strong>Budget:</strong> Shared hosting with FTP is often cheaper; cloud services may have free tiers but can cost more at scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Future growth:</strong> If you anticipate traffic spikes or plan to add APIs, cloud hosting gives you the flexibility to scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In this lesson you practiced uploading files via FTP, secured the transfer with SFTP, and explored cloud hosting options. Next week we will dive into continuous deployment pipelines that automate the entire process.</p>', 'Deploying a Website', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(140, 153, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: website analytics dashboard</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=website+analytics+dashboard\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/website+analytics+dashboard\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/website+analytics+dashboard/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/website+analytics+dashboard/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=website+analytics+dashboard\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Analytics</h3><p>Welcome to Day 89 of our beginner web development course! Today we dive into the world of web analytics, a critical component for understanding how visitors interact with your site. By the end of this lesson you’ll know what Google Analytics is, how to set up basic tracking on your website, and how to interpret user behavior data to improve your digital presence.</p><h3>What is Google Analytics?</h3><p>Google Analytics is a free, cloud‑based service that collects, processes, and reports website traffic data. It provides insights into:</p><ul><li><strong>Audience</strong> – who visits your site (demographics, interests, devices)</li><li><strong>Acquisition</strong> – how visitors find you (search engines, social media, referrals)</li><li><strong>Behavior</strong> – what they do once they’re there (page views, time on page, navigation paths)</li><li><strong>Conversions</strong> – whether they complete desired actions (sign‑ups, purchases, downloads)</li></ul><p>Google Analytics uses a small JavaScript snippet, called the Global Site Tag (<code>gtag.js</code>), that you embed in every page you want to track. When a visitor loads a page, the tag sends data back to Google’s servers where it is aggregated and displayed in the Analytics dashboard.</p><h3>Setting Up Website Tracking</h3><p>Follow these steps to get started:</p><ol><li><strong>Create a Google Analytics account</strong>: Go to <a href=\"https://analytics.google.com\" target=\"_blank\">analytics.google.com</a> and sign up with your Google account. Choose “Web” as the property type.</li><li><strong>Get your tracking ID</strong>: After setting up the property, you’ll receive a tracking ID that looks like <code>UA-XXXXXXXXX-X</code> for Universal Analytics or <code>G-XXXXXXXXXX</code> for GA4.</li><li><strong>Install the Global Site Tag</strong>: Add the following code just before the closing <code>&lt;/head&gt;</code> tag on every page:</li></ol><pre><code>&lt;!-- Global site tag (gtag.js) - Google Analytics --&gt;<br>&lt;script async src=\"https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-XXXXXXXXXX\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;<br>&lt;script&gt;<br>  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];<br>  function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}<br>  gtag(\'js\', new Date());<br>  gtag(\'config\', \'G-XXXXXXXXXX\');<br>&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p>Replace <code>G-XXXXXXXXXX</code> with your actual tracking ID. Once the tag is live, data will start flowing into your Analytics account within 24 hours.</p><h3>Understanding User Behavior</h3><p>User behavior data helps you answer questions like:</p><ul><li>Which pages keep visitors engaged?</li><li>Where do users drop off?</li><li>What devices and browsers do they use?</li></ul><p>Key metrics to monitor:</p><ul><li><strong>Pageviews</strong> – total number of pages viewed.</li><li><strong>Unique Pageviews</strong> – number of sessions during which the page was viewed.</li><li><strong>Bounce Rate</strong> – percentage of single‑page sessions.</li><li><strong>Average Session Duration</strong> – average time visitors spend on your site.</li><li><strong>Goal Completions</strong> – number of times visitors complete a predefined goal.</li></ul><p>Use the <strong>Behavior Flow</strong> visualization to see the path users take through your site. This can reveal friction points and opportunities for optimization.</p><h3>Practical Exercise</h3><p>1. Create a dummy website with three pages: Home, About, and Contact.</p><p>2. Install the Global Site Tag on each page.</p><p>3. In Google Analytics, set up a simple goal: “Contact Form Submission.”</p><p>4. Visit your site from multiple devices and observe how the data appears in real time.</p><p>5. Identify one improvement you could make based on the behavior data (e.g., adding a call‑to‑action button on the Home page).</p><h3>Why Analytics Matters for Beginners</h3><p>As a new developer, you might focus on building features, but analytics gives you a feedback loop:</p><ul><li>It validates whether your design choices resonate with users.</li><li>It highlights bugs or usability issues that aren’t obvious during development.</li><li>It provides measurable goals for future projects.</li></ul><p>Even a simple analytics setup can dramatically improve the quality of your work and prepare you for more advanced data‑driven decision making.</p><h3>Next Steps</h3><p>In the next lesson, we’ll explore advanced tracking techniques such as event tracking, custom dimensions, and integrating Google Analytics with other tools like Google Search Console. For now, experiment with the basic setup and start collecting data.</p>', 'Introduction to Web Analytics', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(141, 154, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: final project showcase</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=final+project+showcase\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/final+project+showcase\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/final+project+showcase/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/final+project+showcase/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=final+project+showcase\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Project Showcase</h3>\n<p>Welcome to the final day of our beginner web development course! Today is all about celebrating the work you’ve done over the past weeks. Your <strong>final project</strong> is a complete, functional website that demonstrates the fundamentals you’ve learned: HTML structure, CSS styling, and basic JavaScript interactivity. This session will give you the chance to present your site to the class, receive constructive feedback, and reflect on the development process.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prepare a short demo:</strong> Spend 2–3 minutes walking through your site. Highlight the key features, the design decisions, and any challenges you overcame.</li>\n<li><strong>Live coding snippet:</strong> Show a small piece of JavaScript or CSS that you wrote that solved a specific problem. Use <code>&lt;code&gt;</code> tags to display the snippet clearly.</li>\n<li><strong>Explain your folder structure:</strong> Show how you organized your files (index.html, styles.css, script.js, assets/). Discuss why this structure helps with maintenance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember to keep your presentation concise and focused. The goal is to demonstrate what you’ve built and to show your understanding of the concepts, not to perform a full code walkthrough.</p>\n\n<h3>Code Review</h3>\n<p>After the showcase, we’ll dive into a structured code review. Code reviews are a vital part of professional web development, helping teams catch bugs, improve readability, and maintain consistency. In this class, we’ll follow a simple review checklist:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HTML Validity:</strong> Are tags properly closed? Are semantic elements used (e.g., <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code>)?</li>\n<li><strong>CSS Organization:</strong> Is the CSS modular? Are class names meaningful and consistent? Do you use a naming convention like BEM?</li>\n<li><strong>JavaScript Practices:</strong> Are variables declared with <code>let</code> or <code>const</code> instead of <code>var</code>? Is the code free of console.log statements? Are functions concise?</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> Are images using <code>alt</code> attributes? Are interactive elements keyboard-navigable?</li>\n<li><strong>Performance:</strong> Are you minimizing HTTP requests? Do you use <code>&lt;link rel=\"preload\"…&gt;</code> for critical resources?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>During the review, each student will pair up and critique each other’s code. Use the <strong>peer review rubric</strong> provided in the handout to guide your feedback. Focus on both strengths and areas for improvement. When giving feedback, be specific: instead of saying “your CSS looks messy,” point out a particular selector that could be simplified.</p>\n\n<h3>Feedback</h3>\n<p>Feedback is a two-way street. While you’ll receive critiques from classmates and the instructor, you’ll also have the chance to give feedback. This practice builds communication skills and fosters a collaborative learning environment. Here’s how to give effective feedback:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Be constructive:</strong> Highlight what works well before suggesting changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Use the “I” statement:</strong> Say, “I think the button could be more accessible by adding a <code>role=\"button\"</code> attribute,” rather than “you did it wrong.”</li>\n<li><strong>Prioritize:</strong> Focus on the most critical issues first, such as broken links or missing alt text.</li>\n<li><strong>Offer solutions:</strong> Suggest concrete improvements, like “use <code>display: flex;</code> to center the header.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After the peer review, we’ll have a group discussion. Each student will share one key takeaway from the feedback they received and how they plan to apply it to future projects. This reflection helps cement the learning and encourages continuous improvement.</p>\n<p>Finally, we’ll wrap up with a quick recap of the course milestones: setting up a project, structuring files, writing semantic HTML, styling with CSS, adding interactivity with JavaScript, and now, presenting and reviewing your work. You’ve come a long way from the first “Hello, World!” page to a polished, fully functional website. Celebrate your progress, and keep iterating on the skills you’ve gained!</p>', 'Final Project Submission and Review', 1, '2026-04-07 09:00:24', '2026-04-07 09:00:24'),
(142, 155, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming setup</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming+setup\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming+setup\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming+setup/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming+setup/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming+setup\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Welcome to Python Programming</h3><p>Python is a high-level, interpreted programming language that is widely used for various purposes such as web development, scientific computing, data analysis, artificial intelligence, and more. It was created in the late 1980s by Guido van Rossum and was first released in 1991.</p><p>Python is known for its simplicity, readability, and ease of use, making it an ideal language for beginners. It has a vast collection of libraries and frameworks that make it a popular choice among developers.</p><h3>Python Basics</h3><p>Before we dive into setting up the environment, let\'s cover some basic concepts of Python. Python is an object-oriented language that supports various programming paradigms such as procedural, functional, and imperative programming.</p><ul><li><strong>Indentation</strong>: Python uses indentation to define the structure of the code. It is essential to use four spaces for each level of indentation.</li><li><strong>Variables</strong>: In Python, you don\'t need to declare variables before using them. You can assign a value to a variable using the assignment operator (=).</li><li><strong>Print Function</strong>: The print function is used to output text to the screen. You can use it to print strings, numbers, and other data types.</li></ul><p>Here\'s an example of a simple Python program:</p><code>x = 5<br>y = 10<br>print(x + y)</code><p>This program assigns the value 5 to the variable x and 10 to the variable y. Then, it prints the sum of x and y using the print function.</p><h3>Setting up Python</h3><p>To start programming in Python, you need to have Python installed on your computer. You can download the latest version of Python from the official Python website.</p><ul><li><strong>Windows</strong>: Download the Python installer for Windows and follow the installation instructions.</li><li><strong>Mac</strong>: You can install Python using Homebrew or download the installer from the official Python website.</li><li><strong>Linux</strong>: You can install Python using the package manager or download the installer from the official Python website.</li></ul><p>Once you have installed Python, you can verify the installation by opening a terminal or command prompt and typing <code>python --version</code>. This should display the version of Python that you just installed.</p><h3>Text Editors</h3><p>A text editor is a program that allows you to create and edit text files. There are many text editors available, and some popular ones include:</p><ul><li><strong>Notepad++</strong>: A free text editor for Windows.</li><li><strong>Sublime Text</strong>: A popular text editor for Windows, Mac, and Linux.</li><li><strong>Atom</strong>: A free and open-source text editor for Windows, Mac, and Linux.</li><li><strong>Visual Studio Code</strong>: A free and open-source text editor for Windows, Mac, and Linux.</li></ul><p>When choosing a text editor, consider the following factors:</p><ul><li><strong>Syntax Highlighting</strong>: The ability of the text editor to highlight the syntax of the programming language.</li><li><strong>Code Completion</strong>: The ability of the text editor to complete the code as you type.</li><li><strong>Debugging</strong>: The ability of the text editor to debug the code and identify errors.</li></ul><p>Now that you have set up Python and chosen a text editor, you are ready to start programming in Python.</p>', 'Introduction to Python and Setting up the Environment', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(143, 156, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Basic Syntax and Data Types in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 2 of the Python course. In this lesson, we will explore the basic syntax and data types in Python. Python is a high-level, easy-to-learn language that is widely used for various purposes such as web development, scientific computing, and data analysis.</p><h3>Variables in Python</h3><p>In Python, a variable is a name given to a value. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in a program. You can think of a variable as a labeled box where you can store a value. To assign a value to a variable, you use the assignment operator (=). For example:</p><code>x = 5</code><p>This code assigns the value 5 to the variable x.</p><p>In Python, you don\'t need to declare the type of a variable before using it. Python can automatically detect the type of a variable based on the value assigned to it. However, it\'s a good practice to use meaningful variable names to make your code more readable.</p><h3>Data Types in Python</h3><p>Python has several built-in data types. Here are some of the most common data types:</p><ul><li><strong>Integers</strong>: Integers are whole numbers, either positive, negative, or zero. For example: 1, 2, 3, etc.</li><li><strong>Floats</strong>: Floats are real numbers, which can have a fractional part. For example: 3.14, -0.5, etc.</li><li><strong>Strings</strong>: Strings are sequences of characters, such as words, sentences, or paragraphs. Strings are enclosed in quotes (either single quotes or double quotes). For example: \'hello\', \"hello\", etc.</li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: Boolean values are either True or False.</li><li><strong>List</strong>: Lists are ordered collections of values, which can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are enclosed in square brackets []. For example: [1, 2, 3], [\'a\', \'b\', \'c\'], etc.</li><li><strong>Tuple</strong>: Tuples are ordered, immutable collections of values, which can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are enclosed in parentheses (). For example: (1, 2, 3), (\'a\', \'b\', \'c\'), etc.</li></ul><h3>Basic Operators in Python</h3><p>Python has various operators for performing arithmetic, comparison, logical, and assignment operations. Here are some of the most common operators:</p><h3>Arithmetic Operators</h3><ul><li><strong>+</strong>: Addition</li><li><strong>-</strong>: Subtraction</li><li><strong>*</strong>: Multiplication</li><li><strong>/</strong>: Division</li><li><strong>%</strong>: Modulus (remainder)</li><li><strong>**</strong>: Exponentiation</li></ul><p>For example:</p><code>x = 5<br>y = 3<br>print(x + y)  # Output: 8<br>print(x - y)  # Output: 2<br>print(x * y)  # Output: 15<br>print(x / y)  # Output: 1.6666666666666667<br>print(x % y)  # Output: 2<br>print(x ** y)  # Output: 125</code><h3>Comparison Operators</h3><ul><li><strong>==</strong>: Equal to</li><li><strong>!=</strong>: Not equal to</li><li><strong>&gt;</strong>: Greater than</li><li><strong>&lt;</strong>: Less than</li><li><strong>&gt;=</strong>: Greater than or equal to</li><li><strong>&lt;=</strong>: Less than or equal to</li></ul><p>For example:</p><code>x = 5<br>y = 3<br>print(x == y)  # Output: False<br>print(x != y)  # Output: True<br>print(x > y)  # Output: True<br>print(x < y)  # Output: False<br>print(x >= y)  # Output: True<br>print(x <= y)  # Output: False</code><h3>Logical Operators</h3><ul><li><strong>and</strong>: Logical and</li><li><strong>or</strong>: Logical or</li><li><strong>not</strong>: Logical not</li></ul><p>For example:</p><code>x = 5<br>y = 3<br>print(x > y and x == 5)  # Output: True<br>print(x > y or x == 3)  # Output: True<br>print(not x > y)  # Output: False</code><p>In this lesson, we covered the basic syntax and data types in Python, including variables, data types, and basic operators. We also learned how to use these concepts to perform various operations.</p>', 'Basic Syntax and Data Types in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(144, 157, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python control structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Control Structures in Python</h3><p>Control structures are the building blocks of any programming language, and Python is no exception. They allow you to control the flow of your program, making decisions, repeating tasks, and skipping over code. In this lesson, we will cover three essential control structures in Python: If-Else statements, For loops, and While loops.</p><h3>If-Else Statements</h3><p>If-Else statements are used to make decisions in your program. They allow you to execute a block of code if a certain condition is true, and another block of code if the condition is false. The basic syntax of an If-Else statement is:</p><code>if condition:\n    # code to execute if condition is true\nelse:\n    # code to execute if condition is false</code><p>For example:</p><code>age = 25\nif age >= 18:\n    print(\'You are an adult\')\nelse:\n    print(\'You are a minor\')</code><p>In this example, the program checks if the age is greater than or equal to 18. If it is, it prints \'You are an adult\'. If not, it prints \'You are a minor\'.</p><h3>For Loops</h3><p>For loops are used to repeat a block of code for a specified number of times. They are particularly useful when you need to iterate over a sequence, such as a list or a string. The basic syntax of a For loop is:</p><code>for variable in sequence:\n    # code to execute</code><p>For example:</p><code>fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'cherry\']\nfor fruit in fruits:\n    print(fruit)</code><p>In this example, the program prints each fruit in the list.</p><h3>While Loops</h3><p>While loops are used to repeat a block of code while a certain condition is true. They are similar to For loops, but they do not require a sequence to iterate over. The basic syntax of a While loop is:</p><code>while condition:\n    # code to execute</code><p>For example:</p><code>i = 0\nwhile i < 5:\n    print(i)\n    i += 1</code><p>In this example, the program prints the numbers 0 to 4.</p><h3>Nested Control Structures</h3><p>Control structures can be nested inside each other. For example, you can have an If-Else statement inside a For loop, or a While loop inside an If-Else statement. This allows you to create complex logic and make decisions based on multiple conditions.</p><p>For example:</p><code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nfor number in numbers:\n    if number % 2 == 0:\n        print(f\'{number} is even\')\n    else:\n        print(f\'{number} is odd\')</code><p>In this example, the program checks each number in the list to see if it is even or odd, and prints the result.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><ul><li>Use meaningful variable names to make your code easy to understand.</li><li>Use comments to explain what your code is doing.</li><li>Use indentation to make your code easy to read.</li><li>Test your code thoroughly to make sure it works as expected.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can write clean, efficient, and effective code that uses control structures to make decisions and repeat tasks.</p>', 'Control Structures in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
