(145, 158, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: functions in python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=functions+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/functions+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/functions+in+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/functions+in+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=functions+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Day 4: Functions in Python</h2>\n<h3>Defining Functions in Python</h3>\n<p>Functions are a fundamental concept in Python programming. They allow you to group a set of statements together to perform a specific task. A function can take arguments, perform some operations, and return a value. In this topic, we will learn how to define functions in Python.</p>\n<p>Here is a basic syntax of defining a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def function_name(parameters):\n    # function body\n</code></pre>\n<p>Let\'s consider an example:</p>\n<pre><code>def greet(name):\n    print(\'Hello, \' + name + \'!\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function takes a parameter \'name\' and prints a greeting message. To call this function, we can use the following code:</p>\n<pre><code>greet(\'John\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will output: Hello, John!</p>\n<p>Functions can also take multiple parameters:</p>\n<pre><code>def greet(name, age):\n    print(\'Hello, \' + name + \'! You are \' + str(age) + \' years old.\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>Functions can also return values:</p>\n<pre><code>def add(x, y):\n    return x + y\n</code></pre>\n<p>To call this function and store the returned value in a variable, we can use the following code:</p>\n<pre><code>result = add(5, 3)\nprint(result)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will output: 8</p>\n<h3>Function Arguments</h3>\n<p>Functions can take different types of arguments:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Positional arguments:</strong> These are arguments that are passed to the function in a specific order. For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def greet(name, age):\n    print(\'Hello, \' + name + \'! You are \' + str(age) + \' years old.\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>To call this function with positional arguments, we can use the following code:</p>\n<pre><code>greet(\'John\', 30)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will output: Hello, John! You are 30 years old.</p>\n<li><strong>Keyword arguments:</strong> These are arguments that are passed to the function using their names. For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def greet(name, age):\n    print(\'Hello, \' + name + \'! You are \' + str(age) + \' years old.\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>To call this function with keyword arguments, we can use the following code:</p>\n<pre><code>greet(age=30, name=\'John\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will output: Hello, John! You are 30 years old.</p>\n<li><strong>Default arguments:</strong> These are arguments that have a default value. If a value is not passed for this argument, the default value will be used. For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def greet(name, age=30):\n    print(\'Hello, \' + name + \'! You are \' + str(age) + \' years old.\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>To call this function with default arguments, we can use the following code:</p>\n<pre><code>greet(\'John\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will output: Hello, John! You are 30 years old.</p>\n<li><strong>Variable arguments:</strong> These are arguments that can take any number of values. For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def greet(*names):\n    for name in names:\n        print(\'Hello, \' + name + \'!\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>To call this function with variable arguments, we can use the following code:</p>\n<pre><code>greet(\'John\', \'Alice\', \'Bob\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will output: Hello, John! Hello, Alice! Hello, Bob!</p>\n</ul>\n<h3>Return Types</h3>\n<p>Functions can return different types of values, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Integers:</strong> For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def add(x, y):\n    return x + y\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function returns an integer value.</p>\n<li><strong>Floats:</strong> For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def divide(x, y):\n    return x / y\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function returns a float value.</p>\n<li><strong>Strings:</strong> For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def greet(name):\n    return \'Hello, \' + name + \'!\'\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function returns a string value.</p>\n<li><strong>Lists:</strong> For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def get_names():\n    return [\'John\', \'Alice\', \'Bob\']\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function returns a list value.</p>\n<li><strong>Tuples:</strong> For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def get_info():\n    return (\'John\', 30, \'Software Engineer\')\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function returns a tuple value.</p>\n<li><strong>Dictionaries:</strong> For example:</p>\n<pre><code>def get_info():\n    return {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \' occupation\': \'Software Engineer\'}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function returns a dictionary value.</p>\n</ul>\n<p>Quiz:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>What is the syntax of defining a function in Python?</li>\n    <li>What are the different types of arguments that a function can take?</li>\n    <li>What are the different types of values that a function can return?</li>\n</ul>\n', 'Functions in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(146, 159, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python lists tuples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+lists+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+lists+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+lists+tuples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+lists+tuples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+lists+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Lists and Tuples in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 5 of the Python course. Today, we will be covering two of the most important data structures in Python: Lists and Tuples. We will also be discussing Indexing and Slicing, which are essential concepts when working with these data structures.</p><h3>Lists</h3><p>In Python, a list is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are denoted by square brackets <code>[]</code> and are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation.</p><p>Here is an example of a list in Python:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code><p>Lists have several methods that can be used to manipulate them, including <code>append()</code>, <code>insert()</code>, <code>remove()</code>, and <code>sort()</code>.</p><ul><li><code>append()</code>: adds an item to the end of the list</li><li><code>insert()</code>: inserts an item at a specified position in the list</li><li><code>remove()</code>: removes the first occurrence of an item in the list</li><li><code>sort()</code>: sorts the items in the list in ascending order</li></ul><h3>Tuples</h3><p>In Python, a tuple is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are denoted by parentheses <code>()</code> and are immutable, meaning they cannot be modified after creation.</p><p>Here is an example of a tuple in Python:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)</code><p>Tuples have several methods that can be used to manipulate them, including <code>index()</code> and <code>count()</code>.</p><ul><li><code>index()</code>: returns the index of the first occurrence of an item in the tuple</li><li><code>count()</code>: returns the number of occurrences of an item in the tuple</li></ul><h3>Indexing</h3><p>In Python, indexing is used to access specific items in a list or tuple. Indexing is zero-based, meaning the first item in a list or tuple has an index of 0.</p><p>Here is an example of indexing in Python:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[0])  # prints 1</code><p>Indexing can also be used to access items from the end of a list or tuple using negative indices.</p><p>Here is an example of negative indexing in Python:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[-1])  # prints 5</code><h3>Slicing</h3><p>In Python, slicing is used to access a subset of items in a list or tuple. Slicing is done using a colon <code>:</code> and can be used to access a range of items.</p><p>Here is an example of slicing in Python:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[1:3])  # prints [2, 3]</code><p>Slicing can also be used to access a subset of items from the end of a list or tuple using negative indices.</p><p>Here is an example of negative slicing in Python:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[-2:])  # prints [4, 5]</code><p>In conclusion, lists and tuples are two of the most important data structures in Python. Indexing and slicing are essential concepts when working with these data structures. With practice and experience, you will become proficient in using lists, tuples, indexing, and slicing to solve real-world problems.</p>', 'Lists and Tuples in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(147, 160, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python dictionaries and sets</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+dictionaries+and+sets\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+dictionaries+and+sets\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+dictionaries+and+sets/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+dictionaries+and+sets/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+dictionaries+and+sets\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Dictionaries and Sets in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 6 of our Python course. Today, we will be covering two important data structures in Python: Dictionaries and Sets. These data structures are used to store and manipulate data in a more efficient and organized way.</p><h3>Dictionaries</h3><p>A dictionary in Python is an unordered collection of key-value pairs. It is a mutable data type that stores mappings of unique keys to values. Dictionaries are also known as associative arrays or hash tables.</p><p>A dictionary is defined by placing a sequence of key-value pairs between curly braces {}, where each key-value pair is separated by a comma. The key-value pairs are separated by a colon (:), where the key is on the left and the value is on the right.</p><p>Here is an example of a dictionary:</p><code>person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \'city\': \'New York\'}</code><p>In this example, \'name\', \'age\', and \'city\' are the keys, and \'John\', 30, and \'New York\' are the values.</p><h3>Accessing Dictionary Elements</h3><p>To access a value in a dictionary, you can use the key. For example:</p><code>print(person[\'name\'])  # Output: John</code><p>If the key is not found in the dictionary, Python will raise a KeyError.</p><h3>Updating Dictionary Elements</h3><p>To update a value in a dictionary, you can assign a new value to the key. For example:</p><code>person[\'age\'] = 31</code><p>This will update the value of the \'age\' key to 31.</p><h3>Adding New Elements to a Dictionary</h3><p>To add a new key-value pair to a dictionary, you can assign a value to a new key. For example:</p><code>person[\'country\'] = \'USA\'</code><p>This will add a new key-value pair to the dictionary.</p><h3>Removing Elements from a Dictionary</h3><p>To remove a key-value pair from a dictionary, you can use the del statement. For example:</p><code>del person[\'city\']</code><p>This will remove the \'city\' key-value pair from the dictionary.</p><h3>Sets</h3><p>A set in Python is an unordered collection of unique elements. It is a mutable data type that stores a collection of unique elements without duplicates.</p><p>A set is defined by placing a sequence of elements between curly braces {} or by using the set() function.</p><p>Here is an example of a set:</p><code>colors = {\'red\', \'green\', \'blue\'}</code><p>In this example, \'red\', \'green\', and \'blue\' are the elements of the set.</p><h3>Set Operations</h3><p>Sets support various mathematical operations, such as union, intersection, and difference.</p><ul><li><strong>Union:</strong> The union of two sets is a set containing all elements from both sets.</li><li><strong>Intersection:</strong> The intersection of two sets is a set containing all elements that are common to both sets.</li><li><strong>Difference:</strong> The difference of two sets is a set containing all elements that are in the first set but not in the second set.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of set operations:</p><code>set1 = {1, 2, 3}</code><code>set2 = {3, 4, 5}</code><code>print(set1 | set2)  # Output: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} (union)</code><code>print(set1 & set2)  # Output: {3} (intersection)</code><code>print(set1 - set2)  # Output: {1, 2} (difference)</code><h3>Dictionary Methods</h3><p>Dictionaries have various methods that can be used to manipulate and access the data. Here are some of the most commonly used dictionary methods:</p><ul><li><strong>keys():</strong> Returns a view object that displays a list of all the keys in the dictionary.</li><li><strong>values():</strong> Returns a view object that displays a list of all the values in the dictionary.</li><li><strong>items():</strong> Returns a view object that displays a list of all the key-value pairs in the dictionary.</li><li><strong>get():</strong> Returns the value for the given key if it exists in the dictionary. If not, it returns a default value.</li><li><strong>pop():</strong> Removes and returns the value for the given key if it exists in the dictionary.</li><li><strong>update():</strong> Updates the dictionary with the key-value pairs from another dictionary or from an iterable of key-value pairs.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of dictionary methods:</p><code>person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}</code><code>print(person.keys())  # Output: dict_keys([\'name\', \'age\'])</code><code>print(person.values())  # Output: dict_values([\'John\', 30])</code><code>print(person.items())  # Output: dict_items([(\'name\', \'John\'), (\'age\', 30)])</code><code>print(person.get(\'name\'))  # Output: John</code><code>print(person.pop(\'age\'))  # Output: 30</code><code>person.update({\'city\': \'New York\'})</code><code>print(person)  # Output: {\'name\': \'John\', \'city\': \'New York\'}</code><p>In conclusion, dictionaries and sets are two important data structures in Python that can be used to store and manipulate data in a more efficient and organized way. Dictionaries are used to store key-value pairs, while sets are used to store unique elements. Dictionary methods can be used to access and manipulate the data in a dictionary.</p>', 'Dictionaries and Sets in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(148, 161, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Practice and Review of Python Basics</h3><p>Welcome to Day 7 of our Python course. Today, we will be practicing and reviewing the basics of Python programming. This will help solidify your understanding of the concepts and prepare you for more advanced topics.</p><p>First, let\'s review the basic syntax and data types in Python. Python is a high-level language that supports multiple programming paradigms, including procedural, object-oriented, and functional programming. It has a simple syntax and is relatively easy to learn.</p><h3>Practice Exercises</h3><p>To get started with practicing Python, you can try the following exercises:</p><ul><li><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Write a Python program to print the numbers from 1 to 10.</li><li><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Write a Python program to calculate the sum of all numbers in a list.</li><li><strong>Exercise 3:</strong> Write a Python program to find the maximum number in a list.</li></ul><p>Here are some sample solutions to these exercises:</p><ul><li><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> <code>for i in range(1, 11): print(i)</code></li><li><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> <code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]; sum_of_numbers = sum(numbers); print(sum_of_numbers)</code></li><li><strong>Exercise 3:</strong> <code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]; max_number = max(numbers); print(max_number)</code></li></ul><h3>Review of Basics</h3><p>Now, let\'s review some of the basic concepts in Python:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables:</strong> In Python, you can assign a value to a variable using the assignment operator (=). For example: <code>x = 5</code></li><li><strong>Data Types:</strong> Python has several built-in data types, including integers, floats, strings, lists, and dictionaries. For example: <code>x = 5  # integer</code>, <code>y = 3.14  # float</code>, <code>name = \'John\'  # string</code></li><li><strong>Control Structures:</strong> Python has several control structures, including if-else statements, for loops, and while loops. For example: <code>if x > 5: print(\'x is greater than 5\')</code>, <code>for i in range(5): print(i)</code></li></ul><h3>Common Errors</h3><p>When writing Python code, there are several common errors to watch out for:</p><ul><li><strong>Syntax Errors:</strong> Python has a simple syntax, but it\'s still possible to make mistakes. For example, forgetting a colon at the end of a def statement will result in a syntax error.</li><li><strong>Runtime Errors:</strong> These errors occur when your code is running and encounters an error. For example, trying to divide by zero will result in a runtime error.</li><li><strong>Logic Errors:</strong> These errors occur when your code is running correctly, but not producing the desired output. For example, using the wrong variable name can result in a logic error.</li></ul><p>By being aware of these common errors, you can write more robust and reliable code.</p>', 'Practice and Review of Python Basics', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(149, 162, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Data Structures in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 8 of the Python course. Today, we will be exploring the basics of data structures in Python, including lists, tuples, and dictionaries. Data structures are a fundamental concept in programming and are used to store and organize data in a way that makes it efficient to access and manipulate.</p><h3>Lists</h3><p>A list in Python is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are denoted by square brackets <code>[]</code> and are used to store multiple values in a single variable.</p><p>Here are some key features of lists:</p><ul><li>Lists are <strong>ordered</strong>, meaning that the items in the list have a specific order.</li><li>Lists are <strong>mutable</strong>, meaning that they can be modified after they are created.</li><li>Lists are <strong>indexed</strong>, meaning that each item in the list has a specific index that can be used to access it.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to create and use a list in Python:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[0])  # prints 1<br>my_list.append(6)<br>print(my_list)  # prints [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]</code><h3>Tuples</h3><p>A tuple in Python is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are denoted by parentheses <code>()</code> and are used to store multiple values in a single variable.</p><p>Here are some key features of tuples:</p><ul><li>Tuples are <strong>ordered</strong>, meaning that the items in the tuple have a specific order.</li><li>Tuples are <strong>immutable</strong>, meaning that they cannot be modified after they are created.</li><li>Tuples are <strong>indexed</strong>, meaning that each item in the tuple has a specific index that can be used to access it.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to create and use a tuple in Python:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)<br>print(my_tuple[0])  # prints 1<br># my_tuple[0] = 10  # this would raise an error</code><h3>Dictionaries</h3><p>A dictionary in Python is a collection of key-value pairs. Dictionaries are denoted by curly brackets <code>{}</code> and are used to store data in a way that makes it easy to access and manipulate.</p><p>Here are some key features of dictionaries:</p><ul><li>Dictionaries are <strong>unordered</strong>, meaning that the items in the dictionary do not have a specific order.</li><li>Dictionaries are <strong>mutable</strong>, meaning that they can be modified after they are created.</li><li>Dictionaries are <strong>keyed</strong>, meaning that each item in the dictionary has a specific key that can be used to access it.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to create and use a dictionary in Python:</p><code>my_dict = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}<br>print(my_dict[\'name\'])  # prints John<br>my_dict[\'country\'] = \'USA\'<br>print(my_dict)  # prints {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \'country\': \'USA\'}</code><p>In conclusion, lists, tuples, and dictionaries are all important data structures in Python, and each has its own unique features and uses. By understanding how to use these data structures, you can write more efficient and effective code.</p>', 'Introduction to Data Structures in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(150, 163, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python lists tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+lists+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+lists+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Working with Lists in Python</h3><p>Lists are a fundamental data structure in Python, and they are used to store multiple values in a single variable. In this lesson, we will explore the different methods and techniques that can be used to work with lists in Python.</p><h3>List Methods</h3><p>Python provides a variety of methods that can be used to manipulate and transform lists. Some of the most commonly used list methods include:</p><ul><li><code>append()</code>: adds a new element to the end of the list</li><li><code>extend()</code>: adds multiple new elements to the end of the list</li><li><code>insert()</code>: inserts a new element at a specified position in the list</li><li><code>remove()</code>: removes the first occurrence of a specified element in the list</li><li><code>sort()</code>: sorts the elements of the list in ascending order</li><li><code>reverse()</code>: reverses the order of the elements in the list</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how these methods can be used:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]<br>my_list.append(4)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4]<br>my_list.extend([5, 6, 7])<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]<br>my_list.insert(0, 0)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]<br>my_list.remove(4)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7]<br>my_list.sort()<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7]<br>my_list.reverse()<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [7, 6, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0]</code><h3>List Slicing</h3><p>List slicing is a technique that can be used to extract a subset of elements from a list. The basic syntax for list slicing is <code>my_list[start:stop:step]</code>, where:</p><ul><li><code>start</code> is the index of the first element to include in the slice</li><li><code>stop</code> is the index of the first element to exclude from the slice</li><li><code>step</code> is the increment between elements in the slice</li></ul><p>Here are a few examples of list slicing:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]<br>print(my_list[1:3])  # Output: [2, 3]<br>print(my_list[1:6:2])  # Output: [2, 4, 6]<br>print(my_list[:3])  # Output: [1, 2, 3]<br>print(my_list[3:])  # Output: [4, 5, 6, 7]<br>print(my_list[:])  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]</code><h3>List Comprehensions</h3><p>List comprehensions are a concise way to create new lists from existing lists or other iterables. The basic syntax for a list comprehension is <code>[expression for variable in iterable]</code>, where:</p><ul><li><code>expression</code> is the operation that you want to perform on each element of the iterable</li><li><code>variable</code> is the name given to the element being processed</li><li><code>iterable</code> is the list or other iterable that you want to process</li></ul><p>Here are a few examples of list comprehensions:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>double_list = [x * 2 for x in my_list]<br>print(double_list)  # Output: [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]<br>even_list = [x for x in my_list if x % 2 == 0]<br>print(even_list)  # Output: [2, 4]<br>square_list = [x ** 2 for x in my_list]<br>print(square_list)  # Output: [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]</code><p>In this lesson, we have covered the different methods and techniques that can be used to work with lists in Python, including list methods, list slicing, and list comprehensions. <strong>Practice these techniques to become more proficient in working with lists.</strong></p>', 'Working with Lists in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(151, 164, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python functions tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Functions in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 10 of our Python course. Today, we will be covering one of the most fundamental concepts in programming: functions. Functions are reusable blocks of code that take arguments, perform a specific task, and return a value. In this lesson, we will explore function definitions, function arguments, and return types.</p><h3>Function Definitions</h3><p>A function definition in Python consists of the <code>def</code> keyword followed by the function name and a list of parameters in parentheses. The code block within the function is denoted by indentation. Here is a simple example of a function definition:</p><code>def greet(name):<br>    print(\'Hello, \' + name)</code><p>In this example, <code>greet</code> is the function name, and <code>name</code> is the parameter. The function prints out a personalized greeting message.</p><h3>Function Arguments</h3><p>Function arguments are the values passed to a function when it is called. These values are assigned to the parameters defined in the function definition. There are several types of function arguments in Python:</p><ul><li><strong>Positional Arguments</strong>: These are the most common type of argument. They are assigned to the parameters in the order they are defined.</li><li><strong>Keyword Arguments</strong>: These are assigned to parameters using the parameter name. They can be used to make the code more readable and to avoid having to pass all the arguments in the correct order.</li><li><strong>Default Arguments</strong>: These are assigned a default value if no value is passed when the function is called.</li><li><strong>Variable Arguments</strong>: These allow a function to accept any number of arguments.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of these types of arguments:</p><code>def greet(name, age):<br>    print(\'Hello, \' + name + \'. You are \' + str(age) + \' years old.\')<br><br>greet(\'John\', 30)  # Positional arguments<br>greet(name=\'Jane\', age=25)  # Keyword arguments<br><br>def greet(name, age=30):<br>    print(\'Hello, \' + name + \'. You are \' + str(age) + \' years old.\')<br><br>greet(\'John\')  # Default argument<br><br>def greet(*args):<br>    for arg in args:<br>        print(\'Hello, \' + arg)<br><br>greet(\'John\', \'Jane\', \'Bob\')  # Variable arguments</code><h3>Return Types</h3><p>A function can return a value using the <code>return</code> statement. The return type can be any data type, including strings, integers, floats, lists, dictionaries, and more. A function can also return multiple values by separating them with commas.</p><code>def add(a, b):<br>    return a + b<br><br>result = add(2, 3)<br>print(result)  # Output: 5<br><br>def get_user_info():<br>    name = \'John\'<br>    age = 30<br>    return name, age<br><br>name, age = get_user_info()<br>print(name)  # Output: John<br>print(age)   # Output: 30</code><p>In conclusion, functions are a powerful tool in Python that allow you to reuse code, reduce repetition, and make your programs more efficient. By understanding function definitions, function arguments, and return types, you can write more effective and readable code.</p>', 'Understanding Functions in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(152, 165, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>File Input/Output in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 11 of the Python course. Today, we will be covering one of the essential topics in programming: File Input/Output. This topic is crucial as it allows you to store and retrieve data from files, which is a fundamental aspect of any application. In this lesson, we will be discussing how to read from files, write to files, and handle exceptions that may occur during these operations.</p><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>Python provides several ways to read from files. The most common method is by using the <code>open()</code> function, which returns a file object. This object has several methods that can be used to read from the file, such as <code>read()</code>, <code>readline()</code>, and <code>readlines()</code>.</p><ul><li><code>read()</code>: This method reads the entire contents of the file and returns it as a string.</li><li><code>readline()</code>: This method reads a single line from the file and returns it as a string.</li><li><code>readlines()</code>: This method reads all lines from the file and returns them as a list of strings.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use the <code>open()</code> function to read from a file:</p><code>file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;r&quot;)\ncontent = file.read()\nprint(content)\nfile.close()</code><p>In this example, we open a file called <strong>example.txt</strong> in read mode (<strong>&quot;r&quot;</strong>) and assign it to the <strong>file</strong> variable. We then use the <code>read()</code> method to read the contents of the file and print it. Finally, we close the file using the <code>close()</code> method.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>Python also provides several ways to write to files. The most common method is by using the <code>open()</code> function, which returns a file object. This object has several methods that can be used to write to the file, such as <code>write()</code> and <code>writelines()</code>.</p><ul><li><code>write()</code>: This method writes a string to the file.</li><li><code>writelines()</code>: This method writes a list of strings to the file.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use the <code>open()</code> function to write to a file:</p><code>file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;w&quot;)\nfile.write(&quot;Hello, World!&quot;)\nfile.close()</code><p>In this example, we open a file called <strong>example.txt</strong> in write mode (<strong>&quot;w&quot;</strong>) and assign it to the <strong>file</strong> variable. We then use the <code>write()</code> method to write the string <strong>&quot;Hello, World!&quot;</strong> to the file. Finally, we close the file using the <code>close()</code> method.</p><h3>Exception Handling</h3><p>When working with files, it is essential to handle exceptions that may occur during file operations. Python provides a <code>try-except</code> block that can be used to catch and handle exceptions.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use a <code>try-except</code> block to handle exceptions when reading from a file:</p><code>try:\n    file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;r&quot;)\n    content = file.read()\n    print(content)\n    file.close()\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    print(&quot;The file does not exist.&quot;)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(&quot;An error occurred: &quot;, str(e))</code><p>In this example, we use a <code>try-except</code> block to catch exceptions that may occur when reading from a file. If the file does not exist, a <code>FileNotFoundError</code> exception is raised, and we print an error message. If any other exception occurs, we catch it using the <code>Exception</code> class and print an error message.</p><p>In conclusion, file input/output is an essential topic in programming, and Python provides several ways to read from and write to files. It is also crucial to handle exceptions that may occur during file operations using a <code>try-except</code> block.</p>', 'File Input/Output in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(153, 166, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python oop concepts</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming in Python</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of OOP in Python, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class. For example:</p><code>class Car:</code><p>This class can have attributes (data) and methods (functions). Attributes are used to describe the characteristics of the class, while methods are used to define the actions that the class can perform.</p><p>Here is an example of a <code>Car</code> class with attributes and methods:</p><code>class Car:\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year):\n        self.brand = brand\n        self.model = model\n        self.year = year\n        self.mileage = 0\n\n    def drive(self, miles):\n        self.mileage += miles\n\n    def describe_car(self):\n        print(f\'This car is a {self.year} {self.brand} {self.model} with {self.mileage} miles.\')</code><h3>Objects</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class. You can create multiple objects from a single class, and each object will have its own set of attributes and methods. To create an object, you use the class name followed by parentheses containing the required arguments.</p><p>For example, to create a <code>Car</code> object, you would use the following code:</p><code>my_car = Car(\'Toyota\', \'Corolla\', 2015)</code><p>This will create a new <code>Car</code> object called <code>my_car</code> with the specified attributes.</p><h3>Inheritance</h3><p>Inheritance is a mechanism that allows one class to inherit the attributes and methods of another class. The class that is being inherited from is called the parent class, while the class that is doing the inheriting is called the child class.</p><p>In Python, inheritance is implemented using the <code>(ParentClass)</code> syntax after the class name. For example:</p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, battery_size):\n        super().__init__(brand, model, year)\n        self.battery_size = battery_size\n\n    def describe_battery(self):\n        print(f\'This car has a {self.battery_size} kWh battery.\')</code><p>In this example, the <code>ElectricCar</code> class is inheriting from the <code>Car</code> class and adding an additional attribute and method.</p><h3>Benefits of OOP</h3><p>OOP provides several benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Code Reusability</strong>: OOP allows you to write code that can be reused in multiple places, reducing the amount of code that needs to be written and maintained.</li><li><strong>Easier Maintenance</strong>: OOP makes it easier to modify and extend code, as changes can be made at the class level rather than having to modify individual objects.</li><li><strong>Improved Readability</strong>: OOP promotes code organization and structure, making it easier to read and understand.</li></ul><p>By using classes, objects, and inheritance, you can create complex and robust programs that are easy to maintain and extend.</p>', 'Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
