(163, 176, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with Files in Python</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore how to work with files in Python. Files are a crucial part of any programming language, and Python provides several ways to interact with them. We will cover the basics of file input/output, reading and writing text files, and working with CSV files.</p><h3>File Input/Output</h3><p>Python provides several functions to perform file input/output operations. The most commonly used functions are <code>open()</code>, <code>read()</code>, <code>write()</code>, and <code>close()</code>. The <code>open()</code> function is used to open a file, and it returns a file object. The <code>read()</code> function is used to read the contents of a file, and the <code>write()</code> function is used to write data to a file. The <code>close()</code> function is used to close a file.</p><p>The <code>open()</code> function takes two arguments: the name of the file and the mode in which to open the file. The mode can be <code>\'r\'</code> for reading, <code>\'w\'</code> for writing, or <code>\'a\'</code> for appending. For example:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')</code><p>This will open a file named <code>example.txt</code> in read mode.</p><h3>Reading and Writing Text Files</h3><p>Once a file is opened, we can read or write data to it. To read data from a file, we can use the <code>read()</code> function. This function returns the contents of the file as a string.</p><p>For example:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')<br>data = file.read()<br>print(data)<br>file.close()</code><p>This will print the contents of the file to the console.</p><p>To write data to a file, we can use the <code>write()</code> function. This function takes a string as an argument and writes it to the file.</p><p>For example:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')<br>file.write(\'Hello, World!\')<br>file.close()</code><p>This will write the string <code>\'Hello, World!\'</code> to the file.</p><h3>Working with CSV Files</h3><p>CSV (Comma Separated Values) files are a type of text file that contains data separated by commas. Python provides a built-in module called <code>csv</code> to work with CSV files.</p><p>To read a CSV file, we can use the <code>reader()</code> function from the <code>csv</code> module. This function returns a reader object that we can use to iterate over the rows in the CSV file.</p><p>For example:</p><code>import csv<br>with open(\'example.csv\', \'r\') as file:<br>    reader = csv.reader(file)<br>    for row in reader:<br>        print(row)</code><p>This will print each row in the CSV file as a list.</p><p>To write data to a CSV file, we can use the <code>writer()</code> function from the <code>csv</code> module. This function returns a writer object that we can use to write rows to the CSV file.</p><p>For example:</p><code>import csv<br>with open(\'example.csv\', \'w\') as file:<br>    writer = csv.writer(file)<br>    writer.writerow([\'Name\', \'Age\'])<br>    writer.writerow([\'John\', 25])</code><p>This will write two rows to the CSV file.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><ul><li>Always close a file after you are done with it to free up system resources.</li><li>Use the <code>with</code> statement to open a file, as it will automatically close the file for you.</li><li>Use the <code>csv</code> module to work with CSV files, as it provides a convenient and efficient way to read and write CSV data.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can ensure that your code is efficient, readable, and maintainable.</p><p>In conclusion, working with files in Python is a crucial part of any programming task. By understanding how to read and write text files, and how to work with CSV files, you can perform a wide range of tasks, from data analysis to file processing. With practice and experience, you will become proficient in working with files in Python.</p>', 'Working with Files in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(164, 177, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python libraries</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+libraries/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+libraries/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Libraries</h3><p>Welcome to Day 23 of our Python course. Today, we will be exploring three important Python libraries: Requests, Beautiful Soup, and Scikit-learn. These libraries are widely used in web development, data scraping, and machine learning tasks.</p><h3>Requests Library</h3><p>The Requests library is used for making HTTP requests in Python. It allows you to send HTTP requests and returns the server’s response. This library is useful for web scraping, API interactions, and other tasks that require making HTTP requests.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use the Requests library to make a GET request:</p><code>import requests<br>response = requests.get(\'https://www.google.com\')<br>print(response.status_code)</code><p>In this example, we import the Requests library and use the <strong>get</strong> method to make a GET request to Google. The <strong>status_code</strong> attribute of the response object is then printed to the console.</p><h3>Beautiful Soup Library</h3><p>The Beautiful Soup library is used for parsing HTML and XML documents. It creates a parse tree from page source code that can be used to extract data in a hierarchical and more readable manner.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use the Beautiful Soup library to parse an HTML document:</p><code>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup<br>import requests<br>response = requests.get(\'https://www.google.com\')<br>soup = BeautifulSoup(response.text, \'html.parser\')<br>print(soup.title.string)</code><p>In this example, we import the Beautiful Soup library and use the <strong>requests</strong> library to make a GET request to Google. We then create a Beautiful Soup object and specify the parser library. Finally, we print the title of the HTML document.</p><h3>Scikit-learn Library</h3><p>The Scikit-learn library is a machine learning library for Python. It provides a wide range of algorithms for classification, regression, clustering, and other tasks.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use the Scikit-learn library to create a simple classifier:</p><code>from sklearn.datasets import load_iris<br>from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split<br>from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression<br>iris = load_iris()<br>X = iris.data<br>y = iris.target<br>X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2)<br>logreg = LogisticRegression()<br>logreg.fit(X_train, y_train)<br>print(logreg.score(X_test, y_test))</code><p>In this example, we import the necessary modules from Scikit-learn and load the iris dataset. We then split the dataset into training and testing sets, create a logistic regression classifier, and train the classifier using the training data. Finally, we print the accuracy of the classifier on the testing data.</p><p>These libraries are just a few examples of the many libraries available in Python. By mastering these libraries, you can perform a wide range of tasks, from web development to machine learning.</p><p>Some key concepts to take away from this lesson are:</p><ul><li>The Requests library is used for making HTTP requests in Python.</li><li>The Beautiful Soup library is used for parsing HTML and XML documents.</li><li>The Scikit-learn library is a machine learning library for Python.</li><li>These libraries can be used for a wide range of tasks, including web development, data scraping, and machine learning.</li></ul>', 'Introduction to Python Libraries', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(165, 178, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Data Structures in Python</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the fundamental data structures in Python, including Lists, Tuples, and Dictionaries. These data structures are essential for storing and manipulating data in Python.</p><h3>Lists</h3><p>A list is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are denoted by square brackets [] and are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of lists:</p><ul><li>Ordered: Lists maintain the order in which elements were added.</li><li>Indexed: List elements can be accessed using their index, which starts at 0.</li><li>Mutable: Lists can be modified after creation.</li><li>Dynamic size: Lists can grow or shrink dynamically as elements are added or removed.</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a list:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[0])  # Output: 1<br>my_list.append(6)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]</code><h3>Tuples</h3><p>A tuple is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are denoted by parentheses () and are immutable, meaning they cannot be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of tuples:</p><ul><li>Ordered: Tuples maintain the order in which elements were added.</li><li>Indexed: Tuple elements can be accessed using their index, which starts at 0.</li><li>Immutable: Tuples cannot be modified after creation.</li><li>Fixed size: Tuples have a fixed size that cannot be changed after creation.</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a tuple:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)<br>print(my_tuple[0])  # Output: 1<br># my_tuple[0] = 10  # This will raise an error because tuples are immutable</code><h3>Dictionaries</h3><p>A dictionary is a collection of key-value pairs where each key is unique and maps to a specific value. Dictionaries are denoted by curly brackets {} and are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of dictionaries:</p><ul><li>Unordered: Dictionaries do not maintain the order in which key-value pairs were added.</li><li>Key-value pairs: Dictionary elements are accessed using their key.</li><li>Mutable: Dictionaries can be modified after creation.</li><li>Dynamic size: Dictionaries can grow or shrink dynamically as key-value pairs are added or removed.</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a dictionary:</p><code>my_dict = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}<br>print(my_dict[\'name\'])  # Output: John<br>my_dict[\'country\'] = \'USA\'<br>print(my_dict)  # Output: {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \'country\': \'USA\'}</code><p>In summary, lists, tuples, and dictionaries are essential data structures in Python, each with their own strengths and weaknesses. Understanding how to use these data structures is crucial for any Python programmer.</p>', 'Data Structures in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(166, 179, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python control structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Control Structures in Python</h3><p>Control structures are used to control the flow of a program. They determine which parts of the code to execute, how many times to execute them, and in what order. In this lesson, we will cover three types of control structures in Python: If-Else statements, For loops, and While loops.</p><h3>If-Else Statements</h3><p>If-Else statements are used to execute different blocks of code based on certain conditions. The basic syntax of an If-Else statement is:</p><code>if condition:\n    # code to execute if condition is true\nelse:\n    # code to execute if condition is false</code><p>Here, the <strong>condition</strong> is a boolean expression that evaluates to either True or False. If the condition is True, the code under the <strong>if</strong> block will be executed. If the condition is False, the code under the <strong>else</strong> block will be executed.</p><p>For example:</p><code>x = 5\nif x > 10:\n    print(&quot;x is greater than 10&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(&quot;x is less than or equal to 10&quot;)</code><p>This will output: <strong>x is less than or equal to 10</strong></p><h3>For Loops</h3><p>For loops are used to execute a block of code repeatedly for a specified number of times. The basic syntax of a For loop is:</p><code>for variable in iterable:\n    # code to execute</code><p>Here, the <strong>variable</strong> is the name given to the item being processed in the current iteration, and the <strong>iterable</strong> is the sequence of items to be processed.</p><p>For example:</p><code>fruits = [&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;banana&quot;, &quot;cherry&quot;]\nfor fruit in fruits:\n    print(fruit)</code><p>This will output:</p><ul><li>apple</li><li>banana</li><li>cherry</li></ul><h3>While Loops</h3><p>While loops are used to execute a block of code repeatedly as long as a certain condition is true. The basic syntax of a While loop is:</p><code>while condition:\n    # code to execute</code><p>Here, the <strong>condition</strong> is a boolean expression that evaluates to either True or False. As long as the condition is True, the code under the <strong>while</strong> block will be executed.</p><p>For example:</p><code>x = 0\nwhile x < 5:\n    print(x)\n    x += 1</code><p>This will output:</p><ul><li>0</li><li>1</li><li>2</li><li>3</li><li>4</li></ul><p>In summary, control structures are essential in programming as they allow us to control the flow of a program, make decisions, and repeat tasks. If-Else statements are used for decision-making, For loops are used for iterating over sequences, and While loops are used for repeating tasks as long as a condition is true.</p>', 'Control Structures in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(167, 180, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python functions tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Functions in Python</h3><p>Functions are a crucial concept in programming, and Python is no exception. In this lesson, we will explore the world of functions in Python, including how to define them, work with function arguments, and understand return types.</p><h3>Defining Functions</h3><p>In Python, a function is defined using the <code>def</code> keyword followed by the function name and a list of parameters in parentheses. The code block within the function is denoted by indentation.</p><p>Here\'s a simple example of a function that prints a greeting message:</p><code>def greet(name):<br>    print(f\'Hello, {name}!\')</code><p>In this example, <code>greet</code> is the function name, and <code>name</code> is the parameter. The function takes a string argument and prints out a greeting message.</p><h3>Function Arguments</h3><p>Function arguments are the values passed to a function when it\'s called. In Python, you can pass arguments in several ways:</p><ul><li><strong>Positional arguments</strong>: These are the most common type of argument. You pass the values in the same order as the parameters in the function definition.</li><li><strong>Keyword arguments</strong>: You can pass arguments using the parameter name as a keyword. This allows you to pass arguments in any order.</li><li><strong>Default arguments</strong>: You can assign a default value to a parameter in the function definition. If no value is passed for that parameter, the default value is used.</li><li><strong>Variable-length arguments</strong>: You can use the <code>*args</code> syntax to pass a variable number of positional arguments, and the <code>**kwargs</code> syntax to pass a variable number of keyword arguments.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples:</p><code>def greet(name, age):<br>    print(f\'Hello, {name}! You are {age} years old.\')<br><br#greet(\'John\', 30)  # Positional arguments<br>greet(name=\'Jane\', age=25)  # Keyword arguments<br><br>def greet(name, age=30):<br>    print(f\'Hello, {name}! You are {age} years old.\')<br><br#greet(\'John\')  # Default argument<br><br>def greet(*args):<br>    for name in args:<br>        print(f\'Hello, {name}!\')<br><br#greet(\'John\', \'Jane\', \'Bob\')  # Variable-length arguments</code><h3>Return Types</h3><p>A function can return a value using the <code>return</code> statement. The return type can be any Python object, including integers, strings, lists, dictionaries, and more.</p><p>Here\'s an example of a function that returns a value:</p><code>def add(a, b):<br>    return a + b<br><br>result = add(2, 3)<br>print(result)  # Output: 5</code><p>In this example, the <code>add</code> function takes two integer arguments and returns their sum.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with functions in Python:</p><ul><li>Use descriptive function names and parameter names.</li><li>Keep functions short and focused on a single task.</li><li>Use default arguments and variable-length arguments when necessary.</li><li>Return values instead of printing them directly.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can write clean, readable, and maintainable code that\'s easy to understand and use.</p>', 'Functions in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(168, 181, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python oop concepts</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming in Python</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In this chapter, we will learn about the basics of OOP in Python, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behaviors of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class. For example:</p><code>class Car:</code><p>This class can have attributes (data) and methods (functions). Attributes are the data members of the class, while methods are the functions that belong to the class.</p><p>Here\'s an example of a class with attributes and methods:</p><code>class Car:<br>    def __init__(self, brand, model, year):<br>        self.brand = brand<br>        self.model = model<br>        self.year = year<br>    def print_details(self):<br>        print(f\'Brand: {self.brand}, Model: {self.model}, Year: {self.year}\')<br></code><p>In this example, the <code>Car</code> class has three attributes: <code>brand</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code>. The class also has a method called <code>print_details</code> that prints out the details of the car.</p><h3>Objects</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class. In other words, an object is a variable that has the properties and behaviors defined by its class. To create an object, we use the class name followed by parentheses containing the required arguments.</p><p>For example, to create an object of the <code>Car</code> class, we can do the following:</p><code>my_car = Car(\'Toyota\', \'Corolla\', 2015)</code><p>This creates a new object called <code>my_car</code> that has the attributes and methods defined by the <code>Car</code> class.</p><p>We can access the attributes and methods of the object using the dot notation. For example:</p><code>my_car.print_details()</code><p>This will print out the details of the car.</p><h3>Inheritance</h3><p>Inheritance is the mechanism by which one class can inherit the properties and behaviors of another class. The class that is being inherited from is called the parent or superclass, while the class that is doing the inheriting is called the child or subclass.</p><p>In Python, inheritance is implemented using the <code>(ParentClass)</code> syntax. For example:</p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):</code><p>This defines a new class called <code>ElectricCar</code> that inherits from the <code>Car</code> class.</p><p>The <code>ElectricCar</code> class has all the attributes and methods of the <code>Car</code> class, plus any additional attributes and methods that it defines itself.</p><p>Here\'s an example of a subclass that adds a new attribute and method:</p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):<br>    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, battery_size):<br>        super().__init__(brand, model, year)<br>        self.battery_size = battery_size<br>    def print_battery_size(self):<br>        print(f\'Battery Size: {self.battery_size}\')<br></code><p>In this example, the <code>ElectricCar</code> class adds a new attribute called <code>battery_size</code> and a new method called <code>print_battery_size</code>. The <code>super().__init__(brand, model, year)</code> line calls the constructor of the parent class to initialize the <code>brand</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code> attributes.</p><h3>Benefits of OOP</h3><p>OOP provides several benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Code Reusability</strong>: OOP allows us to write code that can be reused in multiple contexts, reducing code duplication and improving maintainability.</li><li><strong>Modularity</strong>: OOP allows us to break down complex systems into smaller, independent modules that can be developed and tested separately.</li><li><strong>Easier Debugging</strong>: OOP makes it easier to identify and fix errors, since each module or class has a specific responsibility and can be tested independently.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, OOP is a powerful programming paradigm that allows us to write more organized, maintainable, and efficient code. By using classes, objects, and inheritance, we can create complex systems that are easier to understand and modify.</p>', 'Object-Oriented Programming in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(169, 182, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Review and Practice</h3><p>Welcome to Day 28 of the Python course. Today, we will review the topics covered in the previous lessons and practice solving problems. This will help reinforce your understanding of the concepts and prepare you for more advanced topics.</p><h3>Review of Previous Topics</h3><p>In the previous lessons, we covered a range of topics, including variables, data types, control structures, functions, and modules. Let\'s take a quick look at each of these topics:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: We learned how to declare and assign values to variables using the assignment operator (=). We also learned about the different data types in Python, including integers, floats, strings, and booleans.</li><li><strong>Data Types</strong>: We covered the different data types in Python, including lists, tuples, dictionaries, and sets. We learned how to create and manipulate these data structures, and how to use them to store and retrieve data.</li><li><strong>Control Structures</strong>: We learned about the different control structures in Python, including if-else statements, for loops, and while loops. We learned how to use these structures to control the flow of our programs and make decisions based on conditions.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: We learned how to define and call functions in Python. We learned about the different types of functions, including built-in functions and user-defined functions, and how to use them to reuse code and simplify our programs.</li><li><strong>Modules</strong>: We learned about the different modules in Python, including the math module, the random module, and the time module. We learned how to import and use these modules to add functionality to our programs.</li></ul><h3>Practice Problems</h3><p>Now that we have reviewed the previous topics, let\'s practice solving some problems. Here are a few examples:</p><ul><li>Write a program that asks the user for their name and age, and then prints out a greeting message with their name and age.</li><li>Write a program that calculates the area and perimeter of a rectangle, given the length and width.</li><li>Write a program that simulates a coin toss, and then asks the user to guess the result.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how you might solve the first problem:</p><code>name = input(&quot;What is your name? &quot;)\nage = int(input(&quot;How old are you? &quot;))\nprint(&quot;Hello, &quot; + name + &quot;! You are &quot; + str(age) + &quot; years old.&quot;)</code><h3>Project Ideas</h3><p>Now that you have practiced solving some problems, let\'s think about some project ideas. Here are a few examples:</p><ul><li>Build a calculator that can perform basic arithmetic operations, such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.</li><li>Build a game that allows the user to guess a random number, and then provides feedback on their guess.</li><li>Build a program that simulates a bank account, and allows the user to deposit and withdraw money.</li></ul><p>Remember to choose a project that interests you, and to have fun with it. Don\'t be afraid to ask for help if you get stuck, and don\'t be afraid to try new things and experiment with different solutions.</p>', 'Review and Practice', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(170, 183, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to File Input/Output in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 29 of our Python course. Today, we will be exploring the world of file input/output in Python. File input/output is an essential aspect of programming, as it allows us to store and retrieve data from files. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of file handling, reading files, and writing files.</p><h3>File Handling</h3><p>File handling is the process of creating, reading, writing, and deleting files. Python provides several built-in functions and methods to perform these operations. To work with files in Python, we need to open them first. The <code>open()</code> function is used to open a file. It returns a file object, which we can use to read or write to the file.</p><p>The <code>open()</code> function takes two arguments: the name of the file and the mode in which we want to open the file. The mode can be <code>\'r\'</code> for reading, <code>\'w\'</code> for writing, or <code>\'a\'</code> for appending. If we want to open a file in read and write mode, we can use <code>\'r+\'</code> or <code>\'w+\'</code>.</p><ul><li><code>open(\'file.txt\', \'r\')</code> opens the file in read mode.</li><li><code>open(\'file.txt\', \'w\')</code> opens the file in write mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>open(\'file.txt\', \'a\')</code> opens the file in append mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li></ul><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>Once we have opened a file, we can read its contents using the <code>read()</code> method. This method returns the entire contents of the file as a string.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read a file:</p><code>file = open(\'file.txt\', \'r\')<br>contents = file.read()<br>print(contents)<br>file.close()</code><p>We can also read a file line by line using the <code>readline()</code> method. This method returns the next line in the file as a string.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read a file line by line:</p><code>file = open(\'file.txt\', \'r\')<br>while True:<br>    line = file.readline()<br>    if not line:<br>        break<br>    print(line.strip())<br>file.close()</code><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>To write to a file, we can use the <code>write()</code> method. This method takes a string as an argument and writes it to the file.</p><p>Here is an example of how to write to a file:</p><code>file = open(\'file.txt\', \'w\')<br>file.write(\'Hello, World!\')<br>file.close()</code><p>If we want to write multiple lines to a file, we can use the <code>writelines()</code> method. This method takes a list of strings as an argument and writes each string to the file as a separate line.</p><p>Here is an example of how to write multiple lines to a file:</p><code>lines = [\'Hello, World!\', \'This is a test.\', \'Goodbye, World!\']<br>file = open(\'file.txt\', \'w\')<br>file.writelines([line + \'\n\' for line in lines])<br>file.close()</code><p>It is <strong>very important</strong> to close the file after we are done with it. If we do not close the file, it may remain open and cause problems. We can close a file using the <code>close()</code> method.</p><p>Alternatively, we can use the <code>with</code> statement to open a file. The <code>with</code> statement automatically closes the file when we are done with it, even if an error occurs.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use the <code>with</code> statement:</p><code>with open(\'file.txt\', \'r\') as file:<br>    contents = file.read()<br>    print(contents)</code><p>In this example, the file is automatically closed when we are done with it, so we do not need to call the <code>close()</code> method.</p>', 'Introduction to File Input/Output in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(171, 184, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv json files</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+json+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+json+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with CSV and JSON Files in Python</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore how to work with CSV and JSON files in Python. These file formats are widely used for storing and exchanging data, and being able to read and write them is an essential skill for any Python developer.</p><h3>CSV Files</h3><p>A CSV (Comma Separated Values) file is a plain text file that stores tabular data. Each line of the file represents a row of data, and each value in the row is separated by a comma. CSV files are often used to import and export data from spreadsheets and databases.</p><p>To work with CSV files in Python, we use the <code>csv</code> module. This module provides functions for reading and writing CSV files. Here is an example of how to read a CSV file:</p><code>import csv<br/>with open(\'example.csv\', \'r\') as file:<br/>    reader = csv.reader(file)<br/>    for row in reader:<br/>        print(row)</code><p>This code opens the <code>example.csv</code> file and reads it row by row, printing each row to the console.</p><h3>Writing to a CSV File</h3><p>To write to a CSV file, we use the <code>csv.writer</code> function. Here is an example:</p><code>import csv<br/>with open(\'example.csv\', \'w\', newline=\'\') as file:<br/>    writer = csv.writer(file)<br/>    writer.writerow([\'Name\', \'Age\', \'City\'])<br/>    writer.writerow([\'John\', 25, \'New York\'])<br/>    writer.writerow([\'Alice\', 30, \'Los Angeles\'])</code><p>This code opens the <code>example.csv</code> file and writes three rows of data to it.</p><h3>JSON Files</h3><p>A JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) file is a text file that stores data in a format that is easy for humans to read and write and easy for machines to parse. JSON files are often used to exchange data between web servers and web applications.</p><p>To work with JSON files in Python, we use the <code>json</code> module. This module provides functions for reading and writing JSON files. Here is an example of how to read a JSON file:</p><code>import json<br/>with open(\'example.json\', \'r\') as file:<br/>    data = json.load(file)<br/>    print(data)</code><p>This code opens the <code>example.json</code> file and reads the data into a Python object.</p><h3>Writing to a JSON File</h3><p>To write to a JSON file, we use the <code>json.dump</code> function. Here is an example:</p><code>import json<br/>data = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 25, \'city\': \'New York\'}<br/>with open(\'example.json\', \'w\') as file:<br/>    json.dump(data, file)</code><p>This code opens the <code>example.json</code> file and writes the <code>data</code> object to it.</p><h3>Data Parsing</h3><p>Data parsing is the process of converting data from one format to another. In the context of CSV and JSON files, data parsing involves converting the data from the file format to a Python object that we can work with.</p><p>The <code>csv</code> and <code>json</code> modules provide functions for parsing data from CSV and JSON files. We have already seen examples of how to use these functions to read and write CSV and JSON files.</p><h3>Common Use Cases</h3><p>Here are some common use cases for working with CSV and JSON files in Python:</p><ul><li><strong>Data Import/Export</strong>: CSV and JSON files are often used to import and export data from spreadsheets and databases.</li><li><strong>Web Development</strong>: JSON files are often used to exchange data between web servers and web applications.</li><li><strong>Data Analysis</strong>: CSV files are often used to store and analyze data in data analysis and science applications.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, working with CSV and JSON files is an essential skill for any Python developer. The <code>csv</code> and <code>json</code> modules provide functions for reading and writing these file formats, and data parsing is the process of converting data from one format to another.</p>', 'Working with CSV and JSON Files in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
