(172, 185, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python error handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+error+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+error+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Error Handling in Python</h3><p>Error handling is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to anticipate and manage errors that may occur during the execution of your code, preventing it from crashing and providing a better user experience instead. In this lesson, we will explore the concepts of try-except blocks, error types, and debugging in Python.</p><h3>Try-Except Blocks</h3><p>A try-except block is a mechanism in Python that allows you to enclose a block of code that might raise an exception. If an exception occurs, the code in the except block will be executed. The basic syntax of a try-except block is as follows:</p><code>try:\n    # code that might raise an exception\nexcept ExceptionType:\n    # code to handle the exception\n</code><p>The <strong>try</strong> block contains the code that might raise an exception, while the <strong>except</strong> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception occurs. You can have multiple except blocks to handle different types of exceptions.</p><h3>Error Types</h3><p>Python has a built-in hierarchy of exceptions. The base class for all exceptions is <strong>BaseException</strong>, which has two main subclasses: <strong>SystemExit</strong> and <strong>Exception</strong>. The <strong>Exception</strong> class is further divided into several subclasses, including:</p><ul><li><strong>TypeError</strong>: raised when a variable is not of the expected type</li><li><strong>ValueError</strong>: raised when a function or operation receives an argument with an incorrect value</li><li><strong>ZeroDivisionError</strong>: raised when the divisor is zero in a division operation</li><li><strong>IndexError</strong>: raised when a sequence is indexed out of range</li><li><strong>KeyError</strong>: raised when a key is not found in a dictionary</li></ul><p>Understanding the different types of exceptions is essential to writing effective error handling code.</p><h3>Debugging</h3><p>Debugging is the process of identifying and fixing errors in your code. Python provides several tools to help you debug your code, including:</p><ul><li><strong>print()</strong> statements: can be used to print the values of variables at different points in your code</li><li><strong>pdb</strong> module: provides a built-in debugger that allows you to step through your code, set breakpoints, and inspect variables</li><li><strong>logging</strong> module: allows you to log events and errors in your code, making it easier to diagnose issues</li></ul><p>By using these tools, you can identify and fix errors in your code, making it more reliable and efficient.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when using try-except blocks and debugging:</p><ul><li><strong>Keep try blocks short</strong>: try to keep the code in the try block as short as possible to make it easier to identify the source of the exception</li><li><strong>Handle specific exceptions</strong>: instead of catching the general <strong>Exception</strong> class, try to catch specific exceptions that you anticipate might occur</li><li><strong>Provide informative error messages</strong>: when handling an exception, provide an informative error message that will help the user understand what went wrong</li><li><strong>Test your code thoroughly</strong>: test your code with different inputs and scenarios to ensure that it works as expected and handles errors correctly</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can write more robust and reliable code that handles errors effectively.</p>', 'Understanding Error Handling in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(173, 186, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python modules and packages</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+modules+and+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+modules+and+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Modules and Packages</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the world of Python modules and packages. Modules are pre-written code that can be imported into our programs to perform specific tasks, while packages are collections of related modules. We will learn how to import modules, create packages, and use module functions to enhance our programming skills.</p><h3>Importing Modules</h3><p>Python has a vast collection of modules that can be easily imported into our programs. To import a module, we use the <code>import</code> statement followed by the name of the module. For example, to import the <code>math</code> module, we would use the following code:</p><code>import math</code><p>Once a module is imported, we can access its functions and variables using the dot notation. For instance, to use the <code>sin</code> function from the <code>math</code> module, we would use <code>math.sin()</code>.</p><p>There are several ways to import modules in Python:</p><ul><li><code>import module_name</code>: This imports the entire module and makes all its functions and variables available.</li><li><code>from module_name import function_name</code>: This imports a specific function or variable from the module.</li><li><code>from module_name import *</code>: This imports all functions and variables from the module, making them available for use without the need for the dot notation.</li></ul><h3>Creating Packages</h3><p>A package is a collection of related modules that can be easily distributed and installed. To create a package, we need to create a directory with an <code>__init__.py</code> file inside it. The <code>__init__.py</code> file can be empty, but it\'s used to indicate that the directory should be treated as a package.</p><p>For example, let\'s say we want to create a package called <code>my_package</code> with two modules: <code>module1.py</code> and <code>module2.py</code>. We would create the following directory structure:</p><ul><li><code>my_package</code></li><li>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>__init__.py</code></li><li>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>module1.py</code></li><li>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>module2.py</code></li></ul><p>Once we have created the package, we can import its modules using the dot notation. For instance, to import <code>module1</code> from <code>my_package</code>, we would use <code>from my_package import module1</code>.</p><h3>Module Functions</h3><p>Modules can contain functions that can be used to perform specific tasks. For example, the <code>math</code> module has functions like <code>sin</code>, <code>cos</code>, and <code>tan</code> that can be used to perform trigonometric operations.</p><p>To use a module function, we need to import the module and then call the function using the dot notation. For instance, to use the <code>sin</code> function from the <code>math</code> module, we would use the following code:</p><code>import math<br>x = math.sin(30)</code><p>Some modules also have variables that can be used to access specific values. For example, the <code>math</code> module has a variable called <code>pi</code> that contains the value of pi.</p><p>Modules can also be used to organize our code and make it more reusable. By putting related functions and variables into a module, we can easily import and use them in other parts of our program.</p><p>In summary, Python modules and packages are powerful tools that can help us write more efficient and organized code. By importing modules and using their functions and variables, we can perform a wide range of tasks and enhance our programming skills.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Modules are pre-written code that can be imported into our programs to perform specific tasks.</li><li>Packages are collections of related modules that can be easily distributed and installed.</li><li>We can import modules using the <code>import</code> statement, and access their functions and variables using the dot notation.</li><li>We can create packages by creating a directory with an <code>__init__.py</code> file inside it.</li><li>Modules can contain functions and variables that can be used to perform specific tasks.</li></ul>', 'Exploring Python Modules and Packages', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(174, 187, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: matplotlib data visualization</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=matplotlib+data+visualization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/matplotlib+data+visualization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/matplotlib+data+visualization/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/matplotlib+data+visualization/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=matplotlib+data+visualization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Data Visualization with Matplotlib</h3><p>Welcome to Day 33 of our Python course, where we will explore the world of data visualization using Matplotlib. Data visualization is a crucial step in data analysis, as it allows us to communicate complex data insights in a clear and concise manner. Matplotlib is a popular Python library used for creating static, animated, and interactive visualizations in Python.</p><h3>Matplotlib Basics</h3><p>Before we dive into the world of data visualization, let\'s cover the basics of Matplotlib. Matplotlib is a comprehensive library that provides a wide range of visualization tools, including line plots, scatter plots, histograms, and more. To get started with Matplotlib, you need to import the library and create a figure and axis object.</p><p>Here\'s a simple example of how to create a line plot using Matplotlib:</p><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\ny = [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]\nplt.plot(x, y)\nplt.show()</code><p>This code will create a simple line plot with the x-values on the x-axis and the corresponding y-values on the y-axis.</p><h3>Plotting Functions</h3><p>Matplotlib provides a wide range of plotting functions that can be used to create different types of visualizations. Some of the most commonly used plotting functions include:</p><ul><li><strong>plt.plot()</strong>: Creates a line plot</li><li><strong>plt.scatter()</strong>: Creates a scatter plot</li><li><strong>plt.bar()</strong>: Creates a bar chart</li><li><strong>plt.hist()</strong>: Creates a histogram</li><li><strong>plt.pie()</strong>: Creates a pie chart</li></ul><p>Each of these functions has its own set of parameters that can be used to customize the appearance of the plot. For example, you can use the <strong>label</strong> parameter to add labels to your plot, or the <strong>title</strong> parameter to add a title to your plot.</p><p>Here\'s an example of how to create a scatter plot using Matplotlib:</p><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\ny = [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]\nplt.scatter(x, y)\nplt.xlabel(\'X-axis\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Y-axis\')\nplt.title(\'Scatter Plot\')\nplt.show()</code><h3>Customization</h3><p>One of the most powerful features of Matplotlib is its ability to customize the appearance of your plots. You can use a wide range of options to change the colors, fonts, and layouts of your plots. For example, you can use the <strong>plt.style.use()</strong> function to change the style of your plot, or the <strong>plt.rcParams</strong> dictionary to change the default settings of your plot.</p><p>Here\'s an example of how to customize the appearance of a line plot:</p><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\ny = [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]\nplt.plot(x, y, color=\'red\', linewidth=2, label=\'Line Plot\')\nplt.xlabel(\'X-axis\', fontsize=14)\nplt.ylabel(\'Y-axis\', fontsize=14)\nplt.title(\'Customized Line Plot\', fontsize=18)\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()</code><p>In this example, we\'ve changed the color of the line to red, increased the line width to 2, and added labels and a title to the plot. We\'ve also changed the font sizes of the labels and title to make them more readable.</p><p>In conclusion, Matplotlib is a powerful library that provides a wide range of tools for creating high-quality visualizations in Python. By mastering the basics of Matplotlib and learning how to customize the appearance of your plots, you can create informative and engaging visualizations that help to communicate complex data insights.</p>', 'Introduction to Data Visualization with Matplotlib', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(175, 188, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: pandas logo</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=pandas+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/pandas+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/pandas+logo/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/pandas+logo/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=pandas+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Pandas for Data Manipulation</h3><p>Welcome to Day 34 of our Python course, where we will be exploring the powerful library of Pandas for data manipulation. Pandas is a popular and widely-used library in the data science community, providing data structures and functions to efficiently handle structured data, including tabular data such as spreadsheets and SQL tables.</p><h3>Pandas Basics</h3><p>Pandas is built on top of the Python programming language and is particularly suited for data analysis and manipulation. The core data structures in Pandas are Series (1-dimensional labeled array) and DataFrames (2-dimensional labeled data structure with columns of potentially different types).</p><p>To start working with Pandas, you need to import the library. You can do this by using the following code: <code>import pandas as pd</code>. The <strong>as pd</strong> part assigns the alias <strong>pd</strong> to the Pandas library, making it easier to use its functions and classes.</p><h3>DataFrames</h3><p>A DataFrame is a 2-dimensional labeled data structure with columns of potentially different types. It is similar to an Excel spreadsheet or a table in a relational database. You can create a DataFrame from a dictionary, where the keys are the column names and the values are lists of data.</p><p>Here is an example of creating a DataFrame: <code>data = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\', \'Linda\'], \'Age\': [28, 24, 35, 32], \'Country\': [\'USA\', \'UK\', \'Australia\', \'Germany\']}; df = pd.DataFrame(data)</code>. This will create a DataFrame with three columns (Name, Age, and Country) and four rows.</p><p>You can also use the <code>head()</code> function to display the first few rows of the DataFrame, and the <code>info()</code> function to display a concise summary of the DataFrame.</p><h3>Data Cleaning</h3><p>Data cleaning is an essential step in the data analysis process. It involves removing or correcting invalid, inconsistent, or duplicate data. Pandas provides several functions to clean and preprocess data, including <code>dropna()</code> to remove rows with missing values, <code>drop_duplicates()</code> to remove duplicate rows, and <code>fillna()</code> to fill missing values.</p><p>Here are some examples of data cleaning functions: <ul><li><code>df.dropna()</code>: Remove rows with missing values.</li><li><code>df.drop_duplicates()</code>: Remove duplicate rows.</li><li><code>df.fillna(\'Unknown\')</code>: Fill missing values with the string \'Unknown\'.</li></ul></p><p>In addition to these functions, Pandas also provides several other functions for data manipulation, such as <code>groupby()</code> to group data by one or more columns, <code>sort_values()</code> to sort data by one or more columns, and <code>pivot_table()</code> to create a spreadsheet-style pivot table.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we have covered the basics of Pandas, including DataFrames and data cleaning. We have also seen how to create a DataFrame, display its contents, and perform basic data cleaning operations. With practice and experience, you will become proficient in using Pandas for data manipulation and analysis.</p>', 'Working with Pandas for Data Manipulation', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(176, 189, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: pandas data analysis</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=pandas+data+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/pandas+data+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/pandas+data+analysis/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/pandas+data+analysis/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=pandas+data+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Practicing with Advanced Pandas Functions</h3><p>Welcome to Day 35 of our Python course, where we will be exploring advanced Pandas functions. In this lesson, we will cover GroupBy functions, merging DataFrames, and data analysis.</p><h3>GroupBy Functions</h3><p>GroupBy functions are used to group data by one or more columns and perform operations on those groups. The <code>groupby()</code> function is used to group data, and then we can use various functions such as <code>mean()</code>, <code>sum()</code>, <code>max()</code>, and <code>min()</code> to perform calculations on the grouped data.</p><p>For example, let\'s say we have a DataFrame with sales data and we want to calculate the total sales by region:</p><code>import pandas as pd\n\ndata = {\'Region\': [\'North\', \'North\', \'South\', \'South\', \'East\', \'East\'],\n        \'Sales\': [100, 200, 50, 75, 150, 225]}\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\n\ngrouped_df = df.groupby(\'Region\')[\'Sales\'].sum()\n\nprint(grouped_df)</code><p>This will output the total sales for each region.</p><h3>Merging DataFrames</h3><p>Merging DataFrames is used to combine two or more DataFrames based on a common column. There are several types of merges, including inner merge, left merge, right merge, and outer merge.</p><p>For example, let\'s say we have two DataFrames, one with customer information and one with order information, and we want to merge them based on the customer ID:</p><code>import pandas as pd\n\ncustomer_data = {\'CustomerID\': [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\n                  \'Name\': [\'John\', \'Jane\', \'Bob\', \'Alice\', \'Mike\']}\n\norder_data = {\'CustomerID\': [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\n               \'OrderID\': [101, 102, 103, 104, 105]}\n\ncustomer_df = pd.DataFrame(customer_data)\n\norder_df = pd.DataFrame(order_data)\n\nmerged_df = pd.merge(customer_df, order_df, on=\'CustomerID\')\n\nprint(merged_df)</code><p>This will output a new DataFrame with the customer information and order information merged together.</p><h3>Data Analysis</h3><p>Data analysis is the process of extracting insights and patterns from data. Pandas provides several functions for data analysis, including <code>describe()</code>, <code>corr()</code>, and <code>pivot_table()</code>.</p><p>For example, let\'s say we have a DataFrame with exam scores and we want to calculate the mean, median, and standard deviation of the scores:</p><code>import pandas as pd\n\ndata = {\'Score\': [90, 80, 70, 60, 50]}\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\n\nprint(df.describe())</code><p>This will output a summary of the exam scores, including the mean, median, and standard deviation.</p><p>In this lesson, we covered GroupBy functions, merging DataFrames, and data analysis using Pandas. These functions are essential for data manipulation and analysis, and are used extensively in data science and scientific computing.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>GroupBy functions are used to group data by one or more columns and perform operations on those groups.</li><li>Merging DataFrames is used to combine two or more DataFrames based on a common column.</li><li>Data analysis is the process of extracting insights and patterns from data using Pandas functions such as <code>describe()</code>, <code>corr()</code>, and <code>pivot_table()</code>.</li></ul>', 'Practicing with Advanced Pandas Functions', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(177, 190, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to File Input/Output in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 36 of our Python course. Today, we will be exploring the world of file input/output in Python. File input/output is a crucial aspect of programming, as it allows us to store and retrieve data from files. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of reading and writing files in Python.</p><h3>What is File Input/Output?</h3><p>File input/output refers to the process of reading and writing data to files. Files can be thought of as containers that store data in a persistent manner, meaning that the data remains even after the program has finished executing. In Python, we can work with files using the built-in <code>open()</code> function, which returns a file object.</p><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>To read a file in Python, we use the <code>open()</code> function in read mode (<code>\'r\'</code>). The <code>open()</code> function takes two arguments: the name of the file and the mode in which to open the file. For example:</p><ul><li><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')</code> opens a file named <code>example.txt</code> in read mode.</li><li><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r+\')</code> opens a file named <code>example.txt</code> in read and write mode.</li></ul><p>Once we have opened the file, we can read its contents using the <code>read()</code> method. For example:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')\ncontents = file.read()\nprint(contents)</code><p>This code opens a file named <code>example.txt</code>, reads its contents, and prints them to the console.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>To write to a file in Python, we use the <code>open()</code> function in write mode (<code>\'w\'</code>) or append mode (<code>\'a\'</code>). For example:</p><ul><li><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')</code> opens a file named <code>example.txt</code> in write mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file does exist, its contents will be overwritten.</li><li><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'a\')</code> opens a file named <code>example.txt</code> in append mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file does exist, new data will be appended to the end of the file.</li></ul><p>Once we have opened the file, we can write to it using the <code>write()</code> method. For example:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')\nfile.write(\'Hello, World!\')\nfile.close()</code><p>This code opens a file named <code>example.txt</code>, writes the string <code>\'Hello, World!\'</code> to it, and then closes the file.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>When working with files in Python, it is essential to follow best practices to avoid errors and ensure that your code is efficient and secure. Here are some best practices to keep in mind:</p><ul><li>Always close files after you are finished with them to free up system resources.</li><li>Use the <code>with</code> statement to ensure that files are properly closed, even if an error occurs.</li><li>Be cautious when working with files in write mode, as you can easily overwrite existing data.</li><li>Use try-except blocks to handle errors that may occur when working with files.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can ensure that your code is reliable, efficient, and secure.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we have covered the basics of file input/output in Python. We have learned how to read and write files using the <code>open()</code> function, and we have discussed best practices for working with files. With this knowledge, you can start working with files in your own Python programs.</p><p>Remember to practice what you have learned today, and don\'t hesitate to ask if you have any questions. In the next lesson, we will continue to explore the world of file input/output in Python.</p>', 'Introduction to File Input/Output in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(178, 191, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv files</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with CSV Files in Python</h3><p>CSV (Comma Separated Values) files are widely used for storing and exchanging data between different applications. In this lesson, we will learn how to work with CSV files in Python, including reading and writing CSV files.</p><h3>What are CSV Files?</h3><p>CSV files are plain text files that contain data separated by commas. Each line in a CSV file represents a row of data, and each comma-separated value represents a column. CSV files are often used to import and export data from spreadsheets, databases, and other applications.</p><h3>Reading CSV Files</h3><p>To read a CSV file in Python, we can use the built-in <code>csv</code> module. Here is an example of how to read a CSV file:</p><ul><li>Import the <code>csv</code> module: <code>import csv</code></li><li>Open the CSV file in read mode: <code>with open(\'example.csv\', \'r\') as file</code></li><li>Create a CSV reader object: <code>reader = csv.reader(file)</code></li><li>Iterate over the rows in the CSV file: <code>for row in reader</code></li></ul><p>Here is the complete code:</p><code>import csv<br>with open(\'example.csv\', \'r\') as file:<br>    reader = csv.reader(file)<br>    for row in reader:<br>        print(row)</code><h3>Writing CSV Files</h3><p>To write a CSV file in Python, we can use the built-in <code>csv</code> module. Here is an example of how to write a CSV file:</p><ul><li>Import the <code>csv</code> module: <code>import csv</code></li><li>Open the CSV file in write mode: <code>with open(\'example.csv\', \'w\', newline=\'\') as file</code></li><li>Create a CSV writer object: <code>writer = csv.writer(file)</code></li><li>Write rows to the CSV file: <code>writer.writerow([1, 2, 3])</code></li></ul><p>Here is the complete code:</p><code>import csv<br>with open(\'example.csv\', \'w\', newline=\'\') as file:<br>    writer = csv.writer(file)<br>    writer.writerow([1, 2, 3])<br>    writer.writerow([4, 5, 6])</code><h3>Best Practices for Working with CSV Files</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with CSV files:</p><ul><li><strong>Use the correct delimiter</strong>: Make sure to use the correct delimiter (e.g. comma, tab, semicolon) when reading and writing CSV files.</li><li><strong>Handle quotes and escapes</strong>: Be aware of how quotes and escapes are handled in CSV files, and use the correct options when reading and writing CSV files.</li><li><strong>Use newline=\'\' when writing CSV files</strong>: To avoid issues with newline characters, use the <code>newline=\'\'</code> option when opening the CSV file in write mode.</li></ul><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we learned how to work with CSV files in Python, including reading and writing CSV files. We also covered some best practices to keep in mind when working with CSV files.</p>', 'Working with CSV Files in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(179, 192, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python json data</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+json+data\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+json+data\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+json+data/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+json+data/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+json+data\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding JSON Data in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 38 of our Python course, where we will be exploring the world of JSON data. JSON, or JavaScript Object Notation, is a lightweight data interchange format that is widely used for exchanging data between web servers, web applications, and mobile apps.</p><p>JSON data is easy to read and write, and it is language-independent, making it a popular choice for data exchange. In this lesson, we will learn how to work with JSON data in Python, including parsing JSON data and generating JSON data.</p><h3>What is JSON Data?</h3><p>JSON data is a text-based format that represents data as key-value pairs, arrays, and objects. It is similar to Python dictionaries, but with a few key differences. Here are some key features of JSON data:</p><ul><li><strong>Key-value pairs</strong>: JSON data is made up of key-value pairs, where each key is a string and each value can be a string, number, boolean, array, or object.</li><li><strong>Arrays</strong>: JSON arrays are ordered collections of values, which can be of any data type, including strings, numbers, booleans, and objects.</li><li><strong>Objects</strong>: JSON objects are unordered collections of key-value pairs, where each key is a string and each value can be of any data type.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of JSON data:</p><code>{\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30, \"city\": \"New York\"}</code><p>This JSON object has three key-value pairs: \"name\" with value \"John\", \"age\" with value 30, and \"city\" with value \"New York\".</p><h3>Parsing JSON Data</h3><p>To work with JSON data in Python, we need to parse it into a Python object. We can use the <code>json</code> module to parse JSON data. Here is an example:</p><code>import json<br/>json_data = \'{\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30, \"city\": \"New York\"}\'<br/>data = json.loads(json_data)<br/>print(data)</code><p>This code imports the <code>json</code> module, defines a string of JSON data, and then uses the <code>loads</code> function to parse the JSON data into a Python dictionary. The resulting dictionary is then printed to the console.</p><p>The <code>loads</code> function can also handle more complex JSON data, such as arrays and nested objects. For example:</p><code>import json<br/>json_data = \'{\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30, \"city\": \"New York\", \"hobbies\": [\"reading\", \"running\", \"swimming\"]}\'<br/>data = json.loads(json_data)<br/>print(data)</code><p>This code parses a JSON object with a nested array of hobbies.</p><h3>Generating JSON Data</h3><p>To generate JSON data in Python, we can use the <code>dumps</code> function from the <code>json</code> module. Here is an example:</p><code>import json<br/>data = {\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30, \"city\": \"New York\"}<br/>json_data = json.dumps(data)<br/>print(json_data)</code><p>This code defines a Python dictionary, and then uses the <code>dumps</code> function to convert the dictionary into a JSON string. The resulting JSON string is then printed to the console.</p><p>The <code>dumps</code> function can also handle more complex data structures, such as arrays and nested objects. For example:</p><code>import json<br/>data = {\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30, \"city\": \"New York\", \"hobbies\": [\"reading\", \"running\", \"swimming\"]}<br/>json_data = json.dumps(data)<br/>print(json_data)</code><p>This code generates a JSON object with a nested array of hobbies.</p><p>In conclusion, working with JSON data in Python is easy and straightforward. We can use the <code>json</code> module to parse JSON data into Python objects, and to generate JSON data from Python objects. With this knowledge, you can now work with JSON data in your own Python projects.</p>', 'Understanding JSON Data in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(180, 193, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Practicing File Input/Output with Exercises</h3><p>Welcome to Day 39 of our Python course. Today, we will be practicing file input/output with exercises. File input/output is an essential concept in programming, and it\'s crucial to understand how to work with files in Python. In this lesson, we will cover file input/output exercises, error handling, and best practices.</p><h3>File Input/Output Exercises</h3><p>Let\'s start with some exercises to get you familiar with file input/output in Python. Below are a few examples:</p><ul><li><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Write a Python program to read a file and print its contents.</li><li><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Write a Python program to write a string to a file.</li><li><strong>Exercise 3:</strong> Write a Python program to append a string to a file.</li></ul><p>Here are the solutions to the above exercises:</p><ul><li><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> <code>with open(\'file.txt\', \'r\') as f: print(f.read())</code></li><li><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> <code>with open(\'file.txt\', \'w\') as f: f.write(\'Hello, World!\')</code></li><li><strong>Exercise 3:</strong> <code>with open(\'file.txt\', \'a\') as f: f.write(\'This is appended text.\')</code></li></ul><h3>Error Handling</h3><p>Error handling is crucial when working with files in Python. If a file does not exist or cannot be opened for some reason, Python will raise an error. We can use try/except blocks to handle these errors. Here is an example:</p><p><code>try: with open(\'file.txt\', \'r\') as f: print(f.read()) except FileNotFoundError: print(\'The file does not exist.\') except Exception as e: print(\'An error occurred:\', e)</code></p><p>In the above example, we are trying to open a file and read its contents. If the file does not exist, a <code>FileNotFoundError</code> is raised, and we print an error message. If any other error occurs, we catch it with the <code>Exception</code> class and print the error message.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with files in Python:</p><ul><li><strong>Always close files:</strong> It\'s essential to close files after you are done with them to free up system resources. You can use the <code>with</code> statement to ensure that files are closed automatically.</li><li><strong>Use try/except blocks:</strong> Use try/except blocks to handle errors that may occur when working with files.</li><li><strong>Check file existence:</strong> Before trying to open a file, check if it exists to avoid errors.</li><li><strong>Specify file modes:</strong> Always specify the file mode when opening a file to avoid unexpected behavior.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can ensure that your file input/output code is robust and reliable.</p>', 'Practicing File Input/Output with Exercises', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(181, 194, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python pickling tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+pickling+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+pickling+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+pickling+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+pickling+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+pickling+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Pickling in Python</h3><p>Pickling is a process in Python where you can convert Python objects into a byte stream. This byte stream can then be stored in a file or database, and later reconstructed into a Python object. Pickling is a way to achieve object serialization, which is the process of converting an object into a format that can be written to a file or sent over a network.</p><h3>Why Pickling is Useful</h3><p>Pickling is useful for several reasons. It allows you to save complex Python objects, such as lists, dictionaries, and class instances, to a file. You can then load these objects back into your Python program, and they will be in the same state as when you saved them. Pickling is also useful for caching the results of expensive computations, so that you don\'t have to repeat them every time your program runs.</p><h3>How to Pickle an Object</h3><p>To pickle an object, you use the <code>pickle.dump()</code> function. This function takes two arguments: the object you want to pickle, and a file-like object that the pickled object will be written to. Here is an example of how to pickle a list:</p><code>import pickle\nmy_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nwith open(\'my_list.pkl\', \'wb\') as f:\n    pickle.dump(my_list, f)</code><p>In this example, we first import the <code>pickle</code> module. We then create a list called <code>my_list</code> and open a file called <code>my_list.pkl</code> in binary write mode. We then use the <code>pickle.dump()</code> function to pickle the list and write it to the file.</p><h3>How to Unpickle an Object</h3><p>To unpickle an object, you use the <code>pickle.load()</code> function. This function takes a file-like object as an argument, and returns the unpickled object. Here is an example of how to unpickle a list:</p><code>import pickle\nwith open(\'my_list.pkl\', \'rb\') as f:\n    my_list = pickle.load(f)\nprint(my_list)</code><p>In this example, we first import the <code>pickle</code> module. We then open the file <code>my_list.pkl</code> in binary read mode, and use the <code>pickle.load()</code> function to unpickle the list. We then print the list to the console.</p><h3>Serialization</h3><p>Serialization is the process of converting an object into a format that can be written to a file or sent over a network. Pickling is one way to achieve serialization in Python. There are other ways to achieve serialization, such as using the <code>json</code> module to convert objects to JSON, or using a library like <code>msgpack</code> to convert objects to a binary format.</p><h3>Best Practices for Pickling</h3><ul><li><strong>Only unpickle data from trusted sources</strong>: Unpickling data from an untrusted source can pose a security risk, as it can execute arbitrary code.</li><li><strong>Use the latest version of the pickle protocol</strong>: The pickle protocol has changed over time, and using the latest version can help ensure compatibility with future versions of Python.</li><li><strong>Avoid pickling large objects</strong>: Pickling large objects can be slow and use a lot of memory.</li></ul><p>Pickling is a powerful tool in Python, and can be used to achieve object serialization. However, it should be used with caution, and only with trusted data. By following best practices and using the latest version of the pickle protocol, you can safely and effectively use pickling in your Python programs.</p>', 'Learning about Pickling in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(182, 195, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python os module</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+os+module\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+os+module\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+os+module/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+os+module/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+os+module\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to the OS Module in Python</h3><p>The OS module in Python provides a way to use operating system dependent functionality. This module offers a portable way to use operating system dependent functionality. The OS module allows you to interact with the file system, processes, and environment variables.</p><h3>File System Interactions</h3><p>The OS module provides several functions to interact with the file system. You can use the <code>os.getcwd()</code> function to get the current working directory. The <code>os.chdir()</code> function is used to change the current working directory.</p><ul><li><code>os.mkdir()</code> : Create a new directory</li><li><code>os.rmdir()</code> : Remove an empty directory</li><li><code>os.rename()</code> : Rename a file or directory</li><li><code>os.remove()</code> : Remove a file</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use these functions:</p><code>import os\n# Get the current working directory\nprint(os.getcwd())\n# Create a new directory\nos.mkdir(\'new_directory\')\n# Change the current working directory\nos.chdir(\'new_directory\')\n# Get the current working directory\nprint(os.getcwd())\n# Remove the directory</code><h3>Process Management</h3><p>The OS module also provides several functions to manage processes. You can use the <code>os.system()</code> function to execute a system command. The <code>os.execv()</code> function is used to execute a new program, replacing the current process image.</p><ul><li><code>os.system()</code> : Execute a system command</li><li><code>os.execv()</code> : Execute a new program, replacing the current process image</li><li><code>os.fork()</code> : Create a new process by duplicating an existing one</li><li><code>os.kill()</code> : Send a signal to a process</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use these functions:</p><code>import os\n# Execute a system command\nos.system(\'ls -l\')\n# Create a new process by duplicating an existing one\npid = os.fork()\nif pid == 0:\n    # This is the child process\n    print(\'This is the child process\')\nelse:\n    # This is the parent process\n    print(\'This is the parent process\')</code><h3>Environment Variables</h3><p>The OS module provides several functions to interact with environment variables. You can use the <code>os.environ</code> dictionary to access and modify environment variables.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use these functions:</p><code>import os\n# Get the value of an environment variable\nprint(os.environ[\'PATH\'])\n# Set the value of an environment variable\nos.environ[\'VARIABLE\'] = \'value\'\n# Get the value of an environment variable\nprint(os.environ[\'VARIABLE\'])</code><p>In this chapter, we have learned about the OS module in Python and how to use it to interact with the file system, processes, and environment variables. We have also seen examples of how to use the functions provided by the OS module.</p>', 'Exploring the OS Module in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(183, 196, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Reviewing File Input/Output Concepts</h3><p>Welcome to Day 42 of our Python course. Today, we will review the concepts of file input/output that we have covered so far. File input/output is an essential aspect of programming, as it allows us to store and retrieve data from files.</p><p>In Python, we can use the built-in <code>open()</code> function to open a file and perform various operations such as reading, writing, and appending. The <code>open()</code> function returns a file object, which we can use to interact with the file.</p><h3>File Modes</h3><p>When opening a file, we need to specify the mode in which we want to open it. The mode can be one of the following:</p><ul><li><code>\'r\'</code>: Open the file for reading.</li><li><code>\'w\'</code>: Open the file for writing. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file already exists, its contents will be deleted.</li><li><code>\'a\'</code>: Open the file for appending. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file already exists, new data will be added to the end of the file.</li><li><code>\'r+\'</code>: Open the file for reading and writing.</li><li><code>\'w+\'</code>: Open the file for reading and writing. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file already exists, its contents will be deleted.</li><li><code>\'a+\'</code>: Open the file for reading and appending. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file already exists, new data will be added to the end of the file.</li></ul><p>It\'s essential to choose the correct mode when opening a file to avoid data loss or unexpected behavior.</p><h3>Reading from a File</h3><p>To read from a file, we can use the <code>read()</code> method of the file object. This method returns the contents of the file as a string.</p><p>For example:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')\ncontents = file.read()\nprint(contents)\nfile.close()</code><p>This code opens the file \'example.txt\' in read mode, reads its contents, and prints them to the console.</p><h3>Writing to a File</h3><p>To write to a file, we can use the <code>write()</code> method of the file object. This method takes a string as an argument and writes it to the file.</p><p>For example:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')\nfile.write(\'Hello, World!\')\nfile.close()</code><p>This code opens the file \'example.txt\' in write mode, writes the string \'Hello, World!\' to it, and closes the file.</p><h3>Applying File Input/Output Concepts</h3><p>Now that we have reviewed the basics of file input/output, let\'s apply these concepts to a real-world scenario. Suppose we want to create a simple text editor that allows users to create, read, and write text files.</p><p>We can use the concepts we have learned to implement this functionality. For example, we can use the <code>open()</code> function to open a file in read or write mode, and the <code>read()</code> and <code>write()</code> methods to interact with the file.</p><h3>Quiz Time!</h3><p>It\'s time to test your knowledge of file input/output concepts. Take this quiz to assess your understanding of the topics covered today.</p>', 'Reviewing File Input/Output Concepts and Quiz', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(184, 197, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python modules tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+modules+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+modules+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+modules+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+modules+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+modules+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Working with Python Modules</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the world of Python modules. Modules are pre-written code that can be imported into your Python programs to perform specific tasks. They are a crucial part of Python programming and can save you a lot of time and effort.</p><h3>Importing Modules</h3><p>To use a module in your Python program, you need to import it. There are several ways to import modules in Python. The most common way is to use the <code>import</code> statement. For example, to import the <code>math</code> module, you would use the following code:</p><code>import math</code><p>This imports the entire <code>math</code> module, and you can access its functions and variables using the dot notation. For example, to use the <code>sin</code> function from the <code>math</code> module, you would use the following code:</p><code>import math<br>x = math.sin(30)</code><p>You can also import specific functions or variables from a module using the <code>from</code> keyword. For example:</p><code>from math import sin<br>x = sin(30)</code><p>This imports only the <code>sin</code> function from the <code>math</code> module, and you can use it directly without the dot notation.</p><h3>Creating Modules</h3><p>Creating your own modules is a great way to organize your code and make it reusable. A module is simply a Python file with a <code>.py</code> extension. To create a module, you can create a new Python file and add your functions, classes, and variables to it.</p><p>For example, let\'s create a module called <code>mymodule.py</code> with a function called <code>greet</code>:</p><code># mymodule.py<br>def greet(name):<br>    print(f&quot;Hello, {name}!&quot;)</code><p>To use this module in another Python program, you can import it using the <code>import</code> statement:</p><code>import mymodule<br>mymodule.greet(&quot;John&quot;)</code><p>This will output: <code>Hello, John!</code></p><h3>Module Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with modules:</p><ul><li><strong>Keep your modules organized</strong>: Keep your modules organized by grouping related functions and variables together.</li><li><strong>Use meaningful names</strong>: Use meaningful names for your modules, functions, and variables to make your code easy to understand.</li><li><strong>Document your modules</strong>: Document your modules using docstrings to make it easy for others to understand how to use them.</li><li><strong>Test your modules</strong>: Test your modules thoroughly to ensure they work as expected.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can create high-quality modules that are easy to use and maintain.</p>', 'Working with Python Modules', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(185, 198, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Data Structures</h3><p>In this chapter, we will be introducing the basic data structures in Python. Data structures are a way to store and organize data in a program, and they are essential for any programming language. We will be covering three main data structures: Lists, Tuples, and Dictionaries.</p><h3>Lists</h3><p>A list is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are denoted by square brackets [] and are mutable, meaning they can be changed after they are created.</p><p>Here are some examples of lists:</p><ul><li><code>fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'cherry\']</code></li><li><code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code></li><li><code>mixed = [\'apple\', 1, 3.14, [1, 2, 3]]</code></li></ul><p>Lists have several methods that can be used to manipulate them, such as <code>append()</code>, <code>insert()</code>, <code>remove()</code>, and <code>sort()</code>.</p><h3>Tuples</h3><p>A tuple is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are denoted by parentheses () and are immutable, meaning they cannot be changed after they are created.</p><p>Here are some examples of tuples:</p><ul><li><code>fruits = (\'apple\', \'banana\', \'cherry\')</code></li><li><code>numbers = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)</code></li><li><code>mixed = (\'apple\', 1, 3.14, (1, 2, 3))</code></li></ul><p>Tuples have several methods that can be used to manipulate them, such as <code>index()</code> and <code>count()</code>.</p><h3>Dictionaries</h3><p>A dictionary is a collection of key-value pairs. Dictionaries are denoted by curly brackets {} and are mutable, meaning they can be changed after they are created.</p><p>Here are some examples of dictionaries:</p><ul><li><code>person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \'city\': \'New York\'}</code></li><li><code>colors = {\'red\': \'#FF0000\', \'green\': \'#00FF00\', \'blue\': \'#0000FF\'}</code></li></ul><p>Dictionaries have several methods that can be used to manipulate them, such as <code>keys()</code>, <code>values()</code>, and <code>items()</code>.</p><p>Some <strong>key</strong> points to remember about data structures:</p><ul><li>Lists are mutable and denoted by square brackets []</li><li>Tuples are immutable and denoted by parentheses ()</li><li>Dictionaries are mutable and denoted by curly brackets {}</li><li>Data structures are essential for any programming language</li></ul>', 'Introduction to Data Structures', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(186, 199, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to File Input/Output in Python</h3><p>File input/output is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to read and write data to files, which can be useful for storing and retrieving data, logging, and many other applications. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of reading and writing files in Python, including the different file modes.</p><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>To read a file in Python, you can use the built-in <code>open()</code> function, which returns a file object. The <code>open()</code> function takes two arguments: the name of the file and the mode in which to open the file. The mode can be either <code>\'r\'</code> for read mode or <code>\'w\'</code> for write mode.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read a file:</p><ul><li>Open the file in read mode using <code>open(\'filename.txt\', \'r\')</code></li><li>Read the contents of the file using the <code>read()</code> method</li><li>Close the file using the <code>close()</code> method</li></ul><p>Example code:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')<br>contents = file.read()<br>print(contents)<br>file.close()</code><p>This code opens a file called <code>example.txt</code> in read mode, reads the contents of the file, prints the contents, and then closes the file.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>To write to a file in Python, you can use the <code>open()</code> function in write mode. The <code>open()</code> function will create a new file if the file does not exist, and it will overwrite the contents of the file if it does exist.</p><p>Here is an example of how to write to a file:</p><ul><li>Open the file in write mode using <code>open(\'filename.txt\', \'w\')</code></li><li>Write to the file using the <code>write()</code> method</li><li>Close the file using the <code>close()</code> method</li></ul><p>Example code:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')<br>file.write(\'Hello, world!\')<br>file.close()</code><p>This code opens a file called <code>example.txt</code> in write mode, writes the string <code>\'Hello, world!\'</code> to the file, and then closes the file.</p><h3>File Modes</h3><p>There are several file modes that you can use when opening a file in Python. The most common file modes are:</p><ul><li><code>\'r\'</code>: Open the file in read mode. This is the default mode.</li><li><code>\'w\'</code>: Open the file in write mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file does exist, its contents will be overwritten.</li><li><code>\'a\'</code>: Open the file in append mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file does exist, new data will be appended to the end of the file.</li><li><code>\'r+\'</code>: Open the file in read and write mode.</li><li><code>\'w+\'</code>: Open the file in read and write mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file does exist, its contents will be overwritten.</li><li><code>\'a+\'</code>: Open the file in read and append mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If the file does exist, new data will be appended to the end of the file.</li></ul><p>It is <strong>important to note</strong> that when you open a file in write mode, the file\'s contents will be overwritten if the file already exists. If you want to append to the file instead of overwriting it, you should open the file in append mode.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with files in Python:</p><ul><li>Always close the file when you are finished with it to avoid memory leaks.</li><li>Use the <code>with</code> statement to open the file, which will automatically close the file when you are finished with it.</li><li>Be careful when working with files, as you can easily overwrite or delete important data.</li></ul>', 'File Input/Output in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(187, 200, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python exception handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Exception Handling in Python</h3><p>Exception handling is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to handle and manage errors that may occur during the execution of your code, making your programs more robust and reliable. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of exception handling in Python, including try/except blocks, error types, and raising exceptions.</p><h3>Try/Except Blocks</h3><p>A try/except block is used to catch and handle exceptions that may occur in your code. The basic syntax of a try/except block is as follows:</p><code>try:\n    # code that may raise an exception\nexcept ExceptionType:\n    # code to handle the exception\n</code><p>The <strong>try</strong> block contains the code that may raise an exception, while the <strong>except</strong> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception is raised.</p><h3>Error Types</h3><p>Python has a number of built-in exception types that can be used to handle different types of errors. Some common error types include:</p><ul><li><strong>SyntaxError</strong>: raised when there is an error in the syntax of your code</li><li><strong>TypeError</strong>: raised when a variable is not of the expected type</li><li><strong>ValueError</strong>: raised when a function or operation receives an argument with an incorrect value</li><li><strong>IOError</strong>: raised when an input/output operation fails</li><li><strong>ImportError</strong>: raised when a module cannot be imported</li></ul><p>You can use these error types in your except blocks to catch and handle specific types of exceptions.</p><h3>Raising Exceptions</h3><p>In addition to catching exceptions, you can also raise exceptions in your code using the <strong>raise</strong> statement. The basic syntax of the raise statement is as follows:</p><code>raise ExceptionType(message)</code><p>The <strong>ExceptionType</strong> is the type of exception to be raised, and the <strong>message</strong> is a string that describes the exception.</p><h3>Example Use Cases</h3><p>Here are a few examples of how you can use try/except blocks and raise exceptions in your code:</p><code>try:\n    x = 5 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(&quot;Cannot divide by zero!&quot;)\n\ntry:\n    raise ValueError(&quot;Invalid value&quot;)\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)\n</code><p>In the first example, we use a try/except block to catch a <strong>ZeroDivisionError</strong> that is raised when we attempt to divide by zero. In the second example, we use the <strong>raise</strong> statement to raise a <strong>ValueError</strong> with a custom message.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are a few best practices to keep in mind when using exception handling in Python:</p><ul><li><strong>Be specific</strong>: When catching exceptions, be specific about the type of exception you are catching. Avoid catching the general <strong>Exception</strong> class, as this can mask other errors in your code.</li><li><strong>Keep it short</strong>: Keep your try/except blocks as short as possible. This makes it easier to understand what code is being executed and what exceptions are being caught.</li><li><strong>Use logging</strong>: Use logging to record information about exceptions that are raised. This can help you diagnose and debug issues in your code.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices and using exception handling effectively, you can write more robust and reliable code in Python.</p>', 'Exception Handling in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(188, 201, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: object-oriented programming in python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=object-oriented+programming+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/object-oriented+programming+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/object-oriented+programming+in+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/object-oriented+programming+in+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=object-oriented+programming+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of OOP, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. It is essentially a design pattern or a template that defines the characteristics of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class.</p><p>For example:</p><code>class Car:<br>    def __init__(self, brand, model, year):<br>        self.brand = brand<br>        self.model = model<br>        self.year = year<br>    def print_details(self):<br>        print(f\'Brand: {self.brand}, Model: {self.model}, Year: {self.year}\')</code><p>In this example, <code>Car</code> is a class that has three attributes: <code>brand</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code>. The <code>__init__</code> method is a special method that is called when an object is created from the class, and it is used to initialize the attributes of the class. The <code>print_details</code> method is a regular method that can be called on an object to print its details.</p><h3>Objects</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class. It has its own set of attributes (data) and methods (functions). In Python, an object is created using the class name followed by parentheses that contain the arguments to be passed to the <code>__init__</code> method.</p><p>For example:</p><code>my_car = Car(\'Toyota\', \'Corolla\', 2015)</code><p>This creates an object called <code>my_car</code> that is an instance of the <code>Car</code> class. The object has its own set of attributes, such as <code>brand</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code>, which are initialized with the values passed to the <code>__init__</code> method.</p><p>The object can also call methods, such as:</p><code>my_car.print_details()</code><p>This will print the details of the <code>my_car</code> object.</p><h3>Inheritance</h3><p>Inheritance is a mechanism that allows one class to inherit the properties and behavior of another class. The class that is being inherited from is called the parent class or superclass, and the class that is doing the inheriting is called the child class or subclass.</p><p>In Python, inheritance is implemented using the <code>(ParentClass)</code> syntax after the class name.</p><p>For example:</p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):<br>    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, battery_size):<br>        super().__init__(brand, model, year)<br>        self.battery_size = battery_size<br>    def print_battery_size(self):<br>        print(f\'Battery Size: {self.battery_size}\')</code><p>In this example, the <code>ElectricCar</code> class inherits from the <code>Car</code> class and adds an additional attribute called <code>battery_size</code>. The <code>super().__init__(brand, model, year)</code> line calls the <code>__init__</code> method of the parent class to initialize the attributes that are inherited from the parent class.</p><p>The benefits of inheritance include:</p><ul><li><strong>Code Reusability</strong>: Inheritance allows us to reuse the code of the parent class in the child class, which reduces code duplication and improves maintainability.</li><li><strong>Easier Maintenance</strong>: If we need to make changes to the parent class, we can do so without affecting the child class, which makes maintenance easier.</li><li><strong>Improved Readability</strong>: Inheritance makes the code more readable by allowing us to group related classes together and show the relationships between them.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, object-oriented programming is a powerful paradigm that allows us to write reusable, maintainable, and readable code. Classes, objects, and inheritance are the building blocks of OOP, and understanding them is essential for any programmer.</p>', 'Understanding Object-Oriented Programming', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(189, 202, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python libraries</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+libraries/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+libraries/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with Python Libraries</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore three essential Python libraries: Requests, Pandas, and NumPy. These libraries are widely used in various applications such as web scraping, data analysis, and scientific computing.</p><h3>Requests Library</h3><p>The Requests library is used for making HTTP requests in Python. It allows you to send HTTP requests and returns server responses. This library is useful for web scraping, API interactions, and other web-related tasks.</p><ul><li><strong>Features of Requests Library:</strong></li><li>Simple and intuitive API</li><li>Support for international domains and URLs</li><li>Connection keep-alive and connection pooling</li><li>Support for both HTTP and HTTPS protocols</li></ul><p>Here\'s an example of how to use the Requests library to make a GET request:</p><code>import requests<br>response = requests.get(\'https://www.example.com\')<br>print(response.status_code)</code><p>In this example, we import the Requests library and use the get() function to make a GET request to the specified URL. The status_code attribute of the response object is then printed to the console.</p><h3>Pandas Library</h3><p>The Pandas library is a powerful data analysis tool in Python. It provides data structures and functions for efficiently handling structured data, including tabular data such as spreadsheets and SQL tables.</p><ul><li><strong>Features of Pandas Library:</strong></li><li>Data structures: Series (1-dimensional labeled array) and DataFrame (2-dimensional labeled data structure)</li><li>Data operations: filtering, sorting, grouping, merging, reshaping, and pivoting</li><li>Data input/output: reading and writing CSV, Excel, and other file formats</li><li>Data analysis: statistical functions, data cleaning, and data transformation</li></ul><p>Here\'s an example of how to use the Pandas library to create a DataFrame and perform basic data operations:</p><code>import pandas as pd<br>data = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\', \'Linda\'],<br>\'Age\': [28, 24, 35, 32]}<br>df = pd.DataFrame(data)<br>print(df)</code><p>In this example, we import the Pandas library and create a dictionary with two keys: \'Name\' and \'Age\'. We then create a DataFrame from this dictionary and print it to the console.</p><h3>NumPy Library</h3><p>The NumPy library is a library for working with arrays and mathematical operations in Python. It provides support for large, multi-dimensional arrays and matrices, and is the foundation of most scientific computing in Python.</p><ul><li><strong>Features of NumPy Library:</strong></li><li>N-dimensional array object</li><li>Basic mathematical operations: arithmetic, comparison, and logical operations</li><li>Advanced mathematical operations: linear algebra, random number generation, and statistics</li><li>Integration with other libraries: SciPy, Pandas, and Matplotlib</li></ul><p>Here\'s an example of how to use the NumPy library to create an array and perform basic mathematical operations:</p><code>import numpy as np<br>arr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])<br>print(arr + 2)</code><p>In this example, we import the NumPy library and create an array with five elements. We then add 2 to each element of the array using the \'+\' operator and print the result to the console.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we explored the Requests, Pandas, and NumPy libraries in Python. These libraries are essential tools for any Python developer, and are widely used in various applications such as web scraping, data analysis, and scientific computing. By mastering these libraries, you can unlock the full potential of Python and take your programming skills to the next level.</p>', 'Working with Python Libraries', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(190, 203, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming book</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming+book\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming+book\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming+book/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming+book/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming+book\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Review and Practice</h3><p>Welcome to Day 49 of our Python course. Today, we will review the basics of Python, data structures, and file input/output. This review will help solidify your understanding of these concepts and prepare you for more advanced topics.</p><h3>Python Basics</h3><p>Python is a high-level, interpreted language that is easy to learn and understand. It has a simple syntax and is often used for web development, scientific computing, and data analysis. Some of the basic concepts in Python include:</p><ul><li>Variables: Used to store and manipulate data.</li><li>Data Types: Include integers, floats, strings, lists, dictionaries, and more.</li><li>Control Structures: Include if/else statements, for loops, and while loops.</li><li>Functions: Reusable blocks of code that take arguments and return values.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a simple Python function: <code>def greet(name): print(&quot;Hello, &quot; + name)</code>. You can call this function with a name, like <code>greet(&quot;John&quot;)</code>, to print out a greeting.</p><h3>Data Structures</h3><p>Data structures are used to store and organize data in a program. Some common data structures in Python include:</p><ul><li>Lists: Ordered collections of items that can be of any data type.</li><li>Tuples: Ordered, immutable collections of items that can be of any data type.</li><li>Dictionaries: Unordered collections of key-value pairs.</li><li>Sets: Unordered collections of unique items.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of creating and using a list: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3] print(my_list[0])</code>. This will output the first item in the list, which is <code>1</code>.</p><h3>File Input/Output</h3><p>File input/output is used to read and write data to files. Some common functions used for file input/output in Python include:</p><ul><li><code>open()</code>: Opens a file and returns a file object.</li><li><code>read()</code>: Reads data from a file.</li><li><code>write()</code>: Writes data to a file.</li><li><code>close()</code>: Closes a file.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of reading and writing to a file: <code>file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;w&quot;) file.write(&quot;Hello, World!&quot;) file.close() file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;r&quot;) print(file.read())</code>. This will create a new file called <code>example.txt</code>, write <code>Hello, World!</code> to it, and then read and print out the contents of the file.</p><p>Remember to always <strong>close</strong> a file when you are done with it to free up system resources.</p><h3>Practice</h3><p>Now it\'s your turn to practice. Try writing some Python code to:</p><ul><li>Create a list of numbers and print out the first and last items.</li><li>Write a function that takes a name and age as arguments and prints out a greeting.</li><li>Read and write data to a file.</li></ul><p>Don\'t worry if you get stuck - just try your best and have fun.</p>', 'Review and Practice', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(191, 204, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to File Input/Output</h3><p>Welcome to Day 50 of the Python course. Today, we will be covering one of the essential topics in programming: File Input/Output. File I/O allows your program to read and write files, which is crucial for storing and retrieving data. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of reading and writing files in Python, as well as the different file modes.</p><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>Python provides several ways to read files, but the most common method is by using the <code>open()</code> function in read mode (<code>\'r\'</code>). When you open a file in read mode, you can read the contents of the file using the <code>read()</code> method.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read a file:</p><ul><li>First, you need to open the file using the <code>open()</code> function.</li><li>Then, you can use the <code>read()</code> method to read the contents of the file.</li><li>Finally, you need to close the file using the <code>close()</code> method.</li></ul><p>Here is some sample code:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')<br>contents = file.read()<br>print(contents)<br>file.close()</code><p>This code opens a file called <code>example.txt</code> in read mode, reads the contents of the file, prints the contents, and then closes the file.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>Python also provides several ways to write files, but the most common method is by using the <code>open()</code> function in write mode (<code>\'w\'</code>). When you open a file in write mode, you can write to the file using the <code>write()</code> method.</p><p>Here is an example of how to write to a file:</p><ul><li>First, you need to open the file using the <code>open()</code> function.</li><li>Then, you can use the <code>write()</code> method to write to the file.</li><li>Finally, you need to close the file using the <code>close()</code> method.</li></ul><p>Here is some sample code:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')<br>file.write(\'Hello, World!\')<br>file.close()</code><p>This code opens a file called <code>example.txt</code> in write mode, writes the string <code>\'Hello, World!\'</code> to the file, and then closes the file.</p><h3>File Modes</h3><p>When you open a file in Python, you need to specify the mode in which you want to open the file. The mode is specified as a string, and it can be one of the following:</p><ul><li><code>\'r\'</code>: Open the file in read mode. This is the default mode.</li><li><code>\'w\'</code>: Open the file in write mode. If the file already exists, its contents will be deleted. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a\'</code>: Open the file in append mode. If the file already exists, new data will be appended to the end of the file. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'r+\'</code>: Open the file in read and write mode.</li><li><code>\'w+\'</code>: Open the file in read and write mode. If the file already exists, its contents will be deleted. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a+\'</code>: Open the file in read and append mode. If the file already exists, new data will be appended to the end of the file. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'x\'</code>: Open the file in create mode. If the file already exists, the operation will fail. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'b\'</code>: Open the file in binary mode.</li><li><code>\'t\'</code>: Open the file in text mode. This is the default mode.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of how to use the different file modes:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')  # Open the file in read mode<br>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')  # Open the file in write mode<br>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'a\')  # Open the file in append mode</code><p>In summary, file input/output is an essential topic in programming, and Python provides several ways to read and write files. The <code>open()</code> function is used to open a file, and the <code>read()</code> and <code>write()</code> methods are used to read and write to the file. The file mode is specified as a string, and it can be one of the several modes available in Python.</p>', 'Introduction to File Input/Output', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(192, 205, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with CSV Files</h3><p>In this lesson, we will be exploring how to work with CSV (Comma Separated Values) files in Python. CSV files are widely used for storing and exchanging data between different applications. Python provides a built-in module called <strong>csv</strong> that allows us to easily read and write CSV files.</p><h3>The csv Module</h3><p>The <strong>csv</strong> module provides classes for reading and writing tabular data in CSV format. The module contains two main classes: <strong>reader</strong> and <strong>writer</strong>. The <strong>reader</strong> class is used to read CSV files, while the <strong>writer</strong> class is used to write CSV files.</p><h3>Reader Object</h3><p>The <strong>reader</strong> object is created by calling the <code>csv.reader()</code> function and passing a file object as an argument. The <strong>reader</strong> object allows us to iterate over the rows in a CSV file. Each row is returned as a list of strings, where each string represents a cell in the row.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use the <strong>reader</strong> object:</p><ul><li>Open a CSV file in read mode using the <code>open()</code> function.</li><li>Create a <strong>reader</strong> object by calling the <code>csv.reader()</code> function and passing the file object as an argument.</li><li>Use a <code>for</code> loop to iterate over the rows in the CSV file.</li></ul><p>Example code:</p><code>import csv<br>with open(\'example.csv\', \'r\') as file:<br>    reader = csv.reader(file)<br>    for row in reader:<br>        print(row)</code><h3>Writer Object</h3><p>The <strong>writer</strong> object is created by calling the <code>csv.writer()</code> function and passing a file object as an argument. The <strong>writer</strong> object allows us to write data to a CSV file. We can use the <code>writerow()</code> method to write a single row, or the <code>writerows()</code> method to write multiple rows.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use the <strong>writer</strong> object:</p><ul><li>Open a CSV file in write mode using the <code>open()</code> function.</li><li>Create a <strong>writer</strong> object by calling the <code>csv.writer()</code> function and passing the file object as an argument.</li><li>Use the <code>writerow()</code> method to write a single row to the CSV file.</li><li>Use the <code>writerows()</code> method to write multiple rows to the CSV file.</li></ul><p>Example code:</p><code>import csv<br>with open(\'example.csv\', \'w\', newline=\'\') as file:<br>    writer = csv.writer(file)<br>    writer.writerow([\'Name\', \'Age\', \'City\'])<br>    writer.writerow([\'John Doe\', 30, \'New York\'])<br>    writer.writerow([\'Jane Doe\', 25, \'Los Angeles\'])</code><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we learned how to work with CSV files in Python using the <strong>csv</strong> module. We covered the <strong>reader</strong> and <strong>writer</strong> objects, and how to use them to read and write CSV files. With this knowledge, you can now work with CSV files in your own Python programs.</p>', 'Working with CSV Files', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(193, 206, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python exception handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Exception Handling in Python</h3><p>Exception handling is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to handle errors and exceptions that may occur during the execution of your code, making it more robust and reliable. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of exception handling in Python, including the try-except block, raising exceptions, and exception types.</p><h3>Try-Except Block</h3><p>The try-except block is the most basic form of exception handling in Python. It consists of two parts: the try block and the except block. The try block contains the code that may potentially raise an exception, while the except block contains the code that will be executed if an exception is raised.</p><p>Here is an example of a try-except block:</p><code>try:\n    # code that may raise an exception\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    # code that will be executed if an exception is raised\n    print(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")</code><p>In this example, the try block attempts to divide 1 by 0, which raises a ZeroDivisionError. The except block catches this exception and prints a message indicating that division by zero is not allowed.</p><h3>Raising Exceptions</h3><p>Raising exceptions is a way to explicitly throw an exception in your code. This can be useful for indicating that something has gone wrong or for forcing the program to exit a certain block of code.</p><p>Here is an example of raising an exception:</p><code>raise ValueError(\"Invalid input!\")</code><p>This code raises a ValueError with the message \"Invalid input!\".</p><h3>Exception Types</h3><p>Python has a wide range of built-in exception types that can be used to handle different types of errors. Here are some of the most common exception types:</p><ul><li><strong>ZeroDivisionError</strong>: raised when attempting to divide by zero</li><li><strong>TypeError</strong>: raised when attempting to perform an operation on a value of the wrong type</li><li><strong>ValueError</strong>: raised when a function or operation receives an argument with an incorrect value</li><li><strong>IndexError</strong>: raised when attempting to access an element outside the bounds of a sequence</li><li><strong>KeyError</strong>: raised when attempting to access a key that does not exist in a dictionary</li></ul><p>Here is an example of using exception types:</p><code>try:\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")\ntry:\n    x = \"hello\" + 1\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"Cannot add string and integer!\")</code><p>In this example, the first try-except block catches a ZeroDivisionError, while the second try-except block catches a TypeError.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when using exception handling:</p><ul><li>Always use specific exception types instead of catching the general Exception class</li><li>Keep the code in the try block as short as possible to avoid catching unrelated exceptions</li><li>Always provide a meaningful error message when raising an exception</li><li>Use exception handling to handle errors, not to control the flow of your program</li></ul>', 'Exception Handling in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(194, 207, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python lambda functions</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+lambda+functions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+lambda+functions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+lambda+functions/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+lambda+functions/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+lambda+functions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Lambda Functions</h3><p>Welcome to day 53 of the Python course. Today, we will be covering one of the most powerful and useful features in Python: lambda functions. Also known as anonymous functions, lambda functions are small, one-line functions that can be defined inline within a larger expression.</p><p>So, why do we need lambda functions? The answer is simple: they provide a concise way to create small, one-time use functions. They are often used in combination with other functions that take a function as an argument, such as the <strong>map</strong> function, which we will cover later in this lesson.</p><h3>Anonymous Functions</h3><p>As mentioned earlier, lambda functions are also known as anonymous functions. This is because they are not declared with a name. Instead, they are defined using the <code>lambda</code> keyword, followed by an argument list, a colon, and an expression.</p><p>Here is an example of a simple lambda function:</p><p><code>add_five = lambda x: x + 5</code></p><p>This lambda function takes one argument, <code>x</code>, and returns the result of adding 5 to <code>x</code>. We can then call this function like any other function:</p><p><code>result = add_five(10)</code></p><p><code>print(result)  # Outputs: 15</code></p><h3>Lambda Syntax</h3><p>The syntax for a lambda function is as follows:</p><ul><li><code>lambda</code>: This is the keyword used to define a lambda function.</li><li><code>arguments</code>: This is a comma-separated list of variables that will be passed to the function.</li><li><code>:</code>: This is the colon that separates the argument list from the expression.</li><li><code>expression</code>: This is the code that will be executed when the function is called.</li></ul><p>Here are a few more examples of lambda functions:</p><p><code>double = lambda x: x * 2</code></p><p><code>square = lambda x: x ** 2</code></p><p><code>greet = lambda name: \'Hello, \' + name</code></p><h3>Map Function</h3><p>The <strong>map</strong> function is a built-in Python function that applies a given function to each item of an iterable (such as a list or tuple) and returns a list of the results.</p><p>The syntax for the <strong>map</strong> function is as follows:</p><p><code>map(function, iterable)</code></p><p>Here, <code>function</code> is the function that will be applied to each item in the <code>iterable</code>.</p><p>Here is an example of using the <strong>map</strong> function with a lambda function:</p><p><code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code></p><p><code>doubled_numbers = list(map(lambda x: x * 2, numbers))</code></p><p><code>print(doubled_numbers)  # Outputs: [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]</code></p><p>As you can see, the <strong>map</strong> function applied the lambda function to each item in the <code>numbers</code> list, doubling each number.</p><p>In conclusion, lambda functions are a powerful tool in Python that can be used to create small, one-time use functions. They are often used in combination with other functions, such as the <strong>map</strong> function, to perform complex operations on data.</p>', 'Understanding Lambda Functions', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(195, 208, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python modules tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+modules+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+modules+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+modules+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+modules+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+modules+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Modules</h3><p>Welcome to Day 54 of our Python course. In this lesson, we will be covering the basics of Python modules, including the import statement, module types, and module installation.</p><p>Python modules are pre-written code that can be used to perform specific tasks. They are a crucial part of the Python ecosystem and are used extensively in real-world applications. By the end of this lesson, you will have a solid understanding of how to use Python modules in your own projects.</p><h3>The Import Statement</h3><p>The import statement is used to bring a module into your Python program. The basic syntax of the import statement is: <code>import module_name</code>. For example, to import the math module, you would use the following code: <code>import math</code>.</p><p>Once a module is imported, you can use its functions and variables in your program. For example, the math module has a function called sqrt() that can be used to calculate the square root of a number. Here is an example of how to use the sqrt() function: <code>import math; result = math.sqrt(16); print(result)</code>.</p><h3>Module Types</h3><p>There are several types of Python modules, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Built-in Modules</strong>: These are modules that come pre-installed with Python. Examples of built-in modules include the math module, the random module, and the time module.</li><li><strong>External Modules</strong>: These are modules that are not included with Python and must be installed separately. Examples of external modules include the requests module and the pandas module.</li><li><strong>Custom Modules</strong>: These are modules that are created by the user. Custom modules can be used to organize code and make it reusable.</li></ul><p>Each type of module has its own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of which type to use will depend on the specific needs of your project.</p><h3>Module Installation</h3><p>External modules can be installed using the pip package manager. The basic syntax of the pip install command is: <code>pip install module_name</code>. For example, to install the requests module, you would use the following code: <code>pip install requests</code>.</p><p>Once a module is installed, it can be imported into your Python program using the import statement. For example, to import the requests module, you would use the following code: <code>import requests</code>.</p><p>It is also possible to install modules from source code. This can be done by downloading the source code for the module and then running the setup.py file. The setup.py file is a script that installs the module and its dependencies.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with Python modules:</p><ul><li><strong>Use the import statement to bring modules into your program</strong>: This will make your code more readable and maintainable.</li><li><strong>Use the pip package manager to install external modules</strong>: This will make it easy to install and manage external modules.</li><li><strong>Create custom modules to organize your code</strong>: This will make your code more reusable and maintainable.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can write more efficient and effective Python code.</p>', 'Introduction to Python Modules', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(196, 209, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python packages</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with Python Packages</h3><p>Welcome to Day 55 of the Python course. In this lesson, we will be covering the basics of working with Python packages. A package in Python is a collection of modules and subpackages that are used to organize and structure code. Packages are essential for building large and complex applications, as they allow developers to modularize their code and reuse functionality.</p><h3>Package Structure</h3><p>A Python package typically consists of a directory with an <code>__init__.py</code> file. The <code>__init__.py</code> file can be empty, but it is required to indicate that the directory should be treated as a package. Inside the package directory, you can have multiple modules (Python files) and subpackages (subdirectories with their own <code>__init__.py</code> files).</p><p>Here is an example of a simple package structure:</p><ul><li><strong>mypackage</strong>: the package directory</li><li><strong>__init__.py</strong>: the package initialization file</li><li><strong>module1.py</strong>: a module inside the package</li><li><strong>module2.py</strong>: another module inside the package</li><li><strong>subpackage</strong>: a subpackage directory</li><li><strong>__init__.py</strong>: the subpackage initialization file</li><li><strong>submodule.py</strong>: a module inside the subpackage</li></ul><h3>Package Installation</h3><p>There are several ways to install Python packages. The most common method is to use pip, the Python package manager. Pip is included with Python, so you don\'t need to install it separately. To install a package using pip, you can use the following command:</p><code>pip install package_name</code><p>Replace <code>package_name</code> with the name of the package you want to install. You can also install packages from source by downloading the package files and running the setup script:</p><code>python setup.py install</code><h3>Package Management</h3><p>Package management is an essential part of working with Python packages. You can use pip to list all installed packages, update packages, and uninstall packages. Here are some common pip commands:</p><ul><li><code>pip list</code>: list all installed packages</li><li><code>pip freeze</code>: list all installed packages and their versions</li><li><code>pip install --upgrade package_name</code>: update a package to the latest version</li><li><code>pip uninstall package_name</code>: uninstall a package</li></ul><p>In addition to pip, you can also use other package managers like conda or virtualenv to manage your packages.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with Python packages:</p><ul><li><strong>Use a virtual environment</strong>: a virtual environment allows you to isolate your packages and dependencies from the system Python environment.</li><li><strong>Use pip to install packages</strong>: pip is the recommended package manager for Python.</li><li><strong>Keep your packages up to date</strong>: regularly update your packages to ensure you have the latest security patches and features.</li></ul>', 'Working with Python Packages', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(197, 210, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 56: Review and Practice</h3><p>Welcome to Day 56 of the Python course. Today, we will review the concepts we have learned so far and practice solving problems. We will also work on a project to reinforce our understanding of the material.</p><h3>Review of Concepts</h3><p>Let\'s start by reviewing some of the key concepts we have learned in the course. These include:</p><ul><li>Variables and data types</li><li>Control structures (if/else, for loops, while loops)</li><li>Functions</li><li>Lists and tuples</li><li>Dictionaries</li></ul><p>We have also learned about object-oriented programming, including classes and objects, inheritance, and polymorphism.</p><h3>Practice Problems</h3><p>Now, let\'s practice solving some problems. These problems will help you reinforce your understanding of the concepts and identify areas where you need more practice.</p><p>For example, let\'s say we want to write a program that asks the user for their name and age, and then prints out a greeting message with their name and age. We can use the following code:</p><code>name = input(&quot;What is your name? &quot;)\nage = int(input(&quot;How old are you? &quot;))\nprint(&quot;Hello, &quot; + name + &quot;! You are &quot; + str(age) + &quot; years old.&quot;)</code><p>This code uses the <strong>input</strong> function to get the user\'s name and age, and then uses the <strong>print</strong> function to print out the greeting message.</p><h3>Project Work</h3><p>For our project, let\'s create a simple calculator program that allows the user to perform basic arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division). We can use the following code:</p><code>def add(x, y):\n    return x + y\n\ndef subtract(x, y):\n    return x - y\n\ndef multiply(x, y):\n    return x * y\n\ndef divide(x, y):\n    if y == 0:\n        return &quot;Error: cannot divide by zero&quot;\n    else:\n        return x / y\n\nprint(&quot;Welcome to the calculator program!&quot;)\nwhile True:\n    print(&quot;What would you like to do?&quot;)\n    print(&quot;1. Add&quot;)\n    print(&quot;2. Subtract&quot;)\n    print(&quot;3. Multiply&quot;)\n    print(&quot;4. Divide&quot;)\n    print(&quot;5. Quit&quot;)\n    choice = input(&quot;Enter your choice: &quot;)\n    if choice == &quot;1&quot;:\n        num1 = float(input(&quot;Enter the first number: &quot;))\n        num2 = float(input(&quot;Enter the second number: &quot;))\n        print(&quot;The result is: &quot; + str(add(num1, num2)))\n    elif choice == &quot;2&quot;:\n        num1 = float(input(&quot;Enter the first number: &quot;))\n        num2 = float(input(&quot;Enter the second number: &quot;))\n        print(&quot;The result is: &quot; + str(subtract(num1, num2)))\n    elif choice == &quot;3&quot;:\n        num1 = float(input(&quot;Enter the first number: &quot;))\n        num2 = float(input(&quot;Enter the second number: &quot;))\n        print(&quot;The result is: &quot; + str(multiply(num1, num2)))\n    elif choice == &quot;4&quot;:\n        num1 = float(input(&quot;Enter the first number: &quot;))\n        num2 = float(input(&quot;Enter the second number: &quot;))\n        print(&quot;The result is: &quot; + str(divide(num1, num2)))\n    elif choice == &quot;5&quot;:\n        print(&quot;Goodbye!&quot;)\n        break\n    else:\n        print(&quot;Invalid choice. Please try again.&quot;)</code><p>This code defines four functions for the basic arithmetic operations, and then uses a <strong>while</strong> loop to repeatedly ask the user for their choice of operation until they choose to quit.</p><h3>Quiz</h3><p>Now, let\'s test your knowledge with a quiz. Choose the correct answer for each question.</p>', 'Review and Practice', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(198, 211, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python File Input/Output</h3><p>Welcome to Day 57 of the Python course. In this lesson, we will be covering one of the essential topics in programming: file input/output. File input/output, also known as file I/O, is the process of reading and writing data to files. This is a crucial concept in programming as it allows us to store and retrieve data from files, which is necessary for many applications.</p><p>In Python, file I/O is achieved using the built-in <code>open()</code> function, which returns a file object. This file object can be used to read from or write to the file. In this lesson, we will be covering the basics of file handling, reading files, and writing files.</p><h3>File Handling</h3><p>Before we can read from or write to a file, we need to open it. The <code>open()</code> function takes two arguments: the name of the file and the mode in which to open the file. The mode can be one of the following:</p><ul><li><code>\'r\'</code>: Open the file for reading. This is the default mode.</li><li><code>\'w\'</code>: Open the file for writing. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If it does exist, its contents will be deleted.</li><li><code>\'a\'</code>: Open the file for appending. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If it does exist, new data will be added to the end of the file.</li><li><code>\'r+\'</code>: Open the file for reading and writing.</li><li><code>\'w+\'</code>: Open the file for reading and writing. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If it does exist, its contents will be deleted.</li><li><code>\'a+\'</code>: Open the file for reading and appending. If the file does not exist, it will be created. If it does exist, new data will be added to the end of the file.</li></ul><p>Once we have opened the file, we can use the <code>close()</code> method to close it. It is good practice to close the file when we are finished with it to free up system resources.</p><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>To read from a file, we can use the <code>read()</code> method. This method returns the entire contents of the file as a string. We can also use the <code>readline()</code> method to read one line at a time, or the <code>readlines()</code> method to read all lines into a list.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read from a file:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')<br>contents = file.read()<br>print(contents)<br>file.close()</code><p>This code opens the file <code>example.txt</code> in read mode, reads the entire contents of the file into the <code>contents</code> variable, prints the contents, and then closes the file.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>To write to a file, we can use the <code>write()</code> method. This method takes a string as an argument and writes it to the file. We can also use the <code>writelines()</code> method to write a list of strings to the file.</p><p>Here is an example of how to write to a file:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')<br>file.write(\'Hello, world!\')<br>file.close()</code><p>This code opens the file <code>example.txt</code> in write mode, writes the string <code>Hello, world!</code> to the file, and then closes the file.</p><p>It is also possible to use the <code>with</code> statement to open a file. This statement automatically closes the file when we are finished with it, even if an error occurs. Here is an example:</p><code>with open(\'example.txt\', \'r\') as file:<br>    contents = file.read()<br>    print(contents)</code><p>This code opens the file <code>example.txt</code> in read mode, reads the entire contents of the file into the <code>contents</code> variable, prints the contents, and then automatically closes the file.</p><p>In conclusion, file input/output is an essential topic in programming, and Python provides a simple and easy-to-use interface for reading and writing files. By using the <code>open()</code> function and the various methods of the file object, we can read from and write to files with ease.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>The <code>open()</code> function is used to open a file.</li><li>The mode in which to open the file can be one of <code>\'r\'</code>, <code>\'w\'</code>, <code>\'a\'</code>, <code>\'r+\'</code>, <code>\'w+\'</code>, or <code>\'a+\'</code>.</li><li>The <code>close()</code> method is used to close a file.</li><li>The <code>read()</code> method is used to read from a file.</li><li>The <code>write()</code> method is used to write to a file.</li><li>The <code>with</code> statement can be used to automatically close a file when we are finished with it.</li></ul>', 'Introduction to Python File Input/Output', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(199, 212, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv files</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with CSV Files in Python</h3><p>CSV (Comma Separated Values) files are widely used for storing and exchanging data between different applications. Python provides a built-in module called <code>csv</code> for working with CSV files. In this lesson, we will learn how to read and write CSV files using Python.</p><h3>What are CSV Files?</h3><p>CSV files are plain text files that contain data separated by commas. Each line in the file represents a record, and each record is divided into fields by commas. CSV files are often used for importing and exporting data from spreadsheets, databases, and other applications.</p><h3>Reading CSV Files</h3><p>To read a CSV file in Python, you can use the <code>csv.reader</code> function. This function returns a reader object that iterates over the lines in the file. Here is an example of how to use it:</p><code>import csv<br/>with open(\'example.csv\', \'r\') as file:<br/>    reader = csv.reader(file)<br/>    for row in reader:<br/>        print(row)</code><p>This code opens the file <code>example.csv</code> in read mode and creates a reader object. The <code>for</code> loop then iterates over the lines in the file and prints each row.</p><h3>Writing CSV Files</h3><p>To write a CSV file in Python, you can use the <code>csv.writer</code> function. This function returns a writer object that allows you to write rows to the file. Here is an example of how to use it:</p><code>import csv<br/>with open(\'example.csv\', \'w\', newline=\'\') as file:<br/>    writer = csv.writer(file)<br/>    writer.writerow([\'Name\', \'Age\', \'City\'])<br/>    writer.writerow([\'John\', 25, \'New York\'])<br/>    writer.writerow([\'Alice\', 30, \'Los Angeles\'])</code><p>This code opens the file <code>example.csv</code> in write mode and creates a writer object. The <code>writerow</code> method is then used to write three rows to the file.</p><h3>Working with CSV Files using the <code>pandas</code> Library</h3><p>The <code>pandas</code> library provides a more convenient way to work with CSV files. You can use the <code>read_csv</code> function to read a CSV file and the <code>to_csv</code> function to write a CSV file. Here is an example of how to use them:</p><code>import pandas as pd<br/>df = pd.read_csv(\'example.csv\')<br/>print(df)<br/><br/>df = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Alice\'], \'Age\': [25, 30], \'City\': [\'New York\', \'Los Angeles\']})<br/>df.to_csv(\'example.csv\', index=False)</code><p>This code reads the CSV file <code>example.csv</code> into a DataFrame and prints it. It then creates a new DataFrame and writes it to the CSV file.</p><h3>Best Practices for Working with CSV Files</h3><ul><li>Always specify the encoding when opening a CSV file to avoid encoding errors.</li><li>Use the <code>newline=\'\'</code> parameter when opening a CSV file in write mode to avoid extra blank lines.</li><li>Use the <code>index=False</code> parameter when writing a DataFrame to a CSV file to avoid writing row indices.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices and using the <code>csv</code> and <code>pandas</code> libraries, you can easily work with CSV files in Python.</p>', 'Working with CSV Files in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
