(200, 213, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python exception handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Python Exception Handling</h3><p>Exception handling is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to handle errors and exceptions that may occur during the execution of your code, making your programs more robust and reliable. In this lesson, we will explore the concepts of try-except blocks, error handling, and exception types.</p><h3>Try-Except Blocks</h3><p>A try-except block is a construct in Python that allows you to enclose a block of code that may potentially raise an exception. The <code>try</code> block contains the code that may raise an exception, while the <code>except</code> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception is raised.</p><p>Here is a basic example of a try-except block:</p><code>try:\n    # Code that may raise an exception\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised\n    print(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")</code><p>In this example, the <code>try</code> block attempts to divide 1 by 0, which raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. The <code>except</code> block catches this exception and prints an error message.</p><h3>Error Handling</h3><p>Error handling is the process of anticipating, detecting, and responding to errors that may occur during the execution of your code. Python provides several ways to handle errors, including try-except blocks, error types, and error messages.</p><p>Here are some best practices for error handling in Python:</p><ul><li><strong>Anticipate errors</strong>: Think about the potential errors that may occur in your code and plan for them.</li><li><strong>Use try-except blocks</strong>: Use try-except blocks to enclose code that may raise exceptions.</li><li><strong>Handle specific exceptions</strong>: Handle specific exceptions instead of catching all exceptions with a bare <code>except</code> clause.</li><li><strong>Provide informative error messages</strong>: Provide informative error messages to help users understand what went wrong.</li></ul><h3>Exception Types</h3><p>Python has a hierarchy of exception types that can be used to handle specific types of errors. Here are some common exception types:</p><ul><li><code>Exception</code>: The base class for all exceptions.</li><li><code>TypeError</code>: Raised when a variable is not of the expected type.</li><li><code>ValueError</code>: Raised when a variable has an incorrect value.</li><li><code>ZeroDivisionError</code>: Raised when attempting to divide by zero.</li><li><code>IOError</code>: Raised when an I/O operation fails.</li><li><code>ImportError</code>: Raised when a module cannot be imported.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to handle different exception types:</p><code>try:\n    # Code that may raise an exception\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept TypeError:\n    # Code that will be executed if a TypeError is raised\n    print(\"Type error!\")\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    # Code that will be executed if a ZeroDivisionError is raised\n    print(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")\nexcept Exception as e:\n    # Code that will be executed if any other exception is raised\n    print(\"An error occurred: \" + str(e))</code><p>In this example, the code attempts to divide 1 by 0, which raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. The <code>except</code> block catches this exception and prints an error message. If any other exception is raised, the code catches it with the bare <code>except</code> clause and prints a generic error message.</p>', 'Understanding Python Exception Handling', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(201, 214, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python oop basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+oop+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+oop+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+oop+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+oop+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+oop+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Object-Oriented Programming Basics</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of OOP in Python, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class. For example:</p><code>class Car:<br>    def __init__(self, brand, model, year):<br>        self.brand = brand<br>        self.model = model<br>        self.year = year<br>    def honk(self):<br>        print(&quot;Honk!&quot;)</code><p>In this example, the <code>Car</code> class has three attributes: <code>brand</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code>. It also has a method called <code>honk</code> that prints &quot;Honk!&quot; to the console.</p><h3>Objects</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class. In other words, it is a specific entity that has its own set of attributes and methods. To create an object, we use the class name followed by parentheses that contain the required arguments. For example:</p><code>my_car = Car(&quot;Toyota&quot;, &quot;Corolla&quot;, 2015)</code><p>This creates a new object called <code>my_car</code> that is an instance of the <code>Car</code> class. We can access the attributes and methods of the object using the dot notation. For example:</p><code>print(my_car.brand)  # Output: Toyota<br>my_car.honk()  # Output: Honk!</code><h3>Inheritance</h3><p>Inheritance is a mechanism that allows one class to inherit the properties and behavior of another class. The child class inherits all the attributes and methods of the parent class and can also add new attributes and methods or override the ones inherited from the parent class. For example:</p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):<br>    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, battery_capacity):<br>        super().__init__(brand, model, year)<br>        self.battery_capacity = battery_capacity<br>    def charge(self):<br>        print(&quot;Charging...&quot;)</code><p>In this example, the <code>ElectricCar</code> class inherits from the <code>Car</code> class and adds a new attribute called <code>battery_capacity</code> and a new method called <code>charge</code>. The <code>super()</code> function is used to call the constructor of the parent class.</p><h3>Benefits of OOP</h3><p>OOP provides several benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Code Reusability</strong>: OOP allows us to reuse code by creating classes that can be instantiated multiple times.</li><li><strong>Modularity</strong>: OOP allows us to break down a complex system into smaller, independent modules that can be developed and tested separately.</li><li><strong>Easier Maintenance</strong>: OOP makes it easier to modify and maintain code by allowing us to make changes at the class level rather than at the individual object level.</li></ul><h3>Common OOP Terms</h3><p>Here are some common OOP terms that you should know:</p><ul><li><strong>Encapsulation</strong>: The concept of hiding the implementation details of an object from the outside world.</li><li><strong>Abstraction</strong>: The concept of showing only the necessary information to the outside world while hiding the implementation details.</li><li><strong>Polymorphism</strong>: The ability of an object to take on multiple forms.</li></ul>', 'Python Object-Oriented Programming Basics', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(202, 215, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python decorators example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+decorators+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+decorators+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+decorators+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+decorators+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+decorators+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Decorators</h3><p>Python decorators are a powerful feature that allows programmers to modify the behavior of functions or classes. They are often used to extend the functionality of existing code without permanently modifying it. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of function and class decorators, and discuss some common use cases.</p><h3>Function Decorators</h3><p>A function decorator is a small function that takes another function as an argument and extends its behavior without permanently modifying it. The syntax for a function decorator is as follows:</p><code>def my_decorator(func):<br>    def wrapper():<br>        # code to be executed before the function is called<br>        func()<br>        # code to be executed after the function is called<br>    return wrapper<br><br>@my_decorator<br>def my_function():<br>    # code to be executed by the function<br>    pass</code><p>In this example, <code>my_decorator</code> is a function that takes <code>my_function</code> as an argument. The <code>wrapper</code> function is then returned by <code>my_decorator</code> and is used to extend the behavior of <code>my_function</code>. The <code>@my_decorator</code> syntax before the definition of <code>my_function</code> is just a shortcut for <code>my_function = my_decorator(my_function)</code>.</p><h3>Class Decorators</h3><p>A class decorator is similar to a function decorator, but it is used to modify the behavior of a class. The syntax for a class decorator is as follows:</p><code>def my_decorator(cls):<br>    class WrapperClass:<br>        def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):<br>            self.wrapped = cls(*args, **kwargs)<br>    return WrapperClass<br><br>@my_decorator<br>class MyClass:<br>    def __init__(self, value):<br>        self.value = value<br>    def my_method(self):<br>        pass</code><p>In this example, <code>my_decorator</code> is a function that takes <code>MyClass</code> as an argument. The <code>WrapperClass</code> is then returned by <code>my_decorator</code> and is used to extend the behavior of <code>MyClass</code>. The <code>@my_decorator</code> syntax before the definition of <code>MyClass</code> is just a shortcut for <code>MyClass = my_decorator(MyClass)</code>.</p><h3>Use Cases</h3><p>Decorators have many use cases, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Logging</strong>: Decorators can be used to log information about function calls, such as the input arguments and the output values.</li><li><strong>Authentication</strong>: Decorators can be used to check if a user is authenticated before allowing them to call a function.</li><li><strong>Timing</strong>: Decorators can be used to measure the execution time of a function.</li><li><strong>Caching</strong>: Decorators can be used to cache the results of a function so that they can be reused instead of recalculated.</li></ul><p>Some examples of how decorators can be used in real-world applications include:</p><ul><li><strong>Web development</strong>: Decorators can be used to handle tasks such as authentication, caching, and logging in web applications.</li><li><strong>Data analysis</strong>: Decorators can be used to handle tasks such as data cleaning, data transformation, and data visualization in data analysis applications.</li><li><strong>Machine learning</strong>: Decorators can be used to handle tasks such as data preprocessing, model training, and model evaluation in machine learning applications.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, decorators are a powerful feature in Python that can be used to modify the behavior of functions and classes. They have many use cases, including logging, authentication, timing, and caching, and can be used in a variety of real-world applications.</p>', 'Introduction to Python Decorators', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(203, 216, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python generators and iterators</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+generators+and+iterators\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+generators+and+iterators\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+generators+and+iterators/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+generators+and+iterators/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+generators+and+iterators\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Generators and Iterators</h3><p>In this lesson, we will be covering one of the most powerful features of Python: generators and iterators. These tools allow you to create sequences of data on the fly, without having to store them all in memory at once. This is particularly useful when dealing with large datasets or infinite sequences.</p><h3>What are Iterators?</h3><p>An iterator is an object that allows you to iterate over a sequence of data, such as a list or a string. When you use a for loop to iterate over a sequence, you are actually using an iterator behind the scenes. The iterator keeps track of the current position in the sequence and returns the next item each time it is called.</p><p>Here is an example of how you can create an iterator from a list:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>my_iter = iter(my_list)<br>print(next(my_iter))  # prints 1<br>print(next(my_iter))  # prints 2<br>print(next(my_iter))  # prints 3</code><h3>What are Generators?</h3><p>A generator is a special type of iterator that can be used to generate a sequence of data on the fly. Unlike a regular iterator, which returns the next item from a pre-existing sequence, a generator creates each item as it is needed. This makes generators particularly useful for creating infinite sequences or sequences that are too large to fit into memory.</p><p>Generators are created using functions and the <strong>yield</strong> keyword. When a generator function is called, it returns a generator iterator, which can be used to retrieve the generated values.</p><p>Here is an example of a simple generator function:</p><code>def my_gen():<br>    yield 1<br>    yield 2<br>    yield 3<br><br>my_generator = my_gen()<br>print(next(my_generator))  # prints 1<br>print(next(my_generator))  # prints 2<br>print(next(my_generator))  # prints 3</code><h3>The Yield Keyword</h3><p>The <strong>yield</strong> keyword is used to define a generator function. When a generator function is called, it executes until it reaches a <strong>yield</strong> statement, at which point it returns the yielded value and pauses its execution. The next time the generator is called, it resumes execution from where it left off, until it reaches the next <strong>yield</strong> statement.</p><p>Here is an example of how the <strong>yield</strong> keyword works:</p><code>def my_gen():<br>    print(\'Before yield 1\')<br>    yield 1<br>    print(\'After yield 1\')<br>    yield 2<br>    print(\'After yield 2\')<br><br>my_generator = my_gen()<br>print(next(my_generator))  # prints \'Before yield 1\' and then 1<br>print(next(my_generator))  # prints \'After yield 1\' and then 2</code><h3>Benefits of Generators and Iterators</h3><ul><li><strong>Memory Efficiency</strong>: Generators and iterators allow you to process large datasets without having to load them all into memory at once.</li><li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: Generators and iterators can be used to create complex sequences of data that would be difficult or impossible to create using traditional methods.</li><li><strong>Readability</strong>: Generators and iterators can make your code more readable by allowing you to break up complex operations into smaller, more manageable pieces.</li></ul><h3>Common Use Cases for Generators and Iterators</h3><ul><li><strong>Reading Large Files</strong>: Generators and iterators can be used to read large files one line at a time, rather than loading the entire file into memory.</li><li><strong>Creating Infinite Sequences</strong>: Generators can be used to create infinite sequences, such as a sequence of random numbers.</li><li><strong>Processing Data in Chunks</strong>: Generators and iterators can be used to process large datasets in chunks, rather than all at once.</li></ul>', 'Python Generators and Iterators', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(204, 217, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Advanced Python Concepts Review</h3><p>Welcome to Day 63 of our Python course, where we will review advanced Python concepts, best practices, and common pitfalls. This review is crucial to solidify your understanding of the language and to become a proficient Python programmer.</p><h3>Review of Advanced Topics</h3><p>In the previous lessons, we covered various advanced topics such as decorators, generators, and asynchronous programming. Let\'s quickly review these topics to ensure you have a solid grasp of the concepts.</p><ul><li><strong>Decorators</strong>: Decorators are a special type of function that can modify or extend the behavior of another function. They are often used for logging, authentication, and caching.</li><li><strong>Generators</strong>: Generators are a type of iterable, like lists or tuples, but they do not store all the values in memory at once. They are useful for creating sequences of values on the fly.</li><li><strong>Asynchronous Programming</strong>: Asynchronous programming is a technique that allows your program to execute multiple tasks concurrently, improving performance and responsiveness.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a decorator that logs the execution time of a function: <code>import time\nfrom functools import wraps\ndef timer_decorator(func):\n    @wraps(func)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        start_time = time.time()\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        end_time = time.time()\n        print(f&quot;Function {func.__name__} took {end_time - start_time} seconds to execute.&quot;)\n        return result\n    return wrapper</code></p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Following best practices is essential to writing clean, readable, and maintainable code. Here are some best practices to keep in mind:</p><ul><li><strong>Use meaningful variable names</strong>: Variable names should be descriptive and indicate the purpose of the variable.</li><li><strong>Use functions to organize code</strong>: Functions help to break down large codebases into smaller, manageable chunks.</li><li><strong>Use comments to explain code</strong>: Comments help to explain the purpose of the code and make it easier for others to understand.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a well-structured function that follows best practices: <code>def calculate_area(length, width):\n    # Calculate the area of a rectangle\n    area = length * width\n    return area</code></p><h3>Common Pitfalls</h3><p>There are several common pitfalls to watch out for when writing Python code. Here are a few:</p><ul><li><strong>Mutating default argument values</strong>: Default argument values are only evaluated once, so mutating them can have unexpected consequences.</li><li><strong>Using mutable objects as default arguments</strong>: Mutable objects, such as lists or dictionaries, can be modified unexpectedly if used as default arguments.</li><li><strong>Not handling exceptions properly</strong>: Failing to handle exceptions can lead to unexpected behavior or crashes.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to avoid mutating default argument values: <code>def append_to_list(value, list=None):\n    if list is None:\n        list = []\n    list.append(value)\n    return list</code></p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we reviewed advanced Python concepts, best practices, and common pitfalls. By following best practices and avoiding common pitfalls, you can write clean, readable, and maintainable code.</p>', 'Advanced Python Concepts Review', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(205, 218, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python File Input/Output</h3><p>Welcome to Day 64 of the Python course. Today, we will be covering the basics of file handling in Python, including reading and writing files. File input/output is an essential aspect of programming, as it allows us to store and retrieve data from files.</p><p>In this lesson, we will learn how to work with files in Python, including how to open, read, write, and close files. We will also cover the different modes in which files can be opened, such as read-only, write-only, and append modes.</p><h3>File Handling</h3><p>File handling is the process of working with files in a programming language. In Python, we can work with files using the built-in <code>open()</code> function, which returns a file object. This file object can be used to read, write, and close the file.</p><p>The general syntax for opening a file in Python is:</p><code>file_object = open(\'file_name\', \'mode\')</code><p>Where <code>file_name</code> is the name of the file you want to open, and <code>mode</code> is the mode in which you want to open the file.</p><p>The following are the different modes in which a file can be opened:</p><ul><li><code>\'r\'</code>: Open the file in read-only mode. This is the default mode.</li><li><code>\'w\'</code>: Open the file in write-only mode. If the file already exists, its contents will be deleted. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a\'</code>: Open the file in append mode. If the file already exists, new data will be appended to the end of the file. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'r+\'</code>: Open the file in read and write mode.</li><li><code>\'w+\'</code>: Open the file in read and write mode. If the file already exists, its contents will be deleted. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a+\'</code>: Open the file in read and append mode. If the file already exists, new data will be appended to the end of the file. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li></ul><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>To read a file in Python, you can use the <code>read()</code> method of the file object. This method returns the contents of the file as a string.</p><p>For example:</p><code>file_object = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')<br>contents = file_object.read()<br>print(contents)<br>file_object.close()</code><p>This code opens the file <code>example.txt</code> in read-only mode, reads its contents, prints the contents, and then closes the file.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>To write to a file in Python, you can use the <code>write()</code> method of the file object. This method writes a string to the file.</p><p>For example:</p><code>file_object = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')<br>file_object.write(\'Hello, World!\')<br>file_object.close()</code><p>This code opens the file <code>example.txt</code> in write-only mode, writes the string <code>\'Hello, World!\'</code> to the file, and then closes the file.</p><p><strong>Best Practices:</strong></p><ul><li>Always close the file after you are done with it to free up system resources.</li><li>Use the <code>with</code> statement to automatically close the file when you are done with it.</li><li>Be careful when working with files, as you can easily overwrite or delete important data.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, file input/output is an essential aspect of programming in Python. By using the <code>open()</code> function and the different modes, you can read and write files with ease. Remember to always close the file after you are done with it, and be careful when working with files to avoid overwriting or deleting important data.</p>', 'Introduction to Python File Input/Output', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(206, 219, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv files</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with CSV Files in Python</h3><p>CSV (Comma Separated Values) files are a widely used format for storing and exchanging data. In this day\'s lesson, we will learn how to work with CSV files in Python, including reading and writing them. Python provides a built-in module called <strong>csv</strong> that makes it easy to work with CSV files.</p><h3>What are CSV Files?</h3><p>CSV files are plain text files that contain data separated by commas. Each line in the file represents a row of data, and each comma separates a field or column of data. CSV files are often used to import and export data between different applications, such as spreadsheets, databases, and programming languages.</p><h3>Reading CSV Files</h3><p>To read a CSV file in Python, we can use the <strong>csv.reader</strong> function. This function returns a reader object that iterates over the lines in the CSV file. Here is an example of how to use it:</p><code>import csv<br/>with open(\'example.csv\', \'r\') as file:<br/>    reader = csv.reader(file)<br/>    for row in reader:<br/>        print(row)</code><p>In this example, we open the CSV file in read mode and create a reader object. We then iterate over the rows in the file and print each row.</p><h3>Writing CSV Files</h3><p>To write a CSV file in Python, we can use the <strong>csv.writer</strong> function. This function returns a writer object that allows us to write rows of data to the CSV file. Here is an example of how to use it:</p><code>import csv<br/>with open(\'example.csv\', \'w\', newline=\'\') as file:<br/>    writer = csv.writer(file)<br/>    writer.writerow([\'Name\', \'Age\', \'City\'])<br/>    writer.writerow([\'John\', 25, \'New York\'])<br/>    writer.writerow([\'Alice\', 30, \'Los Angeles\'])</code><p>In this example, we open the CSV file in write mode and create a writer object. We then use the <strong>writerow</strong> method to write rows of data to the file.</p><h3>Working with CSV Files Using the pandas Library</h3><p>The <strong>pandas</strong> library is a popular library for data analysis in Python. It provides a powerful data structure called the <strong>DataFrame</strong> that makes it easy to work with CSV files. Here is an example of how to use it:</p><code>import pandas as pd<br/>df = pd.read_csv(\'example.csv\')<br/>print(df)<br/>df.to_csv(\'example.csv\', index=False)</code><p>In this example, we use the <strong>read_csv</strong> function to read the CSV file into a DataFrame. We then print the DataFrame and use the <strong>to_csv</strong> method to write it back to the CSV file.</p><h3>Best Practices for Working with CSV Files</h3><ul><li>Always specify the encoding when opening a CSV file to avoid encoding errors.</li><li>Use the <strong>newline</strong> parameter when opening a CSV file in write mode to avoid extra newline characters.</li><li>Use the <strong>csv.QUOTE_ALL</strong> parameter to quote all fields in the CSV file.</li><li>Use the <strong>pandas</strong> library for data analysis and manipulation.</li></ul><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this day\'s lesson, we learned how to work with CSV files in Python, including reading and writing them. We also learned how to use the <strong>pandas</strong> library for data analysis and manipulation. By following best practices and using the right libraries, we can easily work with CSV files in Python.</p>', 'Working with CSV Files in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(207, 220, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python exception handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Python Exception Handling</h3><p>Exception handling is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to handle errors and exceptions that may occur during the execution of your code, making your programs more robust and reliable. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of exception handling in Python, including try-except blocks, error types, and raising exceptions.</p><h3>Try-Except Blocks</h3><p>A try-except block is used to enclose a section of code that might potentially raise an exception. The <code>try</code> block contains the code that might raise an exception, while the <code>except</code> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception is raised.</p><p>Here is a basic example of a try-except block:</p><code>try:\n    # Code that might raise an exception\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised\n    print(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")</code><p>In this example, the <code>try</code> block attempts to divide 1 by 0, which raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. The <code>except</code> block catches this exception and prints an error message.</p><h3>Error Types</h3><p>Python has a variety of built-in error types that can be used to handle different types of exceptions. Some common error types include:</p><ul><li><code>ZeroDivisionError</code>: Raised when you attempt to divide by zero.</li><li><code>TypeError</code>: Raised when you attempt to perform an operation on a value of the wrong type.</li><li><code>ValueError</code>: Raised when a function or operation receives an argument with an incorrect value.</li><li><code>IndexError</code>: Raised when you attempt to access an element in a sequence that does not exist.</li><li><code>KeyError</code>: Raised when you attempt to access a key in a dictionary that does not exist.</li></ul><p>You can use these error types to catch specific exceptions and handle them accordingly.</p><h3>Raising Exceptions</h3><p>Python also allows you to raise exceptions manually using the <code>raise</code> statement. This can be useful for creating custom error messages or for signaling that an error has occurred.</p><p>Here is an example of raising an exception:</p><code>def divide(x, y):\n    if y == 0:\n        raise ZeroDivisionError(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")\n    return x / y</code><p>In this example, the <code>divide</code> function raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code> if the user attempts to divide by zero.</p><p>Exception handling is an important aspect of programming in Python, and it can help you create more robust and reliable programs. By using try-except blocks, error types, and raising exceptions, you can handle errors and exceptions in a way that is both efficient and effective.</p>', 'Understanding Python Exception Handling', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(208, 221, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python modules packages</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+modules+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+modules+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+modules+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+modules+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+modules+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Implementing Python Modules and Packages</h3><p>Welcome to Day 67 of our Python course. In this lesson, we will be covering the concepts of modules, packages, and importing modules in Python. These are essential building blocks of any large Python program, and understanding how to use them will help you to write more efficient and organized code.</p><p>A <strong>module</strong> is a file that contains a collection of related functions, classes, and variables. Modules are used to organize code into logical units that can be easily reused in other parts of a program. Python has a vast collection of built-in modules that can be used for various tasks such as file I/O, networking, and data structures.</p><h3>Creating and Using Modules</h3><p>To create a module, you simply need to write a Python file with a .py extension. For example, let\'s say we want to create a module called <code>math_utils.py</code> that contains some basic mathematical functions.</p><p>We can define the following functions in our <code>math_utils.py</code> file:</p><ul><li><code>add(a, b)</code>: returns the sum of two numbers</li><li><code>subtract(a, b)</code>: returns the difference of two numbers</li><li><code>multiply(a, b)</code>: returns the product of two numbers</li><li><code>divide(a, b)</code>: returns the quotient of two numbers</li></ul><p>Once we have defined our module, we can use it in another Python file by importing it. There are several ways to import a module in Python:</p><ul><li><code>import module_name</code>: imports the entire module</li><li><code>from module_name import function_name</code>: imports a specific function from the module</li><li><code>from module_name import *</code>: imports all functions and variables from the module</li></ul><p>For example, if we want to use the <code>add</code> function from our <code>math_utils</code> module, we can import it like this:</p><code>from math_utils import add<br>result = add(2, 3)<br>print(result)  # Output: 5</code><h3>Packages</h3><p>A <strong>package</strong> is a collection of related modules and subpackages. Packages are used to organize modules into a hierarchical structure that can be easily installed and imported. A package is essentially a directory that contains an <code>__init__.py</code> file, which is an empty file that indicates that the directory should be treated as a package.</p><p>For example, let\'s say we want to create a package called <code>math_package</code> that contains our <code>math_utils</code> module, as well as another module called <code>advanced_math</code>. We can create the following directory structure:</p><ul><li><code>math_package</code></li><li>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>__init__.py</code></li><li>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>math_utils.py</code></li><li>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>advanced_math.py</code></li></ul><p>We can then import our modules like this:</p><code>from math_package import math_utils<br>from math_package import advanced_math</code><h3>Importing Modules</h3><p>There are several ways to import modules in Python, including:</p><ul><li><code>import module_name</code>: imports the entire module</li><li><code>from module_name import function_name</code>: imports a specific function from the module</li><li><code>from module_name import *</code>: imports all functions and variables from the module</li><li><code>import module_name as alias</code>: imports the module and assigns it an alias</li></ul><p>For example:</p><code>import math_utils as mu<br>result = mu.add(2, 3)<br>print(result)  # Output: 5</code><p>In conclusion, modules and packages are essential components of any large Python program. By organizing our code into logical units, we can make it more efficient, readable, and maintainable. In the next lesson, we will be covering more advanced topics in Python programming.</p>', 'Implementing Python Modules and Packages', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(209, 222, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python debugging tools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+debugging+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+debugging+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+debugging+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+debugging+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+debugging+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Debugging Techniques and Tools</h3><p>Debugging is an essential part of the programming process. It involves identifying and fixing errors or bugs in the code. Python provides several debugging techniques and tools that can help you debug your code efficiently. In this lesson, we will explore the different debugging techniques and tools available in Python.</p><h3>What is Debugging?</h3><p>Debugging is the process of finding and fixing errors in the code. It involves identifying the source of the error, understanding the cause of the error, and making the necessary changes to fix the error. Debugging can be a time-consuming process, but it is an essential part of the programming process.</p><h3>Why is Debugging Important?</h3><p>Debugging is important because it helps to ensure that the code is working correctly and efficiently. It also helps to prevent errors from occurring in the first place. By debugging your code, you can:</p><ul><li>Identify and fix errors before they cause problems</li><li>Improve the performance and efficiency of your code</li><li>Reduce the risk of errors and bugs</li><li>Ensure that your code is working as intended</li></ul><h3>Python Debugging Techniques</h3><p>Python provides several debugging techniques that can help you debug your code. Some of the most common techniques include:</p><ul><li><strong>Print Statements</strong>: Print statements can be used to print out the values of variables and expressions at different points in the code. This can help you understand what is happening in the code and identify where errors are occurring.</li><li><strong>PDB Module</strong>: The PDB module is a built-in Python module that provides a debugger. It allows you to set breakpoints, inspect variables, and step through the code line by line.</li><li><strong>Logging</strong>: Logging can be used to record events and errors in the code. This can help you identify where errors are occurring and what is happening in the code.</li></ul><h3>Using Print Statements for Debugging</h3><p>Print statements can be used to print out the values of variables and expressions at different points in the code. This can help you understand what is happening in the code and identify where errors are occurring. For example:</p><code>def add_numbers(a, b):<br>    print(&quot;The value of a is:&quot;, a)<br>    print(&quot;The value of b is:&quot;, b)<br>    result = a + b<br>    print(&quot;The result is:&quot;, result)<br>    return result<br><br>add_numbers(2, 3)</code><p>This code will print out the values of a, b, and the result of the addition. This can help you understand what is happening in the code and identify where errors are occurring.</p><h3>Using the PDB Module for Debugging</h3><p>The PDB module is a built-in Python module that provides a debugger. It allows you to set breakpoints, inspect variables, and step through the code line by line. To use the PDB module, you need to import it and then call the <code>set_trace()</code> function. For example:</p><code>import pdb<br><br>def add_numbers(a, b):<br>    pdb.set_trace()<br>    result = a + b<br>    return result<br><br>add_numbers(2, 3)</code><p>This code will start the debugger at the point where the <code>set_trace()</code> function is called. You can then use the debugger to inspect variables, step through the code line by line, and set breakpoints.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In conclusion, debugging is an essential part of the programming process. Python provides several debugging techniques and tools that can help you debug your code efficiently. By using print statements, the PDB module, and logging, you can identify and fix errors in your code and ensure that it is working correctly and efficiently.</p>', 'Python Debugging Techniques and Tools', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(210, 223, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python coding best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+coding+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+coding+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+coding+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+coding+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+coding+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Best Practices for Python Coding and Documentation</h3><p>Welcome to Day 69 of our Python course. In this lesson, we will cover the importance of following best practices for coding and documentation in Python. This includes adhering to the PEP 8 style guide, using docstrings, and writing effective code comments.</p><p>Python has an official style guide called PEP 8, which provides guidelines for coding conventions, such as naming variables, functions, and classes, as well as guidelines for code indentation and spacing. Following PEP 8 makes your code more readable and maintainable.</p><h3>PEP 8 Guidelines</h3><ul><li><strong>Indentation</strong>: Use 4 spaces for indentation, and avoid using tabs.</li><li><strong>Line Length</strong>: Limit your lines to 79 characters or less.</li><li><strong>Naming Conventions</strong>: Use lowercase letters with words separated by underscores for variable and function names.</li><li><strong>Spacing</strong>: Use two blank lines to separate top-level functions and classes, and one blank line to separate methods within a class.</li></ul><p>Let\'s look at an example of how PEP 8 guidelines can improve the readability of your code:</p><code>def calculate_area(width, height):\n    # calculate area\n    area = width * height\n    return area</code><p>becomes:</p><code>def calculate_area(width: int, height: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the area of a rectangle.\n\n    Args:\n        width (int): The width of the rectangle.\n        height (int): The height of the rectangle.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The area of the rectangle.\n    \"\"\"\n    area = width * height\n    return area</code><h3>Docstrings</h3><p>Docstrings are strings that are used to document functions, classes, and modules. They are typically written as a string literal that occurs as the first statement in a function, class, or module. Docstrings are used to provide a description of what the function, class, or module does, as well as any other relevant information.</p><p>Let\'s look at an example of a docstring:</p><code>def greet(name: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Print a personalized greeting message.\n\n    Args:\n        name (str): The name of the person to greet.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(f\"Hello, {name}!\")</code><h3>Code Comments</h3><p>Code comments are used to explain what your code is doing. They are typically written as a line of text that starts with the # symbol. Code comments should be used sparingly, as they can make your code more difficult to read if overused.</p><p>Let\'s look at an example of a code comment:</p><code>def calculate_area(width: int, height: int) -> int:\n    # calculate area by multiplying width and height\n    area = width * height\n    return area</code><p>By following PEP 8 guidelines, using docstrings, and writing effective code comments, you can make your code more readable, maintainable, and efficient. Remember, the key to writing good code is to make it easy for others (and yourself) to understand what your code is doing.</p>', 'Best Practices for Python Coding and Documentation', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(211, 224, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming internship</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming+internship\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming+internship\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming+internship/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming+internship/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming+internship\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Review and Assessment of Python Internship Progress</h3><p>Welcome to Day 70 of the Python course. Today, we will review the progress made during the internship and assess the skills learned. This is an essential step in identifying areas of improvement and setting future goals.</p><h3>Internship Review</h3><p>The internship is a critical component of the Python course, providing hands-on experience and real-world applications of the concepts learned. During the internship, you worked on various projects, collaborated with peers, and received feedback from mentors. Now, it\'s time to reflect on your experiences and identify key takeaways.</p><ul><li>Review the projects you completed during the internship, highlighting successes and challenges faced.</li><li>Identify the skills you developed, such as data analysis, machine learning, or web development.</li><li>Reflect on the feedback received from mentors and peers, and think about how you can apply it to future projects.</li></ul><h3>Progress Assessment</h3><p>To assess your progress, let\'s evaluate your understanding of the concepts learned during the internship. Consider the following areas:</p><ul><li><strong>Data Structures</strong>: arrays, lists, dictionaries, and sets.</li><li><strong>Algorithms</strong>: sorting, searching, and graph traversal.</li><li><strong>Object-Oriented Programming</strong>: classes, objects, inheritance, and polymorphism.</li><li><strong>Data Analysis</strong>: data cleaning, visualization, and statistical analysis.</li></ul><p>Assess your proficiency in these areas by answering the following questions:</p><ul><li>Can you explain the difference between a list and a dictionary in Python?</li><li>How would you implement a sorting algorithm in Python?</li><li>What is the purpose of inheritance in object-oriented programming?</li></ul><h3>Future Goals</h3><p>Based on your progress assessment, set specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) goals for the next phase of your Python journey. Consider the following:</p><ul><li>What skills do you want to develop further?</li><li>What projects do you want to work on?</li><li>What are your long-term career goals in the field of Python programming?</li></ul><p>Example of a SMART goal: <code>I want to develop a web scraper using Python and BeautifulSoup within the next 6 weeks, dedicating 5 hours per week to the project.</code></p><h3>Best Practices for Future Success</h3><p>To ensure continued success in your Python journey, follow these best practices:</p><ul><li>Practice regularly, using online platforms or personal projects.</li><li>Stay up-to-date with industry trends and new technologies.</li><li>Join online communities or forums to connect with other Python developers.</li><li>Read books or articles on advanced topics, such as machine learning or data science.</li></ul>', 'Review and Assessment of Python Internship Progress', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(212, 225, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Data Structures</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the fundamental data structures in Python, including lists, tuples, and dictionaries. These data structures are essential for storing and manipulating data in Python.</p><h3>Lists</h3><p>A list is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are denoted by square brackets <code>[]</code> and are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of lists:</p><ul><li>Lists are ordered, meaning that the order of the items in the list matters.</li><li>Lists are mutable, meaning that they can be modified after creation.</li><li>Lists can contain duplicate values.</li></ul><p>Some common list operations include:</p><ul><li><code>append()</code>: adds an item to the end of the list.</li><li><code>insert()</code>: inserts an item at a specified position in the list.</li><li><code>remove()</code>: removes the first occurrence of an item in the list.</li><li><code>sort()</code>: sorts the items in the list in ascending order.</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a list:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>my_list.append(6)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br>my_list.insert(0, 0)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br>my_list.remove(0)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]</code><h3>Tuples</h3><p>A tuple is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are denoted by parentheses <code>()</code> and are immutable, meaning they cannot be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of tuples:</p><ul><li>Tuples are ordered, meaning that the order of the items in the tuple matters.</li><li>Tuples are immutable, meaning that they cannot be modified after creation.</li><li>Tuples can contain duplicate values.</li></ul><p>Some common tuple operations include:</p><ul><li><code>index()</code>: returns the index of the first occurrence of an item in the tuple.</li><li><code>count()</code>: returns the number of occurrences of an item in the tuple.</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a tuple:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)<br>print(my_tuple.index(3))  # Output: 2<br>print(my_tuple.count(3))  # Output: 1</code><h3>Dictionaries</h3><p>A dictionary is a collection of key-value pairs where each key is unique and maps to a specific value. Dictionaries are denoted by curly brackets <code>{}</code> and are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of dictionaries:</p><ul><li>Dictionaries are unordered, meaning that the order of the key-value pairs does not matter.</li><li>Dictionaries are mutable, meaning that they can be modified after creation.</li><li>Dictionaries cannot contain duplicate keys.</li></ul><p>Some common dictionary operations include:</p><ul><li><code>keys()</code>: returns a list of all the keys in the dictionary.</li><li><code>values()</code>: returns a list of all the values in the dictionary.</li><li><code>items()</code>: returns a list of all the key-value pairs in the dictionary.</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a dictionary:</p><code>my_dict = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}<br>print(my_dict.keys())  # Output: dict_keys([\'name\', \'age\'])<br>print(my_dict.values())  # Output: dict_values([\'John\', 30])<br>print(my_dict.items())  # Output: dict_items([(\'name\', \'John\'), (\'age\', 30)])</code><p>In conclusion, lists, tuples, and dictionaries are essential data structures in Python that can be used to store and manipulate data. Understanding the characteristics and operations of each data structure is crucial for effective programming in Python.</p>', 'Introduction to Python Data Structures', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(213, 226, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: list methods, list comprehensions, list slicing, python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=list+methods%2C+list+comprehensions%2C+list+slicing%2C+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/list+methods%2C+list+comprehensions%2C+list+slicing%2C+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/list+methods%2C+list+comprehensions%2C+list+slicing%2C+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/list+methods%2C+list+comprehensions%2C+list+slicing%2C+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=list+methods%2C+list+comprehensions%2C+list+slicing%2C+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Working with Lists in Python</h2><p>In this lesson, we will cover the basics of working with lists in Python. We will learn about list methods, list comprehensions, and list slicing.</p><h3>List Methods</h3><p>List methods are built-in functions that can be used to manipulate lists in Python. Here are some common list methods:</p><ul><li><code>append(item)</code> - Adds an item to the end of the list.</li><li><code>extend(iterable)</code> - Adds all items from the iterable to the end of the list.</li><li><code>insert(index, item)</code> - Inserts an item at a specific index in the list.</li><li><code>remove(item)</code> - Removes the first occurrence of the item in the list.</li><li><code>pop(index)</code> - Removes and returns the item at a specific index in the list.</li><li><code>index(item)</code> - Returns the index of the first occurrence of the item in the list.</li><li><code>count(item)</code> - Returns the number of occurrences of the item in the list.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use some of these methods:</p><p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code></p><p><code>my_list.append(6)</code> - Output: <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]</code></p><p><code>my_list.extend([7, 8, 9])</code> - Output: <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code></p><p><code>my_list.insert(2, 10)</code> - Output: <code>[1, 2, 10, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code></p><p><code>my_list.remove(10)</code> - Output: <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code></p><p><code>my_list.pop(2)</code> - Output: <code>3</code> and <code>[1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code></p><p><code>my_list.index(5)</code> - Output: <code>4</code></p><p><code>my_list.count(5)</code> - Output: <code>1</code></p><h3>List Comprehensions</h3><p>List comprehensions are a concise way to create lists in Python. They consist of an expression followed by a for clause, then zero or more for or if clauses. The result is a new list resulting from evaluating the expression in the context of the for and if clauses which follow it.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use list comprehensions:</p><p><code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code></p><p><code>double_numbers = [x * 2 for x in numbers]</code> - Output: <code>[2, 4, 6, 8, 10]</code></p><p><code>even_numbers = [x for x in numbers if x % 2 == 0]</code> - Output: <code>[2, 4]</code></p><p><code>square_numbers = [x ** 2 for x in numbers]</code> - Output: <code>[1, 4, 9, 16, 25]</code></p><h3>List Slicing</h3><p>List slicing is a way to extract a subset of elements from a list in Python. It consists of a start index, a stop index, and a step size. The start index is inclusive, the stop index is exclusive, and the step size is the number of elements to skip between each element in the subset.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use list slicing:</p><p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code></p><p><code>my_list[:3]</code> - Output: <code>[1, 2, 3]</code></p><p><code>my_list[3:6]</code> - Output: <code>[4, 5, 6]</code></p><p><code>my_list[::2]</code> - Output: <code>[1, 3, 5, 7, 9]</code></p><p><code>my_list[::-1]</code> - Output: <code>[9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</code></p><h2>Quiz</h2><ul><li>What is the output of the following code: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code> <code>my_list.append(6)</code> <code>print(my_list)</code></li><li>What is the output of the following code: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code> <code>my_list.extend([7, 8, 9])</code> <code>print(my_list)</code></li><li>What is the output of the following code: <code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code> <code>double_numbers = [x * 2 for x in numbers]</code> <code>print(double_numbers)</code></li><li>What is the output of the following code: <code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code> <code>even_numbers = [x for x in numbers if x % 2 == 0]</code> <code>print(even_numbers)</code></li><li>What is the output of the following code: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code> <code>print(my_list[:3])</code></li><li>What is the output of the following code: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code> <code>print(my_list[3:6])</code></li><li>What is the output of the following code: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code> <code>print(my_list[::2])</code></li><li>What is the output of the following code: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code> <code>print(my_list[::-1])</code></li></ul>', 'Working with Lists in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(214, 227, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python tuples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+tuples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+tuples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Tuples in Python</h3><p>Tuples are a fundamental data structure in Python, used to store multiple values in a single variable. In this lesson, we will explore tuple methods, tuple unpacking, and tuple concatenation.</p><h3>Tuple Methods</h3><p>Python provides several built-in methods for working with tuples. Some of the most commonly used tuple methods include:</p><ul><li><code>index()</code>: Returns the index of the first occurrence of the specified value.</li><li><code>count()</code>: Returns the number of occurrences of the specified value.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of using tuple methods:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 2, 4)<br>print(my_tuple.index(2))  # Output: 1<br>print(my_tuple.count(2))  # Output: 2</code><h3>Tuple Unpacking</h3><p>Tuple unpacking is a feature in Python that allows you to assign the values of a tuple to multiple variables. This can be useful when working with functions that return multiple values.</p><p>Here is an example of tuple unpacking:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)<br>a, b, c = my_tuple<br>print(a)  # Output: 1<br>print(b)  # Output: 2<br>print(c)  # Output: 3</code><p>You can also use tuple unpacking with functions that return multiple values:</p><code>def my_function():<br>    return 1, 2, 3<br><br>a, b, c = my_function()<br>print(a)  # Output: 1<br>print(b)  # Output: 2<br>print(c)  # Output: 3</code><h3>Tuple Concatenation</h3><p>Tuple concatenation is the process of combining two or more tuples into a single tuple. This can be done using the <code>+</code> operator.</p><p>Here is an example of tuple concatenation:</p><code>tuple1 = (1, 2, 3)<br>tuple2 = (4, 5, 6)<br>tuple3 = tuple1 + tuple2<br>print(tuple3)  # Output: (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6)</code><p>You can also use the <code>+</code> operator to concatenate a tuple with a list or other iterable:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)<br>my_list = [4, 5, 6]<br>my_tuple += tuple(my_list)<br>print(my_tuple)  # Output: (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6)</code><p>In summary, tuples are a powerful data structure in Python that can be used to store multiple values in a single variable. Tuple methods, such as <code>index()</code> and <code>count()</code>, can be used to manipulate and inspect tuples. Tuple unpacking allows you to assign the values of a tuple to multiple variables, and tuple concatenation allows you to combine two or more tuples into a single tuple.</p>', 'Understanding Tuples in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(215, 228, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Python dictionary methods dictionary comprehensions dictionary iteration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Python+dictionary+methods+dictionary+comprehensions+dictionary+iteration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Python+dictionary+methods+dictionary+comprehensions+dictionary+iteration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Python+dictionary+methods+dictionary+comprehensions+dictionary+iteration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Python+dictionary+methods+dictionary+comprehensions+dictionary+iteration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Python+dictionary+methods+dictionary+comprehensions+dictionary+iteration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Diving into Dictionaries in Python</h2>\n  <p>In this lesson, we will be exploring <strong>Dictionary Methods</strong>, <strong>Dictionary Comprehensions</strong>, and <strong>Dictionary Iteration</strong>. Dictionaries are an essential data structure in Python, and mastering these concepts will help you write more efficient and effective code.</p>\n  <h3>Dictionary Methods</h3>\n  <p>Dictionaries have several built-in methods that allow you to manipulate and access their data. Here are some of the most commonly used dictionary methods:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><code>keys()</code> - Returns a view object that displays a list of all the available keys in the dictionary.</li>\n    <li><code>values()</code> - Returns a view object that displays a list of all the available values in the dictionary.</li>\n    <li><code>items()</code> - Returns a view object that displays a list of all the available key-value pairs in the dictionary.</li>\n    <li><code>clear()</code> - Removes all items from the dictionary.</li>\n    <li><code>pop(key, default=None)</code> - Removes the specified key-value pair from the dictionary and returns the value. If the key does not exist, it returns the default value.</li>\n    <li><code>popitem()</code> - Removes and returns a random key-value pair from the dictionary.</li>\n    <li><code>update(other)</code> - Updates the dictionary with the key-value pairs from the other dictionary.</li>\n    <li><code>get(key, default=None)</code> - Returns the value for the specified key if it exists in the dictionary. If not, it returns the default value.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Here\'s an example of how to use these methods:</p>\n  <code>\n    # Create a dictionary\n    person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \'city\': \'New York\'}\n    \n    # Print all keys\n    print(person.keys())  # Output: dict_keys([\'name\', \'age\', \'city\'])\n    \n    # Print all values\n    print(person.values())  # Output: dict_values([\'John\', 30, \'New York\'])\n    \n    # Print all key-value pairs\n    print(person.items())  # Output: dict_items([(\'name\', \'John\'), (\'age\', 30), (\'city\', \'New York\')])\n    \n    # Remove all items\n    person.clear()\n    print(person)  # Output: {}\n    \n    # Remove a key-value pair\n    person[\'name\'] = \'Jane\'\n    print(person.pop(\'name\'))  # Output: Jane\n    \n    # Update the dictionary\n    person.update({\'age\': 31, \'country\': \'USA\'})\n    print(person)  # Output: {\'age\': 31, \'country\': \'USA\'}\n    \n    # Get a value\n    print(person.get(\'age\'))  # Output: 31\n  </code>\n  <h3>Dictionary Comprehensions</h3>\n  <p>Dictionary comprehensions are a concise way to create dictionaries from other iterables. They consist of an expression followed by a for clause, then zero or more for or if clauses. The result is a new dictionary.</p>\n  <p>Here\'s an example of how to use dictionary comprehensions:</p>\n  <code>\n    # Create a dictionary\n    squares = {x: x**2 for x in range(10)}\n    print(squares)  # Output: {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9, 4: 16, 5: 25, 6: 36, 7: 49, 8: 64, 9: 81}\n  </code>\n  <h3>Dictionary Iteration</h3>\n  <p>There are several ways to iterate over dictionaries in Python. Here are a few examples:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><code>for key in dictionary:</code> - Iterates over the keys in the dictionary.</li>\n    <li><code>for value in dictionary.values():</code> - Iterates over the values in the dictionary.</li>\n    <li><code>for key, value in dictionary.items():</code> - Iterates over the key-value pairs in the dictionary.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Here\'s an example of how to use these methods:</p>\n  <code>\n    # Create a dictionary\n    person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \'city\': \'New York\'}\n    \n    # Iterate over keys\n    for key in person:\n        print(key)\n    # Output: name\n    # Output: age\n    # Output: city\n    \n    # Iterate over values\n    for value in person.values():\n        print(value)\n    # Output: John\n    # Output: 30\n    # Output: New York\n    \n    # Iterate over key-value pairs\n    for key, value in person.items():\n        print(f\"{key}: {value}\")\n    # Output: name: John\n    # Output: age: 30\n    # Output: city: New York\n  </code>\n  <h3>Quiz</h3>\n  <p>Here are some practice questions to help you reinforce your understanding of dictionary methods, dictionary comprehensions, and dictionary iteration:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>What is the output of the following code?\n      <code>\n        person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}\n        print(person.keys())\n      </code>\n    </li>\n    <li>What is the output of the following code?\n      <code>\n        person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}\n        print(person.values())\n      </code>\n    </li>\n    <li>What is the output of the following code?\n      <code>\n        person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}\n        print(person.items())\n      </code>\n    </li>\n    <li>What is the output of the following code?\n      <code>\n        squares = {x: x**2 for x in range(10)}\n        print(squares)\n      </code>\n    </li>\n    <li>What is the output of the following code?\n      <code>\n        person = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}\n        for key in person:\n            print(key)\n      </code>\n    </li>\n  </ul>', 'Diving into Dictionaries in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(216, 229, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><html><head><title>Practicing with Python Data Structures</title></head><body><h1>Practicing with Python Data Structures</h1><p>Welcome to Day 75 of our Python course! Today, we will be practicing with Python data structures. Data structures are a crucial part of programming and are used to store and organize data in a way that makes it easy to access, modify, and manipulate.</p><h2>Data Structure Exercises</h2><p>Here are some exercises to help you practice with Python data structures:</p><ul><li><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Create a list of your favorite foods. Use the <code>len()</code> function to find the length of the list.</li><li><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Create a dictionary to store information about your favorite books. Use the <code>keys()</code> and <code>values()</code> functions to find the keys and values in the dictionary.</li><li><strong>Exercise 3:</strong> Create a set to store unique names. Use the <code>add()</code> method to add names to the set and the <code>remove()</code> method to remove names from the set.</li><li><strong>Exercise 4:</strong> Create a tuple to store information about your favorite movies. Use the <code>index()</code> method to find the index of a movie in the tuple.</li></ul><p>To complete these exercises, you can use the following code:</p><code>\n# Exercise 1\nfoods = [\'pizza\', \'sushi\', \'tacos\']\nprint(len(foods))\n\n# Exercise 2\nbooks = {\'title\': \'To Kill a Mockingbird\', \'author\': \'Harper Lee\', \'year\': 1960}\nprint(books.keys())\nprint(books.values())\n\n# Exercise 3\nnames = set()\nnames.add(\'John\')\nnames.add(\'Jane\')\nnames.remove(\'John\')\nprint(names)\n\n# Exercise 4\nmovies = (\'The Shawshank Redemption\', \'The Godfather\', \'The Dark Knight\')\nprint(movies.index(\'The Shawshank Redemption\'))\n</code><h2>Data Structure Projects</h2><p>Here are some project ideas to help you practice with Python data structures:</p><ul><li><strong>Project 1:</strong> Create a program to store and organize your favorite songs. Use a dictionary to store information about each song, including the title, artist, and genre.</li><li><strong>Project 2:</strong> Create a program to store and organize your favorite sports teams. Use a list to store information about each team, including the team name, location, and players.</li><li><strong>Project 3:</strong> Create a program to store and organize your favorite books. Use a set to store unique book titles and a dictionary to store information about each book, including the title, author, and year published.</li></ul><p>To complete these projects, you can use the following code:</p><code>\n# Project 1\nsongs = {}\nsongs[\'title\'] = \'Happy\'\nsongs[\'artist\'] = \'Pharrell Williams\'\nsongs[\'genre\'] = \'Pop\'\nprint(songs)\n\n# Project 2\nteams = []\nteams.append({\'name\': \'New York Yankees\', \'location\': \'New York\', \'players\': [\'Aaron Judge\', \'Gerrit Cole\']})\nteams.append({\'name\': \'Los Angeles Dodgers\', \'location\': \'Los Angeles\', \'players\': [\'Mookie Betts\', \'Clayton Kershaw\']})\nprint(teams)\n\n# Project 3\nbooks = set()\nbooks.add(\'To Kill a Mockingbird\')\nbooks.add(\'The Great Gatsby\')\nprint(books)\nbooks_info = {}\nbooks_info[\'To Kill a Mockingbird\'] = {\'author\': \'Harper Lee\', \'year\': 1960}\nbooks_info[\'The Great Gatsby\'] = {\'author\': \'F. Scott Fitzgerald\', \'year\': 1925}\nprint(books_info)\n</code><h2>Data Structure Examples</h2><p>Here are some examples of how data structures are used in real-world applications:</p><ul><li><strong>Example 1:</strong> A web browser uses a tree data structure to store and organize web pages. Each node in the tree represents a web page, and the edges represent the links between pages.</li><li><strong>Example 2:</strong> A social media platform uses a graph data structure to store and organize user relationships. Each node in the graph represents a user, and the edges represent the friendships between users.</li><li><strong>Example 3:</strong> A database management system uses a hash table data structure to store and organize data. Each key in the hash table represents a unique identifier, and the value represents the corresponding data.</li></ul><p>To learn more about these examples, you can use the following code:</p><code>\n# Example 1\nclass Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.children = []\n\nclass Tree:\n    def __init__(self, root):\n        self.root = Node(root)\n\n    def add_node(self, parent, child):\n        node = self.find_node(parent)\n        if node:\n            node.children.append(Node(child))\n\n    def find_node(self, value):\n        if self.root.value == value:\n            return self.root\n        for child in self.root.children:\n            node = self.find_node(value, child)\n            if node:\n                return node\n        return None\n\n# Example 2\nclass User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        self.friends = []\n\nclass Graph:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.users = {}\n\n    def add_user(self, name):\n        self.users[name] = User(name)\n\n    def add_friendship(self, user1, user2):\n        self.users[user1].friends.append(self.users[user2])\n\n# Example 3\nclass HashTable:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.size = 10\n        self.table = [[] for _ in range(self.size)]\n\n    def hash(self, key):\n        return hash(key) % self.size\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        index = self.hash(key)\n        for pair in self.table[index]:\n            if pair[0] == key:\n                pair[1] = value\n                return\n        self.table[index].append([key, value])\n</code>', 'Practicing with Python Data Structures', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
