(217, 230, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures review</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><html><body><h1>Reviewing Python Data Structures</h1><p>In this lesson, we will be reviewing three fundamental data structures in Python: lists, tuples, and dictionaries.</p><h2>Review of Lists</h2><p>Lists are ordered collections of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. They are denoted by square brackets <code>[]</code> and can be created using the following syntax:</p><ul><li><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, \'hello\', 4.5]</code></li></ul><p>Lists are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation. Here are some common list methods:</p><ul><li><code>append(item)</code> adds an item to the end of the list</li><li><code>insert(index, item)</code> inserts an item at a specified index</li><li><code>remove(item)</code> removes the first occurrence of an item</li><li><code>sort()</code> sorts the list in-place</li><li><code>reverse()</code> reverses the list in-place</li></ul><p>Example of using list methods:</p><pre><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]\nmy_list.append(4)\nprint(my_list)  # [1, 2, 3, 4]\nmy_list.insert(1, 2.5)\nprint(my_list)  # [1, 2.5, 2, 3, 4]\nmy_list.remove(2)\nprint(my_list)  # [1, 2.5, 3, 4]\nmy_list.sort()\nprint(my_list)  # [1, 2.5, 3, 4]\nmy_list.reverse()\nprint(my_list)  # [4, 3, 2.5, 1]</code></pre><h2>Review of Tuples</h2><p>Tuples are ordered, immutable collections of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. They are denoted by parentheses <code>()</code> and can be created using the following syntax:</p><ul><li><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, \'hello\', 4.5)</code></li></ul><p>Tuples are immutable, meaning they cannot be modified after creation. Here are some common tuple methods:</p><ul><li><code>index(item)</code> returns the index of the first occurrence of an item</li><li><code>count(item)</code> returns the number of occurrences of an item</li></ul><p>Example of using tuple methods:</p><pre><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)\nprint(my_tuple.index(2))  # 1\nprint(my_tuple.count(1))  # 1</code></pre><h2>Review of Dictionaries</h2><p>Dictionaries are unordered collections of key-value pairs. They are denoted by curly brackets <code>{}</code> and can be created using the following syntax:</p><ul><li><code>my_dict = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}</code></li></ul><p>Dictionaries are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation. Here are some common dictionary methods:</p><ul><li><code>keys()</code> returns a view object that displays a list of all keys in the dictionary</li><li><code>values()</code> returns a view object that displays a list of all values in the dictionary</li><li><code>items()</code> returns a view object that displays a list of all key-value pairs in the dictionary</li><li><code>update(other_dict)</code> updates the dictionary with the key-value pairs from another dictionary</li><li><code>pop(key)</code> removes and returns the value for a specified key</li></ul><p>Example of using dictionary methods:</p><pre><code>my_dict = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}\nprint(my_dict.keys())  # dict_keys([\'name\', \'age\'])\nprint(my_dict.values())  # dict_values([\'John\', 30])\nprint(my_dict.items())  # dict_items([(\'name\', \'John\'), (\'age\', 30)])\nmy_dict.update({\'city\': \'New York\'})\nprint(my_dict)  # {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \'city\': \'New York\'}\nprint(my_dict.pop(\'age\'))  # 30</code></pre>', 'Reviewing Python Data Structures', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(218, 231, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures assessment</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures+assessment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures+assessment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures+assessment/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures+assessment/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures+assessment\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Assessment of Python Data Structures Knowledge</h1>', 'Assessment of Python Data Structures Knowledge', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(219, 232, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python web scraping</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+web+scraping\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+web+scraping\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+web+scraping/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+web+scraping/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+web+scraping\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div>Array', 'Introduction to Web Scraping with Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(220, 233, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv json pandas</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+json+pandas\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+json+pandas\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+json+pandas/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+json+pandas/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+json+pandas\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Day 79: Working with CSV and JSON Files in Python</h2>\n<p>Welcome to Day 79 of our Python course! Today, we\'re going to explore how to work with CSV and JSON files in Python. We\'ll also be using the Pandas library to make our lives easier.</p>\n<h3>What are CSV and JSON Files?</h3>\n<p>CSV stands for Comma Separated Values, and it\'s a type of file that stores tabular data. Each row in the file represents a record, and each column represents a field. JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation, and it\'s a lightweight data interchange format that\'s easy to read and write.</p>\n<h3>Why Use CSV and JSON Files?</h3>\n<p>CSV and JSON files are widely used in data science and web development. They\'re easy to read and write, and they can be easily imported and exported from most programming languages. They\'re also great for storing and transferring data between different systems.</p>\n<h3>Working with CSV Files in Python</h3>\n<p>In Python, we can use the `csv` module to work with CSV files. The `csv` module provides classes for reading and writing CSV files. We can also use the `pandas` library to make our lives easier.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reading a CSV File:</strong> We can use the `csv.reader` class to read a CSV file. The `csv.reader` class returns an iterator that produces rows from the CSV file.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing a CSV File:</strong> We can use the `csv.writer` class to write a CSV file. The `csv.writer` class returns an object that we can use to write rows to the CSV file.</li>\n<li><strong>Pandas and CSV Files:</strong> We can use the `pandas` library to read and write CSV files. The `pandas` library provides a `read_csv` function that we can use to read a CSV file, and a `to_csv` function that we can use to write a CSV file.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how we can use the `csv` module to read and write a CSV file:</p>\n<code>\nimport csv\n\n# Reading a CSV File\nwith open(\'data.csv\', \'r\') as file:\n    reader = csv.reader(file)\n    for row in reader:\n        print(row)\n\n# Writing a CSV File\nwith open(\'data.csv\', \'w\') as file:\n    writer = csv.writer(file)\n    writer.writerow([\'Name\', \'Age\'])\n    writer.writerow([\'John\', 25])\n    writer.writerow([\'Jane\', 30])\n</code>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how we can use the `pandas` library to read and write a CSV file:</p>\n<code>\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Reading a CSV File\ndf = pd.read_csv(\'data.csv\')\nprint(df)\n\n# Writing a CSV File\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Jane\'], \'Age\': [25, 30]})\ndf.to_csv(\'data.csv\', index=False)\n</code>\n<h3>Working with JSON Files in Python</h3>\n<p>In Python, we can use the `json` module to work with JSON files. The `json` module provides functions for encoding and decoding JSON data.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reading a JSON File:</strong> We can use the `json.load` function to read a JSON file. The `json.load` function returns a Python object that we can use to access the JSON data.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing a JSON File:</strong> We can use the `json.dump` function to write a JSON file. The `json.dump` function takes a Python object and writes it to a JSON file.</li>\n<li><strong>Pandas and JSON Files:</strong> We can use the `pandas` library to read and write JSON files. The `pandas` library provides a `read_json` function that we can use to read a JSON file, and a `to_json` function that we can use to write a JSON file.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how we can use the `json` module to read and write a JSON file:</p>\n<code>\nimport json\n\n# Reading a JSON File\nwith open(\'data.json\', \'r\') as file:\n    data = json.load(file)\n    print(data)\n\n# Writing a JSON File\ndata = {\'Name\': \'John\', \'Age\': 25}\nwith open(\'data.json\', \'w\') as file:\n    json.dump(data, file)\n</code>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how we can use the `pandas` library to read and write a JSON file:</p>\n<code>\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Reading a JSON File\ndf = pd.read_json(\'data.json\')\nprint(df)\n\n# Writing a JSON File\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Jane\'], \'Age\': [25, 30]})\ndf.to_json(\'data.json\', orient=\'records\')\n</code>\n<h3>Quiz Time!</h3>\n<p>Now it\'s time to test your knowledge! Here are some questions to try:</p>\n<quiz>\n<li>What is the purpose of the `csv` module in Python?</li>\n<li>How can we use the `pandas` library to read a CSV file?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between the `json.load` and `json.dump` functions?</li>\n</quiz>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>That\'s it for today\'s lesson! We learned how to work with CSV and JSON files in Python, and how to use the `pandas` library to make our lives easier. Remember to practice what you\'ve learned by working on some exercises and projects.</p>', 'Working with CSV and JSON Files in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(221, 234, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: data visualization matplotlib seaborn</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=data+visualization+matplotlib+seaborn\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/data+visualization+matplotlib+seaborn\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/data+visualization+matplotlib+seaborn/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/data+visualization+matplotlib+seaborn/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=data+visualization+matplotlib+seaborn\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Exploring Data Visualization with Matplotlib and Seaborn</h2>\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>Data visualization is a crucial aspect of data analysis, allowing us to communicate insights and trends in a clear and concise manner. In this lesson, we\'ll explore two popular Python libraries for data visualization: Matplotlib and Seaborn.</p>\n<h3>Installing Required Libraries</h3>\n<p>To get started, we\'ll need to install the required libraries. Run the following command in your terminal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>pip install matplotlib</code></li>\n<li><code>pip install seaborn</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Matplotlib Basics</h3>\n<p>Matplotlib is a comprehensive library for creating high-quality 2D and 3D plots. Let\'s create a simple line plot using Matplotlib:</p>\n<pre><code>\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\ny = [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]\n\nplt.plot(x, y)\nplt.title(\'Line Plot\')\nplt.xlabel(\'X-axis\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Y-axis\')\nplt.show()\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code creates a line plot with the x-axis ranging from 1 to 5 and the y-axis ranging from 1 to 25.</p>\n<h3>Customizing Plots with Matplotlib</h3>\n<p>Matplotlib offers a wide range of customization options. Let\'s create a plot with a custom title, labels, and colors:</p>\n<pre><code>\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\ny = [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]\n\nplt.plot(x, y, label=\'Line 1\', color=\'red\')\nplt.plot(x, [1, 9, 25, 49, 81], label=\'Line 2\', color=\'green\')\nplt.title(\'Customized Plot\')\nplt.xlabel(\'X-axis\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Y-axis\')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code creates a plot with two lines, each with a custom label and color.</p>\n<h3>Seaborn Basics</h3>\n<p>Seaborn is a visualization library built on top of Matplotlib. It provides a high-level interface for creating informative and attractive statistical graphics. Let\'s create a simple scatter plot using Seaborn:</p>\n<pre><code>\nimport seaborn as sns\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nsns.set()\ntips = sns.load_dataset(\'tips\')\nplt.scatter(tips[\'total_bill\'], tips[\'tip\'])\nplt.title(\'Scatter Plot\')\nplt.xlabel(\'Total Bill\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Tip\')\nplt.show()\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code creates a scatter plot with the total bill on the x-axis and the tip on the y-axis.</p>\n<h3>Customizing Plots with Seaborn</h3>\n<p>Seaborn offers a wide range of customization options. Let\'s create a plot with a custom title, labels, and colors:</p>\n<pre><code>\nimport seaborn as sns\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nsns.set()\ntips = sns.load_dataset(\'tips\')\nplt.scatter(tips[\'total_bill\'], tips[\'tip\'], label=\'Scatter Plot\', color=\'blue\')\nplt.title(\'Customized Plot\')\nplt.xlabel(\'Total Bill\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Tip\')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code creates a plot with a scatter plot, custom title, labels, and colors.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>In this lesson, we\'ve explored the basics of data visualization with Matplotlib and Seaborn. We\'ve created simple plots, customized plots, and loaded datasets using Seaborn. With this knowledge, you\'re ready to start exploring the world of data visualization.</p>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What is the purpose of Matplotlib?</strong></li>\n<li><strong>What is the purpose of Seaborn?</strong></li>\n<li><strong>How do you install Matplotlib?</strong></li>\n<li><strong>How do you install Seaborn?</strong></li>\n<li><strong>What is the difference between Matplotlib and Seaborn?</strong></li>\n</ul>\n', 'Exploring Data Visualization with Matplotlib and Seaborn', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(222, 235, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: object-oriented programming python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=object-oriented+programming+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/object-oriented+programming+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/object-oriented+programming+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/object-oriented+programming+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=object-oriented+programming+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Object-Oriented Programming in Python</h3>\n              <p>Today, we\'re going to explore one of the most fundamental concepts in programming: Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in Python. We\'ll cover classes, objects, and inheritance, and by the end of this lesson, you\'ll have a solid understanding of how to use these concepts in your own Python projects.</p>\n\n              <h4>Classes</h4>\n              <p>A class is a template for creating objects. It defines the properties and behaviors of an object. Think of a class as a blueprint for building a house. Just as a blueprint defines the layout, materials, and features of a house, a class defines the characteristics and actions of an object.</p>\n\n              <p>In Python, you can define a class using the `class` keyword followed by the name of the class. Here\'s an example:</p>\n              <code>\n                class Dog:\n                  pass\n              </code>\n              <p>This defines a class called `Dog`. The `pass` statement is a placeholder that indicates the class is defined, but it doesn\'t do anything yet.</p>\n\n              <p>To create an object from a class, you use the `()` operator. Here\'s an example:</p>\n              <code>\n                my_dog = Dog()\n              </code>\n              <p>This creates an object called `my_dog` from the `Dog` class.</p>\n\n              <h4>Objects</h4>\n              <p>An object is an instance of a class. It has its own set of attributes (data) and methods (functions). Think of an object as a specific instance of a class. For example, if you have a `Dog` class, an object could be a specific dog named `Fido`.</p>\n\n              <p>Objects can have attributes, which are data that describe the object. For example, a dog object might have attributes for its name, age, and breed. You can access an object\'s attributes using dot notation. Here\'s an example:</p>\n              <code>\n                my_dog.name = \'Fido\'\n                my_dog.age = 3\n                print(my_dog.name)  # Output: Fido\n                print(my_dog.age)   # Output: 3\n              </code>\n              <p>This code creates a `Dog` object called `my_dog` and sets its `name` and `age` attributes to `\'Fido\'` and `3`, respectively. It then prints the values of the `name` and `age` attributes.</p>\n\n              <h4>Inheritance</h4>\n              <p>Inheritance is a mechanism that allows one class to inherit the properties and behaviors of another class. The child class (the class that inherits) inherits all the attributes and methods of the parent class (the class that is being inherited from). Think of inheritance as a way of creating a new class that is a modified version of an existing class.</p>\n\n              <p>In Python, you can use the `class` keyword followed by the name of the child class and the parent class in parentheses to define a child class that inherits from a parent class. Here\'s an example:</p>\n              <code>\n                class Animal:\n                  def __init__(self, name):\n                    self.name = name\n\n                  def speak(self):\n                    print(\'The animal makes a sound.\')\n\n                class Dog(Animal):\n                  def __init__(self, name, breed):\n                    super().__init__(name)\n                    self.breed = breed\n\n                  def speak(self):\n                    print(\'The dog barks.\')\n\n                my_dog = Dog(\'Fido\', \'Golden Retriever\')\n                print(my_dog.name)  # Output: Fido\n                print(my_dog.breed)  # Output: Golden Retriever\n                my_dog.speak()  # Output: The dog barks.\n              </code>\n              <p>This code defines an `Animal` class with an `__init__` method that takes a `name` parameter and a `speak` method that prints a message. It then defines a `Dog` class that inherits from the `Animal` class and adds a `breed` attribute and a modified `speak` method. Finally, it creates a `Dog` object called `my_dog` and demonstrates how to access its attributes and methods.</p>\n\n              <h4>Quiz</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li>What is a class in Python?</li>\n                <li>What is an object in Python?</li>\n                <li>What is inheritance in Python?</li>\n                <li>How do you define a class in Python?</li>\n                <li>How do you create an object from a class in Python?</li>\n                <li>How do you access an object\'s attributes in Python?</li>\n                <li>How do you define a child class that inherits from a parent class in Python?</li>\n              </ul>', 'Understanding Object-Oriented Programming in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(223, 236, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Scikit-Learn Machine Learning Supervised Learning Python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Scikit-Learn+Machine+Learning+Supervised+Learning+Python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Scikit-Learn+Machine+Learning+Supervised+Learning+Python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Scikit-Learn+Machine+Learning+Supervised+Learning+Python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Scikit-Learn+Machine+Learning+Supervised+Learning+Python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Scikit-Learn+Machine+Learning+Supervised+Learning+Python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 82: Introduction to Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 82 of our Python journey! Today marks an exciting new chapter as we delve into the fascinating world of <strong>Machine Learning (ML)</strong>. You\'ve built a strong foundation in Python programming, and now we\'ll see how those skills can be applied to enable computers to learn from data. We\'ll specifically focus on a powerful Python library called <strong>Scikit-Learn</strong>, which makes implementing ML algorithms incredibly accessible.</p>\n\n<h3>What is Machine Learning?</h3>\n<p><strong>Machine Learning</strong> is a subset of Artificial Intelligence (AI) that empowers computer systems to learn from data, identify patterns, and make decisions or predictions with minimal human intervention. Unlike traditional programming, where you explicitly write rules for every possible scenario, in Machine Learning, you provide the machine with data, and it learns the rules or patterns itself.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Traditional Programming:</strong> You write code (rules) to process input and get output. Example: <code>if age &lt; 18: print(\"Minor\")</code></li>\n    <li><strong>Machine Learning:</strong> You provide input data and desired output, and the machine learns the rules. Example: Give it images of cats and dogs, tell it which is which, and it learns to classify new images.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Think of it like this: instead of telling a child every single rule about what makes a cat a cat, you show them many pictures of cats and dogs, labeling each one. Eventually, the child learns to distinguish between them on their own. Machine Learning algorithms work similarly, learning from vast amounts of labeled data.</p>\n\n<h3>Understanding Supervised Learning</h3>\n<p>Machine Learning is broadly categorized into three main types: Supervised Learning, Unsupervised Learning, and Reinforcement Learning. For beginners, <strong>Supervised Learning</strong> is the most common starting point and what we\'ll focus on today.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Supervised Learning</strong>, the algorithm learns from a dataset that includes both input features (the data points) and corresponding correct output labels (the answers). It\'s like having a teacher (the \'supervisor\') who provides the correct answers during the learning phase. The goal is for the model to learn the mapping from inputs to outputs so that it can accurately predict the output for new, unseen input data.</p>\n<p>Supervised Learning problems are typically divided into two categories:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Regression:</strong> This type of problem involves predicting a <strong>continuous numerical value</strong>. The output is a number within a range.\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Predicting house prices based on features like size and location, forecasting stock prices, predicting temperature, estimating a person\'s age.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Classification:</strong> This type of problem involves predicting a <strong>categorical label or class</strong>. The output is one of a finite set of categories.\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Determining if an email is spam or not spam (binary classification), identifying different types of animals in an image (multi-class classification), diagnosing a disease (yes/no).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Introducing Scikit-Learn</h3>\n<p>Now that we have a basic understanding of Machine Learning and Supervised Learning, how do we actually implement these ideas in Python? Enter <strong>Scikit-Learn</strong>!</p>\n<p><strong>Scikit-Learn</strong> (often abbreviated as <code>sklearn</code>) is a free software machine learning library for the Python programming language. It features various classification, regression, and clustering algorithms, including support vector machines, random forests, gradient boosting, k-means, and DBSCAN, and is designed to interoperate with the Python numerical and scientific libraries NumPy and SciPy.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Why Scikit-Learn?</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Simplicity and Efficiency:</strong> It provides a consistent API (Application Programming Interface) for all its models, making it easy to learn and use.</li>\n            <li><strong>Comprehensive:</strong> It offers a wide range of state-of-the-art machine learning algorithms.</li>\n            <li><strong>Well-documented:</strong> Excellent documentation and examples are available.</li>\n            <li><strong>Open Source:</strong> It\'s free to use and has a large, active community.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Installation:</strong> If you don\'t have it already, you can install Scikit-Learn using pip:\n        <p><code>pip install scikit-learn</code></p>\n        <p>You\'ll also typically need NumPy and Matplotlib for data handling and visualization, which are often installed alongside Scikit-Learn or can be installed separately: <code>pip install numpy matplotlib</code></p>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The Scikit-Learn Workflow: A Glimpse</h3>\n<p>Most machine learning tasks with Scikit-Learn follow a consistent pattern. Understanding this workflow is key to using the library effectively:</p>\n<ol>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Data Preparation:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Load your dataset.</li>\n            <li>Separate your data into <strong>features (X)</strong>, which are the input variables, and the <strong>target (y)</strong>, which is the output variable you want to predict.</li>\n            <li>Split your data into a <strong>training set</strong> and a <strong>testing set</strong>. The model learns from the training set and is evaluated on the testing set to see how well it generalizes to new data.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Model Selection:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Choose an appropriate machine learning algorithm for your task (e.g., <code>LinearRegression</code> for regression, <code>KNeighborsClassifier</code> for classification).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Training the Model:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Create an instance of your chosen model.</li>\n            <li>Use the <code>.fit()</code> method to train the model on your training data (<code>X_train</code>, <code>y_train</code>). This is where the model learns the patterns.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Making Predictions:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Once trained, use the <code>.predict()</code> method to make predictions on new, unseen data (typically your <code>X_test</code>).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Evaluating the Model:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Compare the model\'s predictions (<code>y_pred</code>) with the actual target values from the test set (<code>y_test</code>) using various metrics (e.g., accuracy for classification, mean squared error for regression).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Hands-on Example: Simple Classification with Scikit-Learn</h3>\n<p>Let\'s put this workflow into practice with a classic classification problem: the Iris dataset. This dataset contains measurements of iris flowers and their corresponding species, making it perfect for demonstrating supervised classification.</p>\n<p>We\'ll use the <code>KNeighborsClassifier</code>, a simple yet effective classification algorithm.</p>\n\n<pre><code>\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\n\n# 1. Data Preparation\n# Load the Iris dataset\niris = load_iris()\nX = iris.data  # Features (sepal length, sepal width, petal length, petal width)\ny = iris.target # Target (species: 0, 1, 2)\n\n# Split data into training and testing sets\n# test_size=0.3 means 30% of data for testing, 70% for training\n# random_state ensures reproducibility of the split\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=42)\n\nprint(f\"Training features shape: {X_train.shape}\")\nprint(f\"Testing features shape: {X_test.shape}\")\nprint(f\"Training target shape: {y_train.shape}\")\nprint(f\"Testing target shape: {y_test.shape}\")\n\n# 2. Model Selection\n# Create a K-Nearest Neighbors classifier object\n# n_neighbors is a hyperparameter, here we choose 3 neighbors\nknn = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=3)\n\n# 3. Training the Model\n# Train the model using the training data\nknn.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nprint(\"\\nModel trained successfully!\")\n\n# 4. Making Predictions\n# Make predictions on the test set\ny_pred = knn.predict(X_test)\n\nprint(f\"\\nFirst 5 actual labels: {y_test[:5]}\")\nprint(f\"First 5 predicted labels: {y_pred[:5]}\")\n\n# 5. Evaluating the Model\n# Calculate the accuracy of the model\naccuracy = accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred)\nprint(f\"\\nModel Accuracy: {accuracy:.2f}\")\n\n# You can also make a prediction for a single new data point (example)\n# Let\'s imagine a new flower with these measurements:\nnew_flower_measurements = np.array([[5.1, 3.5, 1.4, 0.2]]) # Example from original dataset\npredicted_species = knn.predict(new_flower_measurements)\nprint(f\"\\nPredicted species for new flower {new_flower_measurements[0]}: {predicted_species[0]}\")\nprint(f\"(0=setosa, 1=versicolor, 2=virginica)\")\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>In this example, we loaded the Iris dataset, split it into training and testing sets, initialized a K-Nearest Neighbors classifier, trained it on the training data, made predictions on the test data, and finally evaluated its accuracy. An accuracy of 1.00 (or close to it) means the model correctly predicted the species for all or almost all the flowers in the test set, which is excellent for this simple dataset!</p>\n\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Congratulations on taking your first steps into Machine Learning! Today, you\'ve learned what Machine Learning is, specifically delved into <strong>Supervised Learning</strong> with its two main types (Regression and Classification), and been introduced to the powerful <strong>Scikit-Learn</strong> library. You\'ve also seen the fundamental workflow for building an ML model and even executed a simple classification example. This is just the beginning of a vast and exciting field, and Scikit-Learn will be an invaluable tool as you continue to explore!</p>', 'Introduction to Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(224, 237, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: short search query: &#039;sqlite database in python&#039;</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=short+search+query%3A+%27sqlite+database+in+python%27\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/short+search+query%3A+%27sqlite+database+in+python%27\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/short+search+query%3A+%27sqlite+database+in+python%27/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/short+search+query%3A+%27sqlite+database+in+python%27/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=short+search+query%3A+%27sqlite+database+in+python%27\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Day 83: Working with Databases in Python using SQLite</h2>\n<h3>Introduction to Databases and SQLite</h3>\n<p>In this lesson, we will explore the basics of working with databases in Python using SQLite. A database is a collection of organized data that can be easily accessed, managed, and updated. SQLite is a lightweight disk-based database that doesn\'t require a separate server process and allows accessing the database using a non-standard variant of the SQL query language.</p>\n<p>We will cover the following topics in this lesson:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Introduction to SQLite</li>\n    <li>Basic SQL commands</li>\n    <li>Connecting to a SQLite database in Python</li>\n    <li>Creating and manipulating tables in SQLite</li>\n    <li>Inserting, updating, and deleting data in SQLite</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Introduction to SQLite</h3>\n<p>SQLite is a self-contained, file-based database that can be easily embedded into applications. It doesn\'t require a separate server process and can be accessed using a non-standard variant of the SQL query language. SQLite is widely used in web applications, desktop applications, and mobile applications due to its simplicity and ease of use.</p>\n<p>Here are some key features of SQLite:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Self-contained, file-based database</li>\n    <li>No separate server process required</li>\n    <li>Non-standard variant of SQL query language</li>\n    <li>Supports concurrency and transactions</li>\n    <li>Supports various data types, including integers, strings, and dates</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Basic SQL Commands</h3>\n<p>SQL (Structured Query Language) is a standard language for accessing and managing data in relational databases. SQLite supports most of the standard SQL commands, including:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>SELECT: Retrieves data from a database table</li>\n    <li>INSERT: Inserts new data into a database table</li>\n    <li>UPDATE: Updates existing data in a database table</li>\n    <li>DELETE: Deletes data from a database table</li>\n    <li>CREATE: Creates a new database table or index</li>\n    <li>DROP: Deletes a database table or index</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Connecting to a SQLite Database in Python</h3>\n<p>To connect to a SQLite database in Python, you can use the sqlite3 module, which is a built-in module in Python\'s standard library. Here\'s an example of how to connect to a SQLite database:</p>\n<code>\nimport sqlite3\n\n# Connect to the SQLite database\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\'example.db\')\n\n# Create a cursor object\nc = conn.cursor()\n\n# Close the connection\nconn.close()\n</code>\n<h3>Creating and Manipulating Tables in SQLite</h3>\n<p>To create a new table in SQLite, you can use the CREATE TABLE command. Here\'s an example of how to create a new table:</p>\n<code>\nimport sqlite3\n\n# Connect to the SQLite database\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\'example.db\')\n\n# Create a cursor object\nc = conn.cursor()\n\n# Create a new table\nc.execute(\'\'\'\n    CREATE TABLE users (\n        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n        name TEXT NOT NULL,\n        email TEXT NOT NULL\n    )\n\'\'\')\n\n# Commit the changes\nconn.commit()\n\n# Close the connection\nconn.close()\n</code>\n<h3>Inserting, Updating, and Deleting Data in SQLite</h3>\n<p>To insert new data into a table, you can use the INSERT INTO command. Here\'s an example of how to insert new data into the users table:</p>\n<code>\nimport sqlite3\n\n# Connect to the SQLite database\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\'example.db\')\n\n# Create a cursor object\nc = conn.cursor()\n\n# Insert new data into the users table\nc.execute(\"INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (\'John Doe\', \'john@example.com\')\")\n\n# Commit the changes\nconn.commit()\n\n# Close the connection\nconn.close()\n</code>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ul>\n    <li>What is SQLite?</li>\n    <li>What are some key features of SQLite?</li>\n    <li>What are some basic SQL commands?</li>\n    <li>How do you connect to a SQLite database in Python?</li>\n    <li>How do you create a new table in SQLite?</li>\n    <li>How do you insert new data into a table in SQLite?</li>\n</ul>\n<code>\n# Quiz questions and answers\nquiz = [\n    {\'question\': \'What is SQLite?\', \'answer\': \'A self-contained, file-based database that can be easily embedded into applications.\'},\n    {\'question\': \'What are some key features of SQLite?\', \'answer\': \'Self-contained, file-based database, no separate server process required, non-standard variant of SQL query language, supports concurrency and transactions, supports various data types.\'},\n    {\'question\': \'What are some basic SQL commands?\', \'answer\': \'SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, CREATE, DROP.\'},\n    {\'question\': \'How do you connect to a SQLite database in Python?\', \'answer\': \'Using the sqlite3 module, which is a built-in module in Python\\\'s standard library.\'},\n    {\'question\': \'How do you create a new table in SQLite?\', \'answer\': \'Using the CREATE TABLE command.\'},\n    {\'question\': \'How do you insert new data into a table in SQLite?\', \'answer\': \'Using the INSERT INTO command.\'}\n]\n</code>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>In this lesson, we covered the basics of working with databases in Python using SQLite. We learned about the key features of SQLite, basic SQL commands, and how to connect to a SQLite database in Python. We also learned how to create and manipulate tables in SQLite, as well as how to insert, update, and delete data in SQLite.</p>\n<h3>Image Search Query</h3>\n<p>short search query: \'sqlite database in python\'</p>\n', 'Working with Databases in Python using SQLite', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41');
