(225, 238, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Python review and practice concepts diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Python+review+and+practice+concepts+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Python+review+and+practice+concepts+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Python+review+and+practice+concepts+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Python+review+and+practice+concepts+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Python+review+and+practice+concepts+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 84: Review and Practice of Python Concepts</h3>\n\n<p>Welcome to the final day of our beginner Python course! Today, we will review core concepts, solve practice problems, and explore project ideas to consolidate your learning.</p>\n\n<h3>Python Review</h3>\n\n<p>Let’s recap essential topics:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Functions:</strong> Reusable blocks of code defined with <code>def</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Loops:</strong> <code>for</code> and <code>while</code> loops for iteration.</li>\n  <li><strong>Data Structures:</strong> Lists, tuples, dictionaries, and sets.</li>\n  <li><strong>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP):</strong> Classes, objects, and inheritance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Error Handling:</strong> Using <code>try-except</code> blocks to manage exceptions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Practice Problems</h3>\n\n<p>Solve these problems to reinforce your skills:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Fibonacci Sequence Generator</strong>\n    <p>Write a function that returns the first <code>n</code> Fibonacci numbers.</code>\n    <code>def fibonacci(n):\n        sequence = []\n        a, b = 0, 1\n        while len(sequence) < n:\n            sequence.append(a)\n            a, b = b, a + b\n        return sequence</code>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Word Counter</strong>\n    <p>Create a program that counts the number of words in a text file.</code>\n    <code>with open(\'file.txt\', \'r\') as f:\n        text = f.read()\n        words = text.split()\n        print(len(words))</code>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Temperature Converter</strong>\n    <p>Build a function to convert Celsius to Fahrenheit and vice versa.</code>\n    <code>def convert_temp(value, unit):\n        if unit == \'C\':\n            return (value * 9/5) + 32\n        else:\n            return (value - 32) * 5/9</code>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Project Ideas</h3>\n\n<p>Try building these projects to apply your knowledge:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>To-Do List Application</strong>\n    <p>Allow users to add, delete, and save tasks to a file.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Simple Calculator</strong>\n    <p>Perform arithmetic operations using OOP principles.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Number Guessing Game</strong>\n    <p>Generate a random number and let users guess until they get it right.</p>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Congratulations! You’ve completed the beginner Python course. Keep practicing to master advanced topics!</p>', 'Review and Practice of Python Concepts', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(226, 239, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Python decorators, generators, context managers</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Python+decorators%2C+generators%2C+context+managers\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Python+decorators%2C+generators%2C+context+managers\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Python+decorators%2C+generators%2C+context+managers/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Python+decorators%2C+generators%2C+context+managers/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Python+decorators%2C+generators%2C+context+managers\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Day 85: Introduction to Advanced Python Topics</h1>\n  <h2>Table of Contents</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li><a href=\"#decorators\">1. Decorators</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#generators\">2. Generators</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#context-managers\">3. Context Managers</a></li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h2>1. Decorators</h2>\n  <p>Decorators are a powerful feature in Python that allows you to modify the behavior of a function or a class without permanently changing its implementation.</p>\n  <p>A decorator is a small function that takes another function as an argument and returns a new function that \'wraps\' the original function.</p>\n  <p>Here\'s a simple example of a decorator:</p>\n  <code>\n    def my_decorator(func):\n      def wrapper():\n        print(\"Something is happening before the function is called.\")\n        func()\n        print(\"Something is happening after the function is called.\")\n      return wrapper\n  </code>\n  <p>To use the decorator, you would do the following:</p>\n  <code>\n    @my_decorator\n    def say_hello():\n      print(\"Hello!\")\n  </code>\n  <p>This is equivalent to:</p>\n  <code>\n    say_hello = my_decorator(say_hello)\n  </code>\n  <p>Decorators are often used to implement cross-cutting concerns such as logging, authentication, and error handling.</p>\n\n  <h2>2. Generators</h2>\n  <p>Generators are a type of iterable in Python that can be used to generate a sequence of values on-the-fly, rather than computing them all at once and storing them in memory.</p>\n  <p>Generators are defined using the `yield` keyword, which is used to produce a value and suspend the execution of the function until the next value is requested.</p>\n  <p>Here\'s a simple example of a generator:</p>\n  <code>\n    def infinite_sequence():\n      num = 0\n      while True:\n        yield num\n        num += 1\n  </code>\n  <p>You can use the `next()` function to retrieve the next value from the generator:</p>\n  <code>\n    seq = infinite_sequence()\n    print(next(seq))  # prints 0\n    print(next(seq))  # prints 1\n  </code>\n  <p>Generators are often used to implement lazy loading, where data is loaded only when it\'s actually needed.</p>\n\n  <h2>3. Context Managers</h2>\n  <p>Context managers are a type of object that can be used to manage resources such as files, connections, and locks.</p>\n  <p>Context managers are defined using the `__enter__` and `__exit__` methods, which are called when the context manager is entered and exited, respectively.</p>\n  <p>Here\'s a simple example of a context manager:</p>\n  <code>\n    class ManagedFile:\n      def __enter__(self):\n        self.file = open(\"example.txt\", \"w\")\n        return self.file\n      def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc_val, exc_tb):\n        self.file.close()\n  </code>\n  <p>You can use the `with` statement to use the context manager:</p>\n  <code>\n    with ManagedFile() as file:\n      file.write(\"Hello, world!\")\n  </code>\n  <p>Context managers are often used to implement try-finally blocks, where resources are released regardless of whether an exception is thrown.</p>\n\n  <h2>Quiz</h2>\n  <p>What is the purpose of a decorator in Python?</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>To modify the behavior of a function or class</li>\n    <li>To implement cross-cutting concerns</li>\n    <li>To create a new function</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>What is the purpose of a generator in Python?</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>To create a sequence of values</li>\n    <li>To implement lazy loading</li>\n    <li>To create a new function</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>What is the purpose of a context manager in Python?</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>To manage resources</li>\n    <li>To implement try-finally blocks</li>\n    <li>To create a new function</li>\n  </ul>', 'Introduction to Advanced Python Topics', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(227, 240, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: sqlalchemy database connection</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=sqlalchemy+database+connection\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/sqlalchemy+database+connection\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/sqlalchemy+database+connection/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/sqlalchemy+database+connection/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=sqlalchemy+database+connection\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Working with Databases in Python</h1>\n<p>Today, we will be covering the basics of working with databases in Python. We will be using SQLAlchemy for database interactions and Pandas for data manipulation. By the end of this day, you will be able to connect to a database, create and drop tables, and perform CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations.</p>\n<h2>Database Connection</h2>\n<p>The first step in working with a database is to connect to it. We will be using the `sqlite3` library for this example, but you can use any other database library such as PostgreSQL or MySQL.</p>\n<code>import sqlite3</code>\n<p>We create a connection object using the `connect()` function and specify the database file name.</p>\n<code>conn = sqlite3.connect(\'my_database.db\')</code>\n<p>This will create a new database file called `my_database.db` in the current working directory.</p>\n<h3>SQLAlchemy</h3>\n<p>SQLAlchemy is a popular ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) tool for Python. It allows you to interact with the database using Python objects rather than writing raw SQL queries.</p>\n<code>from sqlalchemy import create_engine, Column, Integer, String</code>\n<p>We create an engine object using the `create_engine()` function and specify the database URL.</p>\n<code>engine = create_engine(\'sqlite:///my_database.db\')</code>\n<p>This will create a new engine object that we can use to interact with the database.</p>\n<h2>Pandas</h2>\n<p>Pandas is a powerful data manipulation library in Python. We will use it to create and manipulate data frames.</p>\n<code>import pandas as pd</code>\n<p>We create a data frame using the `DataFrame()` function and specify the data.</p>\n<code>data = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Jane\'], \'Age\': [25, 30]}\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)</code>\n<p>This will create a new data frame called `df` with the specified data.</p>\n<h3>Creating Tables</h3>\n<p>We can create tables using the `create_table()` function in SQLAlchemy.</p>\n<code>from sqlalchemy import Table, MetaData\nmeta = MetaData()\nusers = Table(\'users\', meta,\n    Column(\'id\', Integer, primary_key=True),\n    Column(\'name\', String),\n    Column(\'age\', Integer)\n)\nmeta.create_all(engine)</code>\n<p>This will create a new table called `users` with the specified columns.</p>\n<h3>Dropping Tables</h3>\n<p>We can drop tables using the `drop_table()` function in SQLAlchemy.</p>\n<code>meta.drop_all(engine)</code>\n<p>This will drop the `users` table.</p>\n<h3>CRUD Operations</h3>\n<p>We can perform CRUD operations using the `insert()`, `select()`, `update()`, and `delete()` functions in SQLAlchemy.</p>\n<code>from sqlalchemy import insert, select\n# Insert a new user\nstmt = insert(users).values(name=\'John\', age=25)\nresult = engine.execute(stmt)\n# Select all users\nstmt = select([users])\nresult = engine.execute(stmt)\n# Update a user\nstmt = users.update().where(users.c.id == 1).values(age=26)\nresult = engine.execute(stmt)\n# Delete a user\nstmt = users.delete().where(users.c.id == 1)\nresult = engine.execute(stmt)</code>\n<p>This will perform the specified CRUD operations.</p>\n<h2>Quiz</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the purpose of SQLAlchemy?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between `create_engine()` and `connect()` functions?</li>\n<li>How do you create a new data frame using Pandas?</li>\n<li>How do you create a new table using SQLAlchemy?</li>\n<li>How do you drop a table using SQLAlchemy?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Answer these questions in the comments below.</p>\n', 'Working with Databases in Python', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(228, 241, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Python web development frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Python+web+development+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Python+web+development+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Python+web+development+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Python+web+development+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Python+web+development+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div>Array', 'Python Web Development Frameworks', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(229, 242, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: short search query: &#039;python coding best practices&#039;</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=short+search+query%3A+%27python+coding+best+practices%27\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/short+search+query%3A+%27python+coding+best+practices%27\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/short+search+query%3A+%27python+coding+best+practices%27/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/short+search+query%3A+%27python+coding+best+practices%27/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=short+search+query%3A+%27python+coding+best+practices%27\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Best Practices for Python Coding</h1>\n<h2>Day 88: PEP 8, Code Review, and Testing</h2>\n<p>Welcome to day 88 of our Python programming course. In this lesson, we will cover best practices for writing clean, efficient, and readable Python code. We will discuss PEP 8, code review, and testing.</p>\n<h3>PEP 8: Style Guide for Python Code</h3>\n<p>PEP 8 is a set of guidelines for writing Python code that is easy to read and maintain. It covers topics such as naming conventions, indentation, and comments. Let\'s take a look at some of the key points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Indentation:</strong> Use 4 spaces for indentation, not tabs.</li>\n<li><strong>Naming Conventions:</strong> Use lowercase letters with words separated by underscores. For example, `hello_world` instead of `HelloWorld`.</li>\n<li><strong>Comments:</strong> Use the `#` symbol for comments. For example: `# This is a comment`.</li>\n<li><strong>Line Length:</strong> Keep lines of code to 79 characters or less.</li>\n<li><strong>Blank Lines:</strong> Use blank lines to separate logical sections of code.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a Python function that follows PEP 8:</p>\n<code>\ndef hello_world():\n    # This is a comment\n    print(\'Hello, World!\')\n</code>\n<h3>Code Review</h3>\n<p>Code review is an essential part of the development process. It involves reviewing code written by others to ensure it is correct, efficient, and easy to understand. Here are some tips for effective code review:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Read the code carefully:</strong> Take the time to understand the code and its purpose.</li>\n<li><strong>Look for errors:</strong> Check for syntax errors, logical errors, and bugs.</li>\n<li><strong>Check for performance:</strong> Ensure the code is efficient and doesn\'t waste resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Check for readability:</strong> Ensure the code is easy to understand and follows PEP 8.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a code review:</p>\n<code>\n# Original code\ndef hello_world():\n    print(\'Hello, World!\')\n\n# Reviewed code\ndef hello_world():\n    # This is a comment\n    print(\'Hello, World!\')\n</code>\n<h3>Testing</h3>\n<p>Testing is an essential part of the development process. It involves writing code to ensure that other code works as expected. Here are some tips for effective testing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Write unit tests:</strong> Write tests for individual functions or methods.</li>\n<li><strong>Write integration tests:</strong> Write tests for how multiple functions or methods work together.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a testing framework:</strong> Use a framework like `unittest` to make testing easier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a unit test:</p>\n<code>\nimport unittest\n\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nclass TestAdd(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_add(self):\n        self.assertEqual(add(2, 3), 5)\n</code>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What is PEP 8?</li>\n<li>What is code review?</li>\n<li>What is testing?</li>\n<li>What are some tips for effective code review?</li>\n<li>What are some tips for effective testing?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PEP 8 is a set of guidelines for writing Python code that is easy to read and maintain.</li>\n<li>Code review is the process of reviewing code written by others to ensure it is correct, efficient, and easy to understand.</li>\n<li>Testing is the process of writing code to ensure that other code works as expected.</li>\n<li>Some tips for effective code review include reading the code carefully, looking for errors, checking for performance, and checking for readability.</li>\n<li>Some tips for effective testing include writing unit tests, writing integration tests, and using a testing framework.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Image Query:</h4>\n<p>short search query: \'python coding best practices\'</p>\n', 'Best Practices for Python Coding', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(230, 243, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data science machine learning numpy scipy scikit-learn</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+science+machine+learning+numpy+scipy+scikit-learn\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+science+machine+learning+numpy+scipy+scikit-learn\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+science+machine+learning+numpy+scipy+scikit-learn/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+science+machine+learning+numpy+scipy+scikit-learn/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+science+machine+learning+numpy+scipy+scikit-learn\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div>\n    <h1>Python for Data Science and Machine Learning - Day 89</h1>\n    <h2>Table of Contents</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><a href=\'#numpy\'>NumPy</a></li>\n      <li><a href=\'#scipy\'>SciPy</a></li>\n      <li><a href=\'#scikit-learn\'>Scikit-learn</a></li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3 id=\'numpy\'>NumPy</h3>\n    <p>NumPy is a library for the Python programming language, adding support for large, multi-dimensional arrays and matrices, along with a large collection of high-level mathematical functions to operate on these arrays.</p>\n    <p>The array data type in NumPy is called ndarray. It\'s the core data structure of NumPy and can be thought of as a table of numbers.</p>\n    <p>Basic operations on NumPy arrays include:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><code>array[index]</code> - Accessing elements in the array</li>\n      <li><code>array[index] = value</code> - Assigning a value to an element in the array</li>\n      <li><code>array.shape</code> - Getting the shape of the array</li>\n      <li><code>array.size</code> - Getting the size of the array</li>\n      <li><code>array.dtype</code> - Getting the data type of the array</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Example of creating a NumPy array:</p>\n    <code>\n      import numpy as np\n      arr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n      print(arr)\n    </code>\n    <p>Example of accessing elements in a NumPy array:</p>\n    <code>\n      import numpy as np\n      arr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n      print(arr[0])\n    </code>\n    <h3 id=\'scipy\'>SciPy</h3>\n    <p>SciPy is a library for scientific and technical computing. It provides functions for scientific and engineering applications, including signal processing, linear algebra, optimization, statistics, and more.</p>\n    <p>Some of the key features of SciPy include:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Signal Processing</strong> - SciPy provides functions for filtering, convolution, and other signal processing tasks</li>\n      <li><strong>Linear Algebra</strong> - SciPy provides functions for solving systems of linear equations, finding eigenvalues and eigenvectors, and more</li>\n      <li><strong>Optimization</strong> - SciPy provides functions for minimizing or maximizing functions, including linear and nonlinear programming</li>\n      <li><strong>Statistics</strong> - SciPy provides functions for statistical analysis, including hypothesis testing and confidence intervals</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Example of using SciPy to find the roots of a polynomial:</p>\n    <code>\n      import numpy as np\n      from scipy import optimize\n      def func(x):\n        return x**2 + 2*x + 1\n      roots = optimize.root(func, 0)\n      print(roots)\n    </code>\n    <h3 id=\'scikit-learn\'>Scikit-learn</h3>\n    <p>Scikit-learn is a library for machine learning in Python. It provides a wide range of algorithms for classification, regression, clustering, and more.</p>\n    <p>Some of the key features of Scikit-learn include:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Classification</strong> - Scikit-learn provides algorithms for classification, including linear and logistic regression, decision trees, and more</li>\n      <li><strong>Regression</strong> - Scikit-learn provides algorithms for regression, including linear and polynomial regression, decision trees, and more</li>\n      <li><strong>Clustering</strong> - Scikit-learn provides algorithms for clustering, including k-means and hierarchical clustering</li>\n      <li><strong>Model Selection</strong> - Scikit-learn provides functions for selecting the best model for a given problem</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Example of using Scikit-learn to train a logistic regression model:</p>\n    <code>\n      from sklearn import datasets\n      from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n      from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n      from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\n      iris = datasets.load_iris()\n      X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(iris.data, iris.target, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)\n      clf = LogisticRegression()\n      clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n      y_pred = clf.predict(X_test)\n      print(accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred))\n    </code>\n    <h2>Quiz</h2>\n    <p>Here are some questions to test your understanding of NumPy, SciPy, and Scikit-learn.</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>What is the data type of a NumPy array?</li>\n      <li>What is the shape of a NumPy array?</li>\n      <li>How do you access elements in a NumPy array?</li>\n      <li>What is the difference between SciPy and Scikit-learn?</li>\n      <li>How do you train a logistic regression model using Scikit-learn?</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Solutions will be provided at the end of the quiz.</p>\n  ', 'Python for Data Science and Machine Learning', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(231, 244, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python code review</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+code+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+code+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+code+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+code+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+code+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Final Project Presentations and Review</h1><h2>Project Showcase</h2><p>Welcome to the final day of our Python course! Today, we will be showcasing the projects you\'ve been working on throughout the course. Please take a few minutes to review each project and provide feedback to your peers.</p><p>Each project will be presented in the following format:</p><ul><li>Project Name</li><li>Project Description</li><li>Code Snippet</li><li>Discussion</li></ul><p>Let\'s get started!</p><h2>Project 1: To-Do List App</h2><p>Project Name: To-Do List App</p><p>Project Description: A simple to-do list app that allows users to create, read, update, and delete tasks.</p><code>class ToDoList:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.tasks = {}\n\n    def add_task(self, task):\n        self.tasks[task] = True\n\n    def view_tasks(self):\n        for task in self.tasks:\n            print(task)\n</code><p>Discussion: This project demonstrates a basic understanding of object-oriented programming and data structures.</p><h2>Project 2: Game Development</h2><p>Project Name: Game Development</p><p>Project Description: A simple game where the user has to navigate a character through a maze.</p><code>import pygame\n\nclass Game:\n    def __init__(self):\n        pygame.init()\n        self.screen = pygame.display.set_mode((800, 600))\n\n    def run(self):\n        while True:\n            for event in pygame.event.get():\n                if event.type == pygame.QUIT:\n                    pygame.quit()\n                    sys.exit()\n\n            self.screen.fill((255, 255, 255))\n            pygame.display.flip()\n            pygame.time.Clock().tick(60)\n</code><p>Discussion: This project demonstrates a basic understanding of game development using the Pygame library.</p><h2>Code Review</h2><p>Now that we\'ve seen the projects, it\'s time for a code review! Please take a few minutes to review the code and provide feedback to the project owners.</p><p>Some things to consider during the code review:</p><ul><li>Readability: Is the code easy to read and understand?</li><li>Organization: Is the code well-organized and structured?</li><li>Efficiency: Is the code efficient and optimized for performance?</li><li>Best Practices: Are the best practices followed, such as using functions and variables?</li></ul><h2>Feedback</h2><p>Finally, please provide feedback to the project owners. This can be in the form of comments, suggestions, or even a simple \'great job!\' or \'keep working on it!\'. Remember, feedback is a crucial part of the learning process, and it will help you grow as a developer.</p>', 'Final Project Presentations and Review', 1, '2026-04-19 10:27:41', '2026-04-19 10:27:41'),
(232, 245, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Basics</h3><p>Welcome to the world of Python programming. In this course, we will be covering the basics of Python and how to use it to build applications. On the first day, we will be introducing variables, data types, and basic operators.</p><h3>Variables in Python</h3><p>In Python, a variable is a name given to a value. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in a program. You can think of a variable as a labeled box where you can store a value. To assign a value to a variable, you use the assignment operator (=).</p><p>For example, to assign the value 10 to a variable named x, you would use the following code: <code>x = 10</code>. After this assignment, you can use the variable x in your program to refer to the value 10.</p><p>Here are some key things to know about variables in Python:</p><ul><li>Variable names can contain letters, numbers, and underscores, but they cannot start with a number.</li><li>Variable names are case-sensitive, so x and X are two different variables.</li><li>You can assign a new value to a variable at any time, and the old value will be lost.</li></ul><h3>Data Types in Python</h3><p>Python has several built-in data types that you can use to store different types of data. The main data types are:</p><ul><li><strong>Integers</strong>: whole numbers, like 1, 2, or 3.</li><li><strong>Floats</strong>: decimal numbers, like 3.14 or -0.5.</li><li><strong>Strings</strong>: sequences of characters, like \'hello\' or \"hello\". Strings can be enclosed in single quotes or double quotes.</li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: a logical value that can be either True or False.</li><li><strong>List</strong>: an ordered collection of values, like [1, 2, 3] or [\'a\', \'b\', \'c\'].</li><li><strong>Tuple</strong>: an ordered, immutable collection of values, like (1, 2, 3) or (\'a\', \'b\', \'c).</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of how to use these data types: <code>x = 10  # integer</code>, <code>y = 3.14  # float</code>, <code>name = \'John\'  # string</code>, <code>is_admin = True  # boolean</code>, <code>numbers = [1, 2, 3]  # list</code>, <code>colors = (\'red\', \'green\', \'blue\')  # tuple</code></p><h3>Basic Operators in Python</h3><p>Python has several basic operators that you can use to perform arithmetic, comparison, logical, and assignment operations. Here are some of the most common operators:</p><ul><li><strong>Arithmetic operators</strong>: + (addition), - (subtraction), * (multiplication), / (division), ** (exponentiation), % (modulus).</li><li><strong>Comparison operators</strong>: == (equal), != (not equal), > (greater than), < (less than), >= (greater than or equal), <= (less than or equal).</li><li><strong>Logical operators</strong>: and (logical and), or (logical or), not (logical not).</li><li><strong>Assignment operators</strong>: = (assignment), += (addition assignment), -= (subtraction assignment), *= (multiplication assignment), /= (division assignment).</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of how to use these operators: <code>x = 5 + 3</code>, <code>y = 10 * 2</code>, <code>is_equal = x == y</code>, <code>is_greater = x > y</code>, <code>x += 5</code></p><p>We hope this introduction to Python basics has been helpful. In the next lesson, we will be covering more advanced topics in Python.</p>', 'Introduction to Python Basics', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(233, 246, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python control structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Control Structures in Python</h3><p>Control structures are the building blocks of any programming language, and Python is no exception. They allow you to control the flow of your program, making decisions and repeating tasks as needed. In this lesson, we\'ll be covering three fundamental control structures in Python: conditional statements, loops, and functions.</p><h3>Conditional Statements</h3><p>Conditional statements, also known as if-else statements, allow you to make decisions based on conditions or rules. They consist of an if clause, an optional else clause, and an optional elif clause. The basic syntax is as follows:</p><code>if condition:<br>    # code to execute if condition is true<br>elif condition:<br>    # code to execute if condition is true<br>else:<br>    # code to execute if all conditions are false</code><p>Here\'s an example of a simple if-else statement:</p><code>x = 5<br>if x > 10:<br>    print(&quot;x is greater than 10&quot;)<br>else:<br>    print(&quot;x is less than or equal to 10&quot;)</code><p>This code checks if the value of x is greater than 10. If it is, it prints &quot;x is greater than 10&quot;. Otherwise, it prints &quot;x is less than or equal to 10&quot;.</p><h3>Loops</h3><p>Loops allow you to repeat a block of code over and over again. There are two main types of loops in Python: for loops and while loops.</p><h3>For Loops</h3><p>A for loop iterates over a sequence (such as a list, tuple, or string) and executes a block of code for each item in the sequence. The basic syntax is as follows:</p><code>for variable in sequence:<br>    # code to execute for each item in the sequence</code><p>Here\'s an example of a for loop:</p><code>fruits = [&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;banana&quot;, &quot;cherry&quot;]<br>for fruit in fruits:<br>    print(fruit)</code><p>This code prints each fruit in the fruits list.</p><h3>While Loops</h3><p>A while loop executes a block of code as long as a certain condition is true. The basic syntax is as follows:</p><code>while condition:<br>    # code to execute while condition is true</code><p>Here\'s an example of a while loop:</p><code>x = 0<br>while x < 5:<br>    print(x)<br>    x += 1</code><p>This code prints the numbers 0 through 4.</p><h3>Functions</h3><p>Functions are reusable blocks of code that take arguments and return values. They allow you to organize your code, reduce repetition, and make your programs more modular and efficient. The basic syntax is as follows:</p><code>def function_name(arguments):<br>    # code to execute</code><p>Here\'s an example of a simple function:</p><code>def greet(name):<br>    print(&quot;Hello, &quot; + name + &quot;!\\&quot;)<br>greet(&quot;John&quot;)</code><p>This code defines a function called greet that takes a name as an argument and prints a greeting message. It then calls the function with the argument &quot;John&quot;.</p><p>Functions can also return values using the return statement:</p><code>def add(x, y):<br>    return x + y<br>result = add(2, 3)<br>print(result)</code><p>This code defines a function called add that takes two numbers as arguments and returns their sum. It then calls the function with the arguments 2 and 3 and prints the result.</p><h3>Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Conditional statements</strong>: if-else statements that allow you to make decisions based on conditions or rules.</li><li><strong>Loops</strong>: for loops and while loops that allow you to repeat a block of code over and over again.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: reusable blocks of code that take arguments and return values.</li></ul><p>By mastering these control structures, you\'ll be able to write more efficient, effective, and modular code. Remember to practice, practice, practice to reinforce your understanding of these concepts!</p>', 'Control Structures in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(234, 247, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python lists tuples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+lists+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+lists+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+lists+tuples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+lists+tuples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+lists+tuples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Lists and Tuples in Python</h3><p>In this lesson, we will cover one of the most fundamental data structures in Python: lists and tuples. Lists and tuples are used to store multiple values in a single variable. The main difference between lists and tuples is that lists are mutable, meaning they can be changed after creation, while tuples are immutable, meaning they cannot be changed after creation.</p><h3>List Operations</h3><p>Lists in Python are defined by placing values between square brackets <code>[]</code>. Here are some common list operations:</p><ul><li><strong>Indexing</strong>: Accessing a specific element in the list by its index. In Python, indexing starts at 0. For example: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]; print(my_list[0])</code> would print <code>1</code>.</li><li><strong>Slicing</strong>: Accessing a subset of elements in the list by specifying a range of indices. For example: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]; print(my_list[0:2])</code> would print <code>[1, 2]</code>.</li><li><strong>Append</strong>: Adding a new element to the end of the list. For example: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]; my_list.append(4); print(my_list)</code> would print <code>[1, 2, 3, 4]</code>.</li><li><strong>Insert</strong>: Inserting a new element at a specific position in the list. For example: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]; my_list.insert(1, 4); print(my_list)</code> would print <code>[1, 4, 2, 3]</code>.</li><li><strong>Remove</strong>: Removing the first occurrence of a specified element in the list. For example: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]; my_list.remove(2); print(my_list)</code> would print <code>[1, 3]</code>.</li><li><strong>Sort</strong>: Sorting the elements of the list in ascending order. For example: <code>my_list = [3, 2, 1]; my_list.sort(); print(my_list)</code> would print <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>.</li></ul><h3>Tuple Operations</h3><p>Tuples in Python are defined by placing values between parentheses <code>()</code>. Here are some common tuple operations:</p><ul><li><strong>Indexing</strong>: Accessing a specific element in the tuple by its index. In Python, indexing starts at 0. For example: <code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3); print(my_tuple[0])</code> would print <code>1</code>.</li><li><strong>Slicing</strong>: Accessing a subset of elements in the tuple by specifying a range of indices. For example: <code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3); print(my_tuple[0:2])</code> would print <code>(1, 2)</code>.</li><li><strong>Concatenation</strong>: Combining two or more tuples into a single tuple. For example: <code>tuple1 = (1, 2); tuple2 = (3, 4); print(tuple1 + tuple2)</code> would print <code>(1, 2, 3, 4)</code>.</li><li><strong>Repetition</strong>: Repeating a tuple a specified number of times. For example: <code>my_tuple = (1, 2); print(my_tuple * 3)</code> would print <code>(1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2)</code>.</li></ul><h3>Indexing and Slicing</h3><p>Indexing and slicing are used to access specific elements or subsets of elements in lists and tuples. The syntax for indexing and slicing is as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Indexing</strong>: <code>my_list[index]</code> or <code>my_tuple[index]</code></li><li><strong>Slicing</strong>: <code>my_list[start:stop]</code> or <code>my_tuple[start:stop]</code></li></ul><p>In slicing, the <code>start</code> index is inclusive, while the <code>stop</code> index is exclusive. For example: <code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]; print(my_list[0:2])</code> would print <code>[1, 2]</code>, not <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>.</p>', 'Lists and Tuples in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14');
