(235, 248, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python dictionaries and sets</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+dictionaries+and+sets\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+dictionaries+and+sets\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+dictionaries+and+sets/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+dictionaries+and+sets/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+dictionaries+and+sets\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Dictionaries and Sets in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 4 of our Python course. Today, we will be covering two important data structures in Python: dictionaries and sets. These data structures are used to store and manipulate data in a more efficient way.</p><h3>Dictionary Operations</h3><p>A dictionary in Python is an unordered collection of key-value pairs. It is used to store data in a way that it can be easily accessed and modified. Here are some common dictionary operations:</p><ul><li><strong>Creating a Dictionary</strong>: A dictionary can be created using the <code>{}</code> syntax or the <code>dict()</code> function.</li><li><strong>Accessing Dictionary Elements</strong>: Dictionary elements can be accessed using their keys. For example, if we have a dictionary <code>d = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}</code>, we can access the value of the key \'name\' using <code>d[\'name\']</code>.</li><li><strong>Updating Dictionary Elements</strong>: Dictionary elements can be updated using their keys. For example, if we have a dictionary <code>d = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}</code>, we can update the value of the key \'age\' using <code>d[\'age\'] = 31</code>.</li><li><strong>Adding New Dictionary Elements</strong>: New dictionary elements can be added using their keys. For example, if we have a dictionary <code>d = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}</code>, we can add a new element with the key \'city\' using <code>d[\'city\'] = \'New York\'</code>.</li><li><strong>Removing Dictionary Elements</strong>: Dictionary elements can be removed using the <code>del</code> statement or the <code>pop()</code> method. For example, if we have a dictionary <code>d = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}</code>, we can remove the element with the key \'age\' using <code>del d[\'age\']</code> or <code>d.pop(\'age\')</code>.</li></ul><h3>Set Operations</h3><p>A set in Python is an unordered collection of unique elements. It is used to store data in a way that it can be easily accessed and manipulated. Here are some common set operations:</p><ul><li><strong>Creating a Set</strong>: A set can be created using the <code>set()</code> function or the <code>{}</code> syntax.</li><li><strong>Adding Elements to a Set</strong>: Elements can be added to a set using the <code>add()</code> method. For example, if we have a set <code>s = {1, 2, 3}</code>, we can add a new element with the value 4 using <code>s.add(4)</code>.</li><li><strong>Removing Elements from a Set</strong>: Elements can be removed from a set using the <code>remove()</code> method or the <code>discard()</code> method. For example, if we have a set <code>s = {1, 2, 3}</code>, we can remove the element with the value 2 using <code>s.remove(2)</code> or <code>s.discard(2)</code>.</li><li><strong>Set Union</strong>: The union of two sets can be calculated using the <code>union()</code> method or the <code>|</code> operator. For example, if we have two sets <code>s1 = {1, 2, 3}</code> and <code>s2 = {3, 4, 5}</code>, we can calculate their union using <code>s1.union(s2)</code> or <code>s1 | s2</code>.</li><li><strong>Set Intersection</strong>: The intersection of two sets can be calculated using the <code>intersection()</code> method or the <code>&</code> operator. For example, if we have two sets <code>s1 = {1, 2, 3}</code> and <code>s2 = {3, 4, 5}</code>, we can calculate their intersection using <code>s1.intersection(s2)</code> or <code>s1 & s2</code>.</li><li><strong>Set Difference</strong>: The difference of two sets can be calculated using the <code>difference()</code> method or the <code>-</code> operator. For example, if we have two sets <code>s1 = {1, 2, 3}</code> and <code>s2 = {3, 4, 5}</code>, we can calculate their difference using <code>s1.difference(s2)</code> or <code>s1 - s2</code>.</li></ul><h3>Key-Value Pairs</h3><p>In a dictionary, each element is a key-value pair. The key is used to access the value. Here are some important things to know about key-value pairs:</p><ul><li><strong>Keys Must Be Unique</strong>: In a dictionary, all keys must be unique. If we try to add a new key-value pair with a key that already exists, the old value will be replaced with the new value.</li><li><strong>Keys Must Be Immutable</strong>: In a dictionary, all keys must be immutable. This means that we cannot use lists or dictionaries as keys.</li><li><strong>Values Can Be Anything</strong>: In a dictionary, the values can be anything, including lists, dictionaries, and other mutable types.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use dictionaries and sets in Python:</p><code># Create a dictionary\nperson = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}\n\n# Access a dictionary element\nprint(person[\'name\'])\n\n# Update a dictionary element\nperson[\'age\'] = 31\nprint(person[\'age\'])\n\n# Add a new dictionary element\nperson[\'city\'] = \'New York\'\nprint(person[\'city\'])\n\n# Remove a dictionary element\ndel person[\'age\']\nprint(person)\n\n# Create a set\nnumbers = {1, 2, 3}\n\n# Add an element to a set\nnumbers.add(4)\nprint(numbers)\n\n# Remove an element from a set\nnumbers.remove(2)\nprint(numbers)\n\n# Calculate the union of two sets\nset1 = {1, 2, 3}\nset2 = {3, 4, 5}\nprint(set1.union(set2))\n\n# Calculate the intersection of two sets\nprint(set1.intersection(set2))\n\n# Calculate the difference of two sets\nprint(set1.difference(set2))</code><p>We hope this helps! Let us know if you have any questions or need further clarification.</p>', 'Dictionaries and Sets in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(236, 249, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: object-oriented programming in python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=object-oriented+programming+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/object-oriented+programming+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/object-oriented+programming+in+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/object-oriented+programming+in+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=object-oriented+programming+in+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Day 5: Object-Oriented Programming in Python</h2>\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In Python, OOP is a powerful tool for organizing and structuring code.</p>\n<h3>Classes</h3>\n<p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. In Python, classes are defined using the `class` keyword.</p>\n<pre><code>\nclass Car:\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year):\n        self.brand = brand\n        self.model = model\n        self.year = year\n\n    def start_engine(self):\n        print(\"Vroom!\")\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here, `Car` is a class that has two methods: `__init__` and `start_engine`. The `__init__` method is a special method that is called when an object is created from the class. It is used to initialize the attributes of the class.</p>\n<h3>Objects</h3>\n<p>An object is an instance of a class. It has its own set of attributes and methods. In Python, objects are created by calling the class with the `()` operator.</p>\n<pre><code>\nmy_car = Car(\"Toyota\", \"Camry\", 2020)\nprint(my_car.brand)  # Output: Toyota\nprint(my_car.model)  # Output: Camry\nprint(my_car.year)   # Output: 2020\nmy_car.start_engine()  # Output: Vroom!\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Inheritance</h3>\n<p>Inheritance is a mechanism in OOP that allows one class to inherit the properties and behavior of another class. The class that is being inherited is called the parent class, and the class that is doing the inheriting is called the child class.</p>\n<pre><code>\nclass ElectricCar(Car):\n    def __init__(self, brand, model, year, battery_capacity):\n        super().__init__(brand, model, year)\n        self.battery_capacity = battery_capacity\n\n    def charge_battery(self):\n        print(\"Charging battery...\")\n\nmy_electric_car = ElectricCar(\"Tesla\", \"Model S\", 2020, 100)\nprint(my_electric_car.brand)  # Output: Tesla\nprint(my_electric_car.model)  # Output: Model S\nprint(my_electric_car.year)   # Output: 2020\nprint(my_electric_car.battery_capacity)  # Output: 100\nmy_electric_car.start_engine()  # Output: Vroom!\nmy_electric_car.charge_battery()  # Output: Charging battery...\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the difference between a class and an object?</li>\n<li>How do you define a class in Python?</li>\n<li>What is the purpose of the `__init__` method in a class?</li>\n<li>How do you create an object from a class?</li>\n<li>What is inheritance in OOP, and how is it implemented in Python?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz Answers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A class is a blueprint or template that defines the properties and behavior of an object, while an object is an instance of a class.</li>\n<li>You define a class in Python using the `class` keyword.</li>\n<li>The `__init__` method is used to initialize the attributes of a class.</li>\n<li>You create an object from a class by calling the class with the `()` operator.</li>\n<li>Inheritance is a mechanism in OOP that allows one class to inherit the properties and behavior of another class.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Image Query</h3>\n<a href=\'https://www.google.com/search?q=object-oriented+programming+in+python\'>Object-Oriented Programming in Python</a>', 'Object-Oriented Programming in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(237, 250, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output exceptions</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output+exceptions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output+exceptions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output+exceptions/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output+exceptions/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output+exceptions\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>File Input/Output and Exceptions in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 6 of our Python course. Today, we will be covering two important topics in Python: File Input/Output and Exceptions. These topics are crucial for any programmer to understand, as they are used extensively in real-world applications.</p><h3>File Handling</h3><p>File handling is the process of reading and writing files in Python. Python provides several built-in functions to handle files, including <code>open()</code>, <code>read()</code>, <code>write()</code>, and <code>close()</code>. The <code>open()</code> function is used to open a file, and it returns a file object. The <code>read()</code> function is used to read the contents of a file, and the <code>write()</code> function is used to write to a file. The <code>close()</code> function is used to close a file.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use these functions:</p><code>file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;r&quot;)<br>content = file.read()<br>print(content)<br>file.close()</code><p>In this example, we open a file called <code>example.txt</code> in read mode (<code>&quot;r&quot;</code>), read the contents of the file, print the contents, and then close the file.</p><h3>Exception Handling</h3><p>Exception handling is the process of handling errors that occur during the execution of a program. Python provides a <code>try</code> block to enclose the code that might raise an exception, and an <code>except</code> block to handle the exception. The <code>try</code> block is used to define a block of code where an exception might occur, and the <code>except</code> block is used to define a block of code that will be executed if an exception occurs.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use exception handling:</p><code>try:<br>    file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;r&quot;)<br>    content = file.read()<br>    print(content)<br>    file.close()<br>except FileNotFoundError:<br>    print(&quot;The file does not exist&quot;)</code><p>In this example, we try to open a file called <code>example.txt</code> and read its contents. If the file does not exist, a <code>FileNotFoundError</code> exception is raised, and we print an error message.</p><h3>Error Types</h3><p>There are several types of errors that can occur in Python, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Syntax Errors</strong>: These occur when there is an error in the syntax of the code, such as a missing colon or a mismatched bracket.</li><li><strong>Runtime Errors</strong>: These occur when an error occurs during the execution of the code, such as a division by zero or an out-of-range value.</li><li><strong>Logic Errors</strong>: These occur when there is an error in the logic of the code, such as an incorrect algorithm or a misunderstanding of the problem.</li><li><strong>File Errors</strong>: These occur when there is an error related to files, such as a file not found or a permission denied error.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of error types:</p><code>try:<br>    x = 5 / 0<br>except ZeroDivisionError:<br>    print(&quot;Cannot divide by zero&quot;)<br><br>try:<br>    file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;r&quot;)<br>except FileNotFoundError:<br>    print(&quot;The file does not exist&quot;)</code><p>In the first example, we try to divide by zero, which raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code> exception. In the second example, we try to open a file that does not exist, which raises a <code>FileNotFoundError</code> exception.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with files and exceptions:</p><ul><li>Always use exception handling when working with files to handle potential errors.</li><li>Use the <code>with</code> statement to ensure that files are properly closed after use.</li><li>Use the <code>try</code> and <code>except</code> blocks to handle specific exceptions and provide informative error messages.</li><li>Test your code thoroughly to catch and handle any potential errors.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can write more robust and reliable code that handles potential errors and exceptions.</p>', 'File Input/Output and Exceptions in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(238, 251, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python modules and packages</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+modules+and+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+modules+and+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Modules and Packages</h3><p>Python modules and packages are essential components of the Python ecosystem. They allow developers to organize and reuse code, making it easier to maintain and extend their projects. In this lesson, we will explore how to import modules, create packages, and use module functions.</p><h3>Importing Modules</h3><p>In Python, you can import modules using the <code>import</code> statement. There are several ways to import modules, including:</p><ul><li><code>import module_name</code>: This imports the entire module and makes all its functions, classes, and variables available.</li><li><code>from module_name import function_or_class</code>: This imports a specific function or class from the module.</li><li><code>from module_name import *</code>: This imports all functions, classes, and variables from the module, making them available in the current namespace.</li></ul><p>For example, to import the <code>math</code> module, you can use the following code:</p><code>import math</code><p>Once imported, you can use the module\'s functions and classes, such as <code>math.sqrt()</code> or <code>math.pi</code>.</p><h3>Creating Packages</h3><p>A package in Python is a directory that contains a set of related modules and subpackages. To create a package, you need to create a directory with an <code>__init__.py</code> file inside it. The <code>__init__.py</code> file can be empty, but it\'s required to indicate that the directory is a package.</p><p>For example, let\'s create a package called <code>mymath</code> with two modules: <code>geometry</code> and <code>algebra</code>. The directory structure would look like this:</p><ul><li><code>mymath/</code></li><li><code>mymath/__init__.py</code></li><li><code>mymath/geometry.py</code></li><li><code>mymath/algebra.py</code></li></ul><p>Inside the <code>geometry.py</code> module, you can define functions and classes related to geometry, such as <code>calculate_area()</code> or <code>calculate_perimeter()</code>.</p><h3>Module Functions</h3><p>Module functions are functions that are defined inside a module. They can be used to perform specific tasks or calculations. For example, the <code>math</code> module has a function called <code>sin()</code> that calculates the sine of a given angle.</p><p>To use a module function, you need to import the module and then call the function using the module name. For example:</p><code>import math<br>result = math.sin(3.14)</code><p>Alternatively, you can import the function directly and use it without the module name:</p><code>from math import sin<br>result = sin(3.14)</code><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>When working with modules and packages, it\'s essential to follow best practices to ensure that your code is organized, readable, and maintainable. Here are some tips:</p><ul><li>Use meaningful and descriptive names for your modules and packages.</li><li>Keep your modules and packages organized by grouping related functions and classes together.</li><li>Use <code>__init__.py</code> files to indicate that a directory is a package.</li><li>Document your modules and packages using docstrings and comments.</li></ul>', 'Python Modules and Packages', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(239, 252, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Data Structures</h3><p>In this chapter, we will cover the basics of data structures in Python. Data structures are a way to organize and store data in a program, and they are essential for any programming language. We will be covering three types of data structures: Lists, Tuples, and Dictionaries.</p><h3>Lists</h3><p>A list is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are denoted by square brackets <code>[]</code> and are mutable, meaning they can be changed after creation.</p><ul><li>Lists are ordered, meaning that the items in the list have a specific order.</li><li>Lists are mutable, meaning that they can be changed after creation.</li><li>Lists are defined by square brackets <code>[]</code>.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to create a list in Python:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code><p>We can also access specific items in the list using their index. The index of the first item in the list is <code>0</code>, the index of the second item is <code>1</code>, and so on.</p><code>print(my_list[0])  # prints 1</code><p>We can also use negative indices to access items from the end of the list. The index of the last item in the list is <code>-1</code>, the index of the second to last item is <code>-2</code>, and so on.</p><code>print(my_list[-1])  # prints 5</code><h3>Tuples</h3><p>A tuple is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are denoted by parentheses <code>()</code> and are immutable, meaning they cannot be changed after creation.</p><ul><li>Tuples are ordered, meaning that the items in the tuple have a specific order.</li><li>Tuples are immutable, meaning that they cannot be changed after creation.</li><li>Tuples are defined by parentheses <code>()</code>.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to create a tuple in Python:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)</code><p>We can access specific items in the tuple using their index, just like with lists.</p><code>print(my_tuple[0])  # prints 1</code><p>However, we cannot change the items in the tuple after creation.</p><code>try:</code><code>    my_tuple[0] = 10</code><code>except TypeError:</code><code>    print(\"Tuples are immutable!\")</code><h3>Dictionaries</h3><p>A dictionary is a collection of key-value pairs. Dictionaries are denoted by curly brackets <code>{}</code> and are mutable, meaning they can be changed after creation.</p><ul><li>Dictionaries are unordered, meaning that the items in the dictionary do not have a specific order.</li><li>Dictionaries are mutable, meaning that they can be changed after creation.</li><li>Dictionaries are defined by curly brackets <code>{}</code>.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to create a dictionary in Python:</p><code>my_dict = {\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30}</code><p>We can access specific items in the dictionary using their key.</p><code>print(my_dict[\"name\"])  # prints John</code><p>We can also add new items to the dictionary after creation.</p><code>my_dict[\"city\"] = \"New York\"</code><p>And we can also change the value of an existing item in the dictionary.</p><code>my_dict[\"age\"] = 31</code><p>In conclusion, lists, tuples, and dictionaries are three types of data structures in Python. Lists are ordered and mutable, tuples are ordered and immutable, and dictionaries are unordered and mutable. Each data structure has its own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of which one to use depends on the specific needs of the program.</p>', 'Introduction to Data Structures', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(240, 253, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python lists tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+lists+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+lists+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with Lists</h3><p>In this chapter, we will be covering one of the most fundamental data structures in Python: lists. Lists are ordered collections of values that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. They are denoted by square brackets [] and are used to store multiple items in a single variable.</p><h3>List Indexing</h3><p>List indexing is used to access specific elements in a list. In Python, list indices start at 0, which means the first element in a list is at index 0, the second element is at index 1, and so on. We can access an element in a list by using its index in square brackets.</p><p>For example, if we have a list called <code>fruits</code> with the elements \'apple\', \'banana\', and \'cherry\', we can access the first element like this: <code>fruits[0]</code>. This will return the string \'apple\'.</p><p>We can also use negative indices to access elements from the end of the list. For example, <code>fruits[-1]</code> will return the last element in the list, which is \'cherry\'.</p><ul><li><strong>Positive Indexing:</strong> starts from the beginning of the list, with the first element at index 0.</li><li><strong>Negative Indexing:</strong> starts from the end of the list, with the last element at index -1.</li></ul><h3>List Slicing</h3><p>List slicing is used to extract a subset of elements from a list. We can slice a list by using a colon : to specify the start and end indices of the subset. For example, if we have a list called <code>numbers</code> with the elements 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5, we can slice the list to get the elements 2 and 3 like this: <code>numbers[1:3]</code>.</p><p>The general syntax for list slicing is <code>list[start:end]</code>, where <code>start</code> is the index of the first element to include in the slice, and <code>end</code> is the index of the first element to exclude from the slice.</p><p>We can also omit the start or end index to slice the list from the beginning or end. For example, <code>numbers[:3]</code> will return the elements 1, 2, and 3, and <code>numbers[2:]</code> will return the elements 3, 4, and 5.</p><ul><li><strong>Slicing with Start and End Indices:</strong> <code>list[start:end]</code></li><li><strong>Slicing from the Beginning:</strong> <code>list[:end]</code></li><li><strong>Slicing to the End:</strong> <code>list[start:]</code></li></ul><h3>List Methods</h3><p>Python provides several methods that can be used to manipulate lists. Here are some of the most commonly used list methods:</p><ul><li><strong><code>append()</code></strong>: adds an element to the end of the list.</li><li><strong><code>extend()</code></strong>: adds multiple elements to the end of the list.</li><li><strong><code>insert()</code></strong>: inserts an element at a specified index in the list.</li><li><strong><code>remove()</code></strong>: removes the first occurrence of an element in the list.</li><li><strong><code>sort()</code></strong>: sorts the elements in the list in ascending order.</li><li><strong><code>reverse()</code></strong>: reverses the order of the elements in the list.</li></ul><p>For example, if we have a list called <code>colors</code> with the elements \'red\', \'green\', and \'blue\', we can use the <code>append()</code> method to add the element \'yellow\' to the end of the list like this: <code>colors.append(\'yellow\')</code>.</p><p>We can also use the <code>extend()</code> method to add multiple elements to the end of the list. For example, <code>colors.extend([\'purple\', \'orange\'])</code> will add the elements \'purple\' and \'orange\' to the end of the list.</p>', 'Working with Lists', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(241, 254, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python control structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Control Structures in Python</h3><p>Control structures are the building blocks of any programming language, and Python is no exception. They allow you to control the flow of your program, making decisions, repeating tasks, and skipping over code. In this lesson, we will cover three fundamental control structures in Python: If-Else statements, For loops, and While loops.</p><h3>If-Else Statements</h3><p>If-Else statements are used to make decisions in your program. They allow you to execute different blocks of code based on certain conditions. The basic syntax of an If-Else statement is:</p><code>if condition:\n    # code to execute if condition is true\nelse:\n    # code to execute if condition is false</code><p>Here, <strong>condition</strong> is a boolean expression that evaluates to either True or False. If the condition is True, the code under the <strong>if</strong> block is executed. If the condition is False, the code under the <strong>else</strong> block is executed.</p><p>Let\'s consider an example:</p><code>x = 5\nif x > 10:\n    print(&quot;x is greater than 10&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(&quot;x is less than or equal to 10&quot;)</code><p>In this example, the output will be <strong>x is less than or equal to 10</strong> because the condition <strong>x > 10</strong> is False.</p><h3>For Loops</h3><p>For loops are used to repeat a block of code for a specified number of times. The basic syntax of a For loop is:</p><code>for variable in iterable:\n    # code to execute</code><p>Here, <strong>variable</strong> is the name given to the item being processed, and <strong>iterable</strong> is the sequence of items to be processed. The loop will iterate over each item in the iterable and execute the code under the <strong>for</strong> block.</p><p>Let\'s consider an example:</p><code>fruits = [&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;banana&quot;, &quot;cherry&quot;]\nfor fruit in fruits:\n    print(fruit)</code><p>In this example, the output will be:</p><ul><li>apple</li><li>banana</li><li>cherry</li></ul><p>The loop will iterate over each item in the <strong>fruits</strong> list and print it.</p><h3>While Loops</h3><p>While loops are used to repeat a block of code while a certain condition is true. The basic syntax of a While loop is:</p><code>while condition:\n    # code to execute</code><p>Here, <strong>condition</strong> is a boolean expression that evaluates to either True or False. The loop will continue to execute the code under the <strong>while</strong> block as long as the condition is True.</p><p>Let\'s consider an example:</p><code>x = 0\nwhile x < 5:\n    print(x)\n    x += 1</code><p>In this example, the output will be:</p><ul><li>0</li><li>1</li><li>2</li><li>3</li><li>4</li></ul><p>The loop will continue to execute the code under the <strong>while</strong> block until the condition <strong>x < 5</strong> is False.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we covered three fundamental control structures in Python: If-Else statements, For loops, and While loops. These control structures allow you to control the flow of your program, making decisions, repeating tasks, and skipping over code. With practice and experience, you will become proficient in using these control structures to write efficient and effective Python programs.</p>', 'Control Structures in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(242, 255, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python functions tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Functions in Python</h3><p>In this chapter, we will be covering one of the most important concepts in programming: functions. Functions are reusable blocks of code that take arguments, perform a specific task, and return a result. They are essential for writing efficient, modular, and readable code.</p><h3>Defining Functions</h3><p>To define a function in Python, we use the <code>def</code> keyword followed by the function name and a list of parameters in parentheses. The code block within the function is denoted by indentation.</p><p>Here is a simple example of a function that prints a greeting message:</p><code>def greet(name):<br>    print(f&quot;Hello, {name}!&quot;)</code><p>In this example, <code>greet</code> is the function name, and <code>name</code> is the parameter. We can call this function by passing a value for the <code>name</code> parameter, like this:</p><code>greet(&quot;John&quot;)</code><p>This will output: <code>Hello, John!</code></p><h3>Function Arguments</h3><p>Functions can take multiple arguments, which are specified in the function definition. We can also specify default values for arguments, which are used if the argument is not provided when the function is called.</p><p>Here is an example of a function that takes two arguments:</p><code>def add(x, y):<br>    return x + y</code><p>We can call this function by passing two values, like this:</p><code>result = add(2, 3)<br>print(result)  # Output: 5</code><p>We can also specify default values for arguments, like this:</p><code>def greet(name, message=&quot;Hello&quot;):<br>    print(f&quot;{message}, {name}!&quot;)</code><p>In this example, if we don&#39;t provide a value for the <code>message</code> argument, it will default to <code>Hello</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>Positional arguments</strong>: These are arguments that are passed in the correct order, without specifying the parameter name.</li><li><strong>Keyword arguments</strong>: These are arguments that are passed using the parameter name, regardless of the order.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of using positional and keyword arguments:</p><code>def greet(name, message):<br>    print(f&quot;{message}, {name}!&quot;)<br><br>greet(&quot;John&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot;)  # Positional arguments<br>greet(name=&quot;John&quot;, message=&quot;Hello&quot;)  # Keyword arguments<br>greet(&quot;John&quot;, message=&quot;Hello&quot;)  # Mixing positional and keyword arguments</code><h3>Return Statements</h3><p>Functions can return values using the <code>return</code> statement. If a function doesn&#39;t explicitly return a value, it will return <code>None</code> by default.</p><p>Here is an example of a function that returns a value:</p><code>def add(x, y):<br>    return x + y<br><br>result = add(2, 3)<br>print(result)  # Output: 5</code><p>We can also return multiple values from a function, like this:</p><code>def calculate_stats(numbers):<br>    mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)<br>    median = sorted(numbers)[len(numbers) // 2]<br>    return mean, median<br><br>mean, median = calculate_stats([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])<br>print(mean)  # Output: 3.0<br>print(median)  # Output: 3</code><p>In this example, the function returns two values, which are unpacked into the <code>mean</code> and <code>median</code> variables.</p>', 'Functions in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(243, 256, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: object oriented programming python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=object+oriented+programming+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/object+oriented+programming+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/object+oriented+programming+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/object+oriented+programming+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=object+oriented+programming+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In this chapter, we will explore the basics of OOP in Python, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class. For example:</p><p><code>class Car:</code></p><p>This class can have attributes (data) and methods (functions). Attributes are the data members of the class, while methods are the functions that belong to the class.</p><p>For example:</p><p><code>class Car:</code></p><p><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, year):</code></p><p><code>        self.color = color</code></p><p><code>        self.model = model</code></p><p><code>        self.year = year</code></p><p><code>    def print_details(self):</code></p><p><code>        print(f&quot;This car is a {self.year} {self.color} {self.model}&quot;)</code></p><h3>Objects</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class. In other words, an object is a variable that has the attributes and methods of a class. To create an object, we use the class name followed by parentheses containing the required arguments.</p><p>For example:</p><p><code>my_car = Car(&quot;red&quot;, &quot;Toyota&quot;, 2015)</code></p><p>We can then access the attributes and methods of the object using the dot notation.</p><p>For example:</p><p><code>my_car.print_details()</code></p><p>This will output: <strong>This car is a 2015 red Toyota</strong></p><h3>Inheritance</h3><p>Inheritance is a mechanism in OOP that allows one class to inherit the attributes and methods of another class. The class that is being inherited from is called the parent class or superclass, while the class that is doing the inheriting is called the child class or subclass.</p><p>In Python, inheritance is implemented using the <code>(ParentClass)</code> syntax. For example:</p><p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):</code></p><p><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, year, battery_size):</code></p><p><code>        super().__init__(color, model, year)</code></p><p><code>        self.battery_size = battery_size</code></p><p><code>    def print_battery_size(self):</code></p><p><code>        print(f&quot;This car has a {self.battery_size} kWh battery&quot;)</code></p><p>The <code>ElectricCar</code> class inherits all the attributes and methods of the <code>Car</code> class and adds its own attributes and methods.</p><h3>Benefits of OOP</h3><ul><li><strong>Code Reusability</strong>: OOP allows us to reuse code by creating classes that can be instantiated multiple times.</li><li><strong>Modularity</strong>: OOP allows us to break down complex systems into smaller, more manageable modules.</li><li><strong>Easier Maintenance</strong>: OOP makes it easier to modify and maintain code by allowing us to make changes at the class level rather than the individual object level.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, Object-Oriented Programming is a powerful paradigm that allows us to create complex systems by defining classes, creating objects, and using inheritance to reuse code.</p>', 'Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14');
