(244, 257, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output exception handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>File Input/Output and Exception Handling</h3><p>Welcome to Day 13 of the Python course. Today, we will be covering two important topics: File Input/Output and Exception Handling. These topics are crucial in building robust and reliable Python programs.</p><h3>Reading and Writing Files</h3><p>In Python, you can read and write files using the built-in <code>open()</code> function. This function returns a file object, which can be used to read or write files. The basic syntax of the <code>open()</code> function is as follows:</p><p><code>file_object = open(file_name, mode)</code></p><p>Where <code>file_name</code> is the name of the file you want to open, and <code>mode</code> is the mode in which you want to open the file. The mode can be one of the following:</p><ul><li><code>\'r\'</code>: Open the file in read mode. This is the default mode.</li><li><code>\'w\'</code>: Open the file in write mode. If the file already exists, its contents will be deleted. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a\'</code>: Open the file in append mode. If the file already exists, new data will be appended to the end of the file. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'r+\'</code>: Open the file in read and write mode.</li><li><code>\'w+\'</code>: Open the file in read and write mode. If the file already exists, its contents will be deleted. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a+\'</code>: Open the file in read and append mode. If the file already exists, new data will be appended to the end of the file. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li></ul><p>Once you have opened a file, you can read or write data to it using the <code>read()</code> and <code>write()</code> methods, respectively. For example:</p><p><code>file_object = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')</code></p><p><code>file_object.write(\'Hello, World!\')</code></p><p><code>file_object.close()</code></p><h3>Try-Except Blocks</h3><p>Try-except blocks are used to handle exceptions in Python. An exception is an error that occurs during the execution of a program. The basic syntax of a try-except block is as follows:</p><p><code>try:</code></p><p><code>    # Code that may raise an exception</code></p><p><code>except ExceptionType:</code></p><p><code>    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised</code></p><p>For example:</p><p><code>try:</code></p><p><code>    file_object = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')</code></p><p><code>    print(file_object.read())</code></p><p><code>except FileNotFoundError:</code></p><p><code>    print(\'The file does not exist\')</code></p><h3>Error Handling</h3><p>Error handling is the process of anticipating, detecting, and resolving errors in a program. Python provides several built-in exceptions that you can use to handle errors. Some common exceptions include:</p><ul><li><code>FileNotFoundError</code>: Raised when a file or directory is not found.</li><li><code>PermissionError</code>: Raised when you do not have permission to access a file or directory.</li><li><code>IOError</code>: Raised when an I/O operation fails.</li><li><code>TypeError</code>: Raised when a variable is not of the expected type.</li><li><code>ValueError</code>: Raised when a function or operation receives an argument with an incorrect value.</li></ul><p>You can also create your own custom exceptions using the <code>raise</code> statement. For example:</p><p><code>raise ValueError(\'Invalid input\')</code></p><p>In addition to try-except blocks, you can also use the <code>finally</code> block to specify code that will be executed regardless of whether an exception is raised or not. The basic syntax of a try-except-finally block is as follows:</p><p><code>try:</code></p><p><code>    # Code that may raise an exception</code></p><p><code>except ExceptionType:</code></p><p><code>    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised</code></p><p><code>finally:</code></p><p><code>    # Code that will be executed regardless of whether an exception is raised or not</code></p><p>For example:</p><p><code>try:</code></p><p><code>    file_object = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')</code></p><p><code>    print(file_object.read())</code></p><p><code>except FileNotFoundError:</code></p><p><code>    print(\'The file does not exist\')</code></p><p><code>finally:</code></p><p><code>    file_object.close()</code></p><p>In conclusion, file input/output and exception handling are important topics in Python programming. By using the <code>open()</code> function and try-except blocks, you can read and write files, and handle errors in a robust and reliable way.</p>', 'File Input/Output and Exception Handling', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(245, 258, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 14: Review and Practice</h3><p>Welcome to Day 14 of our Python course. Today, we will review the concepts we have covered so far and practice them with some exercises. We will also discuss some project ideas to help you apply your knowledge.</p><h3>Review of Previous Concepts</h3><p>In the previous days, we have covered various topics such as variables, data types, control structures, functions, and modules. Let\'s quickly review each of these topics:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables</strong>: We learned how to declare and use variables in Python. We also learned about the different data types such as integers, floats, strings, and booleans.</li><li><strong>Data Types</strong>: We learned about the different data types in Python, including lists, tuples, dictionaries, and sets.</li><li><strong>Control Structures</strong>: We learned about the different control structures in Python, including if-else statements, for loops, and while loops.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: We learned how to define and use functions in Python. We also learned about function arguments and return types.</li><li><strong>Modules</strong>: We learned how to import and use modules in Python. We also learned about the different types of modules, including built-in modules and external modules.</li></ul><h3>Practice Exercises</h3><p>Now that we have reviewed the previous concepts, let\'s practice them with some exercises. Here are a few exercises to get you started:</p><ul><li>Write a Python program to calculate the area and perimeter of a rectangle.</li><li>Write a Python program to calculate the sum of all the elements in a list.</li><li>Write a Python program to find the maximum and minimum elements in a list.</li><li>Write a Python program to sort a list of elements in ascending order.</li><li>Write a Python program to find the average of all the elements in a list.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how you can solve the first exercise:<code>def calculate_area_and_perimeter(length, width):\n    area = length * width\n    perimeter = 2 * (length + width)\n    return area, perimeter\n\nlength = 5\nwidth = 3\narea, perimeter = calculate_area_and_perimeter(length, width)\nprint(&quot;Area: &quot;, area)\nprint(&quot;Perimeter: &quot;, perimeter)</code></p><h3>Project Ideas</h3><p>Now that you have practiced the previous concepts, let\'s discuss some project ideas to help you apply your knowledge. Here are a few ideas:</p><ul><li>Build a calculator program that can perform basic arithmetic operations.</li><li>Build a program that can simulate a game of rock, paper, scissors.</li><li>Build a program that can generate a random password.</li><li>Build a program that can simulate a bank account system.</li><li>Build a program that can simulate a weather forecasting system.</li></ul><p>These are just a few ideas to get you started. You can think of many other projects that you can build using Python.</p>', 'Review and Practice', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(246, 259, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Data Structures</h3><p>In this chapter, we will cover the basics of Python data structures, including lists, tuples, and dictionaries. These data structures are essential for storing and manipulating data in Python.</p><h3>Lists</h3><p>A list in Python is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are denoted by square brackets <code>[]</code> and are used to store multiple values in a single variable.</p><p>Here is an example of how to create a list in Python:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code><p>Lists are <strong>mutable</strong>, meaning they can be modified after creation. You can add or remove elements from a list using various methods, such as <code>append()</code>, <code>insert()</code>, <code>remove()</code>, and <code>pop()</code>.</p><ul><li><code>append()</code>: adds an element to the end of the list</li><li><code>insert()</code>: adds an element at a specified position in the list</li><li><code>remove()</code>: removes the first occurrence of an element in the list</li><li><code>pop()</code>: removes an element at a specified position in the list</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use these methods:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>my_list.append(6)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br>my_list.insert(2, 7)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 7, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br>my_list.remove(7)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br>my_list.pop(2)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code><h3>Tuples</h3><p>A tuple in Python is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are denoted by parentheses <code>()</code> and are used to store multiple values in a single variable.</p><p>Here is an example of how to create a tuple in Python:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)</code><p>Tuples are <strong>immutable</strong>, meaning they cannot be modified after creation. You cannot add or remove elements from a tuple.</p><p>Here is an example of how to access elements in a tuple:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)<br>print(my_tuple[0])  # Output: 1<br>print(my_tuple[1])  # Output: 2</code><h3>Dictionaries</h3><p>A dictionary in Python is a collection of key-value pairs. Dictionaries are denoted by curly brackets <code>{}</code> and are used to store data in a way that is easy to access and manipulate.</p><p>Here is an example of how to create a dictionary in Python:</p><code>my_dict = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}</code><p>Dictionaries are <strong>mutable</strong>, meaning they can be modified after creation. You can add or remove key-value pairs from a dictionary using various methods, such as <code>keys()</code>, <code>values()</code>, and <code>items()</code>.</p><ul><li><code>keys()</code>: returns a list of all the keys in the dictionary</li><li><code>values()</code>: returns a list of all the values in the dictionary</li><li><code>items()</code>: returns a list of all the key-value pairs in the dictionary</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use these methods:</p><code>my_dict = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}<br>print(my_dict.keys())  # Output: dict_keys([\'name\', \'age\'])<br>print(my_dict.values())  # Output: dict_values([\'John\', 30])<br>print(my_dict.items())  # Output: dict_items([(\'name\', \'John\'), (\'age\', 30)])</code><p>In conclusion, lists, tuples, and dictionaries are all important data structures in Python. Lists are mutable and can be used to store multiple values in a single variable. Tuples are immutable and can be used to store multiple values in a single variable. Dictionaries are mutable and can be used to store data in a way that is easy to access and manipulate.</p>', 'Introduction to Python Data Structures', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(247, 260, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python lists tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+lists+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+lists+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+lists+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Working with Lists in Python</h3><p>In this chapter, we will cover the basics of working with lists in Python. Lists are a fundamental data structure in Python and are used to store collections of items. We will explore list indexing, list slicing, and list methods.</p><h3>List Indexing</h3><p>List indexing is used to access specific elements in a list. In Python, list indices start at 0, which means the first element in a list is at index 0, the second element is at index 1, and so on.</p><p>Here is an example of list indexing:<code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[0])  # prints 1<br>print(my_list[1])  # prints 2<br>print(my_list[2])  # prints 3</code></p><p>We can also use negative indices to access elements from the end of the list. For example, <code>my_list[-1]</code> would return the last element in the list, <code>my_list[-2]</code> would return the second to last element, and so on.</p><h3>List Slicing</h3><p>List slicing is used to extract a subset of elements from a list. The syntax for list slicing is <code>my_list[start:stop:step]</code>, where:</p><ul><li><strong>start</strong>: The starting index of the slice. If omitted, it defaults to 0.</li><li><strong>stop</strong>: The ending index of the slice. If omitted, it defaults to the end of the list.</li><li><strong>step</strong>: The increment between elements in the slice. If omitted, it defaults to 1.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of list slicing:<code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[1:3])  # prints [2, 3]<br>print(my_list[1:])  # prints [2, 3, 4, 5]<br>print(my_list[:3])  # prints [1, 2, 3]<br>print(my_list[::2])  # prints [1, 3, 5]</code></p><h3>List Methods</h3><p>Python provides several list methods that can be used to manipulate and interact with lists. Here are some of the most commonly used list methods:</p><ul><li><strong>append()</strong>: Adds an element to the end of the list.</li><li><strong>extend()</strong>: Adds multiple elements to the end of the list.</li><li><strong>insert()</strong>: Inserts an element at a specified position in the list.</li><li><strong>remove()</strong>: Removes the first occurrence of an element in the list.</li><li><strong>sort()</strong>: Sorts the elements of the list in place.</li><li><strong>reverse()</strong>: Reverses the elements of the list in place.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of using list methods:<code>my_list = [1, 2, 3]<br>my_list.append(4)<br>print(my_list)  # prints [1, 2, 3, 4]<br><br>my_list.extend([5, 6])<br>print(my_list)  # prints [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br><br>my_list.insert(0, 0)<br>print(my_list)  # prints [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br><br>my_list.remove(0)<br>print(my_list)  # prints [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br><br>my_list.sort(reverse=True)<br>print(my_list)  # prints [6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]<br><br>my_list.reverse()<br>print(my_list)  # prints [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]</code></p><p>In conclusion, lists are a powerful data structure in Python, and understanding how to work with them is essential for any Python programmer. By mastering list indexing, list slicing, and list methods, you can write more efficient and effective code.</p>', 'Working with Lists in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(248, 261, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python control structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Python Control Structures</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the different control structures available in Python. Control structures are used to control the flow of a program\'s execution. They determine which parts of the code are executed, how many times, and in what order.</p><h3>If-Else Statements</h3><p>An if-else statement is used to execute a block of code if a certain condition is true. If the condition is false, another block of code can be executed. The basic syntax of an if-else statement is:</p><code>if condition:\n    # code to execute if condition is true\nelse:\n    # code to execute if condition is false</code><p>Here\'s an example of an if-else statement:</p><code>age = 25\nif age >= 18:\n    print(\'You are an adult\')\nelse:\n    print(\'You are a minor\')</code><p>In this example, the code checks if the age is greater than or equal to 18. If it is, it prints \'You are an adult\'. Otherwise, it prints \'You are a minor\'.</p><h3>For Loops</h3><p>A for loop is used to execute a block of code repeatedly for a specified number of times. The basic syntax of a for loop is:</p><code>for variable in iterable:\n    # code to execute</code><p>Here\'s an example of a for loop:</p><code>fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'cherry\']\nfor fruit in fruits:\n    print(fruit)</code><p>In this example, the code loops through the fruits list and prints each fruit.</p><h3>While Loops</h3><p>A while loop is used to execute a block of code repeatedly while a certain condition is true. The basic syntax of a while loop is:</p><code>while condition:\n    # code to execute</code><p>Here\'s an example of a while loop:</p><code>i = 0\nwhile i < 5:\n    print(i)\n    i += 1</code><p>In this example, the code loops through the numbers 0 to 4 and prints each number.</p><p>Some key points to note when using control structures:</p><ul><li><strong>Indentation is crucial</strong>: Python uses indentation to determine the block of code to execute. Make sure to indent your code correctly.</li><li><strong>Conditions must be boolean</strong>: The condition in an if-else statement or while loop must evaluate to a boolean value (True or False).</li><li><strong>Loops can be nested</strong>: You can have a loop inside another loop. Just make sure to indent correctly.</li></ul><p>Now that you\'ve learned about control structures, try practicing with some examples. You can use online platforms or your local Python environment to test your code.</p>', 'Understanding Python Control Structures', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(249, 262, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python functions tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+functions+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+functions+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Functions in Python</h3><p>In this chapter, we will be covering one of the most important concepts in programming: functions. Functions are reusable blocks of code that take arguments, perform a specific task, and return a result. They are essential for organizing and structuring your code, making it more readable, maintainable, and efficient.</p><h3>Defining Functions</h3><p>To define a function in Python, you use the <code>def</code> keyword followed by the function name and a list of arguments in parentheses. The code block within the function is indented under the function definition.</p><p>Here is an example of a simple function that prints a message to the console:</p><code>def greet(name):<br>    print(f&quot;Hello, {name}!&quot;)</code><p>In this example, <code>greet</code> is the function name, and <code>name</code> is the argument. The function takes a <code>name</code> as input and prints out a personalized greeting message.</p><h3>Function Arguments</h3><p>Function arguments are the values passed to a function when it is called. They can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, lists, dictionaries, and more.</p><p>There are several types of function arguments in Python:</p><ul><li><strong>Positional arguments</strong>: These are the most common type of argument. They are defined in the function definition and are passed to the function in the same order.</li><li><strong>Keyword arguments</strong>: These are arguments that are passed to a function using the argument name. They can be passed in any order and are useful for making the code more readable.</li><li><strong>Default arguments</strong>: These are arguments that have a default value assigned to them in the function definition. If no value is passed for a default argument, the default value is used.</li><li><strong>Variable arguments</strong>: These are arguments that can take any number of values. They are defined using the <code>*args</code> syntax.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of a function that takes multiple types of arguments:</p><code>def greet(name, age=30, *hobbies):<br>    print(f&quot;Hello, {name}! You are {age} years old.&quot;)<br>    if hobbies:<br>        print(f&quot;Your hobbies are: {\', \'.join(hobbies)}&quot;)</code><p>In this example, <code>name</code> is a positional argument, <code>age</code> is a default argument, and <code>hobbies</code> is a variable argument.</p><h3>Return Statements</h3><p>A return statement is used to exit a function and send a value back to the caller. The <code>return</code> keyword is used to specify the value to be returned.</p><p>Here is an example of a function that returns a value:</p><code>def add(a, b):<br>    result = a + b<br>    return result</code><p>In this example, the function <code>add</code> takes two arguments, <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, adds them together, and returns the result.</p><p>Functions can also return multiple values using tuples, lists, or dictionaries.</p><code>def get_user_info():<br>    name = &quot;John Doe&quot;<br>    age = 30<br>    return name, age</code><p>In this example, the function <code>get_user_info</code> returns a tuple containing the user\'s name and age.</p><h3>Best Practices for Using Functions</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when using functions:</p><ul><li>Keep functions short and focused on a single task.</li><li>Use descriptive and concise function names.</li><li>Use arguments and return values to pass data between functions.</li><li>Avoid using global variables within functions.</li><li>Test functions thoroughly to ensure they work as expected.</li></ul>', 'Functions in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(250, 263, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python exception handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Exception Handling in Python</h3><p>Exception handling is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to manage and respond to errors that may occur during the execution of your code. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of exception handling, including try-except blocks, error types, and raising exceptions.</p><h3>Try-Except Blocks</h3><p>A try-except block is a fundamental construct in Python that allows you to enclose a block of code that may potentially raise an exception. The <code>try</code> block contains the code that might raise an exception, while the <code>except</code> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception is raised.</p><p>Here is an example of a simple try-except block:</p><p><code>try:</code><br><code>    # Code that may raise an exception</code><br><code>except ExceptionType:</code><br><code>    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised</code></p><p>In this example, the <code>try</code> block contains the code that may raise an exception, and the <code>except</code> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception of type <code>ExceptionType</code> is raised.</p><h3>Error Types</h3><p>Python has a built-in hierarchy of exception classes that can be used to handle different types of errors. Some common error types include:</p><ul><li><code>ValueError</code>: Raised when a function or operation receives an argument with an incorrect value.</li><li><code>TypeError</code>: Raised when a function or operation is applied to an object of an incorrect type.</li><li><code>IndexError</code>: Raised when a sequence (such as a list or tuple) is indexed out of range.</li><li><code>KeyError</code>: Raised when a mapping (such as a dictionary) is indexed with a key that does not exist.</li><li><code>IOError</code>: Raised when an I/O operation (such as reading or writing to a file) fails.</li></ul><p>These error types can be used in the <code>except</code> block to catch and handle specific types of exceptions.</p><h3>Raising Exceptions</h3><p>In addition to catching exceptions, you can also raise exceptions explicitly using the <code>raise</code> statement. This can be useful for signaling errors or exceptional conditions in your code.</p><p>Here is an example of raising an exception:</p><p><code>raise ValueError(&quot;Invalid input&quot;)</code></p><p>In this example, the <code>raise</code> statement raises a <code>ValueError</code> exception with the message <code>&quot;Invalid input&quot;</code>.</p><p>Best practices for raising exceptions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Be specific</strong>: Raise specific exceptions that accurately reflect the error or condition that occurred.</li><li><strong>Provide context</strong>: Include relevant information or context in the exception message to help with debugging.</li><li><strong>Document exceptions</strong>: Document the exceptions that your code may raise, including the types of exceptions and the conditions under which they are raised.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can write robust and reliable code that handles exceptions effectively and provides useful feedback to users.</p>', 'Exception Handling in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(251, 264, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>File Input/Output in Python</h3><p>File input/output is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to read and write data to files, which can be useful for storing and retrieving data. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of file input/output in Python, including reading files, writing files, and file modes.</p><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>To read a file in Python, you can use the <code>open()</code> function, which returns a file object. The file object has several methods and attributes that can be used to read and manipulate the file. The basic syntax for reading a file is as follows:</p><ul><li><code>file = open(\'filename.txt\', \'r\')</code>: Opens the file in read mode.</li><li><code>content = file.read()</code>: Reads the entire contents of the file.</li><li><code>file.close()</code>: Closes the file.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to read a file:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')\ncontent = file.read()\nprint(content)\nfile.close()</code><p>This will print the entire contents of the file to the console.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>To write to a file in Python, you can use the <code>open()</code> function with the \'w\' mode. The basic syntax for writing to a file is as follows:</p><ul><li><code>file = open(\'filename.txt\', \'w\')</code>: Opens the file in write mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>file.write(\'Hello, World!\')</code>: Writes the string \'Hello, World!\' to the file.</li><li><code>file.close()</code>: Closes the file.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to write to a file:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')\nfile.write(\'Hello, World!\')\nfile.close()</code><p>This will create a new file called example.txt and write the string \'Hello, World!\' to it.</p><h3>File Modes</h3><p>When opening a file in Python, you can specify the mode in which the file should be opened. The mode determines what operations can be performed on the file. The following are some common file modes:</p><ul><li><code>\'r\'</code>: Read mode. The file can be read, but not written.</li><li><code>\'w\'</code>: Write mode. The file can be written, but not read. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a\'</code>: Append mode. The file can be written, but not read. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'r+\'</code>: Read and write mode. The file can be both read and written.</li><li><code>\'w+\'</code>: Read and write mode. The file can be both read and written. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a+\'</code>: Read and append mode. The file can be read and written, but all writes will be appended to the end of the file.</li></ul><p>It is <strong>important</strong> to note that when opening a file in write mode (\'w\'), the file will be truncated if it already exists. This means that any existing data in the file will be deleted. To avoid this, you can open the file in append mode (\'a\') instead.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>When working with files in Python, it is a good idea to follow some best practices to ensure that your code is safe and efficient:</p><ul><li>Always close the file when you are finished with it to avoid memory leaks.</li><li>Use the <code>with</code> statement to automatically close the file when you are finished with it.</li><li>Be careful when opening files in write mode, as this can delete existing data.</li><li>Use try/except blocks to handle any errors that may occur when working with files.</li></ul>', 'File Input/Output in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(252, 265, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Review and Assessment of Python Fundamentals</h3><p>Welcome to Day 21 of the Python course. Today, we will review the fundamentals of Python programming, including variables, data types, and control structures. This review will help you assess your understanding of these concepts and identify areas where you need more practice.</p><h3>Variables in Python</h3><p>In Python, a variable is a name given to a value. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in a program. You can think of a variable as a labeled box where you can store a value. The value stored in a variable can be changed, and the variable can be used in expressions and statements.</p><p>To assign a value to a variable, you use the assignment operator (=). For example: <code>x = 5</code> assigns the value 5 to the variable x. You can also assign a value to a variable using the <code>input()</code> function, which reads input from the user.</p><h3>Data Types in Python</h3><p>Python has several built-in data types, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Integers</strong>: whole numbers, either positive, negative, or zero. Example: <code>x = 5</code></li><li><strong>Floats</strong>: decimal numbers. Example: <code>x = 3.14</code></li><li><strong>Strings</strong>: sequences of characters, such as words or sentences. Example: <code>x = \'hello\'</code></li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: a logical value that can be either True or False. Example: <code>x = True</code></li><li><strong>List</strong>: an ordered collection of values. Example: <code>x = [1, 2, 3]</code></li><li><strong>Tuple</strong>: an ordered, immutable collection of values. Example: <code>x = (1, 2, 3)</code></li></ul><p>These data types are the building blocks of any Python program, and understanding how to use them is essential for any Python programmer.</p><h3>Control Structures in Python</h3><p>Control structures are used to control the flow of a program\'s execution. The most common control structures in Python are:</p><ul><li><strong>If-else statements</strong>: used to make decisions based on conditions. Example: <code>if x > 5: print(\'x is greater than 5\')</code></li><li><strong>For loops</strong>: used to iterate over a sequence of values. Example: <code>for x in range(5): print(x)</code></li><li><strong>While loops</strong>: used to repeat a block of code while a condition is true. Example: <code>while x < 5: print(x); x += 1</code></li></ul><p>These control structures are used to write more complex programs that can make decisions, repeat tasks, and iterate over data.</p><p>Now that we have reviewed the fundamentals of Python programming, it\'s time to assess your understanding of these concepts. Take the quiz below to test your knowledge.</p>', 'Review and Assessment of Python Fundamentals', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14');
