(262, 275, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python error handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+error+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+error+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Error Handling and Exception Management</h3><p>Error handling and exception management are crucial aspects of programming in Python. They allow you to anticipate, detect, and respond to errors that may occur during the execution of your code. In this lesson, we will explore the concepts of try-except blocks, error types, and raising exceptions.</p><h3>Try-Except Blocks</h3><p>A try-except block is a mechanism that allows you to enclose a block of code that may potentially raise an exception. The <code>try</code> block contains the code that you want to execute, while the <code>except</code> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception is raised.</p><p>Here is a basic example of a try-except block:</p><code>try:\n    # Code that may raise an exception\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised\n    print(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")</code><p>In this example, the <code>try</code> block attempts to divide 1 by 0, which raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. The <code>except</code> block catches this exception and prints an error message.</p><h3>Error Types</h3><p>Python has a variety of built-in error types that can be used to handle different types of exceptions. Some common error types include:</p><ul><li><code>TypeError</code>: Raised when a variable is not of the expected type.</li><li><code>ValueError</code>: Raised when a function or operation receives an argument with an incorrect value.</li><li><code>ZeroDivisionError</code>: Raised when a program attempts to divide by zero.</li><li><code>IndexError</code>: Raised when a program attempts to access an element outside the bounds of a list or tuple.</li><li><code>KeyError</code>: Raised when a program attempts to access a key that does not exist in a dictionary.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how you can use these error types in a try-except block:</p><code>try:\n    # Code that may raise an exception\n    x = 1 + \"hello\"\nexcept TypeError:\n    # Code that will be executed if a TypeError is raised\n    print(\"Invalid operation!\")</code><h3>Raising Exceptions</h3><p>In addition to catching exceptions, you can also raise your own exceptions using the <code>raise</code> statement. This allows you to signal that an error has occurred and provide a custom error message.</p><p>Here is an example of how you can raise an exception:</p><code>def divide(x, y):\n    if y == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")\n    return x / y</code><p>In this example, the <code>divide</code> function raises a <code>ValueError</code> if the user attempts to divide by zero.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with try-except blocks and error types:</p><ul><li>Always use specific error types instead of catching the general <code>Exception</code> class.</li><li>Keep the code in the <code>try</code> block as short as possible to avoid catching unrelated exceptions.</li><li>Use the <code>as</code> keyword to assign the exception to a variable, allowing you to access its attributes and methods.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices and using try-except blocks and error types effectively, you can write more robust and reliable code that handles errors and exceptions with ease.</p>', 'Error Handling and Exception Management', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(263, 276, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: object oriented programming</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=object+oriented+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/object+oriented+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/object+oriented+programming/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/object+oriented+programming/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=object+oriented+programming\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In this chapter, we will delve into the world of OOP and explore its fundamental concepts, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. It is essentially a design pattern or a template that defines the characteristics of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class.</p><p>For example:</p><code>class Car:</code><p>This defines a class named <code>Car</code>. We can then define attributes and methods within this class to describe the characteristics and behavior of a car.</p><ul><li><strong>Attributes</strong>: These are the data members of a class that are used to describe the characteristics of an object. For example, in the <code>Car</code> class, we might have attributes such as <code>color</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code>.</li><li><strong>Methods</strong>: These are the functions that are defined within a class and are used to describe the behavior of an object. For example, in the <code>Car</code> class, we might have methods such as <code>start_engine</code> and <code>accelerate</code>.</li></ul><h3>Objects</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class, and has its own set of attributes and methods. In other words, an object is a real-world entity that has its own characteristics and behavior, which are defined by the class.</p><p>For example, if we have a <code>Car</code> class, we can create an object of this class like this:</p><code>my_car = Car()</code><p>This creates a new object called <code>my_car</code> that is an instance of the <code>Car</code> class. We can then access the attributes and methods of this object using the dot notation.</p><p>For example:</p><code>my_car.color = \'red\'</code><code>my_car.start_engine()</code><h3>Inheritance</h3><p>Inheritance is the mechanism by which one class can inherit the attributes and methods of another class. The class that is being inherited from is called the <strong>parent class</strong> or <strong>superclass</strong>, and the class that is doing the inheriting is called the <strong>child class</strong> or <strong>subclass</strong>.</p><p>In Python, inheritance is implemented using the <code>(ParentClass)</code> syntax. For example:</p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):</code><p>This defines a new class called <code>ElectricCar</code> that inherits from the <code>Car</code> class. The <code>ElectricCar</code> class has access to all the attributes and methods of the <code>Car</code> class, and can also add new attributes and methods or override the ones inherited from the parent class.</p><p>Inheritance is useful for creating a hierarchy of classes that are related to each other. It allows us to create a more modular and reusable code, and to avoid duplicating code.</p><p>Here is an example of how we can use inheritance to create a hierarchy of classes:</p><code>class Vehicle:</code><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, year):</code><code>        self.color = color</code><code>        self.model = model</code><code>        self.year = year</code><code>    def start_engine(self):</code><code>        print(\'The engine is started\')</code><code>class Car(Vehicle):</code><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, year, num_doors):</code><code>        super().__init__(color, model, year)</code><code>        self.num_doors = num_doors</code><code>    def lock_doors(self):</code><code>        print(\'The doors are locked\')</code><code>class ElectricCar(Car):</code><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, year, num_doors, battery_capacity):</code><code>        super().__init__(color, model, year, num_doors)</code><code>        self.battery_capacity = battery_capacity</code><code>    def charge_battery(self):</code><code>        print(\'The battery is charged\')</code><p>In this example, we have a hierarchy of classes: <code>Vehicle</code>, <code>Car</code>, and <code>ElectricCar</code>. The <code>Car</code> class inherits from the <code>Vehicle</code> class, and the <code>ElectricCar</code> class inherits from the <code>Car</code> class. Each class adds new attributes and methods or overrides the ones inherited from the parent class.</p>', 'Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(264, 277, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python polymorphism encapsulation</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+polymorphism+encapsulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+polymorphism+encapsulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+polymorphism+encapsulation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+polymorphism+encapsulation/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+polymorphism+encapsulation\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Polymorphism and Encapsulation</h3><p>Polymorphism and encapsulation are two fundamental concepts in object-oriented programming (OOP). Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms, while encapsulation is the concept of hiding the implementation details of an object from the outside world. In this lesson, we will explore method overloading, method overriding, and data hiding, which are all related to polymorphism and encapsulation.</p><h3>Method Overloading</h3><p>Method overloading is a technique where multiple methods with the same name can be defined, but with different parameter lists. This allows objects of different classes to be treated as objects of a common superclass. In Python, method overloading is not directly supported, but it can be achieved using optional parameters or default values.</p><p>For example, consider a class <code>Calculator</code> with a method <code>add</code> that can take either two or three arguments:</p><pre><code>class Calculator:\n    def add(self, a, b, c=None):\n        if c is None:\n            return a + b\n        else:\n            return a + b + c\n\ncalc = Calculator()\nprint(calc.add(2, 3))  # Output: 5\nprint(calc.add(2, 3, 4))  # Output: 9</code></pre><h3>Method Overriding</h3><p>Method overriding is a technique where a subclass provides a different implementation of a method that is already defined in its superclass. This allows the subclass to specialize the behavior of the method for its own specific needs.</p><p>For example, consider a class <code>Shape</code> with a method <code>area</code> that calculates the area of the shape. A subclass <code>Circle</code> can override this method to provide its own implementation:</p><pre><code>class Shape:\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Circle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, radius):\n        self.radius = radius\n\n    def area(self):\n        return 3.14 * self.radius ** 2\ncircle = Circle(5)\nprint(circle.area())  # Output: 78.5</code></pre><h3>Data Hiding</h3><p>Data hiding is a technique where the implementation details of an object are hidden from the outside world. This is achieved by making the data members of the object private, and providing public methods to access and modify them.</p><p>In Python, data hiding can be achieved using single underscore (<code>_</code>) or double underscore (<code>__</code>) prefix before the attribute name. The single underscore prefix is a convention indicating that the attribute is intended to be private, while the double underscore prefix invokes name mangling, making it more difficult to access the attribute from outside the class.</p><p>For example, consider a class <code>BankAccount</code> with a private attribute <code>__balance</code> and public methods to deposit and withdraw money:</p><pre><code>class BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, balance=0):\n        self.__balance = balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        self.__balance += amount\n\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        if amount > self.__balance:\n            print(&quot;Insufficient balance&quot;)\n        else:\n            self.__balance -= amount\n\n    def get_balance(self):\n        return self.__balance\n\naccount = BankAccount(1000)\naccount.deposit(500)\naccount.withdraw(200)\nprint(account.get_balance())  # Output: 1300</code></pre><p>In conclusion, polymorphism and encapsulation are essential concepts in OOP that help to create robust, flexible, and maintainable software systems. Method overloading, method overriding, and data hiding are techniques that support these concepts, and are widely used in programming languages such as Python.</p>', 'Understanding Polymorphism and Encapsulation', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(265, 278, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python modules packages</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+modules+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+modules+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+modules+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+modules+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+modules+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Modules and Packages</h3><p>In Python, modules and packages are used to organize and structure code. A module is a single file that contains a set of related functions, classes, and variables, while a package is a collection of related modules. In this lesson, we will learn how to import modules, create packages, and use module aliases.</p><h3>Importing Modules</h3><p>Python has a vast collection of built-in modules that can be imported into our programs. To import a module, we use the <code>import</code> statement. For example, to import the <code>math</code> module, we use the following code:</p><code>import math</code><p>This imports the entire <code>math</code> module, and we can access its functions and variables using the dot notation. For example, to use the <code>sin</code> function from the <code>math</code> module, we use the following code:</p><code>import math<br>x = math.sin(30)</code><p>We can also import specific functions or variables from a module using the <code>from</code> keyword. For example, to import only the <code>sin</code> function from the <code>math</code> module, we use the following code:</p><code>from math import sin<br>x = sin(30)</code><h3>Creating Packages</h3><p>A package is a collection of related modules. To create a package, we create a directory with a <code>__init__.py</code> file inside it. The <code>__init__.py</code> file can be empty, but it is required to make the directory a package. For example, let\'s create a package called <code>mymath</code> with two modules: <code>basic.py</code> and <code>advanced.py</code>.</p><ul><li>Create a directory called <code>mymath</code></li><li>Create a file called <code>__init__.py</code> inside the <code>mymath</code> directory</li><li>Create a file called <code>basic.py</code> inside the <code>mymath</code> directory with the following code: <code>def add(x, y):<br>    return x + y</code></li><li>Create a file called <code>advanced.py</code> inside the <code>mymath</code> directory with the following code: <code>def multiply(x, y):<br>    return x * y</code></li></ul><p>To import the <code>basic</code> module from the <code>mymath</code> package, we use the following code:</p><code>from mymath import basic<br>x = basic.add(2, 3)</code><h3>Module Aliases</h3><p>Module aliases are used to give a shorter name to a module. To create a module alias, we use the <code>as</code> keyword. For example, to import the <code>math</code> module with the alias <code>m</code>, we use the following code:</p><code>import math as m<br>x = m.sin(30)</code><p>Module aliases are useful when we need to use a module with a long name repeatedly in our code. They can make our code more readable and easier to write.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with modules and packages:</p><ul><li>Use meaningful names for your modules and packages</li><li>Keep related modules together in a package</li><li>Use module aliases to make your code more readable</li><li>Avoid using the same name for a module and a variable</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, we can make our code more organized, readable, and maintainable.</p>', 'Working with Modules and Packages', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(266, 279, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python coding standards</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+coding+standards\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+coding+standards\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+coding+standards/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+coding+standards/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+coding+standards\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Best Practices for Code Organization and Reusability</h3><p>Welcome to day 35 of our beginner Python course. Today, we will be discussing the importance of code organization and reusability. Writing clean, readable, and maintainable code is crucial for any programmer. In this lesson, we will cover code refactoring, code review, and coding standards.</p><h3>Code Refactoring</h3><p>Code refactoring is the process of restructuring existing computer code without changing its external behavior. It involves simplifying and clarifying the code, making it more efficient, and reducing complexity. Refactoring is essential for maintaining and improving the quality of the codebase.</p><ul><li><strong>Why Refactor Code?</strong><ul><li>To improve code readability and understandability</li><li>To reduce code duplication and improve reusability</li><li>To improve code performance and efficiency</li><li>To fix bugs and improve code reliability</li></ul></li><li><strong>Best Practices for Code Refactoring</strong><ul><li>Keep it simple and concise</li><li>Avoid unnecessary complexity</li><li>Use meaningful variable names and comments</li><li>Test thoroughly after refactoring</li></ul></li></ul><h3>Code Review</h3><p>Code review is the process of examining someone else\'s code to find and fix mistakes, improve code quality, and ensure that the code meets the required standards. It involves checking the code for syntax errors, logic errors, and adherence to coding standards.</p><ul><li><strong>Benefits of Code Review</strong><ul><li>Improves code quality and reliability</li><li>Reduces bugs and errors</li><li>Enhances code readability and maintainability</li><li>Fosters knowledge sharing and collaboration among team members</li></ul></li><li><strong>Best Practices for Code Review</strong><ul><li>Be constructive and respectful</li><li>Focus on the code, not the person</li><li>Use clear and concise language</li><li>Test the code before reviewing</li></ul></li></ul><h3>Coding Standards</h3><p>Coding standards are a set of guidelines and rules that define how code should be written. They ensure consistency, readability, and maintainability of the codebase. Coding standards cover aspects such as naming conventions, indentation, comments, and error handling.</p><ul><li><strong>Importance of Coding Standards</strong><ul><li>Ensures consistency across the codebase</li><li>Improves code readability and understandability</li><li>Reduces errors and bugs</li><li>Enhances collaboration and knowledge sharing among team members</li></ul></li><li><strong>Best Practices for Coding Standards</strong><ul><li>Establish clear and concise guidelines</li><li>Use automated tools to enforce standards</li><li>Provide training and resources for team members</li><li>Regularly review and update standards</li></ul></li></ul><p>Example of coding standards in Python:<code>def greet(name: str) -> None:\n    print(f\"Hello, {name}!\")</code></p><p>In this example, we are using a clear and concise naming convention, proper indentation, and a docstring to explain the function\'s purpose.</p>', 'Best Practices for Code Organization and Reusability', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(267, 280, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to File Input/Output in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 36 of our Python course. Today, we will be exploring the world of file input/output in Python. File input/output is a crucial aspect of programming, as it allows us to store and retrieve data from files. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of file handling, reading files, and writing files.</p><h3>File Handling</h3><p>Before we dive into reading and writing files, let\'s first understand the basics of file handling. File handling refers to the process of creating, opening, reading, writing, and closing files. Python provides a built-in module called <code>os</code> that allows us to interact with the operating system and perform file operations.</p><ul><li>The <code>os</code> module provides functions such as <code>os.mkdir()</code> to create a new directory, <code>os.rmdir()</code> to remove a directory, and <code>os.remove()</code> to delete a file.</li><li>We can also use the <code>os.path</code> module to check if a file exists, get the file size, and get the file type.</li></ul><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>Now that we have covered the basics of file handling, let\'s move on to reading files. Python provides several ways to read files, including:</p><ul><li><code>open()</code> function: This function returns a file object that can be used to read or write files. The <code>open()</code> function takes two arguments: the file name and the mode.</li><li><code>read()</code> method: This method reads the contents of the file and returns a string.</li><li><code>readline()</code> method: This method reads a single line from the file and returns a string.</li><li><code>readlines()</code> method: This method reads all lines from the file and returns a list of strings.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to read a file using the <code>open()</code> function and the <code>read()</code> method:</p><code>file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;r&quot;)<br>contents = file.read()<br>print(contents)<br>file.close()</code><p>In this example, we open the file <code>example.txt</code> in read mode (<code>&quot;r&quot;</code>), read the contents of the file using the <code>read()</code> method, and then close the file using the <code>close()</code> method.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>Writing files is similar to reading files, except that we use the <code>w</code> mode instead of the <code>r</code> mode. Here is an example of how to write to a file using the <code>open()</code> function and the <code>write()</code> method:</p><code>file = open(&quot;example.txt&quot;, &quot;w&quot;)<br>file.write(&quot;Hello, World!&quot;)<br>file.close()</code><p>In this example, we open the file <code>example.txt</code> in write mode (<code>&quot;w&quot;</code>), write the string <code>&quot;Hello, World!&quot;</code> to the file using the <code>write()</code> method, and then close the file using the <code>close()</code> method.</p><p><strong>Important Note:</strong> When you open a file in write mode, the file is truncated if it already exists. If you want to append to the file instead of overwriting it, you can use the <code>a</code> mode instead of the <code>w</code> mode.</p><p>In conclusion, file input/output is a fundamental aspect of programming, and Python provides a simple and intuitive way to interact with files. By using the <code>open()</code> function, <code>read()</code> method, <code>write()</code> method, and other file-related functions, you can create, read, and write files with ease.</p>', 'Introduction to File Input/Output in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(268, 281, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv files</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with CSV Files in Python</h3><p>On this day, we will be exploring how to work with CSV files in Python. CSV stands for Comma Separated Values, and it is a widely used file format for storing and exchanging data. We will be using the pandas library, which is a powerful tool for data analysis in Python.</p><h3>What are CSV Files?</h3><p>CSV files are plain text files that store data in a tabular format, with each row representing a single record and each column representing a field or attribute of that record. CSV files are widely used in many fields, including business, science, and engineering, because they are easy to read and write, and can be imported and exported by many different applications.</p><h3>Introduction to the Pandas Library</h3><p>The pandas library is a powerful tool for data analysis in Python. It provides data structures and functions to efficiently handle structured data, including tabular data such as spreadsheets and SQL tables. The pandas library is built on top of the NumPy library, and it provides a comprehensive set of tools for data manipulation and analysis.</p><h3>Reading CSV Files with Pandas</h3><p>To read a CSV file using pandas, you can use the <code>read_csv</code> function. This function takes the path to the CSV file as an argument, and returns a DataFrame object, which is a two-dimensional table of data with columns of potentially different types.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read a CSV file using pandas: <code>import pandas as pd; df = pd.read_csv(\'data.csv\')</code>. In this example, we import the pandas library, and then use the <code>read_csv</code> function to read the CSV file named \'data.csv\' into a DataFrame object called <code>df</code>.</p><h3>Writing CSV Files with Pandas</h3><p>To write a CSV file using pandas, you can use the <code>to_csv</code> function. This function takes the path to the CSV file as an argument, and writes the DataFrame object to the CSV file.</p><p>Here is an example of how to write a CSV file using pandas: <code>df.to_csv(\'data.csv\', index=False)</code>. In this example, we use the <code>to_csv</code> function to write the DataFrame object <code>df</code> to the CSV file named \'data.csv\'. The <code>index=False</code> argument tells pandas not to write row indices into the CSV file.</p><h3>Data Analysis with Pandas</h3><p>Once you have read a CSV file into a DataFrame object, you can use the pandas library to perform a wide range of data analysis tasks. For example, you can use the <code>head</code> function to view the first few rows of the DataFrame, or the <code>info</code> function to view a summary of the DataFrame.</p><p>Here are some examples of data analysis tasks you can perform with pandas: <ul><li><strong>Viewing the first few rows of the DataFrame</strong>: <code>df.head()</code></li><li><strong>Viewing a summary of the DataFrame</strong>: <code>df.info()</code></li><li><strong>Filtering the DataFrame</strong>: <code>df[df[\'column\'] > 0]</code></li><li><strong>Grouping the DataFrame</strong>: <code>df.groupby(\'column\')</code></li><li><strong>Sorting the DataFrame</strong>: <code>df.sort_values(\'column\')</code></li></ul></p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this lesson, we have learned how to work with CSV files in Python using the pandas library. We have covered how to read and write CSV files, and how to perform a wide range of data analysis tasks using the pandas library. With this knowledge, you will be able to work with CSV files and perform data analysis tasks with ease.</p>', 'Working with CSV Files in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(269, 282, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python exception handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Exception Handling in Python</h3><p>Exception handling is a crucial aspect of programming in Python. It allows you to manage and handle errors that may occur during the execution of your code. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of exception handling, including try-except blocks, error handling, and debugging.</p><h3>Try-Except Blocks</h3><p>A try-except block is used to handle exceptions that may occur in your code. The <code>try</code> block contains the code that may potentially raise an exception, while the <code>except</code> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception is raised.</p><p>Here is an example of a try-except block:</p><pre><code>try:\n    # Code that may raise an exception\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised\n    print(\"Error: Cannot divide by zero!\")</code></pre><p>In this example, the <code>try</code> block attempts to divide 1 by 0, which raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. The <code>except</code> block catches this exception and prints an error message.</p><h3>Error Handling</h3><p>Error handling is the process of managing and handling errors that may occur in your code. There are several types of errors that can occur, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Syntax Errors</strong>: These occur when there is an error in the syntax of your code.</li><li><strong>Runtime Errors</strong>: These occur when an error occurs during the execution of your code.</li><li><strong>Logic Errors</strong>: These occur when there is an error in the logic of your code.</li></ul><p>To handle errors, you can use try-except blocks to catch and handle exceptions. You can also use the <code>finally</code> block to execute code regardless of whether an exception was raised or not.</p><p>Here is an example of error handling:</p><pre><code>try:\n    # Code that may raise an exception\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised\n    print(\"Error: Cannot divide by zero!\")\nfinally:\n    # Code that will be executed regardless of whether an exception was raised or not\n    print(\"Execution complete.\")</code></pre><h3>Debugging</h3><p>Debugging is the process of finding and fixing errors in your code. There are several tools and techniques that can be used for debugging, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Print Statements</strong>: These can be used to print out the values of variables and expressions during the execution of your code.</li><li><strong>Debugger</strong>: This is a tool that allows you to step through your code line by line and examine the values of variables and expressions.</li><li><strong>Logging</strong>: This involves writing messages to a log file during the execution of your code.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of debugging using print statements:</p><pre><code>x = 5\ny = 0\ntry:\n    # Code that may raise an exception\n    result = x / y\n    print(\"Result: \", result)\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    # Code that will be executed if an exception is raised\n    print(\"Error: Cannot divide by zero!\")\n    print(\"x: \", x)\n    print(\"y: \", y)</code></pre><p>In this example, the print statements are used to print out the values of x and y when an exception is raised.</p>', 'Exception Handling in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(270, 283, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python oop concepts</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming in Python</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In Python, OOP is used to create reusable and modular code. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of OOP in Python, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes in Python</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class. For example:</p><p><code>class Car:</code></p><p>A class can have attributes (data) and methods (functions). Attributes are used to store data, while methods are used to perform actions. For example:</p><p><code>class Car:</code><br><code>    def __init__(self, color, model):</code><br><code>        self.color = color</code><br><code>        self.model = model</code><br><code>    def start_engine(self):</code><br><code>        print(&quot;The engine is started&quot;)</code></p><p>In this example, <code>color</code> and <code>model</code> are attributes, while <code>start_engine</code> is a method.</p><h3>Objects in Python</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class. In other words, an object is a realization of a class. To create an object, we use the class name followed by parentheses containing the required arguments. For example:</p><p><code>my_car = Car(&quot;Red&quot;, &quot;Toyota&quot;)</code></p><p>Now, <code>my_car</code> is an object of the <code>Car</code> class. We can access the attributes and methods of the class using the object. For example:</p><p><code>print(my_car.color)  # Output: Red</code><br><code>my_car.start_engine()  # Output: The engine is started</code></p><h3>Inheritance in Python</h3><p>Inheritance is a mechanism in OOP that allows one class to inherit the properties and behavior of another class. The class that is being inherited is called the parent or superclass, while the class that is doing the inheriting is called the child or subclass. For example:</p><p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):</code><br><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, battery_capacity):</code><br><code>        super().__init__(color, model)</code><br><code>        self.battery_capacity = battery_capacity</code><br><code>    def charge_battery(self):</code><br><code>        print(&quot;The battery is charging&quot;)</code></p><p>In this example, <code>ElectricCar</code> is a subclass of <code>Car</code>. The <code>ElectricCar</code> class inherits all the attributes and methods of the <code>Car</code> class and also adds new attributes and methods.</p><h3>Benefits of OOP</h3><p>OOP provides several benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Code Reusability</strong>: OOP allows us to create reusable code, which reduces the amount of code that needs to be written and maintained.</li><li><strong>Modularity</strong>: OOP allows us to break down a complex system into smaller, independent modules, which makes it easier to develop and maintain.</li><li><strong>Easier Debugging</strong>: OOP makes it easier to identify and fix errors, as the code is organized into smaller, more manageable modules.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, OOP is a powerful programming paradigm that allows us to create reusable, modular, and maintainable code. Classes, objects, and inheritance are the fundamental concepts of OOP in Python, and understanding these concepts is essential for any Python programmer.</p>', 'Understanding Object-Oriented Programming in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14');
