(271, 284, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python decorators</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+decorators\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+decorators\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+decorators/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+decorators/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+decorators\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Implementing Decorators in Python</h3><p>Decorators are a powerful feature in Python that allows programmers to modify the behavior of function or class. They allow us to wrap another function in order to extend the behavior of the wrapped function, without permanently modifying it.</p><p>In this section, we will cover the basics of decorators, including how to define and use them. We will also discuss how to use function arguments with decorators.</p><h3>What are Decorators?</h3><p>A decorator is a small function that takes another function as an argument and extends the behavior of the latter function without explicitly modifying it. The original function is still accessible, but now it has additional functionality.</p><p>Here is a basic example of a decorator:</p><code>def my_decorator(func):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def wrapper():<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;print(&quot;Something is happening before the function is called.&quot;)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;func()<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;print(&quot;Something is happening after the function is called.&quot;)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;return wrapper<br><br>def say_whee():<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;print(&quot;Whee!&quot;)<br><br>say_whee = my_decorator(say_whee)<br>say_whee()</code><p>This will output:</p><code>Something is happening before the function is called.<br>Whee!<br>Something is happening after the function is called.</code><h3>Why Use Decorators?</h3><p>Decorators are useful for a variety of purposes, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Logging</strong>: You can use decorators to log information about function calls, such as the input arguments and the output values.</li><li><strong>Authentication</strong>: You can use decorators to check if a user is authenticated before allowing them to call a function.</li><li><strong>Timing functions</strong>: You can use decorators to time how long a function takes to execute.</li></ul><h3>Using the @ Symbol</h3><p>Instead of using the <code>my_decorator</code> function to decorate the <code>say_whee</code> function, we can use the <code>@</code> symbol to make the code more readable:</p><code>@my_decorator<br>def say_whee():<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;print(&quot;Whee!&quot;)</code><p>This is equivalent to the previous example, but it is more concise and readable.</p><h3>Decorators with Arguments</h3><p>Sometimes, we want to pass arguments to a decorator. We can do this by adding another layer to the decorator:</p><code>def repeat(num_repeats):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def decorator_repeat(func):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;for i in range(num_repeats):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;func(*args, **kwargs)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;return wrapper<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;return decorator_repeat<br><br>@repeat(4)<br>def greet(name):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;print(f&quot;Hello {name}&quot;)</code><p>This will output:</p><code>Hello John<br>Hello John<br>Hello John<br>Hello John</code><h3>Functions as Arguments</h3><p>Functions are first-class citizens in Python, which means they can be passed as arguments to other functions. This is useful when we want to write higher-order functions, which are functions that take other functions as arguments or return functions as output.</p><p>Here is an example of a function that takes another function as an argument:</p><code>def twice(func):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;func(*args, **kwargs)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;func(*args, **kwargs)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;return wrapper<br><br>def hello(name):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;print(f&quot;Hello {name}&quot;)</code><p>We can use the <code>twice</code> function to call the <code>hello</code> function twice:</p><code>hello = twice(hello)<br>hello(&quot;John&quot;)</code><p>This will output:</p><code>Hello John<br>Hello John</code><p>In conclusion, decorators are a powerful feature in Python that allow us to modify the behavior of functions or classes. They are useful for a variety of purposes, including logging, authentication, and timing functions. We can use the <code>@</code> symbol to make the code more readable, and we can pass arguments to decorators by adding another layer to the decorator.</p>', 'Implementing Decorators in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(272, 285, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: flask web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=flask+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/flask+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/flask+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/flask+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=flask+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Development with Flask</h3><p>Welcome to Day 41 of our Python course, where we will be exploring the world of web development using the Flask framework. Flask is a micro web framework written in Python, and it is an excellent choice for building small to medium-sized web applications.</p><h3>What is Flask?</h3><p>Flask is a lightweight framework that allows you to build web applications quickly and efficiently. It is often referred to as a micro framework because it does not require a lot of overhead or configuration to get started. Flask is ideal for building small web applications, prototyping, and proof-of-concept projects.</p><h3>Key Features of Flask</h3><ul><li><strong>Lightweight</strong>: Flask is a very lightweight framework, making it easy to learn and use.</li><li><strong>Flexible</strong>: Flask is a flexible framework that allows you to build web applications using a variety of templates, databases, and caching systems.</li><li><strong>Extensive Libraries</strong>: Flask has an extensive collection of libraries and extensions that make it easy to perform common web development tasks.</li></ul><h3>Setting up Flask</h3><p>To get started with Flask, you will need to install it using pip, the Python package manager. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal: <code>pip install flask</code>. Once Flask is installed, you can create a new Flask application by creating a new Python file and importing the Flask class: <code>from flask import Flask</code>. You can then create a new instance of the Flask class, passing in the name of your application: <code>app = Flask(__name__)</code>.</p><h3>Routing in Flask</h3><p>Routing is the process of mapping URLs to specific functions in your Flask application. In Flask, you can use the <code>@app.route()</code> decorator to define routes for your application. For example: <code>@app.route(\'/\')</code> would define a route for the root URL of your application. You can also use the <code>@app.route()</code> decorator to define routes for other URLs, such as <code>@app.route(\'/about\')</code> or <code>@app.route(\'/contact\')</code>.</p><h3>Templates in Flask</h3><p>Templates are a way to separate the presentation layer of your application from the logic layer. In Flask, you can use the Jinja2 templating engine to render templates. To use templates in Flask, you will need to create a new directory called <code>templates</code> in the root of your application, and then create a new HTML file inside of that directory. You can then use the <code>render_template()</code> function to render the template: <code>return render_template(\'index.html\')</code>.</p><h3>Example Code</h3><p>Here is an example of a simple Flask application that uses routing and templates: <code>from flask import Flask, render_template<br>app = Flask(__name__)<br><br>@app.route(\'/\')<br>def index():<br>    return render_template(\'index.html\')<br><br>@app.route(\'/about\')<br>def about():<br>    return render_template(\'about.html\')<br><br>if __name__ == \'__main__\':<br>    app.run()</code></p>', 'Introduction to Web Development with Flask', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(273, 286, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: flask web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=flask+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/flask+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/flask+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/flask+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=flask+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building a Simple Web Application with Flask</h3><p>Welcome to Day 42 of the Python course. Today, we will be learning about building a simple web application using Flask, a popular Python web framework. We will cover topics such as creating a Flask app, database integration, and user authentication.</p><h3>Creating a Flask App</h3><p>Flask is a micro web framework that is ideal for building small web applications. To create a Flask app, you need to import the Flask class and create an instance of it. Here is an example of how to create a simple Flask app:</p><code>from flask import Flask\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(\'/\')\ndef home():\n    return \'Hello, World!\'\n\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\n    app.run()</code><p>This code creates a Flask app that displays \'Hello, World!\' when you navigate to the root URL.</p><h3>Database Integration</h3><p>To store and retrieve data in a Flask app, you need to integrate a database. Flask supports various databases, including SQLite, MySQL, and PostgreSQL. Here is an example of how to integrate a SQLite database into a Flask app:</p><code>from flask import Flask, g\nimport sqlite3\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# Connect to the database\ndef get_db():\n    db = getattr(g, \'_database\', None)\n    if db is None:\n        db = g._database = sqlite3.connect(\'database.db\')\n    return db</code><p>This code connects to a SQLite database file named \'database.db\'.</p><h3>User Authentication</h3><p>User authentication is an important aspect of web development. It involves verifying the identity of users and restricting access to certain parts of the application. Flask provides various libraries and tools for implementing user authentication, including Flask-Login and Flask-Security. Here is an example of how to implement basic user authentication using Flask-Login:</p><code>from flask import Flask, redirect, url_for\nfrom flask_login import LoginManager, UserMixin, login_user, logout_user\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# Define a user class\nclass User(UserMixin):\n    def __init__(self, id, username, password):\n        self.id = id\n        self.username = username\n        self.password = password\n\n# Create a login manager\nlogin_manager = LoginManager(app)\n\n# Load a user\n@login_manager.user_loader\ndef load_user(user_id):\n    return User.query.get(int(user_id))</code><p>This code defines a user class and creates a login manager using Flask-Login.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>When building a Flask app, it\'s essential to follow best practices to ensure the app is secure, scalable, and maintainable. Here are some best practices to keep in mind:</p><ul><li><strong>Use a virtual environment</strong>: A virtual environment helps to isolate the app\'s dependencies and prevent conflicts with other packages.</li><li><strong>Use a version control system</strong>: A version control system such as Git helps to track changes and collaborate with other developers.</li><li><strong>Test the app</strong>: Testing the app helps to identify and fix bugs before deployment.</li><li><strong>Use a secure password</strong>: Using a secure password helps to prevent unauthorized access to the app.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can build a secure, scalable, and maintainable Flask app.</p>', 'Building a Simple Web Application with Flask', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(274, 287, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python web scraping</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+web+scraping\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+web+scraping\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+web+scraping/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+web+scraping/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+web+scraping\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Web Scraping with Python</h3><p>Web scraping is the process of automatically extracting data from websites, web pages, and online documents. Python is a popular language used for web scraping due to its extensive libraries and tools. In this lesson, we will introduce you to the basics of web scraping using Python.</p><p>There are several libraries available in Python for web scraping, but we will focus on two of the most popular ones: Beautiful Soup and Requests.</p><h3>Beautiful Soup</h3><p>Beautiful Soup is a Python library used for parsing HTML and XML documents. It creates a parse tree from page source code that can be used to extract data in a hierarchical and more readable manner.</p><p>Here is an example of how to use Beautiful Soup to parse an HTML document:</p><code>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup\nimport requests\n\nurl = \'http://example.com\'\nresponse = requests.get(url)\nsoup = BeautifulSoup(response.text, \'html.parser\')\nprint(soup.title.string)</code><p>In this example, we first import the necessary libraries. We then send a GET request to the URL using the Requests library. The response from the server is stored in the <strong>response</strong> variable. We then create a Beautiful Soup object and specify the parser library. Finally, we print the title of the webpage.</p><h3>Requests</h3><p>The Requests library is used to send HTTP requests and returns server responses. It is often used in conjunction with Beautiful Soup to fetch the HTML content of a webpage.</p><p>Here are the different types of HTTP requests that can be sent using the Requests library:</p><ul><li>GET: used to retrieve data from a server</li><li>POST: used to send data to a server for processing</li><li>PUT: used to update existing data on a server</li><li>DELETE: used to delete data from a server</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use the Requests library to send a GET request:</p><code>import requests\nurl = \'http://example.com\'\nresponse = requests.get(url)\nprint(response.status_code)</code><p>In this example, we send a GET request to the URL and print the status code of the response.</p><h3>Web Scraping</h3><p>Web scraping involves navigating a website, searching for the data you need, and then extracting it. Here are the steps involved in web scraping:</p><ul><li>Navigate to the website: use the Requests library to send a GET request to the URL of the webpage you want to scrape</li><li>Inspect the webpage: use the developer tools in your browser to inspect the HTML elements of the webpage and identify the data you want to extract</li><li>Parse the HTML: use Beautiful Soup to parse the HTML content of the webpage and create a parse tree</li><li>Extract the data: use the Beautiful Soup object to navigate the parse tree and extract the data you need</li><li>Store the data: store the extracted data in a file or database for later use</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use Beautiful Soup and Requests to extract data from a webpage:</p><code>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup\nimport requests\n\nurl = \'http://example.com\'\nresponse = requests.get(url)\nsoup = BeautifulSoup(response.text, \'html.parser\')\nlinks = soup.find_all(\'a\')\nfor link in links:\n    print(link.get(\'href\'))</code><p>In this example, we navigate to the webpage, parse the HTML content, and extract all the links on the webpage.</p>', 'Introduction to Web Scraping with Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(275, 288, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv json files</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+json+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+json+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Working with CSV and JSON Files in Python</h3><p>In this lesson, we will learn how to work with CSV and JSON files in Python. These file formats are widely used for storing and exchanging data, and being able to read and write them is an essential skill for any Python programmer.</p><h3>What are CSV Files?</h3><p>A CSV (Comma Separated Values) file is a plain text file that contains a list of values separated by commas. Each line in the file represents a single record, and each value in the line represents a field or column in the record. CSV files are often used to import and export data from spreadsheets and databases.</p><p>For example, a CSV file might contain the following data:</p><code>name,age,city\nJohn,25,New York\nJane,30,London\nBob,35,Paris</code><p>This data represents three records, each with three fields: name, age, and city.</p><h3>What are JSON Files?</h3><p>A JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) file is a text file that contains data in a format similar to JavaScript objects. JSON files are often used to exchange data between web servers and web applications.</p><p>For example, a JSON file might contain the following data:</p><code>{\n    \"name\": \"John\",\n    \"age\": 25,\n    \"city\": \"New York\"\n}\n{\n    \"name\": \"Jane\",\n    \"age\": 30,\n    \"city\": \"London\"\n}\n{\n    \"name\": \"Bob\",\n    \"age\": 35,\n    \"city\": \"Paris\"\n}</code><p>This data represents three objects, each with three properties: name, age, and city.</p><h3>Working with CSV Files in Python</h3><p>Python provides several ways to work with CSV files, including the <strong>csv</strong> module and the <strong>pandas</strong> library. The <strong>csv</strong> module provides functions for reading and writing CSV files, while the <strong>pandas</strong> library provides a more convenient and powerful way to work with CSV files.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read a CSV file using the <strong>csv</strong> module:</p><code>import csv\nwith open(\'data.csv\', \'r\') as file:\n    reader = csv.reader(file)\n    for row in reader:\n        print(row)</code><p>This code opens a file called <strong>data.csv</strong> and reads it row by row, printing each row to the console.</p><h3>Working with JSON Files in Python</h3><p>Python provides several ways to work with JSON files, including the <strong>json</strong> module. The <strong>json</strong> module provides functions for reading and writing JSON files.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read a JSON file using the <strong>json</strong> module:</p><code>import json\nwith open(\'data.json\', \'r\') as file:\n    data = json.load(file)\n    print(data)</code><p>This code opens a file called <strong>data.json</strong> and reads it into a Python object, printing the object to the console.</p><h3>Using Pandas to Work with CSV and JSON Files</h3><p>The <strong>pandas</strong> library provides a powerful way to work with CSV and JSON files. The <strong>read_csv</strong> function can be used to read a CSV file into a <strong>DataFrame</strong> object, while the <strong>read_json</strong> function can be used to read a JSON file into a <strong>DataFrame</strong> object.</p><p>Here is an example of how to read a CSV file using <strong>pandas</strong>:</p><code>import pandas as pd\ndata = pd.read_csv(\'data.csv\')\nprint(data)</code><p>This code reads a CSV file called <strong>data.csv</strong> into a <strong>DataFrame</strong> object and prints the object to the console.</p><p>Similarly, here is an example of how to read a JSON file using <strong>pandas</strong>:</p><code>import pandas as pd\ndata = pd.read_json(\'data.json\')\nprint(data)</code><p>This code reads a JSON file called <strong>data.json</strong> into a <strong>DataFrame</strong> object and prints the object to the console.</p><h3>Key Takeaways</h3><ul><li>CSV files are plain text files that contain a list of values separated by commas.</li><li>JSON files are text files that contain data in a format similar to JavaScript objects.</li><li>Python provides several ways to work with CSV and JSON files, including the <strong>csv</strong> and <strong>json</strong> modules, and the <strong>pandas</strong> library.</li><li>The <strong>pandas</strong> library provides a powerful way to work with CSV and JSON files, including the <strong>read_csv</strong> and <strong>read_json</strong> functions.</li></ul>', 'Working with CSV and JSON Files in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(276, 289, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python oop concepts</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Object-Oriented Programming in Python</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In this lesson, we will explore the basics of OOP in Python, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class. For example:</p><code>class Car:</code><p>This class can have attributes (data) and methods (functions). Attributes are used to describe the characteristics of the class, while methods are used to define the actions that the class can perform.</p><p>For example, a <code>Car</code> class might have attributes such as <code>color</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code>, and methods such as <code>start_engine</code> and <code>accelerate</code>.</p><h3>Objects</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class. It has its own set of attributes (data) and methods (functions). In Python, an object is created using the class name followed by parentheses containing the arguments. For example:</p><code>my_car = Car(&#39;red&#39;, &#39;Toyota&#39;, 2020)</code><p>This creates a new object called <code>my_car</code> that is an instance of the <code>Car</code> class. The object has its own set of attributes, such as <code>color</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code>, which can be accessed using the dot notation.</p><p>For example:</p><code>print(my_car.color)  # Output: red</code><h3>Inheritance</h3><p>Inheritance is a mechanism that allows one class to inherit the attributes and methods of another class. The child class inherits all the attributes and methods of the parent class and can also add new attributes and methods or override the ones inherited from the parent class.</p><p>In Python, inheritance is implemented using the <code>(ParentClass)</code> syntax. For example:</p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):</code><p>This defines a new class called <code>ElectricCar</code> that inherits all the attributes and methods of the <code>Car</code> class. The <code>ElectricCar</code> class can also add new attributes and methods or override the ones inherited from the <code>Car</code> class.</p><h3>Benefits of OOP</h3><p>OOP provides several benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Code Reusability</strong>: OOP allows for code reusability, which means that a class can be used to create multiple objects, reducing the amount of code that needs to be written.</li><li><strong>Modularity</strong>: OOP allows for modularity, which means that a program can be broken down into smaller, independent modules that can be developed and tested separately.</li><li><strong>Easier Maintenance</strong>: OOP makes it easier to maintain and modify code, as changes can be made at the class level rather than at the individual object level.</li></ul><h3>Example Use Case</h3><p>Here is an example of how OOP can be used to model a simple banking system:</p><code>class BankAccount:</code><p>This class has attributes such as <code>account_number</code>, <code>balance</code>, and methods such as <code>deposit</code> and <code>withdraw</code>.</p><code>class SavingsAccount(BankAccount):</code><p>This class inherits all the attributes and methods of the <code>BankAccount</code> class and adds a new attribute <code>interest_rate</code> and a new method <code>calculate_interest</code>.</p><p>This is just a simple example, but it illustrates how OOP can be used to model complex systems and relationships.</p>', 'Understanding Object-Oriented Programming in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(277, 290, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python\'s Built-in Data Structures</h3><p>Python has several built-in data structures that can be used to store and manipulate data. In this lesson, we will explore three of the most commonly used data structures: Lists, Tuples, and Dictionaries.</p><h3>Lists</h3><p>A list in Python is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other lists. Lists are denoted by square brackets <code>[]</code> and are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of lists:</p><ul><li>Lists are ordered, meaning that the items in the list have a specific order.</li><li>Lists are mutable, meaning that they can be modified after creation.</li><li>Lists can contain duplicate values.</li><li>Lists can be nested, meaning that a list can contain another list as an item.</li></ul><p>Some common list operations include:</p><ul><li><code>append()</code>: adds an item to the end of the list</li><li><code>insert()</code>: inserts an item at a specific position in the list</li><li><code>remove()</code>: removes the first occurrence of an item in the list</li><li><code>sort()</code>: sorts the items in the list in ascending order</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a list:</p><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>my_list.append(6)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]<br>my_list.insert(0, 0)<br>print(my_list)  # Output: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]</code><h3>Tuples</h3><p>A tuple in Python is a collection of items that can be of any data type, including strings, integers, floats, and other tuples. Tuples are denoted by parentheses <code>()</code> and are immutable, meaning they cannot be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of tuples:</p><ul><li>Tuples are ordered, meaning that the items in the tuple have a specific order.</li><li>Tuples are immutable, meaning that they cannot be modified after creation.</li><li>Tuples can contain duplicate values.</li><li>Tuples can be nested, meaning that a tuple can contain another tuple as an item.</li></ul><p>Some common tuple operations include:</p><ul><li><code>index()</code>: returns the index of the first occurrence of an item in the tuple</li><li><code>count()</code>: returns the number of occurrences of an item in the tuple</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a tuple:</p><code>my_tuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)<br>print(my_tuple[0])  # Output: 1<br>print(my_tuple.index(3))  # Output: 2</code><h3>Dictionaries</h3><p>A dictionary in Python is a collection of key-value pairs. Dictionaries are denoted by curly brackets <code>{}</code> and are mutable, meaning they can be modified after creation.</p><p>Here are some key features of dictionaries:</p><ul><li>Dictionaries are unordered, meaning that the items in the dictionary do not have a specific order.</li><li>Dictionaries are mutable, meaning that they can be modified after creation.</li><li>Dictionaries cannot contain duplicate keys.</li><li>Dictionaries can be nested, meaning that a dictionary can contain another dictionary as a value.</li></ul><p>Some common dictionary operations include:</p><ul><li><code>keys()</code>: returns a list of all the keys in the dictionary</li><li><code>values()</code>: returns a list of all the values in the dictionary</li><li><code>items()</code>: returns a list of all the key-value pairs in the dictionary</li></ul><p>Example of creating and manipulating a dictionary:</p><code>my_dict = {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30}<br>print(my_dict[\'name\'])  # Output: John<br>my_dict[\'country\'] = \'USA\'<br>print(my_dict)  # Output: {\'name\': \'John\', \'age\': 30, \'country\': \'USA\'}</code><p>In conclusion, Python\'s built-in data structures are powerful tools for storing and manipulating data. Lists, tuples, and dictionaries each have their own unique features and uses, and understanding how to use them is essential for any Python programmer.</p>', 'Exploring Python\'s Built-in Data Structures', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(278, 291, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python libraries</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+libraries/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+libraries/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+libraries\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python\'s Popular Libraries</h3><p>Welcome to Day 47 of our Python course. Today, we will be exploring three of the most popular and widely used libraries in Python: NumPy, Pandas, and Matplotlib. These libraries are essential for any data scientist or analyst working with Python, and are used extensively in various fields such as science, engineering, and finance.</p><p><strong>NumPy</strong> is a library for working with arrays and mathematical operations. It provides support for large, multi-dimensional arrays and matrices, and is the foundation of most scientific computing in Python. With NumPy, you can perform complex mathematical operations, such as linear algebra and random number generation, with ease.</p><ul><li>Key features of NumPy include:<ul><li>Support for large, multi-dimensional arrays and matrices</li><li>Basic mathematical operations, such as addition and multiplication</li><li>Advanced mathematical operations, such as linear algebra and random number generation</li></ul></li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use NumPy to create and manipulate an array:<code>import numpy as np<br>array = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])<br>print(array)<br>print(np.mean(array))</code></p><p><strong>Pandas</strong> is a library for working with structured data, such as tables and time series. It provides data structures and functions for efficiently handling and analyzing large datasets. With Pandas, you can easily read and write data from various file formats, such as CSV and Excel, and perform data manipulation and analysis tasks, such as filtering and grouping.</p><ul><li>Key features of Pandas include:<ul><li>Data structures, such as Series and DataFrame</li><li>Data manipulation and analysis functions, such as filtering and grouping</li><li>Input/Output functions, such as reading and writing CSV and Excel files</li></ul></li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use Pandas to create and manipulate a DataFrame:<code>import pandas as pd<br>data = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Mary\', \'David\'], \'Age\': [25, 31, 42]}<br>df = pd.DataFrame(data)<br>print(df)<br>print(df[df[\'Age\'] > 30])</code></p><p><strong>Matplotlib</strong> is a library for creating static, animated, and interactive visualizations in Python. It provides a comprehensive set of tools for creating high-quality 2D and 3D plots, charts, and graphs. With Matplotlib, you can easily create a wide range of visualizations, from simple line plots to complex heatmaps and scatter plots.</p><ul><li>Key features of Matplotlib include:<ul><li>Comprehensive set of tools for creating high-quality 2D and 3D plots</li><li>Support for various output formats, such as PDF and PNG</li><li>Customizable visualization options, such as colors and fonts</li></ul></li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use Matplotlib to create a simple line plot:<code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt<br>x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]<br>y = [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]<br>plt.plot(x, y)<br>plt.show()</code></p><p>In conclusion, NumPy, Pandas, and Matplotlib are three essential libraries for any data scientist or analyst working with Python. They provide a wide range of tools and functions for working with arrays, structured data, and visualizations, and are used extensively in various fields. By mastering these libraries, you will be able to efficiently handle and analyze large datasets, and create high-quality visualizations to communicate your findings.</p>', 'Introduction to Python\'s Popular Libraries', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(279, 292, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python coding best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+coding+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+coding+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+coding+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+coding+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+coding+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Best Practices for Coding in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 48 of our Python course, where we will be covering the essential best practices for coding in Python. As a beginner, it\'s crucial to develop good habits from the start to ensure your code is readable, maintainable, and efficient.</p><h3>PEP 8: The Official Python Style Guide</h3><p>PEP 8, also known as the <strong>Python Enhancement Proposal 8</strong>, is the official style guide for Python code. It provides a set of guidelines and conventions for writing clean, readable, and consistent code. Some key aspects of PEP 8 include:</p><ul><li><strong>Indentation</strong>: Use 4 spaces for indentation, and avoid using tabs.</li><li><strong>Line Length</strong>: Limit lines to a maximum of 79 characters, and use line breaks for longer lines.</li><li><strong>Spacing</strong>: Use two blank lines to separate top-level functions and classes, and one blank line to separate methods within a class.</li><li><strong>Naming Conventions</strong>: Use lowercase letters, numbers, and underscores for variable and function names, and avoid using special characters.</li></ul><p>By following PEP 8, you can ensure that your code is consistent with the Python community and easy to read and understand.</p><h3>Code Review: The Importance of Feedback</h3><p>Code review is an essential part of the development process, where you review and provide feedback on someone else\'s code. This process helps to:</p><ul><li><strong>Improve Code Quality</strong>: Identify and fix bugs, improve performance, and enhance readability.</li><li><strong>Share Knowledge</strong>: Learn from others, and share your own knowledge and expertise.</li><li><strong>Enhance Collaboration</strong>: Foster a sense of community, and encourage open communication and feedback.</li></ul><p>When performing a code review, consider the following:</p><ul><li><strong>Readability</strong>: Is the code easy to read and understand?</li><li><strong>Maintainability</strong>: Is the code easy to modify and extend?</li><li><strong>Performance</strong>: Does the code perform well, and are there any potential bottlenecks?</li></ul><p>By providing constructive feedback, you can help others improve their code and develop their skills.</p><h3>Testing: Ensuring Your Code Works</h3><p>Testing is a critical aspect of software development, where you verify that your code works as expected. There are several types of testing, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Unit Testing</strong>: Test individual units of code, such as functions or methods.</li><li><strong>Integration Testing</strong>: Test how different units of code interact with each other.</li><li><strong>System Testing</strong>: Test the entire system, from end to end.</li></ul><p>In Python, you can use the <code>unittest</code> module to write and run tests. For example:</p><code>import unittest\n\nclass TestExample(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_add(self):\n        self.assertEqual(2 + 2, 4)\n\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\n    unittest.main()</code><p>By writing tests, you can ensure that your code works correctly, and catch any bugs or errors early on.</p>', 'Best Practices for Coding in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14');
