(280, 293, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python programming project</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+programming+project\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+programming+project\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+programming+project/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+programming+project/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+programming+project\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Review and Practice with a Comprehensive Project</h3><p>Welcome to Day 49 of the Python course. Today, we will be reviewing and practicing all the concepts we have learned so far by working on a comprehensive project. This project will help us develop problem-solving skills, learn how to debug our code, and understand the importance of project development.</p><p>Project development is an essential part of any programming course. It helps us apply the concepts we have learned to real-world problems and develop a deeper understanding of the language. In this project, we will be creating a simple banking system that allows users to create accounts, deposit and withdraw money, and check their balance.</p><h3>Project Requirements</h3><ul><li>Create a class called <code>BankAccount</code> with the following attributes: account number, account holder\'s name, and balance.</li><li>Implement methods to deposit and withdraw money from the account.</li><li>Implement a method to check the account balance.</li><li>Create a main function to test the <code>BankAccount</code> class.</li></ul><p>Let\'s start by defining the <code>BankAccount</code> class. We will use the following code:</p><code>class BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, account_number, account_holder, balance=0):\n        self.account_number = account_number\n        self.account_holder = account_holder\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        if amount > 0:\n            self.balance += amount\n            print(f\'Deposited {amount}. Current balance is {self.balance}\')\n        else:\n            print(\'Invalid deposit amount\')\n\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        if 0 < amount <= self.balance:\n            self.balance -= amount\n            print(f\'Withdrew {amount}. Current balance is {self.balance}\')\n        else:\n            print(\'Invalid withdrawal amount\')\n\n    def check_balance(self):\n        print(f\'Current balance is {self.balance}\')\n</code><p>Now, let\'s create a main function to test the <code>BankAccount</code> class. We will use the following code:</p><code>def main():\n    account = BankAccount(\'12345\', \'John Doe\', 1000)\n    account.check_balance()\n    account.deposit(500)\n    account.check_balance()\n    account.withdraw(200)\n    account.check_balance()\n\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\n    main()\n</code><h3>Problem Solving</h3><p>Problem solving is an essential part of any programming course. It helps us develop critical thinking skills and learn how to approach complex problems. In this project, we will be solving the following problems:</p><ul><li><strong>Problem 1:</strong> What happens when a user tries to withdraw more money than their current balance?</li><li><strong>Problem 2:</strong> What happens when a user tries to deposit a negative amount?</li></ul><p>Let\'s solve these problems by modifying the <code>withdraw</code> and <code>deposit</code> methods. We will use the following code:</p><code>def withdraw(self, amount):\n    if 0 < amount <= self.balance:\n        self.balance -= amount\n        print(f\'Withdrew {amount}. Current balance is {self.balance}\')\n    elif amount <= 0:\n        print(\'Invalid withdrawal amount\')\n    else:\n        print(\'Insufficient funds\')\n\ndef deposit(self, amount):\n    if amount > 0:\n        self.balance += amount\n        print(f\'Deposited {amount}. Current balance is {self.balance}\')\n    else:\n        print(\'Invalid deposit amount\')\n</code><h3>Debugging</h3><p>Debugging is an essential part of any programming course. It helps us identify and fix errors in our code. In this project, we will be debugging the following errors:</p><ul><li><strong>Error 1:</strong> What happens when a user tries to create an account with a negative balance?</li><li><strong>Error 2:</strong> What happens when a user tries to withdraw more money than their current balance?</li></ul><p>Let\'s debug these errors by modifying the <code>__init__</code> method and the <code>withdraw</code> method. We will use the following code:</p><code>def __init__(self, account_number, account_holder, balance=0):\n    if balance < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\'Invalid balance\')\n    self.account_number = account_number\n    self.account_holder = account_holder\n    self.balance = balance\n</code><p>In conclusion, project development, problem solving, and debugging are essential skills for any programmer. By working on this comprehensive project, we have developed these skills and gained a deeper understanding of the Python language.</p>', 'Review and Practice with a Comprehensive Project', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(281, 294, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python file input output</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+file+input+output/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+file+input+output\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python File Input/Output</h3><p>Welcome to Day 50 of the Python course. Today, we will be covering the basics of file input/output in Python. File input/output is a crucial aspect of programming, as it allows us to store and retrieve data from files. In this lesson, we will learn how to read and write files in Python, as well as understand the different file modes.</p><h3>Reading Files</h3><p>In Python, we can read files using the <code>open()</code> function. This function returns a file object, which we can then use to read the contents of the file. The basic syntax for reading a file is as follows:</p><ul><li><code>file = open(\'filename.txt\', \'r\')</code>: Opens the file in read mode.</li><li><code>content = file.read()</code>: Reads the entire contents of the file.</li><li><code>file.close()</code>: Closes the file.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to read a file:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'r\')\ncontent = file.read()\nprint(content)\nfile.close()</code><p>In this example, we open the file \'example.txt\' in read mode, read the entire contents of the file, print the contents, and then close the file.</p><h3>Writing Files</h3><p>In Python, we can write files using the <code>open()</code> function. This function returns a file object, which we can then use to write to the file. The basic syntax for writing a file is as follows:</p><ul><li><code>file = open(\'filename.txt\', \'w\')</code>: Opens the file in write mode. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>file.write(\'content\')</code>: Writes the specified content to the file.</li><li><code>file.close()</code>: Closes the file.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to write a file:</p><code>file = open(\'example.txt\', \'w\')\nfile.write(\'Hello, World!\')\nfile.close()</code><p>In this example, we open the file \'example.txt\' in write mode, write the string \'Hello, World!\' to the file, and then close the file.</p><h3>File Modes</h3><p>When opening a file in Python, we must specify the file mode. The file mode determines how the file will be opened and what operations can be performed on it. The following are the most common file modes:</p><ul><li><code>\'r\'</code>: Read mode. Opens the file for reading only.</li><li><code>\'w\'</code>: Write mode. Opens the file for writing only. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a\'</code>: Append mode. Opens the file for appending only. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'r+\'</code>: Read and write mode. Opens the file for both reading and writing.</li><li><code>\'w+\'</code>: Read and write mode. Opens the file for both reading and writing. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li><li><code>\'a+\'</code>: Read and append mode. Opens the file for both reading and appending. If the file does not exist, it will be created.</li></ul><p>It is <strong>important to note</strong> that when opening a file in write mode, the entire contents of the file will be deleted. If you want to append to the end of the file instead of overwriting it, you should use append mode.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>When working with files in Python, it is a good idea to follow these best practices:</p><ul><li>Always close the file when you are finished with it.</li><li>Use the <code>with</code> statement to ensure the file is properly closed.</li><li>Specify the file mode when opening the file.</li><li>Be aware of the file path and name.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can ensure that your file input/output operations are safe and efficient.</p>', 'Introduction to Python File Input/Output', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(282, 295, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python csv json files</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+csv+json+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+csv+json+files/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+csv+json+files\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Working with CSV and JSON Files in Python</h3><p>Welcome to Day 51 of our Python course. Today, we will be exploring how to work with CSV and JSON files in Python. These file formats are widely used for data exchange and storage, and understanding how to work with them is essential for any Python developer.</p><h3>Introduction to CSV Files</h3><p>CSV (Comma Separated Values) files are plain text files that store tabular data, such as spreadsheets or tables. Each line in the file represents a row, and each value is separated by a comma. CSV files are widely used for data exchange between different applications and systems.</p><p>The <strong>csv</strong> module in Python provides classes for reading and writing tabular data in CSV format. The module provides two main classes: <code>reader</code> and <code>writer</code>. The <code>reader</code> class is used to read CSV files, while the <code>writer</code> class is used to write CSV files.</p><h3>Reading CSV Files</h3><p>To read a CSV file, you can use the <code>reader</code> class from the <strong>csv</strong> module. Here is an example of how to read a CSV file:</p><code>import csv<br/>with open(\'example.csv\', \'r\') as file:<br/>    reader = csv.reader(file)<br/>    for row in reader:<br/>        print(row)</code><p>This code opens the <code>example.csv</code> file and creates a <code>reader</code> object. The <code>reader</code> object is then used to iterate over each row in the file, and the values are printed to the console.</p><h3>Writing CSV Files</h3><p>To write a CSV file, you can use the <code>writer</code> class from the <strong>csv</strong> module. Here is an example of how to write a CSV file:</p><code>import csv<br/>with open(\'example.csv\', \'w\', newline=\'\') as file:<br/>    writer = csv.writer(file)<br/>    writer.writerow([\'Name\', \'Age\', \'City\'])<br/>    writer.writerow([\'John Doe\', 30, \'New York\'])<br/>    writer.writerow([\'Jane Doe\', 25, \'Los Angeles\'])</code><p>This code opens the <code>example.csv</code> file and creates a <code>writer</code> object. The <code>writer</code> object is then used to write three rows to the file.</p><h3>Introduction to JSON Files</h3><p>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) files are lightweight data interchange format that is easy to read and write. JSON files are widely used for data exchange between web servers and web applications.</p><p>The <strong>json</strong> module in Python provides classes for reading and writing JSON data. The module provides two main functions: <code>load</code> and <code>dump</code>. The <code>load</code> function is used to parse a JSON string into a Python object, while the <code>dump</code> function is used to serialize a Python object into a JSON string.</p><h3>Reading JSON Files</h3><p>To read a JSON file, you can use the <code>load</code> function from the <strong>json</strong> module. Here is an example of how to read a JSON file:</p><code>import json<br/>with open(\'example.json\', \'r\') as file:<br/>    data = json.load(file)<br/>    print(data)</code><p>This code opens the <code>example.json</code> file and parses the JSON data into a Python object. The object is then printed to the console.</p><h3>Writing JSON Files</h3><p>To write a JSON file, you can use the <code>dump</code> function from the <strong>json</strong> module. Here is an example of how to write a JSON file:</p><code>import json<br/>data = {\'name\': \'John Doe\', \'age\': 30, \'city\': \'New York\'}<br/>with open(\'example.json\', \'w\') as file:<br/>    json.dump(data, file)</code><p>This code creates a Python object and serializes it into a JSON string. The JSON string is then written to the <code>example.json</code> file.</p><h3>Data Serialization</h3><p>Data serialization is the process of converting data into a format that can be written to a file or transmitted over a network. In Python, data serialization can be achieved using the <strong>json</strong> module or other libraries such as <strong>pickle</strong>.</p><p>Data serialization is useful when you need to store or transmit complex data structures, such as objects or arrays. By serializing the data, you can easily store or transmit it, and then deserialize it back into its original form when needed.</p><ul><li>The <strong>json</strong> module provides a simple way to serialize and deserialize data in JSON format.</li><li>The <strong>pickle</strong> module provides a way to serialize and deserialize Python objects.</li><li>Data serialization is useful for storing or transmitting complex data structures.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, working with CSV and JSON files is an essential skill for any Python developer. By using the <strong>csv</strong> and <strong>json</strong> modules, you can easily read and write CSV and JSON files, and serialize and deserialize data in JSON format.</p>', 'Working with CSV and JSON Files in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(283, 296, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python exception handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+exception+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+exception+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Exception Handling in Python</h3><p>Exception handling is a crucial aspect of programming in Python, allowing developers to handle and manage errors that may occur during the execution of their code. In this lesson, we will delve into the world of exception handling, exploring try-except blocks, error types, and best practices for handling exceptions.</p><p>So, what is an exception? An exception is an event that occurs during the execution of a program, such as a division by zero or an out-of-range value, that disrupts the normal flow of the program. If not handled properly, exceptions can cause a program to crash or produce unexpected results.</p><h3>Try-Except Blocks</h3><p>A try-except block is a fundamental construct in Python that allows developers to catch and handle exceptions. The basic syntax of a try-except block is as follows:</p><code>try:\n    # code that may raise an exception\nexcept ExceptionType:\n    # code to handle the exception\n</code><p>The <code>try</code> block contains the code that may raise an exception, while the <code>except</code> block contains the code that will be executed if an exception is raised. The <code>ExceptionType</code> specifies the type of exception that the except block can handle.</p><p>Here\'s an example of a try-except block:</p><code>try:\n    x = 5 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(\"Cannot divide by zero!\")\n</code><p>In this example, the <code>try</code> block attempts to divide 5 by 0, which raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. The <code>except</code> block catches this exception and prints an error message.</p><h3>Error Types</h3><p>Python has a wide range of built-in exception types that can be used to handle different types of errors. Some common error types include:</p><ul><li><code>ZeroDivisionError</code>: raised when a program attempts to divide by zero</li><li><code>TypeError</code>: raised when a program attempts to perform an operation on a value of the wrong type</li><li><code>ValueError</code>: raised when a program attempts to use a value that is outside the valid range</li><li><code>IndexError</code>: raised when a program attempts to access an index that is out of range</li><li><code>KeyError</code>: raised when a program attempts to access a key that does not exist in a dictionary</li></ul><p>Developers can also create their own custom exception types by deriving from the <code>Exception</code> class.</p><h3>Exception Handling Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when handling exceptions:</p><ul><li><strong>Be specific</strong>: catch specific exceptions instead of general exceptions to avoid masking other errors</li><li><strong>Keep it simple</strong>: keep the code in the try block as simple as possible to avoid catching unnecessary exceptions</li><li><strong>Log errors</strong>: log errors to track and debug issues</li><li><strong>Avoid bare except clauses</strong>: avoid using bare except clauses, as they can catch exceptions that are not intended to be caught</li><li><strong>Document exceptions</strong>: document the exceptions that a function or method may raise to inform other developers</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, developers can write robust and reliable code that handles exceptions effectively.</p>', 'Understanding Exception Handling in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(284, 297, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python regex tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+regex+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+regex+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+regex+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+regex+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+regex+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Regular Expressions</h3><p>Regular expressions, also known as regex, are a powerful tool in Python that allows you to match patterns in strings. They can be used for a variety of tasks, such as data validation, text processing, and string manipulation.</p><p>In this lesson, we will cover the basics of regex patterns, matching strings, and regex functions in Python.</p><h3>Regex Patterns</h3><p>A regex pattern is a string of characters that defines a search pattern. It can include special characters, character classes, and modifiers. Here are some common regex patterns:</p><ul><li><code>^</code> matches the start of a string</li><li><code>$</code> matches the end of a string</li><li><code>.</code> matches any single character</li><li><code>*</code> matches zero or more of the preceding element</li><li><code>+</code> matches one or more of the preceding element</li><li><code>?</code> matches zero or one of the preceding element</li><li><code>{n}</code> matches exactly n of the preceding element</li><li><code>{n,}</code> matches n or more of the preceding element</li><li><code>{n,m}</code> matches at least n and at most m of the preceding element</li></ul><p>Character classes are used to match a set of characters. Here are some common character classes:</p><ul><li><code>[abc]</code> matches any of the characters a, b, or c</li><li><code>[a-zA-Z]</code> matches any letter (lowercase or uppercase)</li><li><code>[0-9]</code> matches any digit</li><li><code>\\w</code> matches any word character (equivalent to [a-zA-Z0-9_])</li><li><code>\\W</code> matches any non-word character</li><li><code>\\d</code> matches any digit</li><li><code>\\D</code> matches any non-digit</li><li><code>\\s</code> matches any whitespace character</li><li><code>\\S</code> matches any non-whitespace character</li></ul><h3>Matching Strings</h3><p>The <strong>re</strong> module in Python provides support for regex. You can use the <code>search()</code> function to search for a pattern in a string. Here is an example:</p><p><code>import re<br>string = &quot;Hello, my email is example@example.com&quot;<br>pattern = r&quot;\\b[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\\.[A-Z|a-z]{2,}&quot;<br>match = re.search(pattern, string)<br>if match:<br>    print(&quot;Email found:&quot;, match.group())<br>else:<br>    print(&quot;No email found&quot;)</code></p><p>In this example, the <code>search()</code> function returns a match object if the pattern is found in the string. You can use the <code>group()</code> method to get the matched string.</p><h3>Regex Functions</h3><p>The <strong>re</strong> module provides several functions for working with regex. Here are some of the most commonly used functions:</p><ul><li><code>re.search(pattern, string)</code> searches for the pattern in the string and returns a match object</li><li><code>re.match(pattern, string)</code> searches for the pattern at the beginning of the string and returns a match object</li><li><code>re.fullmatch(pattern, string)</code> searches for the pattern in the entire string and returns a match object</li><li><code>re.findall(pattern, string)</code> returns all non-overlapping matches of the pattern in the string as a list of strings</li><li><code>re.sub(pattern, repl, string)</code> replaces the occurrences of the pattern in the string with the replacement string</li></ul><p>Here is an example of using the <code>findall()</code> function:</p><p><code>import re<br>string = &quot;Hello, my email is example@example.com and my phone number is 123-456-7890&quot;<br>pattern = r&quot;\\b[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\\.[A-Z|a-z]{2,}&quot;<br>emails = re.findall(pattern, string)<br>print(&quot;Emails found:&quot;, emails)</code></p><p>In this example, the <code>findall()</code> function returns a list of all emails found in the string.</p>', 'Introduction to Python Regular Expressions', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(285, 298, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python modules and packages</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+modules+and+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+modules+and+packages/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+modules+and+packages\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Python Modules and Packages</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the world of Python modules and packages. Modules are pre-written code that can be imported into our programs to perform specific tasks, while packages are collections of related modules. We will learn how to import modules, create packages, and follow best practices for working with modules.</p><h3>Importing Modules</h3><p>Python has a vast collection of modules that can be imported into our programs. To import a module, we use the <code>import</code> statement followed by the name of the module. For example, to import the <code>math</code> module, we would use the following code:</p><code>import math</code><p>Once a module is imported, we can access its functions and variables using the dot notation. For example, to use the <code>sin</code> function from the <code>math</code> module, we would use the following code:</p><code>import math<br>x = math.sin(30)</code><p>We can also import specific functions or variables from a module using the <code>from</code> keyword. For example:</p><code>from math import sin<br>x = sin(30)</code><h3>Creating Packages</h3><p>A package is a collection of related modules that can be imported and used in our programs. To create a package, we need to create a directory with an <code>__init__.py</code> file inside it. The <code>__init__.py</code> file can be empty, but it is required to indicate that the directory is a package.</p><p>For example, let\'s create a package called <code>my_package</code> with two modules: <code>module1.py</code> and <code>module2.py</code>. The directory structure would look like this:</p><ul><li>my_package<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<li>__init__.py</li><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<li>module1.py</li><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<li>module2.py</li></ul><p>We can then import the modules from the package using the following code:</p><code>from my_package import module1<br>from my_package import module2</code><h3>Module Best Practices</h3><p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when working with modules:</p><ul><li><strong>Keep modules short and focused</strong>: A module should have a single responsibility and should not be too long or complex.</li><li><strong>Use meaningful names</strong>: Module names should be descriptive and easy to understand.</li><li><strong>Use docstrings</strong>: Docstrings are strings that provide documentation for a module, function, or variable. They should be used to explain what a module does and how to use it.</li><li><strong>Test modules thoroughly</strong>: Modules should be tested thoroughly to ensure they work as expected.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, we can create modules that are easy to use, understand, and maintain.</p>', 'Working with Python Modules and Packages', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(286, 299, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python oop concepts</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Understanding Python Object-Oriented Programming</h3><p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that revolves around the concept of objects and classes. In Python, OOP is used to create reusable code and organize complex systems into manageable components. In this lesson, we will explore the fundamental concepts of OOP in Python, including classes, objects, and inheritance.</p><h3>Classes</h3><p>A class is a blueprint or a template that defines the properties and behavior of an object. In Python, a class is defined using the <code>class</code> keyword followed by the name of the class. For example:</p><code>class Car:</code><p>This defines a new class called <code>Car</code>. We can then define attributes and methods within the class.</p><p>Attributes are data members of the class that are used to describe the characteristics of the object. For example:</p><code>class Car:</code><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, year):</code><code>        self.color = color</code><code>        self.model = model</code><code>        self.year = year</code><p>In this example, <code>color</code>, <code>model</code>, and <code>year</code> are attributes of the <code>Car</code> class.</p><h3>Objects</h3><p>An object is an instance of a class. We can create multiple objects from a single class, and each object has its own set of attributes. For example:</p><code>my_car = Car(\'red\', \'Toyota\', 2020)</code><code>your_car = Car(\'blue\', \'Honda\', 2019)</code><p>In this example, <code>my_car</code> and <code>your_car</code> are two separate objects created from the <code>Car</code> class.</p><h3>Methods</h3><p>Methods are functions that belong to a class and are used to perform actions on objects. For example:</p><code>class Car:</code><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, year):</code><code>        self.color = color</code><code>        self.model = model</code><code>        self.year = year</code><code>    def start_engine(self):</code><code>        print(\'The engine is starting...\')</code><p>In this example, <code>start_engine</code> is a method that belongs to the <code>Car</code> class.</p><h3>Inheritance</h3><p>Inheritance is a mechanism in OOP that allows one class to inherit the properties and behavior of another class. The child class inherits all the attributes and methods of the parent class and can also add new attributes and methods or override the ones inherited from the parent class. For example:</p><code>class ElectricCar(Car):</code><code>    def __init__(self, color, model, year, battery_size):</code><code>        super().__init__(color, model, year)</code><code>        self.battery_size = battery_size</code><p>In this example, <code>ElectricCar</code> is a child class that inherits from the <code>Car</code> class. The <code>super()</code> function is used to call the constructor of the parent class.</p><h3>Benefits of OOP</h3><p>OOP provides several benefits, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Code Reusability</strong>: OOP allows us to write reusable code, which reduces the amount of code that needs to be written and maintained.</li><li><strong>Modularity</strong>: OOP allows us to break down complex systems into smaller, manageable components, making it easier to develop and maintain large systems.</li><li><strong>Easier Debugging</strong>: OOP makes it easier to identify and fix errors, as the code is organized into smaller, more manageable components.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, OOP is a powerful programming paradigm that allows us to create reusable code and organize complex systems into manageable components. By understanding classes, objects, inheritance, and other OOP concepts, we can write more efficient, modular, and maintainable code.</p>', 'Understanding Python Object-Oriented Programming', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(287, 300, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python oop concepts</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+oop+concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+oop+concepts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Practicing Python Object-Oriented Programming</h3><p>Welcome to Day 56 of our Python course, where we will dive into the world of object-oriented programming. In this lesson, we will cover class attributes, instance methods, and polymorphism.</p><h3>Class Attributes</h3><p>Class attributes are variables that are shared by all instances of a class. They are defined inside the class definition and are accessed using the class name. Let\'s take a look at an example:</p><code>class Car:\n    wheels = 4\n    def __init__(self, color):\n        self.color = color\n\nmy_car = Car(\'red\')\nprint(Car.wheels)  # Output: 4\nprint(my_car.wheels)  # Output: 4</code><p>In this example, <code>wheels</code> is a class attribute that is shared by all instances of the <code>Car</code> class.</p><h3>Instance Methods</h3><p>Instance methods are functions that belong to an instance of a class. They have access to the instance\'s attributes and can modify them. Let\'s take a look at an example:</p><code>class Car:\n    def __init__(self, color):\n        self.color = color\n    def paint(self, new_color):\n        self.color = new_color\n\nmy_car = Car(\'red\')\nprint(my_car.color)  # Output: red\nmy_car.paint(\'blue\')\nprint(my_car.color)  # Output: blue</code><p>In this example, <code>paint</code> is an instance method that belongs to the <code>my_car</code> instance. It has access to the <code>color</code> attribute and can modify it.</p><h3>Polymorphism</h3><p>Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms. In Python, we can achieve polymorphism using method overriding or method overloading. Let\'s take a look at an example of method overriding:</p><code>class Shape:\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Square(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, side):\n        self.side = side\n    def area(self):\n        return self.side ** 2\n\nclass Circle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, radius):\n        self.radius = radius\n    def area(self):\n        return 3.14 * self.radius ** 2\n\nsquare = Square(4)\nprint(square.area())  # Output: 16\ncircle = Circle(4)\nprint(circle.area())  # Output: 50.24</code><p>In this example, the <code>area</code> method is overridden in the <code>Square</code> and <code>Circle</code> classes to provide a specific implementation for each shape.</p><ul><li>Class attributes are shared by all instances of a class.</li><li>Instance methods belong to an instance of a class and have access to the instance\'s attributes.</li><li>Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms and can be achieved using method overriding or method overloading.</li></ul><p>To practice what you have learned, try creating your own classes with class attributes, instance methods, and polymorphism.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Class attributes are defined inside the class definition and are accessed using the class name.</li><li>Instance methods are functions that belong to an instance of a class and have access to the instance\'s attributes.</li><li>Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms and can be achieved using method overriding or method overloading.</li></ul>', 'Practicing Python Object-Oriented Programming', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:14', '2026-05-25 18:54:14'),
(288, 301, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: pandas data structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=pandas+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/pandas+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/pandas+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/pandas+data+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=pandas+data+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Pandas Data Structures</h3><p>Welcome to Day 57 of the Python course, where we will explore the world of Pandas data structures. Pandas is a powerful library in Python that provides data structures and functions to efficiently handle structured data, including tabular data such as spreadsheets and SQL tables.</p><p>In this lesson, we will cover three main topics: Pandas Series, Pandas DataFrames, and Pandas Indexing. By the end of this lesson, you will have a solid understanding of these data structures and how to work with them.</p><h3>Pandas Series</h3><p>A Pandas Series is a one-dimensional labeled array of values. It is similar to a list, but with additional features such as label-based indexing and support for missing data. A Series can be thought of as a column of a spreadsheet or a column of a SQL table.</p><p>Here is an example of creating a Pandas Series:</p><code>import pandas as pd<br>>series = pd.Series([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], index=[&#39;a&#39;, &#39;b&#39;, &#39;c&#39;, &#39;d&#39;, &#39;e&#39;])<br>>print(series)</code><p>This will output:</p><code>a    1<br>b    2<br>c    3<br>d    4<br>e    5<br>dtype: int64</code><p>As you can see, the Series has a label-based index, which allows us to access the values using the labels.</p><h3>Pandas DataFrames</h3><p>A Pandas DataFrame is a two-dimensional labeled data structure with columns of potentially different types. It is similar to an Excel spreadsheet or a table in a relational database. A DataFrame can be thought of as a collection of Series objects, where each Series represents a column of the DataFrame.</p><p>Here is an example of creating a Pandas DataFrame:</p><code>import pandas as pd<br>>data = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\', \'Linda\'],<br>        &#39;Age&#39;: [28, 24, 35, 32],<br>        &#39;City&#39;: [\'New York\', &#39;Paris&#39;, &#39;Berlin&#39;, &#39;London&#39;]}<br>>df = pd.DataFrame(data)<br>>print(df)</code><p>This will output:</p><code>     Name  Age       City<br>0    John   28    New York<br>1    Anna   24      Paris<br>2   Peter   35     Berlin<br>3   Linda   32     London</code><p>As you can see, the DataFrame has multiple columns, each with a different data type.</p><h3>Pandas Indexing</h3><p>Pandas Indexing is a powerful feature that allows us to access and manipulate the data in a Pandas Series or DataFrame. There are several ways to index a Pandas object, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Label-based indexing</strong>: This involves accessing the data using the labels of the index.</li><li><strong>Positional indexing</strong>: This involves accessing the data using the position of the index.</li><li><strong>Boolean indexing</strong>: This involves accessing the data using a boolean mask.</li></ul><p>Here are some examples of indexing a Pandas Series:</p><code>import pandas as pd<br>>series = pd.Series([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], index=[&#39;a&#39;, &#39;b&#39;, &#39;c&#39;, &#39;d&#39;, &#39;e&#39;])<br>>print(series[&#39;a&#39;])  # label-based indexing<br>>print(series[0])  # positional indexing<br>>print(series[series > 3])  # boolean indexing</code><p>And here are some examples of indexing a Pandas DataFrame:</p><code>import pandas as pd<br>>data = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\', \'Linda\'],<br>        &#39;Age&#39;: [28, 24, 35, 32],<br>        &#39;City&#39;: [\'New York\', &#39;Paris&#39;, &#39;Berlin&#39;, &#39;London&#39;]}<br>>df = pd.DataFrame(data)<br>>print(df.loc[0, &#39;Name&#39;])  # label-based indexing<br>>print(df.iloc[0, 0])  # positional indexing<br>>print(df[df[&#39;Age&#39;] > 30])  # boolean indexing</code><p>In conclusion, Pandas Series, DataFrames, and Indexing are powerful tools for working with structured data in Python. By mastering these data structures and indexing techniques, you will be able to efficiently handle and analyze large datasets.</p>', 'Introduction to Pandas Data Structures', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15');
