(312, 325, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python pandas numpy data analysis</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+pandas+numpy+data+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+pandas+numpy+data+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+pandas+numpy+data+analysis/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+pandas+numpy+data+analysis/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+pandas+numpy+data+analysis\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Day 81: Introduction to Data Analysis with Pandas and NumPy</h1>\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>Data analysis is a crucial step in any data-driven project. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of data manipulation using two powerful libraries in Python: Pandas and NumPy. We will learn how to create, manipulate, and analyze data using these libraries.</p>\n<h3>Topic 1: Pandas DataFrames</h3>\n<p>A Pandas DataFrame is a two-dimensional table of data with rows and columns. It is a powerful data structure that allows us to perform various operations on data, such as filtering, sorting, and grouping.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Creating a DataFrame</strong>: We can create a DataFrame from a dictionary, a list of lists, or a CSV file.</li>\n<li><strong>Indexing and Selecting Data</strong>: We can access specific rows and columns using the loc and iloc methods.</li>\n<li><strong>Merging and Joining DataFrames</strong>: We can merge and join DataFrames using the merge and join methods.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>import pandas as pd\n\n# Create a DataFrame from a dictionary\ndata = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n        \'Age\': [28, 24, 35]}\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\nprint(df)\n\n# Indexing and Selecting Data\nprint(df.loc[0])  # Access the first row\nprint(df.iloc[0])  # Access the first row\nprint(df.loc[0, \'Name\'])  # Access the value in the \'Name\' column of the first row\n\n# Merging and Joining DataFrames\ndf1 = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n                    \'Age\': [28, 24, 35]})\ndf2 = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n                    \'City\': [\'New York\', \'Paris\', \'London\']})\nprint(pd.merge(df1, df2, on=\'Name\'))</code></p>\n<h3>Topic 2: NumPy Arrays</h3>\n<p>A NumPy array is a multi-dimensional array of fixed-size, homogeneous elements. It is a powerful data structure that allows us to perform various operations on data, such as mathematical operations, sorting, and indexing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Creating a NumPy Array</strong>: We can create a NumPy array from a list or a tuple.</li>\n<li><strong>Indexing and Selecting Data</strong>: We can access specific elements using the indexing operator.</li>\n<li><strong>Performing Mathematical Operations</strong>: We can perform various mathematical operations on NumPy arrays, such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>import numpy as np\n\n# Create a NumPy array from a list\narr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\nprint(arr)\n\n# Indexing and Selecting Data\nprint(arr[0])  # Access the first element\nprint(arr[0:3])  # Access the first three elements\n\n# Performing Mathematical Operations\narr1 = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\narr2 = np.array([6, 7, 8, 9, 10])\nprint(arr1 + arr2)  # Add two arrays\nprint(arr1 - arr2)  # Subtract two arrays\nprint(arr1 * arr2)  # Multiply two arrays\nprint(arr1 / arr2)  # Divide two arrays</code></p>\n<h3>Topic 3: Data Manipulation</h3>\n<p>Data manipulation is an essential step in data analysis. We can perform various operations on data, such as filtering, sorting, and grouping, to extract insights and knowledge from the data.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filtering Data</strong>: We can filter data using the query method or the boolean indexing method.</li>\n<li><strong>Sorting Data</strong>: We can sort data using the sort_values method or the nlargest and nsmallest methods.</li>\n<li><strong>Grouping Data</strong>: We can group data using the groupby method.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>import pandas as pd\n\n# Create a DataFrame\ndata = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n        \'Age\': [28, 24, 35],\n        \'Score\': [90, 85, 95]}\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\n\n# Filtering Data\nprint(df.query(\'Age > 30\'))  # Filter data where Age is greater than 30\nprint(df[df[\'Age\'] > 30])  # Filter data where Age is greater than 30 using boolean indexing\n\n# Sorting Data\nprint(df.sort_values(\'Age\'))  # Sort data by Age\nprint(df.nlargest(2, \'Score\'))  # Get the top two rows with the highest Score\nprint(df.nsmallest(2, \'Score\'))  # Get the top two rows with the lowest Score\n\n# Grouping Data\nprint(df.groupby(\'Name\'))  # Group data by Name\nprint(df.groupby(\'Name\')[\'Score\'].mean())  # Group data by Name and calculate the mean of Score</code></p>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<p>Quiz time! Try to solve the following exercises:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create a DataFrame from a dictionary and print the first three rows.</li>\n<li>Create a NumPy array from a list and print the first three elements.</li>\n<li>Filter a DataFrame where Age is greater than 30 and print the result.</li>\n<li>Sort a DataFrame by Age and print the result.</li>\n<li>Group a DataFrame by Name and calculate the mean of Score.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Create a DataFrame from a dictionary\ndata = {\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n        \'Age\': [28, 24, 35]}\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\nprint(df.head(3))\n\n# Create a NumPy array from a list\narr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\nprint(arr[:3])\n\n# Filter a DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n                    \'Age\': [28, 24, 35]})\nprint(df.query(\'Age > 30\'))\n\n# Sort a DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n                    \'Age\': [28, 24, 35]})\nprint(df.sort_values(\'Age\'))\n\n# Group a DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n                    \'Age\': [28, 24, 35],\n                    \'Score\': [90, 85, 95]})\nprint(df.groupby(\'Name\')[\'Score\'].mean())</code></p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>In this lesson, we covered the basics of data manipulation using Pandas and NumPy. We learned how to create, manipulate, and analyze data using these libraries. We also practiced solving exercises to reinforce our understanding of the concepts.</p>\n<h3>Homework</h3>\n<p>Try to solve the following exercises:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create a DataFrame from a CSV file and print the first three rows.</li>\n<li>Create a NumPy array from a list and perform mathematical operations on it.</li>\n<li>Filter a DataFrame where Age is greater than 30 and print the result.</li>\n<li>Sort a DataFrame by Age and print the result.</li>\n<li>Group a DataFrame by Name and calculate the mean of Score.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Create a DataFrame from a CSV file\ndf = pd.read_csv(\'data.csv\')\nprint(df.head(3))\n\n# Create a NumPy array from a list and perform mathematical operations\narr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\nprint(arr + 2)\nprint(arr * 2)\nprint(arr / 2)\n\n# Filter a DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n                    \'Age\': [28, 24, 35]})\nprint(df.query(\'Age > 30\'))\n\n# Sort a DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n                    \'Age\': [28, 24, 35]})\nprint(df.sort_values(\'Age\'))\n\n# Group a DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\'Name\': [\'John\', \'Anna\', \'Peter\'], \n                    \'Age\': [28, 24, 35],\n                    \'Score\': [90, 85, 95]})\nprint(df.groupby(\'Name\')[\'Score\'].mean())</code></p>\n<h3>Resources</h3>\n<p>Check out the following resources for more information:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pandas Documentation: <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/\">https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/</a></li>\n<li>NumPy Documentation: <a href=\"https://numpy.org/doc/\">https://numpy.org/doc/</a></li>\n<li>Data Analysis with Pandas and NumPy: <a href=\"https://www.datacamp.com/tracks/data-science-with-python\">https://www.datacamp.com/tracks/data-science-with-python</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Image Query</h3>\n<p>Search for images related to Pandas and NumPy:</p>\n<p><code>python pandas numpy data analysis</code></p>\n</h1>', 'Introduction to Data Analysis with Pandas and NumPy', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(313, 326, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Matplotlib Seaborn data visualization Python charts plots</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Matplotlib+Seaborn+data+visualization+Python+charts+plots\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Matplotlib+Seaborn+data+visualization+Python+charts+plots\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Matplotlib+Seaborn+data+visualization+Python+charts+plots/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Matplotlib+Seaborn+data+visualization+Python+charts+plots/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Matplotlib+Seaborn+data+visualization+Python+charts+plots\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Data Visualization with Matplotlib and Seaborn</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 82! Today, we\'re diving into the exciting world of data visualization in Python. Visualizing data is a crucial skill for any data professional, as it allows us to understand patterns, trends, and outliers at a glance. We\'ll be using two of Python\'s most popular libraries for this: <strong>Matplotlib</strong> and <strong>Seaborn</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Matplotlib</strong> is the foundational library for creating static, animated, and interactive visualizations in Python. It provides a highly flexible environment for plotting. <strong>Seaborn</strong>, on the other hand, is built on top of Matplotlib and provides a higher-level interface for drawing attractive and informative statistical graphics. It simplifies many common plotting tasks and often produces more aesthetically pleasing plots with less code.</p>\n\n<h3>Plotting Functions: Matplotlib Basics</h3>\n<p>At its core, Matplotlib allows you to create plots using a collection of functions that make Matplotlib work like MATLAB. The most common way to use Matplotlib is through the <code>pyplot</code> module, which is typically imported as <code>plt</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Importing Libraries:</strong> You\'ll almost always start by importing <code>matplotlib.pyplot</code> and often <code>numpy</code> for generating data.</li>\n    <li><strong>Creating a Plot:</strong> The simplest way to create a plot is using <code>plt.plot()</code> for line plots.</li>\n    <li><strong>Adding Labels and Titles:</strong> Functions like <code>plt.xlabel()</code>, <code>plt.ylabel()</code>, and <code>plt.title()</code> help make your plots understandable.</li>\n    <li><strong>Displaying the Plot:</strong> <code>plt.show()</code> displays the plot window.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Example: A Simple Line Plot with Matplotlib</h4>\n<p>Let\'s create a basic line plot showing a sine wave.</p>\n<pre><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Generate some data\nx = np.linspace(0, 10, 100) # 100 points between 0 and 10\ny = np.sin(x)\n\n# Create the plot\nplt.plot(x, y)\n\n# Add labels and title\nplt.xlabel(\"X-axis\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Y-axis\")\nplt.title(\"Simple Sine Wave\")\n\n# Display the plot\nplt.show()</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Chart Types</h3>\n<p>Matplotlib and Seaborn offer a wide array of chart types to visualize different kinds of data relationships. Here are some of the most common ones:</p>\n\n<h4>1. Line Plots (Matplotlib: <code>plt.plot()</code>, Seaborn: <code>sns.lineplot()</code>)</h4>\n<p>Line plots are ideal for showing trends over time or ordered categories. They connect data points with lines.</p>\n<pre><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\nx = np.linspace(0, 10, 100)\ny1 = np.sin(x)\ny2 = np.cos(x)\n\nplt.plot(x, y1, label=\'Sine Wave\', color=\'blue\')\nplt.plot(x, y2, label=\'Cosine Wave\', color=\'red\', linestyle=\'--\')\nplt.xlabel(\"Time\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Amplitude\")\nplt.title(\"Sine and Cosine Waves\")\nplt.legend() # Displays the labels\nplt.show()</code></pre>\n\n<h4>2. Scatter Plots (Matplotlib: <code>plt.scatter()</code>, Seaborn: <code>sns.scatterplot()</code>)</h4>\n<p>Scatter plots are used to observe relationships between two continuous variables. Each point on the plot represents an observation.</p>\n<pre><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Generate random data\nnp.random.seed(42)\nx = np.random.rand(50)\ny = 2 * x + 1 + np.random.randn(50) * 0.2 # Linear relationship with noise\n\nplt.scatter(x, y)\nplt.xlabel(\"Feature X\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Target Y\")\nplt.title(\"Scatter Plot of X vs Y\")\nplt.show()</code></pre>\n\n<h4>3. Bar Charts (Matplotlib: <code>plt.bar()</code>, Seaborn: <code>sns.barplot()</code>)</h4>\n<p>Bar charts are excellent for comparing quantities across different categories. The height of each bar represents the value for that category.</p>\n<pre><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ncategories = [\'A\', \'B\', \'C\', \'D\']\nvalues = [23, 45, 56, 12]\n\nplt.bar(categories, values, color=[\'skyblue\', \'lightcoral\', \'lightgreen\', \'gold\'])\nplt.xlabel(\"Category\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Value\")\nplt.title(\"Bar Chart of Categories\")\nplt.show()</code></pre>\n\n<h4>4. Histograms (Matplotlib: <code>plt.hist()</code>, Seaborn: <code>sns.histplot()</code>)</h4>\n<p>Histograms display the distribution of a single numerical variable. They divide the data into \"bins\" and count how many data points fall into each bin.</p>\n<pre><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Generate random data following a normal distribution\ndata = np.random.randn(1000)\n\nplt.hist(data, bins=30, color=\'purple\', alpha=0.7)\nplt.xlabel(\"Value\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Frequency\")\nplt.title(\"Histogram of Random Data\")\nplt.show()</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Introducing Seaborn for Enhanced Aesthetics and Simplicity</h4>\n<p>Seaborn simplifies the creation of many statistical plots and often produces more visually appealing results with less code, especially when working with Pandas DataFrames. Let\'s see a quick example using Seaborn for a scatter plot.</p>\n<pre><code>import seaborn as sns\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Create a DataFrame for Seaborn\ndata = pd.DataFrame({\n    \'X\': np.random.rand(50),\n    \'Y\': 2 * np.random.rand(50) + 1 + np.random.randn(50) * 0.2,\n    \'Category\': np.random.choice([\'Group 1\', \'Group 2\'], 50)\n})\n\n# Seaborn scatter plot, automatically handling colors by category\nsns.scatterplot(x=\'X\', y=\'Y\', hue=\'Category\', data=data)\nplt.title(\"Seaborn Scatter Plot with Categories\")\nplt.show()</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Customization Options</h3>\n<p>Both Matplotlib and Seaborn offer extensive options to customize your plots to make them more informative and visually appealing.</p>\n\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Labels and Titles:</strong> As seen, <code>plt.xlabel()</code>, <code>plt.ylabel()</code>, <code>plt.title()</code> are fundamental.</li>\n    <li><strong>Legends:</strong> Use <code>plt.legend()</code> to display labels for different data series. Remember to add a <code>label</code> argument to your plotting functions (e.g., <code>plt.plot(x, y, label=\'My Data\')</code>).</li>\n    <li><strong>Colors, Markers, and Line Styles:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><code>color=\'red\'</code> or <code>color=\'#FF5733\'</code> (hex code)</li>\n            <li><code>marker=\'o\'</code> (circle), <code>\'x\'</code>, <code>\'^\'</code> (triangle), etc.</li>\n            <li><code>linestyle=\'--\'</code> (dashed), <code>\'-\'</code> (solid), <code>\':\'</code> (dotted), <code>\'-.\'</code> (dash-dot)</li>\n            <li><code>linewidth</code> for line thickness, <code>markersize</code> for marker size.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n    <li><strong>Figure Size:</strong> Control the overall size of your plot using <code>plt.figure(figsize=(width, height))</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Axis Limits:</strong> Set the range of your axes with <code>plt.xlim(min, max)</code> and <code>plt.ylim(min, max)</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Grid:</strong> Add a grid to your plot with <code>plt.grid(True)</code> for easier reading of values.</li>\n    <li><strong>Saving Plots:</strong> Save your plots to a file (e.g., PNG, JPG, PDF) using <code>plt.savefig(\'my_plot.png\')</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Seaborn Styles and Palettes:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Seaborn comes with built-in themes: <code>sns.set_style(\'darkgrid\')</code>, <code>\'whitegrid\'</code>, <code>\'dark\'</code>, <code>\'white\'</code>, <code>\'ticks\'</code>.</li>\n            <li>You can also change the color palette for your plots: <code>sns.set_palette(\'viridis\')</code>, <code>\'plasma\'</code>, <code>\'RdBu\'</code>, etc.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Example: Customizing a Plot</h4>\n<pre><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\nimport seaborn as sns\n\n# Apply a Seaborn style for better aesthetics by default\nsns.set_style(\'whitegrid\')\nsns.set_palette(\'coolwarm\')\n\nx = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100)\ny = np.sin(x)\nz = np.cos(x)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 6)) # Set figure size\n\nplt.plot(x, y, label=\'Sine\', color=\'green\', linestyle=\'-\', linewidth=2, marker=\'o\', markersize=4)\nplt.plot(x, z, label=\'Cosine\', color=\'red\', linestyle=\'--\', linewidth=2, marker=\'x\', markersize=4)\n\nplt.xlabel(\"Angle (radians)\", fontsize=12)\nplt.ylabel(\"Value\", fontsize=12)\nplt.title(\"Customized Sine and Cosine Waves\", fontsize=14, fontweight=\'bold\')\n\nplt.xlim(0, 2 * np.pi) # Set x-axis limits\nplt.ylim(-1.2, 1.2) # Set y-axis limits\n\nplt.legend(loc=\'upper right\', fontsize=10)\nplt.grid(True, linestyle=\':\', alpha=0.7)\n\nplt.tight_layout() # Adjust plot to prevent labels from overlapping\nplt.savefig(\'custom_waves.png\') # Save the plot\nplt.show()</code></pre>\n\n<p>This concludes our introduction to data visualization with Matplotlib and Seaborn. Practice these concepts, and you\'ll be able to create powerful and insightful visualizations for your data!</p>', 'Data Visualization with Matplotlib and Seaborn', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(314, 327, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python database tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+database+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+database+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+database+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+database+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+database+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Day 83: Working with Databases in Python</h1>\n<p>Welcome to Day 83 of our Python course! Today, we\'re going to explore working with databases in Python. We\'ll cover the basics of SQL, connect to a database, and learn how to perform CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations.</p>\n<h2>SQL Basics</h2>\n<p>SQL, or Structured Query Language, is a programming language designed for managing and manipulating data in relational database management systems. It\'s a fundamental skill for any data analyst or developer.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SELECT Statement:</strong> The SELECT statement is used to retrieve data from a database. It\'s the most common SQL statement and is used to fetch data from one or more tables.</li>\n<li><strong>INSERT Statement:</strong> The INSERT statement is used to add new data to a database table.</li>\n<li><strong>UPDATE Statement:</strong> The UPDATE statement is used to modify existing data in a database table.</li>\n<li><strong>DELETE Statement:</strong> The DELETE statement is used to remove data from a database table.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let\'s take a look at some basic SQL syntax:</p>\n<code>\nSELECT * FROM customers WHERE country=\'USA\';\nINSERT INTO customers (name, email, country) VALUES (\'John Doe\', \'john@example.com\', \'USA\');\nUPDATE customers SET country=\'Canada\' WHERE name=\'John Doe\';\nDELETE FROM customers WHERE name=\'John Doe\';\n</code>\n<h2>Database Connection</h2>\n<p>Before we can perform any database operations, we need to connect to the database. In Python, we can use the sqlite3 module to connect to a SQLite database.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how to connect to a SQLite database:</p>\n<code>\nimport sqlite3\n\n# Connect to the database\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\'example.db\')\nc = conn.cursor()\n</code>\n<p>Once we have a connection object, we can execute SQL statements using the cursor object.</p>\n<h2>CRUD Operations</h2>\n<p>Now that we have a connection to the database, let\'s perform some CRUD operations.</p>\n<p><strong>Create:</strong> Let\'s create a new customer in the database.</p>\n<code>\n# Create a new customer\nc.execute(\"INSERT INTO customers (name, email, country) VALUES (\'Jane Doe\', \'jane@example.com\', \'USA\')\")\nconn.commit()\n</code>\n<p><strong>Read:</strong> Let\'s retrieve all customers from the database.</p>\n<code>\n# Retrieve all customers\nc.execute(\"SELECT * FROM customers\")\nrows = c.fetchall()\nfor row in rows:\n    print(row)\n</code>\n<p><strong>Update:</strong> Let\'s update the country of a customer.</p>\n<code>\n# Update the country of a customer\nc.execute(\"UPDATE customers SET country=\'Canada\' WHERE name=\'Jane Doe\'\")\nconn.commit()\n</code>\n<p><strong>Delete:</strong> Let\'s delete a customer from the database.</p>\n<code>\n# Delete a customer\nc.execute(\"DELETE FROM customers WHERE name=\'Jane Doe\'\")\nconn.commit()\n</code>\n<h2>Quiz</h2>\n<p>Try answering these questions to test your understanding of working with databases in Python:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the purpose of the SELECT statement in SQL?</li>\n<li>How do you connect to a SQLite database using Python?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between the INSERT and UPDATE statements in SQL?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember to check your answers and learn from your mistakes!</p>\n<h2>Image Search Query</h2>\n<p>Search for \'Python database tutorial\' to find relevant images and resources.</p>\n<h3>What\'s Next?</h3>\n<p>In the next lesson, we\'ll explore more advanced database concepts and learn how to use a relational database management system like MySQL.</p>', 'Working with Databases in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(315, 328, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python final project planning</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+final+project+planning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+final+project+planning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+final+project+planning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+final+project+planning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+final+project+planning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Final Project Preparation and Review</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 84! Today we’ll prepare for your final Python project by exploring ideas, reviewing code, and reinforcing best practices. Let’s dive in!</p>\n\n<h3>Project Ideas</h3>\n<p>Choose one of these beginner-friendly projects to apply your skills:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>To-Do List App:</strong> Build a command-line app to add/remove tasks. Use lists and file I/O to save data.</li>\n  <li><strong>Weather App:</strong> Fetch weather data using an API (e.g., OpenWeather). Practice JSON parsing and API requests.</li>\n  <li><strong>Simple Game:</strong> Create Tic-Tac-Toe with basic AI. Focus on loops, conditionals, and functions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Start with pseudocode to outline logic before writing code.</p>\n\n<h3>Code Review</h3>\n<p>Code reviews help improve quality and catch bugs. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Check for <strong>PEP8 compliance</strong> (indentation, spacing, naming).</li>\n  <li>Test for <strong>logic errors</strong> (e.g., off-by-one loops, incorrect conditionals).</li>\n  <li>Look for <strong>redundant code</strong> (e.g., repeated calculations).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> Review this snippet:</p>\n<code>\ndef calculate_sum(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nresult = calculate_sum(5, \'10\')\n</code>\n<p><strong>Issue:</strong> Mixing integer and string inputs causes a type error. Add type checks or error handling.</p>\n\n<h3>Best Practices</h3>\n<p>Follow these guidelines for clean, maintainable code:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Meaningful names:</strong> Use <code>user_age</code> instead of <code>u_a</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Comments:</strong> Explain <em>why</em> code exists, not <em>what</em> it does.</li>\n  <li><strong>Functions:</strong> Keep them short (1-2 tasks) and reusable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Error handling:</strong> Use <code>try-except</code> blocks for robustness.</li>\n  <li><strong>Version control:</strong> Commit changes frequently with clear messages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Use <code>__doc__</code> strings to document functions:</p>\n<code>\ndef greet(name):\n    \"\"\"Print a greeting message.\"\"\"\n    print(f\'Hello, {name}!\')\n</code>\n\n<h3>Next Steps</h3>\n<p>1. Finalize your project idea and create a GitHub repository.<br>2. Write pseudocode and share it for feedback.<br>3. Start coding with clear, modular functions.</p>', 'Final Project Preparation and Review', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(316, 329, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: flask web development python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=flask+web+development+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/flask+web+development+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/flask+web+development+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/flask+web+development+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=flask+web+development+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Introduction to Web Development with Flask</h1>\n<h2>What is Flask?</h2>\n<p>Flask is a micro web framework written in Python. It is classified as a microframework because it does not require particular tools or libraries. It has no database abstraction layer, form validation, or any other components where pre-existing third-party libraries provide common functions.</p>\n<h3>Key Features of Flask</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Lightweight: Flask is a small and lightweight framework that makes it easy to learn and use.</li>\n<li>Flexible: Flask allows you to build web applications quickly and efficiently.</li>\n<li>Extensive Library Support: Flask supports a wide range of libraries and tools that can be used to extend its functionality.</li>\n<li>Unit Testing: Flask provides a built-in unit testing framework that makes it easy to write and run tests for your application.</li>\n<li>Debugging: Flask provides a built-in debugger that makes it easy to debug your application.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Why Use Flask?</h2>\n<p>Flask is a popular choice for building web applications because it is easy to learn and use, flexible, and has a wide range of libraries and tools available. It is also a good choice for building small to medium-sized web applications.</p>\n<h3>Basic Structure of a Flask Application</h3>\n<p>A Flask application typically consists of the following components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>App: The app is the core of the Flask application. It is responsible for handling requests and sending responses.</li>\n<li>Routes: Routes are used to map URLs to specific functions in the application. For example, you might have a route for the home page and another route for a login page.</li>\n<li>Templates: Templates are used to render HTML pages. Flask provides a built-in templating engine that makes it easy to render templates.</li>\n<li>Models: Models are used to interact with the database. Flask provides a built-in ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) system that makes it easy to interact with the database.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Example of a Simple Flask Application</h3>\n<p>Here is an example of a simple Flask application that displays a \"Hello, World!\" message:</p>\n<pre><code>\nfrom flask import Flask\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(\"/\")\ndef hello_world():\n    return \"Hello, World!\"\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run()\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Getting Started with Flask</h2>\n<p>To get started with Flask, you will need to have Python installed on your computer. You can download the latest version of Python from the official Python website.</p>\n<p>Once you have Python installed, you can install Flask using pip:</p>\n<pre><code>\npip install flask\n</code></pre>\n<p>After you have installed Flask, you can create a new Flask application by creating a new Python file and importing the Flask module:</p>\n<pre><code>\nfrom flask import Flask\napp = Flask(__name__)\n</code></pre>\n<p>From there, you can start building your application by defining routes and templates.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>In this lesson, we introduced the basics of Flask and how to get started with building a web application using Flask. We covered the key features of Flask, the basic structure of a Flask application, and how to create a simple Flask application. We also covered how to get started with Flask and how to install the necessary libraries and tools.</p>\n<h2>Quiz</h2>\n<p>Here are a few questions to test your understanding of the material:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What is Flask?</li>\n<li>What are the key features of Flask?</li>\n<li>What is the basic structure of a Flask application?</li>\n<li>How do you create a simple Flask application?</li>\n<li>How do you get started with Flask?</li>\n</ul>\n</h1>', 'Introduction to Web Development with Flask', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(317, 330, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: database-python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=database-python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/database-python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/database-python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/database-python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=database-python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>Working with Databases in Python</h1>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Welcome to Day 86 of our Python course! Today, we\'re going to explore the world of databases in Python. Databases are an essential part of any application, allowing us to store, manage, and retrieve data efficiently. In this lesson, we\'ll cover the basics of databases, SQL, and how to interact with them using Python.</p>\n<h2>What are Databases?</h2>\n<p>A database is a collection of organized data that can be easily accessed, managed, and updated. It\'s like a digital filing cabinet where you can store and retrieve information as needed. Databases come in various types, including relational databases (e.g., MySQL, PostgreSQL), NoSQL databases (e.g., MongoDB, Cassandra), and cloud-based databases (e.g., Amazon DynamoDB, Google Cloud Firestore).</p>\n<h2>SQL Basics</h2>\n<p>SQL (Structured Query Language) is a programming language designed for managing and manipulating data in relational databases. It\'s used to perform various operations, such as creating, reading, updating, and deleting data. Here are some basic SQL concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Create</strong>: Creates a new database or table.</li>\n<li><strong>Select</strong>: Retrieves data from a database or table.</li>\n<li><strong>Insert</strong>: Adds new data to a database or table.</li>\n<li><strong>Update</strong>: Modifies existing data in a database or table.</li>\n<li><strong>Delete</strong>: Deletes data from a database or table.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>SQL Syntax</h2>\n<p>SQL syntax consists of commands, clauses, and functions. Here\'s a simple example of a SQL query:</p>\n<code>\nSELECT * FROM customers\nWHERE country=\'USA\'\n</code>\n<p>This SQL query selects all data from the \'customers\' table where the \'country\' column is \'USA\'.</p>\n<h2>Python and Databases</h2>\n<p>Python provides several libraries and frameworks for interacting with databases, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>sqlite3</strong>: A built-in library for working with SQLite databases.</li>\n<li><strong>psycopg2</strong>: A library for working with PostgreSQL databases.</li>\n<li><strong>mysql-connector-python</strong>: A library for working with MySQL databases.</li>\n<li><strong>SQLAlchemy</strong>: An ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) library for working with various databases.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Example: Creating a SQLite Database with Python</h2>\n<p>We\'ll use the sqlite3 library to create a SQLite database and perform basic CRUD operations.</p>\n<code>\nimport sqlite3\n\n# Connect to the database\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\'example.db\')\n\n# Create a cursor object\ncur = conn.cursor()\n\n# Create a table\ncur.execute(\'\'\'\n    CREATE TABLE customers (\n        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n        name TEXT NOT NULL,\n        email TEXT NOT NULL\n    )\n\'\'\')\n\n# Insert data\ncur.execute(\"INSERT INTO customers (name, email) VALUES (\'John Doe\', \'john@example.com\')\")\n\n# Commit the changes\nconn.commit()\n\n# Close the connection\nconn.close()\n</code>\n<h2>Quiz Time!</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the purpose of a database?</li>\n<li>What is SQL, and what is it used for?</li>\n<li>What are some common types of databases?</li>\n<li>How do you create a new table in a database using SQL?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between a library and a framework in Python?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Take your time to answer these questions, and don\'t hesitate to ask if you have any doubts!</p>\n<h2>Image Search Query</h2>\n<p>database-python</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>That\'s it for today\'s lesson! You now have a solid understanding of databases, SQL, and how to interact with them using Python. Remember to practice and experiment with different libraries and frameworks to become proficient in working with databases in Python.</p>\n<h2>Homework</h2>\n<p>Complete the quiz above and try to create a simple database using SQLite or another library/framework of your choice.</p>\n</h1>', 'Working with Databases in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(318, 331, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Pandas and Matplotlib data visualization examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Pandas+and+Matplotlib+data+visualization+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Pandas+and+Matplotlib+data+visualization+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Pandas+and+Matplotlib+data+visualization+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Pandas+and+Matplotlib+data+visualization+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Pandas+and+Matplotlib+data+visualization+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 87: Data Analysis and Visualization with Pandas and Matplotlib</h3><p>Welcome to Day 87! Today, we explore two essential Python libraries for data analysis and visualization: <strong>Pandas</strong> and <strong>Matplotlib</strong>. These tools empower you to clean, analyze, and visualize data efficiently.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Concepts in Pandas</h3><p><strong>Pandas</strong> is a powerful library for data manipulation and analysis. Its core data structures are <code>DataFrame</code> and <code>Series</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>DataFrame</strong>: A 2D table-like structure with rows and columns.</li>\n  <li><strong>Series</strong>: A 1D array with labeled indexes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Basic Operations</h4>\n<p>Here’s how to load data and perform common tasks:</p>\n<code>\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load data\ndf = pd.read_csv(\'data.csv\')\n\n# Display first 5 rows\ndf.head()\n\n# Get summary statistics\ndf.describe()\n\n# Check for missing values\ndf.isnull().sum()\n\n# Drop rows with missing values\ndf.dropna()\n\n# Fill missing values\ndf.fillna(0)\n\n# Group data and calculate mean\ndf.groupby(\'Category\')[\'Sales\'].mean()\n</code>\n\n<h3>Introduction to Matplotlib</h3><p><strong>Matplotlib</strong> is a plotting library for creating static, animated, and interactive visualizations in Python. It integrates seamlessly with Pandas.</p>\n\n<h4>Basic Plotting</h4>\n<p>Here’s how to create common plots:</p>\n<code>\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# Line plot\nplt.plot(x, y)\nplt.title(\'Line Plot\')\nplt.xlabel(\'X-axis\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Y-axis\')\nplt.show()\n\n# Bar plot\nplt.bar(categories, values)\nplt.title(\'Bar Chart\')\nplt.xticks(rotation=45)\nplt.show()\n\n# Scatter plot\nplt.scatter(x, y)\nplt.title(\'Scatter Plot\')\nplt.show()\n</code>\n\n<h3>Combining Pandas and Matplotlib</h3><p>Use Pandas’ built-in plotting methods with Matplotlib for streamlined workflows:</p>\n<code>\n# Line plot using Pandas\ndf.plot(x=\'Date\', y=\'Sales\', kind=\'line\')\n\n# Histogram\ndf[\'Age\'].plot(kind=\'hist\', bins=10)\n\n# Subplots for multiple visualizations\nfig, axs = plt.subplots(2, 2)\ndf.plot(kind=\'bar\', ax=axs[0,0])\ndf.plot(kind=\'line\', ax=axs[0,1])\ndf.plot(kind=\'scatter\', x=\'X\', y=\'Y\', ax=axs[1,0])\ndf.plot(kind=\'hist\', ax=axs[1,1])\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n</code>\n\n<h3>Real-World Data Analysis Example</h3><p>Let’s analyze sales data:</p>\n<code>\n# Load dataset\ndf = pd.read_csv(\'sales.csv\')\n\n# Clean data\ndf = df.dropna()\n\n# Calculate total sales per region\ntotal_sales = df.groupby(\'Region\')[\'Sales\'].sum()\n\n# Plot results\ntotal_sales.plot(kind=\'bar\', color=\'skyblue\')\nplt.title(\'Total Sales by Region\')\nplt.xlabel(\'Region\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Total Sales ($M)\')\nplt.show()\n</code>\n\n<h3>Best Practices</h3><ul>\n  <li>Always clean and preprocess data before analysis.</li>\n  <li>Use descriptive labels and titles in plots.</li>\n  <li>Choose appropriate visualization types for your data (e.g., bar charts for categories, line plots for trends).</li>\n</ul>', 'Data Analysis and Visualization with Pandas and Matplotlib', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15');
