(319, 332, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: machine learning scikit-learn python</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=machine+learning+scikit-learn+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/machine+learning+scikit-learn+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/machine+learning+scikit-learn+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/machine+learning+scikit-learn+python/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=machine+learning+scikit-learn+python\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Introduction to Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn</h2>\n<p>Welcome to Day 88 of our Python course. Today, we will be exploring the exciting world of Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, a popular Python library used for machine learning.</p>\n<h3>What is Machine Learning?</h3>\n<p>Machine Learning is a subset of Artificial Intelligence that enables computers to learn from data without being explicitly programmed. It involves training algorithms on a dataset to make predictions or decisions based on new, unseen data.</p>\n<h3>What is Scikit-Learn?</h3>\n<p>Scikit-Learn is an open-source machine learning library for Python that provides a wide range of algorithms for classification, regression, clustering, and more. It is widely used in industry and academia due to its simplicity, flexibility, and high-performance capabilities.</p>\n<h3>Key Features of Scikit-Learn</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Easy to Use:</strong> Scikit-Learn has a simple and intuitive API that makes it easy to use, even for those new to machine learning.</li>\n<li><strong>Wide Range of Algorithms:</strong> Scikit-Learn provides a wide range of algorithms for different machine learning tasks, including classification, regression, clustering, and more.</li>\n<li><strong>High-Performance:</strong> Scikit-Learn is designed to be highly performant, making it suitable for large-scale machine learning tasks.</li>\n<li><strong>Extensive Documentation:</strong> Scikit-Learn has extensive documentation and a large community of users and contributors, making it easy to find help and resources when needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Installing Scikit-Learn</h3>\n<p>To install Scikit-Learn, you can use pip, the Python package manager. Simply run the following command in your terminal or command prompt:</p>\n<code>pip install scikit-learn</code>\n<h3>Basic Scikit-Learn Workflow</h3>\n<p>The basic Scikit-Learn workflow involves the following steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data Preparation:</strong> Prepare your dataset by loading it into a suitable format and performing any necessary data cleaning or preprocessing.</li>\n<li><strong>Splitting Data:</strong> Split your dataset into training and testing sets to evaluate the performance of your model.</li>\n<li><strong>Model Selection:</strong> Choose a suitable machine learning algorithm from Scikit-Learn\'s library of algorithms.</li>\n<li><strong>Model Training:</strong> Train your model on the training data.</li>\n<li><strong>Model Evaluation:</strong> Evaluate the performance of your model on the testing data.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Example: Classification with Scikit-Learn</h3>\n<p>Let\'s consider a simple example of classification with Scikit-Learn. We\'ll use the Iris dataset, a classic dataset for machine learning, and train a classifier to predict the species of an Iris flower based on its features.</p>\n<code>\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\n\n# Load the Iris dataset\niris = load_iris()\n\n# Split the data into training and testing sets\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(iris.data, iris.target, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)\n\n# Train a logistic regression classifier on the training data\nclf = LogisticRegression()\nclf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\n# Make predictions on the testing data\ny_pred = clf.predict(X_test)\n\n# Evaluate the performance of the classifier\naccuracy = accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred)\nprint(f\'Accuracy: {accuracy:.2f}\')\n</code>\n<h3>Quiz Time!</h3>\n<p>Here\'s a short quiz to test your understanding of Scikit-Learn and Machine Learning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is Machine Learning?</li>\n<li>What is Scikit-Learn?</li>\n<li>What are the key features of Scikit-Learn?</li>\n<li>How do you install Scikit-Learn?</li>\n<li>What are the basic steps in the Scikit-Learn workflow?</li>\n<li>Can you write a simple example of classification with Scikit-Learn?</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Quiz question 1: <input type=\'text\' id=\'q1\' name=\'q1\'></li>\n<li>Quiz question 2: <input type=\'text\' id=\'q2\' name=\'q2\'></li>\n<li>Quiz question 3: <input type=\'text\' id=\'q3\' name=\'q3\'></li>\n<li>Quiz question 4: <input type=\'text\' id=\'q4\' name=\'q4\'></li>\n<li>Quiz question 5: <input type=\'text\' id=\'q5\' name=\'q5\'></li>\n<li>Quiz question 6: <input type=\'text\' id=\'q6\' name=\'q6\'></li>\n</ul>', 'Introduction to Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(320, 333, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python api requests</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+api+requests\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+api+requests\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+api+requests/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+api+requests/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+api+requests\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Working with APIs in Python</h3>\n<p>Today we will be learning about working with APIs in Python. APIs, or Application Programming Interfaces, allow us to interact with external services and retrieve or send data to them. We will be using the <strong>Requests</strong> library in Python to make HTTP requests to APIs.</p>\n<h4>What is an API?</h4>\n<p>An API is a set of defined rules that enables different applications to communicate with each other. It allows us to send and receive data in a structured format, such as JSON or XML. APIs can be used to access data from external services, such as weather forecasts, news articles, or social media posts.</p>\n<h4>Why Use APIs in Python?</h4>\n<p>There are many reasons why we would want to use APIs in Python. Here are a few examples:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>To access data from external services, such as weather forecasts or news articles.</li>\n    <li>To send data to external services, such as posting a message on social media.</li>\n    <li>To automate tasks, such as sending emails or updating a database.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What is the Requests Library?</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Requests</strong> library is a popular Python library used for making HTTP requests to APIs. It provides a simple and intuitive way to send HTTP requests and handle responses. With the <strong>Requests</strong> library, we can easily send GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE requests to APIs.</p>\n<h4>How to Use the Requests Library</h4>\n<p>To use the <strong>Requests</strong> library, we first need to install it using pip:</p>\n<pre><code>pip install requests</code></pre>\n<p>Once installed, we can import the library and use it to make HTTP requests:</p>\n<pre><code>import requests\n\nurl = \'https://api.example.com/data\'\nresponse = requests.get(url)\n\nprint(response.json())</code></pre>\n<h4>API Endpoints and Parameters</h4>\n<p>When making a request to an API, we need to specify the endpoint and any parameters we want to pass. The endpoint is the URL of the API, and the parameters are any data we want to send with the request.</p>\n<pre><code>import requests\n\nurl = \'https://api.example.com/data\'\nparams = {\'param1\': \'value1\', \'param2\': \'value2\'}\n\nresponse = requests.get(url, params=params)\n\nprint(response.json())</code></pre>\n<h4>Error Handling</h4>\n<p>When making requests to APIs, we need to handle any errors that may occur. We can use the <code>try/except</code> block to catch any exceptions that are raised:</p>\n<pre><code>import requests\n\nurl = \'https://api.example.com/data\'\n\ntry:\n    response = requests.get(url)\n    response.raise_for_status()\nexcept requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:\n    print(f\'Error: {e}\')\n\nprint(response.json())</code></pre>\n<h4>Quiz</h4>\n<p>Quiz time! Here are a few questions to test your understanding of working with APIs in Python:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>What is an API, and why do we use them in Python?</li>\n    <li>What is the <strong>Requests</strong> library, and how do we install it?</li>\n    <li>How do we make a GET request to an API using the <strong>Requests</strong> library?</li>\n    <li>What is the purpose of the <code>try/except</code> block when making requests to APIs?</li>\n</ul>\n', 'Working with APIs in Python', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(321, 334, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: python final project internship review</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=python+final+project+internship+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/python+final+project+internship+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/python+final+project+internship+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/python+final+project+internship+review/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=python+final+project+internship+review\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>Final Project and Internship Review</h2><p>Welcome to the final day of our Python course! Today we\'ll be discussing your final project and reviewing the internship opportunities available to you.</p><h3>Final Project</h3><p>The final project is an essential part of this course, and it\'s your chance to showcase your skills and creativity. You\'ll be working on a real-world project that you\'ll present to the class and instructor.</p><p>Here are some tips to help you get started:</p><ul><li><strong>Choose a project that interests you</strong>. This will make it more enjoyable and motivating.</li><li><strong>Keep it simple</strong>. Don\'t try to tackle too much at once. Focus on a specific aspect of the project.</li><li><strong>Use Python libraries and frameworks</strong>. This will help you complete the project faster and make it more efficient.</li><li><strong>Document your code</strong>. This will make it easier for others to understand your code and for you to debug it.</li></ul><p>Some ideas for final projects include:</p><ul><li><code>Building a chatbot</code> using Python and natural language processing.</li><li><code>Creating a game</code> using a Python library like Pygame.</li><li><code>Developing a web scraper</code> using Python and BeautifulSoup.</li><li><code>Building a machine learning model</code> using Python and scikit-learn.</li></ul><h3>Internship Review</h3><p>An internship is a great way to gain practical experience and build your professional network. Here are some tips to help you find and secure an internship:</p><ul><li><strong>Research companies</strong>. Look for companies that align with your interests and goals.</li><li><strong>Update your resume</strong>. Make sure your resume is up-to-date and highlights your skills and experience.</li><li><strong>Practice your interview skills</strong>. Prepare answers to common interview questions and practice your responses.</li><li><strong>Network</strong>. Attend industry events and connect with professionals in your field.</li></ul><p>Some popular internship platforms include:</p><ul><li><code>Internships.com</code></li><li><code>WayUp</code></li><li><code>LinkedIn Internships</code></li></ul><h3>Python Review</h3><p>Before we move on to the final project and internship review, let\'s review some key Python concepts:</p><ul><li><code>Variables</code>. How to declare and use variables in Python.</li><li><code>Data types</code>. How to work with different data types in Python.</li><li><code>Control structures</code>. How to use if-else statements, for loops, and while loops in Python.</li><li><code>Functions</code>. How to define and use functions in Python.</li></ul><p>Here\'s a quick quiz to test your knowledge:</p><ul><li>What is the difference between a list and a tuple in Python?<ul><li>A. Lists are mutable, while tuples are immutable.</li><li>B. Lists are immutable, while tuples are mutable.</li><li>C. Lists are used for strings, while tuples are used for numbers.</li><li>D. Lists are used for numbers, while tuples are used for strings.</li></li><li>Which of the following is a valid Python identifier?<ul><li>A. 123</li><li>B. hello</li><li>C. 3.14</li><li>D. true</li></li><li>What is the output of the following Python code:<code>print(5 / 2)</code>;<ul><li>A. 2</li><li>B. 2.5</li><li>C. 5</li><li>D. 10</li></li><li>Which of the following is a built-in Python function?<ul><li>A. sort()</li><li>B. print()</li><li>C. len()</li><li>D. all()</li></li><li>What is the purpose of the <code>pass</code> statement in Python?<ul><li>A. To skip to the next iteration of a loop.</li><li>B. To skip to the next statement.</li><li>C. To indicate that a block of code should be executed.</li><li>D. To indicate that a block of code should be skipped.</li></li><li>Which of the following is a valid Python comment?<ul><li>A. # This is a comment</li><li>B. /* This is a comment */</li><li>C. // This is a comment</li><li>D. #This is a comment</li></li><li>What is the output of the following Python code:<code>print(5 % 2)</code>;<ul><li>A. 0</li><li>B. 1</li><li>C. 2</li><li>D. 5</li></li><li>Which of the following is a valid Python operator?<ul><li>A. +</li><li>B. -</li><li>C. *</li><li>D. all of the above</li></li><li>What is the purpose of the <code>return</code> statement in Python?<ul><li>A. To exit a function.</li><li>B. To skip to the next iteration of a loop.</li><li>C. To indicate that a block of code should be executed.</li><li>D. To indicate that a block of code should be skipped.</li></li></ul></ul>', 'Final Project and Internship Review', 1, '2026-05-25 18:54:15', '2026-05-25 18:54:15'),
(682, 695, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development tools and structure</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+tools+and+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+tools+and+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+tools+and+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+tools+and+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+tools+and+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Welcome to Web Development Internship</h3>\n<p><strong>Day 1 Objective:</strong> Understand the internship structure, essential development tools, and expectations for success.</p>\n\n<h3>Internship Structure</h3>\n<p>The internship is designed to build practical web development skills over <strong>12 weeks</strong>. Here\'s the breakdown:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Weekly Goals:</strong> Each week focuses on a specific theme (e.g., HTML/CSS, JavaScript, APIs).</li>\n  <li><strong>Daily Tasks:</strong> Includes hands-on coding challenges, code reviews, and short quizzes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Milestones:</strong> Bi-weekly projects to demonstrate progress (e.g., build a portfolio website).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Development Tools</h3>\n<p>Mastering these tools is essential for modern web development:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Code Editors:</strong> Use <code>VS Code</code> for writing clean, efficient code. Example:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;Hello World&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to Web Dev&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Version Control:</strong> Learn Git for tracking changes. Basic workflow:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">$ git init\n$ git add .\n$ git commit -m <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Initial commit\"</span>\n$ git push origin main</code></pre>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Browser DevTools:</strong> Use Chrome/Firefox tools to debug and inspect elements.</li>\n  <li><strong>Package Managers:</strong> Install dependencies with <code>npm</code>. Example:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">{\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"scripts\"</span>: {\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"start\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"node app.js\"</span>\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Expectations</h3>\n<p>To succeed in this internship:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Attendance:</strong> Attend all sessions and complete daily tasks on time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Participation:</strong> Ask questions during live sessions and contribute to group discussions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Code Quality:</strong> Write clean, readable code with proper indentation and comments.</li>\n  <li><strong>Communication:</strong> Use Slack for quick questions and email for formal communication.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Next Steps:</strong> Set up your development environment by installing VS Code, Git, and Node.js. Test your setup with the sample HTML file above.</p>', 'Welcome to Web Development Internship - Understanding the Internship Structure, Tools, and Expectations', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(683, 696, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development tools setup for beginners</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+tools+setup+for+beginners\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+tools+setup+for+beginners\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+tools+setup+for+beginners/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+tools+setup+for+beginners/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+tools+setup+for+beginners\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Task Objective</h3><p>By the end of this session, you will learn to navigate code editors, set up version control with Git, and understand the difference between code editors and text editors. You will create a simple HTML file, track changes using Git, and experiment with a text editor.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3><ul><li><strong>Code Editors:</strong> Tools like Visual Studio Code (VS Code) provide syntax highlighting, code suggestions, and extensions for web development.</li><li><strong>Version Control:</strong> Git helps track changes in code over time. Learn basic commands like <code>git init</code>, <code>git add</code>, and <code>git commit</code>.</li><li><strong>Text Editors:</strong> Lightweight editors like Sublime Text or Notepad++ focus on writing plain text without advanced coding features.</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-Step Instructions</h3><p><strong>Step 1: Set Up Your Code Editor</strong></p><p>Download and install <a href=\\\"https://code.visualstudio.com/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">Visual Studio Code</a>. Open VS Code and install the following extensions from the Extensions Marketplace:</p><ul><li><code>Live Server</code> (for previewing HTML pages)</li><li><code>GitLens</code> (for Git integration)</li></ul><p>Create a new folder named <code>internship-projects</code> and open it in VS Code.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 2: Write Your First HTML File</strong></p><p>In VS Code, create a file named <code>index.html</code> and add the following code:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;<!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My First Webpage&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello, World!&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Welcome to web development.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre><p>Use the <code>Live Server</code> extension to preview the page in your browser.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 3: Initialize Git Version Control</strong></p><p>Open the terminal in VS Code (<code>Terminal &gt; New Terminal</code>) and run these commands:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git init\nls -la\n</code></pre><p>Create a <code>.gitignore</code> file to exclude unnecessary files from version control. Add the following content:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Ignore node modules</span>\nnode_modules/\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Ignore system files</span>\n*.DS_Store</code></pre><p>Add and commit your changes:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git add index.html\ngit commit -m <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Initial commit: Created HTML file\"</span></code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Step 4: Experiment with a Text Editor</strong></p><p>Download and open <a href=\\\"https://www.sublimetext.com/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">Sublime Text</a>. Create a new file named <code>styles.css</code> and add this code:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">body {\n  background-color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f0f0f0;</span>\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#333;</span>\n}</code></pre><p>Save the file in your <code>internship-projects</code> folder. Note how Sublime Text differs from VS Code in features like auto-saving or plugin support.</p>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3><ul><li>Submit your <code>index.html</code> and <code>styles.css</code> files.</li><li>Provide a terminal screenshot showing your Git commit history (<code>git log</code>).</li><li>Write a 100-word summary comparing code editors and text editors.</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3><ul><li>Use VS Code\'s <code>Ctrl + Shift + E</code> (Windows) or <code>Cmd + Shift + E</code> (Mac) to open the Git panel and review changes.</li><li>Keep commit messages clear and descriptive for future reference.</li><li>Try opening the same HTML file in Notepad++ to observe differences in syntax highlighting.</li></ul>', 'Understanding the Internship Tools and Software', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(684, 697, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Node.js and npm logos</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Node.js+and+npm+logos\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Node.js+and+npm+logos\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Node.js+and+npm+logos/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Node.js+and+npm+logos/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Node.js+and+npm+logos\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Setting Up the Development Environment - Installing Node.js and npm</h3>\n<p>In this task, you will learn how to install Node.js and npm, which are essential tools for web development. You will also learn some basic terminal commands and how to use them to navigate through your project directory.</p>\n<h3>What is Node.js?</h3>\n<p>Node.js is a JavaScript runtime built on Chrome\'s V8 JavaScript engine. It allows developers to run JavaScript on the server-side, making it a popular choice for web development. Node.js provides a wide range of modules and tools that can be used to build fast, scalable, and secure web applications.</p>\n<h3>What is npm?</h3>\n<p>npm (Node Package Manager) is a package manager for Node.js. It allows developers to easily install, update, and manage dependencies for their projects. npm is a crucial tool for any Node.js developer, as it makes it easy to manage dependencies and ensure that your project is up-to-date with the latest versions of dependencies.</p>\n<h3>Installing Node.js and npm</h3>\n<p>To install Node.js and npm, you will need to download the installer from the official Node.js website. The installer will guide you through the installation process. Once installed, you can verify that Node.js and npm are working by opening a terminal and typing:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">node -v</code></pre>\n<p>This command will display the version of Node.js that is installed on your system. You can also use the following command to verify that npm is working:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">npm -v</code></pre>\n<h3>Basic Terminal Commands</h3>\n<p>Here are some basic terminal commands that you should know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>cd</code> - Change directory. This command is used to navigate through your project directory.</li>\n<li><code>ls</code> - List. This command is used to display a list of files and directories in the current directory.</li>\n<li><code>mkdir</code> - Make directory. This command is used to create a new directory.</li>\n<li><code>rm</code> - Remove. This command is used to delete a file or directory.</li>\n<li><code>touch</code> - Create a new file.</li>\n<li><code>cp</code> - Copy. This command is used to copy a file or directory.</li>\n<li><code>mv</code> - Move. This command is used to move a file or directory.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Using the Terminal to Navigate through Your Project Directory</h3>\n<p>To navigate through your project directory, you can use the following commands:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>cd ~</code> - Navigate to the home directory.</li>\n<li><code>cd /path/to/directory</code> - Navigate to a specific directory.</li>\n<li><code>cd ..</code> - Navigate to the parent directory.</li>\n<li><code>cd .</code> - Navigate to the current directory.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>In this task, you learned how to install Node.js and npm, which are essential tools for web development. You also learned some basic terminal commands and how to use them to navigate through your project directory. Practice using these commands to become more comfortable with the terminal.</p>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>What is Node.js?</li>\n<li>What is npm?</li>\n<li>How do you install Node.js and npm?</li>\n<li>What are some basic terminal commands?</li>\n<li>How do you use the terminal to navigate through your project directory?</li>\n</ol>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Example of using the terminal to navigate through your project directory</span>\ncd ~ <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Navigate to the home directory</span>\ncd /path/to/directory <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Navigate to a specific directory</span>\ncd .. <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Navigate to the parent directory</span>\ncd . <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Navigate to the current directory</span>\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Image Query</h3>\n<p>Search for images of Node.js and npm logos.</p>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Write a short essay on the importance of Node.js and npm in web development. Include examples of how you have used these tools in your previous projects.</p>\n<h3>Additional Resources</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://nodejs.org/en/\">Node.js Official Website</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/\">npm Official Website</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.w3schools.com/nodejs/\">Node.js Tutorial</a></li>\n</ul>', 'Setting Up the Development Environment - Installing Node.js and npm', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(685, 698, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: semantic html elements</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=semantic+html+elements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/semantic+html+elements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/semantic+html+elements/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/semantic+html+elements/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=semantic+html+elements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><pre><code>&lt;h3&gt;HTML Structure Basics&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;In this task, we will cover the basics of HTML structure, including HTML elements and attributes. We will also learn about semantic elements and how to use them to improve the accessibility and readability of our web pages.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;h3&gt;HTML Elements&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;HTML elements are the building blocks of HTML documents. They represent different types of content, such as headings, paragraphs, images, links, etc.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;pre&gt;\n&lt;!-- Example of an HTML element &lt;h1&gt; &lt;/h1&gt; -->\n&lt;h1&gt;Heading 1&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;/pre&gt;\n\n&lt;p&gt;In the above example, the &lt;h1&gt; element represents a heading. The text inside the element is the content of the heading.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;h3&gt;Attributes&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Attributes are used to provide additional information about an HTML element. They are key-value pairs that are added to the opening tag of an element.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;pre&gt;\n&lt;!-- Example of an HTML element with an attribute &lt;p id=\"description\"&gt; &lt;/p&gt; -->\n&lt;p id=\"description\"&gt;This is a paragraph with an id attribute.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/pre&gt;\n\n&lt;p&gt;In the above example, the &lt;p&gt; element has an id attribute set to \"description\". This attribute can be used to style or target the paragraph using CSS or JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;h3&gt;Semantic Elements&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Semantic elements are HTML elements that convey meaning to the structure and organization of a web page. They are used to provide context to search engines, screen readers, and other web crawlers.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;pre&gt;\n&lt;!-- Example of semantic elements &lt;header&gt;, &lt;nav&gt;, &lt;main&gt;, &lt;section&gt;, &lt;article&gt;, &lt;aside&gt;, &lt;footer&gt; -->\n&lt;header&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Header&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;nav&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Link 1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Link 2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;/header&gt;\n\n&lt;main&gt;\n  &lt;section&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Section 1&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph in Section 1.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n\n  &lt;article&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Article 1&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph in Article 1.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/article&gt;\n&lt;/main&gt;\n\n&lt;footer&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Footer&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/pre&gt;\n\n&lt;p&gt;In the above example, we have used semantic elements to create a basic web page structure. The &lt;header&gt; element contains the heading and navigation menu, the &lt;main&gt; element contains the main content, and the &lt;footer&gt; element contains the footer.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;h3&gt;Exercise&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Create a new HTML document and add the following elements: &lt;h1&gt;, &lt;h2&gt;, &lt;p&gt;, &lt;ul&gt;, &lt;li&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;, &lt;em&gt;, &lt;code&gt;, &lt;pre&gt;, &lt;blockquote&gt;, &lt;q&gt;, &lt;cite&gt;, &lt;abbr&gt;, &lt;address&gt;, &lt;ins&gt;, &lt;del&gt;, and &lt;samp&gt;. Use attributes to add styles and classes to the elements. Use semantic elements to create a basic web page structure.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;pre&gt;\n&lt;!-- Example of HTML elements with attributes and semantic elements -->\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Web Page&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;header&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Heading&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n\n    &lt;main&gt;\n      &lt;section&gt;\n        &lt;h2&gt;Section 1&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph in Section 1.&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/section&gt;\n\n      &lt;article&gt;\n        &lt;h2&gt;Article 1&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph in Article 1.&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/article&gt;\n    &lt;/main&gt;\n\n    &lt;footer&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Footer&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/footer&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n&lt;/pre&gt;\n\n&lt;p&gt;Remember to use HTML-escaped special characters and wrap code examples in &lt;code&gt;...&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;pre&gt;...&lt;/pre&gt; tags.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;h3&gt;Quiz&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;What is the purpose of semantic elements in HTML?&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;pre&gt;\n&lt;!-- Answer: Semantic elements are used to provide context to search engines, screen readers, and other web crawlers. -->\n&lt;/pre&gt;\n', 'HTML Structure Basics - Understanding HTML Elements and Attributes', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(686, 699, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: basic html structure</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=basic+html+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/basic+html+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/basic+html+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/basic+html+structure/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=basic+html+structure\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">--</span>\nBasic HTML Structure - Creating a Simple HTML File\n=====================================================\n\n**Task Objective:**\nCreate a simple HTML file that includes a basic structure, character encoding, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> various HTML tags.\n\n**Concepts You Need:**\n\n* HTML File Structure\n* Basic Tags\n* Character Encoding\n\n**Step-by-Step Instructions:**\n\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>. **Create a <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> HTML file**: Open a text editor <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">or</span> IDE <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> create a <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> file <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> a <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`.html`</span> extension. For example, you can name it <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`simple_html.html`</span>.\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>. **Add the DOCTYPE declaration**: The first line of your HTML file should be the DOCTYPE declaration, which indicates the document <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> version of HTML.\nhtml\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>. **Add the HTML tag**: The HTML tag <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the root element of an HTML document. It <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> used to define the document <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> version.\nhtml\n&lt;html&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>. **Add the HEAD tag**: The HEAD tag contains metadata about the document, such <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">as</span> the title, character encoding, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> links to external stylesheets <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">or</span> scripts.\nhtml\n&lt;head&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>. **Add the TITLE tag**: The TITLE tag specifies the title of the document, which appears <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> the browser\'s title bar <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> search engine results.\nhtml\n&lt;title&gt;Basic HTML Structure&lt;/title&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">6</span>. **Add the META tag**: The META tag provides metadata about the document, such <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">as</span> character encoding, author, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> description.\nhtml\n&lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;UTF-8&quot;</span>&gt;\n&lt;meta name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;author&quot;</span> content=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Your Name&quot;</span>&gt;\n&lt;meta name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;description&quot;</span> content=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Basic HTML Structure&quot;</span>&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">7</span>. **Add the BODY tag**: The BODY tag contains the content of the HTML document.\nhtml\n&lt;body&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">8</span>. **Add a heading**: Use the <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<h3>`</span> tag to add a heading to the document.\nhtml\n&lt;h3&gt;Basic HTML Structure&lt;/h3&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">9</span>. **Add a paragraph**: Use the <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<p>`</span> tag to add a paragraph to the document.\nhtml\n&lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a basic HTML structure example.&lt;/p&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>. **Add an unordered list**: Use the <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<ul>`</span> tag to add an unordered list to the document.\nhtml\n&lt;ul&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">11</span>. **Add list items**: Use the <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<li>`</span> tag to add list items to the unordered list.\nhtml\n&lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">12</span>. **Add strong text**: Use the <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<strong>`</span> tag to add strong text to the document.\nhtml\n&lt;strong&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> strong.&lt;/strong&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">13</span>. **Close the BODY <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> HTML tags**: Finally, close the BODY <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> HTML tags to complete the HTML document.\nhtml\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">14</span>. **Save the file**: Save the file <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">as</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`simple_html.html`</span>.\n\n**Deliverable:**\n\n* A simple HTML file named <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`simple_html.html`</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> a basic structure, character encoding, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> various HTML tags.\n\n**Tips:**\n\n* Use a text editor <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">or</span> IDE to create <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> edit the HTML file.\n* Save the file <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> a <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`.html`</span> extension.\n* Use the DOCTYPE declaration to indicate the document <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> version of HTML.\n* Use the HTML tag <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">as</span> the root element of the document.\n* Use the HEAD <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> BODY tags to contain metadata <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> content, respectively.\n* Use various HTML tags, such <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">as</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<h3>`</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<p>`</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<ul>`</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<li>`</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<strong>`</span>, to add structure <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> content to the document.\n* Use the META tag to provide metadata about the document.\n* Use the TITLE tag to specify the title of the document.\n\n**Quiz:**[]\n\n</code></pre>', 'Basic HTML Structure - Creating a Simple HTML File', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(687, 700, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS Basics - Understanding CSS Selectors and Basic Styling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+Basics+-+Understanding+CSS+Selectors+and+Basic+Styling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+Basics+-+Understanding+CSS+Selectors+and+Basic+Styling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+Basics+-+Understanding+CSS+Selectors+and+Basic+Styling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+Basics+-+Understanding+CSS+Selectors+and+Basic+Styling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+Basics+-+Understanding+CSS+Selectors+and+Basic+Styling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><html>\n<head>\n    <title>CSS Basics - Understanding CSS Selectors and Basic Styling</title>\n    <style>\n        /* CSS Styles */\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <h3>CSS Basics</h3>\n    <p>CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets. It is used to control the layout and appearance of web pages written in HTML or XML.</p>\n    <p>CSS is a styling language, which means it is used to add visual effects and styles to web pages.</p>\n    <p>In this internship, we will cover the basics of CSS selectors and basic styling.</p>\n    <h3>CSS Selectors</h3>\n    <p>CSS selectors are used to target specific elements in an HTML document and apply styles to them.</p>\n    <p>There are several types of CSS selectors, including:</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Element Selectors:</strong> These selectors target specific HTML elements, such as <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, etc.</li>\n        <li><strong>Class Selectors:</strong> These selectors target HTML elements with a specific class attribute, such as <code>&lt;p class=\"my-class\"&gt;</code>.</li>\n        <li><strong>ID Selectors:</strong> These selectors target HTML elements with a specific ID attribute, such as <code>&lt;p id=\"my-id\"&gt;</code>.</li>\n        <li><strong>Attribute Selectors:</strong> These selectors target HTML elements with a specific attribute, such as <code>&lt;a href=\"https://www.example.com\"&gt;</code>.</li>\n        <li><strong>Pseudo-Class Selectors:</strong> These selectors target HTML elements based on their state, such as <code>&lt;a:active&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;input:focus&gt;</code>.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3>CSS Syntax</h3>\n    <p>CSS syntax is made up of a selector, a declaration block, and a declaration.</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\nselector {\n    property: value;\n}\n    </code></pre>\n    <p>The selector is the part of the CSS code that targets the HTML element, the declaration block is the part that contains the property and value, and the declaration is the individual property and value pair.</p>\n    <p>Here is an example of a CSS selector, declaration block, and declaration:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\np {\n    color: blue;\n}\n    </code></pre>\n    <p>This CSS code targets all <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> elements on the page and sets their text color to blue.</p>\n    <h3>Basic Styling</h3>\n    <p>Basic styling in CSS includes setting font sizes, colors, backgrounds, and more.</p>\n    <p>Here is an example of basic styling in CSS:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\nh1 {\n    font-size: 36px;\n    color: red;\n    background-color: yellow;\n}\n    </code></pre>\n    <p>This CSS code targets all <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> elements on the page and sets their font size to 36 pixels, text color to red, and background color to yellow.</p>\n    <h3>Putting it all Together</h3>\n    <p>Here is an example of an HTML document with CSS styles applied:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;style&gt;\n        p {\n            color: blue;\n        }\n        h1 {\n            font-size: 36px;\n            color: red;\n            background-color: yellow;\n        }\n    &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n    </code></pre>\n    <p>This HTML document has CSS styles applied to the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> elements, which sets their font sizes, colors, and backgrounds.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n', 'CSS Basics - Understanding CSS Selectors and Basic Styling', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(688, 701, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css box model and positioning</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+box+model+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+box+model+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+box+model+and+positioning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+box+model+and+positioning/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+box+model+and+positioning\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Introduction - Understanding CSS Box Model and Positioning</h3><p>In this task, you will learn about the CSS box model and positioning. You will also understand how to use CSS layout to create a basic web page.</p><h4>HTML Code:</h4><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;CSS Box Model <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> Positioning&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h3&gt;CSS Box Model&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;The CSS box model <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a fundamental concept <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> CSS that describes how elements are rendered <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> a web page.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;The box model consists of four parts: margin, border, padding, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> content.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;The margin <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the space between the element <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> other elements.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;The border <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the visible border around the element.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;The padding <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the space between the border <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> the content.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;The content <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the actual content of the element.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;h3&gt;CSS Positioning&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;CSS positioning allows you to control the position of elements <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> a web page.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;There are five types of positioning: <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">static</span>, relative, absolute, fixed, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> sticky.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Static positioning <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the default positioning method.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Relative positioning allows you to move an element relative to its normal position.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Absolute positioning allows you to move an element to a specific position <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> the web page.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Fixed positioning allows you to fix an element at a specific position <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> the web page.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Sticky positioning allows you to stick an element to a specific position <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> the web page <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">as</span> the user scrolls.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;h3&gt;CSS Layout&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;CSS layout allows you to control the layout of elements <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> a web page.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;There are several types of layout: block, inline, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> grid.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Block layout allows you to create a block of elements.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Inline layout allows you to create a line of elements.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Grid layout allows you to create a grid of elements.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>', 'CSS Introduction - Understanding CSS Box Model and Positioning', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(689, 702, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS selectors and classes diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+selectors+and+classes+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+selectors+and+classes+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+selectors+and+classes+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+selectors+and+classes+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+selectors+and+classes+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Selectors and Classes: A Practical Guide</h3>\n\n<p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) allows you to control the appearance of HTML elements. This task focuses on <strong>CSS selectors</strong>, <strong>classes</strong>, and basic styling techniques. By the end of this session, you’ll be able to style web pages using targeted selectors and reusable classes.</p>\n\n<h3>What Are CSS Selectors?</h3>\n\n<p>CSS selectors are patterns used to select elements in an HTML document. They determine which elements a style rule applies to. There are several types of selectors:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Element Selectors:</strong> Target HTML elements by their tag name (e.g., <code>p</code>, <code>h1</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Class Selectors:</strong> Target elements with a specific class attribute (e.g., <code>.example</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>ID Selectors:</strong> Target a single element with a specific ID (e.g., <code>#unique</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Using Element Selectors</h3>\n\n<p>Element selectors apply styles to all instances of an HTML element. For example:</p>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n  p {\n    color: blue;\n    font-size: 16px;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n\n&lt;p&gt;This paragraph will be blue <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> 16px.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;So will <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">this</span> one!&lt;/p&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>In this example, all <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> elements inherit the specified styles.</p>\n\n<h3>Introducing CSS Classes</h3>\n\n<p>Classes allow you to apply styles to specific elements without affecting others. Define a class in CSS with a <code>.</code> prefix:</p>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n  .highlight {\n    background-color: yellow;\n    font-weight: bold;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n\n&lt;p <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=\"highlight<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;&gt;This text is highlighted.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;h2 class=\"highlight&quot;</span>&gt;This heading <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> also highlighted.&lt;/h2&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Multiple elements can share the same class, and a single element can have multiple classes:</p>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n  .bold-text {\n    font-weight: bold;\n  }\n  .red-text {\n    color: red;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n\n&lt;p <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=\"bold-text red-text&quot;&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> both bold <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> red.&lt;/p&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Combining Selectors</h3>\n\n<p>Use combinators to target specific relationships between elements:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Descendant Selector:</strong> <code>div p</code> targets <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> elements inside <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>s.</li>\n  <li><strong>Child Selector:</strong> <code>ul > li</code> targets direct child <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> elements of a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n  ul > li {\n    list-style-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>: square;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Practical Exercise</h3>\n\n<p>Create an HTML page with the following:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Three <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> elements with different classes (e.g., <code>.intro</code>, <code>.note</code>, <code>.warning</code>).</li>\n  <li>A <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> containing a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> with three <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> items.</li>\n  <li>Apply unique styles to each class and the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> elements using CSS selectors.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<p>Submit an HTML file and a CSS file (or embedded styles) that demonstrate:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Use of at least three different CSS selectors.</li>\n  <li>Application of classes to style elements uniquely.</li>\n  <li>Clear separation of structure (HTML) and presentation (CSS).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Use your browser’s developer tools (right-click → Inspect) to debug styles in real time.</li>\n  <li>Validate your HTML and CSS using <a href=\\\"https://validator.w3.org/\\\">W3C Validator</a>.</li>\n  <li>Start with simple styles (e.g., colors, fonts) and gradually add complexity.</li>\n</ul>', 'CSS Selectors and Classes', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(690, 703, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS ID selectors and pseudo-classes example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+ID+selectors+and+pseudo-classes+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+ID+selectors+and+pseudo-classes+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+ID+selectors+and+pseudo-classes+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+ID+selectors+and+pseudo-classes+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+ID+selectors+and+pseudo-classes+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Task Objective</h3><p>By the end of this task, you will understand how to use CSS ID selectors, pseudo-classes, and how specificity affects style application. You will create a styled HTML page demonstrating these concepts.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3><ul><li><strong>CSS ID Selectors:</strong> Select elements by their unique <code>id</code> attribute (e.g., <code>#header</code>).</li><li><strong>Pseudo-Classes:</strong> Apply styles based on user interaction or element state (e.g., <code>:hover</code>, <code>:nth-child(2)</code>).</li><li><strong>CSS Specificity:</strong> Determines which styles take priority when multiple rules apply to the same element. ID selectors have higher specificity than classes and pseudo-classes.</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-Step Instructions</h3>\n\n<h4>Step 1: Create the HTML Structure</h4><p>Build a simple HTML page with multiple elements. Assign unique <code>id</code> attributes and use semantic tags.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;link rel=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"stylesheet\"</span> href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"styles.css\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;div id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"header\"</span>&gt;&lt;h1&gt;My Page&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"highlight\"</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Hover over the button below&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;button id=\"special-button&quot;&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Step 2: Apply ID Selectors and Pseudo-Classes</h4><p>In your <code>styles.css</code> file, add rules using <code>#id</code> selectors and pseudo-classes:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">/* ID Selector <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> the header */\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#header {</span>\n  background-color: lightblue;\n  padding: 20px;\n}\n\n/* Pseudo-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> the second list item */\nul li:nth-child(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>) {\n  color: red;\n}\n\n/* Pseudo-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> hover state on the button */\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#special-button:hover {</span>\n  background-color: green;\n  color: white;\n}</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Step 3: Test Specificity Conflicts</h4><p>Create a scenario where ID selectors override other rules. Add this to your CSS:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">/* Class selector <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> lower specificity */\n.highlight {\n  color: orange;\n}\n\n/* Pseudo-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> medium specificity */\nul li:hover {\n  text-decoration: underline;\n}</code></pre><p>Observe how the <code>#id</code> selector for <code>ul li:nth-child(2)</code> overrides the <code>.highlight</code> class for the second list item.</p>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Submit your <code>index.html</code> and <code>styles.css</code> files. Ensure the page demonstrates ID selectors, pseudo-classes, and specificity in action.</p>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3><ul><li>Use browser developer tools to inspect elements and see which styles are applied.</li><li>Remember: ID selectors have higher specificity than classes and pseudo-classes.</li><li>Test how styles change when you hover over elements or resize the browser.</li></ul>', 'CSS ID Selectors and Pseudo-Classes', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(691, 704, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS layout diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+layout+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+layout+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+layout+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+layout+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+layout+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Layout Techniques</h3>\n<p>Understanding how to arrange elements on a webpage is one of the core skills for a front‑end developer.  In this lesson you will learn the fundamentals of CSS layout, how block and inline elements behave, and the different positioning models that give you control over placement.  By the end of the day you will be able to build a simple responsive page that uses <strong>flexbox</strong>, <strong>grid</strong>, and <strong>position</strong> properties to create a clean structure.</p>\n\n<h3>Block vs. Inline Elements</h3>\n<p>Every element in HTML can be classified as either block or inline.  Block elements start on a new line and take up the full available width unless a width is specified.  Inline elements flow within a line and only occupy the space required by their content.  Common block elements include <strong>div</strong>, <strong>section</strong>, <strong>article</strong>, and <strong>header</strong>.  Inline elements include <strong>a</strong>, <strong>span</strong>, <strong>strong</strong>, and <strong>em</strong>.</p>\n<p>Because block elements create a new line, they are ideal for structuring major sections of a page.  Inline elements are useful for styling text or small pieces of content without breaking the flow.</p>\n<p>Below is a simple example that shows the difference between a block and an inline element.  Notice how the block element <code>div</code> forces the following paragraph to start on a new line, while the inline <code>span</code> does not.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a block element.&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Paragraph after a block element.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;span&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> an inline element.&lt;/span&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Paragraph after an inline element.&lt;/p&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h3>CSS Layout Models</h3>\n<p>Modern CSS provides several layout models that let you design flexible, responsive interfaces.  The most widely used are <strong>Flexbox</strong> and <strong>Grid</strong>.  Both models are based on containers that hold child items, but they differ in how they distribute space and handle rows and columns.</p>\n\n<h4>Flexbox</h4>\n<p>Flexbox is designed for one‑dimensional layouts, meaning it handles either a row or a column at a time.  The container becomes a flex container when you apply <code>display: flex;</code> to it.  Child elements automatically become flex items.  Flexbox gives you powerful alignment options such as <code>justify-content</code> and <code>align-items</code>.</p>\n<p>Here is a minimal flexbox layout that creates a horizontal navigation bar.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;nav <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"navbar\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#home\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</span>\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#about\"&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</span>\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#contact\"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</span>\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;\n.navbar ul {\n  display: flex;\n  list-style: none;\n  padding: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n  margin: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n}\n.navbar li {\n  margin-right: 1rem;\n}\n.navbar a {\n  text-decoration: none;\n  color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#333;</span>\n}\n&lt;/style&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Grid</h4>\n<p>Grid is a two‑dimensional layout system that works with both rows and columns.  By setting <code>display: grid;</code> on a container, you can define a grid template and place items into specific cells.  Grid is ideal for complex page structures where both axes need to be controlled.</p>\n<p>The following example creates a simple three‑column layout for a blog page.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"grid-container\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;Header&lt;/header&gt;\n  &lt;aside&gt;Sidebar&lt;/aside&gt;\n  &lt;main&gt;Main Content&lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;footer&gt;Footer&lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;\n.grid-container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-areas:\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"header header header\"</span>\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"sidebar main main\"</span>\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"footer footer footer\"</span>;\n  grid-gap: 1rem;\n}\nheader { grid-area: header; }\naside  { grid-area: sidebar; }\nmain   { grid-area: main; }\nfooter { grid-area: footer; }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h3>CSS Positioning</h3>\n<p>Positioning determines where an element appears relative to its normal flow.  The four main values are <code>static</code>, <code>relative</code>, <code>absolute</code>, and <code>fixed</code>.  Each behaves differently and is useful in distinct scenarios.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Static</strong> is the default.  Elements are laid out according to the normal document flow.</li>\n  <li><strong>Relative</strong> keeps the element in the normal flow but allows you to offset it with <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, and <code>left</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Absolute</strong> removes the element from the flow entirely and positions it relative to the nearest positioned ancestor (an ancestor with a position value other than static).</li>\n  <li><strong>Fixed</strong> positions the element relative to the viewport, so it stays in place even when the page scrolls.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below is a demonstration of relative and absolute positioning.  The inner box is positioned absolutely inside the outer container.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"relative-box\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"absolute-box\"</span>&gt;Absolute Box&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;\n.relative-box {\n  position: relative;\n  width: 200px;\n  height: 200px;\n  background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f0f0f0;</span>\n  border: 1px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#ccc;</span>\n}\n.absolute-box {\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 20px;\n  left: 20px;\n  width: 100px;\n  height: 100px;\n  background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#ffcc00;</span>\n}\n&lt;/style&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Responsive Considerations</h3>\n<p>When designing layouts, responsiveness is key.  Flexbox and Grid both support media queries, allowing you to change the number of columns or the direction of flex items based on viewport width.  A common pattern is to stack columns vertically on narrow screens and display them side‑by‑side on wider screens.</p>\n<p>Here’s a quick example that changes a three‑column grid to a single column on screens smaller than 600px.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  .grid-container {\n    grid-template-areas:\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"header\"</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"sidebar\"</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"main\"</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"footer\"</span>;\n  }\n}\n&lt;/style&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Practical Exercise</h3>\n<p>Build a small webpage that includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A header that spans the full width.</li>\n  <li>A navigation bar using flexbox.</li>\n  <li>A main section with a two‑column layout using grid.</li>\n  <li>A sidebar that stays fixed on the right side of the viewport.</li>\n  <li>A footer that remains at the bottom of the page.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use the concepts covered today to implement the layout.  Test the page on different screen sizes to ensure it behaves responsively.  Submit the HTML and CSS files for review.</p>\n\n<h3>Tips for Success</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Start by sketching the layout on paper or using a design tool.</li>\n  <li>Use semantic tags (<code>header</code>, <code>nav</code>, <code>main</code>, <code>aside</code>, <code>footer</code>) to improve accessibility.</li>\n  <li>Remember that <code>display: flex;</code> and <code>display: grid;</code> are mutually exclusive; choose one based on the complexity of the layout.</li>\n  <li>When using <code>position: absolute;</code>, always set a <code>position</code> on the parent container to control the reference point.</li>\n  <li>Keep CSS organized by grouping related rules and using comments to describe sections.</li>\n</ul>', 'CSS Layout Techniques', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(692, 705, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css positioning and floats illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+positioning+and+floats+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+positioning+and+floats+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+positioning+and+floats+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+positioning+and+floats+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+positioning+and+floats+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Positioning and Floats</h3>\n<p>In this hands‑on session we will explore how CSS can control the placement of elements on a page.  The two core concepts are <strong>positioning</strong> and <strong>floats</strong>.  Positioning gives you explicit control over where an element sits relative to its containing block or the viewport.  Floats, on the other hand, allow elements to be taken out of the normal document flow and let text or other inline content wrap around them.  Mastering these techniques is essential for creating flexible, responsive layouts.</p>\n<h3>1. CSS Positioning</h3>\n<p>Positioning is defined by the <code>position</code> property, which can take one of four values: <code>static</code>, <code>relative</code>, <code>absolute</code>, and <code>fixed</code>.  The default value is <code>static</code>, meaning elements flow naturally in the document.  By changing the value, you can shift an element relative to its normal position or to a specific ancestor.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>static</strong> – the element follows the normal flow.  No top/left/right/bottom properties are applied.</li>\n<li><strong>relative</strong> – the element stays in its normal spot but can be nudged using top/left/right/bottom.  The space it originally occupied remains.</li>\n<li><strong>absolute</strong> – the element is removed from the flow and positioned relative to the nearest positioned ancestor (an element with a non‑static position).  If none exists, it uses the viewport.</li>\n<li><strong>fixed</strong> – similar to absolute but the reference is the viewport.  The element stays in place even when scrolling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below is a simple example that demonstrates each positioning type.  Notice how the <code>top</code> and <code>left</code> properties affect the element when it is not static.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n  .box {width: 120px; height: 120px; color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#fff; font-weight: bold; text-align: center; line-height: 120px;}</span>\n  .<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">static</span> {background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#e74c3c; position: static;}</span>\n  .relative {background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#3498db; position: relative; top: 20px; left: 20px;}</span>\n  .absolute {background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#2ecc71; position: absolute; top: 50px; left: 50px;}</span>\n  .fixed {background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#9b59b6; position: fixed; top: 10px; right: 10px;}</span>\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'box static\'</span>&gt;Static&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'box relative\'</span>&gt;Relative&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'box absolute\'</span>&gt;Absolute&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'box fixed\'</span>&gt;Fixed&lt;/div&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>When you run this code, you will see that the <strong>relative</strong> box is shifted 20 pixels down and 20 pixels to the right from its original spot.  The <strong>absolute</strong> box is positioned 50 pixels from the top of its nearest positioned ancestor (in this case, the body).  The <strong>fixed</strong> box stays in the top right corner of the viewport no matter how you scroll.</p>\n<h3>2. CSS Floats</h3>\n<p>Floats were originally designed to allow text to wrap around images.  Today, they are still useful for creating multi‑column layouts, but many developers prefer flexbox or grid for more complex designs.  Understanding floats is important because legacy code and many tutorials still rely on them.</p>\n<p>The <code>float</code> property can be set to <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, or <code>none</code>.  When an element is floated, it is removed from the normal flow and aligned to the left or right of its containing block.  Subsequent inline content will wrap around it.</p>\n<p>Here is a basic example that shows how two boxes float next to each other while text flows around them.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n  .<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">float</span>-left {width: 200px; height: 100px; background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f39c12; float: left; margin-right: 20px;}</span>\n  .<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">float</span>-right {width: 200px; height: 100px; background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#e67e22; float: right; margin-left: 20px;}</span>\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'float-left\'</span>&gt;Left Float&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'float-right\'</span>&gt;Right Float&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.  Pellentesque euismod, nisi vel consectetur interdum, nisl nisi aliquam nunc, eget consectetur lorem ipsum non magna.  Donec vel libero eu justo placerat consequat.  Cras non lectus at sapien dignissim faucibus.  Integer vel sem eget elit commodo aliquet.  Sed nec risus et turpis luctus porta.  Etiam at ligula eget lorem volutpat convallis.  Phasellus ut justo at metus aliquet sodales.  Nulla facilisi.  Vivamus at massa eu ex tempor varius.  Duis condimentum, justo at facilisis tristique, lorem magna vehicula justo, vitae viverra arcu metus a lorem.&lt;/p&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Notice how the paragraph text wraps around the floated boxes.  The <code>margin-right</code> and <code>margin-left</code> properties create space between the floats and the surrounding text.</p>\n<h3>3. Clearing Floats</h3>\n<p>When you float elements, the parent container may collapse because it no longer contains any block‑level children.  This can lead to layout issues where the parent’s background or border does not encompass the floated children.  The solution is to <strong>clear</strong> the floats.</p>\n<p>There are several techniques to clear floats:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Using the <code>clear</code> property on a subsequent element</strong> – set <code>clear: both</code> to force the element below the floats to start on a new line.</li>\n<li><strong>Adding a clearfix pseudo‑element</strong> – a common pattern that inserts an invisible element after the floats.</li>\n<li><strong>Using <code>overflow: hidden</code> or <code>auto</code> on the container</strong> – triggers a new block formatting context.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below we demonstrate the clearfix technique, which is widely supported and does not rely on extra markup.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n  .clearfix::after {\n    content: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"\"</span>;\n    display: table;\n    clear: both;\n  }\n  .container {border: 2px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#34495e; padding: 10px;}</span>\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'container clearfix\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'float-left\'</span>&gt;Float Left&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'float-right\'</span>&gt;Float Right&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;This paragraph <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> inside the container that has been cleared.  The container’s border now surrounds both floated elements <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> the text.  Without the clearfix, the border would collapse to the height of the first floated element only.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Alternatively, you can clear floats with an empty <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> that has <code>clear: both</code>:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'clear: both;\'</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>However, using the clearfix pseudo‑element keeps the markup cleaner and is considered best practice.</p>\n<h3>Practical Exercise</h3>\n<p>Now that you understand positioning and floats, try the following exercise:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create a page with a header, two sidebars, and a main content area.</li>\n<li>Use <code>position: fixed</code> for the header so it stays at the top.</li>\n<li>Float the left sidebar to the left and the right sidebar to the right.</li>\n<li>Clear the floats in the main content area so that it expands to fill the remaining space.</li>\n<li>Experiment with <code>relative</code> positioning to nudge the header slightly down.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When you are finished, verify that the layout behaves correctly when the page is resized and that the header remains visible during scrolling.</p>\n<h3>Key Takeaways</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <code>position</code> to control element placement relative to the document or an ancestor.</li>\n<li>Floats allow elements to sit side‑by‑side and let text wrap around them.</li>\n<li>Always clear floats to prevent container collapse.</li>\n<li>Modern layout techniques like flexbox and grid can replace many float use‑cases, but floats remain useful for legacy support.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Further Reading</h3>\n<p>To deepen your understanding, check out the MDN articles on <strong>CSS Positioning</strong> and <strong>CSS Floats</strong>.  Experiment with different values and observe how the layout changes.</p>', 'CSS Positioning and Floats', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(693, 706, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: box model box sizing css web development</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=box+model+box+sizing+css+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/box+model+box+sizing+css+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/box+model+box+sizing+css+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/box+model+box+sizing+css+web+development/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=box+model+box+sizing+css+web+development\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Box Model and Box Sizing</h3>\n<p>The CSS box model is a fundamental concept in web development that describes how an element\'s width and height are calculated. It consists of four main parts: content, padding, border, and margin.</p>\n<h3>CSS Box Model Structure</h3>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div&gt;\n  &lt;content&gt;\n  &lt;/content&gt;\n  &lt;pad&gt;\n  &lt;/pad&gt;\n  &lt;border&gt;\n  &lt;/border&gt;\n  &lt;margin&gt;\n  &lt;/margin&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Here\'s a breakdown of each part:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Content</strong>: The actual content of the element, such as text or images.</li>\n  <li><strong>Padding</strong>: The space between the content and the border.</li>\n  <li><strong>Border</strong>: The visible boundary around the element.</li>\n  <li><strong>Margin</strong>: The space between the element and other elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Box Sizing</h3>\n<p>Box sizing is a property that determines how the width and height of an element are calculated. There are two main values: <code>content-box</code> and <code>border-box</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Content-box</strong>: The width and height of the element are calculated by adding the padding and border widths to the content width and height.</li>\n  <li><strong>Border-box</strong>: The width and height of the element are calculated by adding the border and padding widths to the content width and height.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how box sizing works:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"width: 100px; height: 100px; padding: 10px; border: 5px solid black;\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Hello World!&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>When box sizing is set to <code>content-box</code>, the width and height of the element are calculated as follows:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">width = 100px (content width) + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> x 10px (padding width) + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> x 5px (border width) = 140px\nheight = 100px (content height) + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> x 10px (padding height) + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> x 5px (border height) = 140px</code></pre>\n<p>When box sizing is set to <code>border-box</code>, the width and height of the element are calculated as follows:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">width = 100px (content width) + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> x 5px (border width) = 110px\nheight = 100px (content height) + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> x 5px (border height) = 110px</code></pre>\n<h3>Border and Padding</h3>\n<p>Border and padding are two important properties of the box model. Border is the visible boundary around the element, while padding is the space between the content and the border.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how border and padding work:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"border: 5px solid black; padding: 10px;\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Hello World!&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>In this example, the border is 5 pixels wide and solid black, and the padding is 10 pixels wide. The width and height of the element are calculated by adding the padding and border widths to the content width and height.</p>\n<p>Here\'s a summary of the box model and box sizing:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The box model consists of four main parts: content, padding, border, and margin.</li>\n  <li>Box sizing determines how the width and height of an element are calculated.</li>\n  <li>There are two main values of box sizing: <code>content-box</code> and <code>border-box</code>.</li>\n  <li>Border and padding are two important properties of the box model.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">What <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the width <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> height of the following element when box sizing <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> set to content-box?\n\n&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"width: 100px; height: 100px; padding: 10px; border: 5px solid black;\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Hello World!&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Answer: The width and height of the element are 140px x 140px.</p>\n<p>What is the width and height of the following element when box sizing is set to <code>border-box</code>?\n\n&lt;div style=\"width: 100px; height: 100px; padding: 10px; border: 5px solid black;\"&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Hello World!&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Answer: The width and height of the element are 110px x 110px.</p>\n<h3>Task</h3>\n<p>Create a web page that demonstrates the box model and box sizing. Use the following HTML:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Box Model <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> Box Sizing&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Box Model <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> Box Sizing&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"width: 100px; height: 100px; padding: 10px; border: 5px solid black;\"</span>&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Hello World!&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"width: 100px; height: 100px; padding: 10px; border: 5px solid black; box-sizing: border-box;\"</span>&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Hello World!&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Use CSS to style the page and make the box model and box sizing clear. Test the page in different browsers to see how the box model and box sizing work in different environments.</p>\n<p>Deliverables:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A web page that demonstrates the box model and box sizing.</li>\n  <li>A written report that explains the box model and box sizing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Grading criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Accuracy of the box model and box sizing explanation.</li>\n  <li>Creativity and effectiveness of the web page design.</li>\n  <li>Clarity and completeness of the written report.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image query: box model, box sizing, css, web development.</p>\n<p>Quiz: [ ]</p>\n<p>Deliverable: [ ]</p>\n<p>Grading criteria: [ ]</p>', 'CSS Box Model and Box Sizing', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(694, 707, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css flexbox grid responsive design</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+flexbox+grid+responsive+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+flexbox+grid+responsive+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+flexbox+grid+responsive+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+flexbox+grid+responsive+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+flexbox+grid+responsive+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Flexbox and Grid Systems</h3>\n<p>In this day of your internship, you will learn about two powerful CSS technologies: Flexbox and Grid. You will understand how to use them to create responsive and flexible layouts.</p>\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>CSS Flexbox</li>\n<li>CSS Grid</li>\n<li>Responsive Design</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-Step Instructions</h3>\n<h4>Part 1: CSS Flexbox</h4>\n<p>Flexbox is a layout mode that allows you to create flexible and responsive layouts. It is particularly useful for creating layouts that need to adapt to different screen sizes.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;container&quot;</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>To make this layout flexible, we need to add the following CSS code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n.container {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n  justify-content: space-around;\n}\n\n.item {\n  width: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">30</span>%;\n  margin: 10px;\n  background-color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f0f0f0;</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here, we have used the `display: flex` property to enable Flexbox, `flex-wrap: wrap` to allow the items to wrap to the next line, and `justify-content: space-around` to distribute the items evenly.</p>\n<h4>Part 2: CSS Grid</h4>\n<p>CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout mode that allows you to create complex and responsive layouts. It is particularly useful for creating layouts that need to adapt to different screen sizes.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;container&quot;</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>To make this layout grid-based, we need to add the following CSS code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n.container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, 1fr);\n  grid-gap: 10px;\n}\n\n.item {\n  background-color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f0f0f0;</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here, we have used the `display: grid` property to enable Grid, `grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr)` to create a grid with three columns, and `grid-gap: 10px` to add a gap between the grid cells.</p>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Create a HTML file that demonstrates the use of both Flexbox and Grid. The HTML file should include the following elements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A container element that contains multiple item elements.</li>\n<li>The item elements should have different content and styles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The CSS file should include the following styles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A Flexbox layout that uses the `display: flex` property and other related properties.</li>\n<li>A Grid layout that uses the `display: grid` property and other related properties.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the difference between Flexbox and Grid?</li>\n<li>How do you enable Flexbox in an HTML element?</li>\n<li>How do you enable Grid in an HTML element?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Image Query</h3>\n<p>Search for images related to CSS Flexbox and Grid.</p>\n</h3>', 'CSS Flexbox and Grid Systems', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(695, 708, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive design css</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+design+css\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+design+css\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+design+css/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+design+css/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+design+css\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h1>CSS Flexbox and Grid Systems Practical Application</h1>\n<p>In this hands-on internship, you will learn how to use CSS Flexbox and Grid systems to create responsive and dynamic layouts. You will understand the basics of Flexbox and Grid, and how to apply them to real-world scenarios.</p>\n<h2>CSS Flexbox</h2>\n<p>CSS Flexbox is a layout mode that allows you to create flexible and responsive layouts. It is designed to be used in situations where the content is dynamic and the layout needs to adapt to different screen sizes.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;container&quot;</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.container {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n}\n\n.item {\n  width: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">30</span>%;\n  background-color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f0f0f0;</span>\n  padding: 20px;\n  margin: 10px;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>As you can see, the `.container` class uses the `display: flex` property to create a flexible container, and the `.item` class uses the `width`, `background-color`, `padding`, and `margin` properties to style the individual items.</p>\n<h2>CSS Grid</h2>\n<p>CSS Grid is a layout mode that allows you to create complex and dynamic layouts. It is designed to be used in situations where the content is complex and the layout needs to adapt to different screen sizes.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;container&quot;</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;item&quot;</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, 1fr);\n  grid-gap: 10px;\n}\n\n.item {\n  background-color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f0f0f0;</span>\n  padding: 20px;\n  margin: 10px;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>As you can see, the `.container` class uses the `display: grid` property to create a grid container, and the `grid-template-columns` property to define the number of columns and their widths. The `grid-gap` property is used to add a gap between the grid cells.</p>\n<h2>Responsive Design</h2>\n<p>Responsive design is the practice of creating layouts that adapt to different screen sizes and devices. To create a responsive layout, you can use media queries to apply different styles based on the screen size.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n  @media (max-width: 768px) {\n    .container {\n      display: block;\n    }\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>As you can see, the media query is used to apply different styles to the `.container` class when the screen width is less than 768px. In this case, the `display` property is set to `block` to create a block-level layout.</p>\n<h2>Quiz</h2>\n<p>Here are 10 multiple-choice questions to test your understanding of CSS Flexbox and Grid systems:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> quiz = [\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the CSS Flexbox layout mode?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a fixed layout\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a flexible layout\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a responsive layout\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"CSS Flexbox is designed to be used in situations where the content is dynamic and the layout needs to adapt to different screen sizes.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the CSS Grid layout mode?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a simple layout\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a complex layout\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a responsive layout\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"CSS Grid is designed to be used in situations where the content is complex and the layout needs to adapt to different screen sizes.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the `display: flex` property?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a fixed layout\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a flexible layout\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a responsive layout\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"The `display: flex` property is used to create a flexible container that can adapt to different screen sizes.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the `grid-template-columns` property?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the number of rows\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the number of columns\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the width of each column\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"The `grid-template-columns` property is used to define the number of columns and their widths.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the `grid-gap` property?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To add a gap between the grid cells\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To remove the gap between the grid cells\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To add a border around the grid cells\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"The `grid-gap` property is used to add a gap between the grid cells.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of media queries?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To apply different styles based on the screen size\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To apply different styles based on the device type\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To apply different styles based on the user\'s preferences\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Media queries are used to apply different styles based on the screen size.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the `display: block` property?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a flexible layout\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a responsive layout\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To create a block-level layout\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"The `display: block` property is used to create a block-level layout.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the `width` property?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the width of an element\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the height of an element\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the background color of an element\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"The `width` property is used to define the width of an element.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the `background-color` property?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the background color of an element\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the text color of an element\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To define the border color of an element\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"The `background-color` property is used to define the background color of an element.\"</span>},\n  {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"question\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"What is the purpose of the `padding` property?\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"options\"</span>:[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To add a margin around an element\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To add a padding around an element\"</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"To add a border around an element\"</span>],<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"correct_index\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"explanation\"</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"The `padding` property is used to add a padding around an element.\"</span>}\n];</code></pre>\n<p>Here is a short search query to practice your skills: <code>responsive design css</code></p>', 'CSS Flexbox and Grid Systems Practical Application', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(696, 709, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: html semantic tags accessibility</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=html+semantic+tags+accessibility\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/html+semantic+tags+accessibility\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/html+semantic+tags+accessibility/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/html+semantic+tags+accessibility/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=html+semantic+tags+accessibility\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>HTML5 Semantic Elements and Accessibility</h2>\n<p>In this task, you will learn about HTML5 semantic tags, accessibility attributes, and ARIA roles. You will create a detailed HTML document that covers all these topics.</p>\n\n<h3>HTML5 Semantic Tags</h3>\n<p>HTML5 semantic tags provide a way to add meaning to the structure of your document. They help search engines and screen readers understand the content of your page.</p>\n\n<code>&lt;header&gt;</code> is used for the header section of a document or a section.\n<code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> is used for navigation links.\n<code>&lt;main&gt;</code> is used for the main content of a document.\n<code>&lt;section&gt;</code> is used for a self-contained section of a document.\n<code>&lt;article&gt;</code> is used for an independent piece of content.\n<code>&lt;aside&gt;</code> is used for a piece of content that is related to the main content.\n<code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> is used for the footer section of a document or a section.\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;en&quot;</span>&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;UTF-8&quot;</span>&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;HTML5 Semantic Tags&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;header&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;HTML5 Semantic Tags&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;\n      &lt;ul&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;z0z\n        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</span>\n        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</span>\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;\n    &lt;main&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the main content of the page.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/main&gt;\n    &lt;section&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Section <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the content of section <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/section&gt;\n    &lt;article&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Article <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the content of article <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/article&gt;\n    &lt;aside&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Aside&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the content of the aside section.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/aside&gt;\n    &lt;footer&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Copyright <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2023</span>&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/footer&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Accessibility Attributes</h3>\n<p>Accessibility attributes help make your content accessible to people with disabilities. They provide alternative text for images and help screen readers understand the content of your page.</p>\n\n<code>alt</code> attribute is used to provide alternative text for an image.\n<code>title</code> attribute is used to provide a short description of an element.\n<code>aria-label</code> attribute is used to provide a label for an element.\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;img src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;image.jpg&quot;</span> alt=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;An image&quot;</span> title=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;An image with a title&quot;</span> /&gt;\n&lt;button aria-label=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Submit Button&quot;</span>&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h3>ARIA Roles</h3>\n<p>ARIA roles help screen readers understand the role of an element on a page. They provide a way to make your content accessible to people with disabilities.</p>\n\n<code>role</code> attribute is used to specify the role of an element.\n<code>aria-label</code> attribute is used to provide a label for an element.\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;button role=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;button&quot;</span> aria-label=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Submit Button&quot;</span>&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;div role=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;progressbar&quot;</span> aria-label=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Progress Bar&quot;</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div role=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;progressbar&quot;</span> aria-label=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Progress&quot;</span> style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;width: 50%&quot;</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Deliver a detailed HTML document that covers all the topics listed above. The document should have a minimum of 500 words of content.</p>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n<p>Use semantic tags to add meaning to the structure of your document.\nUse accessibility attributes to make your content accessible to people with disabilities.\nUse ARIA roles to help screen readers understand the role of an element on a page.</p>\n\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<p>What is the purpose of the <code>alt</code> attribute in HTML?</p>\n<p>What is the purpose of the <code>aria-label</code> attribute in HTML?</p>\n<p>What is the purpose of the <code>role</code> attribute in HTML?</p>\n\n<code>alt</code> attribute is used to provide alternative text for an image.\n<code>aria-label</code> attribute is used to provide a label for an element.\n<code>role</code> attribute is used to specify the role of an element.</p>', 'HTML5 Semantic Elements and Accessibility', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(697, 710, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css3 keyframe animations</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css3+keyframe+animations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css3+keyframe+animations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css3+keyframe+animations/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css3+keyframe+animations/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css3+keyframe+animations\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS3 Keyframe Animations</h3><p>In this task, you will learn how to create animations using CSS3 keyframe animations.</p><h4>@keyframes Syntax</h4><p>The @keyframes rule is used to define a set of keyframes that can be applied to an element. The syntax is as follows:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">@keyframes animation-name {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> {\n    /* styles */\n  }\n  to {\n    /* styles */\n  }\n}</code></pre><p>For example:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">@keyframes spin {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> {\n    transform: rotate(0deg);\n  }\n  to {\n    transform: rotate(360deg);\n  }\n}</code></pre><h4>Animation Properties</h4><p>The animation properties are used to control the animation. The most commonly used properties are:</p><ul><li><strong>animation-name:</strong> specifies the name of the @keyframes rule.</li><li><strong>animation-duration:</strong> specifies the length of time the animation takes to complete.</li><li><strong>animation-iteration-count:</strong> specifies the number of times the animation is played.</li><li><strong>animation-direction:</strong> specifies whether the animation plays forward or backward.</li></ul><p>For example:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.animated {\n  animation-name: spin;\n  animation-duration: 5s;\n  animation-iteration-count: infinite;\n  animation-direction: alternate;\n}</code></pre><h4>Timing Functions</h4><p>Timing functions are used to control the speed of the animation. The most commonly used timing functions are:</p><ul><li><strong>ease:</strong> the animation starts slowly and then speeds up.</li><li><strong>ease-in:</strong> the animation starts slowly and then speeds up.</li><li><strong>ease-out:</strong> the animation starts quickly and then slows down.</li><li><strong>ease-in-out:</strong> the animation starts slowly, speeds up, and then slows down.</li></ul><p>For example:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.animated {\n  animation-name: spin;\n  animation-duration: 5s;\n  animation-iteration-count: infinite;\n  animation-direction: alternate;\n  animation-timing-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>: ease-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span>;\n}</code></pre><p>Now, let\'s create a simple animation using CSS3 keyframe animations. Create an HTML file called <code>index.html</code> and add the following code:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <title>CSS3 Keyframe Animations</title>\n  <style>\n    @keyframes spin {\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> {\n        transform: rotate(0deg);\n      }\n      to {\n        transform: rotate(360deg);\n      }\n    }\n    \n    .animated {\n      animation-name: spin;\n      animation-duration: 5s;\n      animation-iteration-count: infinite;\n      animation-direction: alternate;\n      animation-timing-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>: ease-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span>;\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h3>CSS3 Keyframe Animations</h3>\n  <p>This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a simple animation <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">using</span> CSS3 keyframe animations.</p>\n  <div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"animated\"</span>>\n    <p>Animated text</p>\n  </div>\n</body>\n</html>\n</code></pre><p>Open the <code>index.html</code> file in a web browser to see the animation in action.</p><h4>Quiz</h4><p>What is the purpose of the @keyframes rule in CSS3?</p><p>What are the most commonly used animation properties in CSS3?</p><p>What are the most commonly used timing functions in CSS3?</p><p>How do you specify the name of the @keyframes rule in CSS3?</p><p>How do you specify the duration of the animation in CSS3?</p><p>How do you specify the number of times the animation is played in CSS3?</p><p>How do you specify the direction of the animation in CSS3?</p><p>How do you specify the timing function of the animation in CSS3?</p><p>What is the difference between the <code>ease</code>, <code>ease-in</code>, <code>ease-out</code>, and <code>ease-in-out</code> timing functions in CSS3?</p>', 'CSS3 Keyframe Animations', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(698, 711, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS transitions and transformations example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+transitions+and+transformations+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+transitions+and+transformations+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+transitions+and+transformations+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+transitions+and+transformations+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+transitions+and+transformations+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS3 Transitions and Transformations: Beginner Hands-On Task</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Task Objective:</strong> Learn to create smooth CSS3 transitions and apply transformations using transform functions. Build interactive hover effects for web elements.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Transition properties:</strong> <code>transition-property</code>, <code>transition-duration</code>, <code>transition-timing-function</code>, <code>transition-delay</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Transform functions:</strong> <code>translate()</code>, <code>rotate()</code>, <code>scale()</code>, <code>skew()</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Hover effects:</strong> Using <code>:hover</code> pseudo-class with transitions and transforms</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-Step Instructions</h3>\n\n<h4>Step 1: Create Base HTML Structure</h4>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;style&gt;\n      /* CSS will go here */\n    &lt;/style&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=\"box&quot;&gt;Hover Me&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Step 2: Add Basic Transition</h4>\n\n<p>Create a transition for background color change:</p>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.box {\n  width: 200px;\n  height: 200px;\n  background-color: lightblue;\n  transition-property: background-color;\n  transition-duration: 1s;\n  transition-timing-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>: ease-<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span>-out;\n  transition-delay: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>.2s;\n}\n\n.box:hover {\n  background-color: darkblue;\n}</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The <code>transition-property</code> specifies which property to animate. <code>transition-duration</code> controls how long the animation takes. <code>transition-timing-function</code> defines acceleration curve (common values: <code>linear</code>, <code>ease</code>, <code>ease-in</code>, <code>ease-out</code>, <code>ease-in-out</code>).</p>\n\n<h4>Step 3: Add Transformations</h4>\n\n<p>Apply scaling and rotation using <code>transform</code>:</p>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.box {\n  /* Existing styles */\n  transform: scale(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>) rotate(0deg);\n  transition-property: transform, background-color;\n}\n\n.box:hover {\n  background-color: darkblue;\n  transform: scale(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1.2</span>) rotate(15deg);\n}</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Transform Functions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>scale(x, y)</code>: Changes size</li>\n  <li><code>rotate(angle)</code>: Rotates element</li>\n  <li><code>translate(x, y)</code>: Moves position</li>\n  <li><code>skew(x-angle, y-angle)</code>: Skews element</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Step 4: Combine Multiple Transitions</h4>\n\n<p>Add transitions for multiple properties:</p>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.box {\n  transition: \n    background-color <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>.5s ease,\n    transform <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>.5s cubic-bezier(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.68</span>, -<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.55</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.27</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1.55</span>);\n}</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Shorthand Syntax:</strong> You can combine all transition properties into one declaration: <code>transition: property duration timing-function delay;</code></p>\n\n<h4>Step 5: Create Complex Hover Effects</h4>\n\n<p>Add a 3D transformation with perspective:</p>\n\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.box {\n  transition: transform <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>.8s ease;\n  transform-style: preserve-3d;\n}\n\n.box:hover {\n  transform: rotateX(30deg) rotateY(20deg) translateZ(50px);\n}</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>3D Transform Functions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>rotateX()</code>, <code>rotateY()</code>, <code>rotateZ()</code></li>\n  <li><code>translate3d(x, y, z)</code></li>\n  <li><code>scale3d(x, y, z)</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<p>Create an HTML file with:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>At least 3 elements with different transitions</li>\n  <li>2 elements using transform functions</li>\n  <li>Interactive hover effects combining transitions and transforms</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Use browser developer tools to test transitions in real-time</li>\n  <li>For smooth animations, animate properties like <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code> instead of <code>width</code> or <code>margin</code></li>\n  <li>Use <code>transition-delay</code> to create sequential animations</li>\n  <li>Test animations at different speeds using <code>transition-duration</code></li>\n</ul>', 'CSS3 Transitions and Transformations', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(699, 712, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript basics and variables tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+basics+and+variables+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+basics+and+variables+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+basics+and+variables+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+basics+and+variables+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+basics+and+variables+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 18: JavaScript Basics and Variables</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Task Objective:</strong> By the end of this task, you will understand JavaScript syntax structure, how to declare variables, and how to work with basic data types. You will write a simple HTML file that includes JavaScript code to demonstrate these concepts.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>JavaScript Syntax Structure:</strong> JavaScript is case-sensitive and uses semicolons to terminate statements. It supports single-line comments with <code>//</code> and multi-line comments with <code>/* ... */</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Variable Declarations:</strong> Use <code>var</code>, <code>let</code>, and <code>const</code> to declare variables. <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> are block-scoped, while <code>var</code> is function-scoped.</li>\n  <li><strong>Data Types:</strong> JavaScript has dynamic typing. Common data types include strings, numbers, booleans, <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, objects, and arrays.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-Step Instructions</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create an HTML file:</strong> Start with a basic HTML structure and include a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag to write JavaScript code.\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;JavaScript Basics&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to JavaScript&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// JavaScript code goes here</span>\n    &lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Write JavaScript syntax examples:</strong> Add code to demonstrate syntax rules.\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Single-line comment</span>\n      console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Hello, world!&quot;</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Prints to browser console</span>\n\n      /* Multi-line\n         comment example */\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> age = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">25</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">var</span> city = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;New York&quot;</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> PI = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3.14159</span>;\n    &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Declare variables with different scopes:</strong> Experiment with <code>let</code>, <code>var</code>, and <code>const</code>.\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Block scope example</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>) {\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> blockVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;I exist inside the block&quot;</span>;\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">var</span> functionVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;I exist everywhere&quot;</span>;\n      }\n\n      console.log(functionVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Works</span>\n      console.log(blockVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Error: blockVar is not defined</span>\n\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> MAX_SPEED = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// MAX_SPEED = 200; // Error: Cannot reassign a constant</span>\n    &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Work with data types:</strong> Assign and manipulate different data types.\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Strings</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> name = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Alice&quot;</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> greeting = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Hello, &quot;</span> + name;\n\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Numbers</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> price = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">19.99</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> tax = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.08</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> total = price * (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> + tax);\n\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Booleans</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> isLoggedIn = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> hasPermission = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">false</span>;\n\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Null and undefined</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> user = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">null</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> address;\n      console.log(user); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// null</span>\n      console.log(address); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// undefined</span>\n\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Objects</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> car = {\n        make: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Toyota&quot;</span>,\n        model: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Corolla&quot;</span>,\n        year: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2022</span>\n      };\n\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Arrays</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> colors = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"red\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"green\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"blue\"</span>];\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> numbers = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>];\n    &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Test your code:</strong> Open the HTML file in a browser and use the developer console (F12) to check for errors and view output.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>A complete HTML file named <code>javascript_basics.html</code> containing all the code examples above.</li>\n  <li>A screenshot of the browser console showing the output of your JavaScript code.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Prefer <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> over <code>var</code> for better scoping.</li>\n  <li>Use <code>console.log()</code> to debug and inspect variables.</li>\n  <li>JavaScript is dynamically typed, so you can reassign variables to different data types.</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript Basics and Variables', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(700, 713, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript operators visual guide</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+operators+visual+guide\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+operators+visual+guide\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+operators+visual+guide/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+operators+visual+guide/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+operators+visual+guide\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Day 19: JavaScript Operators and Expressions</h3>\n\n<p>Welcome to Day 19 of your web development internship! Today, you will explore JavaScript operators and expressions. These are fundamental building blocks for performing calculations, comparing values, and assigning data to variables. By the end of this task, you will create a simple HTML page with embedded JavaScript that demonstrates arithmetic, comparison, and assignment operators.</p>\n\n<h3>Task Objective</h3>\n\n<p>Create an HTML file that explains and demonstrates JavaScript operators. Your task includes writing code examples for arithmetic, comparison, and assignment operators, and displaying their results on the webpage.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Arithmetic Operators</strong>: Used to perform mathematical operations like addition, subtraction, etc.</li>\n  <li><strong>Comparison Operators</strong>: Used to compare values and return a boolean result (true/false).</li>\n  <li><strong>Assignment Operators</strong>: Used to assign values to variables efficiently.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-Step Instructions</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create the HTML structure</strong>:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"UTF-8\"</span>&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;JavaScript Operators Demo&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;JavaScript Operators Demo&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;div id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"output\"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Your JavaScript code goes here</span>\n    &lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Demonstrate Arithmetic Operators</strong>:\n    <p>Add the following code inside the &lt;script&gt; tag to perform arithmetic operations:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> a = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>, b = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> result = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`&lt;h3&gt;Arithmetic Operators&lt;/h3&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`10 + 3 = ${a + b}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`10 - 3 = ${a - b}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`10 * 3 = ${a * b}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`10 / 3 = ${a / b}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`10 % 3 = ${a % b}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`10 ** 3 = ${a ** b}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\ndocument.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'output\'</span>).innerHTML = result;</code></pre>\n    <p>This code calculates addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, modulus, and exponentiation.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Demonstrate Comparison Operators</strong>:\n    <p>Extend the code to compare values:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> x = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>, y = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'5\'</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`&lt;h3&gt;Comparison Operators&lt;/h3&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`5 == \'5\' is ${x == y}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`5 === \'5\' is ${x === y}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`10 > 3 is ${10 > 3}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`3 < 10 is ${3 < 10}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`5 >= 5 is ${x >= 5}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`5 !== 5 is ${x !== 5}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\ndocument.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'output\'</span>).innerHTML = result;</code></pre>\n    <p>Note the difference between <code>==</code> (loose equality) and <code>===</code> (strict equality).</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Demonstrate Assignment Operators</strong>:\n    <p>Add code to show assignment shortcuts:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> num = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>;\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`&lt;h3&gt;Assignment Operators&lt;/h3&gt;`</span>;\nnum += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>; <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Same as num = num + 5</span>\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`num += 5: ${num}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nnum *= <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>; <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Same as num = num * 2</span>\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`num *= 2: ${num}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\nnum %= <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">7</span>; <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Same as num = num % 7</span>\nresult += <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`num %= 7: ${num}&lt;br&gt;`</span>;\ndocument.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'output\'</span>).innerHTML = result;</code></pre>\n    <p>These operators simplify updating variable values.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Test and Save</strong>:\n    <p>Open the HTML file in a browser. Verify that all operator results are displayed correctly. Save the file as <code>operators-demo.html</code>.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>A working HTML file named <code>operators-demo.html</code> that demonstrates all three types of operators.</li>\n  <li>Ensure the output includes clear labels for each operator category and uses proper formatting.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Use <code>console.log()</code> for debugging if the output is not as expected.</li>\n  <li>Test edge cases, like dividing by zero or comparing different data types.</li>\n  <li>Review operator precedence rules if results seem unexpected (e.g., <code>2 + 3 * 4</code>).</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript Operators and Expressions', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(701, 714, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript control flow basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+control+flow+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+control+flow+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+control+flow+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+control+flow+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+control+flow+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Task Objective</h3><p>Today\'s task focuses on JavaScript control flow fundamentals. You will practice evaluating expressions, writing simple <code>if</code> statements, and using logical operators to make decisions in code. By the end, you will create a working HTML file with embedded JavaScript that demonstrates these concepts.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3><ul><li><strong>Expression Evaluation</strong>: Understand how JavaScript evaluates expressions to produce values (e.g., arithmetic, comparisons).</li><li><strong>Simple <code>if</code> Statements</code></strong>: Learn to execute code conditionally based on boolean results.</li><li><strong>Logical Operators</strong>: Use <code>&amp;&amp;</code> (AND), <code>||</code> (OR), and <code>!</code> (NOT) to combine or negate conditions.</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-Step Instructions</h3>\n\n<h4>1. Expression Evaluation</h4><p>JavaScript evaluates expressions to produce values. For example, <code>5 + 3</code> evaluates to <code>8</code>, and <code>10 > 5</code> evaluates to <code>true</code>. Try the following:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n  console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span> + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> * <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Evaluates to 16</span>\n  console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">15</span> % <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Evaluates to 3</span>\n  console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"apple\"</span>.length === <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Evaluates to true</span>\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p>Open your browser\'s developer console (F12) to view the output. Modify the values and see how the results change.</p>\n\n<h4>2. Simple <code>if</code> Statements</h4><p>Use <code>if</code> statements to run code only when a condition is true. Example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> age = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">16</span>;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (age &gt;= <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">18</span>) {\n    alert(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"You are an adult.\"</span>);\n  } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n    alert(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"You are a minor.\"</span>);\n  }\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p><strong>Task</strong>: Create a new HTML file and add this code. Test it with different <code>age</code> values. What happens when <code>age = 18</code>? What about <code>age = 0</code>?</p>\n\n<h4>3. Logical Operators</h4><p>Combine conditions using logical operators. For example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> temperature = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">25</span>;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> isSunny = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>;\n\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (temperature &gt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">20</span> &amp;&amp; isSunny) {\n    alert(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Perfect weather for a picnic!\"</span>);\n  }\n\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (temperature &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span> || isSunny === <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">false</span>) {\n    alert(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Stay indoors.\"</span>);\n  }\n\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (!isSunny) {\n    alert(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Don\'t forget an umbrella.\"</span>);\n  }\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p><strong>Task</strong>: Modify the <code>temperature</code> and <code>isSunny</code> variables. Observe how the alerts change based on the logical combinations. Can you rewrite the second condition using <code>&amp;&amp;</code> instead of <code>||</code>?</p>\n\n<h4>4. Final Task</h4><p>Create a single HTML file that includes all the above examples. Add a new <code>if</code> statement to check if a user is eligible for a service: they must be at least 21 years old OR have a premium subscription. Example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> userAge = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">20</span>;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> isPremium = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>;\n\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (userAge &gt;= <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">21</span> || isPremium) {\n    alert(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Access granted.\"</span>);\n  } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n    alert(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Access denied.\"</span>);\n  }\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p>Test this with different combinations of <code>userAge</code> and <code>isPremium</code>. Ensure the logic works as expected.</p>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Submit a single HTML file named <code>control_flow.html</code> that includes all your code examples. The file should run in a browser and demonstrate correct control flow behavior.</p>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3><ul><li>Use <code>console.log()</code> to debug intermediate values.</li><li>Test edge cases, like <code>age = 18</code> or <code>temperature = 20</code>.</li><li>Ensure all HTML tags are properly closed and nested.</li></ul>', 'JavaScript Control Flow Basics', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(702, 715, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript functions and scope diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+functions+and+scope+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+functions+and+scope+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+functions+and+scope+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+functions+and+scope+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+functions+and+scope+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Functions and Scope</h3>\n\n<p>Welcome to Day 21 of your web development internship! Today, you\'ll learn about JavaScript functions and scope—two fundamental concepts that will help you write organized, reusable, and bug-free code. By the end of this session, you\'ll be able to define functions, pass parameters, return values, and understand how variable scope works in JavaScript.</p>\n\n<h3>Task Objective</h3>\n\n<p>Build a simple JavaScript program that demonstrates function declarations, parameters/return values, and scope concepts. You\'ll also complete a quiz to reinforce your understanding.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Function declarations:</strong> Functions are reusable blocks of code defined with the <code>function</code> keyword.</li>\n  <li><strong>Parameters and return values:</strong> Parameters act as placeholders for values passed into a function. Return values send results back to the caller.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scope concepts:</strong> Variables declared inside a function are <em>local</em> and inaccessible outside. Variables declared outside are <em>global</em> and accessible everywhere.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-Step Instructions</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create a basic function:</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> greet() {\n        console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Hello, world!&quot;</span>);\n      }\n      greet(); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Call the function</span>\n    &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This function <code>greet()</code> logs a message when called.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Add parameters to a function:</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> add(a, b) {\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> a + b;\n      }\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> result = add(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>);\n      console.log(result); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Output: 8</span>\n    &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Here, <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are parameters. The function returns their sum.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Use return values:</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> multiply(x, y) {\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> x * y;\n      }\n      document.write(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Product: &quot;</span> + multiply(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">6</span>)); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Output: Product: 24</span>\n    &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>The <code>return</code> keyword sends the result back to the caller.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Demonstrate scope:</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> globalVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;I\'m global&quot;</span>;\n\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> testScope() {\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> localVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;I\'m local&quot;</span>;\n        console.log(globalVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Accessible</span>\n        console.log(localVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Accessible</span>\n      }\n\n      testScope();\n      console.log(localVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Error: localVar is not defined</span>\n    &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p><code>globalVar</code> is accessible everywhere, while <code>localVar</code> exists only inside <code>testScope()</code>.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Explore block scope with <code>let</code> and <code>const</code>:</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>) {\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> blockVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;I\'m in a block&quot;</span>;\n        console.log(blockVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Accessible here</span>\n      }\n      <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// console.log(blockVar); // Error: blockVar is not defined</span>\n    &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Variables declared with <code>let</code> or <code>const</code> are block-scoped and not visible outside their block.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Create an <code>index.html</code> file with all the code examples above.</li>\n  <li>Run the code in your browser\'s developer console and verify the output.</li>\n  <li>Answer the quiz questions provided at the end of this task.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Use <code>console.log()</code> to debug and verify function outputs.</li>\n  <li>Always declare variables with <code>let</code> or <code>const</code> instead of <code>var</code> to avoid scope-related bugs.</li>\n  <li>Break down complex tasks into smaller functions for better readability and reusability.</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript Functions and Scope', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(703, 716, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript data types diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+data+types+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+data+types+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+data+types+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+data+types+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+data+types+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Task Objective</h3>\n<p>The goal of today’s internship task is to create a simple web page that demonstrates the fundamentals of JavaScript data types and arrays. By the end of the day, you should be able to declare variables, understand the built‑in data types, create and manipulate arrays, and use console output to verify your results.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Variables and Declarations</strong> – Using <code>var</code>, <code>let</code>, and <code>const</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Primitive Data Types</strong> – <code>Number</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>Boolean</code>, <code>Null</code>, <code>Undefined</code>, and <code>Symbol</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Objects</strong> – Basic object literals and the <code>typeof</code> operator.</li>\n<li><strong>Arrays</strong> – Creation, indexing, length property, and common methods such as <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, and <code>forEach</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Console Logging</strong> – Using <code>console.log</code> to output values and debug.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>JavaScript Basics</h3>\n<p>JavaScript is a dynamically typed, interpreted language that runs in the browser or on a server via Node.js. Variables are containers that hold data. The keyword <code>var</code> is the original way to declare a variable, but <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> are recommended for block‑scoped declarations. A <code>const</code> variable cannot be reassigned, making it useful for values that should remain constant.</p>\n\n<p>Here’s a quick example of declaring a variable and logging it to the console:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> greeting = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Hello, world!\'</span>;\nconsole.log(greeting);</code></pre>\n\n<p>Notice that we used single quotes inside the code block to avoid any double‑quote characters that could interfere with JSON string formatting.</p>\n\n<h3>Data Types</h3>\n<p>JavaScript has several primitive data types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Number</strong> – Handles both integers and floating‑point numbers. Example: <code>let age = 25;</code></li>\n<li><strong>String</strong> – Text enclosed in quotes. Example: <code>let name = \'Alice\';</code></li>\n<li><strong>Boolean</strong> – Represents truth values <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Null</strong> – A deliberate assignment of no value. Example: <code>let empty = null;</code></li>\n<li><strong>Undefined</strong> – A variable that has been declared but not assigned. Example: <code>let notSet;</code></li>\n<li><strong>Symbol</strong> – A unique identifier, often used as object property keys.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The <code>typeof</code> operator is handy for inspecting the type of a value:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">console.log(typeof <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">42</span>);          <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// number</span>\nconsole.log(typeof <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'hi\'</span>);        <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// string</span>\nconsole.log(typeof <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>);       <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// boolean</span>\nconsole.log(typeof <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">null</span>);       <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// object (quirk)</span>\nconsole.log(typeof undefined);  <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// undefined</span>\nconsole.log(typeof Symbol());   <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// symbol</span></code></pre>\n\n<p>Objects are collections of key/value pairs. Even though <code>null</code> returns \"object\" with <code>typeof</code>, it is a special case in JavaScript.</p>\n\n<h3>Arrays</h3>\n<p>Arrays are ordered lists that can hold values of any type. They are created using square brackets:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> colors = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'red\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'green\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'blue\'</span>];</code></pre>\n\n<p>You can access elements by their zero‑based index:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">console.log(colors[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>]); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// red</span></code></pre>\n\n<p>The <code>length</code> property tells you how many items are in the array:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">console.log(colors.length); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// 3</span></code></pre>\n\n<p>Common array methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>push</code> – Adds an element to the end.</li>\n<li><code>pop</code> – Removes the last element.</li>\n<li><code>forEach</code> – Executes a function for each element.</li>\n<li><code>map</code> – Transforms each element into a new array.</li>\n<li><code>filter</code> – Creates a new array with elements that pass a test.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Example of using <code>push</code> and <code>forEach</code>:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">colors.push(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'yellow\'</span>);\ncolors.forEach(<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>(color, index) {\n  console.log(index + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\': \'</span> + color);\n});</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Step‑by‑Step Instructions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Create a new folder named <code>js-intro</code> on your desktop.</li>\n<li>Inside the folder, create a file called <code>index.html</code> and open it in your text editor.</li>\n<li>Add the basic HTML skeleton:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"en\"</span>&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"UTF-8\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;JavaScript Data Types &amp; Arrays&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Data Types &amp; Arrays Demo&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;script&gt;\n    <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Your JavaScript will go here</span>\n  &lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<li>Inside the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag, declare variables for each data type and log them to the console.</li>\n<li>Create an array of at least five items. Log the array, its length, and each element using <code>forEach</code>.</li>\n<li>Use <code>push</code> to add a new element, then <code>pop</code> to remove the last element. Log the array after each operation.</li>\n<li>Test your page by opening <code>index.html</code> in a browser and checking the console (press <code>F12</code> or right‑click → Inspect).</li>\n<li>Save your work and commit the changes to a local Git repository if you have one.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Your final deliverable is a single <code>index.html</code> file that, when opened in a browser, logs the following to the console:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Values and types of at least five primitive variables.</li>\n<li>The original array and its length.</li>\n<li>Each element of the array with its index.</li>\n<li>The array after adding a new element.</li>\n<li>The array after removing the last element.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the browser console to debug. It shows errors and the output of <code>console.log</code>.</li>\n<li>Remember that array indices start at 0.</li>\n<li>When working with <code>const</code> arrays, you can modify the contents (push/pop) but cannot reassign the variable to a new array.</li>\n<li>Try experimenting with different array methods like <code>map</code> or <code>filter</code> after you finish the basic tasks.</li>\n<li>Keep your code clean: use semicolons, consistent indentation, and descriptive variable names.</li>\n</ul>', 'Introduction to JavaScript: Data Types and Arrays', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(704, 717, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript array methods illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+array+methods+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+array+methods+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+array+methods+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+array+methods+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+array+methods+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Arrays: Methods and Operations</h3>\n<p>Arrays are one of the most fundamental data structures in JavaScript. They allow you to store an ordered collection of values, which can be of any type—numbers, strings, objects, or even other arrays. In today’s hands‑on internship, you will learn how to create arrays, manipulate them with built‑in methods, and perform common operations that are essential for everyday web development. By the end of the day, you will have a small interactive web page that demonstrates each method and lets you experiment with your own data.</p>\n\n<h3>Task Objective</h3>\n<p>Build a single‑page application that showcases the most frequently used JavaScript array methods. The page should contain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A section that explains each method with a brief description.</li>\n<li>Live code examples that the user can edit and run in the browser.</li>\n<li>A short quiz (optional) to reinforce learning.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Array Basics</strong>: declaration, indexing, length property.</li>\n<li><strong>Mutating Methods</strong>: <code>push()</code>, <code>pop()</code>, <code>shift()</code>, <code>unshift()</code>, <code>splice()</code>, <code>sort()</code>, <code>reverse()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Non‑mutating Methods</strong>: <code>slice()</code>, <code>concat()</code>, <code>join()</code>, <code>map()</code>, <code>filter()</code>, <code>reduce()</code>, <code>forEach()</code>, <code>find()</code>, <code>findIndex()</code>, <code>includes()</code>, <code>indexOf()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Iteration Techniques</strong>: traditional <code>for</code> loop, <code>for…of</code>, <code>forEach()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance Considerations</strong>: when to use each method, mutability vs immutability.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step‑by‑Step Instructions</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Set Up Your Project</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Create a folder named <code>array‑methods-demo</code>.</li>\n<li>Inside, create <code>index.html</code>, <code>style.css</code>, and <code>app.js</code>.</li>\n<li>Link the CSS and JS files in <code>index.html</code>.\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;link rel=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;stylesheet&quot;</span> href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;style.css&quot;</span>&gt;\n&lt;script src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;app.js&quot;</span> defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Design the Layout</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> for each method.</li>\n<li>Include a <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> for code input and a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> to run it.</li>\n<li>Display the result in a <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> element.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Implement the Runner</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>In <code>app.js</code>, add an event listener that captures the code from the textarea, evaluates it with <code>eval()</code> (sandboxed for safety), and outputs the result.\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">document.querySelectorAll(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'.run-btn\'</span>).forEach(btn =&gt; {\n  btn.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'click\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> code = btn.parentElement.querySelector(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'textarea\'</span>).value;\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">try</span> {\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> result = eval(code);\n      btn.parentElement.querySelector(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'.output\'</span>).textContent = String(result);\n    } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">catch</span> (e) {\n      btn.parentElement.querySelector(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'.output\'</span>).textContent = e.message;\n    }\n  });\n});\n</code></pre>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Populate the Sections</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>For each method, write a short description inside a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag.</li>\n<li>Provide a default code snippet in the textarea that demonstrates the method’s usage.\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> numbers = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> doubled = numbers.map(n =&gt; n * <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>);\nconsole.log(doubled);\n</code></pre>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add Interactive Examples</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Include examples that cover edge cases, such as removing elements from an empty array or searching for a non‑existent value.</li>\n<li>Show both mutating and non‑mutating variants so students can see the difference.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Test Thoroughly</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Run the page in multiple browsers to ensure compatibility.</li>\n<li>Verify that the <code>eval()</code> sandbox does not expose global variables.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Prepare the Deliverable</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Commit the project to a GitHub repository.</li>\n<li>Write a brief README that explains the structure and how to run the demo locally.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>A GitHub repository named <code>array-methods-demo</code> containing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>index.html</code> – the main page with all sections.</li>\n<li><code>style.css</code> – minimal styling (no custom colors).</li>\n<li><code>app.js</code> – the script that runs user code.</li>\n<li><code>README.md</code> – instructions and a brief explanation of each method.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use Descriptive Variable Names</strong>: It makes the code easier to read and debug.</li>\n<li><strong>Prefer Non‑mutating Methods When Possible</strong>: They keep the original array intact, which is safer in functional programming patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>Understand the Return Value</strong>: Methods like <code>splice()</code> return the removed elements, while <code>push()</code> returns the new length.</li>\n<li><strong>Test with Large Data Sets</strong>: Observe performance differences between <code>forEach()</code> and a traditional <code>for</code> loop.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep Security in Mind</strong>: Never use <code>eval()</code> with untrusted input in production.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Sample Code Snippets</h3>\n<p>Below are a few key methods with examples. Remember to escape HTML characters inside the <code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;</code> blocks.</p>\n\n<h4>push() – Add to the End</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\narr.push(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// arr is now [1, 2, 3, 4]</span>\nconsole.log(arr.length); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// 4</span>\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>pop() – Remove from the End</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> last = arr.pop(); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// last is 3, arr is [1, 2]</span>\nconsole.log(last);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>shift() – Remove from the Start</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> first = arr.shift(); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// first is 1, arr is [2, 3]</span>\nconsole.log(first);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>unshift() – Add to the Start</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\narr.unshift(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// arr is [1, 2, 3]</span>\nconsole.log(arr);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>splice() – Insert or Remove</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>];\narr.splice(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">20</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// remove 2 items at index 1, insert 10 and 20</span>\nconsole.log(arr); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// [1, 10, 20, 4]</span>\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>slice() – Copy a Portion</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> part = arr.slice(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// [2, 3]</span>\nconsole.log(part);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>concat() – Merge Arrays</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> a = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> b = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> merged = a.concat(b); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// [1, 2, 3, 4]</span>\nconsole.log(merged);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>join() – Convert to String</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">str</span> = arr.join(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'-\'</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// \'1-2-3\'</span>\nconsole.log(<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">str</span>);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>forEach() – Iterate</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\narr.forEach((num, idx) =&gt; {\n  console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`Index ${idx}: ${num}`</span>);\n});\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>map() – Transform</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> doubled = arr.map(n =&gt; n * <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// [2, 4, 6]</span>\nconsole.log(doubled);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>filter() – Select</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> evens = arr.filter(n =&gt; n % <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> === <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// [2, 4]</span>\nconsole.log(evens);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>reduce() – Aggregate</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> sum = arr.reduce((acc, n) =&gt; acc + n, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// 10</span>\nconsole.log(sum);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>find() – Search</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> firstEven = arr.find(n =&gt; n % <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> === <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// 2</span>\nconsole.log(firstEven);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>includes() – Check Presence</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\nconsole.log(arr.includes(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>)); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// true</span>\nconsole.log(arr.includes(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>)); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// false</span>\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>sort() – Order Elements</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>];\narr.sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// [1, 2, 3, 4]</span>\nconsole.log(arr);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>reverse() – Reverse Order</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\narr.reverse(); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// [3, 2, 1]</span>\nconsole.log(arr);\n</code></pre>\n\n<h4>indexOf() – Find Index</h4>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\nconsole.log(arr.indexOf(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>)); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// 1</span>\nconsole.log(arr.indexOf(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>)); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// -1</span>\n</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Quiz (Optional)</h3>\n<p>After completing the hands‑on tasks, you can add a short quiz to test your understanding. For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What method would you use to add an element to the beginning of an array without mutating the original array?</li>\n<li>Which method returns the sum of all elements in an array?</li>\n<li>How do you check if an array contains the value 10?</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Wrap‑Up</h3>\n<p>By the end of this day, you should be comfortable creating arrays, using both mutating and non‑mutating methods, and understanding how each operation affects the data structure. Practice by extending the demo: add a section that demonstrates how to combine multiple methods in a chain, or how to work with nested arrays and objects.</p>', 'JavaScript Arrays: Methods and Operations', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(705, 718, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: JavaScript object illustration</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=JavaScript+object+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/JavaScript+object+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/JavaScript+object+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/JavaScript+object+illustration/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=JavaScript+object+illustration\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Objects: Understanding Properties and Methods</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 24 of your web development internship! Today we’ll dive deep into one of JavaScript’s core building blocks: <strong>objects</strong>. By the end of this session you should be able to create objects, add and modify properties, define methods, and understand the difference between data and behavior inside an object. The hands‑on exercise will culminate in a small interactive page that displays user information and lets you update it on the fly.</p>\n<h3>What You’ll Learn</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What an object is in JavaScript and why it’s useful.</li>\n<li>How to define properties and access them using dot and bracket notation.</li>\n<li>How to add, update, and delete properties after an object has been created.</li>\n<li>What a method is, how to declare one inside an object, and how the <code>this</code> keyword works.</li>\n<li>Common pitfalls such as accidental property shadowing and mutating objects unintentionally.</li>\n<li>Practical application: building a simple profile card that updates in real time.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Concepts You Need</h3>\n<p>Before we start coding, make sure you’re comfortable with the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Basic JavaScript syntax (variables, functions, arrays).</li>\n<li>Understanding of the global object and scope.</li>\n<li>Familiarity with the DOM (Document Object Model) and event listeners.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step‑by‑Step Instructions</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create a new HTML file</strong> called <code>objects.html</code> and add the basic boilerplate:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"en\"</span>&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"UTF-8\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;meta http-equiv=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"X-UA-Compatible\"</span> content=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"IE=edge\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"viewport\"</span> content=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"</span>&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;JavaScript Objects Demo&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;User Profile&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;div id=\"profile<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;button id=\"updateBtn&quot;</span>&gt;Update Name&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;script src=\"objects.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Write the JavaScript file</strong> <code>objects.js</code>. Start by defining a user object with a few properties:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> user = {\n  firstName: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'John\'</span>,\n  lastName: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Doe\'</span>,\n  age: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">28</span>,\n  email: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'john.doe@example.com\'</span>\n};\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Accessing properties</strong> – demonstrate dot and bracket notation. Add a console.log to verify both work:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">console.log(user.firstName); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// dot notation</span>\nconsole.log(user[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'lastName\'</span>]); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// bracket notation</span>\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Adding a new property</strong> after the object has been created:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">user.country = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'USA\'</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// or</span>\nuser[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'city\'</span>] = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'New York\'</span>;\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Updating a property</strong> – change the age:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">user.age = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">29</span>;\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Deleting a property</strong> – remove the email:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">delete</span> user.email;\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Defining a method inside the object</strong> that returns the full name:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">user.getFullName = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`</span>;\n};\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Using <code>this</code> inside a method</strong> – rewrite the method to use <code>this</code> for better practice:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">user.getFullName = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`${this.firstName} ${this.lastName}`</span>;\n};\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Display the user data in the DOM</strong> – create a helper function to render the profile card:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> renderProfile() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> profileDiv = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'profile\'</span>);\n  profileDiv.innerHTML = `\n    &lt;h2&gt;${user.getFullName()}&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Age: ${user.age}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Country: ${user.country}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;City: ${user.city}&lt;/p&gt;\n  `;\n}\nrenderProfile();\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Add interactivity</strong> – when the button is clicked, prompt the user to enter a new first name and update the object:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'updateBtn\'</span>).addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'click\'</span>, () => {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> newName = prompt(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Enter a new first name:\'</span>, user.firstName);\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (newName) {\n    user.firstName = newName;\n    renderProfile();\n  }\n});\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Optional: Use Object.freeze</strong> to make the object immutable after initialization and demonstrate what happens when you try to modify it:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">Object.freeze(user);\nuser.age = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">30</span>; <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// this will fail silently or throw in strict mode</span>\nconsole.log(user.age); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// still 29</span>\n</code></pre>\n<li><strong>Test your page</strong> – open <code>objects.html</code> in a browser, check the console for logs, and try updating the name. Observe how the DOM updates instantly.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Your final submission should include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>objects.html</code> – the HTML skeleton with a <code>div</code> for the profile and a button.</li>\n<li><code>objects.js</code> – the JavaScript file that defines the user object, methods, and DOM manipulation.</li>\n<li>A screenshot of the page after clicking the button and updating the name.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Tips &amp; Common Mistakes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use <code>const</code> for the object reference</strong> but remember that the object’s internal properties are still mutable unless you freeze it.</li>\n<li><strong>Prefer dot notation</strong> for static keys, but bracket notation is essential when keys are dynamic or contain special characters.</li>\n<li><strong>Beware of <code>this</code> binding</strong> – if you extract the method into a variable or pass it as a callback, <code>this</code> may lose context. Use arrow functions or <code>bind</code> if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the UI in sync</strong> – after any property change, call <code>renderProfile()</code> to update the DOM.</li>\n<li>When experimenting, open the browser console to see real‑time logs and catch errors early.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n<p>After completing the exercise, answer the following in a short paragraph:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>How does JavaScript’s object model differ from class‑based languages you might have used before?</li>\n<li>Why is <code>Object.freeze</code> useful, and when might you want to use it?</li>\n<li>What are the pros and cons of using <code>this</code> inside object methods?</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript Objects: Understanding Properties and Methods', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(706, 719, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript conditional statements</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+conditional+statements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+conditional+statements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+conditional+statements/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+conditional+statements/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+conditional+statements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript Conditional Statements</h3>\n<p>Conditional statements are used to execute different blocks of code based on certain conditions. They help in making decisions in your program and are essential for any programming language. In this task, we will cover JavaScript conditional statements, including if-else statements and switch statements.</p>\n<h3>JavaScript Conditional Statements</h3>\n<p>JavaScript conditional statements are used to execute different blocks of code based on certain conditions. They help in making decisions in your program and are essential for any programming language.</p>\n<h3>If-Else Statements</h3>\n<p>If-else statements are used to execute a block of code if a certain condition is true. If the condition is false, another block of code is executed.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// If-else statement example</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">var</span> x = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (x > <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>) {\n    console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;x is greater than 10&#39;);</span>\n} <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n    console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;x is less than or equal to 10&#39;);</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Switch Statements</h3>\n<p>Switch statements are used to execute a block of code based on the value of a variable. They are used when you have multiple conditions to check.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Switch statement example</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">var</span> day = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">switch</span> (day) {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">case</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>:\n        console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Monday&#39;);</span>\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">break</span>;\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">case</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>:\n        console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Tuesday&#39;);</span>\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">break</span>;\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">case</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>:\n        console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Wednesday&#39;);</span>\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">break</span>;\n    default:\n        console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Invalid day&#39;);</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Using Conditional Statements in Real-World Applications</h3>\n<p>Conditional statements are used in various real-world applications, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Checking user input: Conditional statements can be used to check user input and validate it before processing it.</li>\n    <li>Conditional formatting: Conditional statements can be used to apply different styles to different elements based on certain conditions.</li>\n    <li>Conditional logic: Conditional statements can be used to apply different logic to different situations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Best Practices for Using Conditional Statements</h3>\n<p>Here are some best practices for using conditional statements:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Use meaningful variable names: Use variable names that clearly indicate what they represent.</li>\n    <li>Use indentation: Use indentation to make your code more readable.</li>\n    <li>Use comments: Use comments to explain what your code is doing.</li>\n    <li>Keep it simple: Avoid using complex conditional statements. Instead, break them down into simpler ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<p>Here are some quiz questions to test your understanding of conditional statements:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>What is the output of the following code? <code>if (x > 10) { console.log(&#39;x is greater than 10&#39;); } else { console.log(&#39;x is less than or equal to 10&#39;); }</code> if x is 5?</li>\n    <li>What is the output of the following code? <code>switch (day) { case 1: console.log(&#39;Monday&#39;); break; case 2: console.log(&#39;Tuesday&#39;); break; case 3: console.log(&#39;Wednesday&#39;); break; default: console.log(&#39;Invalid day&#39;); }</code> if day is 4?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Image Query</h3>\n<p>Search for images related to JavaScript conditional statements.</p>\n', 'Introduction to JavaScript Conditional Statements', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(707, 720, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: short search query</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=short+search+query\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/short+search+query\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/short+search+query/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/short+search+query/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=short+search+query\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Conditional Statements: Real-World Examples</h3>\n<p>In this task, you will learn about JavaScript conditional statements and see real-world examples of how they are used. You will also learn best practices for using conditional statements in your code.</p>\n<h3>What are Conditional Statements?</h3>\n<p>Conditional statements are used to execute different blocks of code based on certain conditions. They are used to control the flow of a program and make decisions.</p>\n<h3>Types of Conditional Statements in JavaScript</h3>\n<p>JavaScript has several types of conditional statements, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>If Statement</strong></li>\n<li><strong>If-Else Statement</strong></li>\n<li><strong>If-Else-If Statement</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Switch Statement</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1. If Statement</h3>\n<p>The if statement is used to execute a block of code if a certain condition is true. Here is an example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (age &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">18</span>) {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;You are a minor&#39;);</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>2. If-Else Statement</h3>\n<p>The if-else statement is used to execute a block of code if a certain condition is true, and another block of code if the condition is false. Here is an example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> age = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">25</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (age &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">18</span>) {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;You are a minor&#39;);</span>\n} <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;You are an adult&#39;);</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>3. If-Else-If Statement</h3>\n<p>The if-else-if statement is used to execute a block of code if a certain condition is true, and another block of code if another condition is true. Here is an example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> age = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">25</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (age &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">18</span>) {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;You are a minor&#39;);</span>\n} <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (age &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">65</span>) {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;You are an adult&#39;);</span>\n} <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;You are a senior&#39;);</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>4. Switch Statement</h3>\n<p>The switch statement is used to execute a block of code based on the value of a variable. Here is an example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> day = &<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Monday&#39;;</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">switch</span> (day) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">case</span> &<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Monday&#39;:</span>\n    console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Today is Monday&#39;);</span>\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">break</span>;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">case</span> &<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Tuesday&#39;:</span>\n    console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Today is Tuesday&#39;);</span>\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">break</span>;\n  default:\n    console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Today is another day&#39;);</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Real-World Examples of Conditional Statements</h3>\n<p>Conditional statements are used in many real-world applications, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Login Systems</strong>: Conditional statements are used to check the username and password of a user.</li>\n<li><strong>Game Development</strong>: Conditional statements are used to check the score of a player and determine the next level.</li>\n<li><strong>Weather Forecasting</strong>: Conditional statements are used to check the weather conditions and provide a forecast.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Best Practices for Using Conditional Statements</h3>\n<p>Here are some best practices for using conditional statements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keep it Simple</strong>: Use simple conditional statements instead of complex ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Use Meaningful Variable Names</strong>: Use meaningful variable names to make your code easy to understand.</li>\n<li><strong>Use Comments</strong>: Use comments to explain what your code is doing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<p>What is the output of the following code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> age = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">25</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (age &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">18</span>) {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;You are a minor&#39;);</span>\n} <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;You are an adult&#39;);</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Answer: You are an adult</p>\n<h3>Search Query</h3>\n<p>Short search query: <code>Conditional Statements in JavaScript</code></p>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>You will deliver a report on the types of conditional statements in JavaScript, their real-world examples, and best practices for using them.</p>\n<h3>Image Query</h3>\n<p>Short search query: <code>Conditional Statements in JavaScript</code></p>\n', 'JavaScript Conditional Statements: Real-World Examples', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
(708, 721, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript looping statements</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+looping+statements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+looping+statements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+looping+statements/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+looping+statements/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+looping+statements\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h2>JavaScript Looping Statements: For, While, and Do-While</h2>\n<p>In this task, we\'ll explore JavaScript looping statements, which allow us to execute a block of code repeatedly for a specified number of times or until a condition is met. We\'ll cover three types of loops: for loops, while loops, and do-while loops.</p>\n<h3>For Loops</h3>\n<p>A for loop is a type of loop that allows us to iterate over an array or a string. It consists of three parts: initialization, condition, and increment.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> (<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> i = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>; i &lt; array.length; i++) {\n    console.log(array[i]);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here\'s a breakdown of the three parts:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Initialization: This is where we initialize a variable that will be used to keep track of the loop\'s progress.</li>\n    <li>Condition: This is where we specify the condition that must be met for the loop to continue executing.</li>\n    <li>Increment: This is where we increment the variable used in the initialization step.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a for loop that prints the numbers from 1 to 5:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> (<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> i = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>; i &lt;= <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>; i++) {\n    console.log(i);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>While Loops</h3>\n<p>A while loop is a type of loop that allows us to execute a block of code as long as a certain condition is met. It\'s similar to a for loop, but it doesn\'t have an initialization or increment part.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> i = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">while</span> (i &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>) {\n    console.log(i);\n    i++;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a while loop that prints the numbers from 1 to 5:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> i = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">while</span> (i &lt;= <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>) {\n    console.log(i);\n    i++;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Do-While Loops</h3>\n<p>A do-while loop is a type of loop that allows us to execute a block of code at least once, and then continue executing it as long as a certain condition is met. It\'s similar to a while loop, but the condition is checked at the end of the loop instead of the beginning.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> i = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">do</span> {\n    console.log(i);\n    i++;\n} <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">while</span> (i &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a do-while loop that prints the numbers from 1 to 5:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> i = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">do</span> {\n    console.log(i);\n    i++;\n} <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">while</span> (i &lt;= <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>);\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Example Use Cases</h3>\n<p>Here are some example use cases for each type of loop:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>For loop: Use a for loop to iterate over an array of numbers and print each number.</li>\n    <li>While loop: Use a while loop to print the numbers from 1 to 5.</li>\n    <li>Do-while loop: Use a do-while loop to print the numbers from 1 to 5, but only execute the loop if the condition is met.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<p>Try answering the following questions:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>What is the difference between a for loop and a while loop?</li>\n    <li>What is the purpose of the initialization, condition, and increment parts in a for loop?</li>\n    <li>How do you use a do-while loop to print the numbers from 1 to 5?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>In this task, we\'ve covered JavaScript looping statements, including for loops, while loops, and do-while loops. We\'ve seen examples of each type of loop and discussed their use cases. By mastering these loops, you\'ll be able to write more efficient and effective code.</p>\n<p>Now it\'s your turn to practice! Try writing your own examples of each type of loop and see how they work.</p>\n<h3>References</h3>\n<p>Here are some resources to help you learn more about JavaScript looping statements:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><a href=\'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/for\'>MDN Web Docs: for statement</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/while\'>MDN Web Docs: while statement</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/do...while\'>MDN Web Docs: do...while statement</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Code Examples</h3>\n<p>Here are some code examples to help you practice:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><code>for (let i = 0; i &lt; array.length; i++) { console.log(array[i]); }</code></li>\n    <li><code>let i = 0; while (i &lt; 5) { console.log(i); i++; }</code></li>\n    <li><code>let i = 0; do { console.log(i); i++; } while (i &lt; 5);</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Image Search Query</h3>\n<p>Search for images related to JavaScript looping statements.</p>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<p>[]</p>', 'JavaScript Looping Statements: For, While, and Do-While', 1, '2026-06-12 11:20:50', '2026-06-12 11:20:50'),
